\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for August 28, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Peter Alexander, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, abortion backlash.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT PAT RYAN, (D) NEW YORK: I honestly can`t believe it. I cannot believe it!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republican concerns grow as Democrats show election strength after the Supreme Court`s decision on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): There are five -- five, now -- special elections since the Roe v. Wade decision, and they`ve all come our way.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: The MAGA Republicans have awakened the powerful force in America, the women of this nation!\par \par \uc2 TODD: How abortion is hurting Republican chances of a big red wave in November.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Mar-a-Lago affidavit, 184 classified documents found at Mar-a- Lago in January. And the Justice Department believes the Trump team attempted to obstruct justice.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m not going to comment on it because I don`t know the details.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to Republican congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  of the January 6th Committee.\par \par \uc2 Also, the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): We are fighting for the future of our children, a future for our country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A stalemate at the front, a nuclear reactor in jeopardy and a mystery murder of a Putin ally. Six months into the war, I`ll talk to former NATO Supreme Allied Commander \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2  and former U.S. ambassador to Russia Michael McFaul.\par \par \uc2 And, return to the moon.\par \par \uc2 NASA ADMINISTRATOR BILL NELSON: This time, we`re going back. We`re going to live there. We`re going to learn there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My conversation with \uc2 NASA\uc2  Chief Bill Nelson about tomorrow`s unmanned Artemis I mission and a new era of U.S. space exploration.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Join me for insight and analysis are NBC News chief White House correspondent Peter Alexander; Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for The Boston Globe; Rich Lowry, editor of National Review; and PBS NewsHour chief correspondent Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history. This is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court released its Dobbs abortion decision, Republicans cheered the culmination of a nearly 50-year effort to overturn Roe v. Wade and return the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 But since then, states under Republican control have moved to severely limit access to abortion, four more just in the past few days. It has sparked a surge of Democratic anger and enthusiasm at the polls and is suggesting that the predicted red wave in this year`s midterm elections is being blunted by a strong blue undertow.\par \par \uc2 Since the Dobbs decision, Democrats have topped Joe Biden`s 2020 performance in four special House elections. It`s a sharp turnaround from the special elections that were held prior to the Dobbs decision, where Republicans were overperforming. And of course there was the shocking 18- point defeat in Kansas of that anti-abortion ballot measure at the start of this month. In short, Republicans do have an abortion problem, politically, and you can see they know it by the way many now are backing off their more extreme positions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO, (R) FLORIDA: I`m in favor of laws that protect human life. I do not believe that the dignity and the worth of human life is tied to the circumstances of their conception. But I recognize that that`s not a majority position and therefore I have always said I support bills that have exceptions.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE IN ARIZONA: I support a ban on very late term and partial birth abortion. And most Americans agree with that.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY SMILEY, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE IN WASHINGTON STATE: I`m pro- life, but I oppose a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: At the same time, Democrats are working hard to pivot and make abortion rights the issue of the midterm, like Pat Ryan did in that special election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: How can we be a free country if the government tries to control women`s bodies?\par \par \uc2 That`s not the country I fought to defend.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Lake wouldn`t just ban abortion. She`s criminalize it, force pregnancies for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to do something a little different. We`re going to start this morning with the politics of abortion and our expert panel, which includes PBS NewsHour chief correspondent Amna Nawaz; NBC News chief White House correspondent Peter Alexander; Rich Lowry, the editor of National Review; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, the senior opinion writer for the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rich, I keep coming back to a moment you and I were on the air -- I think it was after the leaked pre-Dobbs decision -- where you were, like, "Look, the midterms are going on, a trajectory in favor of the Republicans, but abortion could change everything." Are we at the "change everything" moment?\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, EDITOR, NATIONAL REVIEW: I don`t know whether it changes everything, but clearly it`s an important change. And I think Republicans have to realize they can`t run and hide from this issue. You try to do that, it`s not going to work. You`re going to get defined in a way you`re not going to like. And you can`t, in most places, adopt a maximalist position.\par \par \uc2 So I think they need to do a version of what Rubio did, say, "I want to protect every unborn child eventually. I realize I have a lot of work to do, public persuasion, on that.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:04]\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, here`s a limit I support that actually is defensible and has public support," and then point at the other side for being extremist for supporting abortion in every circumstance with federal funding.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Kimberly, that is what \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  in Arizona attempted to do. And when he debuted this ad, he also tried to scrub his website. And, you know, we could show, right before he was -- his website on the abortion issue was "I`m 100 percent pro-life." We could put it up. And he was in fact a supporter of the federal personhood law. He got rid of that.\par \par \uc2 Now, they have said -- his spokesperson said he still believes everything that was on there before. But it feels like, you know, they hit on it slightly differently than \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, SENIOR OPINION WRITER, BOSTON \uc2 GLOBE\uc2 : They did. And in that ad, if you listen to what he`s saying and what he`s saying he supports, it`s actually what was allowed under Roe. It was the law before Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s more than just Republican realizing that their rhetoric is wrong right now. I think they`re realizing where their rhetoric meets actual, real-life consequences, whether it`s a 10-year-old victim of a crime who`s being forced into a pregnancy, or a 19-year-old woman in South Carolina whose fertility may be threatened due to the fact that she can`t get the medical care she needs after an incomplete miscarriage. How is that pro-life? Republicans are realizing that they are not doctors; they are not physicians; and they didn`t realize the consequences of what they were supporting.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s funny you bring up the South Carolina incident. I want to actually play that lawmaker. He talked about this -- this person you`re talking about. Her water broke at week 15, and basically it was against South Carolina law for them to do anything about it. Here`s his description.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. NEAL COLLINS, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: She`s going to pass this fetus in the toilet. She is going to have to deal with that on her own. There`s a 50 percent chance, greater than 50 percent chance, that she`s going to lose her uterus. There`s a 10 percent chance that she will develop sepsis and herself die. That weighs on me. I voted for that bill. These are affecting people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amna, this is -- you know, we`ve talked -- every metaphor; they`ve caught the car. And it goes to something Rich said. Republicans weren`t prepared for every aspect of what they needed to talk about when it came to this issue.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ, \uc2 PBS\uc2  NEWSHOUR CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Not nearly. I think that`s abundantly clear. Now, and it`s not just Republicans. You know, I`ve talked to so many folks on the ground. I`ve been reporting on the potential reversal of Roe for so many months leading up to the decision. And you saw even the groups on the ground who were advocating under this very broad pro-life umbrella were not prepared to answer very detailed questions about what does that mean? When do you make exceptions? How do you view IVF? If a child is raped in your state, how do you view that?\par \par \uc2 And you saw divisions within the Republican Party and among self-described conservatives showing up in exactly those cases. The child who was raped and impregnated in Ohio, even state officials there and pro-life advocates on the ground disagreed about how to handle that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, Glenn Youngkin, the governor of Virginia, was in Michigan. Our reporters caught up with him and he`s, "Look. you know, the only people I`m talking to are worried about the cost of living, worried about kids in schools."\par \par \uc2 He was still on the "Pay no attention to the issue of abortion here." I don`t know if that`s a -- if that`s going to be able to hold.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: I was speaking to Republican strategists just yesterday. They say that "That issue is receding -- we`re watching it recede. The big marquee at ever intersection is the price of gas right now. It`s now gone down 70-plus consecutive days."\par \par \uc2 But I was speaking to \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 . You`re speaking about Virginia. I spoke to her yesterday. She launched her campaign. The first political ad out is on the issue of abortion rights. \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , the first ad on the issue of abortion rights. And this, she says, it`s not just about abortion; it`s about freedom, as we saw from Pat Ryan, and Pennsylvania (sic), almost, sort of, co-opting a line from Republicans, from Tea Party -- you know, the Tea Party in the past, like the Democrats are the libertarians here in some way.\par \par \uc2 But the uncertainty, she says, is really benefiting those independents that will now begin leading toward Democrats. The way the economic uncertainty helped Republicans in some way, this uncertainty about what`s really at stake helps Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know -- go ahead, Rich.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: I was just going to say, on these terrible cases with miscarriages, this is another aspect where Republicans, they really need to know the case. They really need to know what these laws say. And every one of these laws has a broad health exception, not just life of the mother but also physical impairment, including losing a uterus is obviously a physical impairment. And this -- this woman in South Carolina did get her miscarriage care. Republicans need to be out there aggressively saying that, making the case. And attorney generals need to be making it clear to the attorneys at hospitals and medical facilities what the law actually says. Because there`s a lot of bad advice being given.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: I think it`s also important to point out, though, with miscarriages, which are much more common than we talk about in this country, these are things we have long known, right, that miscarriages can easily result in sepsis, that there is a risk to having a child in America, in particular for black women, who are three times more likely to die because of pregnancy-related issues. And I do...\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Pro-lifers do not oppose miscarriage treatment. Catholic hospitals that refuse on high moral principle to perform abortions will care for miscarriage. So this...\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: That is true, but in many cases...\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: This is a misunderstanding.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: ... some of those miscarriage cases now have to be litigated on a case-by-case base.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:04]\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: But they -- they shouldn`t be.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: There should be clear guidance on this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The message that carries the day, though, at the end of the day, is not them trying to find the nuance and explain the difference in each state. It`s women like Nancy Davis. This is a Black woman in Louisiana who we heard from just this week who learned that her baby, the fetus in her belly, has -- is not going to have a skull and would die just days after it`s born.\par \par \uc2 As she said -- and I think this was one of the most powerful sound bites we heard in the course of the last several weeks. She said, "I have to carry my baby to bury my baby." And it`s that messaging that is now being heard across the country, as real examples are demonstrated, even where there may be exceptions in those states.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: And there aren`t exceptions in all these cases. I think that`s the problem. These lawmakers don`t even know what`s in these laws, which is why you`re seeing the DOJ stepping in and trying to enforce federal law that requires health-saving care if -- for federally funded hospitals in, sort of, battling where -- in places like Idaho.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: My colleagues, I`ve literally read every single one of these laws, and there is a health exception in every single of these laws, literally.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: I think Merrich Garland would disagree in Idaho, at least.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Rich, the irony here is I had a Republican pollster who`s -- and I`ve said this before -- I`d say he pulled his hair out, but he doesn`t have that much hair anymore. But he`s, like -- he`s, like, saying, "Fifteen weeks," like Kevin Bacon in "Animal House," you know, "Remain calm." He`s frustrated because 15 weeks didn`t have a majority against. You know, it`s not to say it`s the most popular position, but it didn`t have a majority against. And it -- and he`s -- it`s like, if they actually let Roberts get the compromise, it would be Democrats having a political problem.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: But that`s not...\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s the irony here.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: But that`s not the Supreme Court`s rule at...\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: ... at all. And it doesn`t matter. The court should faithfully interpret the Constitution. It doesn`t matter whether it creates a blue wave, a red wave. It doesn`t matter if it puts Republicans in an awkward position because they`re scared and confused and don`t know how to talk about it. That`s not the court`s job.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s -- they`ve -- they have certainly launched what is a massive political change that we`re watching here.\par \par \uc2 You guys were great. We`re going to pause this part of the conversation. I want to now thank the panel.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn to what we learned from Friday`s release of the Mar-a- Lago affidavit. The affidavit revealed that there were 184 classified documents in 15 boxes that were recovered in January. Those documents included 67 that were marked "Confidential," 92 that were marked "Secret" and 25 marked "Top Secret."\par \par \uc2 The heavily redacted affidavit also revealed that the Justice Department feared there was, quote, "probable cause to believe that evidence of obstruction will be found in this month`s search."\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is a member of the January 6th Committee. It`s Republican congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R) ILLINOIS: Thank you. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with the affidavit, with you. It seems as if, if Justice wanted to charge the former president with a crime, doesn`t it appear they already have enough evidence just simply on the mishandling of these classified documents?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Yeah, I mean, look, I -- I`m no expert in, you know, what it takes to indict somebody, you know? I`m a pilot. That`s about it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 But, knowing what I know, I look at this and I go, "My goodness. I mean, Chuck, if any of us in the House of Representatives who, by our election, we have, you know, in essence the highest level of security clearance, if any of us walked out intentionally with even one document from the SCIF, which is where we can go see classified documents at, and, you know, our organization came to us, the House came to us and said, "You have to give this document back," and we refused to do it for years, we`d be in real trouble -- not just one document in this case. We know of 180 plus than what was gotten after this search.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the hypocrisy of folks in my party that spent years chanting "Lock her up" about Hillary Clinton...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: ... because of some deleted e-mails, or, quote/unquote "wiping a server," are now out there defending a man who very clearly did not take the national security of the United States to heart. And it will be up to DOJ whether or not that reaches the level of indictment. But this is disgusting, in my mind. And -- and, look, no president should act this way, obviously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you`re pursuing a different investigation. Whether these documents that we have held are documents that are helpful to your investigation, how are you able to find that out?\par \par \uc2 Is there going to a broad subpoena that you -- your committee issues to get a look at what he was hoarding at Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Yeah, we -- it`s not -- it`s not an answer we have at this moment. I think we have to, kind of, figure out where does this fall?\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to get access to documents that are specifically on national security, and nothing about January 6th, as an example. We`ll never be able to know that. But I think it`s important for us to take a look at, kind of, the broad areas, and if there -- if it seems like there may be something that`s, kind of, in a broad area, potentially. Again, this is stuff that, you know, from what we understand, may even be beyond what Congress, short of being on a specific committee, would have access to.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, when it comes to what Justice may be looking at, I mean, I go back to, if this were simply about him mishandling classified information, and the fact that they`re not pursuing that charge, is it -- is it fair to assume they`re -- they`re looking at something that would be even more serious than this?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: That`s my perception. I mean, look, mishandling classified information is one thing, particularly if it was unintentional. You know, if you accidentally did take something home, there may be consequences, but I think you`d look into the intentionality of that.\par \par \uc2 When you have what we appear to know, just through some of what we`ve seen, you know, they approached the president, the former president, with things that he had. He claimed he either didn`t have them or claimed he gave them up, any of those kinds of stuff, and we know he didn`t. There`s intentionality there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this seems big. And when you -- when you look at some of the president`s biggest, kind of, cheerleaders, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , Jim Jordan, have gone, kind of, silent, that tells you all you need to know, too.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s like they`re basically sitting here saying, you know, "We didn`t start this fire," and walking away, but, you know, they obviously ignited it. They`re the ones that have sat there and -- and told this president everything he did was right; everything he could do would be OK. And they defended him at the beginning of this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When it comes to what you guys are getting ready to lay out to the public in September, can you give us a sense of what are the missing pieces that you didn`t have that you feel like the public is going to understand better come September?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Well, I think the missing piece -- I think there`s going to be a lot of depth that we`re able to build, whether it`s through these hearings or whether it`s through the final report.\par \par \uc2 So if you think about, for instance, the last hearing, the one we \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  and I did. You know, we talked about what was the president doing during that -- the 187 minutes?\par \par \uc2 Well, we did a basically an hour and a half hearing. We probably could have done one that was four or five. So there will be more depth to some of that. Some of the leaks -- I think one of the more intriguing things is going to be some of the financing, right, some of the fund-raising, the fact that, you know, a vast majority of this money was raised under, quote/unquote "Stop the steal," with no intention of doing anything to so called stop a steal. It was all about just raising money. And people were abused that way. So there`s going to be a lot more of that.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have a lot more to find out still about the Secret Service incident. I mean, there`s a whole lot of -- we`re still sitting around, like, "Why don`t we have some of these text messages? Why was some of this stuff hidden?" And I think we will get answers to that by -- by the time we can -- by the time we can present that to the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to respond to something Rich Lowry wrote earlier this week in an op-ed in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . And he was talking about the January 6th Committee and, sort of, the difficulty you may be having in proving a crime. Because he writes this.\par \par \uc2 "Trump engaged in a political offense against our constitutional system that criminal statutes are ill-suited to address, no matter how infamous the conduct."\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: No, not at all. Look, I -- I had a lot of respect for Rich Lowry. I still read him on occasion. It`s been amazing to watch his pivot from, kind of, intellectual conservative to, like, this anti-anti-Trumper. So he`s against people that are against Trump. He`s not necessarily for him. And so he`s constantly with this, "Well, there may be not precedents."\par \par \uc2 Of course there`s not precedents. We have never had a president of the United States attempt a coup against the United States of America. So there`s no precedents. And if we need a new law, let`s get a new law. But I`m quite sure that some of the laws can cover this. The DOJ seems very convinced of it.\par \par \uc2 And secondarily, Rich, our job is not to -- to prosecute Trump. Our job is to present information to the American people to talk about recommendations. Here is what I recommend. And I`m going to say this even before the report. I recommend we never again elect somebody who does not respect the Constitution of the United States, who does not respect the voters, and who abuses people by lying to them. That is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And people can be anti- those that are against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But we`re not against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for any personal reasons, except that we love the United States of America, and he is tearing it down daily.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you a quick political question. This week we saw the primaries in Florida and New York, that special election in New York. I want to read you something Matthew Continetti wrote about the abortion issue and the Republican Party. He noted, "The Journal`s op-ed page says the GOP has an abortion problem. And the problem? Republicans have no idea what to say about abortion."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Do -- do you agree, essentially, with that sentiment, and that this may have changed the political complexion of the midterms?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: I very -- I think it`s definitely changed the complexion of the midterms some. I don`t know how big.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:03]\par \par \uc2 Look, I`ve always taken the position of rape, incest, life of the mother, and, you know, certain weeks, up until 15 weeks or whatever. And that was actually a fairly common thing in the Republican Party. Even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said rape, incest, life of the mother exceptions. And now you`ve been seeing these Republicans go, "There is absolutely no exception."\par \par \uc2 Because somehow, in the Republican Party, the crueler you are, the more likely you are to win a primary. So \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , for instance, can erase his website all he wants and now pretend like he`s some soft-on-this guy, but he didn`t. Because he used it to win a primary.\par \par \uc2 And so, yeah, I think Republicans are in real trouble because we have no leadership. This is the problem, Chuck. So Kevin McCarthy isn`t leading. He`s asking "What does \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  want me to do?" Mitch McConnell`s just trying to save the Senate. He`s probably not going to do that. We have no leaders. And no wonder our base basically believes everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just curious. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said that in some cases she may have to help a Democrat win against, sort of, an anti-democracy Republican. Do you feel that`s what you`re going to be doing over the next couple of years or so?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Yes. Yeah, I mean, look, the biggest issue -- not everybody agrees with me, certainly in my party and even Democrats that like to say things like, "Well, but you`re still a conservative." Yeah, I`m a conservative.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is the biggest threat right now to our country is democracy. And if you have Republicans that are running against even left- wing Democrats that believe in democracy and believe in voting, that person should be elected over somebody who basically would overthrow the will of the people and ultimately destroy this country.\par \par \uc2 This country cannot survive outside of democracy. It will turn into a power struggle between groups of different races, of different ethnicities, of different religions. Because the thing that holds us together is this belief that we can self-govern. Take that away, this country is a mess. And so Republicans that are for that have no place in office. I don`t care what their policy position on taxes are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , Republican from central Illinois. Congressman Kinzinger, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: You bet. Good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, how secure is that threatened nuclear plant in Ukraine? And who is responsible for the car bombing death of a Putin ally in Russia? Former NATO Supreme Allied Commander \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2  and former U.S. ambassador to Russia Michael McFaul both join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. A team from the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2 , \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , is set to visit that endangered nuclear power plant in Ukraine this week. Fighting around the plant has sparked fears of a nuclear catastrophe. And people in the region are now being given iodine pills in -- to prevent radiation-induced cancers in case of a nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 What was supposed to be a lightning-fast Russian military victory, of course, has run into a ferocious Ukrainian defense, and it has now turned into a bloody stalemate on the front lines after six months of war.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:06]\par \par \uc2 So joining me now are former NATO Supreme Allied Commander \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2  and former U.S. ambassador to Russia Michael McFaul.\par \par \uc2 You gentlemen are experts in this area. Appreciate having you both.\par \par \uc2 Jim, let me start with you and Zaporizhzhia, and the nuclear plant. We`ve talked about this before. At what point is there no longer hope that Russia and Ukraine work this out but that the United States or \uc2 NATO\uc2  needs to step in and protect this plant?\par \par \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2 , FORMER NATO SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: We`re getting closer by the day, Chuck. Unfortunately, what really concerns me here is the fact that we don`t have to imagine how bad this can be. It happened in 1986 in Chernobyl. It happened about a decade ago in Fukushima, Japan. We`re looking at billions and billions of dollars, potentially thousands of lives, if this core is breached due to, in this case, an act of war.\par \par \uc2 So the good news here, Chuck, is that the \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , you mentioned a moment ago, appears close to getting a mission, potentially led, by the way, by the head of the \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , an esteemed scientist himself, into the plant.\par \par \uc2 But so far, the Russians are dug in here and they have got three things they want. They want to use this nuclear plant as a sanctuary to conduct attacks. They want to cut off 20 percent of Ukraine`s electricity. And, above all, they want to scare the Europeans. They want a cloud to hang over European thinking on this, pun intended.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 Mike, I want to talk about Putin`s decision this week to expand the size of his military but not launch a draft. He continues to walk this line, it seems, for fear of upsetting his own population. But -- but what does that say that he felt the need to find more troops?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: I think, first and foremost, it says, Chuck, that he has not won this war, that the strategic objectives that he outlined six months ago, he`s failed on all of them. Remember, six months ago he said he was going to unite Ukrainians and Russians, because Ukrainians are just Russians with accents. He failed at that. He failed at de-nazification. He failed at de-militarization. He failed to take the capital of Kyiv. And now he`s just fighting in Donetsk and Kherson. And now he needs to bring new troops just to fight in that stalemated situation.\par \par \uc2 So on the strategic level, I think he`s failed in this war. I don`t see him recovering. But he still wants to focus on taking Donbas. That is his new objective and he needs new soldiers to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike, I want to stay with you a minute here, and Putin`s political situation, the car bombing of a political ally. There`s all sorts of speculation, you know, from false flag to an either unauthorized, authorized Ukrainian operation. But isn`t the simplest explanation that maybe it`s Russians unhappy? And if that`s the case, how lethal is that to the future of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, first, back to your earlier question. That`s why he can`t do a draft across the board because that would make Russians unhappy. Second, we don`t know who killed Daria Dugina. I want to be clear. There`s lots of speculation about it. But immediately after she was attacked, a Russian partisan group, the National Republican Army -- I`d never heard of them, Chuck, and I know all of these obscure groups in Russia -- came out with a manifesto saying that this was part of a partisan war to overthrow Putin. and then a former parliamentary -- an M.P., Ilya Ponomarev is his name -- the only Russian to vote against annexation of Crimea back in 2014, sitting in Kyiv, published their manifesto.\par \par \uc2 Again, I can`t say; I can`t conclude that it`s true or not, but the idea that it exists is something, a new development in this war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. domestic problems. This is being brought to Moscow. That has got to be troubling to Putin.\par \par \uc2 Jim, let me go from the Ukrainian side of things. Winter is coming. And I`m not just doing that to promote tonight`s next episode of "House of Dragon."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 What does the Ukrainian military need to accomplish before winter sets in?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Well, I think to get a dragon and call "Dracarys," and flame- throw the Russians...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... that would be a big help. I don`t think that`s going to happen. What they need to accomplish is exactly what is happening. Take these excellent weapons we are giving them, not quite dragons, but HIMARS, long-range cruise missiles that can reach out and touch the Black Sea fleet, continue these behind enemy lines operations that are starting to look like shots out of Mission Impossible.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:04]\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian offensive, I think, is going to kick off in earnest after Labor Day, Chuck. They`re aware that the world wakes up after Labor Day. They want to show they`ve got the ability to go on the offense. Look for that next.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And are we at -- both -- this is a final question of both of you. It -- Mike, it seems as if the Russians are preparing for a years-long conflict. And Jim, it seems as if Ukraine knows it can`t do years-long conflict.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, how do you view this? How do you see this ending? And then, Jim, finally to you. So, Mike, you first.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: That`s right, Chuck, the Ukrainians don`t want it to be a years- long conflict. It costs a lot. Five billion a year they`re getting from the West. They`ll eventually run out of resources, military, these HIMARS that Admiral Stavridis was just talking about.\par \par \uc2 That`s why they want to go on the offensive in the south. The city of Kherson is what they want to take. They want to push the Russians out for the first time. With respect to the Russians, they think time is on their side and they think the longer the war goes, it`ll play to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 And Putin right now is focusing on trying to take Donbas. What`s striking to me, Chuck, is he`s been focused on trying to take Donbas for six months and still hasn`t achieved that objective. That says to me that maybe Putin is wrong about time being on his side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jim?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I`ll pick up Ambassador McFaul`s points. I think he`s, he`s right on. Above my head is the game of Risk. Both sides are playing it. Putin, I think, has got a burn rate in people, in equipment that is frankly higher than the Ukrainians. Ukrainians are going to count on patience from the West. I`ll take that side of the game of Risk, the one that the Ukrainians hold.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That is quite the signage that you have behind your head there. That`s a cool, old-school looking version of that game that, for some of us, was a staple, between that and Stratego.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Jim Stavridis, Mike McFaul, as always, I appreciate both of you being on here and sharing your expertise.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, President Biden`s decision to forgive up to $20,000 in student debt, if it`s such good politics, why are so many Democrats unhappy about it?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:35:20]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Part two of the panel, if you will. And we`re going to start, Peter, with the decision by President Biden to cancel the student debt. And I say decision -- what`s interesting is it was months to get him to this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here we are, and it is Democrats that are divided amongst themselves about whether this was a good idea.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, I think that`s right. So, the progressives obviously wanted it to be $50,000. Tim Ryan, up for the Senate seat in Ohio right now, is basically saying, you know, this was the wrong decision. You don`t need to be helping --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me put up the names. I mean, it`s a who`s who of some Democrats on the ballot right now that have hit it.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned Ryan, it sends the wrong message. Michael Bennet: It doesn`t solve the underlying problem. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 : Doesn`t address the root problem. Jared Golden in a swing district in Maine; \uc2 Chris Pappas\uc2 , swing district in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Anybody in a swing district or a swing state didn`t seem to love this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yeah, no, that`s exactly right. I mean, this is going to cost $250 billion to $550 billion, depending who you talk to right now. Even \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says that it won`t be inflationary, but it`s a challenging message for the Democrats to sort of defend right now in front of a lot of people, working class Americans, who they say they`re benefiting here. There`s some new ads that are out already running in parts of the country where you see, you know, a mechanic and you see others basically saying --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re, like, playing producer for me. Let me show the ad --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- instead of you describing it, let`s play it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Good.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m breaking my back out here for one reason. I want to pay off some other guy`s debt.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Biden`s plan to pay other people`s college loans using my tax dollars is a great idea.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Biden`s right. You should take my tax dollars to pay off your debts. My family will figure out how to get by with less.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, Amna, the White House pushback on this is, oh, you have PPP loans.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Yeah. yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right? That`s their pushback.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: I mean, it`s clear this issue has brought out the snark on both sides, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: That was unusually troll-y for the White House too, I`d say, to do that. And I actually asked Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  about that and he said, that`s not what this is about at all. We`re talking about apples and oranges.\par \par \uc2 And look, the unfairness message, I think, is one you`re going to see Republicans lean into again and again. Democrats in swing districts, as you mentioned. And the people who say this doesn`t solve the underlying problem are absolutely right. It does not bring down the soaring cost --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They didn`t even attempt --\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: -- of college. Not even a little bit. But it will make a difference in millions of Americans` lives. And it will make some difference. If the average college debt is around $30,000, it`s making a huge dent. It will disproportionately impact low income families, Black and Brown people in America.\par \par \uc2 The question is does it make a difference in the longer term and does it bring them out to vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My political question for the two of you is -- I think this plays out as following: If you benefit from this, you`ll never forget. If you`re upset about it, you`ll probably move on to something else in a week.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: I think that that`s absolutely right. And I think both on the political and the policy side it`s important.\par \par \uc2 First of all, this is something \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  promised to do on the campaign trail. He said immediately. A year plus in is not exactly immediately, but he`s doing it. But it has to be paired, as you said, exactly right, this doesn`t get to the bottom of the issue -- soaring costs of education.\par \par \uc2 And I think people get the idea that people should not be saddled with debt for the rest of their lives based on trying to better themselves and that you have to get to the affordability and you have to get to the -- the racial wealth gap that this exacerbates and move beyond that. I don`t think that people would say, why is that guy -- why is the fire department putting that guy`s house fire out when my house isn`t on fire? That`s a terrible way to try to message all this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now the courts, the courts may -- the courts may say, sorry, you can`t do this, and they throw it back to the Congress.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Yeah, that`s a --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s always possible.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: That`s what I was going to go to. I think the first order of business on this for me is for the president of the United States unilaterally to spend maybe, could be $500 billion, could go up to a trillion, to spend that much money on his own, with no clear congressional authority whatsoever is a grotesque abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 I don`t care what the politics or the, the policy are. The question is: can someone get standing against it, you know, demonstrating some direct harm. But if someone does get standing, this thing will be slammed in the Supreme Court. Yes, Biden promised to do it, and he wanted Congress to do it --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: -- which everyone else thought was necessary until the day before yesterday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, which I think even Speaker Pelosi and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  both seemed to hint at that.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to shift. Speaking of the White House getting aggressive, Amna, President Biden used the F -- he`s not -- he`s used the F-word before, but he used another F-word.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Clearly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, I know. And the F-word I`m talking about is fascism.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it was -- look, that`s quite aggressive.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: It is, and especially for the president. It`s something we`ve heard within Democratic ranks before. And look, I do think he`s being responsive to some degree because we have seen concerns over threats to democracy go up in terms of voter concerns, in terms of what people are paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tops in our poll.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Certainly after the public hearings of the January 6th Committee. And I also think the more we get information about some of these classified documents from Mar-a-Lago, this continues to kind of rise on people`s radars. And this is the president reacting to that and kind of meeting his voters where they are.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And you know, Chuck, what`s interesting about this, when he has, as you described, used the word semi-fascism to describe the MAGA philosophy this week, let`s go back to March, when he was in Poland, and he said of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that he couldn`t stay in power. And that became controversial. The White House, you know, backpedaled on that and walked that back.\par \par \uc2 There`s no backpedaling on this. It`s clear that there`s a more aggressive strategy. We talked about the way that they were handling the debate over student loans --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think he actually soft-pedaled it? Do you think someone said, you should say "fascism," and he said, I`m going to say --\par \par \uc2 STOHR: I`m going to say "semi" --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- "semi-fascism."\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: When I spoke to aides, this wasn`t a, this wasn`t a teleprompter speech.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This is what he`s been thinking.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: They say he said it out loud. This was done not on camera. But it -- it fires up Democrats. It juices up the base because they want to see him be more aggressive on that. But it also does, you know, it does become problematic because, you know, this is a guy who said he wanted to be a unifier.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s my issue, just as a political science geek, does anybody know the definition of socialism and fascism and all of this when we start to throw these words out there? But anyway, we`re -- it`s going to be tossed out there. The -- is the anti-democracy message helping Democrats?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: I think the anti-Trump message helps. To the extent they can make Trump front and center. it helps two political entities in the United States of America: the Democratic Party and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this is true of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search in Mar-a-Lago. Maybe the effect will wear off over time, but it has bonded Republican voters to, to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when they had been slowly falling away from him. And it`s in part because, and you know, I`m very comprehensively pro-law enforcement, but there`s a sheen now of radical doubt about any sort of investigation of, of this sort. And I, I will not believe anything about these documents myself until I basically get a top secret clearance, get to go into a SCIF, and look at them myself.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: But I think it`s --\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: -- because with Russia, there are so many leaks that were spun up - -\par \par \uc2 STOHR: But listen, going back to --\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: -- with so much hysteria --\par \par \uc2 STOHR: -- the first part of your point about whether it`s anti-fascism or anti-Trump, I think you can`t separate those two. And I think even for a lot of Republicans, particularly after the January 6th committee, they have been feeling that. There has been a fatigue. There has been an idea that this is not helping anyone. And there is a push to move away from that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Well, I`m going to have to end it here because I`ve got to go to the moon.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, first, we`re going to talk to you about the midterms are usually a referendum on the party in power. But what if it`s the party out of power that gets punished for the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:45:37]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 The midterms, usually a referendum on the party in power in Washington. And that tends to mean the White House and Congress. But this summer has brought a lot of attention to the party that`s in power in the third branch of government, the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 And the latest NBC News poll shows a lot of lost faith in the Supreme Court following its decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. Let me show you. At the start of this new 6-3 majority court, the public was giving the benefit of the doubt, plus 25, positive over negative.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you can see, the negative number in particular has more than doubled. And now, the Supreme Court is like every other institution here in Washington: underwater.\par \par \uc2 And it`s really across the board, as you can see. Look, across the demographic groups, the Court had net-positive ratings before the Dobbs decision. Now, if you look at this, men underwater by a point. Women, by double digits. Younger folks, by double digits.\par \par \uc2 Really, only older Gen X and younger boomers are still positive on this court, but even their numbers have dropped quite a bit. And if you look at it by party, you can see: 60-point swing among Democrats to the negative, 40-plus-point swing to the Independents, negative. Even Republicans dropped a little bit.\par \par \uc2 There`s really only one part of the Republican coalition that is happy about this court. It won`t surprise you: Evangelical Christians. Because in many ways, this decision was one that this voting group cared about more than any other part of the Republican Party, plus 35 points.\par \par \uc2 The problem for Republicans is it`s not a big enough group to overcome all the negative with swing voters.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, look who`s going back to the moon. My talk with \uc2 NASA\uc2  chief Bill Nelson about our goals to fly to the moon and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:51:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 America is getting back in the moon business. NASA`s Artemis I is scheduled to lift off tomorrow morning from the Kennedy Space Center in Florida. This unmanned flight is to orbit the moon in preparation for landings on the moon and beyond, and it`s supposed to orbit for over a month.\par \par \uc2 This past week, I spoke with former senator and current \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator Bill Nelson, who as a member of the House back in the `80s flew on the space shuttle Columbia.\par \par \uc2 And I began by asking Nelson why it`s taken us so long to return to the moon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: We had to focus that we need to be on the moon for much longer periods of time than just landing like we used to, stayed a couple of days and left.\par \par \uc2 This time, we`re going back, we`re going to live there, we`re going to learn there. We`re going to develop new technologies, all of this so we can go to Mars with humans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the point -- when you say we`re going to live on the moon. Are we living there as scientists or is the goal we`re going to end up living there?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: I don`t think in the short run, we`re going to live there. I think all of this is to develop where we may be living on other worlds. They may be floating worlds. They may be the surface of Mars. But this is just part of our push outward, our quest to explore, to find out what`s out there in this universe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This Artemis I, it`s been -- it`s been a big project in the making. It`s taken years, there`s the usual delays that you have. If this doesn`t work, what does that mean for \uc2 NASA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Well, it`s going to work. This first flight is a test. We test it, we stress it. We make this rocket and the spacecraft do things that we would never do with a human crew.\par \par \uc2 The main purpose of the flight is to test the heat shield because you can`t test that in a lab. So if the heat shield survives and does what it is expected to do, it`s a successful test. So then we`re ready in two years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve made it clear, you`re stress testing it. Meaning you may push this to the limit where it looks like an accident. But if it is, that`s not necessarily a failure.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: You can expect in a test flight that everything is not going to go as you expect it to. That`s part of a test flight. That`s part of, for example, developing aircraft. That`s why you have a test pilot. But we`re pretty confident about this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 NASA\uc2 , for the last 30 years, has had a pretty successful partnership with Russia. Obviously, it is -- seems to look like it`s going to split up.\par \par \uc2 You have been Mr. Optimism. You`re basically -- it seems as if you`re ignoring the rhetoric, and assuming as soon as the war`s over, things will go back to normal. Why are you so optimistic?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Well, let me say at the outset that what Putin has done in Ukraine is inexcusable. It`s a slaughter. It`s, it`s awful.\par \par \uc2 But we were squared off against each other with the Soviet Union in the Cold War. And yet, we had American astronauts and Soviet cosmonauts rendezvoused and docked and live together in \uc2 Apollo\uc2 -Soyuz in the midst of the Cold War, 1975.\par \par \uc2 And that cooperative civilian space program relationship has continued all the way to this day. And despite the horrors that are going on in Ukraine, the professionalism, the relationship between the astronauts and the cosmonauts on board the International Space Station, as well as our two mission controls, one in Houston, one in Moscow, it doesn`t miss a beat.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:15]\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. So let`s turn to China. China`s got an aggressive space program, and yet they seem to want nothing to do with anybody. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Because they`re very secretive. They don`t want any kind of Cooperation, they are non- transparent. They send their rockets to blast their ASAT, and they put all of this space junk that hinders -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are they rushing things? Like when you watch them from afar, does it look like they`re so insistent in trying to catch up to us that they`re rushing and cutting corners?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: They`re not cutting corners, they are good. And they`ve gotten a lot of the technology from everybody else. And as a result, they`re very good.\par \par \uc2 But we have offered, come on and start cooperating with us. They just don`t want to do that. And you see that as evidence in our government to government relations with the Chinese. You see that, for example, they go out and they take the Spratly Islands and say, this is our territory --\par \par \uc2 TODD: How are we going to prevent that from the moon? Because I was thinking about is the moon going to turn into the South China Sea? Where when China gets up there, we`re up there, is it going to be a land grab?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Well, we have to be careful about that. And that`s what I`ve said all along, that we`re in a space race. And we want to get to the south pole of the moon where the resources are, where we think water is. If there`s water, there`s rocket fuel. And we don`t want China suddenly getting there and saying, this is our exclusive territory.\par \par \uc2 That`s what they did with the Spratly Islands.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One last question on the Space Force. There`s always been a military component. First, the Army, then the Air Force, now Space Force.\par \par \uc2 How has things changed? And does Space Force do launches that we`re probably not aware of all the time?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: For the defense of the country, both defensive and offensive, space has become the next battleground. It`s the high ground. And what you`ve seen is not only satellites that give us early warning of any attack on Earth, including a nuclear attack.\par \par \uc2 But you`ve seen the eyes and ears for us to have some certainty of what our adversaries are doing. Those assets now, because of the development of technology, are being challenged. And therefore there are other assets that are developed in order to prevent those challenges.\par \par \uc2 So space is the high ground that you want to retain. And that`s why you`re seeing, as you suggest, the Space Force becoming a major part of our national defense system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, we said that tomorrow`s flight is unmanned. That`s still technically true. But on the voyage will be a Snoopy stuffie, if you will, which will indicate zero gravity when astronaut Snoopy starts to float. And there will be mannequins as well. Think of them as crash test dummies or what \uc2 NASA\uc2  calls, "moonequins."\par \par \uc2 Anyway, good luck, \uc2 NASA\uc2 . And if it`s not tomorrow, a couple other weather windows next Friday and next Monday.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220828ei8s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for August 26, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander, Gabe Gutierrez, Stephanie Gosk,  Miguel Almaguer, Andrea Mitchell, Tom Costello, Julia Ainsley, Kristen  Dahlgren \par \uc2 3,995 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the Justice Department revealing the reasons behind the unprecedented surge at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The redacted affidavit showing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  requested the search after 184 classified documents were found among 15 boxes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  handed over in January. Also, discovered Mr. Trump`s handwritten notes and information on clandestine human sources. How the former president is reacting.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden, our Peter Alexander asking him today is it ever appropriate to take home classified materials? How he responded.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, stocks plunging after a stark warning from the Fed Chairman. Why he says Americans should brace for some pain ahead.\par \par \uc2 The pregnant mother whose fetus was diagnosed with a rare and fatal condition denied an abortion under Louisiana`s trigger law. What she called for today on the steps of the state capitol.\par \par \uc2 Mounting concerns near a Russian-controlled nuclear plant in Ukraine. What authorities are urgently handing out amid fears of radiation leaks?\par \par \uc2 The fleecing of America. Nearly $300 million in COVID relief money stolen. How the Secret Service got it back.\par \par \uc2 And our NBC News Exclusive, inside NASA`s historic \uc2 Apollo\uc2  mission control as America prepares to return to the moon.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening. We are literally reading between the lines. The heavily redacted copy of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s Mar-a-Lago search affidavit made public today describing the what but not the who surrounding classified materials that were removed from the White House and taken to former President Trump`s Florida estate, along with details of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s months long efforts to recover them, and even references to information provided by a significant number of civilian witnesses.\par \par \uc2 In i`s warrant application, the government saying it believed there were contraband, fruits of crime, or other items illegally possessed at the Trump a estate. This after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said among 15 boxes Mr. Trump returned to the National Archives earlier this year, it discovered 184 classified items including 25 top secret documents.\par \par \uc2 Peter Alexander has more of what we`ve learned and what we haven`t.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, three weeks after that unprecedented \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home, new details why the Justice Department pushed to make that controversial move. The 38-page affidavit focuses on what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it found in 15 boxes Mr. Trump returned to the National Archives in January.\par \par \uc2 Mixed in with newspapers, magazines, and presidential correspondents were 184 classified documents. 67 of them marked Confidential, 92 marked Secret, and 25 documents marked Top Secret where the government says their unauthorized disclosure could result an exceptionally grave damage to the national security. Also discovered documents that contain information about clandestine human sources and Mr. Trump`s handwritten notes.\par \par \uc2 The Affidavit reveals more about the timeline. In May of last year, the National Archives first requested the missing records from the former president. In January Mr. Trump turned over those 15 boxes containing that classified information. A month later, the Archives sent a criminal referral to the Justice Department. President Biden authorized the Archive to reject any executive privilege claims by Mr. Trump over the documents, allowing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to examine them. In June, Mr. Trump turned over additional documents.\par \par \uc2 Two months later, the Justice Department submitted that affidavit that a federal judge approved granting the Mar-a-Lago search. In its filing, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  argue there`s probable cause that more classified documents with National Defense information were at Mr. Trump`s residence, and there was probable cause to believe that evidence of obstruction will be found.\par \par \uc2 But the affidavit with entire pages blacked out leaves many unanswered questions about the extraordinary search of a former president`s home that sparked fierce blowback from Republicans and Mr. Trump who today said the judge should never have allowed the break-in of my home and slammed the redacted affidavit is a public relations subterfuge by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ after arguing he`d been turning over documents and that the investigation is politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump had demanded the entire affidavit be made public. But the judge allowed the Justice Department to make substantial redactions to protect its investigation, including any details about witnesses. Tonight, we pressed President Biden who said he had no advanced knowledge edge of the search.\par \par \uc2 Is it ever appropriate for a president to take home with them classified and top-secret documents?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Depending on the circumstance. For example, I have in my home a cabin off base that is completely secure. It`s locked. I have a person with me, military with me. I read it. I lock it back up and give it to the military.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:05:23]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Peter, we`ve learned a bit more here, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is claim that it seized 11 sets of Classified documents during the recent surge. So, where do we go from here with all this?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): You know, Lester, it ultimately be up to the Justice Department and specifically to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to determine whether anyone will be charged with a crime here. Meanwhile, a judge has given former President Trump until the night to further explain his lawsuit calling for a third party, what`s known as a special master to review all the documents. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter Alexander with our top story tonight, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We`ll turn now to the anxious day on Wall Street. The Dow plunging more than 1000 points after Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  warned Americans there may be some pain ahead in the battle to obtain inflation. We get more from Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL EXCHANGE: Price stability is the responsibility of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In his most closely watched speech of the year in Wyoming, today, Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  signaled even more interest rate hikes may be on the way next month.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Restoring price stability will take some time and requires using our tools forcefully to bring demand and supply into better balance.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Since June, the Fed has already raised interest rates three quarters of a percentage point twice. The moves meant to slow the economy and squelch inflation, but --\par \par \uc2 POWELL: They will also bring some pain to households and businesses.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The uncertainty feeling a sell-off on Wall Street. The Dow today down more than 1000 points as consumers wonder what`s next.\par \par \uc2 SHARON EPPERSON, CNBC SENIOR PERSONAL FINANCE CORRESPONDENT: You really want to try to whittle down your credit card debt as much as possible because those weights are going to continue to go up,\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: In Arizona, potential homebuyer Eric Burlingame had one thought when he heard mortgage rates might keep rising. He`s been looking for a home around Phoenix for more than two years and is considering giving up.\par \par \uc2 ERIC BURLINGAME, HOMEBUYER: It`s definitely been a roller coaster. Like, you find all the right houses, but they`re just not in the right neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: In Cape Cod, Ben and Bill`s Chocolate Emporium is feeling the heat to this summer. Inflation means the cost of dairy is skyrocketing, melting their profits.\par \par \uc2 What are you most worried about?\par \par \uc2 JEANNETTE MICHAUD, MANAGER, BEN AND BILL`S CHOCOLATE EMPORIUM: Well, you know, we have to pass that on to the consumer. We can`t absorb all that, you know, as a company and be able to stay afloat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): New data out today shows that consumer spending cooled in July going up just 0.1 percent. That could suggest that interest rate hikes may be working and that Americans are spending less. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana new impact from the state`s abortion trigger law. A mother pregnant with a fetus diagnosed with a fatal condition speaking out after she was denied abortion access. Stephanie Gosk on what she`s demanding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): On the steps of the Louisiana State House today --\par \par \uc2 NANCY DAVIS, DENIED ABORTION: My name is Nancy Davis.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A pregnant woman told her story.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: 10 weeks into my pregnancy, I found out that my baby had a rare birth defect called the acrania. The doctors told me that my baby would die shortly after birth.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Acrania is a rare congenital disorder which prevents a fetus` skull from forming inside the womb. Nancy Davis says her local hospital would not perform an abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Basically they said I had to carry my baby to bury my baby. They seemed confused about the law and afraid of what would happen to them.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Well, the new total ban is being challenged in court, Louisiana still has very strict abortion laws on the books. Doctors face possible prison time for breaking them. The hospital would not comment on this specific case but says there has been much discussion over the interpretation of some of the new legislation regarding treatment for medically futile pregnancies. The hospital supports medical decisions made between the physician and their patient that comply with all state laws and policies.\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers who helped pass the current law say it does allow an abortion in this case.\par \par \uc2 SEN. KATRINA JACKSON (D-LA), STATE SENATOR: This woman is seeking a medical procedure for a pregnancy that is not viable outside of the womb.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Can`t the state fix it by just saying OK, acrania is now one of the conditions that will be an exception.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER VILLAVICENCIO, \uc2 AMERICAN COLLEGE OF OBSTETRICIANS AND GYNECOLOGISTS\uc2 : They could add that on but there`s no possible way to make a comprehensive list of exceptions.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Davis is calling for a special session to clear up the law.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: This is not fair to me and it should not happen to any other woman.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Next week, she says, she`s going out of the state for an abortion. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: With the new \uc2 NFL\uc2  season around the corner, a rookie on the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  is facing disturbing allegations from his time in college. A civil suit accusing him of Taking part in the gang rape of a teen. Miguel Almaguer reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:05]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s a big one.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Two weeks before the start of the \uc2 NFL\uc2  Season, one of the league`s most promising stars facing serious trouble. \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  rookie punter Matt Araiza accused of involvement in the gang rape of a 17-year-old girl last year while he attended \uc2 San Diego State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to a new civil lawsuit, Araiza raped a drunk High School senior at an off-campus party before leading her into a room where she was sexually assaulted by multiple teammates.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILLEON, ATTORNEY FOR THE PLAINTIFF: When a young woman gets raped at 17, she doesn`t forget about it.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The teen says she arrived at the Halloween party with friends after drinking. She says after telling Araiza she was in high school, he gave her a drink she believes not only contained alcohol but other intoxicating substances. She was raped outside, she says, then Araiza`s teammates took turns assaulting her for an hour and a half.\par \par \uc2 The victim`s attorney sharing these photos saying she went to police the next day and underwent a rape exam. Her father speaking out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would like to say to the people that did this to my daughter held accountable.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With \uc2 San Diego State University\uc2 , the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2 , and the \uc2 NFL\uc2  accused of turning a blind eye all released statements not commenting on specifics.\par \par \uc2 KERRY ARMSTRONG, ATTORNEY FOR MATT ARAIZA: He`s adamant that he never forcibly raped her. I hate to say this, but maybe she`s just lying in order to get money.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Today`s headlines come as the \uc2 NFL\uc2  tries to put a high profile sexual assault scandal involving another player behind it. But this new civil case could be followed by a criminal case that`s now under review. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Miguel Almaguer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, authorities are taking emergency measures near a Russian- controlled nuclear plant. Iodine tablets which can help block the absorption of radioactivity are being handed out to residents amid fears that shelling at the facility could trigger a nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 It`s been exactly one year since a suicide bomber killed 13 U.S. troops at the Kabul Airport during the chaotic U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan. And as Andrea Mitchell reports, all these months later, at least 160,000 eligible Afghans and their families still haven`t gotten out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Searing images of Afghans desperate to escape in the final days of the withdrawal, people claim to the wheels of a C17 on the runway at Kabul airport. Then, the suicide bombing at the Abbey gate killing 13 U.S. troops, 11 Marines, a soldier, and a sailor, also dead 170 Afghan civilians. Thousands of Afghans got out but tens of thousands more who had worked for the U.S. and their families were left behind under \uc2 Taliban\uc2  threat.\par \par \uc2 How did you feel when that last play left and you hadn`t gotten you and your family on it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Heartbroken?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: We`re hiding his face because the man the troops called Frank still has relatives back home where he worked as a military and State Department translator for 12 years, was eligible for a special visa and had a State Department badge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When I got to the airport, I showed my passport, I showed my badge, they didn`t accept. They said no. I was so close to burn the badge and just go to the Talibans and tell him that I was doing this. And if you want to kill me, kill me now.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. JAMES REESE (RET), FORMER SPECIAL OPERATIONS COMMANDER, \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : We relied on him, had the biggest heart in the world. He did everything we asked for him.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Frank spent 10 months hiding from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , moving his family from house to house every three days, was one of thousands pleading for help.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP CARUSO, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, NO ONE LEFT BEHIND: People who are telling us, telling me that their children are starving to death, that they`re on the run, that their family members have been killed or arrested or tortured.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One of the mistake that I made was put all of my family on the risk. So, if I knew it, I would have stopped.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: You wouldn`t have done this again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If I anticipated it 12 years ago, I would never.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Frank and his family finally arrived in the U.S. last month with no job or benefits but asking if they could pose in front of the flag.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then I shout out for my kids, guys, we made it.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Andrea Mitchell, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, countdown to lift off as America once again shoots for the moon. We go back to where it all started inside Apollo Mission Control.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 In just under 72 hours, a new \uc2 NASA\uc2  rocket is expected to be on its way to the moon. It`s been 50 years since Mission Control in Houston last guided a lunar landing. Now, \uc2 NASA\uc2  vets are watching this mission with nostalgia and excitement. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Step inside to one of the most iconic symbols of America`s space program. NASA`s original mission control restored as a museum down to the ashtray, RC Cola can, and slide ruler to that day in 1969.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: CAPCOM, we`re a go for landing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Eagle, Houston here. Go for landing, over.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: When \uc2 Apollo\uc2  11 landed on the moon. Nine months later, the drama of \uc2 Apollo\uc2  13 played out here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK, we have a problem here. Houston, we have a problem.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: What`s it like to come back and see this and stand here now?\par \par \uc2 MILTON WINDLER, FORMER FLIGHT DIRECTOR, \uc2 NASA\uc2 : It brings back a few memories.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: We asked \uc2 Apollo\uc2  lead flight director Milt Windler, now 90 years old, to come back.\par \par \uc2 What does this data here tell you? What is this about?\par \par \uc2 WINDLER: It`s what your spacecraft is doing, what the systems are doing.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: And sit down with Rick LaBrode.\par \par \uc2 RICK LABRODE, LEAD FLIGHT DIRECTOR, ARTEMIS: It`s hollowed ground where you walk in here and you still have memorabilia on the wall. It reminds you of the accomplishments that these guys did in the \uc2 Apollo\uc2  days.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The lead flight director for Monday`s Artemis mission to the moon.\par \par \uc2 LABRODE: When these guys did it, nobody had done it before. We`re riding on their shoulders because they figured it out for us and that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 WINDLER: The physics hadn`t changed. F is still equal to MA, it is, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 LABRODE: Good.\par \par \uc2 WINDLER: Anyway, so a lot of things are different. The tools are different.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Newton`s laws of motion are the same, but technology has come a long way since 1969.\par \par \uc2 There is more computing power in my cell phone than you had during the \uc2 Apollo\uc2  days. How did you do that?\par \par \uc2 WINDLER: Well, because that`s all we had. We had what we had.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Do you look back --\par \par \uc2 WINDLER: We didn`t know what we didn`t have.\par \par \uc2 LABRODE: My generation looks back at what they accomplished and wonders how they did it.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Opened in 1965, they ran every \uc2 Apollo\uc2  mission out of here, every moon landing, and then they ran Skylab and shuttle missions, including the loss of Challenger.\par \par \uc2 Today and updated Mission Control runs the space station and the upcoming Artemis moon mission.\par \par \uc2 LABRODE: We learned from their generation how to Plan a mission, a very complex mission, how to go and train it. And that`s -- that is the big part, training. We train it, train it, train it.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: A new generation planning to return to the moon and stay with a nod to the \uc2 Apollo\uc2  team that made it all possible. Tom Costello, NBC News, Houston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s pretty amazing group there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, The Secret Service`s mission to get back billions in stolen COVID funds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now, our "FLEECING OF AMERICA" exclusive. The Secret Service recovering hundreds of millions in stolen COVID relief money. But the agency warning billions are still missing. Julia Ainsley reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIA AINSLEY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): During the depths of the pandemic lockdowns, they were critical government funds designed to keep small businesses afloat. Money many scammers instead decided to steal.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY GUGLIELMI, CHIEF OF COMMUNICATIONS, \uc2 U.S. SECRET SERVICE\uc2 : Like with any goodwill, there`s some bad actors to take advantage.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Tonight, the Secret Service revealing its recovered $286 million siphoned off from pandemic relief given out by the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROY DOTSON, NATIONAL PANDEMIC FRAUD RECOVERY COORDINATOR, \uc2 U.S. SECRET SERVICE\uc2 : It is probably one of the largest financial crime investigations we`ve ever taken on.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: As part of this scheme, agents say scammers open fraudulent accounts at Green Dot Bank with fake and stolen personal information. The bank says it is partner with law enforcement to identify and mitigate the fraud.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to Jason Kane leading the Secret Service`s efforts to recover stolen COVID-19 relief funds.\par \par \uc2 It`s been estimated that hundreds of billions of dollars has gone out fraudulently. Today, you`re talking about a $286 million find. Is that a drop in the bucket?\par \par \uc2 JASON KANE, AGENT IN CHARGE, \uc2 U.S. SECRET SERVICE\uc2 : We have several of those that are ongoing, and those will continue to kind of come out over the coming year as we return them back to taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: So, do you need more funding from Congress to go after more money?\par \par \uc2 KANE: That`s a hard question to say right now to say oh, I need exactly this amount to go after more. I know if I had more, I can do more.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Kane says pandemic relief fraudsters are often overseas in countries where they can evade arrest.\par \par \uc2 KANE: Certain countries don`t always play by the same rules that the United States plays by.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY (on camera): But he says the hunt is ongoing. And all the agency has arrested over 250 people and recovered close to $2.3 billion. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s a lot of money. Julia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, NASA`s first female Launch Director set to make history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Before we go tonight, one more note on Monday`s big launch. History will be made when Artemis one takes off with NASA`s first female Launch Director at the helm. Here`s Kristen Dahlgren.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In hindsight --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One small step for man.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: They are telling words.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE BLACKWELL-THOMPSON, LAUNCH DIRECTOR, NASA`S EXPLORATION GROUND SYSTEMS PROGRAM: There was one woman in this firing room, one woman, out of 400 plus engineers.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: A culture dominated by men even years later when Charlie Blackwell Thompson first visited Kennedy Space Center as a college senior.\par \par \uc2 BLACKWELL-THOMPSON: I thought I would love to be in this room one day.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Today, she isn`t just in the room, but leading it as launch director for Artemis 1, a critical step in returning Americans to the moon and beyond.\par \par \uc2 JANET PETRO, DIRECTOR, \uc2 NASA\uc2  JOHN F. KENNEDY SPACE CENTER: She really is a goddess who runs everything that goes on the processing and the launch operations.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Raised in small town, South Carolina, she was taught to dream big. She raised three children as she worked her way up the \uc2 NASA\uc2  ranks. And on launch day, it`s ultimately her call to utter those much anticipated words.\par \par \uc2 BLACKWELL-THOMPSON: You have a go to watch Artemis 1.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREAN: Well, goosebumps.\par \par \uc2 BLACKWELL-THOMPSON: It gives me goosebumps too.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: For the first time, a female voice.\par \par \uc2 BLACKWELL-THOMPSON: We`ve changed a lot in terms of the makeup of our team. One woman on the team in 1969, today, we`ll have 30 women in the room.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: She calls it just the start.\par \par \uc2 BLACKWELL-THOMPSON: The next set of bootprints that are left on the moon by our astronauts will belong to a woman and a person of color. There are no boundaries. There are no limits. And launch director copies.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: A woman with a mission to inspire. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220827ei8q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Battle Over Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Now to the dramatic shift in abortion rights. So-called trigger laws designed to ban or limit access in the wake of the Supreme Court`s Roe reversal are taking effect in more states. Here`s Kristen Dahlgren.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today, Idaho, Texas, and Tennessee triggering even tougher laws on abortion. Tennessee going from a ban after around six weeks to a near total ban beginning at conception.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LYNN (R-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: It`s a rare exception when the life of the mother is totally in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: How does the law define serious risk and risk to the life of the mother? Is that laid out very carefully?\par \par \uc2 LYNN: Well, in this law, that is -- that determination is left up to a doctor.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But is it up to a doctor to look forward and in their best estimation End a pregnancy before it becomes dangerous for a mother?\par \par \uc2 LYNN: It`s really not -- it has to be that the life of the mother is in danger.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The two-page bill does not include the word exception, instead requiring doctors to prove a procedure was necessary.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY GORDON BONO, PHYSICIAN, TENNESSEE PRIMARY CARE: They really don`t know how to handle certain medical predicaments until a mistake is made or until some case law is cracked -- is comes into being.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: In Texas, except to save the life of the mother, it is now a felony to provide or attempt abortion after fertilization with fines of at least $100,000. While in Idaho, a rare victory for the Biden administration. A judge saying the state cannot punish doctors for performing abortions to save the life of a woman in an emergency room setting.\par \par \uc2 BLAINE CONZATTI, PRESIDENT, IDAHO FAMILY POLICY CENTER: This is just a last ditch effort on the part of a pro-abortion administration to keep abortion legal in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: And late today, after a challenge by North Dakota`s loan abortion clinic, a judge temporarily blocked the state`s plan to outlaw abortions with few exceptions starting tomorrow. Bracing for the ban, the clinic moved across state lines.\par \par \uc2 TAMMI KROMENAKER, DIRECTOR, RED RIVER WOMEN`S CLINIC: People that are most impacted by trigger bans, you know, are exactly the patients we`ve been serving, people who live in a rural area, have difficulty getting to where the clinic is at, Black indigenous people of color.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: With Oklahoma`s stricter law also taking effect Saturday, by the end of the week, it is estimated one in three women will live in a state where abortion is banned or severely restricted. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220826ei8p000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for August 25, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kelly O`Donnell, Kerry Sanders, Kristen Dahlgren,  Priscilla Thompson, Peter Alexander, Gabe Gutierrez, Maggie Vespa \par \uc2 3,935 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Breaking News tonight, the judge`s decision to partially release the Mar-a-Lago search warrant affidavit.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The Federal Magistrate Judge ordering a copy of the affidavit with redactions from the Justice Department to be unsealed. What it could reveal about the reasons behind the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s search at the former president`s home.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, tracking the storm threat on the move. And new images from the flash flooding emergency in Mississippi. Our team in the flood zone tonight.\par \par \uc2 The battle over abortion rights. The states where so-called trigger laws are taking effect today.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, officials saying a Russian controlled nuclear power plant was disconnected from the power grid. New fears of what could happen next.\par \par \uc2 The Uvalde School Board voted unanimously to fire the embattled police chief three months after the massacre. The emotional meeting, parents and survivors speaking out.\par \par \uc2 California greenlighting the most sweeping emissions rules in the nation aiming to ban the sale of new gas-powered cars.\par \par \uc2 And our rare access inside \uc2 NASA\uc2 , suiting up with the astronauts, training to take America back to the moon as we count down to a historic launch.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. We`re all about to learn more of what the government knows that drove it to search former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago estate. A federal judge late today giving the Justice Department until noon Eastern time tomorrow to make public a redacted copy of the affidavit that backed its application for the search warrant.\par \par \uc2 The order issued just hours after the Justice Department`s submitted to the court sections it wants blacked out to protect witnesses, law enforcement, and methods used in the investigation. The full affidavit would describe the government`s case and justification for the unprecedented search. But how much the redacted version will disclose remains an open question tonight.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Donnell is tracking late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): New tonight, granted in part, a decision from the Florida federal magistrate to unseal by noon tomorrow a redacted copy of the affidavit used to authorize the controversial Mar-a-Lago search where \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents seized 11 sets of classified information from former President Trump`s seaside home.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has demanded the whole affidavit be made public. Late today, former Trump`s senior adviser \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  agreed.\par \par \uc2 JARED KUSHNER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISER: So, I think what the judge do is release all the information so people can know whether there`s a serious allegation or this is more manufactured.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Parts of the search warrant affidavit will be blacked out. The judge finding government lawyers made a compelling argument that releasing all of it would identify witnesses and law enforcement and reveal strategy as well as sources and methods. But new information will become public, as the judge described the redactions as narrowly tailored.\par \par \uc2 After President Biden insisted Wednesday he had no warning about the search --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I didn`t have any events notice, none, zero, not one single bit.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL Mr. Trump mocked that response on his social media and lashed out again at the Justice Department and \uc2 FBI\uc2 , claiming their investigation is politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kelly, as I noted at the top, the judge took just a few hours after receiving the Justice Department`s redactions to issue his order.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And that`s unusual. but the judge said this case is of such high public interest. And the affidavit is key to explaining why the Justice Department argued that there would be evidence of potential crimes inside Mar-a-Lago. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell starting us off, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, we`re tracking storms on the move in the south. Millions under flood watches from East Texas to the Florida Panhandle from the same system that brought dangerous flash floods to Mississippi. Carrie Sanders is in the flood zone tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, five million people under flood watch as torrential rains move across the Gulf. This as flash floods pounded parts of Mississippi yesterday derailing trains, washing out roads, and forcing nursing home residents to flee to higher ground.\par \par \uc2 CHOKWE ANTAR LUMUMBA, MAYOR, JACKSON, MISSISSIPPI: If we instruct you that you are in a risk area or a high risk area, please heed that advice.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: While water has receded outside Jackson today, the mayor warning of more floods to come.\par \par \uc2 LUMUMBA: I think it is important that our residents prepare in advance.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Of big concern, the Pearl River running through the city. It`s already nearly three feet above flood stage and could soon impact homes and businesses. That`s only six feet less than the floods that ripped through the same area in 2020, damaging more than 500 homes. Just this week in Florence, more than 100 children and employees had to be rescued from a daycare.\par \par \uc2 In nearby Kenton, the concern is Bear Creek. 80-year-old William James and his 76-year-old wife Arlena are housebound.\par \par \uc2 So, you have to evacuate. Where will you go?\par \par \uc2 ARLENA JAMES, RESIDENT, MISSISSIPPI: Oh my God. Wherever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:05:35]\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: This is far from over. The Pearl River is expected to crest eight feet above flood stage on Tuesday. Meantime, the weather system is moving east dropping two to three inches an hour. Next in its path, the Florida Panhandle. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Kerry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the dramatic shift in abortion rights. So-called trigger laws designed to ban or limit access in the wake of the Supreme Court`s Roe reversal are taking effect in more states. Here`s Kristen Dahlgren.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today, Idaho, Texas, and Tennessee triggering even tougher laws on abortion. Tennessee going from a ban after around six weeks to a near total ban beginning at conception.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LYNN (R-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: It`s a rare exception when the life of the mother is totally in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: How does the law define serious risk and risk to the life of the mother? Is that laid out very carefully?\par \par \uc2 LYNN: Well, in this law, that is -- that determination is left up to a doctor.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But is it up to a doctor to look forward and in their best estimation End a pregnancy before it becomes dangerous for a mother?\par \par \uc2 LYNN: It`s really not -- it has to be that the life of the mother is in danger.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The two-page bill does not include the word exception, instead requiring doctors to prove a procedure was necessary.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY GORDON BONO, PHYSICIAN, TENNESSEE PRIMARY CARE: They really don`t know how to handle certain medical predicaments until a mistake is made or until some case law is cracked -- is comes into being.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: In Texas, except to save the life of the mother, it is now a felony to provide or attempt abortion after fertilization with fines of at least $100,000. While in Idaho, a rare victory for the Biden administration. A judge saying the state cannot punish doctors for performing abortions to save the life of a woman in an emergency room setting.\par \par \uc2 BLAINE CONZATTI, PRESIDENT, IDAHO FAMILY POLICY CENTER: This is just a last ditch effort on the part of a pro-abortion administration to keep abortion legal in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: And late today, after a challenge by North Dakota`s loan abortion clinic, a judge temporarily blocked the state`s plan to outlaw abortions with few exceptions starting tomorrow. Bracing for the ban, the clinic moved across state lines.\par \par \uc2 TAMMI KROMENAKER, DIRECTOR, RED RIVER WOMEN`S CLINIC: People that are most impacted by trigger bans, you know, are exactly the patients we`ve been serving, people who live in a rural area, have difficulty getting to where the clinic is at, Black indigenous people of color.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: With Oklahoma`s stricter law also taking effect Saturday, by the end of the week, it is estimated one in three women will live in a state where abortion is banned or severely restricted. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Three months after the heavily criticized response to the mass shooting in Uvalde, Texas, the school police chief is out of a job. Correspondent Priscilla Thompson is in Uvalde tonight with the emotional fallout.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE ARREDONDO\uc2 , FORMER POLICE CHIEF, UVALDE SCHOOL DISTRICT: 11:32 a.m. this morning --\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Uvalde School`s police chief \uc2 Pete Arredondo\uc2  is out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All in favour. The motion passed unanimously.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: With mounting pressure from parents, the board voted to fire the embattled chief who waited over an hour to storm a classroom and take down the gunman who killed 19 children and two teachers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our babies are dead.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The anger felt by families and students still palpable three months later.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYNE GONZALEZ, FORMER STUDENT, ROBB ELEMENTARY SCHOOL: I have messages for \uc2 Pete Arredondo\uc2  and all the law enforcement over there that day. Turn in your badge and step down. You don`t deserve to wear one.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Some victims` families emerging from the meeting feeling relief.\par \par \uc2 VINCENT SALAZAR, GRANDFATHER OF UVALDE SCHOOL SHOOTING VICTIM: It feels good to finally see some kind of justice being done in Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Javier Cazares lost his 9-year-old daughter Jackie in the shooting.\par \par \uc2 JAVIER CAZARES, FATHER OF UVALDE SCHOOL SHOOTING VICTIM: The work is still not done. We still got more work to do.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Would you like an apology?\par \par \uc2 CAZARES: I already got the apology back then. Right now, no, it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Chief Arredondo refused to attend Wednesday`s meeting. Just prior, his attorney sent the board a scathing 17-page statement calling him the fall guy and the process an unconstitutional public lynching, accusing the board of violating Arredondo`s right to due process and calling for his reinstatement, writing, none of his decisions or actions demonstrate a failure.\par \par \uc2 That despite an investigation by the Texas Legislature which found officers failed to prioritize saving the lives of innocent victims over their own safety. With Arredondo gone, parents are left to reconcile their grief with the memories of their children.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:09]\par \par \uc2 CAZARES: Because of the way she was a forgiving person, she would want us to forgive.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Priscilla Thompson, NBC News, Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In Ukraine, new concerns at a Russian-controlled nuclear plant after officials say a fire temporarily disconnected from the power grid. Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy said emergency generators were activated. Ukraine and Russia blame each other for shelling near the plant.\par \par \uc2 Back home, new debate over President Biden`s plan to forgive student debt for millions of Americans. Some progressives are arguing it doesn`t go far enough, while some moderates and many in the GOP say it`s simply unfair. Peter Alexander is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Triana Downing (PH) is overjoyed.\par \par \uc2 TRIANA DOWNING, HAD STUDENT LOAD DEBT: I did a happy dance. I was -- I was very, very excited.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: After President Biden`s announcement that will knock her student loan debt down from 16 to $6,000. A welcomed relief just a few years after she graduated from \uc2 George Washington University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DOWNING: This is a huge blessing for me. There`s a weight that`s lifted off my shoulders.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But tonight, the president`s executive action is fueling a fierce debate over fairness. The move means the government will raise $10,000 in federal student loans for those making less than $125,000. And $20,000 in student loan debt for lower income Pell Grant recipients.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My plan is responsible and fair. It focuses the benefit on middle class and working families.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The announcement praised by many progressive Democrats, though some wanted the president to go further, while several moderates are critical. Ohio Democrat Tim Ryan warning it sends the wrong message to the millions without a degree working just as hard to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say it`ll force many working-class taxpayers who did not go to college to cover the cost of more affluent people who did.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): This is grossly unfair. But it just gives you an insight in the priorities of the Democratic Party. They`re once again looking out for the interests of wealthy, well-educated elites largely clustered on our coasts.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: A master plumber in Georgia, Gabriel Runyan (PH), is outraged by the President`s decision. He says he and his wife juggled multiple jobs so his wife could pay for college without a loan.\par \par \uc2 What frustrates you most about this?\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL RUNYAN, PLUMBER: We worked hard so we wouldn`t have to get loan. We didn`t want to use the system. So, why did those who use the system get the easy way out?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s still unclear whether this will impact inflation. And the White House says it cannot predict the price tag but one nonpartisan group says it could cost taxpayers $500 billion. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, California strict new rules of the road. Is it the beginning of the end for gas powered cars? And growing threats to \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. How the agency is taking action.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Just breaking, in California, regulators voting to ban the sale of new gasoline-fueled cars by 2035. That major decision today in the Golden State is expected to usher in not just statewide, but likely a global move toward electric plug-in hybrid or hydrogen-run vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  on alert after a rise in threats against the agency. It comes as a new law calls for hiring many more agents. Gabe Gutierrez has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In a letter to \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees, Commissioner \uc2 Charles Rettig\uc2 , a Trump appointee, says he`s ordering a comprehensive review of safety measures at the agency`s facilities nationwide. In recent days, he writes, there has been an abundance of misinformation and false social media posting, some of them with threats directed at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and its employees.\par \par \uc2 LORIE MCCANN, CHAPTER 10 PRESIDENT, CHICAGO \uc2 NATIONAL TREASURY EMPLOYEES UNION\uc2 : We are a target.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The president of the IRS Workers Union in Chicago is telling members to hide their badges when they leave the building.\par \par \uc2 MCCANN: It`s really unfortunate because we`re public servants.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Progress does come.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:02]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The Inflation Reduction Act includes $80 billion in new funding for the \uc2 IRS\uc2  over the next 10 years. The Biden administration says the money is meant to hire new workers after years of underfunding and help prevent high income earners from cheating on their taxes. Some conservatives have falsely claimed that the agency plans to hire 87,000 mostly armed agents.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Those \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents are designed to come after you.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, FOX NEWS: It`s Joe Biden`s new army.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): Are they going to have a strike force that goes in with AK-15s already loaded, ready to shoot some small business person in Iowa?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: In reality, only a fraction of \uc2 IRS\uc2  employees work on criminal investigations and carry firearms.\par \par \uc2 MCCANN: Words have power and they have consequences. And they`re putting federal employees, your fellow Americans in danger by saying this.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: \uc2 John Koskinen\uc2  is a former \uc2 IRS\uc2  Commissioner who faced repeated accusations from GOP lawmakers that the agency unfairly targeted Republican organizations.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KOSKINEN\uc2 , FORMER COMMISSIONER, \uc2 IRS\uc2 : In the last couple of years, it just changed the nature of the threats out there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): This is the first wide ranging security review of its kind at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  since the Oklahoma City bombing in 1995. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re back in a moment with my rare access inside \uc2 NASA\uc2  ahead of their mission to finally return to the moon.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A view this evening of the Artemis 1 rocket sitting on the launch pad in Florida ahead of its planned lift-off on Monday. The rocket, an Orion spacecraft on top critical to America`s plans to return to the moon. I recently visited the Johnson Space Center in Houston where I got a firsthand look at what future lunar explorers will experience.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Sitting on Launch Pad 39B, the Artemis rocket, NASA`s most powerful yet, taller than the Statue of Liberty. 55 engines and motors will lift that nearly 600 million pound rocket skyward on a historic mission, a first step to eventually return Americans to the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 So, how real is this going back to the moon?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA WYCHE, DIRECTOR, NASA JOHNSON SPACE CENTER: Oh, it`s very real. We`re definitely going. We`re going to actually live there. We will have landers go to the moon, we`ll have rovers on the moon, and eventually we`ll have a base camp on the moon.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Once beyond the pull of Earth`s gravity, the Orion capsule perched atop of the rocket, will orbit the moon for 42 days uncrewed but with male and female astronaut dummies loaded with sensors to study the potential effects of the mission on future real astronauts.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Meir is one of 18 veteran astronauts, part of the Artemis team who could fly on the first mission to the moon since 1972. The goal, to learn more about the moon and use it as a jumping off point to Mars.\par \par \uc2 Recently, I met her at Johnson Space Center in Houston to experience some of the training.\par \par \uc2 It`s a big, big rocket you`ve grown up on.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MEIR, ASTRONAUT: Absolutely. Even bigger than the Saturn 5 for the \uc2 Apollo\uc2  missions. You`ve got to get out there in Kennedy Space Center and look up and see it there. And it really gives you some perspective.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Inside the Orion training simulator, we got strapped into our seats. Screens and switches over our heads.\par \par \uc2 MEIR: Here, you can see the different batteries. Over here right now, this is looking at the propulsion system.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: How exciting is it the prospect of going to the moon for you personally?\par \par \uc2 MEIR: It`s incredibly exciting. We haven`t been there since the 70s. And it`s really time to go back. You know, to me, it`s about exploration. It`s just an inherent part of all of us as humans. We had this profound impact on society when we went to the moon the first time with all of this stimulation of resources of all the STEM fields. So, we can expect that all when we go back to the moon again.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And once back on the moon, astronauts will explore the surface. Here on earth, engineers showed me using virtual reality how astronauts will train to walk on the moon, using available data to simulate the lighting conditions, and even what it would be like to climb a ladder to the lander. It even allows me to get a glimpse of home.\par \par \uc2 I want to see the Earth here.\par \par \uc2 To explore the moon further, \uc2 NASA\uc2  is working on a new lunar rover that will allow astronauts to drive on the surface. Using data gathered from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter, they`re able to replicate the deep shadows and craters astronauts will encounter.\par \par \uc2 How do we explore?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BIELSKI, TEAM LEAD, NASA EXPLORATION SYSTEMS SIMULATIONS: You`ll pitch the hand controller forward, and then you can twist it to turn left and right.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But using this, you wouldn`t be able to simulate routes in places you want to go.\par \par \uc2 BIELSKI: Unlike on Earth where your route planner in your car takes into account just where the roads are, you need to be able to accommodate what the terrain looks like, what the lighting is, where you`re going and all along the route.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: \uc2 NASA\uc2  has come a long way from one small step, the return to the moon, and eventually a mission to Mars, a leap that was once beyond our imagination.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Exciting times on \uc2 NASA\uc2 . Sign me up.\par \par \uc2 When we come back., a moving reunion from two survivors of the Highland Park massacre.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Two brothers wounded in the July 4 massacre in Highland Park, Illinois went back to the hospital today where they retreated, this time to say thank you. Here`s Maggie Vespa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For two brothers, walking into this room is a tribute in and of itself. Nicholas and Steven Kolpack returning to Northwestern Medicine Lake Forest Hospital to thank the doctors and nurses who came to their rescue.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS KOLPACK, SURVIVOR, HIGHLAND PARK SHOOTING: There`s always a sense of comfort just being here physically, being able to see the faces.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN KOLPACK, SURVIVOR, HIGHLAND PARK SHOOTING: They cared for us like family.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: That care came on July 4 when at a parade in nearby Highland Park, Illinoism a lone gunman killed seven and wounded dozens, a tragedy that left this suburb forever scarred. The brothers took this photo of Stephen`s kids moments before. Nicholas shot in the right knee and ankle, Stephen in the left calf.\par \par \uc2 S. KOLPACK: I don`t think I really felt anything. I was -- I needed to get the kids out of their.\par \par \uc2 KOLPACK: Doctors went to work.\par \par \uc2 DR. TIMOTHY LOFTUS, NORTHWESTER MEDICINE LAKE FOREST HOSPITAL: We sort of fall back on what we know, and that`s sort of to put calm amongst chaos.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Stephen at risk of losing his leg only cared about his family. His wife also shot in the leg.\par \par \uc2 DR. REGINA ROYAN, NORTHWESTER MEDICINE LAKE FOREST HOSPITAL: And I was like dude, you know, do you need pain medication? He`s just like, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: For both brothers, emergency surgery and physical therapy. Zoe we recovering too. Amid the pain, the family never forgot the strangers who saved them, hospital staff who rarely see patients victories.\par \par \uc2 ROYAN: We don`t often get to see folks walk out of -- walk out of the hospital. We see them when they`re at their worst.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Today feels like a win.\par \par \uc2 ROYAN: Absolutely, a huge win.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Maggie Vepa, NBC News, Lake Forest, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220826ei8p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for August 21, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Symone Sanders-Townsend, Ali Vitali, Marc Caputo \par \uc2 9,580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:17]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, Americans angry, anxious and fired up to vote.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): At the end of the day, if you can`t afford to live, you`re going to vote for change.\par \par \uc2 TODD: With control of Congress on the line, our new NBC News poll shows we may be in uncharted political territory.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): What I`m seeing is a division that I`ve never seen before in this country in my time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Untested candidates, unpredictable turnout and the Trump factor, all shaking up the midterm environment.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R) KENTUCKY: There`s probably a greater likelihood the House flips than the Senate. Candidate quality has lots to do with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guests this morning, the chairman of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, New York Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , and Republican congressman of Kentucky, \uc2 Andy Barr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, crisis in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): We`re seeing so many dedicated teachers leaving the profession.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The nationwide teacher shortage...\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): I was running myself ragged.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... putting children`s learning in jeopardy. Is there anything the Biden administration can do to help?\par \par \uc2 I`ll speak to the secretary of education, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And President Biden takes a victory lap...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN. JR.: We`ve not wavered. We`ve not flinched. And we`ve not given in. Instead, we`re delivering results for the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... capping off a string of legislative victories, signing his massive tax, health care and climate change bill, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets a victory of his own...\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING: I believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to pose a very grave threat and risk to our republic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... ousting his most vocal critic from Congress, even as multiple investigations into him and his allies heat up.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News national political reporter Marc Caputo; Symone Sanders-Townsend, former chief spokeswoman for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali; and Republican strategist Brendan Buck.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history. This is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning to all of you. We are less than three months before the midterms. And our brand-new NBC News poll paints a pretty bleak picture. Americans are angry. They`re disappointed. And they are worried about the future of this country. Just 21 percent of adults say we are headed in the right direction. And once again, three out of four Americans say we are on the wrong track. In fact, this number -- never before in our poll has this wrong-track number been over 70 percent for this long. It`s been nearly a year now. Fifty-eight percent, by the way, say America`s best years are behind us. That`s an all-time high in our poll.\par \par \uc2 So what is behind this rise in pessimism about our country`s future?\par \par \uc2 Well, it starts with this. Americans don`t like either party and they don`t like anyone in power. Take a look at this. Both political parties are very unpopular, one slightly more than the other. It`s a similar story with both the current and former presidents, the former president`s negatives actually rising a bit faster than we`d seen since our last poll. The current and former vice presidents also both deeply unpopular, almost identical. And even the two politicians who have most publicly stood up to the leaders of their own parties, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , well, no one likes them, either. And with their own parties, they are the most unpopular.\par \par \uc2 So add it all up, and this country now has low confidence in a bunch of institutions, Justice Department, the Supreme Court, even our public schools. And for the first time ever, when asked what is the most important issue facing this country, the top answer was not an economic issue. It was threats to the democracy -- higher than cost of living or jobs or guns or even abortion.\par \par \uc2 So this is the environment in which voters are going to the polls this November. They`re pretty angry. In a normal year, these numbers would forecast electoral doom for the party in power. But, boy, these midterms, they`re going to be unlike any we`ve seen in history. And I`ll warn ya, the old rules of politics probably don`t apply.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Are you ready? Are you ready to take back this country?\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): By traditional metrics, Republicans should be headed toward big gains this November. President Biden`s approval rating remains at a paltry 42 percent. The GOP has a two-point lead on which party should control Congress. And three-quarters of Americans think the country is headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): We`ve lost our way.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Look at the country, what it`s come down to. It`s horrible. Everything -- the gas is up; the prices are high for everything.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And yet, interest in the election is up all around, to levels we typically don`t see until October.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): I`m going to vote because I feel like I can`t not vote in this climate.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Democrats shrunk the Republican advantage on interest in the election from eight points to two since May. A major motivating factor for the left?\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Abortion bans...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Abortion.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: For Blake, taking away healthcare freedom for Arizona women is just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Voters disapprove of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade by a 20-point margin, which likely contributes to the six-point enthusiasm advantage among women over men for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): You don`t have to agree with abortion to want to honor other people`s rights.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republicans hope to keep voters focused on costs of living and the economy.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Biden`s reckless spending gave us record inflation that`s crushing working families.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): My number one issue right now, prices, the prices of things. It is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But approval of Biden`s handling of the economy is getting better and gas prices are going down. And in some key states Republicans emerge from bruising primaries with relatively weak nominees, many who were propped up by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: There`s probably a greater likelihood the House flips than the Senate. Senate races are just different. They`re statewide. Candidate quality has a lot to do with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And threats to democracy has replaced costs of living as the top issue on voters` minds, a potential advantage for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2 , (D) WASHINGTON: The MAGA mob did everything they could to stop the peaceful transfer of power. Democracy is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fifty-seven percent of Americans want investigations into President Trump`s actions while president and after to continue. So despite what history tells us, we may be heading into unpredictable territory this November.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): I think both parties are not really addressing the issues. I think they`re both missing the mark.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, joining me now, it`s Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 . He`s a Democrat from New York. He`s also the man in charge of the campaign committee that is designed to keep the House in Democratic hands this November.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with the fact that, look, it -- our poll shows a pretty even playing field. You`ve got anger on both sides of the aisle, which could be usually a driver in midterms. That seems to have equalized. Is it fair to say now that there`s no moral victory for you? You hold the House, that`s a win. You lose the House, that`s a loss.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Yeah, that`s right. But we have had a summer of strength, and we`re going to buck history by making history. We, just in the last few weeks, passed historic legislation to finally lower prescription drug costs, capping seniors` out-of-pocket costs, combating climate change, a 40 percent reduction in just the next eight years in our emissions, veterans` healthcare that the Republicans tried to stop, and of course gun safety legislation. And that`s on top of the American Jobs bill, the CHIPS and Science Act, which will revitalize our manufacturing sector.\par \par \uc2 Look, we know that we need to get things done. That`s the hard path that we`ve chosen. The other side has had a summer of stumbles, obstructing veterans` healthcare, ripping away 50 years of reproductive freedom, and now trying to defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and ignore a serious threat to our national security with Trump`s latest scandal. We`re going to address real problems. We think that`s going to bring it home.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One of the issues, though, I think, that you have to overcome is the amount of open seats, the amount of members of Congress on the democratic side that chose not to run. I`ll put up the list here. It`s quite long. As you can see, we have to use really small font in order to get all the names in. These folks left when they thought this was going to be a midterm that looked like it was going to be a disaster for your party. Do you wish these folks would have re-thought and had had more confidence that the political landscape might have changed?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Listen, we`ve been counted out for a while now, but we are having a comeback. And we have great candidates all over the country, veterans, diverse candidates that look like America, that are a good fit for their direct. And we`re excited to share those candidates with the American people.\par \par \uc2 Look, you`re seeing us come back in the polls. Our front-liners are battle- tested and strong. They have a huge advantage, by the way, over their Republican opponents in terms of their campaigns, their cash on hand. They`re getting their votes right. They have historic deliverables that they`ve brought home to their districts, better roads, better bridges, clean drinking water, an end to this pandemic. Look, we`ve got a story to tell, and we`re going to go out and tell it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the other side has a story that they`re telling, and I want to play a couple of examples. And these are in your front-line districts, where Republicans are hitting them. Here`s one on the economy. This one is without sound here, but it`s hitting the -- what you guys call the Inflation Reduction Act by saying, "Hey, this isn`t touching inflation, higher taxes on American people, American families, and this is all being done in recession."\par \par \uc2 [09:10:02]\par \par \uc2 And there`s also some ads, here, that`s going to roll in here, that hits the Democrats on crime and defund the police.\par \par \uc2 So let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YESLI VEGA, (R) CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: My brother had been gunned down and nearly killed by MS-13 gang members. I knew then that I would join law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. A lot of those front-liners wanted more to counter the defund the police narrative. And Congress ended up not passing anything. Are you concerned that these issues like high costs and crime are still going to trump democracy and abortion in these swing districts?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, let`s look at where we are. Gas prices are more than $1 lower than they were just about 60 days ago, and they`re still going down. That`s good. So the -- so the inflation that goes along with that is coming down as well. It`s not -- not where we want to be, but we`re making real progress. The other side, of course, has no plan to lower costs. They only have a ploy to win power for themselves. So we`re working the problem. They`re working on their own power.\par \par \uc2 And on the other issues you mentioned, my goodness, every Republican voted against lower prescription drug costs and a cap on seniors` out-of-pocket costs of $2,000 a year. That`s the kind of real progress that, under President Biden`s leadership and under Speaker Pelosi`s leadership, we`re getting done.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve got to go tell people what we`re doing. But the other side, again, is interested only in their own power and has taken away 50 years of reproductive freedom, wants to whitewash the attack on our democracy, will do nothing -- this MAGA crowd will do nothing while kids are getting gunned down in their classrooms. We`ve got a plan for your future. They`ve got a plan to win power. And that`s the choice.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of power, there`s a lot of people who thought that you oversaw a very cynical ploy in order to help a Trump-backed opponent in the Peter Meijer district in Michigan. After -- look, you see the result. You got the candidate that you wanted, apparently, there. But you put party over country, did you not? And is that something you`re comfortable with?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Absolutely not did we put party over country. The moral imperative right now, Mr. Todd, is to keep the dangerous MAGA Republicans who voted to overturn our election out of power. There were 139 of them who voted against the results of the election back in January 2021. So this danger didn`t start with the primary in Michigan. But let`s talk about that. We believe that, by running a commercial that called our opponent an extremist who was too conservative for western Michigan, we teed up the choice in the fall. We`ve got a strong pro-choice Democrat there named Hillary Scholten. I hope people will support her. We`re going to win that seat. The big loser in the Michigan primary was Kevin McCarthy and this MAGA crowd, and they know it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But if -- if Mr. Gibbs ends up getting elected, in the words of Peter Meijer, "With a historically unpopular president in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and inflation at 40-year highs, less-electable doesn`t mean un- electable." If this man, Mr. Gibbs, wins, then have you not helped an election denier get into Congress?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Again, this danger didn`t start with Mr. Gibbs. By every measure, he`s the weaker candidate. Don`t take my word for it. The Cook Political Report says it`s far more likely the Democrats are going to win that seat now. That`s doing our job. And let`s remember, Peter Meijer was an anti- choice extremist who also was going to vote to have Jim Jordan run the Judiciary Committee, give you two years of nonsense investigating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  -- there`s some other silliness -- when what we need to do is keep up the progress...\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK, but...\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: ... on climate change, on prescription drug costs, on helping seniors with healthcare.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, when you`re -- you`re on the Democratic side of the aisle saying, "Hey, Republicans need to stand up to their own party and put country over party," and someone like Peter Meijer does, and voted to impeach the president, put his political career on the line, to -- look, I understand the strategic decision that you make, but you`ve seen our poll. There`s a reason everybody hates both political parties. It`s cynical moves like this, is it not?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Chuck, my job is to -- is to win elections for the Democrats, and I take that seriously, because the moral imperative is keeping the gavel out of the hands of Kevin McCarthy, who would have overturned the results of the election. Again, this danger didn`t start with Mr. Gibbs. I understand that there are difficult moral questions, philosophical questions about tactics. That`s always true in politics. You better believe it. But here`s the deal. We are more likely to win that seat. John Gibbs is a weak opponent. He`s an extremist with a vicious anti-choice record. He can`t string a set of policies together that will make sense for western Michigan. We`re going to win that seat. And we believe that, by keeping those dangerous people out of power, we address the larger moral imperative, and that`s our job.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know you`re on the ballot Tuesday. There`s also a special election in New York that basically is testing these two competing messages.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:02]\par \par \uc2 You guys are, on the Democratic side, on abortion; on the Republican side, they`re running on inflation. Is it fair to call that, the winner, the party that wins that special the one that has the upper hand?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Oh, well, that should be a Republican victory. I see that they`re panic-spending in the last week. They`re clearly worried. Look at what happened in Kansas. We won 14 counties that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  carried in Kansas. In the swing congressional district there, it got 68 percent support for abortion rights. In Minnesota, in that special election, we closed the gap in half. And in upstate New York, you know, Pat Ryan, our candidate, he`s a West Point grad; he`s a war hero, started a business. He`s been running a county, Ulster County. He`s a great candidate. I think you`re going to see -- I think you`re going to see him perform very well. And I know the Republicans are panicking.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , the Democrat charged with keeping the House in Democratic hands. Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. joining me now for -- from the other side of the aisle, it`s Republican congressman from Kentucky \uc2 Andy Barr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman Barr, welcome to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ANDY BARR\uc2 , (D) KENTUCKY: Chuck, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you`re no stranger to extraordinarily close elections, to elections that -- midterms that have been as unusual as this one. You`ve seen our poll. I want to start with the top issue. The fact that threats to democracy have overtaken cost of living -- look, six months ago, it seemed like cost -- you guys were going to ride the cost of living issue to the majority. Cost of living is certainty an important issue, but it`s not the only one anymore. Are you concerned that, with lower gas prices, that all of a sudden you guys don`t have the right message to win this?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, Chuck, at the beginning of the program, you made the important point that the American people are angry and anxious, and there`s a reason why the American people are angry and anxious. And it`s because they are worse off under the policies of congressional Democrats and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yes, they are concerned about our country and the future of our country because we`re in a recession, because we`ve had two consecutive quarters of negative economic growth, despite what the White House wants to do in redefining that term. Real wages are down. Credit card debt is up, because people cannot afford this increased cost of living.\par \par \uc2 We are experiencing the highest inflation, Chuck, in 40 years. The average American household is suffering under $5,000 reduced purchasing power. They`re paying more for virtually everything. Gas prices have doubled. There`s an energy crisis in this country because this president has waged a war on energy in this country. We are no longer energy independent. In fact, we have gone from energy dominant to energy desperate in just a year and a half`s time.\par \par \uc2 And we are experiencing a crime wave, 15% increase in homicides because of the defund police movement. And we have no operational control over the southern border. And in my home state of Kentucky, we are experiencing the worst Fentanyl overdose crisis in our country. No, Kentucky is not on the southern border, but we are a border state because our citizens are dying because this administration and this leadership in the Democrat-controlled Congress refuses to follow the law and secure our border, and people are dying because of it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask...\par \par \uc2 BARR: That`s why the American people are anxious and angry in this country and why they want a change.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. What is your plan to deal with inflation? What is the Republican plan to deal with inflation, other than not supporting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  policies? And I say that because what is the proactive agenda here?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, we have a positive agenda. We have a commitment to America. And we`re going to get back to basics. Republicans are going to win landslide elections, not just because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done such a poor job and congressional Democrats have failed the American people on the economy, crime and the border, but because we have a positive agenda to secure the border, to get back to basics, to stop the flow of drugs into our communities, to prevent people on the terror watch lists from coming across the southern border, and to restore the Remain in Mexico policy that this administration just reversed; to finish a border wall infrastructure and technology and resource Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Democrats just passed a bill that would increase the workforce at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  by 87,000 people to come after law-abiding American small-business owners and harass them. We don`t need more \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. We need more Border Patrol agents. And we have a common-sense plan to reduce the cost of living, to lower the cost at the pump. We want to restore American emergency independence. We`re going to end these policies that -- that prevent us from being energy independent. We`re going to stop the politicization of the financing of energy. And we are going to produce more energy to reduce our dependence on foreign sources of energy and also speculative, unreliable sources of energy that this administration is so obsessed about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the \uc2 IRS\uc2  talking point because I just don`t get it. Because a lot of Republicans have talked about dealing with waste, fraud and abuse. The current head of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , who was a Trump appointee, said he didn`t have enough people to -- essentially that the biggest problem we have is people don`t pay the taxes that they`re supposed to pay. If you`re upset about extra \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, stop cheating on your taxes, Congressman. And I`m not saying you personally, but, I mean, I`m talking about in general.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Right, Chuck. Well, no -- look, everybody believes that people should pay their taxes. But the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  looking at this legislation, this reconciliation bill, this inflation expansion act, says that $20 billion of these audits are going to come at the expense of low and middle-income Americans.\par \par \uc2 When -- when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his campaign promised the American people that, oh, taxes were not going to go up for people earning less than $400,000, that was a lie. This bill is going to come at the expense of the American people. Raising taxes on energy producers, job creators and people trying to save for retirement in the middle of a recession -- I don`t know, for the life of me, why they think that`s a winning strategy in a situation where Americans can`t afford gas. They can`t afford groceries. Heck, moms can`t even get baby formula right now. So the economy is not serving the Democrats well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah...\par \par \uc2 BARR: And only in Joe Biden`s America -- only in Joe Biden`s America is the solution to an inflation-induced -- spending-induced inflation crisis more spending. The way to fix inflation is to lower taxes, to repair our supply chains and to produce more energy to lower prices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right...\par \par \uc2 BARR: That`s the solution, and that`s the Republican solution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to respond to what happened to \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  earlier this week. Here`s what she said in her concession speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING: We have candidates for Congress, including here in Wyoming, who refuse to acknowledge that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the 2020 election and suggest that states de-certify their results. Our nation is barreling once again towards crisis, lawlessness and violence. No American should support election deniers for any position of genuine responsibility, where their refusal to follow the rule of law will corrupt our future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Congressman, you voted to certify this election. You`re not one of these folks that goes out there talking about this nonsense. But there are a lot of House Republicans that are being supported by the NRCC and the campaign committee that do. Respond to Congresswoman Cheney there. Do you agree with her warnings and concerns?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I think it goes to show that -- and the election outcomes in a lot of these primaries, it goes to show these congressional seats do not belong to politicians in Washington. They -- these seats belong to the American people. And there is a massive, massive disconnect between the priorities of politicians in Washington and the concerns of the American people.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been back in my district in urban Kentucky, Mount Sterling, Kentucky, in Harrodsburg, Kentucky, Richmond, Kentucky, over the last several weeks. And not once -- not once have any of my constituents, Republican or Democrat, talked about the 2020 election, January 6th, the committee in Washington, or any of these issues. They`re talking about not being able to afford putting food on the table, putting gas in their trucks and not being able to -- to see their grandson again because he died of a Fentanyl overdose because we don`t have control over our southern border. That`s what the American people care about. And politicians that obsess about the past are not in touch with the American people.\par \par \uc2 That`s why the Democrats are going to lose...\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sounds like you don`t believe Republicans...\par \par \uc2 BARR: ... this election big time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you`re saying Republicans who are campaigning on Donald Trump`s election denialism are making a mistake?\par \par \uc2 BARR: What I`m saying is that Republicans are going to win this election because we`re laser-focused on what the American people actually care about, and that is the rising price of gas, the rising price of groceries, the fact that we can`t find baby formula on the shelves, the crime crisis in this country. You know, the -- the Democrats are advocating a dangerous policy of not only defunding the police but eliminating cash bail, eliminating qualified immunity for law enforcement. And homicides are up 15 percent in this country. Americans want security and safety. Democrats are not delivering that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Congressman...\par \par \uc2 BARR: We have a positive agenda to put the country back on the right track.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m giving you a lot of air time here. Congressman \uc2 Andy Barr\uc2 , Republican from Kentucky, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective about this campaign with us as well. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:03]\par \par \uc2 BARR: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, President Biden, he`s having one of his best stretches of his presidency at arguably the best possible time to have it. But can anything overcome Americans` deep dissatisfaction right now? The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here, NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, author of the new book "Electable," about women getting to the White House; NBC News national political reporter Marc Caputo is here; Republican strategist Brendan Buck; and Symone Sanders-Townsend, the former chief spokeswoman for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. I want to set things up here with a bit of an anger issue that we have here. We asked, if -- we asked people in our poll, "Are you so upset about something that you would carry a protest sign for a day?" Over 60 percent said they would. But the breakdown here -- it was nearly 80 percent of Democrats wanted to carry a protest sign. It was only 48 percent of Republicans. We`ve asked this question before, and it`s usually pretty 50-50.\par \par \uc2 This tells you the anger is on the left more than it is on the right. Now, here are the word clouds from the various protest signs. On the Democratic side, it is one phrase that jumps out over all else, "Women`s rights." And if you look in there, almost -- you see a lot of the Dobbs decision influencing those protest signs.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the right here, not anything as dominant. "Impeach Biden" is up top. I`m going to put them together, though, so that you can see here that women`s rights is so dominant it`s the bigger of either of the two.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Buck, normally the out party is the angrier party. Right now it is the in party that is angrier, and that could be a good sign for the left.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: It could be. You know, there`s an argument that they`re upset with Republicans. There`s also been a lot of intra-party fighting on the left, too. I think they`re a little bit angry with each other.\par \par \uc2 But, yeah, this is what`s different. The Roe ruling completely changed politics. What happened in Kansas, with a traditionally conservative state, had a massive Democratic turnout that really nobody saw coming, has really changed -- opened a lot of people`s eyes.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans are engaged too, though. So let`s not -- you know, there`s a lot of issues that they`re fired up about as well. So I don`t think that we should assume that Republicans aren`t going to turn out. But it`s potentially a different type of turnout model than we`re used to in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Symone -- and it isn`t one issue. You know, normally it is one or two issues driving it. And it`s clearly, abortion and democracy are firing up the left. Immigration and just a grievance against Biden fires up the right. That`s certainly there, too. But this is what`s changed. I mean, it does seem like abortion did change everything.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, FORMER CHIEF SPOKESWOMAN FOR VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think it did change everything. And I think it`s important to note that -- you know, I`m from Nebraska. Kansas is right down the road. It -- Republicans had to come out and vote to defeat that amendment. And I think the reality here is, is that rolling back Roe, curbing a women`s ability to make decisions about her own body is not just for women who voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , or women who are Democrats. It`s women, period. It affects men, period, people across this country.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:02]\par \par \uc2 I don`t think it`s just a Democratic issue. Republican women, they get abortions, too.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yeah, but then there`s also the idea, and you see it in that word cloud, women`s rights is in both the blue side and the red side of the sign carrying crowd. But then also the idea of freedom pops up on the red side of that, if you see it on that word cloud.\par \par \uc2 Democrats did a good job in Kansas and Democrats that I`ve talked to are trying to replicate this issue on abortion across the country. The idea of co-opting that message of freedom and trying to apply it to an electorate who, regardless of party stripes, women don`t like the idea of people telling them what they can and cannot do with their health care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Marc, one other thing, we did test the president`s agenda here and the new bill. He`s got some good news in that the bill -- the climate, health care, and tax bill -- starts out net positive, 42 percent good idea, compared to the first time we asked about the Trump tax plan. That was underwater. Obamacare was underwater.\par \par \uc2 So, the good sign for him is that. But let me show you this next slide here which House Republicans will jump on, which is people are skeptical that this thing is actually going to help them. So, a plurality thought "not make a difference" with 36 percent. But 35 percent thought the bill would make things worse for me, 26 percent thought that it would make things better for me. So, a double-edged sword.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: A double-edged sword, sure, but I mean, by and large, people are right. I`m not sure if they`ve really read the bill, but the fact is, like, we`re going to do something about climate change. Well, you`re not going to really notice a difference, if you are going to notice a difference, for decades. I mean, these are things that are kind of built in and baked in.\par \par \uc2 But the real good news here is that there is a shine on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  now. He`s finally got a Congress that looks as if it`s functional and it`s notching wins. And for Biden, who for a period of time was presiding over kind of nominally a totally dysfunctional Congress, the left fighting with the left, Democrats fighting Democrats, great news.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, and Brendan, it does seem to be -- you look at all of a sudden what`s happening on the Republican side, Trump`s got -- ironically, Trump also has gotten - as Biden has gotten better, Trump`s gotten stronger within the party. But that`s not necessarily helpful to the GOP in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah, usually when things are good for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they`re bad for the party. But here`s a situation where he had a raid at his home and all of a sudden, the party is rallying to him. And that`s good for him and I think he`s cleared out a lot of the competition potentially for a nomination.\par \par \uc2 But it puts \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back on the stage. And then when you compare \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that only tends to hurt Democrats. Republicans want this election to be about what \uc2 Andy Barr\uc2  talked about -- cost of living, gas prices, things like that. And then we`re talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , reminding people that this is Donald Trump`s party, it hurts.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s why nobody wants him to announce for president. Just kind of stay away for a little while and let us get over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Symone, is it, is it time to actually believe that the Democrats can keep the House? Or is that a bridge too far at this point?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I think that it is a possibility. I think folks that are saying it`s a foregone conclusion are being way too optimistic.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be a turnout election. And different from Kansas, right? Kansas people were going out to defeat a ballot initiative. In November, it`s not a ballot initiative. People are going out to vote for candidates, which is why I think it is very important that Democrats need not talk about the search on the former president`s home, need not talk about --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You wouldn`t talk about Trump?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: No, you don`t need to talk about it. Look, if talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to get people elected, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  would be the governor of Virginia right now.\par \par \uc2 They need to talk about what Democrats have done, what this bill will mean for families. I don`t think they`re going to see the climate change effects this November, but they are going to see it sooner than ten years. Lowering prescription drugs, jobs, there is something to talk about here. And I think that`s what they should go out and do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I`m going to pause the conversation here. You guys are coming back, but we`re going to have a little bit of an interlude here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to talk about what`s happening as kids go back to school. Are there enough qualified teachers to teach them when they get there?\par \par \uc2 The Education Secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:38]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, schools don`t have the teachers and the staff they need. Many places are scrambling to fill the gaps; Alabama lowering its requirements to receive a teaching certificate. Florida turned their military veterans to lead classrooms, even if they have no qualifications to do that in their degree.\par \par \uc2 Some districts in rural Texas are moving to four-day school weeks. And while states like Tennessee and Michigan are trying to get retired teachers back, Arizona is turning to college students, getting them to teach before they even graduate. And that`s just the tip of the iceberg.\par \par \uc2 So it`s no wonder that in our new poll, it shows that one in three people have little or no confidence in America`s public schools. It is a big problem.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Secretary of Education \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Secretary Cardona, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , SECRETARY OF EDUCATION: Hey, great to be with you again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I`ve seen a lot of your interviews this week and you`ve talked about, look, we`ve got -- there`s a short-term crisis that we`re dealing with. And we`ve got a long-term problem we`ve got to deal with.\par \par \uc2 Look, you`ve thrown money at this, and I say this not disparagingly; the money is there, trying to deal with the short-term teacher retention bonuses in things like this.\par \par \uc2 The problem is a human capital problem, you know? You guys can`t legislate physical teachers for these classrooms.\par \par \uc2 What is some of your answers to the long-term problem?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Well, I`ve seen good examples of what states are doing to address the long-term issue. Well, first of all, let me just say it is back-to- school time. I`m excited that our students, 50 million students, are returning back to their classrooms. This is going to be a great year.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, we are dealing with a shortage issue. But some of the long-term solutions that we must focus on are creating grow-your-own programs. We need to tap the shoulders of our high school students, of our paraeducators who are dedicated in our schools already, and give them a pathway to higher education.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve encouraged, we`ve strongly encouraged, the use of the American Rescue Plan dollars to get some of this work done. And we`re seeing it in Tennessee. They`re working on an apprenticeship program.\par \par \uc2 I visited Las Vegas and the high school there has a partnership with UNLV to get their high school students connected into a teacher preparation program so they can be hired again.\par \par \uc2 And again, I want to stress, Chuck, it`s really important that I stress, the American Rescue Plan dollars should be used for this. Now remember, only half of Congress voted for the American Rescue Plan, but 100 percent of our country can benefit from it. And that`s what we`re encouraging folks to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I tell you though, it`s a bit distressing. You`ve talked about these innovative programs, but at the end of the day, you know, if we would have had a shortage in people training airline pilots, we wouldn`t suddenly say, hey. Let`s see if we can fast-track people who`ve ridden on airplanes while they learn how to teach it.\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, what kind of damage are we going to cause to a generation here who are going to be taught by under-qualified teachers?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Well, if you`ve heard my interviews, you`ve heard me say this teacher shortage issue is a symptom of what I call a teacher respect issue. We need to respect the profession better.\par \par \uc2 College graduates earn 33 percent more than your average teacher when they leave. And adjusted for inflation, over the last 25 years, teachers have made a $29 increase in their salary.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:05]\par \par \uc2 That`s unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we`ve normalized teachers driving Uber on the weekends to make ends meet or working at a restaurant, waiting tables to make ends meet. We have to lift the profession. We`re speaking boldly about that and we want the rest of the country to respond.\par \par \uc2 This didn`t start with the pandemic. It`s really important that we note that. I`ve been in education over 20 years, 25 years. We`ve been talking about fighting for respect in the profession for decades.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. Is there any way of taking the student debt crisis and finding a way to, you know -- I think there`s a lot of people uncomfortable with just canceling student debt without something in return. Is there any way to take student debt and meet the teaching crisis in figuring out a way to incentivize, hey, we`ll wipe away your debt, if you give us five years in the school?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Yeah. Yeah, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 And we do have examples of that across the country. Let me just mention public service loan forgiveness was broken. We fixed it so that our teachers, our nurses can get loan forgiveness right away. We`ve forgiven over $10 billion in public service loan forgiveness to date. And we`re asking folks to go on Studentaid.gov to find out if you`re eligible now.\par \par \uc2 But moving forward, yes, we are encouraging the use of the American Rescue Plan dollars to offset some of those costs. That`s a great way to incentivize teachers. But we`re also saying, why don`t we take our paraeducators and give them some college credits paid for by the American Rescue Plan dollars to address the issue?\par \par \uc2 They`ve proven that they want to be in those schools and those communities. Let`s incentivize them going into the profession, using the American Rescue Plan dollars.\par \par \uc2 And although Representative Barr didn`t vote for the American Rescue Plan, we know his community can benefit from it, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve got to ask you before you go, we`re ten days away from the most recent moratorium on student debt to expire. Should we expect another temporary extension or is a permanent fix coming?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: You know, from day one, we`ve been really focused on making sure we`re protecting our students and our borrowers, $32 billion in debt forgiveness from day one. We know August 31st is a date that many people are waiting to hear something from. We`ve been talking daily about this.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you that American people will hear within the next week or so from the president and the department --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it fair to say it won`t be nothing?\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Well, I don`t have any news to announce today, Chuck. But I will tell you the American people will hear directly from us because we recognize this is an important issue across the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Secretary Cardona, the secretary of education, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 CARDONA: Great to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, the amount of water in the Southwest; it`s shrinking fast, population growing fast.\par \par \uc2 But first, if you don`t already know about First Read, it`s our daily politics newsletter from Meet the Press and the NBC News political unit. Every morning, we`re bringing you the newest polling data, the freshest reporting from the campaign trail, and the sharpest analysis you`ve come to expect from both Meet the Press and NBC News.\par \par \uc2 So sign up. Scan the QR code that`s on your screen right now or go to NBCNews.com/Firstread.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:46:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 This week, the federal government announced more emergency water cuts for states as well as our neighbor to the south along the Colorado River. Arizona and Nevada, as well as parts of Mexico, are going to be required to significantly reduce their water usage from the Colorado River in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is hard to overstate the importance of the Colorado River Basin in the Southwest. It serves 40 million people. Look at the annual economic activity it generates, $1.4 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But we`re in the middle of a 23-year mega drought as well, which is contributing to what has been a low level of water all along the Colorado River Basin. In fact, it is as dry there now as it has been in the last 1,200 years.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you these water levels these days. Right now, we are at some dangerously low levels here, just over a thousand feet. The near high was about 20 years ago, near 1,200 feet there.\par \par \uc2 Let me just put this in perspective. Go back to 1937. That`s when we first started filling Lake Mead, and the level is basically the same.\par \par \uc2 If the lake drops further, to 950 feet, the dam no longer is able to produce hydroelectric power. So forget just a water crisis, then you have an energy crisis, too.\par \par \uc2 Look, there`s a lot of reasons for the diminishing water supply. Climate change is a big part of it. But you also can`t ignore the impact of simply growing population numbers.\par \par \uc2 Look at the cities that are served by the Colorado River Basin. They are among the cities that a lot of Americans have been moving to over the last 20 years, some of the places in the sun and fun of San Diego, Vegas, Denver, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And look at these population gains. In 1990, the area was over 16 million. Now, it`s over 23 million. By 2025, we are looking at 25 million.\par \par \uc2 So, put it all together. The rate of population growth continues as high as it is. We continue with this mega drought.\par \par \uc2 We are going to have more actions like this that become more drastic, more extreme. And then we`re going to be fighting politically over water. It is not a pretty picture.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, two weeks after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home, a majority of Americans still believe these investigations into the former president should continue.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 So Donald Trump`s hold on the Republican Party, we have a poll question that we track here. And it basically, do you identify more -- this is just among Republicans. We asked, do you identify more as a Republican or as a Trump supporter?\par \par \uc2 And let me show you what had happened. We had been watching basically a slow erosion of support for Trump, Marc Caputo. And it was happening. And we saw it in our polls. We saw it in other polls.\par \par \uc2 And then we happened to be in the field right after Mar-a-Lago and right after the search warrant was executed. And so we saw this gap close. We saw a spike in interest in the election among rural voters. That showed you that there was something there.\par \par \uc2 It goes to Brendan`s point before. Whatever it is, when it`s somehow bad for Trump legally, it`s good for Trump politically.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Yeah. And it`s also sort of bad for the Republican Party long term. Because we`re, they are looking at -- and I think I quoted Brendan the other day in my story where we talked to different Republicans who have talked to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they said, he`s over the moon.\par \par \uc2 He was celebrating \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  getting defeated. He`s celebrating these sorts of poll numbers. He`s celebrating the fact that he`s raising more money.\par \par \uc2 And people are thinking, oh my god. You`ve got like four or five criminal investigations into you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You might go to jail --\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Right. Correct.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and you`re celebrating?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Well, as Brendan had said. I`m going to now quote him on this show, right? As he said, he`ll win the Republican primary from a jail cell. And you`re seeing it in these numbers. They feel, his base feels, that any sort of law enforcement action or attack on him is an attack on them personally.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: And I never bought the slippage. I`m sorry. Like, he was out of sight, out of mind --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BUCK: I mean, obviously he was there--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BUCK: --in the polls. I`m not denying that. But he was out of sight, out of mind.\par \par \uc2 Whenever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reemerged, this was going to happen. We`ve seen it. The party is in the image of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Everyone who is not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to act like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That`s because that`s what voters want. So as soon as he came back, they were going to come back to him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting little contradiction in our poll when it comes to investigating Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, overall, 57 percent said all these investigations should continue. But when we asked specifically about January 6th, what`s your confidence in that? A majority have very little or only some confidence. Only 42 percent call themselves a great deal and a fair amount of confidence.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s almost as if the direct criticism of the lack of complete, I guess, equal bipartisanship, if you will, on the January 6th Committee has taken hold with enough voters. But just because you may not trust January 6th doesn`t mean you don`t want Trump investigated. It`s an interesting gap.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, if you want more investigation, certainly you can take your pick of the number of investigations that are actually happening right now. You can look to Georgia. You can look to New York. You can look to the January 6th Committee. You can look down the street from where we are at DOJ.\par \par \uc2 Like, you`ve got options there if you want to see investigations keep going. I think the thing that`s striking to me is that if you want to see investigations that are done in the public eye, you really have no other option other than the January 6th Committee. And I think the work that they do in September is going to be really interesting, especially now that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  --not that she was ever, like, operating under the premise that she had to play nice -- but certainly she is unchained right now at this point.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:11]\par \par \uc2 And the fact that they`re talking to Pence, I think casts this in a really fascinating light now, too.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: I`m sorry. I think there was just also an element of overselling and underdelivering. Certainly when you looked at the social media traffic and what a lot of pundits were saying, you would have thought that this January 6th Committee, like, when they had recently concluded that Trump was going to be clapped in irons and frog marched to Devil`s Island. And that didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But that was never going to be their charge.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: No, I don`t think that`s -- yeah, I don`t think that`s what they said either. And also, I think that the January 6th Committee investigation is not about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  implicitly; the threat to our democracy is very real.\par \par \uc2 It is not -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be -- let`s just say one of these investigations gets him and he goes to jail. The threat to our democracy will still be there. A number of election deniers have won for -- ran for office and won in primaries.\par \par \uc2 The people at the Capitol that day on January 6th, many of them went home. There were thousands of people there. Only a couple hundred have been arrested and convicted. So this is bigger than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I do think that it is a dereliction of duty for folks to make what happened on January 6th solely about this one deranged, very dangerous former president.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But isn`t the January 6th Committee`s work also the fact that they are showing in real time the split in the Republican Party right now? Yeah, the majority is with Trump and rallying around him at this moment. But they have used Republican voices to do the condemning of the former president and his orbit during these hearings, and then of course being in Alaska just recently with Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  saying, well, I really hope we`re not the party of Trump.\par \par \uc2 You are. But at the same time, there`s a few voices that are still there saying, hey, there`s another way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Brendan, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said again in her concession. I`m not going to play this SOT now -- we`ve got less time -- but I will read it fast.\par \par \uc2 I`ve said this in January 6th. I`ll do whatever it takes to ensure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is never again anywhere near the Oval Office, and I mean it.\par \par \uc2 What`s the best way for her to fulfill that promise?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah. I think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  did a really good job looking forward after losing her race. But I think people who don`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , were never Trump Republicans, they do appreciate this was a pretty low point for the anti-Trump movement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, it is.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Now, there were ten people who voted for impeachment. Only two of them have moved forward in their primaries. The road ahead is hard.\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is not -- while I love what she`s doing, she is not reflective of the Republican Party. And maybe she`ll run. Maybe she`ll run as an independent. Maybe she`ll try to primary him.\par \par \uc2 But that lane is incredibly narrow right now. So she`s got to figure out what`s the best way to hurt him and not help \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think if she runs herself as an independent, it could actually help \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Doesn`t it help Trump, if she runs, though, too?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, I think if you`re a Republican voter who doesn`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and it`s a binary choice between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it might be nice to have a \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  where you don`t have to feel dirty voting for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Marc Caputo. What`s \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  thinking now that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got his grievance mojo back?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Well, that`s a good question. I think in Trump world, which is more obsessed with DeSantis than DeSantis is with Trump, I got an image of a meme that was floating around \uc2 Facebook\uc2  from a Republican consultant who`s associated with Trump showing DeSantis welcoming the \uc2 FBI\uc2  into Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 So Trump world actually thinks he loves this. DeSantis I think is just waiting for his opportunity, and it`s not there now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. That is all we have for today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, this Tuesday we are going to be breaking down more primaries. Catch myself and Kristen Welker live on our Meet the Press election special. Florida and New York, two pretty decent-sized states, I`ve heard. Nine p.m., Tuesday, streaming, NBC News Now. Don`t miss it.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be back here next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220821ei8l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Conversation With Clea Shearer About Her Really Personal Journey\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 1,482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Welcome back (inaudible) plaza if the summer break is left you how much, you say, a little less organized than you`d like do not worry, because the incredible ladies, our friends from "The Home Edit", they got you covered.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yes. With a hit \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series, two best selling books and an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account with nearly 7 million followers. Clea Shearer and Joanna Teplin are helping people declutter their zone, one (inaudible) at a time.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, they are and they`re out with their second issue of "The Home Edit" magazine and Clea`s going to join us from her office in Nashville. We`ll talk about the mag in a bit, but Clea as you have been so brave in sharing with the world. You are currently going through chemo with breast cancer. You have been such a warrior. You`ve really been kind to share this journey with everybody. So I know everybody wants to know how you`re doing and how you`re feeling?\par \par \uc2 CLEA SHEARER, CO-WRITER OF "THE HOME EDIT": Hi, guys. I miss you. I wish I was in studio, but I am doing well. I`m plugging away, you know, one day at a time, one week at a time. My motto is cancer free by Christmas, so that`s -- that`s what I`m hoping for.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know, everybody has their own, sort of, mental way that they get through this and you`ve had some difficult, difficult moments when you thought you were done with chemo. You ended up having -- having to go a step back and you started it all up again. So what is it you tell yourself when you wake up in the morning? Or how do you deal with what you`re going through?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Well, my son drew me a picture the other day. We`re at a restaurant and he -- he was just coloring on a kids menu and he drew me a picture that said you`re braver than a lion. And after I was done, like, you know, silently sobbing at that, I-- I realized that every single day I have to be braver than a lion. Not just for me, but for them and I don`t want them to think that their mom is sick. I look at my treatment as something that`s healing, even though sometimes it makes me sick. So I just, you know, every single day, I`m like, I need them to feel that I am just as alive and vibrant as -- as they think I am. And, you know, I need to wake up everyday and be their mom. I think that most of us wake up, who are mom`s with that mentality. So it`s just a little -- little extra push for me these days.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: One of things that you`re good at is finding that silver lining and I loved hearing from you about how you are embracing this season of life, and how, you know, it`s forced you to slow down.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean, we know, Clea that you are amazing. Look behind you, it`s all rainbow organized and you`re a go-getter and you`re used to just being like the type AAAA plus personality. But what has it been like to -- to slow down and -- and embrace a different pace of life?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Well, it`s not normal for me to slow down, and I actually, I -- I think about, you know, before I announced publicly, actually the day before I announced publicly that I had breast cancer. I actually was at the Today offices. I was with Hoda and I confided in Hoda that I had breast cancer and she held my hand Hoda, thank you, and said this is -- you need to slow down and this might be, you know, the way the universe is, kind of, forcing you to slow down a little bit. And I think that that`s exactly right, so this has been a time of learning how to be patient, of learning how to not be in control of things. Of learning how to just be, a little more quiet, to find some solace and peace and calm and not just have to be go, go, go. So it`s a -- it`s a -- it`s a real test for me. This is not normal but I - - I`ve been -- you know, again, I`ve been plugging away and I -- I`m -- I`m going to do it. So I`m -- I`m making it through.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You are doing it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. You definitely are and we just want to celebrate your magazine too. That`s a good thing to celebrate and I -- a little birdie told me that if you leaf through the pages of this edition of your magazine, the second edition. We`ll find someone we know very well and love. You`ve got Ms. Savannah Guthrie in there.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes you will. Savannah`s kitchen, well first of all Savannah herself looking stunning.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 [08:40:10]\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: But Savannah`s kitchen, pantry, you know, our team was so excited to create a hub, you know, for the family and, you know, turn her already gorgeous apartment just that much up.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: How hard was it to organize? How hard was it? Don`t try to make her feel good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: An army.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: No come on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: An army came.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: No, Savannah`s actually pretty well organized already. So it`s - - it`s never hard. You know, this is the second time we`ve -- we`ve organized for Savannah and it`s -- it`s always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: So it took two times. OK. Good.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Savannah has it down.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOTB: She`s making sure we`ve --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The funniest thing that you`re going to appreciate Clea. So I was wearing this dress I`ve had for a lot of years. I think I wore it when I was pregnant. So it`s an old dress and too big for the shoot. So then they`re like, that dress is, you know, dumpy or whatever. You know, we need to tighten it up we`ll just put some chip clips, which you guys had helpfully organized. So I knew just where to find those potato chips Savannah and that`s how we did it.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: Thank you. Thank you. That`s exactly what I wanted to hear.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: That`s the truth of it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, it`s incredible. You guys have your show. You have the magazine. The magazine`s cool because you have a lot of, like, tips and tricks because sometimes I think, I know I`m intimated. I can`t do what you guys do, but you have a lot of tips and tricks in there.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: We do and, you know, in addition to your kitchen we have a lot of other lifestyle things in addition to, kind of, organizing tips and tricks. We have our friend Heather McMahan featured in the issue. She gives, kind of, her guest room dos and don`ts. We have our friend Tan France from "Queer Eye". He gives some fashion advice. So we`re trying to, kind of, run the gambit and -- and feature all sorts of things that could be helpful to people, whether it`s organization, or whether it`s just lifestyle.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well Clea we love you.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: We love you Clea.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You`re an inspiration. Keep going. Keep going. You`re the best.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: By the way, you can preorder the Fall issue of "The Home Edit" feel good organizing magazine starting next Tuesday. By the way, I don`t know who`s apartment that was. It did not look -- the way they took those pictures --\par \par \uc2 HOTB: It`s not yours.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You`ve been there. It doesn`t --\par \par \uc2 HOTB: It`s not.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It looks really good.\par \par \uc2 HOTB: She made you feel good though. All right, coming up next Sterling K. Brown. He`s going full on comedy in this new film. It`s a "mockumentaryyyyyy". We can`t wait to hear all about it but first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220817ei8g0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Honoring An Icon, Tributes Pouring In The World Over For Olivia Newton-John\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Joe Fryer \par \uc2 1,484 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: We turn now to the passing of an icon Olivia Newton-John. She died yesterday at the age of 73. And this morning, family, friends, and her many, many fans around the world are paying tribute to an icon, singer, actress, advocate, she entertained and inspired us for decades. NBC News Now anchor Joe Fryer is here with a look back at her remarkable life. Joe, it`s hard to do this without tears.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS NOW ANCHOR: It really is Savannah. You know in a statement on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , it was her husband who announced that Olivia Newton- John died peacefully at her ranch in Southern California. She was surrounded by friends and family. For years, she had battled breast cancer pushing for more research and never letting her diagnosis interfere with her endless optimism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): In the musical "Grease," Sandy transforms from virtuous cheerleader to stiletto heeled bad girl.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN, SINGER: Tell me about it stud.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): An impressive range that would define Olivia Newton- John`s career. Able to sweetly sing a ballad like "I Honestly Love You," Bella disco tunes in the roller skating fantasy "Xanadu," or turn heads with a sultry makeover in the video for "Physical," billboards number one song of the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:10]\par \par \uc2 Born in England and raised in Australia, her first big break came in the 70s with the song "Let Me Be There." Won a Grammy the first of four in her career, then lightning struck, "Grease" lightning.\par \par \uc2 At 29 years old Newton-John was initially hesitant to play a teenager, the co-star \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  helped convince her they`d go together. In a career that spanned five decades, she sold more than 100 million records worldwide, a legacy that in 2014 brought her to Vegas for a residency, where I asked, what`s her favorite song?\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN: It`s like having a bunch of kids. I don`t think you can pick a favorite. You might have a special one that day that`s behaving really well.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): Her legacy is much more than music. In 1992, she was diagnosed with breast cancer, a disease that returned two more times. She created a Wellness and Research Center in her name, calling herself a cancer thriver.\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN: Cancer can engulf your mind and engulf your being and I try not to live my life with that in my mind all the time. I live beyond it. I love beyond it. I sing beyond it. And I want that for everybody.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): Among the many tributes now pouring in, this from Travolta, you made all of our lives so much better. Your impact was incredible. I love you so much, your Danny, your John. And Olivia`s daughter, Chloe, posted a series of touching photos with her mom taken over the years, family, friends and fans now more than ever, hopelessly devoted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Newton-John`s family called her a symbol of triumphs and hope for sharing her journey with breast cancer for over 30 years. She was a vocal advocate for plant-based treatments and for building a life outside of the disease. So as you heard her say it didn`t engulf your entire being. There was life beyond cancer.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She was an extraordinary person. And one of the things she did so well was handle was so much grace, everyone`s adoration. I mean for a girl of a certain age or a certain era, you know, when we would meet her and see her, it was an overwhelming amount of love and adoration and she was always so kind and humble and gentle. And she`s so fierce as an advocate. And I know so many people are just were heartbroken, we loved her.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: One of our colleagues, Lauren Specter said, she sparkles. I thought that was a lovely way to sum it up.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No question about it. And we`re going to keep talking about her.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes. She spent a lot of time with us over the years here at Today.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She did. She came on the show many, many times. She was so generous to us. We loved her and we`ll talk to a good friend of hers, Jane Seymour, they`ve been friends for 40 years.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Joe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So we`ll going to give lovely Olivia her do coming up.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: She deserves it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 All right, Mr. Roker, you know, I was playing her song.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I know. We were both playing them this morning. And what a multi leveled legacy she leaves, so fantastic. Well, pleasant temperatures will be fantastic around the Great Lakes today. That`ll be good. Another hot one here in the mid-Atlantic northeast, on into New England, we do have heavy rain down through the Ohio River and Mississippi river valleys. We`re going to be talking about that coming up in the next half hour, and a bit of a flood risk out west as we continue to see a lot of moisture making its way up from the south.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on. We`re going to get a look at your local forecast in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that`s your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, Mr. Roker, thank you. Just ahead, you`re going to go one on one with one of Washington`s most influential voices.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , she will join us live, her first live interview since returning from that trip to Taiwan. We`ll talk about the fallout from that, the battles ahead on Capitol Hill, and in the midterms for Democrats. And we`ll get the Speaker`s take on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of the former President`s Florida home, lots to catch up with her about.\par \par \uc2 [07:20:02]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Also we`ve got a story that you really need to see if you`ve got a kid on Snapchat. Kate Snow has a first look at the parental controls that are being rolled out today. It`s the first time this has happened and the hopes that they can create a safer and healthier online experience for all teenagers. But first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Coming up, lots of stars in 1A.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, we`re going to play password with Jimmy Fallon and talk to Mindy Kaling about her hitch.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:20]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:18]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re back 7:30, Tuesday morning, 9th of August 2022. We`re going to head right outside there in a moment and say hello to that happy crowd. They`re sweltering, a little bit, it feels hot and steamy out there. And Sheinelle is with us.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning. Good to have you guys along, we`re going to start with these 7:30 headlines. The father and son convicted of murdering Ahmaud Arbery were both given an additional sentence of life in prison yesterday on federal hate crime charges. Travis and Greg McMichael will serve their sentences in state prison not federal prison, as had been requested by their attorneys. Their neighbor William "Roddie" Bryan was sentenced to 35 years. All three men already are serving life sentences for their convictions in state court.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Their family of Gabby Petito yesterday announced a wrongful death lawsuit against police in Moab, Utah. They accused the Department of failing to properly investigate Petito`s domestic violence case and protect her. The lawsuit seeks $50 million in damages. Petito`s fiance Brian Laundrie admitted killing her in a notebook that was discovered near his body after he took his own life.\par \par \uc2 JONES: David McCullough, a bestselling author and renowned historian has died. He won a Pulitzer Prize in 1993 for his biography on President \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , and another nine years later for a biography on President John Adams. In 2006, McCullough was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the nation`s highest civilian honor. He passed away on Sunday at his home in Massachusetts, surrounded by his five children. David McCullough was 89 years old.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220811ei890005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Today for August 9, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Craig Melvin, Kerry Sanders, Ken Dilanian, Al  Roker, John Torres, Joe Fryer, Sheinelle Jones \par \uc2 6,523 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:19]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Breaking overnight, search and seizure, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  descends on former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home, an unprecedented move, federal agents with a warrant reportedly tied to classified information taken from the White House. Trump from New York claiming Mar-a-Lago was under siege, supporters gathering there in protest. Just ahead, what investigators may have been looking for.\par \par \uc2 One-on-one, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  live in her first interview since that headline making trip to Taiwan, her reaction to China`s dramatic military confrontation since then. And here at home, her take on whether Democrats can hold on to power in the midterms after the President gets his massive domestic legislation passed.\par \par \uc2 Hot and humid, cities extending heat, emergencies up and down the East Coast after another day of record shattering temperatures, Al has got the full forecast, and the good news about relief on the way.\par \par \uc2 Major breakthrough, testing set to begin on a long awaited Lyme disease vaccine, the first in decades. How soon could it be on the market?\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, we`re honoring an icon, tributes pouring in the world over for Olivia Newton-John. We`re going to look back at the actress, singer, and advocate who inspired generations of hopelessly devoted fans, today, Tuesday, August 9th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And good morning, everybody. Welcome to today on a Tuesday morning, so glad to have you along with this. Craig is in for Jota once again. And gosh, before we get to the news just got to say so many people are heartbroken about the passing of Olivia Newton-John, an icon.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Well, and you are one of our biggest fans too. And she spent so much time with us over the years here at Today. We`re going to spend some time this morning looking back at what truly was a remarkable, remarkable life. We`ll do that in just a moment. But first, those stunning developments surrounding former President Trump after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  executed a search warrant at Mar-a-Lago, his home, and club down in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, so here`s a look at the details coming in overnight. The President himself broke the news by issuing a statement confirming the raid and then phoned into a campaign rally for \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  last night. He then alluded to the search again, saying it was another day in paradise and added that this was a strange day.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Meanwhile, protesters quickly gathered outside that home in Florida showing their support for the former president.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But here`s the question, what does it mean for a possible Trump reelection bid in 2024. If he did break the law, would he be barred from future office? What were investigators looking for? And did they find it?\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up, among other things, we`re going to have the first live interview with House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  since her returned from Taiwan. We`ll get her thoughts on this breaking news as well as these foreign policy developments but let`s get started this morning with NBC`s senior national correspondent Kerry Sanders who`s outside Mar-a-Lago for us this morning. Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning, Savannah. You can see there are still some protesters here just beyond there is Mar-a-Lago a senior U.S. official tells NBC News that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  spent much of Monday here at Mar-a-Lago, another official telling NBC News that they were searching for documents that the former president allegedly brought with him after he left office to Mar-a-Lago. Mr. Trump saying that this is all a case of political persecution, but veteran \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents say that it would take a very high bar for a federal judge to sign off on a search warrant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Overnight, supporters gathering outside former President Donald Trump`s private club Mar-a-Lago, Trump in New York departing Trump Tower and ignoring questions from reporters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Trump, can we get your reaction to the rig, sir.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): In his statement issued just before 7 o`clock Monday night, Mr. Trump said Mar-a-Lago was under siege, raided, and occupied by a large group of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents. Claiming it was unannounced and portraying himself as the victim of political persecution, adding the even broke into my safe.\par \par \uc2 According to a source familiar with the search, it was related to classified information, the former president allegedly took with him to Mar-a-Lago after leaving the White House. In February, the National Archives announced that classified material was discovered in 15 boxes