erred all calls to the Department of Justice, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Sanders, we`ve seen some of the Trump supporters are right behind you there in Mar-a-Lago. Let`s bring in Ken Dilanian, NBC`s justice correspondent. Ken, good morning to you. To obtain a search warrant against a former president and in his home is quite an aggressive and extraordinary step. This would have had to go to the highest levels of the Justice Department in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to be approved, would it not?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely, Savannah, not just aggressive. This is unprecedented. And there`s little doubt this move was approved by Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . Look, Justice Department officials aren`t commenting, obviously. But you may recall it in January, the National Archives retrieved 15 boxes of presidential records from Mar-a-Lago, that was with Trump`s permission, because archivist said they found classified material in the boxes, they referred the matter to the DOJ, which began an investigation.\par \par \uc2 Now normally, if an ex-president has documents he shouldn`t have, the government would simply ask for them back. This move suggests something broke down in a big way between Trump in the Archives. And these documents may have contained some very sensitive information. This move doesn`t mean Trump will be charged Savannah, but it`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What would the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have to establish to get a federal judge to authorize a warrant of this nature?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  would have had to convince that federal judge if there was evidence of a crime inside Trump`s Florida home, and that a search was the only way to retrieve it. And multiple people have told us that they were after with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was trying to recover boxes of documents that may have included highly classified information. And then they left there with lots of material, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: How specific do they have to be about exactly what they`re looking for? In other words, can this be some kind of fishing expedition?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: It can`t be a fishing expedition. They have to tell the judge exactly what they`re looking for. And they have to file a document with the court saying what they took. But it`s also true that if they find evidence of other crimes, they`re free to seize that material, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Is there any reason to believe that this warrant and this search has anything to do with something other than the National Archives case, which you just laid out in the 15 boxes that the Archives got and then they refer it to the Justice Department? As we know, there are other investigations, state investigations, for example. Is there any reason to believe this search has anything to do with those other investigations or the January 6th hearings, I should add?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Of course, we`ve asked that question, Savannah. And we`re being told you can`t really rule anything out. We have to be humble about what we don`t know here. There are not only state investigations, there`s a federal grand jury investigation that is getting closer to Trump about what happened around January 6th, and the efforts to overturn the election. You know, Trump`s election lawyer was served a search warrant, a senior Justice Department, a former senior justice former lawyer was served a search warrant, top former White House officials have been called before a grand jury here in Washington in recent weeks, and we`ve reported that that grand jury is asking questions about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So it`s possible, we can`t rule it out that some part of this has to do with that but it`s also possible it doesn`t, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:08]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And very quickly the President has described this as a raid said that Mar-a-Lago was under siege, it was broken into. Do you have any information about how this search was conducted?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Yes, officials I`ve spoken to have pushed back on describing this as a raid. You know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  notified the Secret Service, which guards Mar- a-Lago a few hours before the search, and Trump`s lawyers were also told. He wasn`t there. He was in New York. But it`s also true Savannah that this warrant signed by a judge gave the \uc2 FBI\uc2  the authority to force its way into the house if that`s what agents needed to do. That`s what makes it such a big step. Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Ken Dilanian our justice correspondent, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Another big story this morning, tens of millions continuing to deal with intense heat and humidity, alerts and warnings in effect on both coasts. But a break from that heat is on the horizon. Al Roker is standing by with the first look of the forecast. Good morning to you, buddy.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, well, here`s the good news. OK, so we`ve got this cooler than average air behind this stationary front. It`s not a hardened cold front. But ahead of it, we`ve got that hotter than average warm, humid air coming up. So look at these temperatures, it`s going to feel like 103 in D.C., 100 in New York City, Boston 102, Norfolk 102. One more day of this extreme heat and humidity, look at this buffalo 77 today, Detroit 81, Covington, Kentucky more comfortable 82.\par \par \uc2 Through Friday, the system slowly pushes to the east. By the end of the week, look at these temperatures, New York City, 81 degrees, 84 in Virginia Beach, 77 on Friday, Charleston, Chicago, you`ll be back down to 77. And it`s not just the temperatures because it`s not just the heat, it`s the humidity. High humidity from the Gulf of Mexico has been in here. But today as this front pushes through, they`ll be bringing some severe showers and thunderstorms. But in the meantime, by Thursday, cooler, drier Canadian air, so relief is on the way guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Music to our ears. Thank you very much. Al, we`ll come back to in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Now, though, some encouraging news in the fight against Lyme disease, the most common tick-borne illness. The first potential vaccine in decades is now entering its final phase of testing. And it comes after a recent study showed nearly 15 percent of the world`s population has likely had the condition including hundreds of thousands of Americans every year. NBC`s senior medical correspondent Dr. John Torres is with us in the studio this morning. So Dr. John, let`s just start with how significant is this, this potential vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN TORRES, NBC NEWS SENIOR MEDICAL CORRESPONDENT: This is going to be a very significant breakthrough, once the vaccine gets approved, and they`re looking at getting approval in 2025, so they`re just now entering phase three. And what they`re going to do is they`re going to roll 6,000 people, different areas around the world, including children. And so this is something that`s certainly going to help because we haven`t -- we don`t have a vaccine right now for Lyme disease. And as you mentioned, Lyme disease has essentially exploded across the country over the last few years.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: How many shots are we talking here, just one shot, multiple shots, so it`s been, how long?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: This is a three dose shot regime. It`s three doses over five to six months. And after that, you`ll probably need an annual shot as well. And so it`s going to be, one, it`s a bit more complicated than most vaccines, but certainly will protect against something that can be fairly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And there wasn`t an actual vaccine a couple of decades ago, right?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: There`s a vaccine, it was \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved in 1998. They withdrew it from the market in 2002 because nobody wanted it. They weren`t able to get in anybody`s arms. And so they said you know at the time, there wasn`t that much Lyme disease. And so nobody went after a vaccine until again now we see Lyme disease cropping up and occurring more across the country. So \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  stepped in and said, let`s go ahead and try and do this.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Really quickly, symptoms of Lyme disease once again?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: So essentially starts off like the flu, you get fevers, chills, muscle aches, that target lesion is the telltale symptom, which is basically a rash, it looks like a target, if you see that, you definitely want to get checked.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Dr. John Torres, Dr. John, thank you as always.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We turn now to the passing of an icon Olivia Newton-John. She died yesterday at the age of 73. And this morning, family, friends, and her many, many fans around the world are paying tribute to an icon, singer, actress, advocate, she entertained and inspired us for decades. NBC News Now anchor Joe Fryer is here with a look back at her remarkable life. Joe, it`s hard to do this without tears.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS NOW ANCHOR: It really is Savannah. You know in a statement on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , it was her husband who announced that Olivia Newton- John died peacefully at her ranch in Southern California. She was surrounded by friends and family. For years, she had battled breast cancer pushing for more research and never letting her diagnosis interfere with her endless optimism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): In the musical "Grease," Sandy transforms from virtuous cheerleader to stiletto heeled bad girl.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA NEWTON-JOHN, SINGER: Tell me about it stud.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): An impressive range that would define Olivia Newton- John`s career. Able to sweetly sing a ballad like "I Honestly Love You," Bella disco tunes in the roller skating fantasy "Xanadu," or turn heads with a sultry makeover in the video for "Physical," billboards number one song of the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:10]\par \par \uc2 Born in England and raised in Australia, her first big break came in the 70s with the song "Let Me Be There." Won a Grammy the first of four in her career, then lightning struck, "Grease" lightning.\par \par \uc2 At 29 years old Newton-John was initially hesitant to play a teenager, the co-star \uc2 John Travolta\uc2  helped convince her they`d go together. In a career that spanned five decades, she sold more than 100 million records worldwide, a legacy that in 2014 brought her to Vegas for a residency, where I asked, what`s her favorite song?\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN: It`s like having a bunch of kids. I don`t think you can pick a favorite. You might have a special one that day that`s behaving really well.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): Her legacy is much more than music. In 1992, she was diagnosed with breast cancer, a disease that returned two more times. She created a Wellness and Research Center in her name, calling herself a cancer thriver.\par \par \uc2 NEWTON-JOHN: Cancer can engulf your mind and engulf your being and I try not to live my life with that in my mind all the time. I live beyond it. I love beyond it. I sing beyond it. And I want that for everybody.\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voice-over): Among the many tributes now pouring in, this from Travolta, you made all of our lives so much better. Your impact was incredible. I love you so much, your Danny, your John. And Olivia`s daughter, Chloe, posted a series of touching photos with her mom taken over the years, family, friends and fans now more than ever, hopelessly devoted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Newton-John`s family called her a symbol of triumphs and hope for sharing her journey with breast cancer for over 30 years. She was a vocal advocate for plant-based treatments and for building a life outside of the disease. So as you heard her say it didn`t engulf your entire being. There was life beyond cancer.\par