r \uc2 But nonetheless, we believe in the rule of the law. And that`s what our country is about, and no person is above the law, not even the President of the United States, not even a former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s turn now to your visit to Taiwan. You made history. You were the first speaker to visit in, I guess, more than a generation, 25 years. The visit has resulted in considerable blowbackm and we`ll get to that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But my question to you initially is, why now? You received briefings. You were -- the risks were laid out to you. What was the cost benefit analysis that you conducted? Why was it important to make this trip now?\par \par \uc2 [07:35:15]\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Well, the fact is, we were supposed to make it a couple of months ago, but because of COVID, it was postponed. I`m very proud of the delegation. The Chairman of the Foreign Affairs Committee, Mr. Meeks, the Chairman of Veterans Affairs Committee, Mr. Takano, the Vice Chair of the Ways and Means Committee, Congresswoman DelBene, \uc2 Andy Kim\uc2  of New Jersey, who was a diplomat before coming to Congress, as well as a Congressman \uc2 Raja Krishnamoorthi\uc2 , who is a member of the Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 It was like a six person co-chair committee. A lot of attention was paid to Taiwan. And that`s right. But it was a bigger visit than that. It followed on the President`s direction that we would have a focus on the Asia Pacific. It also took up his initiative of the Indo-Pacific Economic Framework.\par \par \uc2 So we went there to listen. It`s important for us to have parliamentary to parliamentary exchanges, as we again go forward with a new initiative.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Madam Speaker, just to jump in here --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: In Singapore -\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Yes - in Singapore, we had -- in all of the countries, we thanked the members of ASEAN and our Asian friends for their very strong support for the people of Ukraine and condemning Russia for their invasion. And all of the places that we there, we brought our respect -- and to listen to what they had to say about how we go forward, security wise, economic wise, as well as in the terms of governance.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let me just jump in here because --\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: And Taiwan was a part of that trip.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, and it`s only because time is short, I really want to make sure we get into this, because there are reasons to take a trip like that. But as you well know, especially with foreign policy, national security, it often comes down to tradeoffs and timing.\par \par \uc2 Was it worth it now that you see the response? China has taken --\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Absolutely, without any question.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, because China has made a series of stunningly aggressive military moves in Taiwan. I mean, unprecedented in terms of the security of Taiwan. Was it worth it to make this trip now, if that was the response from China?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I have overwhelming bipartisan support for our visit to Taiwan. As speaker of the House, and with the distinguished delegation that I went there with, we were very well received, thousands of people in the streets, received by the President of Taiwan, buildings with big welcoming to us, the people of Taiwan welcomed the visit. The Chinese government may not have. But China will not be allowed to isolate Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 But a few months ago -- a couple of months ago, a delegation went from the Senate, it was bipartisan, it was high powered -- including the chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee, nobody said a word. Nobody said a word.\par \par \uc2 Did you even know about that trip? So, I am not going to -- again, as a supporter of democracy in China, but this is about democracy in Taiwan, we cannot allow the Chinese government to isolate Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 They may say to them, you can`t go to the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 , but they`re not going to say who can go to Taiwan. And yes, it was worth it. And what the Chinese are doing is what they usually do.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Even if it`s on the Chinese, I think the administration is clear that, you know, whatever crisis is upon us now, it is the Chinese who are the instigators of this crisis. That said, was it not foreseeable that they would react this way?\par \par \uc2 And on the issue of timing, this comes at the very moment that the Biden administration is trying to work with China to hold off China from rearming Putin in Ukraine. So, as you well know, there are multiple levels of chess here.\par \par \uc2 So the question is whether a symbolic trip, important as it may be, in any way undermines some of those other also quite important objectives that the administration has?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Well, it may appear to be symbolic to you, but it was very substantial to us, for us to listen to the people in the region about our full agenda, but also not to say, we`re not going to Taiwan, because the Chinese may act up.\par \par \uc2 The President, as this his responsibility, is usually in touch with the Chinese about one thing or another, whether it`s climate -- and I think we have to work with China on the subject of climate --\par \par \uc2 [07:40:03]\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: They have closed dialogue on that since your visit. I guess that`s the point of -- yes.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Well -- what dialogue were you aware of that they were having on climate? They have been shutting down on many things.\par \par \uc2 So, again, this thing of, oh, but they were going to be doing this, and they were going to be doing that -- let`s just say, this is not something that should be dividing us. We are not going to let China isolate Taiwan. And if that -- and, again, if they can ignore a trip of five senators in a bipartisan way, why would they decide on my trip that it would be different? It`s only a matter of months -- and even about a few weeks before we went, a Republican senator went to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 So, again, there`s something wrong with this picture. And I`m used to taking the barbs about men think it`s this or that, but a woman goes -- and it`s a woman president of Taiwan. We are so proud of her. We want to support her courage.\par \par \uc2 And every place I went, we were invited to go.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s talk -\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: So it is a question of what they think is important to them, rather than some -- again, a very strong bipartisan support for the trip.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: But, you know what? Why don`t we just show China that we support Taiwan, it is part of the U.S. -- the Taiwan Relations Act. I didn`t -- we didn`t go there to change our policy. We still support the One China Policy. We go there to acknowledge the status quo, is what our policy is. There was nothing disruptive about that.\par \par \uc2 It was only about saying China is one of the freest societies in the world. Don`t take it from me, that`s from \uc2 Freedom House\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s talk a little bit - yes.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: It`s a strong democracy, courageous people. And it just -- I don`t know why it is -- except there`s some commercial interests who would like to diminish the relationship.\par \par \uc2 But we`re very -- we -- well, tomorrow, we`ll have a press conference, you`ll hear from my other members, about the pride we took in going there, the reception we received, the fact that the Chinese are going to do what they`re going to do. And just because the president of Taiwan is a -- excuse me, the president of China acts like a bully, has his own insecurities, it doesn`t mean that I`m going to have him do my -- the schedule for members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Members of Congress can show their support for countries and democracies who are our friends. And according to the Taiwan Policy Act, Taiwan is our friend.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Speaker Pelosi, we`re really out of time. I don`t want -- the filibuster exists in the House, too, right?\par \par \uc2 Let`s -- let me quickly ask you though about midterm politics, if I could. Which is, you know, clearly the President has just notched a victory. This bill has passed in the Senate, very delicate, I don`t have to tell you about the dance legislatively that took place.\par \par \uc2 Will you ask your members to pass it as is so that this bill can get across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Yes, let`s start with what it does for kitchen table issues to American people -- lower health care costs, lower prescription drug costs. This is historic, that for the first time, \uc2 HHS\uc2 , Secretary of Health and Human Services were able to negotiate for lower prescription drug prices, again, with the subsidies lowering health care costs. It`s remarkable.\par \par \uc2 And then in terms of creating jobs and also saving our planet for future generate -- for now and for future generations -- it`s remarkable. Our members are very pleased. And quite a bit of what is in the bill is what we had worked together, House and Senate, to do.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t have everything we had in the bill, but we`re very pleased with what is in there.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , I`m sorry to cut you off. I wish we had more time, but hopefully we`ll get to have another conversation in the future. Thank you very much. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Thank you. Good morning, thank you\par \par \uc2 JONES: Covered a lot of ground there.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead, a new tool from Snapchat this morning to help parents keep kids safe on social media. Kate Snow has your first look.\par \par \uc2 [07:44:21]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Also, imagine watching the band at your local bar when Carrie Underwood decides to take the stage, apparently just happened. We`ll have that and more right after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Back 7:47. Al has got a quick check of the weather. Mr. Roker, what are we looking at?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, we got some flood watches out west with due to the monsoonal moisture. But we`re also concerned about these flood watches stretching from Kentucky on into Missouri, heavy showers and thunderstorms already starting to fire up. So we are worried about some more flooding unfortunately for our friends in Kentucky, especially with hourly rainfall rates up to three inches from Shreveport all the way to Charleston. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that`s your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, you`re going to love this one. Coming up, the video game legend getting set the tape a chop out of the box office. We`re going to explain on PopStart. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 [07:49:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It sounds just like it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up, we`re going to take a moment and celebrate the remarkable inspiring life of Olivia Newton-John through her many visits with us here Today.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: But first, your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:11]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220809ei89000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for August 7, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Cornell Belcher, Anna Palmer \par \uc2 9,573 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, a political earthquake.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Relief, excitement, joy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That stunning pro-abortion rights vote in red Kansas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: We will not let the government overreach into our personal rights. We just won`t.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: I`m astounded at the turnout.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Beating back a move by conservatives to build on the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: We are in this because we believe very strongly in protecting both women and babies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does vote in in Kansas tell us about what might happen in November`s midterm elections? My guests this morning Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina and Democratic candidate for governor of Georgia, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, Speaker Pelosi`s visit to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Our delegation came here to send an unequivocal message: America stands with Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: China reacts with provocative military exercises near Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: There is no justification for this extreme, disproportionate, and escalatory military response.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I will talk to Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  of the Foreign Relations Committee about growing tensions with China.\par \par \uc2 Also, the Senate`s all-night session with Democrats looking to pass their climate, health, and tax bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: It will reduce inflation. It will lower prescription drug costs. It will fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All against total Republican opposition.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R), FLORIDA: This bill has nothing to do with the real people in the real world are worried about every single day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ll have the latest. Joining me for insight and analysis are: Democratic pollster Cornell Belcher; Anna Palmer, co-founder of Punchbowl News; former North Carolina Governor \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 ; and USA Today Washington bureau chief Susan Page.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press with Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning. It is fair to say there are two conclusions we can draw from Tuesday`s important voting. One is that the history of abortion being more of a motivator for Republicans than for Democrats. Maybe just that. History. When Kansas votes defeated an anti- abortion measure by 18 points, they proved the Democrats` theory of the case that the Supreme Court`s Roe v. Wade decision would energize their voters, particularly in the suburbs. Remember, this was Kansas.\par \par \uc2 The issue sparked a massive turnout in a red state with a clear message: Abortion opponents are overreaching. Nobody saw this kind of landslide result coming. The other lesson is that the GOP remains very much Donald Trump`s party despite a more robust anti-Trump insurgency. Look no further than Arizona where the four Trump-endorsed election-denying candidates all won their Republican nominations for the top four statewide offices. The races may have been closer, but the Trump team won. Are they easier targets for November -- for Democrats to take down in November? Perhaps. Yet it also sets up a potential sweep by some far right candidates who could refuse to certify any victory by any Democrat in 2024 in that state.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with that abortion vote earthquake. It was just the latest data point that tells us this, it is time for rethink everything we thought we knew about this November`s midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: I`m just -- I`m overjoyed.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice-over): After an 18-point defeat of the measure that would have stripped abortion rights from the state`s constitution, Democrats are hoping to take the post-Roe backlash in conservative Kansas nationwide.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Kansas was just the start. If it`s going to happen in Kansas, it`s going to happen in a whole lot of states.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Turnout more than doubled recent midterm primaries in Kansas, approaching presidential levels, on an issue where Republicans have a historically had the enthusiasm advantage.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA OSTMEYER, KANSAS ORGANIZER: I`m astounded at the turnout.\par \par \uc2 PETER NORTHCOTT, KANSANS FOR LIFE: Our movement has endured so much over 50 years. This setback is not going to stop us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kansas Republican Senator Roger Marshall called the results a gut punch.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: It tells us that there were a lot of people interested in the issue in Kansas. There`s no question about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Already this week, Democratic candidates are making it clear on the air waves that they are all in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: He supports a total ban.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Even if I`m right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: You make it illegal and you punish the doctors.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , too dangerous for Arizona.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Millions of women lost their right to choose, Higgins celebrated, because she wants to make abortion illegal.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Abortion will be directly on the ballot in a handful of states, including battleground Michigan where a pending ballot measure would enshrine abortion rights with a constitutional amendment. And Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is trying to make her race against Republican Tudor Dixon a referendum on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Rape, incest, no exceptions except...\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R), MICHIGAN CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: Life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: Is that -- you`re solid there?\par \par \uc2 DIXON: That`s where I am solid.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On Friday in Indiana, Governor \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2  signed a near total ban on abortion, the first state post-Roe to pass sweeping restrictions. The law ends legal abortion completely in Indiana, which has been allowed at up to 22 weeks of pregnancy. But it does include exceptions for the life of the mother, fatal fetal anomalies, and some exceptions for rape and incest, which some Indiana house and senate Republicans tried remove completely.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. RITA FLEMING (D), INDIANA: ... does this apply to a fifth grader who has been a victim of incest?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED: It -- it does.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In the end Indiana Republicans splintered and they were unsuccessful. Looking to the fall, national Republicans are still counting on inflation and the economy to define the electoral map.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), MINORITY LEADER: They don`t have a plan to solve the inflation that they created.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But as Democrats work to turn individual races into referendums on abortion rights, some Republican candidates, worried about their party`s move to the right, are softening their own positions.\par \par \uc2 CHAD HARTMAN, RADIO HOST: No exceptions for rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENSEN (R), MINNESOTA CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: Unless a mother`s life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But now?\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: If I`ve been unclear previously, I want to be clear now. Rape and incest, along with endangering the mother`s mental or physical health are acceptable exceptions.\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R), PENNSYLVANIA CANDIDATE FO GOVERNOR: I don`t give way for exceptions either.\par \par \uc2 The people of Pennsylvania, you decide what abortion looks like. You decide on exceptions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAP)E\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to start to get your reaction to what happened in Kansas. When you look at Kansas and South Carolina, they, in some ways, at least on their presidential vote, mirror each other, 56 percent I think for Trump in one, 55 percent in the other. And here you saw this resounding 59 percent of Kansans reject the idea of essentially getting rid of a constitutional amendment that could have led to rolling back rights to an abortion. Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I think we are seeing it in on both sides of the aisle to the extremities of both sides. On the far left you have folks that want abortion for any reason up until birth. And then on the far right we have states that are trying to ensure that no abortion for any reason, including rape and incest victims and girls, and that`s not OK with the electorate either. And I see it in my own district and my own state, the vast majority of people here are OK with some guardrails, but they don`t want the extremities of either side.\par \par \uc2 And they are seeing states push forward with legislation like in my state of South Carolina, there is legislation that would ban the word abortion on a website or a website server. There are folks that want to ban women from traveling out of state. There are folks like in my state that want to ban abortion for women who are victims of rape, and girls who are victims of incest. And that`s just, you know, both sides have these extremities where the vast majority of people just are not there right now. And there has to be a place for the center on this very emotional issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. You know, everything you describe about sort of the fallout from this, there was a simple way to avoid this fallout and that was keeping Roe. In hindsight, I know you were somebody that thought you wanted to see Roe v. Wade overturned, was that a mistake?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I am staunchly pro-life. But there were issues with Roe, even Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  had issues constitutionally, the right to privacy versus the right to equal protection under the law. You saw \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  40 years ago trying to, you know, look at doing away with it as well. And so I think that there are issues with Roe in terms of the constitutionality of it. And the overturning of it now leaves it to the states, state legislatures in particular, but also Congress can have a role here.\par \par \uc2 But the problem that we have today, Chuck, and I know that you see this every single day, are the political divisions. And because this is such an emotional issue, this is a place where we really should be working together. If you look across the pond, you look at European nations, if you`re even allowed to have one, there are gestational limits. In most countries in Europe, you`re looking at 12 to 15 weeks there.\par \par \uc2 And there are other, you know, exceptions that we should be looking at. We should be ensuring that life of the mother in every instant instance is protected. We should ensure, which is one of the reasons I was one of eight Republicans just a few weeks ago to vote to ensure that women have access to contraceptives, there`s some basic things we can be doing that all of us agree on, the vast majority of people agree on and aren`t fringe- y on the either side of the aisle. But that`s not what we are doing right now.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it`s not. And I think that -- I guess I`m curious, how do you propose, because you bring up the issue that I think is maybe impossible to create which is, if you believe in this, if you view abortion through the eyes of morality, there`s no such thing as moderation. So how do you -- how do you legislate moderation here when neither side is comfortable with that compromise?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I actually don`t think it`s that -- that difficult when you`re looking at it from that perspective, too. If you look at it from Congress -- from our role, I mean, there can be gestational -- reasonable gestational limits for most people. Seventy-five percent of Americans want some guardrails. They support guardrails on abortion whether they`re pro- choice or pro-life. Only 25 percent are really, you know, on the fringes of either side on this particular issue.\par \par \uc2 So if you look at gestational limits, whether it`s 15 weeks or 20 weeks, whether you ensure protection for fetal abnormalities, life of the mother in every instance, or certainly rape and incest victims, there are things in there that we can all agree on as Americans. And particularly in a state, whether it`s South Carolina or any other state, that`s banning abortion altogether or trying to, then women`s access to contraceptives has got to be a guarantee.\par \par \uc2 We have rural counties in South Carolina without a single OB-GYN doctor. How is a woman supposed to get any kind of care, health care, medical care, pre-natal care, care for her infant when those resources don`t exist? And so you`ve got to look at it, I think, from a very, I think, centrist perspective which is where 75 percent of America is. Put in guardrails that they are comfortable with and let`s move forward. And we can do this at the federal level and give some guidance to states or states can do it as well. But we`re seeing the extremities and we`re going to continue to until Congress steps in, see extremities on both sides, which is not where the American are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems like you think Congress has step in here. You brought up the OB-GYN issue. Fifteen states right now want to criminalize the doctor, OK, want to make the -- basically if there is a crime committed here, it`s not the woman, it would be the doctor. As you know, you already talked about the problem of getting OB-GYN into practicing in rural South Carolina. A lot of these OB-GYNs don`t want to practice in states with these restrictive laws because they don`t know -- you know, they have to sit here and wait to declare an emergency to intervene medically. And so they figure, you know what, I`m going to practice medicine in a state that makes the abortion law safe.\par \par \uc2 How do you do this? How do you fix this federally?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It is going to be -- it is going to be an issue. I do think that Congress, because Roe v. Wade was overturned, that we do have an opportunity here to work together to ensure that those things don`t take place. And you can do that at the federal level with legislation. But in this particular issue, it`s going to take Republicans and Democrats working together to get it in a place where you can do it in a bicameral fashion, in a bipartisan fashion, and pass legislation.\par \par \uc2 And I think you can look at gestational limits that are reasonable for most Americans. But also you`ve got -- and one of my concerns is you`ve got states that are going to try to ban women from traveling. That don`t -- that if you`re raped that you`ve got to report it to the police. Well, I was raped when I was 16. And it took me a week to tell my mother. By that time, any evidence would have been gone. And the violation of a woman`s privacy, I can`t tell you how traumatic that event was in my life. In my own home state, they want women to be required and mandated to report when they are raped. And I just can`t even imagine a world where you`re a girl, a teenage girl who has been raped to have to report those things.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, "Handmaid`s Tale" was not supposed to be a road map, right? This is a place where we can be in the center. We can protect life. And we can protect where people are on both sides of the aisle. And so it`s important to take that perspective and that is the perspective that Congress should have on everything that we do, but as you know, we`re just so divided.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bottom line, do you think it is bad if the Republican Party becomes the party of abortion bans?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I am staunchly pro life. I have a 100 percent pro-life voting record. I do think that it will be an issue in November if we are not moderating ourselves, that we are including exceptions for women who have been raped, for girls who are victims of incest, and certainly in every instance where the life of a mother is at stake. And that is a position that I have staked out that`s really important to me personally. But that`s where the vast majority of women are in my state. And I`m going to continue to fight for those things.\par \par \uc2 But we can`t go to the far corners of the right or the far corners of the left because the left wants abortion for any reason up until the birth of a child, which is insane to me and insane to most of America. So somewhere in the middle is where we have got to meet. And I do believe that Congress has a role. And I want to play a part in that role in shaping policy for the future for every American in our country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman Nancy Mace, Republican from South Carolina, her district basically represents a large part of Charleston, South Carolina. Congresswoman, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who is the Democratic nominee for governor Georgia. She`s in a rematch with \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , who defeated her four years ago very narrowly.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: It`s my pleasure to be here. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, let`s start this -- lead conversation is about the impact of what we saw in Kansas and, obviously, post-Dobbs. How front and center do you believe the abortion debate should be for Democrats all around the country, let alone your campaign?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: It is an essential conversation. It is about the freedom of women to direct their lives. It is about health care freedom. And it is cutting across demographics. It`s cutting across ideology. It is cutting across the questions that so often dominate elections at this moment. Because for women this is a health care conversation. It is an economic conversation. It is also just a conversation about whether our citizenship is equal to men in this country, depending on which state you live in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, you even seem to be arguing, it`s like, look, the ups and downs of the economy, you know, that may -- that may motivate you on one hand but, hey, these laws are longer term. I mean, do you even think abortion -- in your mind, abortion right -- the abortion issue almost bigger than the economy long term?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I would say that the economy -- the economic vicissitudes are going to continue. And I would never underplay how important it is for us to think about putting, you know, money in people`s pockets and putting a roof over their heads. But we know for women these abortion rights fights are about their ability to control their lives, to have the ability to participate in the economy, to have the ability to determine whether forced pregnancy is in their future. And so, yes, I would urge every voter to think about the economy is going to go up and down but these laws, when they become solidified, change the future for every women that you know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get some clarity on your position specifically, because you`ve said this. I want to put up what you said very recently on your abortion position. You said this: "My intention is going to be to pass legislation that says a woman has the right to an abortion and that right continues until a physician determines the fetus is viable outside of the body, except in the case of protecting the women`s life or health."\par \par \uc2 How do you put legislation around that?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: You put legislation around that by returning to what we know, which is the structural framework of Roe v. Wade. You do it by not setting arbitrary gestational limits because we know that those rarely actually reflect medical decision-making. This is a medical decision. And when we give it back to doctors, when we tell women and their doctors to make these choices, what we are saying is we respect the responsibility that women have and the obligation that doctors have. That politicians are not scientists and that we should not be setting into law these moving targets that do not reflect the reality that women face when they are sitting in that doctor`s office.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, the -- the person on the right who wants to see some restrictions on abortion is going to argue that even though you`re not saying you`re for abortion all the way up to the end of that, essentially that law would allow abortion all the way up to the end of a pregnancy. Would you want to have legislation to put guardrails on that?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: The guardrails are holding the physician accountable. And that`s the place where I think we`ve gotten a bit far afield. There is no woman who at eight months has not cherished the notion of this pregnancy. If you have conversations with women, you know that that is one of the most difficult moments. And the reality is that to put an arbitrary date in a law devalues the pain and the tragedy of that moment. A doctor is not going to take an action that is not medically required. And what we are saying is that this is a medical decision.\par \par \uc2 I understand that there are those who would feel better if we picked, plucked a date out of the air, but we know that that is not scientifically supportable. But we also know that the tragedy of exceptions, you cannot write every exception into the law. And that is where we find ourselves today in Georgia. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  does not permit these exceptions to be real. And in a state where we have 82 counties without an OB-GYN, 64 counties without a surgeon, 18 counties without a family doctor, and nine without a doctor at all, setting arbitrary limits that are not grounded in the reality of what women face is deeply problematic and, ultimately, cruel.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So this is where -- I mean, you`re having a science conversation and a medical conversation, which is the conversation most women are having. There is also the political reality. If you get elected governor, you`re going to have probably a Republican legislature. So in order to -- you may have to compromise in order to get a better law that won`t be what you want. Are you willing to do that? Are you willing to essentially go from what it is now to 15 weeks if that`s what you`re able to do as governor because that`s the legislature -- you sort of -- you write your laws with the legislature that you have, not the one that you want.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Where we stood in Georgia prior to \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  was a 20-week ban that I also found deeply objectionable. But what \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has done is go so far afield that 72 percent of women -- 72 percent of Georgians disagree what we have right now which is almost a total ban. It -- when you say six-week ban, it`s about 54 percent, but when understand that it is roughly a total ban on abortion, we know that regardless of your political posture, you disagree with where we are.\par \par \uc2 As someone who served in the legislature for 11 years and was lauded by both parties for my ability to navigate, I will certainly get the best law we can but we begin making decisions based on what it should be. And then, yes, you work towards what you can get. But we have to start with a governor who actually believes that a woman should have the right to control her body and to control her economic freedom.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- what I`m just trying to get at here is you`re not going to be rigid to the point of if you don`t get it your way, you`re not going to pass -- you`re not going to change the law?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: That`s not how politics has ever worked, unless, of course, you`re \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  who believes that your extreme agenda should trump everyone else`s decision-making and everyone else`s needs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you quickly about your race. There is a pattern is developing if you just look at the polling. Raphael Warnock seems to have a steady lead on \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in all those same polls seems to have a narrow lead on you. It does appear as if he has got a little bit of -- acquired some Teflon by taking on Trump. Is that making it a little bit harder to defeat him this year? Is that it`s harder to make him a Trump ally?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is doing and excellent job of showing that he is a extremist who has a hard right agenda not in the best interest of Georgia. We just lost Music Midtown, $50 million coming out of our economy because \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  weekend gun laws in Georgia at a time where guns are the number one killer of our children and gun violence we are the number nine state for gun violence. What he has benefited from is having, yes, a primary that was fairly lackluster in the end. But what people are realizing and why he remains below 50 percent is that Georgians realize we do not have to make a choice between a strong economy and an extremist agenda.\par \par \uc2 I`m the only candidate who is proposing a comprehensive plan for how we build our economy and protect our freedoms. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  refuses to do so. And so I believe that this election, as we move into Labor Day, that people are going to continue to pay attention to his extreme position on abortion, his extreme position on guns, his extreme position on refusing to take action to help the people of Georgia when we are suffering from a health care crisis and a housing crisis, and that the more they pay attention, the more they will realize that his high water mark was on that primary day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , in a race we will all be watching very closely from here. Thanks for coming on. Stay safe on the trail.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Thank you so much, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we should let folks know we invited Governor Kemp on, we did not receive a response.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Speaker Pelosi`s visit to Taiwan sparks growing tensions between the United States and China. When we come back, I`m going to talk to Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  of Maryland about that and the all-nighter that they pulled in the Senate. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:25:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. China is choosing to react with fury over Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s visit to Taiwan. The Chinese military has been carrying out some days of threatening exercises on all sides of Taiwan, which China, of course, considers part of its territory. In addition, China has cut off talks with the United States on all sorts of issues, including illegal immigration, drug-trafficking, climate, and even our mid-level military back and forth. And the government sanctioned Pelosi and her family.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now to dive into this a little bit is Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  of Maryland. He is on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2 , welcome back to "Meet the Press." I`ll be honest, looks like you guys have had an all-nighter.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD), FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE: Chuck, we have been here all night and we are still going on the Inflation Reduction Act. Republicans are offering amendment after amendment to try to sabotage the bill but I`m confident we`ll get it over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you -- let me ask you a couple of questions about the bill. The biggest thing that happened was right before the bill went to a vote, and that was the parliamentarian`s decision on the prescription drug benefit. Here`s the bottom line: The good news for Medicare patients is that this bill should lower prescription drug costs if you`re on Medicare. But now that there is a no longer a part of this bill that will effectively protect those not on Medicare, should folks who aren`t on Medicare expect to see the prescription drug companies decide, well, we can`t make money over here so we`re going to raise prices over there? It certainly -- it certainly seems like that`s more likely to happen than not.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, Chuck, first of all, as you said, the centerpiece of this bill was finally giving Medicare the power to negotiate drug prices. And that will reduce drug prices for seniors, in addition to the fact that we have a cap of $2,000 on out-of-pocket costs.\par \par \uc2 Look, I wish we had gotten this other provision in. If we had 10 Republicans, we could get it done. We are going to have to come back to that. And we will be watching the pharmaceutical industry like a hawk. And, hopefully, if they made that kind of move, we would have enough votes to respond. But I just want to stress the fact that since I`ve been here in the Congress and, including my time in the House, we have been trying to get Medicare the power to negotiate drug prices. It looks like we are finally there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But getting them there, it doesn`t impact for the rest -- what can be done? I mean, do you think you`re going to have to pass a law to do this or are you just hoping the pharmaceutical industry, out of the goodness of their heart, doesn`t raise prices for the rest of us?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me first say, we may have a vote later this morning on a $30 monthly cap on insulin. And so Republicans will have a chance to join us in that. According to the parliamentarian, we`re going to need their votes to get that done. So that will be some test.\par \par \uc2 But, look, we are going to keep at this. We want to make sure that we lower drug prices for every American. And it is an important start to get this in place for the Medicare program and tens of millions of seniors around the country, including a million in Maryland, who get Medicare.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Look, I invited you on because I wanted to talk some -- of what happened in Taiwan and what is happening right now to Taiwan. Let me just state this, Speaker Pelosi`s trip, again, the Chinese chose to be outraged. This is on them, not on her. But was her trip worth it, given all of the fallout?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I think it was, Chuck, because we need to be very clear that China doesn`t get to dictate which U.S. officials go to Taiwan and when they go to Taiwan. As you know, we send congressional delegations frequently. A former speaker of the house has been to Taiwan in the past. And what President Xi decided to do was manufacture a crisis over Pelosi`s visit.\par \par \uc2 He has got, as you know, this fall the communist party congress is coming up. He is seeking an unprecedented third term as leader of China. And this is saber-rattling and, you know, chest-thumping by President Xi.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, it does look like that`s what it is. But and so I understand in the moment we are trying to de-escalate. But what do you want to see the United States -- what do you want to see the Biden administration do in the next, say, three to six months to reassure the Vietnams, the Japans, the Philippines who are nervous about what China is doing here? They`re nervous if we`re going to have Taiwan`s back. Do we have to send a message again that we`re going to have their back?\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, I think we`ve been very clear, and the Biden Administration has been clear, that we are adhering to U.S. policy, which is that we recognize our One China Policy, but we`ve also been firm and straight from the very start that we will oppose any effort to reunify Taiwan by force, by violence. And we`ve reassured our Japanese partners and others of that.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Chuck, I think this Chinese action of putting missiles into Japan`s economic zone, exclusive economic zone, is going to backfire. I think it`s going to cause Japan to increase its defense spending and seek even closer military engagement and cooperation with the United States, which is already good.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is going to backfire on China. Yes, we have to deescalate. We also have to be firm in making our policy clear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think it`s inevitable that China`s going to make a run at Taiwan in the next five years?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I don`t think it`s inevitable. We need to help Taiwan make that as difficult as possible for China. And we`ve been doing that by supplying China -- excuse me, supplying Taiwan with military assistance. We need to continue to do that to make Taiwan into the porcupine so that when, you know, China looks at Taiwan, it realizes that this is going to be a hell of a fight and not a winnable fight.\par \par \uc2 And I do think when they look to what Putin tried to do in Ukraine they will see how a determined people can thwart someone like a dictator like Putin, who, of course, thought he was going to walk into Kyiv in a matter of weeks. And that was not possible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, for a long time, no U.S. official wanted to describe our relationship with China in strictly adversarial terms, but how do we not look at the cutting off of military ties, climate ties, immigration, to all these things, and not say we`re in a cold war with China?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, I don`t think we`re in a cold war with China yet. A lot of that depends on what China does next. And there`s no doubt that President Xi has escalated, you know, China`s actions against Taiwan, even before the Pelosi visit, and has been more aggressive throughout the region.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, we have been trying to cooperate with them on these other measures, as you say. I believe they`ll be back at the table on those measures. It is important that we have that military-to-military dialogue. But it is important that we also confront and check China, where it`s tried to export its model of authoritarianism around the world in Africa, and the Middle East and other places.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , it`s been a long night. You were very flexible for us, I know, with the back and forth. You`ve got to get back to voting. I appreciate you spending a few minutes with us and our viewers here to get the latest on this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Thank you. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, that abortion vote in Kansas. Just one reason why everything we thought we knew about the November midterms may not be true after all. Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:16]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here.\par \par \uc2 Anna Palmer, co-founder of Punchbowl News; Democratic pollster Cornell Belcher; the former governor of North Carolina \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2  and the bureau chief here in Washington for "USA Today", Susan Page.\par \par \uc2 Susan, you`re a Kansas native.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : I am.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you even said you didn`t leave the state until you went to college.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It was a shock.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you know this. Speaking of shocks, Kansas native Susan Page, how shocked are you?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I was shocked. I was surprised. Maybe not necessarily that the amendment would go down, but the margin of the victory was, I thought, quite stunning.\par \par \uc2 And it was a sign. You know, Kansas is a conservative state for sure. It`s a state that is very reliably Republican when you look at the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also a state with a populist history and an independent streak. Just consider this: of the last ten elected governors in Kansas, five of them have been Democrats, including the current one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s an amazing stat. You shared it earlier. That is one of those that people are going to go, oh, maybe I didn`t understand Kansas the way I should have.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yeah. It`s a pragmatic state. It is a conservative state. I`ll tell you: this was a shock to people I talked to in my home state on both sides of this issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Eisenhower, \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . There is a similarity there.\par \par \uc2 Pat, your reaction. How shocked were you?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOV. PAT MCCRORY (R), NORTH CAROLINA: Maybe this proves that the Supreme Court was right in returning the decision back to --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Really?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: -- the states and clarifying exactly - I`m a constitutional conservative who believed in overturning Roe versus Wade and letting the states make the decisions. Kansas made a decision, and it probably represents what the majority of Kansas people believe. And that`s what the Supreme Court majority said should happen.\par \par \uc2 Now, the dilemma is this, and this is part of the game, every Republican and Democrat who just got through a primary had filled out surveys either by Planned Parenthood or by a pro-life group which they put their absolute answers on, because I have had to fill out these surveys where, are you for or against in absolute measures? And every person who`s running for office has filled out one of those surveys.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of a sudden, you even see \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  kind of clarifying her remarks. And I guarantee you there`s a survey somewhere out there where she did absolute in order to win a primary to begin with.\par \par \uc2 And this is the dilemma for all the politicians on the right and the left. The people are not on the absolute side. And you heard that from the congresswoman from South Carolina, too. The people are in favor of some restrictions, but reasonable restrictions. And that`s going to be the interesting debate at the state and possibly federal level.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans on my side are a little hypocritical right now because they said, return it to the states. Then a day later, they said, let`s have federal legislation. I think they`re going to be backing off.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. I wonder if \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  regrets that remark. I don`t know, Anna.\par \par \uc2 ANNA PALMER, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah. I mean, I think a lot of senators don`t want this issue, but they feel like they`re getting pressure now to have these kind of rules that are, and kind of bills that are going to be mandates. If you`re Democrats, this is the gift that keeps on giving.\par \par \uc2 The culture wars have shifted on abortion in a way that -- I mean, I remember getting into reporting almost 20 years ago and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  trying to have constitutional amendments and different state amendments so that they could try to turn out and gin up voters. Democrats are going to use it this time all the way to the election.\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: You think?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, Cornell, you`re the party of the -- I mean, the party of the cultural -\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Oh my gosh. Wearing the white suit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- the party of the cultural issues now. So, I mean, if Stacy Abrams wins, she`s going to win on guns and abortion.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, so here`s the thing. I love that the governor`s arguing process because he knows if you`re arguing process in politics, it`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 Look, Chuck, you and I have talked about this conversation. I`ve been on this show and I`ve said the moment that suburban moms -- you know, they`re voting on abortion on the right.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 But the moment that suburban moms think that their right to choose is going to go away, it`s going to become a voting issue and this could change the dynamics of the -- change the dynamics. And that`s what you`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 It`s not only the turnout you saw in Kansas. Right now, we`re getting poll after poll that shows motivation up. Look, six months ago, I thought there were a lot of signs along the road here that looked like 2010. For someone who sat in 2010, a part of the Obama team, it looked like 2010 going in. It doesn`t look like that now.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the enthusiasm for younger voters, you look at the enthusiasm for women voters. Look, in July of 2010, African Americans -- 29 percent of African Americans said that election was more important than the past. As of last week, 60 percent of African Americans say that this election is more important than past elections.\par \par \uc2 You have, they have given us a -- they have awoken a sleeping giant with this issue.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: You may have peaked too early. I think we`re going to go back to the economy and immigration.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look. I -- it is interesting. Corry Bliss, who I really respect a lot on the right side as a Republican strategist, he`s convinced of your theory, that, no, no, no, no, no. This will revert to the norm.\par \par \uc2 This feels different, Susan.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know why it`s not going to fade away and go back to the economy? Economy will be important; no question about that. But people are going to be reminded every day about this issue because states are taking action --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look at Indiana.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: -- including what was in Indiana this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And also, these stories that we see about the repercussions of the Supreme Court decision are going to be on the front pages of newspapers. Who does not know the story of the ten-year-old girl raped in Ohio, forced to go to Indiana to get an abortion? That is -- that is going to keep this issue front and center.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Unintended consequences are going to keep trickling, trickling out. This will not go away. The economy`s a top issue every election cycle. Every election cycle, it`s the economy. But freedom is on the ballot now, and it`s becoming not a referendum -\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: For the Republicans, too, in statehouses. The Senate and Houses - - both the Senate and House seats in state legislatures are gerrymandered, unlike the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . And therefore, they`re worried about the primaries. But now that the primaries are over, I do not think you`ll see a lot of movement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Anna, I get a sense that in this issue too many Republican elected officials thought that their pro-life constituents were more mainstream than they actually were.\par \par \uc2 Because Roger Marshall`s reaction to this, the Kansas senator, he`s like, I can`t believe this. It`s like, wow, you didn`t believe your own polling? Like, if you had polled this, you would have known where Kansas voters were.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Right. I mean, I think you have these politicians that have these kind of more extreme positions and have really staked out those claims. Plus, you have these outside groups for 50 years who have been pushing this issue. Finally got what they wanted, Roe versus Wade, and now they`re moving the whole ball into a whole other game.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Can I just--\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is pro-life now? Define what a pro-life -and I say this, like if you -- I heard Nancy Mace say, I`m pro-life. Well, some pro-lifers say, if you want the exceptions she wants, you`re not pro-life.\par \par \uc2 What is the definition anymore?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: I think, I think both sides are -- "What is pro-choice?" is going to be another question. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  was giving you a transition from pro- choice. I think what you`re going to see is some reasonable guidelines of what abortion is.\par \par \uc2 But be careful, Democrats, on this issue. Don`t overplay your cards, because on surveys, people are still more conservative on the abortion issue, but they aren`t willing to go to the extreme.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Can I get one quick thing -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Last thing.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: One quick thing. When you win with 60 percent in Kansas, it`s not just Democrats. And what you see in polling is that some Republican women, they may not be pro-choice but they don`t like the idea of the government taking away their choices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do they become single-issue voters though?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think they do.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: That`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay. Right. And that`s what we`re going to find out. I think that will tell us a lot in November.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Or stay home.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Before we go to break, we want to -- we want to let you know about something you need to be subscribing to right now. It`s our excellent newsletter, our daily political newsletter for "Meet the Press` called First Read and the NBC News Political Unit.\par \par \uc2 Every morning, we bring you the newest polling data, the freshest reporting from the campaign trail, and of course, the best analysis on the top political stories of the day. Sign up. Scan the QR code on your screen now or go to NBCNews.com/FirstRead. Okay, my promotion`s over.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the good news President Biden just got at the end of perhaps his best week yet in the White House. And it was all while in COVID quarantine. He`s out of that, too.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 And what else are we going to be doing but Kansas? Some people like to think of that state as ruby red, rural. But in terms of racial diversity and education it`s actually a state that looks a lot more like the rest of the country than one might think. And the results of this abortion amendment this week could give us some clues about November.\par \par \uc2 Look, let me -- overall, the "no" vote over-performed Joe Biden`s totals in Kansas across the board, 59 percent. And you can see, I`ll show you here, it was across the board no matter what kind of county.\par \par \uc2 Big blue county, Johnson County, Kansas, that`s right outside of Kansas City, this was a county that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carried with 53 percent. Look what the "no" vote did here, 69 percent, an overperformance of 17 points from the Biden vote.\par \par \uc2 But that actually was duplicated across the state. In fact, in some rural counties even better than that. Here`s Russell County, Kansas, home of \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 , Mr. Republican. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  of course only got 17 percent in Bob Dole`s home county. But look what the "no" vote got here: 45 percent. All over rural Kansas the "no" vote performed somewhere in the thirties or higher. It is one of the explanations for why this won so big.\par \par \uc2 The other thing we saw here was a spike in voter turnout and new voters showing up. Look at this. New voter registrations since the Dobbs decision in Kansas spiked here. You see that? Over 8,000 new voter registrations, 68 percent of them women.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado and Michigan, two other states that we expect to see abortion referendum on the ballot, we`re seeing a similar spike in new registrations favoring women, but not like Kansas. Could this shift even more? This is something to watch, trust me.\par \par \uc2 When we come back \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  takes on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICK CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: He`s a coward. A real man wouldn`t lie to his supporters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s from a new campaign ad the former vice president just made for his daughter Liz. And he had a lot more than that to say about Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Biden got some good news just this morning. He`s tested negative for COVID, and he`s out of isolation and on his way to Rehoboth Beach, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 This comes at the end of what may have been Joe Biden`s best week as president: the killing of Al-Qaeda`s leader, Ayman al-Zawahiri, the abortion vote in Kansas, a huge jobs report on Friday, falling gas prices, and the all-night vote on the Democrats` climate, energy, and health care bill looks like it`s going to go through. Pretty good for a guy with approval ratings in the thirties.\par \par \uc2 Cornell, look, I do think that this midterm has separated out presidential approval from where we are. I think Donald Trump`s had a hand in that. But if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can`t see his approval ratings rise after a week like this well -- you`ve got to ask yourself, is this something he can ever pull out of?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, yes. I mean, we`ve seen it before. But look, the arc -- the narrative arc of a presidency is different from the narrative arc of someone running for Congress, right? They have a four-year arc here.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know, in 2010 Obama`s numbers weren`t great either, right? But he had a story to tell. And, I think going in after the midterms I think Biden`s going to have a really good story to tell about sort of tackling inflation, strong economy, major investments in clean energy, something our base really wanted, you know, supporting women`s choice. I think he`s going to have a pretty strong record to stand on after the midterms. I think the arc of his narrative is going to start going up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Anna, his legislative record is looking pretty good. Who gets the credit for it? Is this a Schumer/Pelosi show? Or is this, you know, Biden`s White House deserves more credit than they`re getting?\par \par \uc2 PALMER: I mean, all of the things you`ve just mentioned the common denominator is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wasn`t involved or leading the charge on any of them publicly. I mean he - on the Hill I think Democrats want to be as far away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right now as they can be.\par \par \uc2 This is going to go all to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the fact that he`s kind of been an untested leader and he has gotten a lot of things done here. Everybody would have thought that this was going to be a much smaller package when it came to this reconciliation bill. They come together with this kind of sneak attack, outwits McConnell.\par \par \uc2 And they get this much larger package through, giving Democrats something to run on.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:01]\par \par \uc2 I mean, Democrats have been kind of dead man walking on Capitol Hill looking towards the midterm election. And now I think they feel like there is some momentum there.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, the weirdness is that presidential approval ratings traditional matter a lot in midterms, especially if they`re as low as they are for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But you have this unprecedented situation where you`ve got two presidents who are big factors in this midterm. You`ve got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and you`ve got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And maybe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is more of a negative for Republicans, more of a positive for Democrats than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is for either one.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: That`s smart.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, I`ve heard that --\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve heard this, Pat, that basically hating Trump is better for the Democrat base than hating Biden is for the Republican base --\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Well, consultants like Cornell are going to want Biden to go back in the basement. That`s how he became president. And he had his best week.\par \par \uc2 You`re right, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  overshadowed him without his permission. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  did the agreement with \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  without him. Actually the best thing was -- for the Democrats, I hate to say it, was Jon Stewart.\par \par \uc2 Jon Stewart put us Republicans regarding veterans on the defensive. And one lesson to both the woke Democrats and the conservative Republicans is don`t pick a fight with a comedian like Jon Stewart or Bill Maher or David Chappelle. They will win. They are quicker than us politicians.\par \par \uc2 And I think right now the only guy on the offensive was the governor of Florida, DeSantis. And yet Trump will not let him share any spotlight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, speaking of Trump, I can`t let the show go without - I mean, this ad I think is going to be remarkable regardless of what happens to \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and that is this \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  direct-to-camera rebuke of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: In our nation`s 246-year-history there has never been an individual who is a greater threat to our republic than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He tried to steal the last election using lies and violence to keep himself in power after the voters had rejected him. He`s a coward. A real man wouldn`t lie to his supporters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Susan, this feels like a declaration more than it feels like some sort of ad campaign for a House race. This feels like a declaration. Team Cheney is coming after Trump wherever he is regardless of what happens --\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Pretty personal --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: But remember when \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  was the GOP? You know, \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 , it`s been 14 years since he left the White House. It is no longer the George Bush, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 /Dick Cheney GOP.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not even close.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It is Donald Trump`s Republican Party. And we see that in the primaries. We`re going to see that I suspect in the Wyoming primary next week.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Was it a Cheney ad? I thought it was the first Biden reelection ad.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it is, been - you know, one thing Anna, the anti-Trump forces in the Republican Party got closer. You know, it was one of these things, like you look at Arizona, you look at these places: Trump won, but it was harder but he`s still winning. And sort of what I -- and I don`t know what the anti-Trump forces do.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Yeah, I agree with you. When you look at it he`s not as strong, right? The tail is not in the same place that it was two, four years ago.\par \par \uc2 But to your point, those are the people that are still on the ballot in the midterms. And he`s not going anywhere. Look at CPAC, right? That is Trump`s Republican Party, and it is on display.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think you`ll ever be invited to CPAC again, what CPAC is today?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Who me?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: No, I never was. I do think the big question for the Senate candidates out there is do you accept a Trump invitation?\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a fascinating decision.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: And by the way, the Democrats have certainly decided they don`t want Biden anywhere around.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think that depends.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: I do, too. I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching. I`m sorry to see Juan Soto go. I know I was hoping he`d stay last week. But go \uc2 Padres\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220807ei8700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The New Law In Indiana Just Passed A Near Total Ban On Abortions The First State To Enact Such A Law Since Roe Was Overturned\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Monica Alba \par \uc2 476 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART: Indiana is now the first state to push through new abortion restrictions since the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade. Monica Alba now on what that means for residents and providers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The motion to concur is placed on its passage and machinists open.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The Republican run state legislature advanced the measure late Friday, despite some GOP lawmakers opposing it for going too far. Republican governor \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2  signing it within minutes of its passage. The sweeping new restrictions go into effect September 15. And include exceptions for when the mother faces risk of death or for victims of rape and incest. Currently, women can get abortions and Indiana up to 22 weeks of pregnancy. Under the bill, the procedure may only be performed in hospitals or affiliated outpatient centers.\par \par \uc2 REP. WENDY MCNAMARA, (R) INDIANA: This bill provides protections for the unborn. Most importantly, it exemplifies the value of human life.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAUREEN BAUER, (D) INDIANA: People are demanding that their voices be heard by this legislature to demand equal rights under the law, to have the same control over their body that is guaranteed to any man in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: The White House calling the ban "devastating" and a "radical step by Republican legislators to take away women`s reproductive rights," urging Congress to pass a law, "restoring the protections of Roe, the only way to secure a woman`s right to choose nationally."\par \par \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , one of Indiana`s biggest employers saying the law will force the U.S. drug maker to look to grow its business elsewhere. It comes just days after Kansas overwhelmingly rejected an amendment that would have removed abortion protections from the state constitution. The outcome seen as a bellwether nationwide for voter opposition to stricter abortion laws, poised to be a key issue in the November midterms. Monica Alba, NBC News, the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: President Biden got some good news today about his health, he tested negative for COVID for the first time in a week, but it is doctor says that out of an abundance of caution, the President will remain in isolation at the White House until he gets a second negative test.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220809ei860008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Democrats Are Hoping To Come Together In Washington For A Test Vote On That Massive Economic Plan Aimed At Reducing Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 1,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voiceover): Good morning, breaking overnight. Indiana outlaws abortion. The state becoming the first in the nation to approve a new near-total ban on abortion since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Why even some Republicans opposed it and what it mean for women and abortion providers in the state?\par \par \uc2 Resounding Rebuke. A Texas jury ordered conspiracy theorist Alex Jones to pay for his lies, more than $45 million to the parents of the Sandy Hook victim, the money on top of another $4 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS, PARENT OF SANDY HOOK VICTIM: Other parents, I wanted to represent you well. I know that you have endured pain, just like I have over the past 10 years. And this is a huge victory for all of you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Jones, apologetic in court, but later defiance on the air. We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Easing Recession Fears? The White House celebrating some hopeful economic news with the U.S. adding more than a half million jobs in July doubling expectations. The economy now gaining back all 22 million jobs lost since the pandemic began. But it comes as the Biden administration faces major foreign policy challenges with Russia and China. We`re live at the White House.\par \par \uc2 All that plus the hunt is on. Hundreds of people stocking through the Florida Everglades this weekend with a shared goal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s nine o`clock. They`re starting to come out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: To catch as many great Burmese pythons as possible.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: What`s your advice to the novices?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Be careful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Be careful.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Why so many people are sussing out snakes? Today, Saturday, August 6, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to TODAY. Thanks for joining us on this Saturday morning. It turns out that snowbirds aren`t the only ones converging on Florida these days. They are overwhelmed by this awful invasive species, the great Burmese pythons sometimes 20 feet in length.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I can barely look at the video. Thank goodness for Kerry Sanders for going down there to get the story for us. I take a hard pass on that, by the way.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. I think you`re wise to do that. We`ll have the latest on the Florida Python Challenge coming up in a bit. But this morning, we do have some breaking news from overnight and that is what happened in Indiana lawmakers. They`re approving a near-total ban on abortions overnight, becoming the first state in the country to enact a new law since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. The Republican governor there, \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2 , signed the bill into law within an hour of the state`s Republican- controlled legislature passing it. Even some Republican lawmakers say the law that goes into effect next month goes too far.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s right. It includes almost no exceptions, permitting abortions only in cases of rape and incest before 10 weeks to protect the life of the mother or if the fetus has a "lethal anomaly."\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This law mandates that the procedures can only take place in hospital facilities. What that means is that all abortion clinics will lose their licenses in Indiana, plus any doctor who performs an illegal abortion will lose their medical license. Abortion rights obviously going to be a very defining issue in this fall`s midterms no better evidence than that would happen in Kansas this week where voters in the largely Red State overwhelmingly voted to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Meantime, Democrats are hoping to come together in Washington today for a test vote on that massive economic plan aimed at reducing inflation now that Arizona senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  has signaled her willingness to agree to the deal. NBC`s Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill with what lies ahead. Ali, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Peter and Kristen, good morning. The -- we`re just hours away now from the starting bell of Democrats trying to pass this reconciliation package. It`s a big one and it`s going to be a lengthy process as they look to pass this health care, tax reform, and deficit reduction deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): This morning, Democrats marching in lockstep.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY): All right, well, we`re feeling pretty good.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Set to kick off a rare Saturday session this afternoon in hopes of notching a big win for their party and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  taking up a package of legislation that boasts hundreds of billions in deficit reduction, while also investing upwards of 350 billion in energy and climate provisions, cutting prescription drug costs, and new tax reforms that forced the wealthy and corporations to pay more. At the center of it all, moderate Democratic Senators Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . Manchin negotiating this bill in secret with Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Sinema, holding her support until she saw her priorities taken into account, including the removal of a carried interest tax rate loophole and $4 billion for drought relief.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Sinema, announcing late Thursday subject to the parliamentarian`s review, I`ll move forward. Now, with Sinema on board, it`s full steam ahead. Not that the road to passage is without its speed bumps.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSAY GRAHAM, (R-SC): What will vote-a-Rama be like? It`d be like hell. They deserve this.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Republicans vowing to make the process as politically painful as possible. Democrats are bracing for that.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Look, they`re going to do lots of amendments. We don`t know what else they will do. But I am -- I am -- as I said, I believe we will have 50 votes to pass this legislation at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Meanwhile, the House is set to come back from their recess next Friday to pass the bill and send it to President Joe Biden`s desk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And look guys, the name of the game here is speed. Democrats want to get this done as quickly as possible because they are very eager to campaign on it in the midterms, no surprise, given these provisions pull well with voters from both parties. Still, the earliest that we`ll see this passed is Sunday, maybe, assuming there`s no surprises in this process. Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, it`s going to be a very busy weekend. All right, Ali Vitali, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And this morning, the White House is touting a red hot jobs report showing more than a half million jobs were added in July, doubling expectations. Now all 22 million jobs lost during the pandemic have been recovered with unemployment falling to 3.5 percent. That matches a 50-year low. Still,\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220809ei8600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 TODAY for August 6, 2022, NBC NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander, Ali Vitali, Monica Alba,  Pri