r \par \uc2 [07:40:02]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It sure is, her name is Marisol Nichols and she`s working as a real life undercover operative helping police stop human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Emilie Ikeda has her story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In her acting career spanning nearly three decades, Marisol Nichols has played detectives, principals.\par \par \uc2 MARISOL NICHOLS, ACTRESS: OK, I won`t suspend her if you do me one favor.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And CEOs.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: I have ton of work to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come with me.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Come with you where?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: She`s best known for her work on hit show "Riverdale".\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Your disrespect will no longer be tolerated, not by me and certainly not by your father when he comes home.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: But it`s a roll out of the spotlight she`s most proud of.\par \par \uc2 It`s described as an undercover operative. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: I`m literally undercover pretending to be someone else into a situation where we`re infiltrating different scenarios.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: As a licensed informant, Nichols helps crack down on human trafficking, a $150 billion industry that involves forced labor and sexual exploitation. One in four victims of modern slavery are children according to the \uc2 International Labor Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leaning on her acting prowess, Nichols has posed as just about everything to lower perpetrators in, even a young child as seen here. What was going through your mind in that first operation?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: I`ll never forget this one guy. He`s like, hey, kiddo. And I wanted to just like you want to reach through the phone and just annihilate this person.\par \par \uc2 And you can`t because I need to -- I need to make this guy and these men come out of hiding, who have done this so many times and are used to doing this by the way, and I need them to show up because I`ve got a roomful of law enforcement with semi-automatic weapons in the room next to me, and they`re waiting to take these guys down and get them off the street.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The 48-year-old partners with police, district attorneys, governments and nonprofits like Operation Underground Railroad, founded by former Special Agent Tim Ballard. That introduction proved to be pivotal, pushing Nichols to the frontlines of rescue operations that have led to the arrest of dozens of people.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Being so close to this, this is dark stuff.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Very.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: How do you cope?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: It`s -- I`m not going to lie. I don`t come home normal. Some of the things that I`ve done brought me to very dark, dark, dark places on the planet.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Nichols says as a survivor of sexual assault in her adolescence, the mission is personal for her.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: And I got raped a bunch, by a bunch of guys. And it was pretty traumatizing. I woke up in the police department. I didn`t know what happened. To this day, I don`t remember much.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Years later, Nichols leaned on the Church of Scientology to pull her away from drugs and improve her mental health.\par \par \uc2 We pressed her on recent accusations in a lawsuit of trafficking within the church, accusations the church denied in a statement to NBC News saying in part, the allegations are both scurrilous and ridiculous. And the lawsuit is both a sham and a scam.\par \par \uc2 Has that ever given you pause?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: No, not in the least. It`s so absurd. And for me. I know what I`ve seen. I know what I`ve experienced in Scientology. It saved my life.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: She said to take a deep dive of the ups and downs in her life and volunteer work in a new podcast released this month.\par \par \uc2 What`s your hope? What`s your goal for your efforts?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: My goal is to have enough good people know about this so that they demand an end to it.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: For today, Emilie Ikeda, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: How incredible. She`s obviously been through so much. And for her to use her voice in that way is pretty remarkable.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s hard to imagine taking the steps that she has, but I think there`s certainly a lot of parents and people in this country that are grateful for what she`s doing to take those predators off the street.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. When you think about the dozens who`ve been arrested for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, we want to switch gears now and say hello to our friend "NBC NEWS NOW" anchor Joe Fryer up in New York for a look at what`s coming up in POPStart. Hi, Joe, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Hi, there. Good morning. Coming up next, say it isn`t so, what we`re learning about the reasons behind Kim Kardashian and Pete Davidson`s big breakup. That`s right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:46:22]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It`s that time of the morning, we are back with POPStart. Hi, Joe.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Hi, there. Good morning. First up, Kim Kardashian and Pete Davidson after nine months of dating, yes, it appears the end of the road is here with the pair ending their relationship this week.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell E! News that long distance and busy schedules made it difficult for Kim and Pete to maintain their relationship.\par \par \uc2 They started dating late last year after sharing some screen time together when Kardashian hosted "SATURDAY NIGHT LIVE".\par \par \uc2 Their whirlwind romance lasted less than a year. But even though it`s now apparently over, you can actually expect to see that romance on the next season of "The Kardashians" with Pete appearing in the trailer for the new show. So, Peter and Kristen something to look forward to there. Right?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, say it isn`t so, I thought they were such a cute couple.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I just feel bad that like the whole next season is going to be reliving their relationship that is now over.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I know, but a reason to tune in, perhaps?\par \par \uc2 FRYER: There you go. Maybe it`ll get them back together. I don`t know. I have no idea but we`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Beyonce. Well, delivering a local traffic report, one journalist decided to put a zing on it. Listen to how she snuck 15 Queen B references into her report this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHEILA WATKO, NBC10 PHILADELPHIA ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER AND TRAFFIC ANCHOR: But traffic is getting heated and it`s starting to break my soul just a little bit. So, let`s take a look at Monroe Township, 322. If you`re thinking it seems slower than usual, it`s not just all up in your mind. It is slower because all lanes are closed in both directions. That is due to a down poll.\par \par \uc2 Now listen, I told you a few minutes ago that 95 was still looking good right around Cottman Avenue. Now, it`s going to look like I`m lying because it`s pretty jammed up but at least I`m a beautiful liar.\par \par \uc2 Let`s also take a look --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: That is Sheila Watko a traffic and entertainment reporter at our NBC station down in Philly. She is a master of slipping music references into her live report. Sheila`s done this with Taylor Swift, Jason Derulo and Big Time Rush.\par \par \uc2 But this week, she was crazy in love with Beyonce`s song. Sheila said she originally planned to do the Renaissance report last Friday when the new album dropped but had to cancel it for breaking news. I think we can all agree it was definitely worth the wait.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Woody Harrelson and adult version and a baby version, the beloved actor is blowing up the internet for finding his unlikely doppelganger. Take a look at Woody and little Baby Cora. This week, Cora`s mom Danielle shared his snap of her daughter alongside a photo of the actor with a caption: OK, but how does our daughter look like Woody Harrelson.\par \par \uc2 Well, the internet could not agree more after thousands of likes and retweets. He actually saw the post and responded in the most Woody Harrelson way possible by writing the little girl a poem.\par \par \uc2 Ode to Cora, you`re an adorable child flattered to be compared. You have a wonderful smile. I just wish I had your hair. Cute.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s so great.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes, a cute story with a little bit of heart. And that Peter and Kristen is your POPStart.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I`m sorry, it doesn`t get any better than that, Joe, you just will never be able to top that. That`s the cutest thing I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: If I`m that baby`s mom, I`d say to my husband -- or the husband --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I`ve got some questions though.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Joe, thanks so much, that was fun.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead right here, the company that is bringing children`s drawings to life by turning them into these one of a kind unforgettable stuffed animals, their smiles. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:51:50]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: We`re back on a Saturday morning with a unique way to showcase your children`s artwork by turning it into something they can hold in their own two hands.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is thanks to a company that takes kids creations and transforms them into stuffed animals.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And really, who doesn`t love stuffed animals, especially one that`s a product of your child`s imagination? I recently met a very deserving group of kids who got to see their drawings come to life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: As surprises go for these kids in South Florida, it may not get better than this.\par \par \uc2 Just ask 5-year-old Denise (PH).\par \par \uc2 As soon as you saw her, what did you say?\par \par \uc2 DENISE: I said, I like this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You`re so excited?\par \par \uc2 DENISE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The special summer delivery courtesy of Scott Meier, who`s on a unique mission.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MEIER, CEO, BUDSIES: The goal is to make the world more huggable.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: What are those moments like for you?\par \par \uc2 MEIER: Oh, my gosh, it is elation. Seeing these kids in their faces, they`re so happy. I`ve never been Santa, but it`s kind of like I`ve got a little taste.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Meier is the CEO of Budsies, a South Florida based company that makes one of a kind stuffed animals from pictures or kid`s drawings.\par \par \uc2 DENISE: It`s a stuffy (PH).\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s a stuffy.\par \par \uc2 DENISE: It`s a picture of me.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Unicorns and dragons. Even Maria`s vibrant princess.\par \par \uc2 Did you draw that all by yourself?\par \par \uc2 MARIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: What colors are in her dress?\par \par \uc2 MARIA: Purple, then blue, yellow and red.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: How is this picture perfect playmate?\par \par \uc2 Who is that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Adan crocodile pirate.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Adan crocodile pirate.\par \par \uc2 What`s your name?\par \par \uc2 GINO: Gino.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Gino, but who`s this in the picture?\par \par \uc2 GINO: Me.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s you? Then who is that?\par \par \uc2 GINO: Me.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: For these children at the Florence Fuller Center (PH) in Boca Raton, it`s an unforgettable gift. Thanks to the generosity of the Boca West Children`s Foundation.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA WEINROTH, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, BOCA WEST CHILDREN`S FOUNDATION: We hand out grants, and there`s very few opportunities for us to engage in the end client. And this was just one of those times. It reaffirms everything we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And these kids aren`t alone. Budsies have turned more than 200,000 drawings into stuffed toys, creating plenty of smiles.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When I open mine, I was so happy.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Why were you so happy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because it`s so soft when I hugged it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: At roughly $140 each for the kids at Florence Fuller, Budsies would likely be out of reach, making this moment that much more memorable.\par \par \uc2 ELLYN OKRENT, CEO, BOCA RATON FULLER CENTER: That brings the opportunity for them to know that they`re valued, that they`re important and that they will be somebody someday.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: What was once only on paper, now something plush.\par \par \uc2 This is your favorite doll now?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Oh my goodness, Peter, I wish I were a kid again. How cool is that?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It is so much fun. These pictures were awesome. But when they come back for these kids right to watch that become something real they can hold on, it was the best and those smiles there`s a lot of crummy stuff we talked about. When you get a chance to celebrate kids like that, it`s all worth it.\par \par \uc2 [07:55:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You can feel their excitement in their cheers, in their faces and you`re excited to doing the story.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Former camp counselor (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It all came through. That is going to do it for us on a Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We hope you have a great weekend. Be safe. Have a good Saturday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copy