 latest on that deadly lightning strike. The rising death toll.\par \par \uc2 The house fire leaving 10 dead including three children. The latest on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Demanding justice. The team who died under the weight of police officers. His family saying he was George Floyd before George Floyd. My investigation tonight.\par \par \uc2 Indiana poised to become the first in the country to pass a near-total ban on abortion after Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 And the unforgettable umpire who`s flipping the script and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening everyone. Pessimism about the state of the American economy turned to welcomed surprise today with new numbers confirming the nation is still cranking out jobs at a brisk pace. A whopping 528,000 new jobs added last month. The unemployment rate dropping to 3.5 percent tying the pre-pandemic low. The figures suggesting the country has not edged off into a recession.\par \par \uc2 Not to be ignored however, inflation still eating into paychecks at a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  poised to raise its key interest rate next month for the fifth time this year in an effort to slow the economy and bring prices down. After suffering bad economic headlines for weeks, an enthusiastic President Biden today trumpeting the job gains along with a steady drop in gas prices. Kelly O`Donnell reporting now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: An unexpected summer scorcher, the hot jobs market. Employers added twice the number of jobs predicted for July, 528,000. Unemployment fell to 3.5 percent, taking the U.S. back to pre-COVID Times.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today, there are more people working in America than before the pandemic began. In fact, there are more people working in America at any point in American history.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading this job growth, leisure and hospitality followed by professional services, health care, transportation and warehousing.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH, U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: And it shows that there is some signs this economy that is still very strong.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: But that undeniably good news overshadowed for millions of Americans by 40-year high inflation. In South Carolina, Angelina Scott faces painful choices.\par \par \uc2 ANGELINA SCOTT, RESIDENT, SOUTH CAROLINA: The economy right now is making me choose between my medication and food, between my medication, food, and bills.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: She and her husband Matthew are both working but can`t cover all their rising expenses.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I feel like the economy is really suffocating and slowly. It feels like no one cares. It doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A July survey finds 51 percent of Americans now don`t have an emergency fund that can cover three months of expenses. And about half of the country`s elderly now struggle to pay for essentials. While a President`s political fate is often linked to the state of the economy, new NBC News reporting finds a restlessness among Democratic donors who would prefer a different nominee for 2024.\par \par \uc2 One democratic fundraiser speculated about the president stepping aside saying there`s going to be a lot of pressure coming up post-midterms, yet most see President Biden as the strongest contender in a potential rematch against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As Americans cope with higher prices, another critical snapshot on the economy is expected Wednesday, the consumer price index, the latest inflation number will be released. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Kelly O`Donnell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Senate Democrats are voicing optimism about passing their big climate tax and health care bill. Ali Vitali is at the Capitol. Ali, how did this finally come together?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  now in lockstep with the rest of her caucus after Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  accepted her changes to this bill including removing a provision that would have closed the carried interest tax loophole which benefits wealthy hedge fund managers, plus adding billions in drought relief funds.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s full steam ahead. Democrats sprinting headlong into a rare Saturday session to pass the signature climate and deficit reduction package. Schumer telling me today he`s confident that his party will stand together to pass this legislation, which at the earliest would be Sunday. Lester?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:26]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Ali, thank you. And just breaking, a jury in Austin, Texas ordering Alex Jones to pay millions in punitive damages.\par \par \uc2 Anne Thompson joins me now. Anne, what happened in court today?\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, this is a big win for the victim`s parents. A Texas jury today ordering Alex Jones and his company to pay more than $45 million in punitive damages to the parents of Jesse Lewis who died in the Sandy Hook School massacre. That`s on top of yesterday`s decision, which means Jones and his company must pay Jesse`s parents a total of $49.3 million. That`s a lot of money but a fraction of the $135 to $270 million a forensics economist says Jones and his business are worth.\par \par \uc2 Jones faces two similar lawsuits from other victims` families. He did not show up in court for today`s verdict. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Anne Thompson, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, some of the busiest airports in the country from D.C. to New York are on ground delays as severe storms move in Thousands of flights delayed or canceled nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, tens of millions of Americans are under heat alerts in the Northeast and the plains. Temperature surging today into the 90s and triple digits. And increasingly severe weather is being tied to climate change. Here`s Sam Brock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The grueling heat that`s gripped the country is showing little sign of letting up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Central air is key.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: More than 60 million people tonight against sweltering under heat alert, with cities in the Northeast seeing their warmest temperatures in a decade. Dallas has now topped triple digits 40 times this year, almost doubling the annual average. Then, there`s the storms. Earth cam video from the Washington Monument showing lightning strikes around the same time four people were struck across from the White House killing three, including a couple in their 70s.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was just in a state of shock. I just couldn`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The ramp and cruelty of a changing climate felt deeply on the Isle de Jean Charles about 90 minutes outside of New Orleans. The signs are everywhere figuratively and literally for the Native American groups who`ve lived here for generations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Were standing with one foot in the water and one foot on land.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Chris Pranay (PA) has spent all 57 years of his life on this land. Now, the Choctaw Nation is part of an unprecedented relocation receiving nearly $50 million in a federal grant to help around 100 people seek higher ground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You being forced because of erosion and hurricanes to move inland.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The move is completely voluntary. But Mother Nature is clearly closing in. The island transforming from 22,000 acres in 1963 to just 300 by 2017.\par \par \uc2 Chris`s house somehow managed to go 20 years without damage from a hurricane until Ida which not only left its mark here, but rip through most of the remaining properties on the island as the barrier of land that used to blunt storms continues to erode.\par \par \uc2 Now, a pathway to a new aisle some 40 houses in Gray, Louisiana which families will occupy any day now.\par \par \uc2 PAT FORBES, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, LOUISIANA OFFICE OF COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT: They can literally withstand 150-mile-an-hour winds.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: In what could be a playbook for future cities.\par \par \uc2 FORBES\uc2 : We know there are more occurrences like this come and not just in Louisiana but around the country and around the world. So, we`ve got to start learning how to do it right.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The harsh reality of Coping With Climate displacement as communities like Isle de Jean Charles look to preserve their past. Sam Brock, NBC News New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A House fire overnight in eastern Pennsylvania killed 10 people including three children. According to a local paper, a volunteer firefighter who responded to the tragic scene said that the victims were all relatives. Tonight, there is an ongoing investigation into what caused that fire.\par \par \uc2 Tonight the battle over abortion is playing out in Indiana as that state moves toward banning almost all abortions. Stephanie Gosk now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Indiana is on the cusp of passing a ban on abortion, the first state to take that step since the supreme court overturn Roe v. Wade medical. The Senate still has to weigh in one last time. It has been anything but a smooth process. The battle waged over the exceptions,\par \par \uc2 KAREN ENGLEMAN, REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE, INDIANA: Ending the life of an unborn child is neither necessary nor an evidence-Pbased treatment for rape.\par \par \uc2 RITA FLEMING, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, INDIANA: Does this apply to a fifth grader who has been a victim of incest?\par \par \uc2 [19:10:04]\par \par \uc2 ENGLEMAN: It does.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In the end, the ban includes exceptions for the mother`s health and for victims of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 DR. NANCY WOZNIAK, OBGYN: The inclination of people is to -- the pregnancy is a result of this, that this is just going to be something that compounds her trauma. And I would just respectfully say, not always.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Dr. Nancy Wozniak is an OBGYN against abortion. She worries some may take advantage of the exceptions.\par \par \uc2 WOZNIAK: There`s always a risk of somebody exploiting that ability to justify getting an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: On the other side, Dr. Caitlin Bernard argues exceptions for rape and incest don`t really protect the victims. She performed an abortion on a 10- year-old rape victim whose story captured headlines and caught President Biden`s attention.\par \par \uc2 From your experience, do women who are the victims of rape and incest want to come forward and talk about it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD, OBGYN: Not even slightly. You know, they`ve been through something that is probably the most traumatic thing they`ve been through.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: When it comes to the health of the mother, there are challenges there as well.\par \par \uc2 It sounds pretty straightforward. You`re allowed to get an abortion if your health is compromised by the pregnancy or your life. Is it straightforward?\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY CALDWELL, OBGYN: Not at all. There is no way that we could come up with a list of exceptions for every possible scenario.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Dr. Amy Caldwell works one day a week at Planned Parenthood but she spends most of her time in the hospital where doctors worry about criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: We are frightened. There have been a lot of group chats. There`s been discussions about you know, do we stay? Do we go?\par \par \uc2 GOSK: You mean, do you leave all the other?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Leave Indiana? Leave the hospital?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The law will likely get passed in the coming days before going to the governor for the final say. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, we are keeping a close eye on soaring tensions between China and the U.S. as China ramps up a massive show of force near Taiwan. Here`s Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): China`s military escalation at a fever pitch, its highest in memory on all fronts, air, sea, land, cyber, and space. Fighter jets circling Taiwan, missiles fired over the island, nine landing in waters off Japan, detouring commercial flights and rattling a major U.S. ally. All of it a furious response to House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s visit supporting the democratic government in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 China claims Taiwan is its territory.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: These provocative actions are a significant escalation. China has chosen to overreact and use Speaker Pelosi`s visit as a pretext to increase provocative military activity in and around the Taiwan Strait.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: All these raising U.S. fears of an accidental confrontation.\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2  (RET), NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL SECURITY AND DIPLOMATIC ANALYST: Upping those fighters that are potentially going nose to nose in and around Taiwan, that`s not Tony Blinken. You know, it`s Goose and Maverick. These are young men and women. They`re fired up.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: In the Taiwan Strait, a Chinese nuclear submarine below the water, warships above.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Within these zones, they`re firing missiles, torpedoes, naval gunfire. It`s a kind of a de facto blockade.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And China has cut off all lines of communication with the U.S. No high level military or naval talks. No cooperation on illegal migration, drug trafficking, or climate change. And China sanctioned Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and her family. Pelosi`s message to China tonight.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): But they will not isolate Taiwan by preventing us to travel there. They are not doing our travel schedule.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea, if I can change subjects slightly here and talk about the tensions with Russia. There`s news following Britney Griner`s prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): Indeed, Lester. Now that the trial is over, Russia`s Foreign Minister now says for the first time they are willing to talk about a prisoner swap but only in private. So, the U.S. is going to test whether \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is really serious. President Biden said today, I`m hopeful we`re working hard. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Andrea Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the baby formula crisis. Why some parents are still struggling to find food for their children? What families need to know?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, now to a story we have been following closely, the baby formula shortage that left many families scrambling. Tonight, many of them say they are still not out of the woods and all this. Here`s Emilie Ikeda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The nightmare that has been ruling parents lives for months --\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Parents are desperately scrambling to find baby formula.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Isn`t over yet. We spoke with mom Felicia Edwyn back in May.\par \par \uc2 FELICIA EDWYN, MOTHER OF TWO: It`s just not a good feeling to be on edge at every moment.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Since then, she and her husband Toby say there`s been little relief.\par \par \uc2 TOBY EDWYN, FATHER OF TWO: It really gets down to alright we only have about a day or twos worth. What`s our next move going to be?\par \par \uc2 F. EDWYN: And that in itself is nerve-racking.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: While the situation has improved some, our look at store shelves this week found big gaps remain where formula should be. The latest nationwide data shows a 27 percent out-of-stock rate for powdered formula. That`s despite the reopening of Abbott`s critical Sturgis manufacturing plant which resumed production earlier this summer, but not yet for all products.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has had domestic manufacturers ramp up production and flown in the equivalent of nearly 64,000,000 eight-ounce bottles of formula from abroad. The White House this week announced more flights to come but also acknowledge that for many families the problem persists.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This has not gone away and we do understand that there`s a lot more work to do.\par \par \uc2 SARAH CHAMBERLAIN, MOTHER: Back ordered, cancelled.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: In New Jersey, Sarah Chamberlain hasn`t been able to order a single can of specialty formula online since April. She now depends on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups to track down the products which her 8-year-old who suffers from a metabolic disorder relies on for 70 percent of her nutrition.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like to be a mother in this kind of situation?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERLAIN: It`s difficult. I mean, her health and her development is at risk without this formula. And that is an incredible burden for parent.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Industry experts anticipate supply to normalize in the fall, a long road ahead for families still weathering a day-to-day struggle. Emilie Ikeda, NBC News Montclair, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next tonight, my report of the death of an unarmed Black teenager in the wake of the George Floyd case where his parents say his death deserves another look.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with the case of a black teenager who died after a struggle with police nearly four years ago, one that Anton Black`s family says has similarities to the death of George Floyd. But Anton Black`s case is not widely known outside Maryland. And now, there are questions about why his death was ruled an accident.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): In Greensboro, Maryland, September 2018, an unarmed Black teenager flees from police. There`s a chase and a struggle all caught on body cam. 19-year-old Anton Black under the weight of police officers crying out to his mother.\par \par \uc2 ANTON BLACK, KILLED IN POLICE CUSTODY: How are you guys doing? My name is Anton Black.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Anton was a star athlete and a budding model.\par \par \uc2 ANTONE BLACK, FATHER OF ANTON BLACK: This is my baby boy, Anton.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And now, his father says Anton is an example of a broken system outraged that his son`s death was declared an accident.\par \par \uc2 ANTONE BLACK: You begging for your life and they don`t get off of him. How is that an accident?\par \par \uc2 My son was George Floyd before George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There is at least one connection between George Floyd`s case and Anton Black`s. Dr. David Fowler, Maryland`s former chief medical examiner who signed off on Anton`s autopsy report testified two years later for officer Derek Chauvin`s defense that Floyd`s death was undetermined.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROGER MITCHELL, FORMER CHIEF MEDICAL EXAMINER, WASHINGTON D.C.: If Anton Black is an accident, and George Floyd is undetermined, then how many other cases that we have no idea about are accidents and undetermined?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dr. Roger Mitchell is an expert in in-custody police deaths.\par \par \uc2 How many in-custody deaths are there in the U.S. each year?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: No one knows.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There are roughly 2000 medical examiners and coroners in America. Dr. Mitchell says there is no central database so no one knows how many people like Anton die in police custody every year. But he says he has a solution, adding a single box on a death certificate.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: A checkbox on the U.S. standard death certificate that allows us to see from a physician`s hand who`s dying in custody no matter what the manner of death so that we can have any objective public health measure of this issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dr. Fowler declined to speak with NBC News. Don`t miss the new episode of "DATELINE" tonight at 10:00 p.m. Eastern, 9:00 Central.\par \par \uc2 When we come back here tonight, how one beloved umpire`s unique style on the field is changing the game and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: While summer softball season hits a fever pitch, one umpire is calling games unlike any other. Boyd Huppert from our KARE 11 in Minneapolis has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LOUIS WILLIAMS IV, UMPIRE: Three balls, two strikes.\par \par \uc2 BOYD HUPPERT, REPORTER, KARE 11: By tradition, umpires are an anonymous bunch. It`s their nature.\par \par \uc2 You know, the umpires name?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: But one field over at the Minnesota girls 10 and under state softball tournament --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: -- everyone knows this name.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My name Blue Lou and you know what I got.\par \par \uc2 KIDS: What do you got?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I got a team that`s hotter than hot.\par \par \uc2 KIDS: How hot is hot?\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: And loves his strike calls.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Strike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He does like the one who read balls.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Part game official.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I believe that we will win.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Part motivational speaker, part acrobat. Everyone knows Blue Lou but who is he?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Louis Williams IV.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Louis Williams IV came to Minnesota to play football at St. Cloud State University.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, I had like a year and a half left of school. They cut the team because of budget issues.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Louis stayed and finished his engineering degree, struck a pose with his mother, and went to work in his field. The field of manufacturing engineering and the infield.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Last year, around this time, I read a book it`s called The Compound Effect. And a book basically was talking about if you got a talent and put it on display for the world to see.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: You might say Louis jumped at the opportunity. How much do they love him?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can he switch?\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: That`s a team pleading to have Louis move from another game to theirs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please, please.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: The girls will encounter plenty of umpires they won`t remember and one they will never forget.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: As long as the kids have fun, I do 100 backflips.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Boyd Huppert, NBC News, Becker, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s terrific. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or