after previously suggesting it was a hoax. And the stunning moment on the stand when Jones is told his legal team accidentally sent his text messages to the other side`s lawyers.\par \par \uc2 The alert tonight on surging credit card debt. What experts say you should do about it right now.\par \par \uc2 And remembering one of the most famous voices in baseball.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. Abortion rights advocates are savoring a significant and rare post-Roe v. Wade victory tonight, not in a state house but at the ballot box in Kansas where voters up-ended assumptions defeating by a wide margin a constitutional amendment that would have opened the door to a ban on abortions in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 The mechanics of the victory mustering an unexpectedly large voter turnout in the mostly red state quickly becoming a case study for Democrats and Republicans as they weigh the potential impact of abortion as an animating issue in the November Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, last night, important wins for Trump-backed candidates in several state primary elections. We have two reports beginning with Kristen Welker on the Kansas vote.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, good evening. This was the first time abortion was on the ballot since Roe v. Wade was overturned six weeks ago. The top takeaway, the issue is energizing for Democrats and even some Republicans, but it`s still not clear what it all means for November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voiceover): Tonight, shockwaves across the country after Kansas voters turned out in unexpected numbers to uphold abortion rights in a red state. Turnout with staggering approaching levels typically seen during a presidential election. 59 percent of Kansans voted to uphold the state`s constitutional right to an abortion currently illegal up to 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m so overjoyed --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Dasha Burns was with abortion rights advocates when the results came in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Relief, excitement, joy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We will not let the government overreach into our personal rights. We just won. We said no.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Tonight, abortion rights opponents vowing to fight on.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE UNDERWOOD, VALUE THEM BOTH COALITION: This is something that`s a long-term effort, of course. We are in this because we believe very strongly in protecting both women and babies.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Democrats are now hoping Kansas is a bellwether for November. At the White House, President Biden looking to capitalize signing an executive order that aims to protect women traveling out of state for abortions\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Voters of Kansas sent a powerful signal that this fall, the American people will vote to preserve and protect the right and refused to let them be ripped away by politicians.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But analysts cautioned Kansas was an election where much of the focus was on the single issue of abortion, and Democrats are still bracing for the possibility of losses with inflation at a 40-year high and President Biden`s approval ratings low.\par \par \uc2 Could what we saw in Kansas foreshadow a game changer in November?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: The abortion issue is an motivating one for voters that Democrats need to turn out to vote, but the economy is looming out there as a very, very big issue for voters, and that`s where Republicans have an advantage.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Abortion likely a top focus in at least six key states where candidates are sharply divided over the issue and where the policy is unsettled. Kristen Welker, NBC News, the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): I`m Vaughn Hillyard in Phoenix. Defining primary night wins for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 -aligned candidates.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R-MI), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I am tough, my friends, and I am just getting going.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Despite weeks of hearings by the January 6 Committee laying out serious allegations against Trump, Republicans Voters on Tuesday stuck by his loyalist.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:03]\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Do what you have to do to get him in. You`ll see a big difference.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Including \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R-AZ) SENATE CANDIDATE: We can and will be Mark Kelly in November.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And other hardline election deniers in the state. And Kari Lake is leading for governor in a race it`s too close to call.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The MAGA movement rose up and voted like the lives depended on it.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Trump also helped oust \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  who testified about the pressure he face to overturn Arizona`s 2020 election. The Arizona House Speaker loss to a Trump-endorsed challenger. In Missouri, Senate candidate \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  with the win.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO) SENATE CANDIDATE: We need America first fighters who will advance the America first agenda.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Over in Michigan, Congressman Peter Meijer, one of 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach Trump last year, lost his race against a Trump ally.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETER MEIJER (R-MI): We`re just sick of the politics of everything be a question of fear or anger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: The GOP`s next major primary is the Wisconsin governor`s race next week, and then Wyoming where \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is trying to fend off a Trump- backed challenger and defend her investigation into January 6. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Vaughn Hillyard, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A military show of force by China tonight in response to House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s historic visit to Taiwan, the self-governed island that China claims as part of its territory. Andrea Mitchell has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Just as House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was waving farewell to Taiwan, China responding by firing warning shots. Its first live fire exercises off Taiwan since 1996. Taiwan says even encroaching into what it considers its territorial waters.\par \par \uc2 China at times sending fighter jets across a critical dividing line between Taiwan and the mainland. According to Taiwan and a senior U.S. official, an aggressive action raising us fears either side could miscalculate.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We come in friendship.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Pelosi meeting with human rights leaders and Taiwan`s democratically-elected president \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen. The speaker is saying the world is in a struggle between autocracy and democracy.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Our delegation came here to send an unequivocal message. America stands with Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: China, which considers the island its territory, is punishing Taiwan for hosting the speaker by blocking some imports but not critical semiconductor chips. Taiwan is the world`s biggest chip manufacturer. G7 foreign ministers today calling out China`s threatening actions. And Pelosi questioning why past trips by male Congress members did not create an outcry from China.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I think that they made a big fuss because I`m Speaker, I guess, I don`t know if that was a reason or an excuse because they didn`t say anything when the men came.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): A senior U.S. official tells NBC News the U.S. does not think China plans to invade now. But the worry is if China`s military gets too close to Taiwan and Taiwan overreact, this could spiral out of control. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A federal grand jury has subpoenaed former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  in its investigation of the January 6 insurrection according to a source familiar with the matter. Cipollone is reportedly the highest- ranking Trump White House official who have been subpoenaed by a grand jury. He has already testified before the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 Flags at the Capitol and White House are at half-staff tonight after the death of Indiana Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2 . She was killed in a car crash north of Indianapolis. Police say another vehicle crossed the center line and hit the car Walorski was traveling in head on. Two members of her staff were also killed. Walorski had served in Congress since 2013.\par \par \uc2 For years he claimed the massacre at Sandy Hook Elementary School was a hoax, but today in Austin, Texas, conspiracy theorist Alex Jones admitted it was real at the first of several trials in which Sandy Hook families are seeking damages. And now, the jury has the case. Here`s Anne Thompson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Alex Jones` stunning admission came during a contentious cross-examination.\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES, RADIO HOST: It`s 100 percent real.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Finally conceding the Sandy Hook school shooting did happen after years of insisting on his InfoWars show the massacre was a hoax.\par \par \uc2 JONES: How could you believe any of it?\par \par \uc2 It`s 100 percent real. And the media still ran with lies that I`m saying it wasn`t real.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The parents of Jesse Lewis, one of 2o first-graders who died in the massacre are seeking $150 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 NEIL HESLIN, PARENT OF SANDY HOOK VICTIM: To restore the honor and legacy of my son that was tarnished by Mr. Jones.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Jones blasted Jesse`s father on a show yesterday.\par \par \uc2 JONES: He is being manipulated by some very bad people. I mean, I`ll just say because I got to be honest. He`s slow.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But in court, he faced Jesse`s mother.\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT LEWIS, PARENT OF SANDY HOOK VICTIM: Jesse was real. I am a real mom.\par \par \uc2 PARENT OF SANDY HOOK VICTIM: Testifying their 6-year-old son and their pain were real. The conspiracy theorists has been a difficult witness.\par \par \uc2 MAYA GUERRA GAMBLE, JUDGE, TEXAS 459TH DISTRICT COURT: This is not your show.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Admonished yesterday by the judge for lying on the stand.\par \par \uc2 GAMBLE: You`re already under oath to tell the truth. You`ve already violated that oath twice today.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And today he was caught in another lie by the plaintiff`s attorney.\par \par \uc2 MARK BANKSTON, LAWYER: Your attorneys messed up and sent me an entire digital copy of your entire cell phone with every text message you`ve sent for the past two years.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Including text messages about Sandy Hook Jones failed to share as ordered by the court.\par \par \uc2 BANKSTON: And that is how I know you lied to me when you said you didn`t have to text messages about Sandy Hook. Did you know that?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The jury will determine what if anything Jones must pay. Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In eastern Kentucky, a desperate search for survivors goes on tonight after the catastrophic flooding that killed at least 37 people. Jesse Kirsch is there for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Isom, Kentucky tonight, this grocery store has nothing to sell.\par \par \uc2 GWEN CHRISTIAN, GROCERY OWNER: : It`s just devastation, total devastation.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Deadly floods gutted Gwen Christian`s supermarket.\par \par \uc2 We can tell about where the water came up because look at this shelf. It looks like nothing ever happened and everything else, mud.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN: What you have to understand is our customers are our family. The people who works in this store is our family. We know each other. We know each other`s pains.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Neighbor Natasha York lost everything.\par \par \uc2 NATASHA YORK, FLOOD VICTIM: I have no belongings. I have no home. I have nothing. I can`t.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: One week after this horrific natural disaster began, residents still lining up for basic supplies. Neighbors helping each other pick up the muddy shattered pieces.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All we can do now start rebuilding and get back to what we lost.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: But many lost something you can`t replace. Among the 37 killed, Gabriel Hensley. His wife says the 30-year-old was heading home from the overnight shift when the floods swept away the father of five. Other families are still looking for closure.\par \par \uc2 TRACY RITCHIE, FATHER STILL MISSING: He`s my everything.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Tracy Ritchie hasn`t heard from her dad since the floods reduced his house to concrete blocks. Now, she`s waiting to see if her DNA matches a victim.\par \par \uc2 RITCHIE: I`d give anything to give my dad a hug, you know. IF they could find him, that`d be wonderful. But I don`t think that`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Grief, compassion, but also perseverance rising from the brutal force of nature. Jesse Kirsch, NBC News, Isom, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The lost their overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn out of the battle for Ukraine. Since the war began, Russia has been accused of abducting thousands of Ukraine`s children. Morgan Chesky now on the urgent effort to get them back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Ukraine says it`s holding its ground in the East thanks to U.S.-made guided missiles known as HIMARS. Major General Viktor Yagun telling us the weapon`s impact vital at slowing rushes advance.\par \par \uc2 You`ve seen the front lines. How would you describe the fighting there right now?\par \par \uc2 He tells me, if the Russian military is able to advance, it`s only a few 100 meters a day. And in Washington, new sanctions targeting Russian elites, including the reported girlfriend of Russian President Putin 39- year-old \uc2 Alina Kabaeva\uc2 , the former Olympic gold medalist.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine launching a new mission to rescue the war`s youngest victims. 5000 children missing somewhere in Russian-occupied territories.\par \par \uc2 KATERYNA PAVLICHENKO, DEPUTY MINISTER OF INTERNAL AFFAIRS, UKRAINE (through translator): Those children were deported and taken forcibly from the territory of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: They tell us 11-year-old Sasha was one of them. Separated from his family at a Mariupol hospital. Months later, reunited with his grandmother who told us his mother is still missing.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell Sasha when he asks where his mother is?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): He is in pain to hear it. With God`s will, maybe this war will end someday and she will come back to her children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: And there is some hopeful news tonight. the Russian Navy ending its blockade of Ukraine`s grain allowing the first ship to pass through. The U.S. calling it a significant step but says as long as Russia continues to fight, the world`s most vulnerable will suffer. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Morgan Chesky in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, why big city mayors far from the southern border are pleading for federal help tonight with a record number of migrants.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The record number of migrants crossing the Southern Border is now stretching resources in cities thousands of miles away. Now, the mayors of New York and Washington DC are pleading for some help. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Far from the U.S. southern border, the mayor of Washington DC, a Democrat, has asked the National Guard to help handle more than 4000 migrants recently bused to the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 MURIEL BOWSER, MAYOR OF WASHINGTON, D.C.: I`ve asked for the deployment of the guard as long as we need the guard to deal with the humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The request comes after Texas his Republican governor in what Democrats call a political stunt started sending migrants to DC. But Republicans blamed the Biden administration after a record number of illegal border crossings in May. Also, according to internal documents reviewed by NBC News, 73 migrants, including 13 children were recently found living in stash houses operated by human smugglers in Northwest DC.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Every single community in America is now a border city. This is a national crisis.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: New York`s mayor is also asking the federal government for help. His office says about 4000 asylum seekers have entered the city`s homeless shelter system since late May.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: If there was ever an all-hands-on-deck moment, this is it.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Have you ever seen numbers like this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not really. Not really.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: \uc2 Catholic Charities\uc2  in New York City is busier than ever.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Right now, this is a new emergency.\par \par \uc2 Gervis and Antonia say they`re from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Why don`t you come?\par \par \uc2 A better life for my daughter, she says. They were among those bussed to the northeast from Texas after a grueling journey through Central America.\par \par \uc2 My daughter said she trusted us but it was hard she says because at one point, we almost ran out of food.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Migrants arrests at the border did drop 14 percent in June after hitting that all-time high the month before. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you. Up next for us tonight, credit card debt is skyrocketing. What you can do to get it under control.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A big day on Wall Street. All three major indexes making significant gains after two days of losses. Americans still feeling the impact of inflation, certainly, with the biggest jump in credit card debt in two decades. But there is some good news to report at the pump. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Colorado, Kealan Thomas and her husband are on a mission, to keep spending down and pay off the last of the $15,000 in credit card debt they ran up after getting married.\par \par \uc2 KEALAN THOMAS, HAS CREDIT CARD DEBT: We want to be able to buy a house and we don`t have a house yet, you know, and we are in our 30s. And that`s hard, but it`s because we made the choice to use the credit cards thinking that we`d be able to pay it off easily.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: As inflation soars, new data shows many Americans are charging it. Credit card debt has jumped 13 percent in one year, the biggest increase in 20 years. And Americans open 233 million new accounts, the most since 2008. While balances still remained below pre-pandemic levels, interest rates now averaging 17.4 percent could soon soar to record highs as the Fed continues hiking rates to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 If you`re carrying heavy credit card debt, financial experts recommend cutting it down as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 TED ROSSMAN, SENIOR ANALYST, BANKRATE: That rates probably three, four, 5x what you`re paying on your mortgage or car loan. So, definitely you want to be paying down the credit card debt.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Transfer balances to cards offering 0 percent introductory rates, perhaps turn to nonprofit financial counselors for help. Meanwhile, the White House today noting some inflation relief, with gas prices now averaging $4.16 a gallon, down $0.85 from the record high set in June.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Experts say gas could fall below $4 a gallon soon though OPEX pledge today to pump even more oil expected to have a minimal impact. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, his voice was iconic is the crack of the bat and the roar of the crowd.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: For decades, he was the voice of the \uc2 LA Dodgers\uc2  many calling Vin Scully who died last night at 94 The Voice of Summer. He wasn`t a player but his play-by-play made him a giant. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY, SPORTS COMMENTATOR: It`s time for Dodger baseball.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With those easy words in his unmistakable voice, Vin Scully cemented a legacy as legendary as those of the players he called.\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: Slow curveball, see you later.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Armed with a mastery of the game and the grace of a folksy friend, Scully brought the field to life during his 67-year career.\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: There`s 29,000 people in the ballpark and a million butterflies.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As the Dodgers play-by-play man, he marked American history when Hank Aaron smash Babe Ruth`s homerun record.\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: A Black man is getting a standing ovation in the Deep South.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: More than a decade later.\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: Look who`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: When an injured Kirk Gibson gave the underdog Dodgers a walk-off World Series game win, Scully perfectly captured what only he could coin.\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: In a year that has been so improbable, the Impossible has happened.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Born in the Bronx in 1927, the slender redhead first began calling games for the Brooklyn Dodgers at 22. When he retired in 2016, he was awarded the Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: I`ve needed you far more than you needed me.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Though Vin Scully never played for the Dodgers, he was the face of a franchise who left the field with the grace of a legend.\par \par \uc2 Miguel Almaguer, NBC News Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220804ei8300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Today Show for August 3, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Craig Melvin, Hallie Jackson, Andrea Mitchell, Peter  Alexander, Jacob Soboroff, Miguel Almaguer, Al Roker, Kerry Sanders, Emilie  Ikeda \par \uc2 6,921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:18]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. On the ballot, overnight, voters in Kansas voted overwhelmingly to protect abortion rights, the nation`s first test at the polls since the overturning of Roe v. Wade. Just ahead the impact on the battles ahead in other states and the takeaways for both parties after a night of closely watched primaries from coast to coast. We`ve got complete coverage.\par \par \uc2 Defiant message, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  wraps up her controversial trip to Taiwan overnight with strong words for China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), U.S. HOUSE SPEAKER: Our delegation came here to send an unequivocal message, America stands with Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This morning, China`s furious reaction saying she`s, playing with fire and the fallout now facing the White House in the wake of that high profile visit.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, a victory for veterans, the Senate passes a long sought bill to help millions of service members exposed to toxic burn pits.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m not sure I`ve ever seen a situation where people who have already given so much had to fight so hard to get so little.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The very latest from Capitol Hill straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 The heat is on again, nearly 100 million Americans from Texas all the way to Maine, set to face a new round of scorching temperatures, major cities expecting to hit triple digits. Al has got everything we need to know.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, brew ha-ha.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s going to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The surprising reason behind a major beer shortage, how long it could last and what you can do to find your cold ones.\par \par \uc2 And legend lost.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIN SCULLY, AMERICAN SPORTS COMMENTATOR: It`s time for Dodger baseball.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Vin Scully, the beloved Hall of Fame voice of the Dodgers has died.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCULLY: High fly ball in the right field, she is gone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Tributes pouring in overnight for the icon who brought us some of the game`s most unforgettable moments for nearly 70 years. We will celebrate his remarkable life, today, Wednesday, August 3rd, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And welcome to Today. So happy you`re joining us on a Wednesday morning. Craig is in for Savannah. There are legends and then there are legends. And Vin Scully was definitely one of them. Wow.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: In fact, here`s a live look right now at this growing memorial at Dodger Stadium. Vin Scully really the voice of baseball for decades. We lost him overnight. We`re going to celebrate him this morning. But to your point, what a remarkable career it was.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Isn`t that incredible. I can`t wait to hear more and more about him.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: More in the tributes to Vin Scully just ahead. But we are going to start this morning with those election results coming in overnight. And there are a lot of headlines that we want to break down for you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Indeed, the first vote held on abortion rights since Roe versus Wade was overturned, played out in the Red State of Kansas. Voters, they are sending a strong message by striking down a proposed amendment that would have removed abortion protections from the state constitution, the Kansas Secretary of State reporting high voter turnout close to the levels of a presidential election. For the latest on that, and then its other key races and what it all means heading into the midterms, we turn to NBC`s Hallie Jackson. Hallie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Hoda, good morning to you. And listen, one of the biggest takeaways from this election is what the results in Kansas could mean for the next election after that surprising victory for abortion rights supporters suggesting the end of Roe versus Wade is motivating voters. And then there`s former President Trump getting what he wanted even with mainstream Republicans putting up a fight, those who back his election lies doing well in their GOP primaries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): In conservative Kansas this morning, a political earthquake and a big win for abortion rights supporters, Kansas voters rejecting an amendment that would have removed abortion protections from the state constitution, in effect, keeping access to the procedure there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So grateful and just overjoyed. We did what Kansas needed us to do.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): President Biden overnight saying the vote makes clear what we know, the majority of Americans agree that women should have access to abortion. It`s the first time abortion has been on the ballot since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. The result in Kansas suggesting the Court`s decision is galvanizing abortion rights supporters less than 100 days until the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:10]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I hope we inspire other states when they get the opportunity to vote to really think about a woman`s choice.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): Tuesday`s primaries also a critical test of former President Trump`s grip on the GOP. And for now, it still appears strong. In Arizona, the scales tipping for him and candidates who have questioned the legitimate results of the 2020 election. NBC News projecting Mr. Trump`s preferred candidate \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  winning the Republican Senate nomination. And in the GOP primary for governor, Kari Lake, whom the former president prefers pulling ahead against her opponent, Karrin Taylor Robson.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ), GOVERNOR CANDIDATE: Do you how our election systems work and they don`t work well.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): Lake, a former T.V. host has amplified Mr. Trump`s lies about a stolen election in 2020 while claiming without evidence her primary race is rigged, question by NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So you know about a crime and yet you`re not reporting to authorities.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And I`m not telling you about that.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): Robson, endorsed by former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says Lake is unfit to hold office.\par \par \uc2 KARRIN TAYLOR ROBSON (R-AZ), GOVERNOR CANDIDATE: The voters of Arizona deserve better for a candidate to come out and say there`s been fraud before a vote has even been cast is crazy. It is disqualifying. And she -- she`s simply isn`t fit for the job.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): And \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  star witness of the January 6th hearings and the Republican Speaker of the Arizona House projected to lose his primary just weeks after testifying. In Michigan, GOP Congressman Peter Meijer, who voted to impeach Mr. Trump over the January 6th insurrection, conceded overnight to his Trump-backed opponent, John Gibbs. Trump endorsed conservative commentator, Tudor Dixon, has emerged as the GOP nominee for governor facing an uphill battle against incumbent Democrat \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R-MI), GOVERNOR NOMINEE: But our work isn`t finished. My friends, we are just getting started. And I hear it`s going to be a rough ride.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): And in Missouri`s Senate GOP primary where it was Eric versus Eric, Attorney General Eric Schmitt, defeating former governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO), GOVERNOR NOMINEE: Are you guys ready to take this country back?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Hallie, let`s get back to that victory for abortion rights in Kansas. So that results surprised a lot of folks. What does that mean for the midterms that are just a couple of months away?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So a couple of things here, Hoda. First, you got to look at the turnout here. It was big, right? Nine hundred thousand people voted here, nearly doubled the number of people who voted back in the 2018 primaries. And when you dig into the numbers, when you really get into it, there`s a suggestion that a sizable number of Republicans voted for protecting the current abortion access in Kansas. You can see that in the drop off of how many Republicans voted in the Kansas primary races versus on this abortion measure. So, yes, big picture, right, when you ask about what are the takeaways here for the midterms, it does suggest that abortion rights post Roe is a motivating factor for voters.\par \par \uc2 But if you`re trying to draw some big conclusions for November, keep in mind, it`s not just going to be abortion on the ballot then, you`re also going to have the economy, inflation, other factors coming into play. So, yes, this is a critical data point. But it`s also, you know, not a straight line from now until November, Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, good perspec