s.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:30]\par \par \uc2 HARRISON FORD, AMERICAN ACTOR: I`m sure you`re real ace. And whatever it is that you do, do.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE GRIFFITH, AMERICAN ACTRESS: Damn straight.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : But how you look.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFITH: I have a head for business and a bad for sin. Is there anything wrong with that?\par \par \uc2 FORD: No, no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No, nothing wrong with that. All right, we are back. You guys that`s a blast from the past. Do you all remember in the 80s?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: "Working Girl."\par \par \uc2 KOTB: "Working Girl" yes, starring Harrison Ford, Melanie Griffith, that was one of those iconic movie.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Iconic for all of the fans of that beloved rom com, get excited, a reboot is reportedly works. Selena Gomez is expected to produce.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And here`s the thing, not the only 80s throwback that`s been brought back Jake Gyllenhaal starring in this reimagined take of another 80s classic "Roadhouse." Cult classic, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`ve never seen "Roadhouse." But I love "Working Girl" though. It`s funny I wonder if this is all because, you know, you know how "Top Gun" was like home runs in row.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: For sure.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Do you think that they`re like, we can do this again?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: All of them.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I think that`s part of the bill. So think, you know, these studios also still own these movies, so they own the ID`s so it`s cheaper to just - -\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Dig in the vault.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Original "Working Girl" made 100 million bucks, six Oscars.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Six Oscars?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Nominations, excuse me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But still for a rom com to get a six Oscar nominations is pretty incredible.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I think people want to go back to the theater. They will go back in the movies.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Have you all been back yet?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You went for "Top Gun"?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: "Top Gun," love it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: What`s wrong with you?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Because I watch animated movies all the time. It`s all I watch.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: We`ll still have to go.\par \par \uc2 All right, guys, still ahead on PopStart, imagine you are on a plane and a star is your flight attendant. We`re going to tell you more about the unexpected role from Jason Momoa.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Plus, they signed an historic agreement deal and now they`re only in high school, we`re going to meet the inspiring sisters who could be the next --\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:05]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220803ei83000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion On The Ballot In Kansas\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Just hours from now, voters in Kansas will go to the polls with abortion on the ballot. It`s a critical task for abortion access at the state level after the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade. Here`s Dasha Burns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This Planned Parenthood is one of just five abortion clinics in Kansas, one of the few left in the entire region.\par \par \uc2 EMLY WALES, CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD GREAT PLAINS: We`re trying to really explain to Kansans we have seen what the crisis looks like. We know what patients faces look like when they realize it`s going to be days on the road. And we`re hoping that Kansans take steps to avoid that crisis here.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: On Tuesday, Kansas will be the first state to vote on abortion since the recent Supreme Court decision. Right now, the state constitution protects abortion rights here, but on the ballot is the value them both amendment which if passed would open the door to restrictions or a ban.\par \par \uc2 For months, volunteers have been knocking on doors.\par \par \uc2 Kansans have been rallying throughout the state, including here at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Activists even holding concerts to get voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Recent polling shows 47 percent support the amendment and 43 percent opposed, the rest undecided. Now, a final push from both sides.\par \par \uc2 This is your first time really canvassing, during the whole --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. Well, I love the pro-life movement my whole life.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What`s at stake here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The right to choose.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Abortion rights activist believe if the amendment passes, an abortion ban would be inevitable while many of those in favor of the change deny that a ban is their goal.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN HUMPHRIES, KANSAS STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We are putting this decision in the hands of the people.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: If passed, the conservative state legislature including representative Susan Humphries would determine the future of abortion in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 HUMPHRIES: And we`ll see what happens. I honestly don`t know I haven`t talked to people about it. I don`t really know what`s next.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Why don`t you talk to people about it? It`s a huge issue. Why haven`t you had that conversation?\par \par \uc2 HUMPHRIES: Because we`re really focused on August 2nd.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A day and a decision putting physicians like Dr. Elizabeth Daly on edge.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH DALY, DOCTOR: I cannot imagine not being able to provide people health care. For me, that would -- that would be really, really hard to go through.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: You`re getting emotional just thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes, I don`t want it to stop on my watch.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The nation now watching the state that`s become a bellwether. Dasha Burns, NBC News, Kansas City, Kansas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us tonight, the American journalist missing in Syria for a decade. My1 exclusive interview with his parents, their message to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220803ei8100003\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for August 1, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kelly O`Donnell, Jesse Kirsch, Miguel Almaguer, Sam  Brock, Dasha Burns, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 3,785 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOST, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Breaking News Tonight, the top leader of al-Qaeda killed in a U.S. counterterrorist strike in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): President Biden still in isolation for COVID Rebound addressing the nation tonight after Ayman Al Zawahiri was taken out by a U.S. strike over the weekend. The successor to \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 , a key planner of the 9/11 attacks, and one of the most wanted man in the world. What we`re learning?\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the new flood threat in Kentucky as the death toll rises to at least 37. The desperate search, 100 still unaccounted for. Our team with the National Guard air-dropping supplies to people trapped in their homes. And the tragedy for one family, their four children drowning in the floods.\par \par \uc2 The explosive wildfire in California turning deadly, the state`s biggest Inferno this year. Our team in the fire zone.\par \par \uc2 The longest sentence yet handed down to a Capitol rioter.\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback Deshaun Watson suspended after two dozen women accused him of sexual misconduct. Why critics say it doesn`t go far enough.\par \par \uc2 And my conversation with the parents of Austin Tice this month, marking 10 years since the American journalists vanished in Syria. Their new plea to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. Breaking right now, a U.S. drone strike in Afghanistan is believed to have killed one of the world`s most notorious terror masterminds wanted in the deaths of hundreds, including Americans abroad. U.S. officials say al-Qaeda leader Ayman Al Zawahiri, successor to \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 , was successfully targeted in a weakened drone strike in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 To this day, Zawahiri has been on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s most wanted list for his role in the 1998 bombings of U.S. embassies in Tanzania and Kenya that killed 224 people including 12 Americans, also a key figure in the 9/11 attack. President Biden addressing the nation tonight about the operation. Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House now with late details.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Lester, the importance of this strike against al-Qaeda`s leader the most significant blow to the terror group since the death of \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  comes with an unusual circumstance for this White House. President Biden has COVID and tonight will address the nation from a balcony so the TV camera will be outside and at a distance of about 15 feet.\par \par \uc2 Officials called this a successful counterterror -- counterterrorism operation importantly with no civilian casualties. And that played out over the weekend in Afghanistan. And of course, it was one year ago this month that the President made his decision to withdraw U.S. forces from that long war and the resulting exit turn chaotic and deadly with an attack on the Kabul airport.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, a very different outcome with the death of the one and 71- year-old terrorists. The President will explain how they found him and how they killed him coming up. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell, thank you. His signature was on many of the worst terrorist attacks of our time. Tonight, our chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel with details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Ayman Al Zawahiri was al-Qaeda as leader succeeding \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 , its public face in dozens of videos attacking the U.S. and its allies. He directed al-Qaeda`s thinking, led its operations, was a key planner of the 9/11 attacks and the 1998 U.S. embassy bombings in East Africa.\par \par \uc2 EVAN KOHLMANN, NBC NEWS TERRORISM ANALYST: We know that it was Al Zawahiri`s urging that Bin Laden really took an interest in attacking the United States.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: He meticulously planned mass murder, tried to arm al-Qaeda with weapons of mass destruction, and wrote its declaration of war against the U.S. His transformation into the world`s most wanted terrorist had an unlikely start. He was born in 1951 into a wealthy Egyptian family of doctors and scholars. He became radicalized as a teenager after joining the fundamentalist \uc2 Muslim Brotherhood\uc2  before training to be a surgeon.\par \par \uc2 At 31, he was one of hundreds arrested after the assassination of Egyptian President Anwar el-Sadat. He was tortured during three years in an Egyptian jail and left seeking revenge.\par \par \uc2 AYMAN AL ZAWAHIRI, LEADER, AL-QAEDA: They intend to kill us.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: By his mid-30s, he was a leader in Egypt`s Islamic Jihad. He met the Saudi \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  who was fighting the Soviets in Afghanistan. They bonded. Zawahiri became bin Laden`s personal doctor. By 1998, the Islamic Jihad and al-Qaeda merged. Zawahiri is second in command.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:03]\par \par \uc2 KOHLMANN: Bin Laden was a relative newcomer to the world of jihad. Zawahiri had well-established credentials. These two uniting provided a team that could not be broken.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: America was their next target, the World Trade Center in their sights. After 9/11, Zawahiri was seen in Afghanistan and then went into hiding, a $25 million award on his head. He kept al-Qaeda alive as ISIS became the West`s main target. Pious, ruthless, the brains behind the world`s worst terrorist attack. Even in his final video, he was calling on Muslims to attack America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Richard, what is the significance that this strike took place in Kabul?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): So, there are indications that it took place not just in Kabul, but right in the center of Kabul, not far from the presidential palace. And the United States has been cooperating, and many people don`t know this, with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in the fight against ISIS. But the counterterrorism cooperation against al-Qaeda has been very poor.\par \par \uc2 So, this seems like it was a target of opportunity for the Americans. The pinnacle of arrogance from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to hosts such as senior leader and the United States sending a message to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  that they won`t tolerate the group once again hosting al-Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Richard Engel with the breaking news tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also in Washington, a federal judge sentenced a convicted rioter who stormed the Capitol on January 6 to more than seven years in prison. Guy Reffitt of Texas was armed with a gun and zip ties. In court today, he called himself an idiot and said he no longer wants to associate with militia groups.\par \par \uc2 Days into a historic flash flood catastrophe, rescuers in Kentucky are still uncovering layers of death and misery. The death toll now at least 37. Tonight, new dramatic images of the desperate search and rescue operation. And Jesse Kirsch has exclusive access with the National Guard as they bring relief from the air.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Eastern Kentucky tonight, flood survivors getting food, water, even diapers by air. The National Guard delivering supplies including donations to thousands of people still largely cut off from the world more than four days after deadly floodwaters tore Eastern Kentucky apart.\par \par \uc2 Some areas so isolated even this Blackhawk helicopter couldn`t easily reach this community where a main road was obliterated.\par \par \uc2 You can see there`s a home down there tucked between the hills and we`re told that the roadway has been completely cut off mainly in food and water and this helicopter is going to bring it to them.\par \par \uc2 The deadly once in 1000-year floods destroyed bridges, ripped down power lines, and snapped homes like toothpicks. Overhead, it`s clear much of the water has receded but the desperate searches continue.\par \par \uc2 COL. DWAYNE LEWIS, NATIONAL GUARD, KENTUCKY: I don`t think we still know how big the situation is. It could go on for a couple of weeks. It could go on for a month.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: At least 37 killed. The death toll likely to climb. Among the lives lost, four children or siblings. The governor said there`ll be funding bodies for weeks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We aren`t going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: The National Guard already rescued more than 1000 people by air, including an 83-year-old woman and her family trapped in their attic.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURNER, KENTUCKY RESIDENT: I did not think we would survived.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Christy Turner`s two kids and husband barely escaped the floodwaters. She and her father-in-law were trapped on the porch when the waters ripped her home from its foundation.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I was just thinking how am I going to survive, how am I going to not drown, how am I going to be able to hold myself up?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Tonight, 12,000 people remain without power, 25,000 without water, and 44,000 under boil advisories. All as more rain continues soaking the region making clean-up for survivors even harder. Some areas hit with another round of flash floods.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: With everything had already been destroyed, it couldn`t destroy much anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: And amid all of this destruction, now there`s concern about excessive heat in the region as so many people are still without power or clean water. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Jesse Kirsch tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In California, deadly wildfire, the state`s biggest of the year, is ripping through a national forest area just miles from the Oregon border. Miguel Almaguer is on the front lines tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh my God. I could feel the flames from here.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The apocalyptic landscapes now commonplace, homes in ruins, trees becoming flame throwers. Tonight, this is the scene as firefighters struggle to save anything they can.\par \par \uc2 Raging near the Oregon border, the McKinney Fire, now the biggest wildfire in California this year, is more than 85 square miles of pure destruction, nearly the size of Baltimore already claiming two lives, a pair trapped in their car when flames overtook them.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My house is gone, all my furniture, all my clothes.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The inferno is burning so hot this massive 40,000-foot column of smoke is creating its own weather system. Lightning storms inside of it sparking new fires and fanning the blaze in multiple directions. With 2000 already forced out of their homes, authorities are urging residents to drop everything and evacuate. But even as flames creek closer to Yreka, doctors Rick and Venus Swenson aren`t going anywhere yet.\par \par \uc2 RICK SWENSON, DOCTOR: We made the decision to stay for the hospital in case the hospital needed us as the\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As the blaze explodes across mountain ranges, deputies called to rescue 60 desperate hikers on the scene at Pacific Crest Trail. Tonight, the Golden State red hot and once again going up in flames.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Lester, there`s still no firm number on how many homes have been destroyed. That number is easily in the dozens. Some 5000 structures still lie in the path of this fire. Now, the weather is cooperating with firefighters today. It`s been more of a wet and gloomy day hear, but that will change. This fire still zero percent contained. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Miguel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 One of the NFL`s top quarterbacks Deshaun Watson of the \uc2 Cleveland Browns\uc2  was suspended for six games today over allegations of sexual misconduct. But the order by a special judge may not be the last word on all this. Sam Brock has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At \uc2 Browns\uc2  practice today, business as usual for Deshaun Watson. But off the field, shockwaves and disbelief after a disciplinary officer hired by the \uc2 NFL\uc2  and its players association suspended the quarterback six games without pay, concluding he engaged in sexual assault and conduct that puts at risk the integrity of the \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 24 women, all massage therapists, filed civil lawsuits for sexual assault. The disciplinary officer relied on the testimony of four of them. In a ruling, she stated while Watson`s behavior does not fall into the category of violent conduct, the allegations of non-violent sexual conduct against him are more egregious than any before reviewed by the \uc2 NFL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Online, outrage. I just don`t think Deshaun Watson should play another game in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . Another person writing, he got suspended one quarter for every woman he sexually assaulted. That`s insane. Both the league and players association have three days to appeal.\par \par \uc2 MIKE FLORIO, NBC SPORTS PRO FOOTBALL TALK: One of the things the \uc2 NFL\uc2  likely will do is see what the reaction has been, what are the columns say in the various newspapers, what are they saying on the sports talk shows?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The final decision comes down to the commissioner. Watson has denied all wrongdoing. No criminal charges were filed and every civil case except one was settled.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN STEFANSKI, HEAD COACH, \uc2 CLEVELAND BROWNS\uc2 : He wants to be the best version of himself. I believe that wholeheartedly.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Watson and the players union have previously stated they don`t plan to appeal the decision and would like the \uc2 NFL\uc2  to do the same. Sam Brock, NBC News Miami.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, abortion on the ballot in Kansas. Voters are about to decide whether it should be restricted in the state in the first vote of its kind after the supreme court overturn Roe v. Wade. Why so many Americans are watching it closely.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Just hours from now, voters in Kansas will go to the polls with abortion on the ballot. It`s a critical task for abortion access at the state level after the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade. Here`s Dasha Burns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This Planned Parenthood is one of just five abortion clinics in Kansas, one of the few left in the entire region.\par \par \uc2 EMLY WALES, CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD GREAT PLAINS: We`re trying to really explain to Kansans we have seen what the crisis looks like. We know what patients faces look like when they realize it`s going to be days on the road. And we`re hoping that Kansans take steps to avoid that crisis here.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: On Tuesday, Kansas will be the first state to vote on abortion since the recent Supreme Court decision. Right now, the state constitution protects abortion rights here, but on the ballot is the value them both amendment which if passed would open the door to restrictions or a ban.\par \par \uc2 For months, volunteers have been knocking on doors.\par \par \uc2 Kansans have been rallying throughout the state, including here at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Activists even holding concerts to get voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Recent polling shows 47 percent support the amendment and 43 percent opposed, the rest undecided. Now, a final push from both sides.\par \par \uc2 This is your first time really canvassing, during the whole --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. Well, I love the pro-life movement my whole life.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What`s at stake here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The right to choose.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Abortion rights activist believe if the amendment passes, an abortion ban would be inevitable while many of those in favor of the change deny that a ban is their goal.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN HUMPHRIES, KANSAS STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We are putting this decision in the hands of the people.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: If passed, the conservative state legislature including representative Susan Humphries would determine the future of abortion in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 HUMPHRIES: And we`ll see what happens. I honestly don`t know I haven`t talked to people about it. I don`t really know what`s next.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Why don`t you talk to people about it? It`s a huge issue. Why haven`t you had that conversation?\par \par \uc2 HUMPHRIES: Because we`re really focused on August 2nd.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A day and a decision putting physicians like Dr. Elizabeth Daly on edge.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH DALY, DOCTOR: I cannot imagine not being able to provide people health care. For me, that would -- that would be really, really hard to go through.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: You`re getting emotional just thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes, I don`t want it to stop on my watch.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The nation now watching the state that`s become a bellwether. Dasha Burns, NBC News, Kansas City, Kansas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us tonight, the American journalist missing in Syria for a decade. My1 exclusive interview with his parents, their message to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: This month marks 10 years since American freelance journalist Austin Tice disappeared in Syria while covering the civil war there. Recently, I met up again with his parents in Texas, as that heartbreaking anniversary approaches. The family with an urgent new plea tonight for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DEBRA TICE, MOTHER OF AUSTIN TICE: He`s the longest-held American.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s been a devastating decade for Debra and Marc Tice. 10 long years without any word from their son Austin who went missing and Syria.\par \par \uc2 Mar and Debra, I`ve been speaking to you for years and you have never ever wavered on your belief that Austin is coming home. What sustains you?\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: I must say my faith.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The freelance journalist and Marine veteran was detained at a checkpoint near Damascus in August 2012.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN TICE, JOURNALIST: Oh, Jesus.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The last time he was seen came a month later when this video was posted online.\par \par \uc2 MARC TICE, FATHER OF AUSTIN TICE: We have faith that Austin is going to come home, but that is going to take some action.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Tice`s have pushed for action with three U.S. administrations.\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: I have anger with the \uc2 United States government\uc2  and frustration.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In May, they met with President Biden and his team.\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: The President gave a very specific directive. The President said, meet with the Syrians. Listen to them. Find out what they want, and work with them.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Do you think there`s any doubt within the administration that Austin is alive?\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: No, there is no doubt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: They believe he`s alive.\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 M. TICE: They did.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But three months later, no progress. And tonight, the Tice`s have a message for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: I would like to tell him, Mr. President, you need to know that no action has been taken on your clear directive.\par \par \uc2 M. TICE: The very obvious step that needs to be taken is for somebody in the \uc2 United States government\uc2  to reach out directly to officials in the Syrian government.\par \par \uc2 D. TICE: And request the meeting.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Tice`s want the U.S. to engage with Syria like they are with the Russians negotiating to bring home Americans Brittney Griner and Paul Whelan, a strategy that worked to free another Marine veteran Trevor Reed.\par \par \uc2 Does the engagement with Russia over Americans give you hope that the administration may be able to focus on others including your son in a better way?\par \par \uc2 M. TICE: Well, I mean, for me, it tells me that it`s absolutely possible for our government to interact with the Syrian government.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, the Tice family remains hopeful even as they celebrate another birthday without their son. Austin will turn 40 next week.\par \par \uc2 M. TICE: We have a kind of peace that he`s alive and waiting for us.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We reached out to the White House who responded saying they are engaged extensively with Syrian officials to try and bring Austin home but that Syria has never acknowledged holding him.\par \par \uc2 Up next tonight, honoring NBA Hall of Famer Bill Russell and his powerful legacy in civil rights.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend Bill Russell who died this weekend at age 88 is being remembered as well tonight as a champion of civil rights. Here`s Anne Thompson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bill Russell, a backbone of the world champion \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Bill Russell never back down on the court or off it. While winning an unmatched 11 \uc2 NBA\uc2  titles in 13 seasons with the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2 , he used his fame and stature to fight for civil rights.\par \par \uc2 BILL RUSSEL, NBA HALL OF FAMER: I did what I thought I could do to have changed it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ROBINSON, FORMER PROFESSIONAL BASKETBALL PLAYER: It is very easy to hide your head to say that, you know, when you -- when you make money and things are going well for you, you don`t want to risk it. But he was willing to put everything on the line to use his position to move our society forward.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: He led a walkout of Black Celtics players when they were refused service at a Kentucky restaurant in 1961, joined Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . in the March on Washington, supported Muhammad Ali, then called Cassius Clay.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nine top Negro athletes meet with Cassius Clay to discuss his anti-draft stand.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: His activism so impressing Jackie Robinson, Russell was asked to be a pallbearer at the baseball legends funeral. Boston in the 50s and 60s, Russell described, as a flea market of racism. The star was taunted and heckled, his home vandalized. Four decades later --\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Bill Russell, the man is someone who stood up for the rights and dignity of all men.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: President Obama giving Russell the Presidential Medal of Freedom and a thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL: He just said that it was guys like me that made it possible for him to be president.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Today in Boston, there is a statue of Russell not at the Boston Garden, but in City Hall Plaza, a champion for all. Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220802ei8100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 What Providers In Some States Are Now Doing Because Of The Surge In Women Traveling In From States Where Abortion Is Illegal?\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 706 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Back now with a fight over abortion and the differing laws often between neighboring states. Indiana lawmakers convened a special session today to consider a near-total ban. While next door in Illinois, a small town clinic has become the only option for many women looking for services. Kristen Dahlgren takes us there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hope clinic, how may I help you?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At Granite City Illinois` Hope Clinic for Women --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are booked out to August.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Calls have tripled since the Dobbs decision. Illinois now surrounded by a growing number of states where the procedure is illegal or restricted.\par \par \uc2 DR. ERIN KING, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, HOPE CLINIC FOR WOMEN: We have patients from Texas traveling nine, 10 hours by car.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: With Missouri just across the river, they were used to treating patients from outside Illinois, but in the last three weeks, scheduled women from at least 16 different states. Now, the clinic says 86 percent of its patients come from out of state.\par \par \uc2 KING: We`ve increased the number of nurses, clinical staff, patient educators, and really importantly front desk staff.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: They help patients navigate what has become a confusing landscape of laws across state lines.\par \par \uc2 KING: They`re worried that it`s illegal where we are, so it`s taking a lot of reassurance. We`re also seeing a lot of relief. Kind of this -- It`s like, I finally got here.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: There are also organizations that help with logistics and the cost of travel. Something Missouri State Representative Mary Elizabeth Coleman wants to see penalized in her state.\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN, MISSOURI REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE: That activity crosses a line from I`m just talking about it with you to I`m actually helping you carry out an illegal act.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But if the activity is legal in Illinois, you still think it should be illegal to book an appointment from Missouri?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: For the business too. It`s -- they`re running basically shadow clinics in the state of Missouri. And that`s what I take issue with.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Crossing borders for abortion services isn`t new. Last year, Laurie Brown Loftus was 20 weeks pregnant when doctors saw severe abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE BROWN LOFTUS, HAD ABORTION: Most babies that have this disorder either die during childbirth or shortly after.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Her family went from posting a baby announcement to making a choice.\par \par \uc2 LOFTUS: I could, you know, kind of carry to term -- sorry.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Or she could travel eight hours from Oklahoma to New Mexico for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 LOFTUS: To not be able to just do something like that here was really, really difficult.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Back in Granite City --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL PARKINSON, MAYOR OF GRANITE CITY, ILLINOIS: My only job as the mayor of the city is to make sure that everybody stays safe, those that are -- that are coming to the clinic, but also for the other side of it that protest.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: As states and small towns now navigate the changing landscape. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News Granite City, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us, what the week ahead could mean for homebuyer shoppers and just about all of us facing an uncertain economy. And the most unexpected catch of the day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A pretty stunning scene off Plymouth, Massachusetts as a large whale breached and landed on a small fishing boat briefly submerging the bow of the boat, but remarkably causing no injuries or major damage. Officials said an abundance of bait fish brought many fishing boats to the area but also attracted whales.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220728ei7p00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for July 25, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Miguel Almaguer, Peter Alexander, Blayne Alexander,  Anne Thompson, Morgan Chesky, Kristen Dahlgren, Tom Costello, Joe Fryer \par \uc2 3,846 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Breaking news tonight, the rapidly spreading fire destroying several homes in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The image is just coming in. The flames engulfing multiple homes in a neighborhood just outside Dallas. Officials ordering evacuations. It comes as another fire in California explodes into that state`s biggest inferno this year. And the brutal heat 37 million under alerts.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the shooting inside Dallas Love Field Airport. A woman opening fire with a handgun. Terrified travelers taking cover behind seats. How police took the shooter down.\par \par \uc2 President Biden making his first appearance in days since his COVID diagnosis saying he`s feeling great and he hopes to fully get back to work. The new evidence revealed by the January 6 Committee. Donald Trump`s handwritten edits to his remarks the day after the attack. What the then- President did not want to say to the rioters.\par \par \uc2 The historic apology from Pope Francis on his visit to North America. His plea for forgiveness to indigenous people.\par \par \uc2 The global health emergency declared over the growing monkeypox outbreak. Inside the CDC response. Why it believes the virus is spreading faster than before.\par \par \uc2 And celebrating the tough guy actor beloved for his roles on both sides of the law.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening. In a summer of extreme temperatures, the worst wildfire of the year is choking the skies of Northern California tonight. Smoke advisories posted in the Bay Area due to a rampaging wildfire more than 175 miles away near Yosemite. It`s already sent thousands in the path of the fire to seek safer ground.\par \par \uc2 The Oak fire broke out Friday near the park and has destroyed several structures. And breaking in Texas tonight, grass fires have destroyed at least eight homes in Balch Springs and forced evacuations there. Meantime, back in California, crews doing all they can to slow that big blaze before it makes another dangerous run.\par \par \uc2 Drought conditions ensuring plentiful fuel for the fire. Miguel Almaguer is on the front lines for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This is what California`s wildfire season looks and sounds like now. Exploding into the largest inferno in the state this year, the Oak fire burning near Yosemite is also the most volatile blaze of the season torching everything in its path.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY MCGUIRE, WILDFIRE VICTIM: I appreciate how that`s (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s OK.\par \par \uc2 MCGUIRE: I`m still haven`t absorbed this.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Even with 2000 firefighters on the ground, the Oak Fire is raging out of control, destroying at least 10 structures including newlywed Steve and Andrea Ward`s home which erupted into a fireball.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She`s looking over my shoulder. And you know, this home that we had just gotten married at two weeks ago, it explodes.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With 3000 people forced to evacuate, 3200 structures still lie in the path of the fire.\par \par \uc2 As the blade breaks off and moves in multiple directions, firefighters are having a tough time staying in front of it. So, now they`re using the help of the air attack.\par \par \uc2 But even a steady line of fire retardant isn`t stopping the blaze. Visible from outer space, the suffocating blanket of smoke has now drifted hundreds of miles choking the skies near the Bay Area. It comes amid deadly temperatures blanketing the country. 37 million under heat alerts. But it`s the Pacific Northwest that could see temperatures rise as high as 115 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Back on the fire line, some good news. The inferno is moving towards burn scars like these where there`s little fuel for flames.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know, it`s just stuff is the thing. It`s just stuff. But it`s still amazing to see what survives.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: For some, the damage is already done. California`s most destructive fire of the year certainly won`t be the last.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Miguel joining us now from the footprint of the fire. Miguel, have crews made much progress today?\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Well, Lester, crews are making progress. This blaze is about 10 percent contained. But as you can see here, the damage is extensive. When the fire tore through this area, it was moving so fast and burning so hot, it literally incinerated everything in a matter of minutes. The people who live in this neighborhood say they will not rebuild Old they don`t have fire insurance. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yes, awful scene there. Miguel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tense moments at Love Field in Dallas today when a woman opened fire at the airport sending passengers running for cover. Police say the woman had been dropped off, went into a restroom, and when she emerged, began firing hitting the ceiling. She was shot and wounded by a police officer and arrested. No one else was injured.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s health is on the rebound tonight. His doctor is saying his COVID symptoms are almost completely resolved. The President working in isolation while slamming his predecessor. Peter Alexander now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): President Biden tonight on the back end of his bout with COVID.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m feeling good. My voice is still raspy. I`ve had every morning, every afternoon -- I mean, actually, every evening, I get a full-blown series of test.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: His doctor writing but his symptoms have almost completely resolved. The President saying he hopes to return to work in person by the end of this week. It comes as his predecessor, former President Trump, is set to return to Washington tomorrow for the first time since he left the White House.\par \par \uc2 Today, a member of the January 6 committee releasing new evidence showing Mr. Trump resisted some of the sharpest criticism had drafted the remarks he was to give the day after the attack, including crossing outlines calling for the rioters to be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you recognize the handwriting?\par \par \uc2 IVANKA TRUMP, FORMER WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: It looks like my father`s handwriting.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The Committee says Mr. Trump deleted a key graph that read: I am directing the Department of Justice to ensure all lawbreakers are prosecuted to the fullest extent of the law. We must send a clear message not with mercy but with justice. Legal consequences must be swift and firm.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you know why he wanted that crossed out?\par \par \uc2 JARED KUSHNER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: I don`t know. Mr. Trump did still criticize the rioters in his speech.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And to those who broke the law, you will pay.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden tonight and a speech to Black Law Enforcement executives, slamming Mr. Trump`s inaction on January 6.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The police were heroes that day. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lacked the courage to act. You can`t be pro-insurrection and pro-cop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Also tonight, we`ve learned that Marc Short, former Vice President Pence`s chief of staff, testified before a federal grand jury investigating the events surrounding the Capitol attack, making him the highest-ranking Trump official known so far to have cooperated with the Justice Department`s investigation. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexandra, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, I`ll discuss the January 6 attack, the investigation, and much more in an exclusive interview with Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, growing concern about monkeypox with the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  declaring a global health emergency. Blayne Alexander has exclusive reporting inside the CDC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): As calls to strengthen the nation`s monkeypox response grow louder and U.S. cases skyrocket from 1400 less than two weeks ago to nearly 3000 today, the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has declared the virus of global health emergency. Dr. Jennifer McQuiston is leading the CDC his response, how big is the CDC response to this virus?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER MCQUISTON, DEPUTY DIRECTOR, DIVISION OF HIGH CONSEQUENCE PATHOGENS AND PATHOLOGY, CDC: Right now, we have over 300 people who are working on this response 24/7.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Rarely fatal, the virus is marked by painful lesions and sores spread through close physical contact. And while monkeypox is not new, the rapid spread is.\par \par \uc2 What`s causing it now to spread in a different way than it has before?\par \par \uc2 MCQUISTON: We`re coming out of COVID, right? It`s been two years that people not traveling, not attending parties, not having fun. And I think there`s some amplification of this that`s happened as a result of people going out living their lives.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And that spread, she says goes beyond just physical contact from respiratory droplets.\par \par \uc2 MCQUISTON: If somebody has lesions inside their mouth or throat, they could potentially shed virus.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: To sharing things like clothing, towels or blankets with someone with an open sore. The vast majority of cases are spreading among men in the LGBTQ community.\par \par \uc2 How important is it though that people outside of that community also don`t close their eyes to this virus?\par \par \uc2 MCQUISTON: That`s really important. As we know throughout history, that infectious diseases don`t stay in only one population. So, perhaps it`s been magnified early on in this population. But it could potentially spread over into other populations.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Two of the latest cases, in children. A cornerstone of the CDC is response, more testing and making more vaccines available, all while still managing COVID.\par \par \uc2 MCQUISTON: There are only so many staff members at CDC. So, when we stood up the monkey pox response, we had to pull people out of COVID.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: She says, if you think you`ve been exposed, isolate, then call a doctor and get tested.\par \par \uc2 MCQUISTON: We`d like to see the curve not keep going up but flatten out and then eventually start to go down. So, that would be my hope in the next three to six months.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And that`s realistic if people take precautions now.\par \par \uc2 MCQUISTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Blayne Alexander, NBC News Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: An extraordinary apology by Pope Francis in Canada asking for forgiveness from its indigenous people for what he called the evil committed by so many Christians at church-run residential schools. Anne Thompson is there.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): On the Canadian prairie, powerful words from Pope Francis.\par \par \uc2 I am sorry, I asked forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 Francis applauded as he spoke near former residential school where indigenous children were abused by Catholic priests and nuns. Forced by the government to attend, the children stripped of their cultures and identities. Policies the pope called catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like to be back here?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPHINE SMALLS, STUDIED AT ERMINESKIN SCHOOL: The memories. There`s a lot of little spirits.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Josephine Smalls went to the Ermineskin School the pope visited. The gymnasium is all that remains.\par \par \uc2 SMALLS: There was a lot of abuse.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Smalls was physically and sexually abused at the school. Tara Carey Hall and Deborah Parker are daughters of abuse victims. The Pope praying today at the graves of some 6000 children who died.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our people have called for the releasing of the records and also the repatriation of those babies. Bring them home.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Ancestors to be remembered even in our interview. Why is that chair here?\par \par \uc2 SMALL: For the ones that kept me here and for the ones that suffered the most that still to this day cannot even talk about it.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Francis initially apologized at the Vatican in April. Father Susai Jesu was there.\par \par \uc2 What difference does it make that it happens in Canada?\par \par \uc2 SUSAI JESU, CATHOLIC PRIEST: People are going to be opening their heart for reconciliation and healing because he is going to be speaking on soil of this land.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: France is welcomed with a headdress today, but not everyone is ready to forgive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Say I`m sorry but show you`re sorry.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: There will be no instant healing. The Pope says this is a time to remember, grieve, and to ask forgiveness. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Anne Thompson tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This evening we`re hearing from the grieving parents of one of two Americans killed recently in battle in Ukraine while there is new fallout from Russia`s attack on a critical port city. Morgan Chesky is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In eastern Ukraine tonight, fierce frontline fighting, leaving two American families in mourning. A Ukrainian commander sharing over the weekend that two Americans, including Luke Lucyszyn, died in battle after traveling here on their own to join the war effort.\par \par \uc2 KATHY LUCYSZYN, MOTHER OF LUKE LUCYSZYN: My heart can`t be heavier now.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Kathy and George Lucyszyn describing their son Luke as a loving father who went abroad wanting to help and found work as a medic.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE LUCYSZYN, FATHER OF LUKE LUCYSZYN: Every time I talked to him, I told him why don`t you just come home?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: The parents say Luke was knocked unconscious by an artillery blast. Then his fellow fighters rushed to help, a Russian tank open fire.\par \par \uc2 K. LUCYSZYN: He didn`t go there to be a hero. He went he went there because he wanted to help people.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Meanwhile, in the port city of Odessa, smoke-filled skies after a Russian missile strike. The attack coming after Russia agreed to end its blockade and allow Ukrainian ships to deliver vital grain, more than 20 million tons that risk of rotting in ships right now.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Russia`s Foreign Minister says their target was strictly military and the deal is still on. But Ukraine`s Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Iryna Vereshchuk\uc2  is doubtful.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any faith that this grain, this food will make it to these countries who are in dire need?\par \par \uc2 IRYNA VERESHCHUK, VICE PRIME MINISTER (through translator): Russia showed to everyone Russia would fire at any corridor at any time whenever it wishes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: And tonight, a senior federal officials says if Russia does not comply with this \uc2 U.N\uc2 . agreement, the Biden administration is working on a plan B to transport Ukraine`s grain and other products out of the country and get it to those who need it most. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Morgan Chesky in Ukraine, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the shifting landscape of abortion. What providers in some states are now doing because of the surge in women traveling in from states where abortion is illegal?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with a fight over abortion and the differing laws often between neighboring states. Indiana lawmakers convened a special session today to consider a near-total ban. While next door in Illinois, a small town clinic has become the only option for many women looking for services. Kristen Dahlgren takes us there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hope clinic, how may I help you?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At Granite City Illinois` Hope Clinic for Women --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are booked out to August.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Calls have tripled since the Dobbs decision. Illinois now surrounded by a growing number of states where the procedure is illegal or restricted.\par \par \uc2 DR. ERIN KING, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, HOPE CLINIC FOR WOMEN: We have patients from Texas traveling nine, 10 hours by car.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: With Missouri just across the river, they were used to treating patients from outside Illinois, but in the last three weeks, scheduled women from at least 16 different states. Now, the clinic says 86 percent of its patients come from out of state.\par \par \uc2 KING: We`ve increased the number of nurses, clinical staff, patient educators, and really importantly front desk staff.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: They help patients navigate what has become a confusing landscape of laws across state lines.\par \par \uc2 KING: They`re worried that it`s illegal where we are, so it`s taking a lot of reassurance. We`re also seeing a lot of relief. Kind of this -- It`s like, I finally got here.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: There are also organizations that help with logistics and the cost of travel. Something Missouri State Representative Mary Elizabeth Coleman wants to see penalized in her state.\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN, MISSOURI REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE: That activity crosses a line from I`m just talking about it with you to I`m actually helping you carry out an illegal act.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But if the activity is legal in Illinois, you still think it should be illegal to book an appointment from Missouri?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: For the business too. It`s -- they`re running basically shadow clinics in the state of Missouri. And that`s what I take issue with.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Crossing borders for abortion services isn`t new. Last year, Laurie Brown Loftus was 20 weeks pregnant when doctors saw severe abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE BROWN LOFTUS, HAD ABORTION: Most babies that have this disorder either die during childbirth or shortly after.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Her family went from posting a baby announcement to making a choice.\par \par \uc2 LOFTUS: I could, you know, kind of carry to term -- sorry.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Or she could travel eight hours from Oklahoma to New Mexico for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 LOFTUS: To not be able to just do something like that here was really, really difficult.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Back in Granite City --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL PARKINSON, MAYOR OF GRANITE CITY, ILLINOIS: My only job as the mayor of the city is to make sure that everybody stays safe, those that are -- that are coming to the clinic, but also for the other side of it that protest.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: As states and small towns now navigate the changing landscape. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News Granite City, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us, what the week ahead could mean for homebuyer shoppers and just about all of us facing an uncertain economy. And the most unexpected catch of the day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A pretty stunning scene off Plymouth, Massachusetts as a large whale breached and landed on a small fishing boat briefly submerging the bow of the boat, but remarkably causing no injuries or major damage. Officials said an abundance of bait fish brought many fishing boats to the area but also attracted whales.\par \par \uc2 And this week, we are going to learn a lot more about the health of our economy like whether we`re on the brink of a recession as the Fed is poised to act again to try and slow the unrelenting rise in the price of so many things we buy. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Washington and across the country, high stakes over four-decade high inflation up nine percent year over year. In Illinois, mother of three and special ed teacher Ellie Alvarado says it`s taking a financial and emotional toll. Clothing up five percent, gas up nearly 60 percent, food prices up 10.5 percent, forcing them to cut back.\par \par \uc2 Ellie Alvarado, MOTHER OF THREE: And now, it`s back to kind of paycheck to paycheck and watching to make sure we`re not going into overdraft.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: At a food bank in Loudoun County, Virginia, so many families are asking for help. They ran out of food after 90 minutes today.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MONTGOMERY, CEO, LOUDOUN COUNTY RELIEF: We are seeing 20 to 25 new families each week that are coming in that are new to us that have never gotten our services before.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile, a new Triple-A poll finds 88 percent of those asked are cutting back on driving due to higher gas prices. While prices have recently dropped, they`re still averaging $4.36 a gallon nationwide. All of it adding pressure on the Fed expected to raise interest rates another three-quarters of a percentage point this week just as second quarter GDP numbers could signal at the economy shrank for two quarters in a row, a common definition for a recession.\par \par \uc2 But Americans are still spending and employers are still hiring. President Biden says a recession is not a done deal.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: God willing. I don`t think we`re going to see recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Financial Planners still suggest paying down debt now since interest rates are likely to keep climbing through this year with the rate cut, not likely until next year at the earliest. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Tom, thanks. Up next, Paul Sorvino, a Hollywood tough guy and so much more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: On the outside, Paul Sorvino looked like he could be a favorite uncle, but in certain roles, he was a force to be reckoned with. Joe Fryer now on the passing of the beloved actor at 83.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Paul Sorvino played characters on both sides of the law from detective Phil Cerreta on NBC`s law and order --\par \par \uc2 PAUL SORVINO, ACTOR: They don`t sell hot guns at Brooks Brothers.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: To mob boss Paulie Ciceroin Goodfellas.\par \par \uc2 SORVINO: Don`t make a joke out of me. Just don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 People come up to me every day, every day. I swear to you, and tell me it`s their favorite movie. It touched a chord in everyone.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: It was a career that spanned half a century was Sorvino gracing not just the screen but also the stage. In 1973, he earned a Tony nomination for the play that championship season. At six foot three, the actor`s range was as broad as his shoulders, speaking Shakespearean and Baz Luhrmann is Romeo and Juliet. Even singing opera. He was a talented tenor.\par \par \uc2 When his daughter Mira Sorvino won an Oscar in 1996, her proud dad was there in the audience.\par \par \uc2 MIRA SORVINO, ACTRESS: When you give me this award, you honor my father, Paul Sorvino, who has taught me everything I know about acting. I love you very much, dad.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Today, Mira is honoring him again. "He was the most wonderful father. I love him so much. I`m sending you love in the stars, dad, as you ascend. Joe Fryer, NBC New.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220726ei7p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A 27-Year-Old Man Facing Charges For The Rape Of A 10-Year-Old Girl Who Had To Travel To Another State To Get An Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Now, to another major story that shining a light on the deep divide in this country over abortion. A 27-year-old man facing charges for the rape of a 10-year-old girl who had to travel to another state to get an abortion. Now, after many conservatives cast doubt on her story, they`re walking it back. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At an arraignment Wednesday in Columbus, Ohio, a judge ordered 27-year-old rape suspect Gerson Fuentes held on $2 million bail.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL MEYER, ASSISTANT PROSECUTOR, FRANKLIN COUNTY: It`s my understanding she in fact just turned 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:02]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: According to court documents, Fuentes confessed to raping and impregnating a 10-year-old girl. A detective says she then traveled from Ohio which has banned most abortions to neighboring Indiana to end the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The case has become the latest flashpoint in the national abortion debate following the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe v. Wade. It was first reported earlier this month by the Indianapolis Star newspaper citing a single source, an OBGYN in Indianapolis. The story went viral. Last week, President Biden brought it up.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Imagine being that little girl. Just -- I`m serious. Just imagine being that little girl, 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But over the next several days, Republicans including Ohio`s Attorney General cast doubt on the store.\par \par \uc2 DAVE YOST\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL, OHIO: We have regular contact with prosecutors and local police and sheriff`s not a whisper anywhere.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Then, yesterday, after the suspect`s arrest, he issued a written statement. My heart aches for the pain suffered by this young child. I am grateful for the diligent work of the Columbus Police Department in securing a confession and getting a rapist off the street.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal editorial board had also previously questioned the story. Now, it`s published a new editorial correcting the record writing, it appears President Biden was accurate. Still, Indiana`s Republican attorney general says he`ll investigate the OBGYN who performed the procedure for failing to report it. That OBGYN says in part, "doctors must be able to give people the medical care they need when and where they need it."\par \par \uc2 The case now highlighting deep divisions in this country as states scrambled to clarify their own laws from supporters of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHELLE BROWN, OBGYN, NORTHWESTERN MEDICINE: It`s a hard truth to realize that these horrible things are going to happen to children and other people in your state because of the really severe restrictions that are placed.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: To those who oppose abortion.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SCHEIDLER, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PRO-LIFE ACTION LEAGUE: With so many questions about really -- what really happened, it`s a real shame that the Biden administration rushed to exploit this poor little girl`s situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And today, Republicans blocked an attempt by Senate Democrats to pass a bill to codify the right of individuals to cross state lines for an abortion. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220716ei7e0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Today Show for July 12, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Al Roker, Carson Daly, Tom Llamas,  Miguel Almaguer, Kristen Welker, Keir Simmons, Emilie Ikeda, Morgan Chesky,  Tom Costello \par \uc2 6,974 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC ANCHOR (voice-over): Good morning. "Out of the Woods," encouraging news overnight in that battle to save California`s giant sequoias from a raging wildfire. We`re live on the front lines where hundreds of firefighters are working around the clock to protect a piece of American history.\par \par \uc2 "The Heat is On." Soaring temperatures moving east with nearly 30 million people waking up to dangerous conditions, and even more on alert for severe storms. Al is tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 "Focus on Extremists." At the latest hearing today, the January 6 Committee shining a light on who was in the crowd that day, and whether President Trump`s words set them into action. We`re live with the latest from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 "Summer Surge." A new strain of COVID being called the most contagious one yet, driving a spike in cases across nearly half the country, closing summer camps and catching a lot of people by surprise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was a wake up call, right? It`s still around and it`s still disruptive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): What`s being done to slow this spread and could some areas be told to mask up all over again.\par \par \uc2 All of that plus, setting sail with vacation prices at an all time high. The one place American families can still get a great deal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have never seen cruise prices like this. And speaking of deals, it is "Prime Time." We`re going to bring you the deepest discounts and best sales from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Day.\par \par \uc2 And "Out of This World," the groundbreaking picture of our universe taken by the most powerful telescope ever made. It`s causing quite a buzz here on Earth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re seeing just a small little portion of universe.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): and giving scientists and space geeks the best look ever and galaxies far, far away. Today, Tuesday, July 12, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Good morning. Great to see you on a, what is today, Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC ANCHOR: Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Tuesday morning. That image is so surprising. It does actually --- doesn`t even look real. The more you look at it, you`re like what is that?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Absolutely beautiful. And just a little slice of our universe today, gives you instant perspective, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Sure does. We are going to start this morning with some good news in the battle to save hundreds of iconic giant sequoia trees in California`s Yosemite National Park. That raging wildfire now about 25% contained. That`s way up from yesterday at this time because at this time yesterday it was 0%.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, good progress to report there. Here`s one image. It`s taken from above the fire. It shows the size and scope, and then there on the ground below sprinklers working to preserve those trees. Big question this morning, will the weather cooperate with firefighters who are battling that blaze?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. We`ve got it all covered, including Al`s forecast that showing some severe weather threats across parts of the United States. But let us start with NBC national correspondent Miguel Almaguer. He`s on the front lines in Yosemite. Good morning, Miguel.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, guys. Good morning. The southern entrance here at Yosemite National Park remained shut down. Park officials are actually asking that we stay in this area at this hour because just down the road from here, the fire is actively burning and trees are falling into the roadway.\par \par \uc2 Now, the good news though sequoia giants, for the most part, have been undamaged. None of them have been destroyed. And as you mentioned, this fire is now about a quarter contained. Firefighters doing all they can to protect these majestic beauties.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): This morning, a race to save one of Earth`s natural treasures, extreme heat and low humidity fueling the Washburn fire in Yosemite National Park, home to hundreds of iconic towering sequoia trees.\par \par \uc2 When you see fire conditions like this, what`s the biggest threat here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re just trying to keep the fire from climbing the trees.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): More than 500 firefighters trying to protect these giants, clearing bone dry vegetation and running sprinklers to keep flames at bay.\par \par \uc2 The firefight here is literally going treetop to treetop. This crew is trying to put out hotspots before it jumps to the other side of the firebreak.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fires edge is active.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): The fire`s outer rim forcing the closure of Yosemite`s popular south entrance, and the evacuation of some 1,600 people from nearby homes and hotels. The famed Mariposa Grove is a top tourist spot for its sequoias, some of the tallest and oldest trees on the planet.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The iconic Grizzly Giant first began sprouting a thousand years BC. Sequoias here can easily stand up to 200 feet tall, higher than Italy`s Leaning Tower of Pisa. These trees long celebrated as natural wonders. During the Civil War, President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  first signed landmark legislation to protect them, Presidents Taft and Roosevelt posing in front of their massive trunks.\par \par \uc2 Although sequoias have adapted to survive flames, they`re no match for modern wildfires, driven by climate change and extreme drought. 20% of giant sequoias had been lost in the last two years alone.\par \par \uc2 BETH PRATT\uc2 , CALIFORNIA REGIONAL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, NATIONAL WILDFIRE FEDERATION: We`ve lost more giant sequoia trees in the past, I think it`s 15 years, than in history.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voice-over): Now firefighters doing all they can to save centuries of trees from this growing inferno.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, Miguel, it`s nice to see that firefighters are making progress but they do still have concerns. What`s their biggest concern this morning?\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Well, one of the piece of good news out here is they`ve been doing prescribed burns in this general area for years to exactly do this, protect those giant sequoias from fires that are part of nature out here. But they`ve also have a bark beetle infestation, which has killed millions of trees across Northern and Southern California. That is fueling the fire, so those two items are keeping firefighters on their toes here. They are making progress but there remains some threat here in Yosemite Valley. Hoda, back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Miguel Almaguer for us there. Miguel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, weather is going to be the biggest factor out there. Al, we turn to you. What are we looking at?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Well, take a look. This is the plume. The smoke forecast from the Washburn fire. This thing extends like 200 miles. And the big problem is the humidity levels. It`s bone dry, 15% to 25%. The winds, not bad, 5 to 15 mph, but hot dry conditions to this week, so heavy smoke and poor air quality in that region.\par \par \uc2 Plus the heat, 36 million of us stretching from the northwest all the way into Texas and parts of the Lower Mississippi River Valley for heat advisories and heat warnings. Today, triple digit temperatures, air temperatures, Dallas, Memphis. We`re looking at Huston. But the heat index is triple digits there, all the way to Raleigh Durham, 98. It`s going to feel like 103 in Jacksonville.\par \par \uc2 H