 It is an epoch defining change.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): Some sample itineraries, a five day Carnival cruise from Tampa to the Caribbean for $56 a day, a five day Royal Caribbean cruise from Galveston to Costa Maya and Cozumel for $60 a day, or a week long celebrity cruise from Fort Lauderdale to Key West, Belize, Mexico and the Bahamas for $84 a day.\par \par \uc2 Californian Edward O`Donnell goes on a retreat in Solvang every year. But given the gas prices in the state right now.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD O`DONNELL, CALIFORNIAN: It`s cheaper to go on a cruise so I did it.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): His four day Carnival cruise to Catalina, and Ensenada cost $139 plus taxes and fees. He says he saved a minimum of $350.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If I had used other services on the ship, it would have been higher but I didn`t go to the specialty restaurants, I don`t drink anymore.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): But will these deep discounts be enough to lure regular cruisers back to the open seas? Travel expert Mark Ellwood says the industry is still combating a big COVID image problem dating back to the early days of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MARK ELLWOOD, TRAVEL EXPERT: There were lots of problems about the way they operated the ships. They have changed those. The air circulation on board has been improved, the protocols have been improved.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): Helping ease concerns, many companies require COVID vaccines for adults and most kids, and the experience is centered on being outside likely on a pool deck.\par \par \uc2 TYNAN S: The cruise business was dead essentially. Now every month is a new high point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Sam, we love that store. I think a lot of people are Googling cruise discounts right now. So, what`s the secret of scoring a great deal?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Maybe the most important tip that I can pass along, Tom, is to work with a travel agent. I know some people maybe historically don`t do that, they do generally charge a commission but it is worth your while in that respect because they have special deals that only they have access to for group rates. Also look out for taxes and fees, a lot of times those are not advertised on the rates on the websites, and then track prices after booking because especially in this environment right now, a cruise company is more than likely to give you the better rate if you find one after you booked, and then moving forward you`ll save bucks that way as well.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Sam, great tips for us. We appreciate that. I see cruising in the future for some reason.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I choose you.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: I don`t know. I see it, something big.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s happening.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Something I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I think we should all go on a cruise.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Live from the Lido Deck.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, exactly. I`m imagining Craig and Al, like Titanic style.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know, at the edge of the -- not going down.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I was going to say this, that didn`t end well.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No. I know. Remember that beautiful shot with Rose --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes I do.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- and Jack.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: But not so much for Jack.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, yes. Just the good part. The romance, the lovely part. My heart will go on.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No? OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What do we have?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We`re going down with the shirt.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We have a lot to --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up tomorrow on Today.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Gosh, even if you`re not planning a vacation, we are going to save you some money. We`re going to wrap it up the top summer grills. Hot prices, a gas grill under $200, a portable grill to take with you anywhere perhaps on your cruise, even a cool gadget. It`ll turn your grill into a pizza oven plus, the pro`s secret weapon, a cooking thermometer that makes all the difference.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Also tomorrow, Craig grills one of the most sought after ever filmmakers in Hollywood about the mysterious new movie. He sat down with Oscar winner Jordan Peele to talk about his film "Nope." This thing has been shrouded in secrecy and people can`t see it. But he`s going to get to the bottom of it. That`s all coming up tomorrow on Today.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s going to be good. Let`s check in with Al now, checking the forecast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hey.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: OK. Hey, guys. Well, you know, \uc2 NOAA\uc2  released its $20 billion disasters. So far this year, we`ve had nine events, a couple of tornadoes (inaudible), severe weather through the plains, the Western drought and the plains drought. So far this year, this is the disaster count is the fifth highest on record to this date. And the last two years, 20 plus billion dollar events in both 2021-2022. That`s the most on record.\par \par \uc2 We are watching an area right now down through the gulf with a 30% chance of developing from Galveston, New Orleans on into the panhandle of Florida, slow development possible. Weather becomes a system or not, it is going to dump a ton of rain down through the gulf. Locally, anywhere from three to five inches could be some flash flooding. For today, severe storms later here in the northeast and some gully washers in the southwest.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that`s your weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Al, thank you. Coming up next in Today`s Can`t Miss, a photo billions of years in the making. Tom Costello on the picture that has \uc2 NASA\uc2  buzzing. Hi, Tom.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC CORRESPONDENT: 4.6 billion light years ago and Captain Kirk must be out there somewhere. We`re looking at what our universe looked like 4.6 billion light years ago where no one has gone before when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:51:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back with Today`s Can`t Miss, and we are getting a stunning brand new view, very first time, on just how big our universe is. So \uc2 NASA\uc2  is unveiling the first full color image from the James Webb Space Telescope. It`s the most powerful, the largest telescope, the most expensive telescope ever launched into space.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: They call it the Gucci telescope. NBC`s Tom Costello joins us now with this exciting event. This is years in the making. And by years, we mean like billions of years. Right, Tom?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yes, that`s right. Space geeks, you have an excuse to geek out this morning. These are the highest deepest resolution, infrared resolution images ever of our universe. So take a look what we`re talking about here. Last night, \uc2 NASA\uc2  and the White House unveiling the first cosmic image, it shows a kaleidoscope of galaxies as they appeared 4.6 billion years ago, light years ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re looking at images now and that astronomer Neil deGrasse Tyson says, if you look at the spiked objects, those are local stars. Everything else out there is a galaxy, thousands of them. Many actually distort into arcs. And \uc2 NASA\uc2 , this morning, releasing even more images on a live video stream one by one. They`re going to show and they are showing a full color spectrum of an exoplanet, the life cycle of stars, how galaxies interact.\par \par \uc2 And as we mentioned, the Webb telescope uses infrared eyes to pull these faint and very distant objects like stars and galaxies into sharp focus. The question is, will it discover signs of life out there? We`ll take a look and look for all of it over the coming hours. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Tom is so happy.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s really cool, Tom.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC ANCHOR: I`m just going to do it. I`m going to run the risk of having my Jessica Simpson chicken of the sea moment here. 4.6 billion years ago --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Five seconds.\par \par \uc2 DALY: -- that`s what it looked like, because it looked like that right now.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We don`t know. We won`t know for another --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`ll know in 4.6 billion. We`re back after this.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I love it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:07]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220712ei7c000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 What The Biden Administration Is Telling Hospitals They Must Do Under Federal Law, Even In States That Ban It\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: The Biden administration is telling hospitals that doctors must perform abortions if the life of a mother is at stake. It comes as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  considers a request to make birth control pills available for sale over the counter. Stephanie Gosk joins me.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, starting with the move by the administration on emergency care, what could that mean?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, today, the Biden administration reminded hospitals that an existing federal law requires them to provide abortions if the life of the mother is at risk, even in states where the procedure is now banned.\par \par \uc2 It says that federal law preempts state laws. Many of which have recently changed. This as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  considers making birth control pills available over the counter for the first time ever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): When the birth control pill was first approved, it ushered in a sexual revolution.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Birth control, especially the pill provided women the choice.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: More than 60 years later, a French company is asking the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to approve an over-the-counter version at a very different time in America`s history, just weeks after the overturning of Roe v. Wade. In that decision, the Supreme Court said the right to contraception would not be affected. But in a concurring opinion, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  suggested it should be.\par \par \uc2 HRA Pharma says it started the \uc2 FDA\uc2  application process seven years ago and the timing is coincidental.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it`s possible that women may go to the doctor less because they no longer need to go to the doctor for the pill?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER VILLAVICENCIO, \uc2 AMERICAN COLLEGE OF OBSTETRICIANS AND GYNECOLOGISTS\uc2 : There is some concern that people only end up going to the doctor because they need birth control. It`s not right to hold or control hostage by a prescription when it is not scientifically necessary just so that we can get people in to see us.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: It comes as the Biden administration faces increased pressure to take action on reproductive rights issues, including potentially declaring a public health emergency over abortion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s something I`ve asked the folks on the medical people on the administration to look at, do I have the authority to do that and what impact that would have?\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But the administration says it will let the \uc2 FDA\uc2  dictate the timeline. The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  is hopeful.\par \par \uc2 VILLAVICENCIO: Over-the-counter birth control is going to be helpful for everyone. But it certainly is going to be helpful for those who are most marginalized in our country.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A major hurdle to getting birth control pills could soon be a thing of the past. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back in a moment with an image that could change the way we see our universe.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: President Biden helped unveil today one of the first-ever full-color images from NASA`s James Webb Space Telescope. The picture shows the deepest, sharpest infrared view of the universe to date taken by the most powerful space telescope ever built. \uc2 NASA\uc2  is expected to release more images tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And some sad news tonight. Monty Norman, the composer of the iconic theme for the James Bond films has died.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Norman was hired to write the score for the first Bond film Dr. No. And the theme has become synonymous with the legendary character ever since. He was 94.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a grandmother taking on a summer job to protect her community and a beloved summer tradition.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220713ei7b000p5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 11, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Miguel Almaguer, Al Roker, Ali Vitali, Kelly  O`Donnell, Janis Mackey Frayer, Ellison Barber, Stephanie Gosk, Ron Allen \par \uc2 3,896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the race against time to save Yosemite historic trees from a raging wildfire.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Fire crews hauling in sprinklers to keep the rapidly spreading fire away from your Yosemite`s Giant Sequoias. Mandatory evacuations of the height of the summer season. Our team there on the ground. The flames fueled by extreme heat tens of millions on alert from the west to the Gulf Coast. Al Roker tracking it.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the judge refusing to delay Steve Bannon`s trial after the Trump ally`s about-face on testifying before the January 6 Committee. Why the Justice Department is casting doubts on his intentions.\par \par \uc2 The surprise moment at the White House. President Biden touting the new bipartisan gun law where the father of a Parkland shooting victim interrupted the speech.\par \par \uc2 And the new total spelling trouble for the president, striking number of Democrats who don`t want him to run again in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The battle over abortion. What the Biden administration is telling hospitals they must do under federal law, even in states that ban it.\par \par \uc2 Caught on camera. Gunshots ringing out at a little league game. Players and parents running for cover.\par \par \uc2 And news tonight about the musical mind behind one of the most iconic movie themes of all time.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening. Ancient history is facing an immediate and uncertain future tonight in one of the most cherished slices of America. A wildfire roaring through parts of California`s Yosemite Park threatening hundreds of giant sequoia, some more than 2000 years old. Tonight, crews literally using a network of sprinklers to create humidity around the towering trees to give them a fighting chance. Remarkable imagery revealing the spread and intensity of the fire which doubled in size over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The fire fueled by the severe drought now affecting over 97 percent of California, along with extreme heat which is not just making things difficult in the West, but extending all the way to the Gulf Coast leaving 36 million under heat alerts. Miguel Almaguer is in the fire zone tonight with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Exploding in size again overnight, Yosemite`s Washburn fire is quickly sweeping through the National Park as more than 500 firefighters lose ground against the out-of-control blaze.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re just trying to keep the fire from climbing the trees.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Torching forest land and fueled by millions of trees, flames now flank the 600 massive and iconic sequoias inside the park, a national treasure under serious threat.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT DICKMAN, BIOLOGIST: They`re incredibly fire-resilient. It`s just the fire is so different right now.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Biologist Garrett Dickman says Yosemite and California has never seen infernos like these, mega-fires fueled by extreme drought, the new normal, incinerating trees that withstood flames for thousands of years.\par \par \uc2 DICKMAN: We`ve lost 20 percent of all Giant Sequoias on Earth in the past two years alone. So, I mean, at this point, every tree counts.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Climate change is playing a role in how these fires burn. They`re now hotter, faster, and more explosive than ever before.\par \par \uc2 Those dangerous conditions creating their own weather system on the ground. The fires powerful updraft launching tree limbs hundreds of feet into the sky, narrowly missing firefighting aircraft.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A branch went right over the top of us.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tracking the fast-moving blaze from above --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fire`s edge is active.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: -- the outer rim of the swirling inferno has forced the closure of Yosemite`s popular south entrance, triggering 1600 evacuations.\par \par \uc2 CARA EXTEN, TOURIST, YOSEMITE NATIONAL PARK: The smoke was overwhelming. By the time we got down into Yosemite Valley, ash was raining down on us.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Inside Yosemite`s fame Mariposa Grove where protected sequoias are more than 2000 years old and over 300 feet high --\par \par \uc2 DICKMAN: I`m standing right in front of the grizzly giant.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: A simple sprinkler system is keeping soil wet around the grizzly giant, arguably the world`s most famous tree. Protective foil now also wraps that historic Galen Clarke cabin. But flames are within striking distance.\par \par \uc2 Crews are lining every hillside they can but this fire is quickly racing from tree to tree. In some areas, they`re simply going to let it burn.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, that burn destroying everything in its path and still threatening to uproot these iconic natural wonders that have stood tall for generations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Miguel joining me now. Miguel, protecting the massive sequoia I know was a major feat. Tonight, the outlook on that seems to be good?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:06]\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. Incredibly, not a single Sequoia has been touched by fire yet. It`s an incredible feat. I want to give you some perspective on just how massive these trees are. Let me turn to our second camera here. That`s a few 100 feet away. There are 600 of these majestic beauties across the valley floor. Not one has been lost to this fire yet.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Blaze is roughly 25 percent contained. It remains an active threat tonight. So, by no means are we out of the woods. Firefighters though here on the ground doing an incredible job. Lester, back to you.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Hats off to them for that incredible work. Miguel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are tracking that extreme heat that`s been fueling those fires. Scorching temperatures being felt by 36 million people from the west to the Gulf Coast. Al Roker joining us now. Al, what are you watching?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Lester, we`re going to start out with the heat. 36 million people from the Pacific Northwest all the way to the Mississippi River Valley for heat advisories, heat warnings. Tomorrow, triple-digit heat indexes from Little Rock, Baton Rouge, Corpus Christi, all the way to San Angelo. And that`s going to last at least one more day.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, seven million people at risk for some severe weather from Chicago, Illinois, all the way to Detroit. We`re also looking tomorrow, this moving into New England all the way down to Washington D.C. for 17 million people. We can`t rule out a tornado or two. And we got this area of disturbed weather could see upwards of 30 percent chance over the next few days of a tropical formation.\par \par \uc2 Whether it becomes a tropical system or not, it`s going to drop anywhere from three to five inches from Louisiana all the way to the panhandle of Florida. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Al Roker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll turn now to a big development of the January 6 investigation. A judge today denying a request from \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  to delay his contempt trial after the Trump insider made an about-face and said he was willing to testify. Ali Vitali is at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Top Trump ally \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  still in the legal crosshairs tonight after a judge ruled his new willingness to testify before the House January 6 Committee will not delay his trial for contempt of Congress. It comes after that stunning reversal from Bannon to cooperate with the very committee he`d previously stonewalled and ridiculed.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , TRUMP CHIEF STRATEGIST: The January 6 Committee is totally irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Federal prosecutors arguing Bannon`s about-face over the weekend was little more than an attempt to change the optics of his contempt on the eve of trial. Bannon has remained defiant while facing misdemeanor charges of up to two years in jail and steep fines if convicted. He`s pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 BANNON: This is going to be the misdemeanor from hell for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Today, the judge ruling Bannon`s team cannot subpoena the House Speaker or members of Congress. The committee is interested in Bannon`s activities in the lead-up to the sixth including these comments the day before.\par \par \uc2 BANNON: All hell is going to break loose tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Over the weekend, Bannon`s attorney saying his new position is not a change of heart but of circumstance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He said he`s willing to comply if they just resolve the privilege issue.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Bannon had argued he could not testify because former President Trump invoked executive privilege. But Saturday, Mr. Trump sent a letter to Bannon saying he was waiving that privilege because "I watched how unfairly you and others have been treated." Though the committee argues executive privilege doesn`t apply.\par \par \uc2 And now the Department of Justice citing Trump`s former lawyer saying Trump never invoked executive privilege over any information or materials. Tonight, among the sticking points over Bannon`s testimony, Bannon wants to testify live and in public, but committee members say not likely.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the committee preparing for Tuesday`s hearing focused on how the mob came to the Capitol, including a former spokesperson for the extremist group, the Oath Keeper, as a live witness. And highly anticipated new tape testimony from former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All this as a new documentary shows Mr. Trump again minimizing his supporters actions that day.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The people went to Washington primarily because they were angry with an election that they think was rigged. A very small portion, as you know, went down to the Capitol. And then a very small portion of them went in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Ali, we`re now learning the committee is again, rescheduling one of the hearings we anticipated for this week.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. The committee rescheduling a hearing we were told was set for Thursday in primetime. Instead, we`ve learned that hearing will now move to next week. A top committee member telling me tonight the changes are due to a constant influx of new information. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Ali Vitali tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At the white house this evening, a speech by President Biden on the new bipartisan gun laws suddenly interrupted by a father of a Parkland shooting victim. A surprising moment coming as the president faces growing dissatisfaction within his own party. Here`s Kelly O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Almost 2000 gathered today on the south lawn to mark the most hard-fought bipartisan achievement of the Biden era. The first new gun safety law in nearly three decades.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Lives will be saved today and tomorrow because of this.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:02]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One father whose son died at Parkland interrupted, shouting that more must be done.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let him talk. Let him talk.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The President hailed the new law as progress, as his political standing appears precarious. A new poll from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  find 64 percent of Democratic voters say they would prefer a different leader in the 2024 election. Mr. Biden`s age cited most. He`d be 82 right after the election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need some younger people in the office. That`s, you know, bottom line.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He should be next to everybody. You know, it`s kind of up there.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: However, the Vice President said Sunday, they expect to be in the race.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He intends to run. And if he does, I intend to run with him.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One clear warning, only 13 percent of Americans said they think the country is on the right track.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like we`re taking so many steps back right now.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: To counter doubters, the White House touts the strong job market and gas prices down 33 cents in the last month. Tomorrow, the President heads to Israel and Saudi Arabia, where he will push to increase oil supply to ease gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Jake Sullivan, National Security Advisor: Can we get that price down and can we keep it down?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): That new polling does offer a positive sign for the White House, showing President Biden a few points ahead of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when voters are asked about a potential rematch in 2024. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell at the White House, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight terrifying moments caught on camera at a little league game. In North Carolina, gunshots ringing out near the ball field east of Raleigh sending players and parents running for cover. The window of a van in the parking lot shot out. Police are investigating but say there`s no evidence it was an active shooter situation. No injuries were reported.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, they are still reeling from the shocking assassination of former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . A private wake held today as investigators scrutinized a church with ties to the suspect`s mother. We get more from Janis Mackey Frayer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANIS MACKEY FRAYER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, mounting questions around the shocking assassination of \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The former Prime Minister`s body arriving at a Tokyo temple for a private wake. Outside, hundreds of mourners paying respect to Japan`s longest-serving leader\par \par \uc2 I think he gave the Japanese something to be proud about, this woman said. I hope you`ll rest in peace.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  making his way to Japan sharing condolences from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We`re friends. And when a friend is hurting, the other friend show up.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: Tonight, police are investigating their own security failures. There were two shots fired. You can see Abe turn around after the first, but security doesn`t subdue the gunman who is able to fire a second time before he`s tackled. Police say the suspected gunman confessed to killing Abe and that he believed the former prime minister was tied to a religious group widely known as the Unification Church, which he says his mother was obsessed over and eventually caused her financial stress.\par \par \uc2 Investigators seized multiple weapons in a raid on his home. Several Japanese media outlets reporting he said he planned to make explosives for the attack but switched to a homemade gun instead. Still, in the wake of tragedy, Japanese voters heading to the polls, an election Abe was campaigning for when he was shot.\par \par \uc2 Leaders holding a moment of silence before announcing results. A landslide victory for Abe`s party and now a huge majority in parliament.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (on camera): A private funeral will be held for \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  on Tuesday, and officials say a public memorial service will be announced at a later time. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Janice, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It`s a race against time in Ukraine where crews are searching for survivors after an apartment building was hit by a Russian missile killing at least 33 people. As Russia gains ground in the east, attacks on civilians appear to be escalating. Ellison Barber is there for us.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the Russian military intensifying its relentless assault on civilians striking an apartment building in Kharkiv. And in eastern Donetsk, Ukrainian officials say dozens are dead after a Russian missile hit this five storey apartment building over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Rescuers diligently combing through the rubble. Families desperate for work on their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 This man is looking for his sister and nephew. He says he is waiting for a miracle. For some, miracles have come. Rescuers pulled nine people from the rubble injured but alive.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy accused Russia of targeting civilians. As Russia now gains ground in eastern Ukraine, attacks like these have forced some Ukrainians to spend their weekends learning first aid and how to spot unexploded munitions.\par \par \uc2 Come look in here. This is just one example of things that people have to now look out for across Ukraine. This is an unexploded ordinance. This one is fake but they can be real and very, very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Irinia took the class.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Unfortunately, we have to live in this reality.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you see this?\par \par \uc2 BARBER: A reality with no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER (on camera): Russia continues to claim they do not target civilians. Meanwhile, Turkey`s president spoke with President Putin on the phone today trying to convince Russia to end its blockade of Ukrainian grain which has sparked a global food crisis. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Ellison Barber in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, is birth control the new Battlefront over reproductive rights? The major move the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is considering. And the deepest image of space ever captured. You`ll see it right here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Biden administration is telling hospitals that doctors must perform abortions if the life of a mother is at stake. It comes as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  considers a request to make birth control pills available for sale over the counter. Stephanie Gosk joins me.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, starting with the move by the administration on emergency care, what could that mean?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, today, the Biden administration reminded hospitals that an existing federal law requires them to provide abortions if the life of the mother is at risk, even in states where the procedure is now banned.\par \par \uc2 It says that federal law preempts state laws. Many of which have recently changed. This as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  considers making birth control pills available over the counter for the first time ever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): When the birth control pill was first approved, it ushered in a sexual revolution.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Birth control, especially the pill provided women the choice.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: More than 60 years later, a French company is asking the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to approve an over-the-counter version at a very different time in America`s history, just weeks after the overturning of Roe v. Wade. In that decision, the Supreme Court said the right to contraception would not be affected. But in a concurring opinion, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  suggested it should be.\par \par \uc2 HRA Pharma says it started the \uc2 FDA\uc2  application process seven years ago and the timing is coincidental.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it`s possible that women may go to the doctor less because they no longer need to go to the doctor for the pill?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER VILLAVICENCIO, \uc2 AMERICAN COLLEGE OF OBSTETRICIANS AND GYNECOLOGISTS\uc2 : There is some concern that people only end up going to the doctor because they need birth control. It`s not right to hold or control hostage by a prescription when it is not scientifically necessary just so that we can get people in to see us.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: It comes as the Biden administration faces increased pressure to take action on reproductive rights issues, including potentially declaring a public health emergency over abortion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s something I`ve asked the folks on the medical people on the administration to look at, do I have the authority to do that and what impact that would have?\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But the administration says it will let the \uc2 FDA\uc2  dictate the timeline. The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  is hopeful.\par \par \uc2 VILLAVICENCIO: Over-the-counter birth control is going to be helpful for everyone. But it certainly is going to be helpful for those who are most marginalized in our country.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A major hurdle to getting birth control pills could soon be a thing of the past. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back in a moment with an image that could change the way we see our universe.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: President Biden helped unveil today one of the first-ever full-color images from NASA`s James Webb Space Telescope. The picture shows the deepest, sharpest infrared view of the universe to date taken by the most powerful space telescope ever built. \uc2 NASA\uc2  is expected to release more images tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And some sad news tonight. Monty Norman, the composer of the iconic theme for the James Bond films has died.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Norman was hired to write the score for the first Bond film Dr. No. And the theme has become synonymous with the legendary character ever since. He was 94.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a grandmother taking on a summer job to protect her community and a beloved summer tradition.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A lifeguard shortage that`s closing pools nationwide is causing some unexpected people to step up. Ron Allen reports from Philadelphia where a grandmother is spending her golden years looking after the kids in her community.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At age 70, Robin Borlandoe came out of retirement this summer hoping to save some young lives.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BORLANDOE, LIFEGUARD: Don`t play on the ropes, baby.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: A mother of three, grandmother of six, answering Philadelphia`s plea for lifeguards to open public swimming pools and help keep kids off the streets.\par \par \uc2 BORLANDOE: I was a lifeguard when I was 16 and loved it. I decided to finally do it to do something for the -- our kids, our community. They`re killing our kids. They have no place to go. The polls are closed all around.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Here in Philadelphia, at least 100 children, 17 and younger, have been shot so far this year.\par \par \uc2 BORLANDOE: I`m in the house with my family. And we hear about 14 shots.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Including just steps from Borlandoes front door.\par \par \uc2 BORLANDOE: And it was three young boys that were shot, killed. When you see it, it`s scary. It`s very sad.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: This is part of your motivation for becoming a lifeguard?\par \par \uc2 BORLANDOE: Yes, that was it. Just to do something, no matter how small, to help out.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Getting the job took determination. She failed to qualify the first time, so she took the test again and again and passed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She`s amazing. I mean, you know, what 70-year-olds do you know is a lifeguard giving back to community like that?\par \par \uc2 BORLANDOE: I`m very much committed to this. This is my reputation. This is my community.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Philly has opened 50 of its 65 pools with the help of some grandmothers like Borlandoe trying to save her city`s children. Ron Allen, NBC News, Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: What a great way to step up. We applaud her. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220712ei7b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for July 10, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Hallie Jackson, Rich Lowry \par \uc2 10,328 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:17]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, nervous Democrats.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: To say that this president has not shown urgency is just -- it`s just false.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Growing criticism from Democrats that President Biden needs to show some fight, from abortion...\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN); We want the president to absolutely crawl into every corner and say "What can we do to help?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... to guns...\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 , (D) ILLINOIS: If you`re angry today, I`m here to tell you, be angry. I`m furious.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... to the economy. Mr. Biden says his options are limited and Republicans are standing in the way.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR: That`s why my plan is not finished and why the results aren`t finished, either.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Republicans` leadership challenge.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: Get Murkowski out of here...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ... because she`s not a Republican.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How much influence does former President Trump still have on the GOP?\par \par \uc2 Maryland`s Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  joins me.\par \par \uc2 Also, former White House counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  testifies before the January 6th committee, after Cassidy Hutchinson`s explosive appearance.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER WHITE HOUSE AIDE AND ASSISTANT TO FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : Please make sure we don`t go up to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to a committee member, Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , about what Cipollone told the committee.\par \par \uc2 And tweet this, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he wants out of his $44 billion deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  over a dispute about spam accounts.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER, HOST, "PIVOT" PODCAST: He`s just reaching out for something to blame, but he has to pay...\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ll hear from Kara Swisher, co-host of Vox Media`s Pivot podcast.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson; Mark Leibovitch of The Atlantic; \uc2 Daniella Gibbs Leger\uc2  of the \uc2 Center For American Progress\uc2 ; and Rich Lowry, editor of National Review.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Good Sunday morning. The phrase you hear most often these days when frustrated Democrats talk about President Biden is "meet the moment," as in, can President Biden meet the moment?\par \par \uc2 Americans arguably elected \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for two reasons, one, because he wasn`t \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; and, two, because he was going to bring some stability and competence to the White House and calm the country down, turn down the volume, if you will.\par \par \uc2 And while Mr. Biden certainly has succeeded in not being \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , many more progressive Democrats complain that the president needs to show more passion, bang the table, raise the volume, take some chances, challenge the boundaries, in other words, meet the moment that these crises have entailed.\par \par \uc2 All the talk about Mr. Biden`s lack of passion is really code for concern about his age. So is the criticism fair? Mr. Biden has actually delivered the presidency he promised. He`s been a grown-up who plays by the rules, some think plays by the rules too well, and he does try to work with Republicans when he can, though some think he shouldn`t try so much.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats` progressive base has always wanted more, and Mr. Biden was never their first choice. So even if the criticism isn`t fair, the sentiment is real, and potential Democratic White House hopefuls are quietly and in some cases not so quietly preparing to have their moment if the president doesn`t meet his.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): What we have going on is a massive human rights violation in our country, and he`s not responding to that level.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): As many Democrats plead for a fighter, President Biden is pointing out the limits of his power.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m just stating a basic fundamental notion. The fastest way to restore Roe is to pass a national law codifying Roe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: After signing an executive order on access to abortion medication and emergency contraception.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you want to change the circumstance for women and even little girls in this country, please go out and vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But some Democrats who did vote in 2020 are frustrated with the administration`s failure to push through their agenda, past the big-ticket legislation and Biden`s own reluctance or inability to use the bully pulpit to change people`s minds.\par \par \uc2 ERICA INGRAM, BIDEN VOTER: Democrats don`t fight hard enough for nothing, like -- like, right now, we might as well be in a recession and they`re not even trying to fight to help or nothing. And Biden, to me, he just talks in circles.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Biden was elected to restore competency and stability to Washington after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But his job approval trails other recent presidents, including Trump, at this point in the election cycle, after a lingering pandemic, the chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan, failure to stem rising costs, failure to pass voting rights legislation and a failure to substantially address climate change or immigration challenges.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, HOST, MSNBC`S "MORNING JOE": Has the Biden administration done a good job handling the border?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. BETO O`ROURKE, (D) TEXAS: They haven`t done enough, no.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Whispers that Biden`s age and leadership style make him ill-suited to run again in 2024 have gone very public.\par \par \uc2 JOE CUNNINGHAM\uc2 , (D) SOUTH CAROLINA, GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: If he served a second term, he would be 86 years old. I`m not sure if any of us know any 86-year-olds who should be running the entire country.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , (D) MICHIGAN: You know, I`m not going to weigh in on whether or not he should run. If he does run, he`ll have my support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Echoed by voters.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE JEAN, BIDEN VOTER: Given what he was handed, he`s really doing a very good job. But we -- we do need younger voices.\par \par \uc2 DAVID COURSEY, DEMOCRATIC VOTER: My understanding, that part of the wager in 2020 was that he was only going to run once.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And some Democrats eyeing 2024 are stepping into the vacuum. California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  even aired TV ads over the July 4th weekend in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIF.: I urge all of you living in Florida to join the fight, or join us in California, where we still believe in freedom.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , who took a trip to New Hampshire last month, had a fiery response to last week`s mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 PRITZKER\uc2 : If you`re angry today, I`m here to tell you, be angry. I`m furious.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now Biden, who ran promising to be a transitional figure to a new generation...\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, I view myself as a bridge, not as anything else.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... is trying to tamp down speculation he may not be on the ballot in 2024, as voters who elected Biden to be above the fray worry he`s not well suited for the current fight ahead.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN WIDES, BIDEN VOTER: I think his energy matters. I want to see him out here fighting for these things. And if he is, then I`ll support him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is the commerce secretary, \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 , also the former governor of Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Raimondo, welcome to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, your purview, in many ways, is the economically, largely. And so let me ask a simple question this way, what do you attribute to the -- to the fact that three-quarters of the country feel like the country is headed in the wrong direction? How much of it is economic, in your view?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: I think quite a bit of it is economic. And specifically, I think it`s inflation. So, you know, look, obviously, it`s -- it`s frustrating. You know, if you were to ask me what do I think of the economy, I would say we have a very strong economy. You know, unemployment is down. We have recovered all the jobs since -- lost in the pandemic. People`s household balance sheets are strong.\par \par \uc2 However, when you go to the grocery store, prices are high. When you fill up your car at the tank, prices are high. And so consumers, Americans, are feeling that. And I think that is largely the reason folks say, as you said, we`re headed in the wrong direction. But if you -- you know, I was just talking to a CEO of a big company and asking him, as I always do talking to people, you know, what do you think about the economy?\par \par \uc2 And he said, "The economy`s strong. Customers are buying. Businesses are strong. People are adding jobs."\par \par \uc2 He said, "We`re trying to talk ourselves into a recession."\par \par \uc2 And I agree with that. You know, I think, fundamentally, the economy`s strong, but if you go to the grocery store every day, getting ready for that summer picnic and prices are high, people feel frustrated. And, you know, we get that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk about a couple things, though, the administration hasn`t done yet that have been thought of as pretty good short-term solutions. I was talking with \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , Democratic congresswoman from Florida, And she -- she pointed out three things, and I`ve heard this from others, getting rid of many of these Trump tariffs and boosting legal immigration and implementing an all-of-the-above energy policy.\par \par \uc2 I want to take the first two. You would like to see some of these Trump tariffs gotten rid of, on household items. You`re not the only one in the administration. Why hasn`t it happened yet?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: The president is in the process of making that decision. I want to say a couple things about this. We should be clear about what lifting tariffs would and wouldn`t do, right, like, lifting tariffs isn`t going to bring down top-line inflation in a very significant way.\par \par \uc2 What it will do, potentially, is help consumers on certain, as you say, household goods. And so, for that reason, given where inflation is, I think it could make sense to do it. But, you know, the president is being thoughtful about this, unlike President Trump. Those tariffs that he imposed made no sense. And so we are briefing him, and I expect he`ll make a decision shortly.\par \par \uc2 And if he decides to lift certain tariffs, it will be because he knows he has to think about doing everything he possibly can to provide any relief to consumers. But he`s going to do it in a thoughtful way that is strategic and also, most important, most important to him and to all of us, is without hurting American workers. And that`s what we`re trying to get done here.\par \par \uc2 With respect to immigration, you know, that`s an issue for Congress to take up. And I think that, you know, it`s a discussion that we should have.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Everybody knows; ask any economist...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t the single biggest driver...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... actually...\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: ... we`re not going to be able to achieve the economic growth...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... right now, with our supply chain problems, with -- with the labor pressure, with all of this, that in many ways this inflationary pressure in some ways is this labor shortage on the lower end of the scale, right?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: In a lot of ways, absolutely, not in some ways. It`s a huge driver, and so increasing the labor supply would certainly help. The other thing we need to do, though, is get women back into the work force. The -- you talk about single biggest issues...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Look at workforce participation of women without a college degree. We have to be there for those women to get child care, to -- you know, reproductive health is on everybody`s mind. That`s going to hurt the economy. You know, child care, health care, skills. Train these women so that they`re able to get jobs that are available today and be productive members of the workforce. If you`re serious about the labor shortage, lean in to making sure women can fully participate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the issue of energy prices, the president proposed a gas tax holiday. There`s been crickets in Congress. What other ideas do you have to lower the price of gas?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: So that is one, and hopefully Congress takes action. The other thing the president is working on is just increasing supply. You know, fundamentally, this inflation we are experiencing, whether it`s gas or goods, is being driven by a lack of supply.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s some good news, right? You saw wholesale gas prices drop significantly recently. I think that`s due to the increased supply. And now we have to make sure consumers feel that at the pump. But the president is pushing producers, domestically and globally, to increase supply so that consumers start to feel it at the pump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You brought up abortion rights. And it`s interesting. Whether it`s inflation, whether it`s in abortion rights, whether it`s yet another gun massacre in Illinois, there is a growing group of Democrats that are frustrated, believe that the president isn`t showing enough energy, isn`t showing enough fight on any of these issues. Is any of this criticism, in your view, justified?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: No. No. Look, it`s not about who`s the best performer. This isn`t about, you know, performative. This is about who`s going to go to work every day and get the job done and deliver for women?\par \par \uc2 I have to say, on a personal level, I first fell in love with Senator Biden way back when, when he was fighting for the Violence Against Women Act. I was an intern at the Department of Justice. This is a guy who has devoted decades to working to help women, to provide women rights. And he`s fighting every day now. He just signed the executive order to preserve women`s health care and reproductive rights, so...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does that executive order -- what does that order do, other than tell everybody to follow the law? I mean, I don`t -- it looks like it`s a glorified press release.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Well, you know, you put your finger on an issue. A lot of -- a lot of the things that are coming the president`s way, he doesn`t have a silver bullet to solve. I mean, this -- these issues have to be solved by Congress or, in this case, the Supreme Court and Congress. So he`s doing what a good executive does, which is go to his team and say, "Give me every option, every tool in my tool box." And that`s what this executive order does. He`s pushing the limits of his authority to stand up for women who have had a right taken away.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I -- look, I know people are frustrated. You know, folks are cranky with a lot of good reason. But we have to focus on who`s going to work every day and solving these problems, not who uses the most fiery language. It`s who`s going to stick up for women and deliver for them, whether it`s health care, reproductive rights or jobs or training or child care? And that`s President Biden.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 , commerce secretary and, as I said, the former governor of Rhode Island, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 From one party`s leadership challenge to another, the Republican Party is also facing one. Former President Trump still wields huge influence on Republicans in Congress. But Republican governors collectively have been able to maintain some independence from him. You`re starting to see that spread.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Republican governor of Maryland, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor Hogan, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve -- you`ve been, sort of, alone on this for years, saying the governors are pushing back. And you`re starting to see more evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 But I want to start overseas. Is there anything to be learned by the party from what the conservatives, the Tories, did with their unpopular and controversial leader?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 , (R) MARYLAND: Well, I think maybe not getting away from what, you know, the basic tenets of your party are and, you know, changing direction. And, you know, I think he did some -- some crazy things that didn`t -- didn`t make the public very happy, and he lost his position.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why isn`t the Americans- -- America`s conservative party doing the same thing with their leader?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, I would argue that we are. It`s just taking longer than it should. As you pointed out, I`ve been talking about this for years now. And I felt like I was on a life boat, you know, all by myself, but now we need a bigger boat. Because more and more people are speaking out every day. You know, I was chair of the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2  and we were pushing back all throughout COVID.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve said that President Trump`s influence on the party was going to diminish over time. It hasn`t happened, you know, rapidly, but it has diminished dramatically. And there are only about 34 percent of Republican primary voters who want to see the president run again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, you`re -- you`re in this awkward spot, and I know you work with a lot of these governors` races around the country. Governor Kemp was able to push back a Trump influence. But you look at what`s happening in Arizona, and there`s a fight there. You look what`s happening in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have a fight there. You look at what`s happening in Michigan, and there`s no anti-Trump candidate because of some really bad campaign tactics, if you will.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You win some, you lose some. Arizona and Michigan are pretty tough ones to lose, no?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, I agree, but it`s an example of the fact that Trump`s influence is diminishing. So I`m -- I`m on the \uc2 RGA\uc2  executive committee. There are five different governors where Trump was attacking them. All of them won. I mean, I went down and did events for Brian Kemp. A couple of my staffers were down working in his campaign. Brian Kemp won by 52 points. And Brad Little won. And \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  won. And a number of other -- all the incumbent governors that Trump went after have won their primaries. But, yeah, national Democrats are now, kind of, colluding with Trump to...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s funny you say that. I`m going to play an ad now, and -- and ask you about it. Here it is. This is one running in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Dan Cox, Donald Trump`s hand-picked candidate for Maryland governor. Cox worked with Trump, trying to prove the last election was a fraud. Dan Cox, too close to Trump, too conservative for Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Obviously, we know they`re trying to say, "Hey, look at who`s the more conservative guy." Now, they`re not being -- they`re not pretending they like the candidate here. Sometimes you see that in these subtle ads. But is this a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  problem or Democratic Party aggressiveness?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, it`s both. But I think this is a perfect example -- you know, the Democratic Party is talking about, you know, defending democracy. But they`re spending tens of millions of dollars to promote, you know, conspiracy theory believing -- you know, this guy that they`re promoting with that ad took two busloads of people to the Capitol on January 6th and called \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  a traitor, while they were saying "Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 !"\par \par \uc2 TODD: But when Trump endorses him, is it the -- I mean, do you blame the Democratic Party for pointing that out?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, Trump shouldn`t endorsing him. But the Democratic Party shouldn`t be spending millions of dollars trying to promote conspiracy theorists.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I hear you, but if -- if Trump wasn`t doing it...\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I agree...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... you would have a better case, right?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Look, nobody`s been standing up more to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than me. And we`re -- I`m going all around the country helping people that Trump is attacking, and we`re winning most of them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Based on what you`ve gleaned from the January 6th Committee and what you`ve taken away, is this -- do you think he should be charged with a crime?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, I think that`s for the Justice Department to decide, because I think one thing the American people do want is to take the politics out of it and get to the facts of what happened. I think the committee`s done a service in bringing a lot of people forward that are finally talking. And we have a lot more information than we did, but that`s something the Justice Department has to decide.\par \par \uc2 Look, a lot of people are now...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... the country. Do you think the country can handle prosecuting a former president?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yeah, I`m not sure they can. But I think, you know, no man is above the law. So if that`s where the facts lead, that`s what has to happen. But, you know, a lot of people are talking about January 6th, but I was actually taking action on January 6th. I sent the Maryland State Police, you know, riot police, and I sent the Maryland National Guard. And they were all the first to arrive to defend the leaders of Congress and the vice president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to change subjects slightly, on the issue of guns. Your state, the Supreme Court ruling on the New York law meant Maryland`s permit law, you were going to have to essentially allow it to happen. Do you feel as if the Supreme Court has constrained your ability to fight guns?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, look, I think -- it really doesn`t do a whole lot. I mean, in Maryland we`ve got some of the toughest gun laws in the country, but it hasn`t stopped the shootings. Some most -- 99 percent of the gun crimes that take place are all illegal guns. It has nothing to do with...\par \par \uc2 TODD: People coming from states that don`t have the Maryland laws, correct?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, there -- there are ghost guns and there are people that are stealing -- trafficking in stolen guns. But the violent crime problem is real, and it doesn`t have much to do with, you know, how you go about registration on concealed carry because the shooters, the repeat violent offenders, they don`t follow gun laws, so...\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I hear you. But look at all of these mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yep.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Too many of them, they`re always -- oh, by the way, the gun was purchased legally.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, and -- and here you have states with red flag laws that can`t be implemented. Is that really the elixir?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, look, I signed a red flag law that does works in Maryland. So we aren`t having some of those issues. I`m for universal background checks and for reasonable restrictions.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:01]\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I`ve always supported the right of law-abiding citizens to go through a process and to be able to, you know, bear arms, but to do everything we possibly can to take guns away from the mentally ill and -- and criminals.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the problem in our state. Our legislature keeps voting against penalties for people that shoot people with guns.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What -- do you the Supreme Court has been too conservative, about right, or maybe too activist?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: You know, I`m not sure whether they`ve been activist or not, and I`m not a constitutional scholar. But I think the Supreme Court and the people that are on there made decisions based on what they thought their interpretation of the law was. And I know a lot of people conflate, you know, how this came about, but, you know, I think some of these issues that hadn`t been heard before, they just found a legal reason for taking the action.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you worried it`s become politicized, the court?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I`m worried about the fact that the country is so dramatically divided and that some of these issues are going to continue to help us be divided. But I think the vast majority of people in America are just frustrated with all the angry rhetoric on both sides.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know you`re thinking about running for president. Why wouldn`t -- what would make you not run?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, look, I -- I`m going to do the best job I can being governor until next January. But -- and I don`t know exactly what the future holds. But I -- I do think that there are an awful lot of people -- I would call them "the exhausted majority" of Americans who are frustrated with the far left and the far right and they want to see us go in a different direction. So -- and I`ve seen nothing that would dissuade me from thinking that -- I think there`s a growing demand for exactly what we`ve done in Maryland over the last eight years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he runs, would ever concede losing a primary?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I`m hopeful that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won`t be running and won`t be the nominee if he does run.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , Republican from Maryland, it`s good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the Trump White House counsel, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , sat down with the January 6th Committee last Friday. I want to talk to committee member \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  about what Cipollone told them and about what we can expect from this week`s hearings.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The January 6th Committee is planning at least two more hearings this week. On Friday former White House counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  testified before the committee for seven hours on President Trump`s efforts to overturn the election. This week`s first hearing will focus on Mr. Trump`s connections to various extremist groups that were involved in the attack. And it`s being led by Democrats \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  and \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Congresswoman Murphy, a Democrat from Florida, joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, welcome to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2 , (D) FLORIDA: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with Pat Cipollone`s testimony. Was it as critical as you thought it would be?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Of course it was. He had relevant information about what was happening in the White House. And we are really grateful to him that he was willing to come before the committee and share that with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, of course there were areas in which he claimed privilege, but it was important for us to understand what the president`s top legal adviser thought about the activities that were happening post-election and in the run-up to January 6th.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can you give me an idea of what he would not touch?\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: He claimed privilege on conversations that related to the advice he provided directly to the president, or conversations with the president. But I think we still got a lot of relevant information from him. And it provides us another perspective on what was happening in the White House in those weeks running up to January 6th that were so critically important.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have seen some reports. Other fellow committee members said Mr. Cipollone did not contradict previous witnesses. Did he confirm testimony that Cassidy Hutchinson gave?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think there was a lot of information that fit into this bigger puzzle that we`re putting together. And we have different voices telling about the same meeting and, more or less, telling the same narrative. Of course you have to understand these are all folks who have had a year plus since the events, and so everybody has a different level of memory or recall on specific details.\par \par \uc2 But the overall message that we have been gathering out of all of these witnesses is that the president knew he had lost the election, or that his advisors had told him he had lost the election, and that he was casting about for ways in which he could retain power and remain the president despite the fact that the democratic will of the American people was to have President Biden be the next elected...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Was it clear that the president was told his actions that he wanted to take were illegal?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think he was given the best possible advice by very talented legal folks, both from Barr at the Department of Justice as well as within the White House in legal counsel. And they gave him very strong advice. What we were able to ascertain from those folks is what their activities were, what they said. And as you have seen in some of the hearings, they said in no unclear terms that these were things that -- these were lines that could not be crossed for the sake of democracy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can you confirm if he said "We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable?"\par \par \uc2 I mean, that was a big, important moment in Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony. Were you able to have him confirm that that is a concern he had?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We were able to get him to confirm the concerns that he did have, his reservations about some of the things that were happening, his desire not to be affiliated with, you know, some of the things, his desire to be on the record, have the...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not affiliated with what? Can you say some of the things, the speech at the Ellipse?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, like the speech at the Ellipse. He didn`t attend the speech at the Ellipse. There were...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did he try to get language struck out of it?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: He -- you know, not to get too much in the details, I think, for legal counsel, they participated and they performed the role that they have when speeches do come to them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Jeffrey Clark scheme, it was referred to that he called that a murder-suicide pact. Was he -- were you able to get that confirmed out of him?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: He made very clear that he thought the Eastman theory, which was this idea that the vice president could somehow unilaterally declare the president the president-elect, or that the pressure on the Department of Justice, he sided with the Department of Justice on their findings of no fraud in the election. He made very clear that he took the side of many of the folks that you`ve already seen come before the committee and was asserting that there wasn`t enough evidence to prove that the election was not free and fair and that, you know, the right thing to do, I think, for democracy is to have a peaceful transfer of power, especially after December 14th, when the states have certified their electors.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One of the more striking responses to supposedly things that Mr. Cipollone said was from \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 . It`s a clip we`ve seen a lot. I want to play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED KUSHNER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISER: I know that, you know, he was always -- him and the team were always saying, "Oh, we`re going to resign; we`re not going to be here if this happens, if that happens." So I, kind of, took it up to just be whining, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Number one, did Mr. Cipollone have any reaction to this idea that his threats for resignation were just simply chalked up to whining?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think, when you look at legal advice that`s given -- because you have to understand, Mr. Cipollone wanted to see President Trump succeed, and so he was giving him the best legal advice he could to ensure his success. And if he got to a place where he felt like he couldn`t morally stand by some sort of action, resigning isn`t an act of whining. It is a ultimate protest that what -- that his advice was being ignored and what the president moved forward with was something he didn`t want to be associated with. So I`m not sure that I would have characterized it as whining.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s go to your hearing on Tuesday. Number one, are we going to see much of Mr. Cipollone`s testimony on Tuesday, or this -- or the first hearing this week?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We are always receiving new information from a lot of different sources, and we are trying to pull the information that the American people most need to hear.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:07]\par \par \uc2 And I imagine that you will be hearing things from Mr. Cipollone, but also from others that were in the White House. The focus of this next hearing will be on the domestic violent extremists, as well as members of Congress, people that the president called in to assist him in this pressure campaign.\par \par \uc2 And this pressure campaign is a follow-on to the previous hearings where we talked about how the president pressured the vice president, pressured the Department of Justice, pressured state election and electors to just call the race in his favor. And he, in the waning days leading up to January 6th, called in additional support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Raskin has called it "convergence," these groups like the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers and what they were doing here. They wouldn`t be here without the president wanting them here.\par \par \uc2 But are you going to be able to provide evidence that`s more than just convergence? That there was an absolute understanding as to why the president invited them to Washington?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Without spoiling anything that comes this week and encouraging folks to tune in to the specifics, what I will say is that we will lay out the body of evidence that we have that talks about how the president`s tweet on the wee hours of December 19th of "Be there, be wild," was a siren call to these folks. And we`ll talk in detail about what that caused them to do, how that caused them to organize, as well as who else was amplifying that message.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you have evidence of specific members of Congress who were somehow involved in amplifying and encouraging these groups to come?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, I think all of that is pretty public. They were quite public about their efforts to amplify the president`s call to use January 6th as a last stand in this effort to remain as president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: So great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, we`ll be watching this week. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, is President Biden the Democrats` best bet in 2024? Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:00]\par \par \uc2 Panel is here. NBC News Senior Washington Correspondent Hallie Jackson, Mark Leibovich of "The Atlantic", and he`s author of the new book "Thank You for Your Servitude," we`re going to get into it in a little bit. Rich Lowry`s editor of National Review, and \uc2 Daniella Gibbs Leger\uc2  is the executive vice president of the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And since the first half of our show was about sort of the hand-wringing going on inside the Democratic Party, Daniella, I`ll let you get first crack here, which is what is the issue at the White House? Are they not meeting the moment? Are expectations too high on the left?\par \par \uc2 What say you?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLA GIBBS LEGER\uc2 , EXEC. VP, \uc2 CENTER FOR AMERICAN PROGRESS\uc2 : Maybe it`s a little bit of both.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Look, it is a national pastime here in D.C. to talk about Democrats infighting. You know, I was using an analogy the other day, it`s like --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dems in disarray, right?\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Dems in disarray, exactly --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t that a meme? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: That is totally a "hashtag this town" thing to say.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of this town.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just what he wanted to hear.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Right. But it`s like these Democrats, we went out and bought a house and they`re sitting outside arguing about what color to paint it. Meanwhile, the Republican Party is literally burning the house down to the ground.\par \par \uc2 That is how I view this argument. Like we -- there are bigger fish to fry. And what you need to be doing is being focused like a laser beam on November. Like, that is what is most important, and making sure the American people know there`s a really big choice.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hallie, what are you hearing out of the White House? What is it that they`re --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: A couple things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: - pushing back?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Because I was on the phone with folks over there yesterday, ahead of the show asking about this exactly thing, knowing we were probably going to talk about it. And there is - I think there is a frustration, obviously.\par \par \uc2 There`s also a sense of, like, listen, we are doing stuff. People just don`t see it, necessarily. Or people don`t see how engaged the president is. It is not a shock to you, I`m sure, Chuck, that that`s what the White House is saying in defense of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But on the flip side of things, I was speaking with a Democratic member of Congress who said, yeah, there is real frustration on a couple of fronts. First, you`ve got frontliners, right? The more moderate folks who are up in these races in the midterms who feel like he`s got to be doing more on the economy. There`s a sense that there`s that good jobs report out, objectively good jobs report out, right, on Friday. Why didn`t the president do more to highlight it?\par \par \uc2 He talked about it, yes. I think there were some people who wanted to see a more forceful response. On the other side you have the progressives that are saying, abortion, guns, where are you on this, right? Yes, he did that event on reproductive rights just this past week, the president did. But there is a sense that I`ve heard from people of saying, where was this the day that the Supreme Court made the decision? Why aren`t you doing X, Y, and Z?\par \par \uc2 I think the White House response is, listen, we had things ready to go the day the decision came down. We`re working on it. We`ve got our legal team doing it. It`s not like they`re hearing these suggestions and ignoring them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, there`s an expression "politics abhors the vacuum." Well, guess who`s seen this vacuum? It`s \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I want to play this ad that he`s running in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA: Freedom. It`s under attack in your state. I urge all of you living in Florida to join the fight, or join us in California, where we still believe in freedom. Freedom of speech, freedom to choose, freedom from hate, and the freedom to love. Don`t let them take your freedom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, look, we can carve this up two ways, Mark. Look, Newsom is - he joined Truth Social, he`s trying to pick fights with Trump, he`s trying to pick a fight with DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 But the messaging in there was fascinating. And I think that`s what you hear from Democrats. Why isn`t the White House -- and this is what he says, where`s the party? I think this is him saying, hey guys, look, try this message.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Exactly. And I think -- you know, to be perfectly clear, I don`t think \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  cares about whether 10 people in Florida move to California because they saw a bunch of ads. I mean, his message is to go national with these ads, as he is --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Get the attention.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Get the attention of people, get the attention of the White House.\par \par \uc2 And look, it`s a compelling message. And \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , frankly, with all due respect to President Biden, delivers it a lot more compellingly than a guy who`s been on the national stage for as long as the current president is.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I think it`s very effective. I think it`s a vacuum that is being filled. And I think, you know, this is probably a product of its own weakness from the White House.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, Rich, we`re going to get to the Republican leadership problems in the next block, but it does feel as if Republicans only will have themselves to blame if they can`t counter this.\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Uh-huh. Well, I just think the Newsom thing is just to promote himself and to get us chatting about him, which is working.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But the freedom message is a fascinating thing to try to reclaim that word.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: He`s going to have to work really hard to reclaim that, given policy in California.\par \par \uc2 But look, on Biden, I think there was this period of euphoria after the election with people kind of puffing him up into something that he wasn`t. And now there`s this period of existential despair when people are realizing what he actually is.\par \par \uc2 He won the presidency by default. He`s nearly 80 years old in a hugely demanding job. And it`s not as though he hasn`t said, from my perspective, incredibly over-the-top things about guns and abortion and voting and everything people want him to rise to the moment on. It`s just he`s not very compelling or vigorous. And that`s just who he is, and it`s not going to get any better.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Well, I would just say, as much as it relates to the 2024 chatter, somebody said this to me and I think there`s some validity to it, that the single biggest thing that`s going to stop this chatter about President Biden in 2024 is when and if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announces he`s running in 2024. Because there is nothing like that in the eyes of, I think, some folks that will solidify the Democratic Party around \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and put an end to the, like, "Dems in disarray" hashtag, at least temporarily.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, but I want to play this ad, Daniella, that`s in North Carolina -- Cheri Beasley making it clear she doesn`t want to be associated with either party.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:02]\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: We all know Washington doesn`t make a lot of sense. So why send the same politicians and expect it to change? The special interests have too much power, and neither political party is getting it right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP) ] TODD: That clearly, look -- that`s the only way she`s going to get to 50 percent in that state. We know those last 5 percent of voters in North Carolina, that tough.\par \par \uc2 Can Democrats succeed and distance themselves from Washington at the same time?\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Yes, they can, because politics are local. This is a local election about what`s happening in North Carolina. And she needs to state clearly what she is going to do to deliver for the people in her state, how she will differentiate herself from what`s happening in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 And again, this is a tale as old as time. People running away from the current occupant of the White House and focusing on how they`re going to be independent and they`re going to be different.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When does that ever work?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: It also never works.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Biden -- well, look --\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: It never works.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LEGER: But you --\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: If the president`s at 38, it doesn`t matter who you are or what you say.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: But we`ve also never had a Supreme Court deliver the types of decisions they deliver. We`ve never had the attention around gun violence happening. I think this is different than other midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Well, but also the face of the opposition party remains \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, you can go to the middle of the Charlotte airport in North Carolina and say, hey, there`s going to be a rematch between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in two years. How do you feel about this?\par \par \uc2 I guarantee you seven out of ten of them are going to say, oh, just, like, kill me now." I mean, it`s just not - this is not an appealing message from the defining figures in both parties right now, which is why an ad like this is very effective, I think.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess the other thing is, is there any way they change this narrative? You said it has to be Trump just has to announce. Is there any success they can have? Or are we in a -- is this baked in until November?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yeah, you mean short of that, basically. I mean, I think \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  - I was struck that she acknowledged to you pretty clearly that inflation is the reason why so many people are unhappy with the state of the country right now. And until, I think, there is -- what, what`s that face?\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I just don`t understand why they haven`t --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t get why they haven`t done some of these small things.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: But this is what --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They proposed a gas tax holiday, right?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: - I think they`re hearing from members and lawmakers, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, you asked her about that, right? And so -- but I do think that that is, to me, when I have these conversations with folks here who are lawmakers, who are coming in and reflecting what their constituents say, it`s so much about the economy and inflation. It is about the Supreme Court decision. But I think there is still a question mark, at least for now, as to how much that`s actually going to drive people to the polls. A lot of people say they will. You`ve heard that in that opening piece. But how much are you going to actually see that until we get to November.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: There`s polling last week that had 5 percent of people as the top issue, abortion, 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we will find out there. I don`t understand. Some ideas to tackle inflation are too small to have an impact. Others are small, but let`s see if it has an impact. They don`t seem to have the same story for every idea that`s floated out there.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, when we come back, a story that takes more than 280 characters to tell. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is trying to drop out of his $44 billion deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kara Swisher joins me next to explain why this matters.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:45:40]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is a man who makes headlines, few bigger than when he announced on Friday that he now wants out of his mega $44 billion deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and take it private. What happened and why?\par \par \uc2 No better person to answer those questions than my friend Kara Swisher, co- host of Vox Media`s Pivot podcast.\par \par \uc2 Kara, welcome back to the show.\par \par \uc2 This feels like the most predictable potential next thing that was going to happen the day he decided to buy it.\par \par \uc2 SWISHER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you`re probably not surprised. But is he still going to own \uc2 Twitter\uc2  anyway?\par \par \uc2 SWISHER: Well, if a court makes him buy it right now. I mean, I think that`s where it`s going to go to. It`s all a legal proceeding now.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`ll be hard for the court to force someone to pay that much money for something, and it`s a little unprecedented. And, of course, they have \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who likes to fight. And so, he`s going to fight with the Delaware courts. He`s going to fight with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He`s going to fight publicly.\par \par \uc2 And so, it could be a big mess and everyone might want to just have him pay the $1 billion breakup fee and go. But it could be a lot higher. He may have to, if \uc2 Twitter\uc2  presses it, he may have to pay a lot more for trying to get out of this deal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what`s it mean for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? I mean, look, this is a public square that certainly there`s plenty of political debate that`s sparked from it. And it matters to a small group of people, but it does matter a lot. What happens to the company? What happens to the product?\par \par \uc2 SWISHER: Nothing good from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has come out of this sort of "\uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is bored" routine that`s been going on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, obviously he`s interested in the company because he likes the product. But a lot of this has become a circus, a ridiculous circus.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it`s not good for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . It puts them in a bad position to have to do this lawsuit, especially with someone who doesn`t care and attack -- you know, sort of attacks them on their own platform, which is kind of ironic in a weird way. But it`s not good for them.\par \par \uc2 They`ve never had a very strong business, which is one of the reasons someone like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  or another buyer could do something with it. It`s still a large platform, even though it`s a small, small, business. It really is.\par \par \uc2 And so, for years, it`s underperformed, whether it`s the stock or the revenues and this and that. And so, you know, there was a lot of promise here, possibility, but nothing good is going to come of this for them. And being in court against someone like that is not good for the business, not good for employee retention, not good for branding, advertisers, so not good.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, if bad for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , good for who? Is this a boon for TikTok? A boon for \uc2 Instagram\uc2 ? Who?\par \par \uc2 SWISHER: I guess. I guess. You know, they`re just watching quietly from the sidelines while this goes on. It can`t be good.\par \par \uc2 They weren`t much of a competitor to \uc2 Facebook\uc2  or TikTok. TikTok`s been growing like crazy. Even Snapchat was doing very well until the recent downturn. Everybody`s been hit, you know, really hard in the stock selloff.\par \par \uc2 But they all were doing fine without \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , so it`s not really going to affect them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. All I know is it`s \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , which means we will all be paying a lot of attention to this drama as it plays out in a Delaware --\par \par \uc2 SWISHER: Or not. Maybe not now, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- chancery court.\par \par \uc2 Maybe not, all right. Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 Kara Swisher, you always make us smarter. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a conservative party dumps its leader after a series of scandals and embarrassment. Why the cases of \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  so far have had very different endings.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:31]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the Republicans. So, Rich, there was a scandal plague, an embarrassing leader of the Conservative Party in the U.K. And there was finally the "enough is enough" moment, and they got rid of Boris.\par \par \uc2 You know, arguably \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  had a moment where he could`ve done the same thing the way the U.K. does, essentially the vote of no confidence. We`re dumping our leader. He can never be leader again.\par \par \uc2 This American conservative party hasn`t done it. Why?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Well, one, it`s a different system. Two, the British Tories have an ingrained culture of stabbing their leaders in the back and in the front without compunction. And if decisions are being made in smoke-filled rooms here, the outcome would be differently about Trump. But that`s not the way it works in our system. And at the end of the day, the issue is Trump has a significant hold on Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 And critics of Trump, enemies of Trump who want him to go away just need to constantly remind themselves the people they need to convince, the sitting Republicans who voted for him twice, they like him. They`re entertained by him. They`re grateful for many things that he did. They hate the media. And they think Trump is often attacked unfairly.\par \par \uc2 So from my perspective, the person who`s likely to win over those voters from Trump eventually is someone Trump critics probably hate as well, someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , rather than a \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hallie, what are you hearing on this?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yeah, I mean, I think that`s right. I think the Trump/DeSantis factor is a fascinating one here. You talked about that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  ad. You talked about the way that DeSantis has kind of become the -- and this is, you know, let me think of a way to put it, but, like, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t run, does \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  take that mantle, right? But it`s a different system he`s got.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Mark and I were talking about it. He`s got a small circle of people around him. It`s not like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  where it`s this sprawling group of advisors, tons of people who are always talking about his plans, et cetera. I do think that if you look at 2024, I think that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is clearly going to run. I do not think, as much as \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  wants to have a hold on the sort of MAGA members of the Republican Party, he does not. I think that that`s a question mark.\par \par \uc2 I think evangelical Republicans he can make the case to. I think the more broad appeal is going to be elusive for him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let`s get to your book, Mr. Leibovich. Because in many ways, if you read your book, "Thank You for Your Servitude" here, you get the explanation for why what happened in the U.K. isn`t yet happening here.\par \par \uc2 And I want to start with this excerpt, because I think it gets at the core of actually the Republicans not understanding Trump. And the censors, be careful.\par \par \uc2 If you`re a Republican in Washington -- and this is, an outgoing Republican member of Congress said this to you -- if you`re a Republican in Washington, the idea is basically to make yourselves as much of a d-head as possible in order to get attention and impress the biggest d-head of all, the guy sitting in the White House.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:09]\par \par \uc2 Finish the anecdote for me, because just what --\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: I`m not going to finish the d-head for you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: But he would not put his name on this quote. Explain to me what he told you.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Because he said I might have to lobby them one day. Isn`t it depressing? I mean, they actually said, isn`t it depressing --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They said it is depressing, not you --\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Yes, the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: No, they made the entire point for me in this book.\par \par \uc2 And look, conversations like this happen again and again in this book --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: -- in "Thank You for Your Servitude." Have to say the title.\par \par \uc2 It is -- you see the gap between, one, what Republicans will say publicly and privately play out again and again and again.\par \par \uc2 And look, it`s not like there`s not a tradition of Republicans pushing back on the leadership of their party in America. I mean, the private view of Republicans towards this president is extremely harsh. It`s extremely mocking. And look, politicians are always going to say different things in public and private before different audiences. I`ve never seen a gap wider than this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay, but Daniella, the thing is, frankly, did this outgoing member of Congress understand the fact that he saw his service in Congress as a bridge to lobbying? Does he not understand that that`s why Trump was able to get these --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right? Like, did he understand that part of the joke?\par \par \uc2 LEGER: Apparently not. I mean, that literally is the problem. And, I mean, I think you`re right. Like, Republicans have to figure out what`s more important to them. Is it the immediacy to power? Or do they care about their legacy? Do they care about what history books are going to write about them?\par \par \uc2 As I say this I`m like, of course they don`t care, because they`ve shown over and over again it`s more important for them to win the next election than to, like, I don`t know, maybe stand up for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  told you, "I`ve never been called this much by a president in my life."\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, he was addicted.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: They`re all addicted.\par \par \uc2 Look, I mean, there are any number of psychological reasons why people do certain things, you know, and make decisions like this. I mean, it`s funny, Rich and I were in the green room before when Governor Hogan said, well, Republicans need to sort of figure out what they need to do.\par \par \uc2 And it`s like, it`s been seven years. I mean, based on the precedent of recent history, the Republican M.O. in dealing with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is fecklessness. And in doing this book, I learned this over and over and over again. And I don`t think the revolution is going to be led by \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , or Kevin McCarthy, or \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, look, they`ve had the chance to do it over and over and over again. If he can survive January 6th, and for a few days it looked like he wasn`t, and be where is today, it`s stunning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, this brings me to this. If he runs, will somebody explain to me how he accepts losing?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Yeah, that`s a big--\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You know the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: That`s a big conundrum. Look, I think there`s probably maybe a 15 percent chance he doesn`t run. But very likely he`s in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems like all these people --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I was going to say--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The only answer is death.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: That was another quote.\par \par \uc2 How many times did you think that?\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: The only plan we have-- this is another former Republican congressman that said, look, we have no plan for this except sitting around hoping he dies.\par \par \uc2 And, again, this quote --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Astonishing.\par \par \uc2 LEGER: This is so depressing--\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: This conversa -- it`s all so depressing. But it`s also a fun book and it`s a great beach read.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: But no, I mean, these conversations happen over and over again. And what`s cast it in such sharp relief, and over the last few months is you see what`s happening in England, you see what`s happening on the January 6th commission. The courage that actually does happen in conservative circles is cast in such sharp relief with the punitive leadership of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is both a fun read, as you said, and absolutely infuriating. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching. Enjoy the rest of your weekend and remember, if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220710ei7a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protesters Marched On The White House Demanding The President Biden Do More To Protect Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Maura Barrett \par \uc2 484 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART: Thousands of abortions rights activists took to the streets across the country today, many with a new focus to push President Biden to do more. Maura Barrett is with the President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, NBC NEWS REPORTER: Protests across the country today from Chicago to New York, but the biggest was in Washington D.C. And while their anger was directed at the Supreme Court ruling, the goal was to pressure President Biden, some even briefly tying themselves to the White House fence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What we have going on is a massive human rights violation in our country. And he`s not responding to that level.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Protesters pushing the President to do more to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just don`t feel like things that have been occurring are really demonstrate what the mass of the American people want.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is not acceptable by any means. And these people need to be held accountable, and they need to do their job.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: The demonstration just 24 hours after Biden signed an executive order to protect some abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I`m asking the Justice Department that much like they did in the civil rights era to do something, do everything in their power to protect these women seeking to invoke their rights.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: The executive order ensures \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved medication for abortion remains widely accessible along with contraception. It also protects patient privacy when women seek out reproductive medical services. But for some on the left, it`s not enough. One abortion rights leader calling for a public health emergency declaration.\par \par \uc2 JEN KLEIN, WHITE HOUSE GENDER POLICY COUNCIL CO-CHAIR: It is definitely not off the table. It also doesn`t release a significant amount of legal authority.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: This as 10 states now have abortion bans in place after a judge in Louisiana lifted a temporary block, as more states with trigger laws are set to take effect this summer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Maura is with the President in Delaware tonight. Maura, the House plans to vote on abortion rights next week?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: On Tuesday, the House will vote to codify Roe v. Wade and to protect interstate travel for abortions. But this is not expected to pass the Senate. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Maura Barrett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220713ei790002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Bring Brittney Home\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Meagan Fitzgerald \par \uc2 2,112 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER: Basketball superstar Brittney Griner will not be at the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  All-Star game in Chicago this weekend, but she will still be front and center. Griner who has been jailed in Russia since February over a drug charge was named an honorary starter for tomorrow`s game. She pleaded guilty this week and now her wife is asking for Griner to be allowed to come home. NBC`s Meagan Fitzgerald has the very latest on her story. Meagan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Peter and Kristen, good morning to you. Yes, it`s going on five months that Brittney Griner has been locked up inside a Russian prison. And you know cries for her release are growing louder. This, as the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, entered a guilty plea to the charges that she`s facing. And the idea here -- the hope rather, is that this expedites this process moving her closer to a possible release.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (voiceover): It`s another morning that \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner is waking up inside a Russian prison. The two-time Olympian pleading guilty just days ago to drug charges after being caught with cannabis oil at a Moscow airport in February.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER BOYKOV, BRITTNEY GRINER`S ATTORNEY: It was unintentionally brought to Russia.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: The high-profile case leading celebrities, athletes, and now, even the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  to speak out.\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , AMERICAN CIVIL RIGHTS ACTIVIST: Bring Brittney home. Bring her home to her wife. We are going to stand with her until you do.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Sharpton joining Griner`s wife, Cherelle, and the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  Friday at a press conference ahead of the All-Star game in Chicago. Her wife, desperate for her release, while thanking President Biden who called her this week.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER: I`m thankful that the administration that was the first one that BG ever voted for, took the time to see her as a person, to see her in the midst of what she`s going through.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: The call from Biden and Vice President Harris coming after Griner penned a letter saying in part. As I sit here in a Russian prison alone with my thoughts and without the protection of my wife, family, friends, Olympic jersey, or any accomplishments, I`m terrified I might be here forever. The president writing back saying the administration will do everything possible to secure her release.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER: I believe every word that she said to him he understood.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Meanwhile, speculation about if Griner`s release will happen through a prisoner swap. Russian state media has said the Kremlin wants arms dealer, Viktor Bout, returned who`s currently serving a 25-year sentence in the U.S. The White House isn`t commenting, but Griner`s family is hopeful all options are on the table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Now, the Kremlin says they won`t even discuss the possibility of a prisoner swap until this trial concludes. But keep in mind this is a process that could take months, Kristen and Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It explains why she was inclined to plead guilty as she did this week. Meagan Fitzgerald, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Time now for first check of the forecast. I want to turn to Somara Theodore. And, Somara, we were all woken up at about 2 a.m. in the morning here in the DC area to that flood alert on our cell phones.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: You and me both. I went to sleep last night watching Stranger Things and I woke up to this blaring sound. I was absolutely terrified. Nonetheless, it was the flood warning, the flash flood warnings in the Washington DC metro area. Take a look right now. We still have that flood threat in place.\par \par \uc2 Flood watch for the entire Mid-Atlantic region, rain pulsating through with some of the heaviest rain down in Southern Maryland into Northern Virginia at the moment. We could see anywhere from two to three inches of rain especially along the coastline in the Carolinas. Timing on this, we`re going to see this rain sticking around through the remainder of the weekend. If you have plans in the Carolinas, we could see some storms a little bit on the stronger side scattered out there today. We`ll talk more about this later. For now, let`s see what`s going on a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: That`s your local forecast, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right, Somara, thank you. Still to come back here, Deal or No Deal, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he`s taken his $44 billion offer to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  off the table, but the social media giant is saying not so fast.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Plus, the dangerous lightning strikes caught on camera. How you can best protect yourself when you`re outside this summer? We`re back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:20:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We`re back now on a Saturday morning with a WEEKLY DOWNLOAD, our look at the week`s other big stories.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And, of course, that means we welcome our friend NBC News Now anchor, Joe Fryer, to the desk with those headlines. Joe, nice to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS HOST: Hey, there. Good morning. Good to see both of you. So among the other stories, we cover this week, new information on the police response to the deadly mass shooting at Robb Elementary School in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voiceover): A new report from the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  found that police had a chance to take out the Uvalde Texas school shooter before he entered the building but did not act in time. Just before the bloodshed at Robb Elementary, a Uvalde police officer had the gunman in his sight but asked his supervisor for permission to shoot the suspect outside the building, a request that went unanswered as the killer made his way inside the school. But the officer, who told investigators he was worried if he missed the shot he might hit students, never took the shot and 21 lives were lost.\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announced he`s stepping down for getting caught in several ethics scandals.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON, MEMBER OF THE PARLIAMENT OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: The herd instinct is powerful. When the herd moves, it moves. Now I want you to know how sad I am to be giving up the best job in the world. But them`s the breaks.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Johnson said he will continue to serve in his position until a new leader is elected.\par \par \uc2 The yoga instructor accused of killing cycling star Anna Moriah Wilson is in jail after being caught in Costa Rica. Authorities say the suspect, Kaitlin Armstrong, was apprehended with noticeable bruising and a change in appearance.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON FILLA, DEPUTY U.S. MARSHALL: According to foreign officials, Armstrong altered her physical appearance, dyed her hair brown, and had a bandage on her nose where she claimed it was from a surfboard incident.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Police say Armstrong and Wilson were involved in a love triangle prior to Wilson`s death.\par \par \uc2 Rock Legend Carlos Santana was hospitalized after collapsing on stage Tuesday night during a show in Michigan. Santana`s representative says he was overtaken by heat exhaustion and dehydration, but he`s doing well now.\par \par \uc2 Some of the week`s wildest moments were caught on camera. Tragedy at an airshow in Michigan last weekend when a jet truck racing two airplanes crashed in a ball of flames, the driver died. Police believed a blown tire was to blame. Police in Madison, Indiana caught people jumping from the second floor of a burning building. No one was seriously hurt. A Georgia monument known as America`s Stonehenge was destroyed earlier this week during a pre-dawn bombing and it was captured by a surveillance camera.\par \par \uc2 And finally, when Marcus Adams was asked by his cousin to be the flower man at her wedding, he decided to put on a show. The viral video has racked up millions of views, don`t worry, the bride says she didn`t feel upstaged at all and thought Marcus was hilarious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The final touch there was one we didn`t show where he kind of went like this with Marcus.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: He`s now inundated with requests to appear at other weddings as the flower guy.\par \par \uc2 [07:25:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I can see why. But not just weddings, let`s just have some of the - - any events.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The show, here.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And start throwing flowers.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I love how he jumps it on his own head.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s the best. You can`t alter it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: A lot of fun. All right, Joe, thank you. Well, still to come on TODAY. Why theater goers worldwide are dressing up in suits to see the newest Minions movie?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, feeling anxious? Hug a cow. We`re going to tell you about the new trend that is moving folks to farms across the country. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL, ACTRESS: What are you, a genius?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know, they`ve got over 100 girls here, so some of you are going to have to go home.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Come on, Doris.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What do you mean some of us?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: How great is that? We are back on this Saturday morning July 9th, 2022. And that is one of many classic scenes from "A League of Their Own", which just turned 30 last week if you can believe it.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Best line for that movie. Are you crying? There`s no crying in baseball. Yes, everybody loves that one.\par \par \uc2 The movie is being remade into a series on Prime Video and Rosie O`Donnell is actually coming back. She`s not playing Doris though.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment Weekly reports that O`Donnell will be playing a local bar owner who takes one of the players under her wing. If you like, you can watch the series next month on Prime Video.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, sign me up for that. Also, as a former softball player, I`m a big fan of that movie.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That was a good one. We want to begin this half an hour with a check of the headlines, two weeks after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, President Biden signed an executive order Friday aimed at safeguarding access to abortion. The order seeks to protect the physical safety and security of patients and providers and clinics.\par \par \uc2 It also aims to make sure federally approved abortion pills remained widely available in addition to contraception.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a judge in Louisiana issued an order Friday that allows the state to enforce its ban on almost all abortions.\par \par \uc2 More abortion rights rallies are planned across the country today, including one right here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, a nearly 500 acre wildfire in Northern California is threatening the largest grove of giant sequoias. The area in Yosemite National Park was closed Friday and hundreds have been ordered to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 300 firefighters are working to control the flames and the dense forest. The cause of the blaze is still under investigation. Lightning sparked wildfires over the past two years have killed about 20 percent of the sequoias, the world`s largest trees.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And check out this emergency plane landing, it was caught on camera. It`s \uc2 GoPro\uc2  video showing the incredible moment a pilot landed his plane on a North Carolina highway last Sunday. He was forced to do it after his engine failed. Amazingly, he avoided power lines and trees. Even the cars driving on four lanes of traffic.\par \par \uc2 No one in the plane or on the ground was injured fortunately. The Marine veteran from Florida just got his pilot`s license last fall and has less than 100 hours of experience.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is an incredible video. Thankfully, no one was hurt there.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220713ei7900002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 TODAY for July 9, 2022, NBC NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Kristen Welker, Janis Mackey Frayer, Kathy Park,  Maura Barrett, Danny Cevallos, Meagan Fitzgerald, Somara Theodore, Joe  Fryer, Cal Perry, Ryan Miller, Gadi Schwartz \par \uc2 7,225 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voiceover): Good morning. Sorrow and Anger. The body of former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  arrives back in Tokyo a day after he was shot and killed at a campaign rally. Growing questions across Japan this morning, how could the assassin get so close, as world leaders more of the loss of Japan`s longest serving Prime Minister?\par \par \uc2 Bye, Bye Birdie. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the world`s richest man announces that his $44 billion deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is off. Musk claiming the social media giant is not being transparent about all of its fake users. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  firing back and threatening to sue. We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Bring Brittney Home. Brittney Griner`s wife, Cherelle, joined by the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  and members of the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  demanding Russia release Griner days after she pleaded guilty to drug charges and thanking the White House for its efforts to get Griner home safely.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, WIFE OF BRITTNEY GRINER: I`m thankful that the administration that was the first one that BG ever voted for, take the time to see her as a person, to see her in the midst of what she`s going through.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Could a prisoner swap be in the works?\par \par \uc2 Emergency Landing. The pilot of a small plane is forced to touchdown in the middle of a highway, all of it while his camera was rolling from inside the cockpit. How he safely brought it down in traffic? All that plus remembering Tony Sirico, the actor best known as Paulie Walnuts on The Sopranos, passing away at the age of 79.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER MOLTISANTI, FICTIONAL NAME: You ever feel like nothing good was ever going to happen to you?\par \par \uc2 PAULIE WALNUTS GUALTIERI, FICTIONAL CHARACTER: Yes. And nothing did, so what?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: How fans and co-stars are remembering him? TODAY, Saturday, July 9, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to TODAY. Thanks for joining us on this Saturday morning. You are a Sopranos fan, and for anybody who loves watching that show, Paulie Walnuts was an unforgettable character for such a tough guy. He really was a beloved figure.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: He sure was. He was a classic. That show was a classic. His friends described him that way, Peter, tough, but big- hearted. We are going to spend some time looking back at his life and legacy later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But first, we are covering several developing stories this morning including what we`re learning about the testimony on Friday from the man who may be the key witness from the January 6 committee has been looking for, former Trump White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  meeting with the panel for nearly eight hours. The question now, will his testimony be played in public next week? And what does it mean for the former president?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And we`re also following new COVID concerns with cases rising across the country again due to a pair of new sub-variants. And now New York City is recommending that people mask up again while they`re indoors. But we want to begin this morning with our top story and that is the investigation into the assassination of Japan`s former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The suspected gunman has admitted to the killing. Still, there are growing questions, how he was able to get so close. NBC`s Janis Mackey Frayer has the very latest on all of it this morning. Janis, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANIS MACKEY FRAYER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Peter, good morning. The death of former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  has shocked Japan, it has shocked the world, and the investigation is raising questions about security in a country unaccustomed to gun violence. Now the assassination was all captured on video. And a warning some of these images are disturbing to watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voiceover): Across Japan, a nation in mourning with growing memorials and mounting questions around the assassination of former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . This morning, a hearse carrying his body drove seven hours from Nara where he died to his home in Tokyo where his family will hold a closed funeral. The Assassination captured in real-time.\par \par \uc2 Abe was giving a campaign speech when there was a shot fire. From another angle, you can see Abe turn briefly. Then this second shot. Abe collapses as the suspected gunman was tackled, what police described as a homemade shotgun made of metal pipes and tape seen lying on the street. Little is known about the 41-year-old suspect Tetsuya Yamagami. Police say he admitted he wanted to kill Abe because of a grudge. What isn`t clear is how an armed man got close enough to Abe to kill him. One of Japan`s most recognizable figures, \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was popular yet polarizing, focused on restoring Japan`s military power, in part to counter the rising threat from China.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:00]\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: He took President Obama to Hiroshima and became the first Japanese leader to visit Pearl Harbor.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE KENNEDY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO JAPAN: Prime Minister Abe was an incredible leader once in a generation and an amazing partner to the United States. So I think both our countries have lost a great leader and a great friend.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: In a country where stringent gun laws make it hard to own a firearm, the brazen attack is deeply shocking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: This mourner said it`s terrifying that something like this could happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: The biggest questions are around security. How is it that an armed man could get past his security detail and approach the riser from behind? And how could he fire a first shot and then a second shot, the shot that killed \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 , before security officers stopped him? These are the questions now facing police. They say they`re opening an internal probe to look at flaws in their security plan. Meanwhile, the funeral for \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  will be held over Monday and Tuesday at a temple in Tokyo and it will be limited to his family and those closest to him, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And, of course, all of it in the country 126 million people that only had one shooting death in all of 2021. Janis Mackey Frayer, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, one of the things that makes it so stark and terrific. Well, also this morning, family and friends of the victims of the Illinois parade shooting are beginning to save their final farewells. It`s less than a week after that horrific day. This is new details emerge about the suspect`s chaotic home life. NBC`s Kathy Park is in Highland Park this morning. Kathy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Kristen, good morning to you. It certainly has been a heartbreaking week for Highland Park. As you can see behind me, there is a growing memorial honoring the lives lost. It is right next to the street forever changed by this tragedy. This community is leaning on each other as the funerals and healing begin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PARK (voiceover): This morning, the community of Highland Park united in grief as funeral services begin for the victims in