determined a motive. Meanwhile, later on, tonight in this community, it will be coming together once again to honor the victims but also to demand an end to gun violence, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Kathy Park. Thank you, Kathy.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Next week will be another critical week for the January 6 committee with at least one more public hearing is scheduled. It comes as house investigators spent hours, Friday, interviewing one of former President Trump`s top advisors, White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . NBC`s Maura Barrett is traveling with President Biden in Delaware this morning and has the very latest. Maura, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning Peter. The January 6 committee has been waiting months to hear testimony from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . He`s the White House Counsel for former President Trump and he represented him in his first impeachment trial. Now, as we`re learning how much Cipollone was willing to reveal behind closed doors, historians are drawing similarities between him and a key witness in the Watergate hearings.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT (voiceover): Nearly eight hours. That`s how long former Trump White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  spent testifying on Capitol Hill, Friday. While he did not answer reporters` questions outside the deposition rooms, inside, he entertained questions from the house January 6 committee behind closed doors but recorded on video for future hearings.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D-CA): I think he was candid with the committee. He was careful in his answers. And I believe that he was honest in his answers.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: A source familiar with Cipollone`s testimony tells NBC News he`s been a cooperative witness within the parameters of his desire to protect executive privilege for the Office of General Counsel. The panel subpoenaed Cipollone after Cassidy Hutchinson`s bombshell testimony late last month, detailing how he urged White House staff to keep Trump away from the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, AIDE TO FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : Cipollone said something to the effect of please make sure we don`t go up to the Capitol, Cassidy. Keep in touch with me. We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen. Mark, something needs to be done or people are going to die and the bloods going to be on your effing hands.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Even with his testimony done in private, there`s anticipation in Washington that Cipollone`s interview will be played in public as soon as this week. At the Watergate hearings in 1973, President Nixon`s White House Counsel John Dean was the star witness.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DEAN, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL: I began by telling the president that there was a cancer growing on the presidency. And if the cancer was not removed, the president himself would be killed by it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: And history may repeat itself as soon as Tuesday at the next public hearing where we may see some of Cipollone`s videotaped testimony. In the meantime, the subject of the hearings, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , is still holding his rallies. He spoke last night in Las Vegas saying Democrats are using laws to criminalize their political opponents. He also revealed that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was hospitalized this week. NBC News has learned that Giuliani had two stents place to fix a heart problem, but he is now resting at home, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Maura Barrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, we want to bring in NBC News Legal Analyst Danny Cevallos. Danny, good morning. Good to be with you. Let`s just dive right in. We know that \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  spoke with the committee earlier this year, but this was his first formal recorded interview, so what could he have said in those eight hours of testimony and why could that be so significant?\par \par \uc2 DANNY CEVALLOS, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Going back to Cassidy Hutchinson`s explosive testimony, one of the things she testified to, was that, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  apparently said to her in the hallway something to the effect of we`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable. Now, if that`s true, if he said that to Hutchinson, who was a relatively low-level staffer, then what are the odds that was the only person that Cipollone said that to? That`s what the committee wants to know. Who else? If that`s true, did Cipollone say that too, and does that group include the president? Because if it does, that means that the president may have been notified from White House counsel that this was a horrible idea.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And, Danny, the big holdup, of course, for the committee trying to get Cipollone to testify were his claims of executive privilege and attorney-client privilege that he wouldn`t be able to share his conversations with the former presidents. So what does that tell us about what he would or would not have been able to speak about?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: In reality, these claims of executive privilege probably are not going to fly, but perhaps Cipollone raised them in order that the committee would force him to come to testify because number one, President Biden has already waived executive privilege, so that would weaken any claim of privilege in this case. But number two, what Trump was doing on January 6 arguably had nothing to do with being the president and Cipollone was White House Counsel. That means counsel for the government, not President Trump, the individual, and not President Trump, the candidate. So that would be a long-shot claim as well.\par \par \uc2 Already courts have denied this claim of privilege in relation to Trump`s attempt to block records from the National Archives, so because Cipollone was White House counsel, and because by the way, White House Counsel Eric Herschmann has already appeared and testified in a very entertaining manner, I should add, for most watchers that would further weaken Cipollone`s claim if you were to make it of executive privilege as White House counsel.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And, Danny, just one more. We know there`s going to be another hearing on Tuesday. What will you be watching for? What are you expecting?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: We`re expecting that this is going to focus on the groups, the extremist groups, who may or may not have planned or been involved with other folks arguably in the Trump circle. I think that`s where we`re going to see the committee focus, at least that`s what they`ve signaled, but we will see who they actually call and what testimony they actually bring out, either via videotape, video of the incidents in question, and live testimony.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, we know you will continue to watch it closely as well. We -- Danny Cevallos, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Basketball superstar Brittney Griner will not be at the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  All-Star game in Chicago this weekend, but she will still be front and center. Griner who has been jailed in Russia since February over a drug charge was named an honorary starter for tomorrow`s game. She pleaded guilty this week and now her wife is asking for Griner to be allowed to come home. NBC`s Meagan Fitzgerald has the very latest on her story. Meagan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Peter and Kristen, good morning to you. Yes, it`s going on five months that Brittney Griner has been locked up inside a Russian prison. And you know cries for her release are growing louder. This, as the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, entered a guilty plea to the charges that she`s facing. And the idea here -- the hope rather, is that this expedites this process moving her closer to a possible release.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO