par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Now, the Kremlin says they won`t even discuss the possibility of a prisoner swap until this trial concludes. But keep in mind this is a process that could take months, Kristen and Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It explains why she was inclined to plead guilty as she did this week. Meagan Fitzgerald, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Time now for first check of the forecast. I want to turn to Somara Theodore. And, Somara, we were all woken up at about 2 a.m. in the morning here in the DC area to that flood alert on our cell phones.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: You and me both. I went to sleep last night watching Stranger Things and I woke up to this blaring sound. I was absolutely terrified. Nonetheless, it was the flood warning, the flash flood warnings in the Washington DC metro area. Take a look right now. We still have that flood threat in place.\par \par \uc2 Flood watch for the entire Mid-Atlantic region, rain pulsating through with some of the heaviest rain down in Southern Maryland into Northern Virginia at the moment. We could see anywhere from two to three inches of rain especially along the coastline in the Carolinas. Timing on this, we`re going to see this rain sticking around through the remainder of the weekend. If you have plans in the Carolinas, we could see some storms a little bit on the stronger side scattered out there today. We`ll talk more about this later. For now, let`s see what`s going on a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: That`s your local forecast, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right, Somara, thank you. Still to come back here, Deal or No Deal, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he`s taken his $44 billion offer to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  off the table, but the social media giant is saying not so fast.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Plus, the dangerous lightning strikes caught on camera. How you can best protect yourself when you`re outside this summer? We`re back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:20:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We`re back now on a Saturday morning with a WEEKLY DOWNLOAD, our look at the week`s other big stories.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And, of course, that means we welcome our friend NBC News Now anchor, Joe Fryer, to the desk with those headlines. Joe, nice to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS HOST: Hey, there. Good morning. Good to see both of you. So among the other stories, we cover this week, new information on the police response to the deadly mass shooting at Robb Elementary School in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voiceover): A new report from the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  found that police had a chance to take out the Uvalde Texas school shooter before he entered the building but did not act in time. Just before the bloodshed at Robb Elementary, a Uvalde police officer had the gunman in his sight but asked his supervisor for permission to shoot the suspect outside the building, a request that went unanswered as the killer made his way inside the school. But the officer, who told investigators he was worried if he missed the shot he might hit students, never took the shot and 21 lives were lost.\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announced he`s stepping down for getting caught in several ethics scandals.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , MEMBER OF THE PARLIAMENT OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: The herd instinct is powerful. When the herd moves, it moves. Now I want you to know how sad I am to be giving up the best job in the world. But them`s the breaks.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Johnson said he will continue to serve in his position until a new leader is elected.\par \par \uc2 The yoga instructor accused of killing cycling star Anna Moriah Wilson is in jail after being caught in Costa Rica. Authorities say the suspect, Kaitlin Armstrong, was apprehended with noticeable bruising and a change in appearance.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON FILLA, DEPUTY U.S. MARSHALL: According to foreign officials, Armstrong altered her physical appearance, dyed her hair brown, and had a bandage on her nose where she claimed it was from a surfboard incident.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Police say Armstrong and Wilson were involved in a love triangle prior to Wilson`s death.\par \par \uc2 Rock Legend Carlos Santana was hospitalized after collapsing on stage Tuesday night during a show in Michigan. Santana`s representative says he was overtaken by heat exhaustion and dehydration, but he`s doing well now.\par \par \uc2 Some of the week`s wildest moments were caught on camera. Tragedy at an airshow in Michigan last weekend when a jet truck racing two airplanes crashed in a ball of flames, the driver died. Police believed a blown tire was to blame. Police in Madison, Indiana caught people jumping from the second floor of a burning building. No one was seriously hurt. A Georgia monument known as America`s Stonehenge was destroyed earlier this week during a pre-dawn bombing and it was captured by a surveillance camera.\par \par \uc2 And finally, when Marcus Adams was asked by his cousin to be the flower man at her wedding, he decided to put on a show. The viral video has racked up millions of views, don`t worry, the bride says she didn`t feel upstaged at all and thought Marcus was hilarious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The final touch there was one we didn`t show where he kind of went like this with Marcus.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: He`s now inundated with requests to appear at other weddings as the flower guy.\par \par \uc2 [07:25:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I can see why. But not just weddings, let`s just have some of the - - any events.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The show, here.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And start throwing flowers.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I love how he jumps it on his own head.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s the best. You can`t alter it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: A lot of fun. All right, Joe, thank you. Well, still to come on TODAY. Why theater goers worldwide are dressing up in suits to see the newest Minions movie?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, feeling anxious? Hug a cow. We`re going to tell you about the new trend that is moving folks to farms across the country. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROSIE O`DONNELL, ACTRESS: What are you, a genius?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know, they`ve got over 100 girls here, so some of you are going to have to go home.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Come on, Doris.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What do you mean some of us?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: How great is that? We are back on this Saturday morning July 9th, 2022. And that is one of many classic scenes from "A League of Their Own", which just turned 30 last week if you can believe it.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Best line for that movie. Are you crying? There`s no crying in baseball. Yes, everybody loves that one.\par \par \uc2 The movie is being remade into a series on Prime Video and Rosie O`Donnell is actually coming back. She`s not playing Doris though.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment Weekly reports that O`Donnell will be playing a local bar owner who takes one of the players under her wing. If you like, you can watch the series next month on Prime Video.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, sign me up for that. Also, as a former softball player, I`m a big fan of that movie.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That was a good one. We want to begin this half an hour with a check of the headlines, two weeks after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, President Biden signed an executive order Friday aimed at safeguarding access to abortion. The order seeks to protect the physical safety and security of patients and providers and clinics.\par \par \uc2 It also aims to make sure federally approved abortion pills remained widely available in addition to contraception.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a judge in Louisiana issued an order Friday that allows the state to enforce its ban on almost all abortions.\par \par \uc2 More abortion rights rallies are planned across the country today, including one right here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, a nearly 500 acre wildfire in Northern California is threatening the largest grove of giant sequoias. The area in Yosemite National Park was closed Friday and hundreds have been ordered to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 300 firefighters are working to control the flames and the dense forest. The cause of the blaze is still under investigation. Lightning sparked wildfires over the past two years have killed about 20 percent of the sequoias, the world`s largest trees.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And check out this emergency plane landing, it was caught on camera. It`s \uc2 GoPro\uc2  video showing the incredible moment a pilot landed his plane on a North Carolina highway last Sunday. He was forced to do it after his engine failed. Amazingly, he avoided power lines and trees. Even the cars driving on four lanes of traffic.\par \par \uc2 No one in the plane or on the ground was injured fortunately. The Marine veteran from Florida just got his pilot`s license last fall and has less than 100 hours of experience.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is an incredible video. Thankfully, no one was hurt there.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, also this morning, a bombshell in the business world. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  the world`s richest man is abandoning his deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Musk made the announcement last night claiming the social media giant isn`t being honest about how many people use their service. And it could lead to a very expensive and ugly legal fight.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Cal Perry is following this one from Los Angeles. Cal, what is the latest in this back and forth?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 You know, in just two months, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has gone from being all in on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to all out. Now trying to back out of the $44 billion deal.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for its part is saying not so fast. A deal is a deal in this ongoing back and forth between the tech giants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY (voice over): It began with a style and a flair that we`re now somewhat accustomed to when it comes to the richest man in the world. But this might end in a courtroom.\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  announcing in a filing with the SEC he wants out of his deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , saying in part that the company made "false and misleading representations, especially when it came to bots or fake accounts".\par \par \uc2 Musk currently worth an estimated $219 billion initially purchased nine percent of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with cash in April.\par \par \uc2 He entertained rumors of a hostile takeover. He trashed talks \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and finally came to an agreement to buy the social media giant for $44 billion in early May. That deal now very much in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s Chairman responding by saying the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board is committed to closing the transaction on the price and terms agreed upon with Mr. Musk, and plans to pursue legal action to enforce the merger agreement.\par \par \uc2 That could lock Musk into the $44 billion purchase price. Another possible outcome for Musk is a $1 billion contractual fine to break the deal.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock closed at 36.81 before the announcement on Friday, the agreed purchase price was 54.20.\par \par \uc2 Some believe his claim about fake \uc2 Twitter\uc2  accounts is an excuse to avoid the full purchase price.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER, "PIVOT" PODCASTER: He`s just reaching out for something to blame but he has to pay. He has to buy the company he`s agreed to it under in a very strict contract.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Even for the eccentric Musk, this has been some kind of week, Business Insider dropping a bombshell revealing that Musk and one of his top executives had twins last year. Musk`s eighth and ninth child. The report citing court documents which NBC News has not seen.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:08]\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Musk has yet to comment officially. But following the report he took to the very platform he no longer wants to by saying he was "doing his best to help the under population crisis".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY (on camera): Now, Musk has also not responded to our request for comment and in addition, he has been unusually quiet on his social media obsession since that SEC filing, no tweets yet. Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. It`s quite a drama continues to unfold. Cal Perry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Somara is back right now with a look at the forecast. Somara, it`s going to be hot for a lot of folks this weekend.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: That`s right. We are talking 43 million people this morning waking up too dangerous heat. Follow me down towards our Gulf Coast states into Texas areas like Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama. That area in Orange is a heat advisory. But we have heat warnings for Dallas, Shreveport, Jackson, right on up into Memphis.\par \par \uc2 How hot are we talking? Triple digits.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s a look. Houston 102 degrees, but it`s going to feel like 107 today. Shreveport, it is going to feel like 113 degrees this afternoon, out towards Dallas feeling like 111.\par \par \uc2 And we know that feel like is what matters, right? And as we head through tomorrow, we`re going to see that shift farther north into parts of the Central Plains including areas like Dodge City coming out 100, even Oklahoma City feeling like 97 degrees tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the thing, East coasts, we can see some heat as well. Now, it`s not going to be quite as hot. We`re not going to be dancing with the triple digits here. But we are going to be in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 92 in D.C. by Tuesday. So, Tuesday is going to be the hot spot for a lot of us on the East Coast. Pittsburgh, upper 80s, New York low to mid 90s as well. 80s out towards Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have some storms to talk about. Severe weather in the upper Midwest and we are looking at a chance for rain on the East Coast. We`ll talk about that a little later. For now, let`s see what`s going on in your neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 RYAN MILLER, STORM TEAM4 METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. I`m Storm Team4 Meteorologist Ryan Miller.\par \par \uc2 Lots of rain across the area from last night has causing flooding right now. And we`re going to continue with rain especially into Southern Maryland here for the balance of your Saturday. On and off rain showers today. Temperatures into the 70s. Overnight rain will start to end. We`ll start to dry out. Some clouds in the morning tomorrow. Sunshine in the afternoon and less humid conditions tomorrow afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures into the 80s. Monday and Tuesday turning hot and it looks like a chance of storms Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: And that`s the weather in your neighborhood. Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Somara, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And just ahead, the story of the mom who is making it her mission to help families of children diagnosed with cancer.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Her important message for parents everywhere. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:20]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We are back now with the story of a mother`s love and perseverance in the face of an awful tragedy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And this is personal for so many of us here. Our friend and our CNN colleague Rene Marsh lost her young son to brain cancer. She`s now making it her mission to make sure other families never have to go through the same pain that she did.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice over): Rene Marsh says March 14th 2019 was the best day of her life. It was the day she became a mom to Blake.\par \par \uc2 RENE MARSH, CNN CORRESPONDENT: When the nurse handed him to me, I had this one tear just fall out of my eye. The depth of my love for Blake is not like anything I`ve had for anyone.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: As he grew, so did his personality.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: He was my best bud. And he loved to dance. He really loved reading.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But one day, Rene noticed a problem with one of Blake`s eyes. A visit to the ophthalmologist led to an MRI and news that rocked their world.\par \par \uc2 How long did it take before they diagnosed him with brain cancer?\par \par \uc2 MARSH: Just a little bit more than 24 hours later. At that moment, I understood what like loss of control meant, because there was nothing I could do to fix it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Blake had pineoblastoma, a rare but fast moving aggressive tumor. He underwent brain surgery just after Christmas 2019. Then he started chemo but his tiny body reacted badly to it. Blake went into cardiac arrest and was rushed to the E.R.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: I said a prayer, please let him survive. Please do not let this be it for my child.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Rene`s prayer was answered that day, Blake survived.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: Blake was treated with chemotherapy drugs that were developed four decades ago. It`s an injustice that a child is being treated with drugs that are developed for adults.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Nearly 5,000 children are diagnosed with brain tumors each year and 1,800 children die from cancer. And yet, just four percent of the billions spent every year on cancer research focuses on pediatric cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAUL NORTHCOTT, ST. JUDE CHILDREN`S RESEARCH HOSPITAL: We need desperately to develop better therapies so that we can give those patients and those families, a better outlook and survival outcome.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: During the long hours at Blake`s bedside, Rene was determined to fight for change. She found comfort in the Bible in the story of David versus Goliath.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: I was facing my own Goliath and that was this cancer that was inside my child`s body. This looks like a big monster, but we will topple it. And I was inspired by that.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Inspiration that led her to tap out the words on her iPhone to read to Blake, her own children`s book, The Miracle Workers: Boy Versus Beast.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: The Miracle Workers will solve every worry, call on them now and they`ll come in a hurry.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Rene had a one person focus group.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: I just saw this smile from air to air and I`m like, I think I nailed it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Sadly, Blake Vince Payne never got a chance to hold the book, he succumbed to the monster that is cancer just a month after his second birthday.\par \par \uc2 But his legacy lives on through Rene and the book she wrote in his honor. 100 percent of the proceeds go to fund fighting pediatric cancer.\par \par \uc2 MARSH: I can`t give him a bath and give him food and dinner and brush his teeth. So, this book is my way of continuing to mother Blake, continuing to just honor him and his life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Such a beautiful boy who leaves behind such an incredible legacy. And so far, Rene has helped raise more than $300,000 for cancer research with a third of that going to St. Jude`s Children`s Research Hospital.\par \par \uc2 And Peter, you can see her strength. It`s so incredible and inspiring. And also, it just stops you cold to hear the fact that he was treated with medicine that was developed for adults for decades ago.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: My wife and I know Rene and her sister Michelle, were so impressed by that whole family and send them strength with that book. The Miracle Workers is one that we now share with other families who are dealing with challenges and gives them strength too.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Absolutely and that is Blake`s legacy. It`s really remarkable. Rene wrote a personal essay by the way about Blake and her fight for pediatric cancer funding on our website. You can read it. Please read it at Today.com.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`re going to be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:47:59]\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Welcome back to the TODAY Show pasture. A nice big hug can easily make anyone who`s having a bad day feel better, whether it`s from a family member or a snuggle from your favorite pets.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But there is now a new trend. Well, or should I call it a, you got that right.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Very well done.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s popping up across the country where people are cuddling cows to help keep them calm.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So, of course, NBC got a short set to check it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GADI SCHWARTZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): At a Southern California farm, it`s lunchtime for some holistic healers. And no it`s not these humans.\par \par \uc2 ELLIE LAKS, FOUNDER, THE GENTLE BARN: Sweet boy.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Instead here, it`s the cows who offer a kind of rejuvenation that you have to see and feel to believe.\par \par \uc2 LAKS: Come on, Lulu.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Ellie Laks runs The Gentle Barn, a place for a cow she and her husband have saved from slaughterhouses are brought to be rehabilitated.\par \par \uc2 LAKS: Jay has saved animals from the darkest places on this planet and brought them home to The Gentle Barn to heal.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: And in that healing, Ellie has found building trust with cows can be therapeutic for people too.\par \par \uc2 LAKS: What made you sign up for a cow hug therapy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I mean, who doesn`t want to hug a cow, right?\par \par \uc2 LAKS: The best way to hug a cow is put your arms around them. But more importantly, put your faces on them and close your eyes and try to find their breathing and kind of connect your breathing with theirs.\par \par \uc2 Whether they`re suffering from depression or anxiety or loneliness. They come to The Gentle Barn. They hug these cows, the cows fill them with hope and with connection. They`re able to exhale for the first time in a long time. Stop thinking and just connect and feel the unconditional love from these cows.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Ellie says after years of struggling with their own trauma, there was something deeply moving in joining a cow in meditation.\par \par \uc2 LAKS: Kneel down beside her and just lean your body on her and put your face on her and close your eyes and just spend a minute with her.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: She feels so big and I feel so little. Just so massive and warm. There`s a serenity here.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:07]\par \par \uc2 LAKS: Hi, Faith.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Her husband Jay tells us Faith the cow is partially blind.\par \par \uc2 JAY WEINER, CO-FOUNDER, THE GENTLE BARN: When we tell a group of blind children that come to The Gentle Barn about her story and how it saved her life that she was blind and that she`s resilient. It inspires them and it gives them hope.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Jay and Ellie say here, every cow has a story, just like the humans who come hoping to share a moment of their tranquility and peace.\par \par \uc2 The sun is shining, breeze is blowing. And we`re cuddling cows.\par \par \uc2 For Today, Gadi Schwartz, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It`s so sweet. I love that the people are rescuing the cows and the cows are rescuing the people right back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Gadi made a new friend.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still to come right here from "Ant Man" to real life superhero, how Paul Rudd surprised one of his newest young fans.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We`re back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Still to come on TODAY, the scary and often deadly scenes when lightning strikes. How you can protect yourself when you see a big storm brewing.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, remembering "Sopranos" star Tony Sirico.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: But first, these messages\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220709ei7900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Battle Over Abortion And Growing Concerns About Private Data Some Apps May Be Collecting On You\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Jake Ward \par \uc2 493 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Many women use their phones and computers to track pregnancies and other sensitive health information. But since Roe v. Wade was overturned, many now fear that information could one day be used against them. Jake Ward has this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARD, NBC NEWS TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Steph Black began using period tracking apps in high school.\par \par \uc2 STEPH BLACK, USES PERIOD TRACKING APP: I`m meticulously tracking what`s happening to me and my body.\par \par \uc2 WARD: But when Roe vs. Wade came under threat, social media exploded with warnings about them with women fearing their digital activity could be used against them.\par \par \uc2 BLACK: If I lived in a state where abortion was actively being criminalized, I would not use a period tracker.\par \par \uc2 WARD: At least 1300 women have been investigated, detained, or arrested for their pregnancy outcomes since 2006. And increasingly, phones provide the evidence. Purvi Patel charged in 2015 with illegally taking abortion pills had her text messages used in court. Prosecutors used Latice Fisher`s web searches for abortion pills to pursue charges against her after a late stillbirth in 2017. The case was later dropped.\par \par \uc2 Now, privacy experts are worried about apps.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA CONTI-COOK, FELLOW, \uc2 FORD FOUNDATION\uc2  TECHNOLOGY: People should secure their devices, secure their communications, and secure their browsing history.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Many free apps sell information about their users to data brokers. Some companies are pivoting. The creators of flow who settled with the FTC in 2021 for allegedly sharing sensitive health data with marketers have just announced an anonymous mode. But Steph Black now says she prefers an app called Euki. It can track periods and sexual encounters and provides information on contraception and abortion without going to a browser. Yuki was originally built by a nonprofit for women in Indonesia where abortion has long been illegal.\par \par \uc2 KELLY BLANCHARD, PRESIDENT, IBIS REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH: That is even more important now that we`re seeing increased restrictions on abortion access in the United States.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Euki is built for the post-Roe world. Its data never leaves the phone, and it has features to prevent anyone from coercing a user into granting access. One secret command seemingly breaks the app for instance.\par \par \uc2 BLACK: You can just say, oh, the app must be crashing.\par \par \uc2 WARD: And personal data about sexual partners, abortion medications, and doctor`s visits can be erased with a tap or on a schedule should the unthinkable happen.\par \par \uc2 BLACK: This is very scary data to just have on your phone.\par \par \uc2 WARD: You wouldn`t trust anybody with this?\par \par \uc2 BLACK: I wouldn`t trust anyone with this.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Jake Ward, NBC News Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back in a moment with incredible video of police risking everything to pull a man from a burning car.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220707ei760005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 6, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Llamas, Pete Williams, Miguel Almaguer, Gabe  Gutierrez, Andrea Mitchell, Jake Ward, Emilie Ikeda, Molly Hunter \par \uc2 3,982 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight the chilling confession for the man charged with a fourth of July parade shooting.\par \par \uc2 The suspect making his first court appearance today after officials say he admitted to opening fire on the crowd in Highland Park, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Police also saying he fled to Wisconsin where he considered unleashing a second attack on a celebration there. And new clues on why he may have chosen the Fourth of July for his rampage.\par \par \uc2 Plus, how was the suspect able to buy five guns after family members reported to police he threatened to kill everyone.\par \par \uc2 And we`re learning more about the victims, among them the parents of a 2- year-old boy, the father gunned down while shielding his son.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the new report on the Uvalde school shooting. Finding an officer had the gunman in his sights before he went into the school. Why he didn`t take the shot.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s call to the wife of Brittney Griner. What he vowed to do after the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  stars handwritten plea to be free from Russia.\par \par \uc2 The January 6 investigation, after weeks of mounting pressure, the key witness now agreeing to testify.\par \par \uc2 And caught on body cam. The fiery car rescue, the hero officers speaking out tonight.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. We`re getting a much fuller picture of the apparent plot, the frightening near-miss for another community, and the heartbreaking death of loss following the July 4 attack on parade-goers in Highland Park, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Today, the state`s attorney there saying the 21-year-old now charged in the murders of seven people has confessed and that he went into details about what he had done. Officials revealing that after the Highland Park shootings, Robert Crimo III considered unleashing a second attack two and a half hours away in Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 While a possible motive is unclear tonight, a series of numbers are being examined as possible clues. His history with police and the guns he own also under scrutiny, as a grieving community opens its aching heart for a child orphaned and the shooting. Tom Llamas starts off our coverage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, officials say Robert Crimo III has confessed telling police he`s the man behind that horrific attack on a holiday parade that killed seven and injured dozens more.\par \par \uc2 ERIC RINEHART, LAKE COUNTY STATE ATTORNEY: His statement was voluntary. He went into details about what he had done.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Police also saying Crimo considered a second shooting in Wisconsin where he drove following the attack in Highland Park.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS COVELLI, DEPUTY CHIEF, LAKE COUNTY SHERIFF`S OFFICE: He seriously contemplated using the firearm in his vehicle to commit another shooting in Madison.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: While no motive has been made public, police have a theory as to why Crimo chose July 4 as the date of the attack. The numbers four and seven visible on this car parked outside of his home\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: He had some type of affinity towards the number four and seven and inverse was seven-four.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The 21-year-old suspect made his first appearance in court today appearing virtually. He`s been held without bond, facing seven counts of first-degree murder with more charges likely. And tonight, questions are growing about how Crimo was able to buy five weapons legally despite being on the radar of law enforcement. I spoke to Steve Greenberg who is representing Crimo`s parents.\par \par \uc2 People are upset with the alleged shooter`s parents. They`re telling me how do they not know something like this wasn`t going to happen? Do the parents in this situation bear some responsibility?\par \par \uc2 STEVE GREENBERG, REPRESENTING CRIMO III`S PARENTS: We can always look at an event and then in hindsight, say, oh, these were the warning signs. There is no history of him ever engaging in a single violent act. It`s easy to say, now, well, the parents should have known but the fact is that nobody knew, nobody saw anything, because there was nothing to see.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Tonight, Highland Park mourn seven lives cut short, including 69- year-old Eduardo Uvaldo who died this morning. His daughter says he was shot in the back of the head while attending the parade with his family. And Katie Goldstein -- Lester spoke yesterday with her heartbroken daughter, Cassie.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE GOLDSTEIN, MOTHER DIED DURING THE HIGHLAND PARK SHOOTING: I can see her face and she was not breathing and there was blood everywhere down the steps, and so I knew she was dead. And then they had to take me away in a police car and I had to spend the day covered in her blood.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: 2-year-old Aiden McCarthy survived but his parents Kevin and Irina did not. The Chicago Sun Times reporting Kevin`s heroic final act, shielding his young son from the bullets.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:01]\par \par \uc2 TONY COLON, EXTENDED FAMILY OF THE MCCARTHY`S: The little boy which now everyone knows as Aiden was underneath the father.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Tony and Irina Colon are part of the McCarthy`s extended family. They felt compelled to help little Aiden so they started a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  for the little boy. The donations taking off like wildfire, growing from a few 1000 to now more than $2 million.\par \par \uc2 IRINA COLON, EXTENDED FAMILY OF THE MCCARTHY`S: It`s overwhelming. People are donating not just money. They`re donating everything, like their time, babysitting, wipes, everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom joins us now from Highland Park. Tom, going back to court today. Could more charges be filed against the suspect?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (on camera): Lester, prosecutors here continue to say that he could face additional charges including attempted murder for all those people that were injured out there in the parade route. If he`s convicted, he faces life in prison without parole. Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, thank you. And we want to take a closer look at the question so many are asking. How was the suspect able to buy guns despite his past threats and two encounters with police? We get more on that now from Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Anyone wanting to buy a gun or ammunition in Illinois must first get a Firearm Owner`s Identification Card issued by the State Police. It can be denied if the applicant "poses a clear and present danger to the community." State police say they were notified in 2019 when Highland Park Police were called at Robert Crimo`s house and told he was threatening to kill people. They confiscated several knives.\par \par \uc2 But in a statement, State Police say because no one was arrested and no one in the family made a formal complaint, the matter was concluded. They also say Crimo told the local police at the time that he did not feel like harming himself or others. And they say the knives were actually returned to Crimo`s father later that day because he said they belong to him. The father`s lawyer says there was no threat.\par \par \uc2 GREENBERG: When the police got there, everyone said the situation wasn`t dangerous. It was overblown. The police didn`t perceive it to be a dangerous situation.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Three months later Crimo applied for a firearm owners ID. Because he was 19, he needed a parent`s permission, and police say his father signed the consent form. The State Police say there was nothing then to indicate that Crimo presented, in the words of a state law, a clear and present danger. If there had been, the card would not have been issued.\par \par \uc2 Advocates of stricter gun control say people in the community and police officers need to be more aware that they can seek to block a person from having or buying guns under the Illinois Red Flag Law.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FEINBLATT, PRESIDENT, EVERYTOWN FOR GUN SAFETY: Obviously, the police at one point felt that it was absolutely necessary to take the knives away. This was somebody who said that he was threatening to kill everyone and you present the evidence before a judge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter, what about the requirement in the new federal law that calls for an enhanced background check for people under 21? Would that made a difference in this case?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Probably not. The new federal law does call for thoroughly checking out a younger person`s background for any juvenile convictions or mental health treatments or commitments. But the police say there were none of those in Crimo`s record. Even so, the new law does provide money to encourage more use of red flag laws, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re also getting stunning new details tonight of the mass shooting at the elementary school in Uvalde, Texas. A new report finding police had another opportunity to stop the gunman before he even entered the school. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Just before the bloodshed at Robb Elementary, a Uvalde police officer had the gunman in his sights, but asked his supervisor for permission to shoot the suspect outside the building, a request that went unanswered as the killer made his way inside the school.\par \par \uc2 The stunning revelation part of a new report released today, reading in part, a reasonable officer would conclude that the use of deadly force was warranted. But the officer who told investigators he was worried if he missed the shot he might hit students never took it and 21 lives were lost.\par \par \uc2 The 24-page report highlighting a number of possible missteps, critical issues investigators say that could have prevented the tragedy that followed. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: As we saw in Uvalde, the gun violence epidemic in this country is hitting children especially hard. It`s now the leading cause of death among kids and teens in the U.S. Gabe Gutierrez takes us inside one hospital trying to change that in our new series crime and consequences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): When Madison Blackman was rushed to the hospital, the bullet had barely missed her brain.\par \par \uc2 MADISON BLACKMAN, HIT BY A BULLET: Immediately I knew I was going to die. I`ve seen black. It was like ink spilled in my eyes.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: A far cry from the carefree social media videos typical of so many 16-year-olds. Instead, one night in January, she says she was out in a car with friends and Brooklyn went out of nowhere --\par \par \uc2 BLACKMAN: The bullets started hitting the car. It sounded like somebody had a handful of maybe 20 balloons and they all started popping one by one.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:08]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Did you immediately realize what it was?\par \par \uc2 BLACKMAN: Yes, I know it was gunshots.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: An ambulance took her here, the ER at Cohen Children`s Medical Center run by \uc2 Northwell Health\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ve never seen numbers like this before.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Where Dr. Chethan Sathya is a pediatric trauma surgeon.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHETHAN SATHYA, PEDIATRIC TRAUMA SURGEON, COHEN CHILDREN`S MEDICAL CENTER: We`ve already seen more kids this year with bullet wounds than any other year total in history at the Children`s Hospital.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: This is the largest level one pediatric trauma center in the state. And over the last few months, it started screaming for something different.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, on this side, it goes through the questions.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Any teenager who comes to the ER for any reason is now asked a series of what some might say are uncomfortable questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How often have you heard guns being shot? And in the past six months, including today, has someone pulled a gun on you?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Nurse Alex Pavis and her colleagues are hoping to not just treat gunshot wounds, but prevent them.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever think that this will be part of your job description?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Shootings are now the leading cause of death for children and teens in the U.S. Every day, 22 of them are shot.\par \par \uc2 The topic of guns is often seen as political, but you see it as a public health issue.\par \par \uc2 SATHYA: It is a public health issue. I mean, it is the number one killer of kids in our country right now.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Six months later, Madison Blackman`s physical wounds are healed. The psychological ones are not.\par \par \uc2 BLACKMAN: Why does it have to be me. What is the reason? Why did I stay alive for? Why did I survive?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Do you think this has gotten worse?\par \par \uc2 BLACKMAN: Absolutely. I feel like after COVID, things have just gotten more ruthless.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Ruthless, a powerful adjective for a 16-year-old.\par \par \uc2 BLACKMAN: Being so young, it makes you feel like you have to grow up so fast in a place like this.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: A young girl who instinctively knew those weren`t balloons, but gunshots. Gabe Gutierrez, NBC News New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And a programming note, I`ll be talking to New York City Mayor Eric Adams about rising crime tomorrow on NIGHTLY NEWS.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn right now though to a major development of the January 6 Committee`s investigation. \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , the former White House Counsel under former President Trump has agreed to testify behind closed doors on Friday according to a person familiar with the situation. The panel has subpoenaed Cipollone last week after a former White House aide said he opposed Mr. Trump`s plan to travel to the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, President Biden facing growing pressure as waiting deeper into the Brittney Griner case. The President calling Griner`s wife today after the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  superstar pleaded with him to help free her from Russia. We get more than all this from Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): After an emotional plea for help from Brittany Griner to President Biden, and a letter from more than 1100 Black women leaders, the President and Vice President Harris called Brittany runner`s wife Cherelle today promising all possible assistance to bring Brittany home. The \uc2 WNBA\uc2  superstar`s head coach Vanessa Nygaard hearing the news from us.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any reaction to the President having called Cherelle after your pressure and all of the other things that we`ve been reporting?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NYGAARD, COACH, \uc2 WNBA\uc2 : To get that call from President Biden and Vice President Harris and to know that they`ve read the letter, and that they`re going to react and respond, this is great, great news. And we hope to have more progress continue.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: In a desperate appeal, the two-time Olympic gold medalist had written Mr. Biden two days ago. "I`m terrified I might be here forever," facing a possible 10-year sentence after Russian authorities say vape cartridges containing cannabis oil were found in her luggage. Now, the President has told Cherelle he`s sending Brittany a personal letter today responding to her handwritten plea.\par \par \uc2 That may relieve the pressure from Griner`s team and friends who are rallying in Phoenix tonight, but not from the family of American businessman Paul Whelan who the U.S. says has been wrongfully detained in Russia since 2018. His family telling NBC News Tonight, the White House outreach should be the same for all wrongful detainees, not just those with celebrity or connections. Andrea Mitchell, NBC News Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, the battle over abortion and growing concerns about private data some apps may be collecting on you. And the race to rescue a man from a burning car. The heartstopping video.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Many women use their phones and computers to track pregnancies and other sensitive health information. But since Roe v. Wade was overturned, many now fear that information could one day be used against them. Jake Ward has this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARD, NBC NEWS TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Steph Black began using period tracking apps in high school.\par \par \uc2 STEPH BLACK, USES PERIOD TRACKING APP: I`m meticulously tracking what`s happening to me and my body.\par \par \uc2 WARD: But when Roe vs. Wade came under threat, social media exploded with warnings about them with women fearing their digital activity could be used against them.\par \par \uc2 BLACK: If I lived in a state where abortion was actively being criminalized, I would not use a period tracker.\par \par \uc2 WARD: At least 1300 women have been investigated, detained, or arrested for their pregnancy outcomes since 2006. And increasingly, phones provide the evidence. Purvi Patel charged in 2015 with illegally taking abortion pills had her text messages used in court. Prosecutors used Latice Fisher`s web searches for abortion pills to pursue charges against her after a late stillbirth in 2017. The case was later dropped.\par \par \uc2 Now, privacy experts are worried about apps.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA CONTI-COOK, FELLOW, \uc2 FORD FOUNDATION\uc2  TECHNOLOGY: People should secure their devices, secure their communications, and secure their browsing history.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Many free apps sell information about their users to data brokers. Some companies are pivoting. The creators of flow who settled with the FTC in 2021 for allegedly sharing sensitive health data with marketers have just announced an anonymous mode. But Steph Black now says she prefers an app called Euki. It can track periods and sexual encounters and provides information on contraception and abortion without going to a browser. Yuki was originally built by a nonprofit for women in Indonesia where abortion has long been illegal.\par \par \uc2 KELLY BLANCHARD, PRESIDENT, IBIS REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH: That is even more important now that we`re seeing increased restrictions on abortion access in the United States.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Euki is built for the post-Roe world. Its data never leaves the phone, and it has features to prevent anyone from coercing a user into granting access. One secret command seemingly breaks the app for instance.\par \par \uc2 BLACK: You can just say, oh, the app must be crashing.\par \par \uc2 WARD: And personal data about sexual partners, abortion medications, and doctor`s visits can be erased with a tap or on a schedule should the unthinkable happen.\par \par \uc2 BLACK: This is very scary data to just have on your phone.\par \par \uc2 WARD: You wouldn`t trust anybody with this?\par \par \uc2 BLACK: I wouldn`t trust anyone with this.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Jake Ward, NBC News Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back in a moment with incredible video of police risking everything to pull a man from a burning car.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back with an incredible rescue. Police braving flames to pull a man from a burning car. Here`s Emilie Ikeda on their extraordinary effort to get him out alive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It`s in moments like this every second matters. A trio of officers furiously trying to pull a driver from his burning car over the weekend. At this point, police in Ridgefield Park, New Jersey had already busted open the car window and used fire extinguishers to slow the blazes spread.\par \par \uc2 The 54-year-old had been locked inside from an electrical issue with the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY D`ELIA, PATROLMAN, RIDGEFIELD PARK POLICE DEPARTMENT: It felt like we only have seconds and you know we had to get them out as quick as we can.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Is there ever concern that you could be injured? Were you worried about your own safety?\par \par \uc2 NICOLAS D`ALTO, PATROLMAN, RIDGEFIELD PARK POLICE DEPARTMENT: Looking back, I realize how close I was to the flames. And I could feel the heat behind me but my focus was solely on him and getting him out\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: With quick thinking and all their strength, officers Anthony D`Elia, Nick D`Alto, and Sergeant Nick Triano were able to pull the driver to safety. Only seconds later, the car was completely engulfed.\par \par \uc2 The driver was able to go home that same day.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS TRIANO, PATROLMAN SUPERVISOR, RIDGEFIELD PARK POLICE DEPARTMENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Is this what motivates you?\par \par \uc2 TRIANO: Yes. For me, I know personally, I`m sure these guys too, was making sure that that happened that night.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: A life-saving and life-changing rescue.\par \par \uc2 Is this a night that you will never forget?\par \par \uc2 TRIANO: As long as I live, I`ll remember every single detail of that night.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Emilie Ikeda, NBC News Ridgefield, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: True heroes. When we come back, the story of one family`s Odyssey from Ukraine to the U.S. and the friend who convinced them to come.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally tonight, three months ago, we met a family on their dangerous trek out of Ukraine. Now, they`ve made it to the U.S. with the help of a friend who wouldn`t take no for an answer. Here`s Molly Hunter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): We meet American Pete Yevnek, his best friend Anton, and wife Ola and their daughter Lisa back in Lviv. Pete traveled from Wisconsin to Ukraine to convince the family to move to safety. So, after fleeing from their home in Kyiv, Anton and Ola were then facing that impossible decision to stay in the country or to leave and for how long.\par \par \uc2 Their train platform goodbye, one of millions over the last four months was wrenching. Pete then accompanied Ola and Lisa, has gone down to Poland and Anton who can`t leave the country left alone. While in Poland, Ola applied to the new U.S. relocation program for Ukrainian refugees. And remarkably, the good news came more quickly than expected.\par \par \uc2 It would be good to stay in the U.S., she says, as long as it`s dangerous in Ukraine. So, once again, Pete boarded a plane to Poland to help a friend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m just so excited to be able to get them away from everything that`s been going on in the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: But this time, they would all be traveling back to the U.S. together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Traveling so far from Ukraine is going to be very difficult for Ola. She`s nervous about it. She`s uncomfortable about it.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: At the airport, Ola calls Anton who`s back in Kyiv once more while on the same continent.\par \par \uc2 We miss you a lot, she says. Lisa wants to see you so badly.\par \par \uc2 Touching down at O`Hare International Airport, Ola and Lisa`s first-ever time in America.\par \par \uc2 I can`t believe this is happening, she says, but I still have high hopes I`ll return to my home country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I might save my child in America. Maybe better safe -- America --\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: Mother and daughter now living with their friend Pete in Wisconsin 5000 miles from Anton and from home but so grateful to be here. Molly Hunter, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220707ei7600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for July 3, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Maggie Vespa, George Solis, Josh Lederman, Matt  Bradley, Priscilla Thompson; Savannah Sellers \par \uc2 3,850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:30:00]\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, growing protests after new police body cam footage shows a black man dying in a hail of gunfire. Authorities in Akron, Ohio say the graphic video shows Jayland Walker shooting at officers from his moving car after he fled a traffic stop and police chased him then running from a car in a ski mask without a weapon. Moments later, officers opened fire. The family`s attorney says he was hit 60 times. Tonight, officials calling for calm as protestors demand answers.\par \par \uc2 More frustration for holiday travelers, new delays and cancelations, and one airline responding tonight after a glitch leaves 12,000 future flights without a pilot.\par \par \uc2 Plus, 16 million Americans facing a new severe weather threat as we head into the 4th of July.\par \par \uc2 The January 6th investigation, a top committee member saying they could make criminal referrals to the Department of Justice involving former president Trump.\par \par \uc2 Air show tragedy, a jet truck driver dying in a fiery stunt. His family now speaking out.\par \par \uc2 The new warning for people looking purchase a pet, some stores are offering to pets for lease with hidden fees that can lead to heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 And one of July 4th`s greatest traditions is back, and sounding better than ever.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. In the moments right before he was shot by police early Monday morning, 25-year-old Jayland Walker was not armed. On that point, everyone agrees. In body cam video released today, you see the long police chase when Walker fled after he was pulled over. Police pointing to this image they say shows him firing at police from his car. His family is questioning that. Police also say they found a gun in the driver`s seat with ammunition and what looks like a wedding ring next to it. But, again, Walker did not have a weapon when he ran from that car and eight officers opened fire.\par \par \uc2 The police chief today said he has a lot of questions, but is letting an independent investigation move forward. Walker`s family called for peaceful protests only, and there were large gatherings in Akron today.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Maggie Vespa. And a warning, there are disturbing images.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS (voice over): Nearly one week after Jayland Walker`s death and facing growing community pressure, officials in Akron, Ohio releasing the body camera footage.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HORRIGAN, MAYOR OF AKRON, OHIO: I won`t mince words, the video you are about to watch is heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: it shows a harrowing police chase followed by a stunning hail of bullets, an estimated 90 rounds fired by eight Akron, Ohio officers killing and leaving 25-year-old Jayland Walker with 60 wounds to the body, according to authorities.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MYLETT, AKRON POLICE CHIEF: My youngest is 25 years of age as well. And I cannot imagine what life would be like without my son.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: The approximately seven minutes of raw video released alongside a second narrated clip gives new insight into what preceded the shooting early Monday morning. Police say they tried to pull Walker over for an unspecified traffic violation and an equipment violation, which is not shown on tape. But they say he took off, driving at high speeds and Walker fired at them. Police saying you can see the flash from the gun from this security camera.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 21 shots fired. That vehicle just had a shot come out of its door.\par \par \uc2 MYLETT: Shot is fired, that changes the nature of the contact.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Authorities say Walker later ran from his car, wearing a ski mask. Officers, investigators say, fired tasers but missed. What followed, that deadly barrage of bullets. Police tonight saying officers said they thought Walker was turning toward them reaching for a gun. Walker was unarmed when he was shot, though police say they found a gun and what appears to be a wedding ring in Walker`s car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shoot somebody 60, 70 times, like nothing inhumane --\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Anger in Akron boiling over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have sons ourselves, it could be any of our sons. And this is just horrible, like that many times?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are done dying like this, in this manner, with this fate. Nobody should ever suffer the fate that Jayland Walker did.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Tonight, the family revealing just a month ago Walker lost his fiance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, we know that immediately after the death of his fiance, he was sad. But he wasn`t exhibiting any kind of behavior that scared the family.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: The family`s attorneys say the 25-year-old food delivery driver had no criminal record, his mother and sister pleading for peace overwhelmed with loss.\par \par \uc2 [18:35:00]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why, why for this to happen in such a manner, such a terrible, terrible way. Just why?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Maggie joins me now. Maggie, what do we know about the eight officers involved in the shooting? Have they been placed on leave?\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Kate, they have. Those eight officers who remained unnamed at this point were placed on paid administrative leave, which we`re told is standard procedure, according to Akron P.D. At the same time, officials say those officers have yet to be interviewed about the shooting, but the police union here saying they absolutely plan to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, an outside state agency is handling the independent investigation into the shooting. And as you saw, the community is demanding answers. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Maggie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s switch now to those holiday travel frustrations. Today brought more cancelations and delays at airports. And for those who thought driving was a better idea, gas prices just went up in some states thanks to a tax hike. George Solis has the late\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, NBC NEWS (voice over): Before the fireworks even begin, tonight, many Americans already blowing up in frustration.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve never come to an airport before wondering whether my flight is going to take off or not. So, it`s a little nerve-racking.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: The last-minute scramble to get home for the 4th of July is on. \uc2 TSA\uc2  screened more than 4.6 million since Friday across the nation`s airports. Those planning ahead still burned by ongoing delays and cancelations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My wife booked two flights, and one of them canceled.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Since Friday, more than 1400 flights have been canceled and more than 15,000 have been delayed.\par \par \uc2 What`s happening at our nation`s airports?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM MCGEE, AMERICAN ECONOMIC LIBERTIES PROJECT AND TRAVEL SENIOR FELLOW: It`s a mess right now. There`s no other way of saying it. I`ve never seen anything like this, not in all these years.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Travel experts say it`s a perfect storm where supply isn`t meeting demand, calling on airlines to be more transparent about their staffing.\par \par \uc2 MCGREE: They have to get in front of this. They have to be honest about it.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  experienced a scheduling glitch Friday night that allowed air crew to drop flights, leaving up to 12,000 flights without pilots through July. The airline, in a statement, said the issue has been fixed, adding they do not anticipate any operational impact.\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: This is not about this one incident this weekend. This is much bigger. As you know, I hate to be negative, but it`s not going to fix itself next week.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Drivers facing other challenges.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think the gas prices are insane.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: The national average now around $4.81 a gallon, still a bargain compared to California, where gas is around $6. And prices are projected to creep even higher. The Golden State alongside six other states all imposed new gas taxes starting Friday. Nonetheless, some drivers still happily shelling out the green to see some red, white and boom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Worth every penny, right, to be with family over 4th of July weekend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: George, I feel like I keep asking this, but when can we expect some relief?\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (on camera): Yes, Kate. Well, \uc2 TSA\uc2  officials say this turbulent travel period will likely continue all summer through July and early August. But good news, there is supposed to be some reliefs past Labor Day when travel usually slows down. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Yes, that`s the end of summer. George, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Bad weather could also add to the travel chaos. 16 million people are at risk for severe storms tomorrow. We`re talking hail, wind and rain. Areas to watch, the plains states, the great lakes region, including Chicago, Madison and Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 Now we turn to Washington and the January 6th committee. Top Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  now saying more witnesses are coming forward and that the panel may end up referring criminal charges against former President Trump to the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Josh Lederman reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tonight, the January 6th committee promising much more to come, disclosing that new witnesses are providing information.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): Every day, we get new people that come forward.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: With more hearings expected this month, Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  saying the committee may ultimately send the Justice Department a criminal referral for former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): There could be more than one criminal referral.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Cheney also raising eyebrows when asked whether she might run for president in 2024. She`s not ruling it out.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I`ll make a decision about `24 down the road.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: But another Republican might not wait that long. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting Trump could soon jump into the race, potentially even before the midterms. President Biden saying those midterm elections will determine the path forward on abortion rights. But frustrated Democrats are demanding the Biden administration fight back harder after the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Don`t be overwhelmed to the point that we are disheartened and we think that we can`t do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: And as Americans hit the road this July 4th holiday, the president also under pressure over soaring gas prices and inflation. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  mogul \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  slamming the president on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for blaming gas prices on big oil companies, saying it`s either straight ahead misdirection or a deep misunderstanding of what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the White House hitting back, portraying Bezos as part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: If everybody cooperates on this, we could bring the price down at least by about a dollar a gallon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN (on camera): After a weekend at Camp David, the president returns tomorrow for 4th of July festivities with those inflation issues top of mind. On Wednesday, the president will speak in Cleveland, Ohio about economic efforts to help middle and low-income Americans. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Josh Lederman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The top security officer for the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is making a plea to end picketing at justice`s homes. She wants officials in Virginia and Maryland to enforce laws to stop that. Demonstrations ramped up after Roe v. Wade was overturned. Last month, police arrested an armed man near Justice Brett Kavanaugh`s home charging him with attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s turn to Ukraine, where Russian forces are gaining ground in the east, claiming they have taken control of a key city.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley is in Ukraine tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tonight, Russia gaining more ground in Ukraine as the country`s defense ministry claims to have taken the Ukrainian city of Lysychansk, giving them control of an eastern province. President Zelenskyy making this promise, we will return, he said. Ukraine does not give anything up.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is once again bringing the fight to Russia itself. Moscow says Ukrainian missiles killed three people in Belgorod, a Russian city near the border. Civilians on both sides are bearing a huge part of the war`s lethal burden with American volunteers also caught in the crosshairs. Bunny Drueke`s son, Alexander, is one of two military veterans captured after they traveled on their own to help Ukraine. She just had her second phone call with him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Alex sounds tired and a little tense.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Alex told her his captors seemed ready to negotiate for his release.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As a mother, of course, I`m a little worried. But I remember that before Alex went over there, he said, mom, if I should die over there, just know that I was doing what I felt was a noble cause.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: It`s unclear who is holding Alex, but Alex told Bunny it`s likely pro-Russian separatists in Eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 When you speak to your son, do you ever have an urge to say, why did you go?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am so darn proud of him for being willing to give up everything to go over and help these people and to defend democracy and to save, hopefully, this from becoming another world war. I`m just so proud of him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY (on camera): While having taken Lysychansk, the Russians are now focusing their efforts further west on the city of Slovyansk. And that`s where Russian shelling killed six people today. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Matt Bradley in Ukraine for us.\par \par \uc2 We have breaking news tonight about a deadly shooting at one of Denmark`s biggest shopping malls. Police say several people were killed after a gunman opened fire in the capital, Copenhagen. The number of victims remains unclear. Witnesses say some people hid in stores, others fled in panic.\par \par \uc2 It happened near an arena where music superstar Harry Styles was supposed to have a concert tonight. That`s been canceled since out of an abundance of caution. Police have arrested a 22-year-old suspect there.\par \par \uc2 Gun violence is rare in Denmark, where they have some of the strictest firearm restrictions in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead tonight, air show tragedy, the driver`s family speaking out after this deadly jet truck crash.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what exactly is pet leasing, and the new warning tonight if you`re considering it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:45:00]\par \par \uc2 NOW: We`re back now with the horror that unfolded at a Michigan air show. It happened yesterday when a truck built with custom jet engines crashed and exploded not far from spectators.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Priscilla Thompson reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tragedy on the tarmac. At an air show in Battle Creek, Michigan on Saturday, Chris Darnell died after his family says the tire of his jet fuel truck blew out, rupturing the gas tanks, which erupted in flames during one of the show`s fiery stunts.\par \par \uc2 Video shows the shockwave jet truck, which can top more than 350 miles per hour, emerging on the other side of the inferno before rolling several times.\par \par \uc2 What went through your time as you saw the truck rolling?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH MATTHEWS, WITNESS: Fear for him. Just seeing it rolling knowing that he`s going over 300 miles per hour, you just can`t imagine somebody would walk away from that.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Elizabeth Matthews was in the crowd with her two kids.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEWS: A lot of people gasped. Some people like started praying for him.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Darnell had been driving trucks for two decades and just celebrated his 40th birthday. His family devastated. We are so sad, Darnell`s father shared in a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post. He was so well loved by everyone who knew him. Chris so loved the air show business, he was living the dream.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEWS: It is a little comforting to know that, you know, he was doing what he loved when it happened.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The show going on today in Darnell`s honor with a tribute in the sky to their missing man.\par \par \uc2 Priscilla Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Still to come tonight, have you heard of pet leasing? What you need to know about the fees and the contracts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Now to a story that will hit home for anyone who wants to bring home a pet. Did you know you can actually lease a dog or cat? But it comes with the risk of hidden fees that could lead to repossession.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Savannah Sellers with what you need to know before signing any agreement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was very heart broken.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH SELLERS, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tammy Harrington and her daughter, Savannah Derby (ph), lost their dog in a car accident.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We had a dog, he wasn`t even a year old, and he got hit by a car.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: So, to fix that broken heart, they headed to the puppy place in Springfield, Massachusetts, and fell in love with Buttercup and Leila.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We just played with these ones.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: The two Shih Tzus cost over $3,000. So, when the pet store offered a financing option, they signed up without reading the fine print.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you`ve got approved then, they could use that towards the dogs.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: It`s a practice known as pet leasing, but many shoppers don`t know the details or the hidden costs.\par \par \uc2 I think when people hear, leasing, they think of a house or a car, not a pet. Is this similar?\par \par \uc2 JENNIE LINTZ, \uc2 ASPCA\uc2  PUPPY MILL INITIATIVE DIRECTOR: It`s the exact same thing as leasing a car. You pay a month at a time, unless you agree to buy out the dog for, of course, an additional amount of money, you technically do not own the dog.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: The lease agreement stated that if Savannah wanted to own her dogs, it would cost her over $6,000, more than double if she had paid up front. But she says the pet store never gave her any paperwork. The Puppy Place could not be reached for comment. It has now closed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They didn`t give us any information actually. Just fill those out and you can take them home on the computer.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: Like a car being repossessed, they said they had no choice but to turn Buttercup and Leila over the dogs over to the Dakin Humane Society.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They sleep in my bed. I mean, they`re not dogs, they`re my kids. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: But Dakin Humane Society knew that pet leasing, though legal in 42 states, was banned here in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 KARINA KING, DAKIN HUMANE SOCIETY OPERATIONS DIRECTOR: When I called them to say you can have your dogs back, it`s okay, they were just so excited, they started crying.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: When NBC News reached out to MyPetFunding, the leasing company that financed Tammy and Savannah`s dogs, their voicemail said they were no longer offering financing for new customers. Experts suggest asking questions and be aware of what you are signing.\par \par \uc2 LINTZ: You obviously want to scrutinize information that`s in front of you very carefully. Ask for the contract to be printed out, which is often not done.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: Savannah`s message after this emotional ordeal --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would just speak up and fight before just giving up your dog.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: Puppy love worthy of a forever home.\par \par \uc2 Savannah Sellers, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: When we come back, it is one of the nation`s most iconic July 4th celebrations. We`ll tell you why this year is so special for the Boston Pops.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: There`s good news tonight about celebrations and the return of a July 4th celebration, the world renowned Boston Pops Esplanade Orchestra now back at its iconic location after two years away during the pandemic. For musicians and for fans, the music more meaningful than ever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice over): Every year on the 4th of July, the Boston Pops fireworks spectacular is music to the city`s ears. Performed by the Boston Pops Esplanade Orchestra, the Independence Day show with fireworks has been a beloved tradition for nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 But in 2020, when the pandemic hit, the concert took a break from playing at its iconic Hatch Band Shell along the Charles River. This year, it`s back here and we were there with the orchestra for rehearsals. Fans couldn`t be more thrilled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s great. So, we even have family from abroad coming to see it with us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re not from Boston, so I think we`re really excited to see them for the first time.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: 19-year-old violinist Mary Davis is returning to hear them here for the fourth time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For me, it`s seeing the orchestra and watching the 1812 overture and to see everyone really connect over that. It`s such a magical experience.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: This year`s event, just as special for members of ensemble, like longtime flautist Rene Crinzer (ph).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The emotion, I guess, is one of gratefulness and real -- a sense of joy that we have the ability to do this, particularly post- COVID. We`re just so grateful to have each other near us that we can actually be together.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: The orchestra getting ready to bring back old favorites from Monday night`s performance. This gathering, emotional for singer Javier Colon.\par \par \uc2 JAVIER COLON, SINGER: I had to fight back some tears singing the song and hearing them, you know, supporting me. It was just -- it`s what dreams are made of, for sure.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Conductor Keith Lockhart hopes the concert reminds everyone what this holiday is really about.\par \par \uc2 KEITH LOCKHART, CONDUCTOR BOSTON POPS ORCHESTRA: The heart and soul of this event is community. There`s one thing that we can provide to people on this 4th of July, it would be that. Everybody comes together again and realizes how important it is for us as human beings and as Americans to be able to share our experiences with each other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (on camera): What a joy. We wish you and your family a meaningful and peaceful holiday.\par \par \uc2 That is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS on this Sunday. I`m Kate Snow. For all of us here at NBC News, stay safe and have a great night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220704ei7300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for July 3, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 9,526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:14]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, that stunning testimony.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER WHITE HOUSE AIDE AND ASSISTANT TO FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : We are watching the Capitol building get defaced over a lie.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson on President Trump learning his January 6th supporters were armed.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: "I don`t effing care that they have weapons; they`re not here to hurt me."\par \par \uc2 TODD: On Mr. Trump insisting he join the mob at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: The president said something to the effect of, "I`m the effing president, take me up to the Capitol now."\par \par \uc2 TODD: And quoting \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  on learning that the rioters wanted to hang Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: "He thinks Mike deserves it. He doesn`t think they`re doing anything wrong."\par \par \uc2 TODD: But now, with challenges to some of her testimony, Republicans are again dismissing the hearings.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE, (R) LOUISIANA: A partisan committee, a witch hunt, just to keep going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , not to get facts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This morning I`ll talk to committee member \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  and to NBC News legal analyst Danny Cevallos about the legal jeopardy Mr. Trump may now be in.\par \par \uc2 Plus, after Roe.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): I don`t think it could get more confusing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Abortion restrictions are being challenged in more than a dozen states...\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): The trigger law is an extremist law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... with abortion rights supporters worried the Biden administration is not fighting hard enough.\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Democrats` growing doubts about President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Democrats have a very deep niche, and it`s time to allow a new generation to emerge and new talent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why more Democrats, mostly off the record, are saying the president shouldn`t run for re-election.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson; NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali; Matthew Continetti of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 ; and Marianna Sotomayor of The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. it`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history. This is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: A good Sunday morning. And I hope you are enjoying this July 4th weekend.\par \par \uc2 But even as the country celebrates the 246th anniversary of our independence, the lines that separate us seem to be growing bolder and brighter by the day, abortion rights advocates versus abortion opponents; Americans eager to limit the availability of guns versus second amendment enthusiasts; climate change versus "Drill, baby, drill;" blue versus red; left versus right.\par \par \uc2 And no single person personifies our disunity or aggravates our wounds more than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . On Tuesday, in the unscheduled fifth hearing of the January 6th Committee, we heard shocking testimony that has been compared to Watergate`s John Dean. Former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson described nothing less than a violent coup attempt urged on by a president who was more active participant than passive observer.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy testified that Mr. Trump knew the mob was armed, wanted to go to the Capitol with them, even agreed that Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  deserved to be hanged -- and was in danger of being declared unfit to serve by a majority of his Cabinet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Knives, guns, in the form of pistols and rifles, bear spray, body armor, spears.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): We`ve got three men walking down the street in fatigues carrying AR-15s.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I overheard the president say something to the effect of, you know, "I don`t effing care that they have weapons. They`re not here to hurt me. Take the effing mags away. Let my people in. They can march to the Capitol from here."\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING, JANUARY 6TH COMMITTEE CO-CHAIR: President Trump was aware that a number of the individuals in the crowd had weapons and were wearing body armor. And here`s what President Trump instructed the crowd to do.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: We`re going to walk down -- and I`ll be there with you. We`re going to walk down to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Tony described him as being irate. The president said something to the effect of, "I`m the effing president. Take me up to the Capitol now," to which Bobby responded, "Sir, we have to go back to the West Wing."\par \par \uc2 The president reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Engel grabbed his arm, said, "Sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We`re going back to the West Wing. We`re not going to the Capitol." Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (chanting): Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ! Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ! Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I remember Pat saying something to the effect of "Mark, we need to do something more. They`re literally calling for the vice president to be effing hung."\par \par \uc2 And Mark had responded something to the effect of, "You heard him, Pat. He thinks Mike deserves it. He doesn`t think they`re doing anything wrong."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:05:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  from California, who is on the January 6th Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D) CALIFORNIA, MEMBER OF JANUARY 6TH COMMITTEE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with your simple reaction to particularly the former president and a couple people around them disputing certain parts of Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony. And I know -- not the larger part but some of the specific details. What do you make of that push-back, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, we always expected Trump world to try and discredit her, and they are not disappointing us in that regard. I thought her testimony was credible. She has nothing to gain by stepping forward and telling the truth. And Trump world has everything to lose by the truth. So they are doing their best to try and attack her, to discredit her. You know, I am not surprised by this effort, but it is not the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can you describe the efforts the committee makes to corroborate some of the charges Ms. Hutchinson made before she appeared? Did you contact Secret Service, some of these other entities?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, we had interviewed Mr. Ornato several times. His memory does not appear to be as precise as hers. We`re -- certainly would welcome them to come back if they wished to do that.\par \par \uc2 But her overall testimony that the president, then president, wanted to go to the Capitol is consistent with other testimony that we have received. Certainly her testimony that she directly overheard President Trump saying that he didn`t care if they had weapons, if the crowd had weapons, that they were not going to hurt him, and that they could march to the Capitol with their weapons after the speech, that was new, and stunning, really.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, there`s always cameras on the presidential motorcade. Have you gotten any footage? It`s possible there is footage of the alleged incident in the SUV. Have you guys subpoenaed that footage, whether it`s from news organizations or the Secret Service?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, we`ll look at everything. But I think it`s important to note Ms. Hutchinson was relating a story that Mr. Ornato told to her. She never -- she wasn`t in the vehicle. She didn`t see it. She was relating what he told her.\par \par \uc2 The important thing is that no one is disputing that Trump wanted to go to the Capitol. He even said so in his speech.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: There`s a lot of evidence that, when he went back to the White House, he still wanted to go to the Capitol and was certainly well aware of the violence that was going on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You -- you made a diplomatic response about Mr. Ornato, saying that he seems to not have a precise memory. Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  told me earlier this week that -- something similar, he did not have clear memories at that time. \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  went further, and he said that Mr. Ornato "likes to lie."\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this. Has Mr. Ornato testified under oath in any of these interviews that you`ve had with him?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: It`s important to note that it`s -- if you lie to Congress, it`s a crime, whether or not you`re under oath, so...\par \par \uc2 TODD: But was he under oath?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: It`s a crime to lie to Congress when you come in for an interview, whether you come in for a deposition. So I`ll -- I`ll say this. Mr. Ornato was a political appointee of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: It raised a lot of eyebrows at the time, that, unlike all prior Secret Service agents protecting the president, he was then also appointed to be deputy assistant to the president. And so he was involved in clearing the square so the president could hold up a Bible in front...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: ... of the church. I mean, he was involved in all of that. He`s part of Trump world.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I understand that. You`ve been hesitant to confirm or deny whether he`s under oath. And I understand what you`re saying. It is still a crime to lie to Congress. Is it that you`re not sure he was under oath, or he was not under oath?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: I believe he was under oath. But, certainly, if he wants to come back and clarify his prior information, he will also be under oath. I think it`s a mistake to focus on whether or not he was lying to Ms. Hutchinson when he relayed that story. The fact is the president knew there -- his crowd was armed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: We heard the Capitol -- the Metropolitan Police describing seeing assault weapons on this crowd.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:05]\par \par \uc2 He wanted to go down to the Capitol with them. He said that in his speech.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: And we have a lot of evidence that that was true, even when he returned to the White House.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . You officially put a subpoena out to him this past week. Why -- why now?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why not two months ago? Why not three months ago? Why not four months ago?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: We have been engaging Mr. Cipollone. He did have an informal interview, or discussion. That was all he was willing to do. But there have been ongoing discussions to see if he would come in and talk further. After Ms. Hutchinson`s testimony that was so informative, it`s very clear that we would like him to come in.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that he is concerned about executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: That`s not -- that`s not an absolute immunity. It`s -- it falls when there is something more important, and that is true in this case. And certainly the current president, Mr. Biden, has waived executive privilege on most occasions...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: ... when it comes to getting the truth about the events leading up to January 6th.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You want him to come in for -- be subpoenaed to come in for a deposition on July 6th. Is that going to happen on July 6th?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, I hope so. You know, our intention is to hear from him. And I think, given the testimony of Ms. Hutchinson that he was trying to prevent crimes being committed on that day...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: I would assume that he would want to come in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to react to this \uc2 New York Times\uc2  lead. "The federal prosecutors working on the case were just as astonished by Hutchinson`s account of former President Donald J. Trump`s increasingly desperate bid to hold on to power as other viewers. The panel did not provide them" -- meaning DOG -- "with videos or transcripts of her taped interviews with committee members beforehand, according to several officials, leaving them feeling blindsided."\par \par \uc2 Is that a fair characterization, that you blindsided Justice on this?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: I don`t think so. We`re not an arm of the Department of Justice. We`re a legislative committee. They have subpoena power. They could subpoena Ms. Hutchinson. I`m surprised they had not done so. We interviewed her four times. I think that`s publicly known at this point. And the fourth interview was very compelling. And it`s obvious she is being intimidated. People are trying to discredit her. People were trying to dissuade her from testifying. The Trump world was paying for her lawyers, which was very problematic for her. She changed lawyers and got an independent lawyer and then proceeded.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re -- I was surprised that the prosecutors were surprised; what are they doing over there? They have a much greater opportunity to enforce their subpoenas than our legislative committee does.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A point some of us were discussing this morning.\par \par \uc2 The last question I would throw at you is the issue of so-called witness tampering. Do you feel like you have enough evidence to prove that is what`s happened?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, I don`t know. That`s -- you know, charging someone with a crime is not our opportunity. We don`t -- can`t do that. We`re a legislative committee. But I will say this. If witnesses are being intimidated, we don`t plan to just sit by and allow that to happen. We`re going to raise a stink about it. We`re going to refer information publicly to the Department of Justice if witnesses are intimidated. Because it`s a crime. It`s a crime to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we going to see Cassidy Hutchinson testify one more time?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: I don`t know yet. I always leave the chairman to announce the future hearings.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , a member of the January 6th Committee, Democrat from California, good to see you. Thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Happy 4th of July.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You, too.\par \par \uc2 The hearings have raised the prospect that former President Trump faces real legal liability for his actions before and on January 6th, among them obstructing a congressional proceeding; conspiracy to defraud the United States; seditious conspiracy; wire fraud; and, as we just were talking about, possible witness tampering.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now to, sort of, go through this, is our NBC News legal analyst Danny Cevallos.\par \par \uc2 So, Danny, you see those five charges there. You`ve watched these hearings with me. Where are the -- which are the charges that the former president ought to really be concerned about?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: You had the one that I think is the most pertinent dead last on that list, and it is witness tampering.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: And the reason I say so is that Congress specifically enacted the Relevance Statute, Section 1512, in order to make it more expansive, to sweep more broadly and cover any official proceeding.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:05]\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t need to be a grand jury proceeding or a judicial proceeding. And the witness doesn`t even have to actually testify. The witness doesn`t even need to have firsthand knowledge. This statute just prevents or criminalizes any kind of harassment or corrupt persuasion in order to prevent a witness from communicating information to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 And I gotta tell you, I mean, I`ve defended cases where the corrupt persuasion standard is in play. And when you look up what it means to do something corruptly, the definition is "an improper purpose." So you`re right back at square one. And the point I make there is that it`s very malleable. And the government knows that. And that`s why they succeed very often, whenever they bring charges.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think it`s an easier case to bring, in some ways...\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: I do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Witness tampering?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: Absolutely. Any time you have that corrupt persuasion standard - - and it appears in other statues in the U.S. Code, it makes it relatively easy to prove an improper purpose. I mean, what`s an improper purpose?\par \par \uc2 It might be preventing this information from going to law enforcement -- not necessarily testifying under oath. That was Congress`s intent when it passed Section 1512, to make this sweep as broadly as possible and protect witnesses.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What about his actions on January 6th?\par \par \uc2 It more and more looks like he was an active participant rather than, as we stated earlier, a passive observer. Is there a "there" there?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: Potentially, at least according to Judge David Carter, who had an opinion several months ago. Now, I caution folks that, in laying out a kind of map for prosecuting Trump and John Eastman for, say, conspiracy to, I guess, defraud the United States or to obstruct a proceeding, if you`re looking for that, this opinion does give, kind of, a road map, but it was in the context of deciding that there was no privilege.\par \par \uc2 So folks should understand it has no direct effect. It`s not the effect of a probable cause finding. It`s almost like an advisory opinion. It`s almost like the judge saying, "Hey, here`s my take on it," but it really only goes to whether or not privilege applies.\par \par \uc2 But folks at the time pointed out, correctly, that this provided a basic road map for DOJ officials, very much like these congressional hearings are providing a road map for the DOJ. But, at the same time, I put an asterisk on that because whatever power the committee has to obtain information, the DOJ has superpowers compared to the committee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that`s exactly what the congresswoman was just saying. And, in fact, when she was, sort of, responding to this idea that Justice was shocked by Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony, and she just said, basically, "You have more powerful subpoenas than Congress does. What are you guys doing?"\par \par \uc2 What does it tell you that a witness surprised them? Does it tell you that their investigation is not as thorough yet?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: It tells me, if that`s true, that Hutchinson must have flown under their radar. And by "their," I mean the DOJ. Because the DOJ has vast abilities to investigate much more than a congressional committee.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Chuck, in the last few years, we`ve all seen what happens if a witness really doesn`t want to comply with a congressional subpoena. "Ah, forget it, I`m not showing up."\par \par \uc2 And it`s up to Congress to try and go through its, well, let`s say, questionable abilities to enforce those subpoenas. Meanwhile, the DOJ, when it wants a subpoena enforced, it enforces the subpoena. They also can go to a judge and get a search warrant, which is even more powerful, where they do the searching themselves. And, in a grand jury proceeding, everything`s secret. We don`t even know what they`re looking at unless and until they indict.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. So let`s take their surprise at their word, that she flew under the radar. What part of this are they -- it does seem as if they`ve got a lot of effort on -- on the people who went after the Capitol. They got a lot of effort on some of the far-right violent groups. They`re doing something on the elector front. We saw all that action. It does not look like they`ve penetrated the West Wing. That`s essentially what they`re admitting here, if they`re shocked by Cassidy -- that they have not yet touched the president. You think that`s a Garland decision?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: It is a Garland decision. I mean, it could be said that all of these committee hearings are for the American public, but they`re also for an audience of one, and that`s \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the DOJ is going to be circumspect about what they have and what they don`t have. Do I think they were truly shocked by what Cassidy Hutchinson said? Only if she stated something under oath the other day that was for the first time, never before disclosed. That`s really, in my mind, the only way the DOJ could have been surprised. Because, Chuck, this is an institution, the DOJ, that was built to investigate. It was built to persuade witnesses to come in and talk to them or to testify.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you see a witness testify under oath, that`s, kind of, the end of the long path. The DOJ has so many tools to get somebody to come in and bare their soul, not the least of which reminding them that they may be in huge trouble for even lying to a federal agent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Realistically, we know how long DOJ has been working on this. When do you think they would feel that they have enough to start pursuing indictments? Are we six months away, a year away? What`s your estimate?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: That`s always the big question, when and do they have enough? And again, the DOJ was designed to investigate. And for, what, since their inception, they do not let you know. They don`t give a status report on where they are with investigations.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:05]\par \par \uc2 They remain silent. And sometimes you or anyone else may be investigated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Counting on people like lawyers, people like you, to tell us, "Hey, this happens and this happened." That`s the only way we find out.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: Exactly. Yeah, I mean, and people who may be targets may never have known they were targets if the DOJ declines to indict. I mean, that`s how secretive they are and how -- how many more tools they have to not only persuade people to come in and talk to them but obtain documents, search warrants, things like that.\par \par \uc2 So this whole part about the Cassidy Hutchinson shocking the DOJ, that might be a little bit of a spin on their part. It has to be, because, if she was anywhere on the radar, the DOJ would have been all over it. And they would have been over it silently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if she`s not on their radar, that makes you wonder how thorough they`re being.\par \par \uc2 Danny Cevallos, I really appreciate it. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Tuesday`s hearing portrayed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a bit out of control and bent on sedition. How much does it matter to a Republican Party that has been in thrall to him? The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back. The panel is here, NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali; from Homeland Security, Secretary Jeh Johnson; Matthew Continetti of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 ; and Marianna Sotomayor of The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  we`ve been here a million times. It started with attacking \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , and we go down the line. And you have "Access Hollywood" tape, and you think, "Boy, this is the one, no this is the one, no, this the one."\par \par \uc2 Matthew, let me start with you. Is this the one?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Is this week "the one?"\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: No. There have been so much...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bold.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: There`s been so much smoke from these smoking guns over six years that it clouds your eyes. And I think there is some questions about the testimony of Ms. Hutchinson. I think it`s still a long way from her testimony to getting Merrick garland to file charges against a former president of the United States. So while I think this is a reminder to the country of Donald Trump`s irresponsible and impeachable actions after the election of 2020, I don`t think it`s a smoking gun.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: But there`s -- isn`t there so much space between those two things, like getting the DOJ to file and anything changing for Trump? There`s so much space between those things. And I think that, when you see people like the Washington Examiner coming out and saying that he shouldn`t be president again, I mean, there are metrics that are changing. And it was always true during the Trump years that Republicans were always more comfortable saying privately in our text messages than they were on television that this was a very, very bad thing.\par \par \uc2 But I think that you`re, sort of, getting to a point where you almost can`t avoid saying that, when the thing in dispute here is like grabbing at a steering wheel, but the thing that`s not in dispute is the fact that he wanted to go to the Capitol and lead the rioters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Marianna, have you heard from Republicans quietly, like, "All right, I`m out" -- or not yet?\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR, THE WASHINGTON POST: Oh, no. I mean, I was texting a couple of aides who worked for lawmakers who are still very close to Trump. And everyone is pushing it aside. They were -- before even Trump said on his social media platform, you know, "Cassidy Hutchinson, none of this is true; she is an outsider to us," these aides were already saying that.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:05]\par \par \uc2 They all say that this is not going to play a role in the midterms; no one`s paying attention to this. If you ask them about `24, though, and what this means for Trump potentially running, that`s when they start to get a little squeamish. But for the...\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s easier for them to say that?\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: Correct.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jeh?\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: I have to begin with this. While a lot of men are hiding under their desks and lawyers` offices, this hearing has really been a profile in courage among women. \uc2 Caroline Edwards\uc2 , Cass Hutchinson and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . I think that this hearing has been choreographed exceptionally well for the attention span of the average American in 2022. I see a case being developed of the criminal charges that Danny laid out, plus giving aid and comfort to an insurrection. What happened on January 6th was the very definition of an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 I`m concerned -- it`s the former federal prosecutor in me that gets you a lot of cred these days on television -- I`m concerned that the committee may have overreached on the incident in the vehicle. It was colorful. It was vivid. It was collateral to the central charge. If -- and it was secondhand hearsay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me pause you there. Because it`s funny you say that. Because the fact that the former president is obsessing over the incident and nothing else of the testimony, I think speaks volumes.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to how he`s characterizing this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This lady yesterday, there`s something wrong with her? Is there something wrong? She said I jumped from a car and I started strangling -- think of this, I started strangling a Secret Service agent?\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Right.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Who I know very well? I grabbed the steering wheel of a car -- that said that I wanted guns at my rally? I didn`t want guns. I have to speak, too, and I don`t want guns...\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Did you grab the steering wheel? Is that -- is there any truth to that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s fascinating is that they try to find -- I mean, what Trump`s always been good at is finding a specific he thinks he can deny to try to cast doubt on everything.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah. Here`s why I would have hesitated on that. If Ali Vitali tells me that Marianna told her that Matt told Marianna...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... that Chuck Todd hit him...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: ... I would want to know exactly what Matt has to say about that incident firsthand.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: If you`re willing to testify to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. It`s good to know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Under oath?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Under oath, a federal law enforcement officer under oath. So the committee perhaps knows something that the rest of us don`t know. But before I went out with the secondhand hearsay, which is going to get a lot of attention, I`d want to know what the firsthand witness has to say, or...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think, by the time she told us the story, it was -- technically, we`re in a third version -- it`s a third-hand account?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It depends on how you interpret hearsay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: But, you know, that testimony would not have been admissible in a courtroom. And as you know, Secret Service agents don`t normally talk about what they see, what they hear from their protectees. I was a protectee of the Secret Service for three years, and they have to be in a position to hear and see all kinds of very sensitive things.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, look, lawmakers were briefed late on what the Cassidy Hutchinson testimony was going to be -- like, the rank and file of the committee were briefed late on this. There was some discussion among them, I`m told, on whether or not this key detail should have been included, and that now they`re at a point where this is out there, right?\par \par \uc2 And we know that people like the vice chair of the committee, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , have really wanted to push the envelope of what the committee includes. This is obviously one of those headline-grabbing details. But I do think that it`s fair to wonder whether or not her testimony would have been as shocking as it was, and I would argue that it would have been, even if you didn`t have just those few details of what happened in the limo. Because, again, and I think this is where the committee continues to come down on it, is they don`t want you to miss the forest from the trees on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, they could have shared the same incident and not with that detail, that he was irate, that he kept urging...\par \par \uc2 VITALI: That he wanted to go.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... and they -- and they -- and the Secret Service had to tell him "No, no, no." It`s the same allegation.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: The bottom line is this hearing in particular, but all of the J6 Committee hearings, have riveted the Beltway.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 And the question is, is it getting beyond the Beltway? And when you look at Republican Party voters, they are slowly less enthusiastic about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, isn`t that a sign that it`s working?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think that`s more -- I think that`s more a function of time and looking for something new than it is all of these salacious details coming from the committee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Marianna, I think you brought -- you hit on something. It is easier to criticize Trump as it`s a bad idea for you to run again than it is to have to directly deal with this, right?\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: Exactly, yes.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, a lot of aides right now are, kind of, wondering when, if Trump decides to run, when does he want to announce?\par \par \uc2 And they don`t want him to talk about...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Whenever the indictment comes down, right?\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: Well, they don`t want him to talk about this at any point in time, ahead of the midterms. Because he is such a liability. He`s such an unknown. Republicans are on the path to take back the majority in the House. It`s possible they do it in the Senate. They don`t want a random announcement by Trump. Because he can be a liability to a lot of swing voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The irony is that, if the Republicans fail, wherever they fail, it`s all Donald Trump`s fault, either directly or indirectly, either how, Matthew, he got involved in primaries for bad candidates. You could argue Roe v. Wade in an indirect way, and obviously January 6th. Like but for -- the "but for" paragraph on the day after the election, if Republicans have a disappointing night, is all going to be Trump, right?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, SENIOR FELLOW, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Think about the Georgia special elections for the Senate on January 5th by 2021, right? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not a political winner for the Republican Party. And yet many Republicans don`t seem able to accept that fact.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jeh, I`m very curious, this little dispute between the committee and Justice.\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER DHS SECRETARY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If Justice was surprised by Cassidy Hutchinson, to me, that sounds like Justice isn`t really pursuing the West Wing yet.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, the dispute between Congress and the Department of Justice is not new. Any incident that is investigated by both branches, there`s always a fight about who gets to go first, whether or not the committee has to turn over all the testimony and so forth. If in fact the Department of Justice didn`t know what Cass Hutchinson was going to say, that suggests that they`re not focused on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`ve got a long way to go, don`t they?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Or they have a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Also makes sense why they want the committee transcripts, right?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: But--\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure, but as \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  said, they have a lot more powerful subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Of course.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: They`re focused now. I am certain they are focused now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s probably a fair point. All right. We`re going to pause there. Up next is: is the Biden Administration fighting hard enough to secure abortion rights? Not according to many Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to talk to \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade has left Democrats both angry and exasperated. Angry, because it took away a right they had supported and counted on for half a century. And exasperated because many Democrats feel the Biden administration was not prepared for something that they had a little bit of lead time on. And they still believe that he`s not doing enough to ensure abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  argues there`s not a lot the administration can do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA HHS SECRETARY: There is no magic bullet. But if there is something we can do, we will find it and we will do it at \uc2 HHS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Secretary Becerra joins me now.\par \par \uc2 And let me pick up on what you said there. You`ve been spending some days trying to find new avenues. Is there any avenue you`ve found that can actually expand abortion rights in places that it`s going to be taken away?\par \par \uc2 [09:35:04]\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Chuck, if you look at our laws and the way we administer them, when the Supreme Court speaks, unless we`re all going to say that the word of the Supreme Court will no longer have value, we have to heed the word of the Supreme Court. And so we will. But we will continue to find every avenue possible to make sure women have access to the care that they need, including abortion care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about one of the proposals that was out there that got shot down pretty quickly. It was something that a couple of Democrats called for, including \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , which is, maybe, temporary clinics on federal land. You know, fight it in the - let the courts say no, and instead try it. Did you guys look into this at all?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: I think we`re continuing to explore everything that`s out there. The difficulty is that simply because it`s an idea doesn`t mean it can go out into practice. And so what we want to make sure is we can put things out into practice. Because you have people who are right now in need of abortion care services. So we`re going to do what we can to give people something as quickly as we can, even if it may not be everything they`d like, we want to make sure we`re providing everything we can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One of the things that maybe the federal government can strengthen is access to the medications for abortion. What actions do you think you can take that would essentially speed up the process at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Well, we want to make sure that medication abortion, which is currently available in America, continues to be such, that it can be accessed by those who need it most. We will continue to protect those federally required aspects of medication abortion. And we`ll do everything we can, where it`s possible, for example, in terms of emergency care, to make sure that women have access to the services that they need.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You said something to my colleague Kate Snow about transporting women to other states. It was something that maybe \uc2 HHS\uc2  would take the lead on and help with officially. Have you looked into that? And can you legally do it?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: We are exploring the opportunity to work with others to make sure that if a woman is trying to access that care that she needs, that we`ll be supportive of that. There are a lot of partners, public and private, who are looking into this. We`re one of those partners.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are you looking in to see? Is it a legal question? Does it have to do with the Hyde Amendment? What is it that you`re concerned about legally?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: All of the above. We have to make sure, Chuck, that we stay within the confines of the law, and that we have the resources to do it, and that our authorities allow us to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s interesting you say, "confines of the law." One of the hallmarks of the last administration was he obviously pushed the envelope and essentially let the courts stop him.\par \par \uc2 There are many Democrats now who say, you know what? Make the courts stop you. Try this. Get caught trying, whether it`s on federal lands, whether it`s on transportation.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like you`ve got a directive from the president that says, don`t push the envelope.\par \par \uc2 Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: I think it`s just the opposite. The president in his first announcement said that he was tasking us at \uc2 HHS\uc2  to take on a number of issues, including medication abortion. And so he has asked us to seek as aggressive a strategy as we can. But unlike the previous administration, we do intend to respect the law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you tell your former congressional Democratic colleagues who just feel as if the administration-- I looked at the list of things that you guys are doing, and there`s not much you can do. I looked. And it`s pretty clear. Because you don`t want to get outside the law, what do you tell these congressional Democrats that feel like the party`s not fighting hard enough?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Well I tell them, "Give us some good ideas." We`re going to explore everything we can. And I also would ask them to please pass a law.\par \par \uc2 They have it in their power, if they can find the votes to actually codify the Roe decision, which is what we need more than anything else. Executive action. We will find what we can and do as much as we can.\par \par \uc2 But when you are stripped of a right, as the Supreme Court has just done to every woman of childbearing age, it is tough to overcome. It took 50 years for us to get as far as we did. Now we have to figure out how to do this. It will not be easy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the bottom line it sounds like the administration is essentially saying, look. If you want to fix it now, Congress is your only route. There`s not much the executive branch can do. Fair?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Well, Chuck, I wouldn`t go that far, because we will find ways to make sure women exercise their rights and that women are protected. It`s just not going to be easy. The fastest route is to reinstitute that right that women had until five justices on the Supreme Court decided to use their authority in ways that deny millions of Americans the right to good health and perhaps even their life.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Have you thought about the fact that at times when you were in Congress there were some big Democratic majorities, was this just something that folks thought was never really at risk and that`s why it never got brought up for codification?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: I`ve been around long enough to know that nothing`s ever totally safe. But remember, we still haven`t even been able to pass the Equal Rights Amendment. And so this country has a way to go. But certainly I don`t believe this decision by this court and Dobbs is going to stand long.\par \par \uc2 This is just not America. We`re not about taking rights. Most people around the world haven`t looked at the U.S. as the beacon of light because we do things like Dobbs. Just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 So I`m confident that we`re going to get past this. It`s just that for now the five justices on the Supreme Court have made life very difficult and, in some cases, impossible for some women.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that means this has to be done legislatively. There is no other way to fix this because the court is the court for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Well, the Court is the Court. The Court can undo the injustice it just besetted on millions of women. But I don`t expect five justices in this court to do that. And so the next-best route is to pass a law to codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , secretary of the Health and Human Services, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir. Thank you. Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Thanks, Todd. Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  suggests that the Supreme Court might want to reconsider other rights, like same-sex marriage. Why that might not be so easy politically.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, data download time.\par \par \uc2 Last week`s Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe had many wondering what could be next. In fact, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wondered himself. He wrote in a concurring decision about his desire to reconsider some other social contract precedents, including 2015`s Obergefell v. Hodges, which legalized same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 But reversing that decision won`t be easy, as it would land among a public that has already rapidly evolved on that issue. As you can see at the start of this century, support for same-sex marriage was actually below 50 percent; just 42 percent of the country supported it, 55 percent were against. You saw all those ballot initiatives. That was the George Bush reelection.\par \par \uc2 And then what you saw, you saw slow agreement of it. President Obama endorses it. The numbers get close to 50. Obergefell happens. It was already at 55. And then after it`s legal, it shoots up to 71. And in fact, the legalization of same-sex marriage appears to have made more people comfortable identifying honestly about what they are.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:09]\par \par \uc2 In fact, in 2012 just 3.5 percent of the country identified as LGBTQ. That number has more than doubled in less than ten years. And it`s being driven mostly by a younger generation who has grown up in a world where same-sex marriage is legal. So, for instance, among Gen-Z, 20 percent of Gen-Z identify as something other than heterosexual. I mean, as you can see, you go down by age groups and you can see those that have lived more in a world with legal same-sex marriage is more likely to be comfortable identifying as something other than heterosexual. And you can just see the changes there.\par \par \uc2 And just to give you a breakdown of the entire LGBTQ population these days, 30 percent are in -- under the age of 25, according to polling. And as you can see, the numbers get a little bit lower. But under 35, you see nearly a quarter is under 35, and then the numbers get lower. But that is what`s happened here, as -- the longer same-sex marriage has been legal, the more comfortable many Americans are identifying publicly.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, some Democrats are saying President Biden might want to think about not running in 2024.\par \par \uc2 You know who`s not listening? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:50:12]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Democrats appear to be getting a bump, at least for now, after the Supreme Court`s abortion decision. But you know who is not benefiting? President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this NPR/Marist poll that we`ve put together here. In April, Republicans had a three point lead in the generic congressional battle. President`s approval rating sat at 41 percent.\par \par \uc2 Flash forward to June. Same pollster. Now it`s Democrats up seven points. Mr. Biden`s approval rating? It actually went down a point. It`s obviously margin of error stuff, at 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 Jeh Johnson, it`s pretty clear that one of the predictions that was out there was that this abortion decision could energize Democrats in a way that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hadn`t. And not only is it energizing Democrats, it`s still not helping \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: You`re asking me the political question.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that voters are just -- and this is probably a hangover from the Trump years -- just in a cranky mood about whoever is the leader these days. We`re just not happy with all the things happening around us, and we tend to want to blame the person in charge right now.\par \par \uc2 On abortion, I think that rather than crafting legislation to somehow codify Roe -- Roe was a constitutional right for 50 years; you can`t create by statute a constitutional right. Congress should devote its energy to, it has -- claiming the power to regulate interstate commerce, that`s in the, in the Constitution -- so that we make it plainly available for any woman in state A, who wants to go to state B to get an abortion, that she should be able to do so. She`s free to do so. Congress can regulate and legislate that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You keep -- you`re nodding your head to that, Matthew.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Oh, I think that`s -- that`d be an effective pro-choice strategy, and achievable, by the way, because you can point to it in the Constitution, which is much harder to do with the abortion right.\par \par \uc2 But I just want to say one thing: it`s not just the voters are cranky; things are bad. We have record inflation. We have a record poor performance in the stock market. We have consumer sentiment plunging.\par \par \uc2 The economy is still the number one issue in voters` minds. And they tie this economy to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his economic policies. It`s different in some of these Senate and gubernatorial races, where voters can make an individual decision based on candidate attributes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, I want to lean on the abortion issue a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Ali, there`s been congressional Democrats very frustrated. I can put up some. Here`s \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 : We just can`t tell people, well, just vote -- vote your problems away. Because they`re looking at us and saying, well, we already voted for you, referring to an all-Democratic control.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  who`s running for governor in Florida: Frustration requires action and there`s no vent for it.\par \par \uc2 And then Elizabeth Warren: Democrats have the tools to fight back, we just need to use them.\par \par \uc2 What are all they -- how are they going to react to \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  and the sort of, there`s not much we can do?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I think that that`s what I hear the most from my sources on the Hill, as well as in advocacy groups is, yes, Congress and the White House are pretty hamstrung in terms of what they can do to tangibly protect women`s right to access this health care, but at the same time, they just want them to do something. Do something at the state level, whether it`s on federal lands, and governors asked Biden about this again today as they met with him -- the idea that they want them to push the envelope on this to the point where, okay, if it does end up in a court somewhere, at least Democrats are trying.\par \par \uc2 And I would also make the point that as much as Republicans want to continue pointing to this as a moment in economics, abortion is an economics issue for women across the country. And I think that women are frustrated. They are enraged, and they`ll vote on this. And the polling is bearing that out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Marianna, there was another sort of tone that I thought Secretary Becerra took, which is like "look, we`re" -- and it`s clear this is coming I think from President Biden -- we`re not going to push the envelope. We`re not going to do what those guys did.\par \par \uc2 And I know this is a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stubbornness -- gosh darn it, we`re going to restore these institutions.\par \par \uc2 And I know a lot of Democrats are going: stop doing it. Get caught trying. Let the courts say no.\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: A hundred percent. That`s exactly what you`re hearing, especially from progressives who, since last Friday, were outside of the Supreme Court basically saying just that, promising women who were there --\par \par \uc2 TODD: How many times did Trump try to build his wall?\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: I mean, a zillion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And how many different ways did he --\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: And House Republicans and Senate also tried to do that. They obviously had their issues. They couldn`t get a lot of immigration stuff done.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the House Democrats are going to try to respond to this. And they, of course, can pass any bill under the sun because they have the majority. But they`re looking at things like, you know, can a woman not be charged, penalized for going and getting an abortion out of state? That`s something that they likely will vote on this upcoming month.\par \par \uc2 Something also is reproductive rights -- reproductive data on apps. Right, exactly. They`re going to try and also put Republicans on the record to codify a number of different things -- gay rights, birth control. But again, it`s going to die in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But there`s concern over that --\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: And nothing`s going to happen there --\par \par \uc2 VITALI: - strategy there too, right?\par \par \uc2 There`s concern that I`ve heard from some who say, "Yes, we want to just do a vote on rape and incest exceptions." There`s a concern that if they did that, especially in the Senate -- as they try to make this a wedge issue in the midterms, which clearly the polling is working -- that it`s going to make some Republicans, and give them the opportunity to look a little bit rational on this, and not as extreme as Democrats want them to be.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: But Democrats can also seem extreme on abortion, it`s important to realize. And if you -- when you get to brass tacks, and you say you want to have unrestricted abortion throughout the pregnancy, many Americans blanch. Americans want to have abortion access in the early stages of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But then they want to increasingly regulate as you get closer to term. That`s something Democrats need to be aware of as well.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Women don`t go to term and have an abortion late in their pregnancy --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I`m talking about the politics and the public opinion.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: This is the problem with Washington. It`s got to be about more than just energizing the base. Focus on the things that are doable to protect a woman`s ability to have an abortion in states where it is legal and safe right now.\par \par \uc2 And there are things Congress can do to protect that right, to go from state A to state B through its interstate commerce power. It`s got to be more than just energizing the base for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let me switch a little bit to this issue of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jeh, I`ll let you take the first crack at it. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is really upset. He feels he`s being disrespected. It feels as if the party has never -- there is a little bit of Rodney Dangerfield in him.\par \par \uc2 You know, they`ve never really given me respect. And all I do is -- you know, they didn`t want me to run in `15 and look what happened in `16. Then they didn`t want me to run in `20, and look what happened. I actually won. Now they don`t want me to run in `24.\par \par \uc2 Does he have a point?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: In a sense, yes. And I don`t know that -- and this is just instinct -- I don`t know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has definitively made up his mind to run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure at some point, he`s going to have that conversation with his family. My advice would be unconventional: make that -- if you`re not running, make that announcement sooner rather than later, even before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That opens the floodgates, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: But it gives others an opportunity to prepare and to get organized for 2024. Do the opposite of what Donald Trump`s going to do, which is to keep us all in suspense until the last minute.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali, where is most of this hand-wringing about Biden coming from? I don`t sense it comes from Congress. I sense it comes from governors and maybe those that want to run for president.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But, of course, it would, right? I mean, I also do think that there is some consternation around the idea that Biden came in saying he would restore institutional norms. And maybe it`s because we`re going through the January 6th hearings. And maybe it`s because the economy looks the way that it does.\par \par \uc2 But there`s this feeling like, you promised me it would be normal, and this doesn`t feel like a normal --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nothing`s normal.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: -- that I want to accept. So certainly, we`re post-normal in whatever sense that that means.\par \par \uc2 I do also think though that -- and I`m not saying this because I have a book coming out about this -- but it feels like there is an agitating moment for female leadership right now. And that could be where part of this is coming from, where people look to the fact that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the vice president. There are female leaders in the party that could be part of it too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Guys, it`s time for hot dogs, fireworks, and all sorts of things. And let`s get to 247 as a country after we celebrate 246.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Please enjoy your Independence Day weekend, and remember what it`s really all about and what America is about.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220703ei7300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for July 2, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Kathy Park, Somara Theodore, Priscilla Thompson,  George Solis, Josh Lederman, Meagan Fitzgerald, Erin Mclaughlin \par \uc2 4,018 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:30:16]\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST: Breaking news tonight on several fronts, the Uvalde School Police Chief with a dramatic announcement late today. And severe storms adding to a holiday travel nightmare for millions. Hundreds of flights canceled, thousands more delayed. 50 million Americans facing a severe weather threat. Plus, we`re tracking Tropical Storm Colin and we hear from one of those airline passengers offered $10,000 to give up his seat.\par \par \uc2 And late today, the controversial Uvalde Schools Police Chief says he will resign from the City Council after facing outrage for delayed action against the gunman. The reaction in Uvalde tonight.\par \par \uc2 A family demanding answers after the fatal police shooting of a young black driver in Akron, Ohio. Their attorney saying he was shot 60 times. The city bracing for more protests with body camera footage set to be released. The latest turn in the legal battle over abortion, the new ruling from the Texas Supreme Court. The last minute rescue at sea. Crew members plucked from raging waters just as their ship was going down.\par \par \uc2 The research just in, how effective are your vitamin supplements. And a new twist on Fourth of July celebrations with prices soaring and fire danger is growing, cities ditching fireworks for drones.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz-Balart. Good evening, 50 million Americans are at risk for severe weather tonight. Storms that are only adding to the chaotic and rocky start to the holiday weekend. Just today, more than 4000 delays and more than 600 cancellations. And late today, a ground stop for arrivals at two of the nation`s busiest airports. Another major headache for families who decided to fly and it might get worse, we`re tracking severe storms putting millions at risk for floods, high winds and even tornadoes in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 There are record numbers on the road this holiday weekend as well. Despite the highest gas prices ever on July 4. We`ll get to the latest storm track shortly, but first Kathy Park starts us off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As Fourth of July celebrations take off. Severe Weather is pushing travel plans off course this holiday weekend. 50 million people at risk of severe weather, strong thunderstorms along the East Coast causing major problems on the roads and in the skies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The bad news is that they were given us a three hour delay.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Today 1000s of cancellations and delays at some of the nation`s busiest airports as a \uc2 TSA\uc2  reported a record breaking Friday. More than 2.4 million passengers screened at security checkpoints. A number we haven`t seen since before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All I know, I think our travel time was 45 hours door to door from Catskill to Orlando.\par \par \uc2 PARK: For many travelers, the challenges are piling up, disruptions leading to an overflow of check bags. The chaos topped off by pricey airfare. The average price of a domestic roundtrip ticket is $437. The most expensive in five years. Airlines placing some of the blame on staffing shortages, stretching from pilots to gate agents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would say if you`re flying through a hub, give yourself a little bit of extra time flying through that hub.\par \par \uc2 PARK: The volume of travelers driving to their destinations is at an all- time high too, roughly 48 million Americans will be hitting the roads this holiday weekend. Even with the average price of gas at $4.82 a gallon, the highest ever during the fourth of July holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very, very expensive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It makes more sense to stay close.\par \par \uc2 PARK: For Jason Aten and his family their flight from Michigan to Alaska took an unexpected turn.\par \par \uc2 JASON ATEN, TRAVELER: The flight attendant actually came on and said that they were looking for volunteers and we`re willing to pay $10,000 for anyone who is willing to give up their seat.\par \par \uc2 PARK: He says, it was a price that Delta was willing to pay on the spot for over booking. And while he didn`t volunteer this time.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): If this were to happen to you again, would you take the money?\par \par \uc2 ATEN: Oh yeah, we`ll buy a house here in Alaska if they offered to us on the way home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Kathy Park is at LaGuardia Airport in New York. Kathy, we`re getting late word about ground stops that will make things even worse.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Jose, that`s right. We are not tracking any severe weather here in New York City. But we have learned that ground stops have been issued for flights heading into LaGuardia and nearby Newark International Airport. It`s still unclear when those ground stops will be lifted. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Kathy Park in New York. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get more on the severe weather threat right now. Meteorologist Somara Theodore joins us. Somara, where are these storms moving next?\par \par \uc2 [18:35:05]\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, Jose. We are looking at strong storms set to impact major travel hubs like Washington, D.C., Philadelphia up towards Hartford in Boston as well. They have severe thunderstorm watches in effect in the Northeast as we head through tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, travel with me down I-95 into the Carolinas where we`re keeping a close eye on Tropical Storm Colin. And that region along the Outer Banks and in areas like Cape Hatteras, they`re set to sea wave heights as high as six feet. So if you`re planning on taking the boat out this weekend, keep that in mind. Rip current risk moderate to high through the weekend. The good news is we`ll see major improvements and the eastern half of the country as we had the your Fourth of July just in time for the fireworks, areas like the Great Lakes so they could see some rain. Jose, back to you.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Somara Theodore in D.C., thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And now to a major development out of Uvalde, Texas where a gunman killed 19 children and two teachers at Robb Elementary School. The school district`s embattled police chief also recently elected to Uvalde city council, announcing he will step down from that job. NBC`s Priscilla Thompson has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Pete Arredondo, the man at the center of the controversy surrounding the police response to the mass shooting at Robb Elementary is quitting City Council. It is in the best interest of the community to step down. To minimize further distractions. He wrote in a letter to the Uvalde community. The you Valley School District Police Chief has been a target of the community`s frustration since the shooting that left 21 dead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These kids were obliterated. My sister was obliterated.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Arredondo was elected to the council just before the shooting and sworn in behind closed doors one week after.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): How did it feel when you heard the news that he`d resigned?\par \par \uc2 JAVIER CAZARES, FATHER OF JACKIE CAZARES: It mean, it`s still a baby step. You know he failed his job. He failed our kids.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Javier Cazares` nine year old daughter Jackie was one of the children killed at Robb elementary.\par \par \uc2 CAZARES: For me it`s never going to be enough because we can`t have our child back. And -- so for me, it`s never going to be enough.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: According to state officials, Arredondo was the unseen commander during the May 24 shooting. They say he ordered officers to wait in the hall for an hour as a gunman was firing inside classrooms. Arredondo maintains he was not the incident commander that day and told the Texas Tribune he didn`t issue any orders. Arredondo`s lawyer did not respond today to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 The city of Uvalde called the decision to resign the quote "right thing to do." For now, Arredondo is on administrative leave from his role as School District Police Chief while authorities response that day remains under investigation by state officials.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Priscilla what is the latest on the investigation into police response to the shooting that day?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Jose, hearings are continuing behind closed doors but the families of the victims say they don`t -- they aren`t getting that information and frustration is growing. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Priscilla Thompson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Akron, Ohio tonight protests are growing after a police chase led to the shooting death of a black man. His family`s attorney says he was shot 60 times. George Solis has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In Akron, Ohio, Fourth of July celebrations are canceled. Instead the city is bracing for protests. Tensions rising this week following the deadly police shooting of 25-year- old Jayland Walker. According to the lawyer for Walker`s family, authorities told the family at least eight police officers fired more than 90 shots 60 of them striking Walker.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA WALKER, MOTHER OF JAYLAND WALKER: I`m sick as a mother that it came to that. If they would have spent five minutes it would never have ended that way.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Police say, Walker posed a deadly threat starting with his refusal to comply with a traffic stop, which sparked a high speed police pursuit during which police say they heard shots fired from Walker`s vehicle. Walker eventually fled from his car. Authority say leading to a foot chase that ended in a parking lot. The family attorney has seen the police body cam video that shows what happened next.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY DICELLO, ATTORNEY FOR WALKER FAMILY: They`re repetitive firing on top of each other. There`s smoke in the air. One officers clip is emptied, reloads with the full clip. And you see all of this in six seconds.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Police who said gun was recovered from Walker`s car will release the body camera footage to the public on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MYLETT, AKRON POLICE CHIEF: As the pursuit goes on, other officers that are responding know that a firearm was discharged and they believed during this pursuit that the officers had been fired upon.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Walker`s cousin says he`s also seen the video.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Is there any chance that he fired up police officers at any point?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DEJOURNETT, CAUSIN OF JAYLAND WALKER: I really don`t know. I didn`t see anything like that. I don`t know. That`s where we`re going to allow the investigation to play itself out.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:08]\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Akron`s Police Department says the officers involved in the shooting are now on paid leave. The State`s Bureau of Criminal Investigations will participate in an investigation into the shooting. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: George Solis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There is a new turn today in the ongoing battle over abortion in Texas. Late last night, the Texas Supreme Court blocked an order allowing abortions to temporarily resume in the state. The Court cited the state`s nearly 100 year old abortion ban rule now making it illegal to perform the procedure anywhere in the state.\par \par \uc2 We have an important update to a story we first brought you last year, it`s about an upstate New York Power Plant at the center of an environmental controversy. The facility uses natural gas to power 1000s of computers around the clock to mine cryptocurrency but now officials are trying to shut it down over climate change concerns. Josh Lederman reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, New York authorities cracking down on crypto mining, denying an air permit for a power plant they say generates electricity mainly for mining Bitcoin, the state citing concerns about climate change. Amid a community outcry.\par \par \uc2 VINNY ALIPERTI, SENECA LAKE BUSINESS OWNER: We hope that this is the start of a domino effect across the country.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Last June, NBC News got an exclusive look at the Greenwich generation plant on Seneca Lake. The plant closed permanently in 2011. But was resurrected six years later by a private equity firm that installed 1000s of power guzzling servers mining Bitcoin. The gas fired turbines cooled by lake water from this pipeline that extends deep into Seneca Lake.\par \par \uc2 JEFF KIRT\uc2 , \uc2 GREENIDGE GENERATION HOLDINGS, INC\uc2 . CEO: The plant has been operating for 80 years and the environmental impact of the plant has never been better than it is right now.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: But New York authorities saying this week that the plant`s emissions have increased drastically over the past few years, driven by its Bitcoin operation. \uc2 Greenidge Generation\uc2  calling the ruling absurd and with no credible legal basis and vowing to keep operating while it appeals saying it`s taken steps that have already lowered emissions and the local Electrical Workers Union lamenting the loss of high tech jobs, calling it a sad day for New York.\par \par \uc2 But Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  applauding the ruling just as she faces a key decision on whether to sign the nation`s first partial moratorium on new crypto mining powered by fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, crypto at a crossroads under growing scrutiny as its value has plummeted, but now consuming more electricity annually than all of Pakistan, according to Bloomberg NEF.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What message should other states be taking from what you`re trying to do in New York state?\par \par \uc2 ANNA KELLES, NEW YORK STATE ASSEMBLYWOMAN: That we can use our legislation to push for innovation in the industry. If you want to be the cryptocurrency capital of the world you can be and you can do it without destroying the environment at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: More states are taking climate action on their own as national efforts face setbacks. The Supreme Court this week weakening the EPA`s authority to cut power plant emissions. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Josh Lederman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the dramatic rescue at sea after a powerful storm battered this sinking ship.\par \par \uc2 Also, an important story for anyone who takes vitamins, what a new study says about whether or not they actually make a difference.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:45:54]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Incredible images tonight of a dramatic rescue at sea survivors pluck by helicopter after ship literally broken too. You can see the ship sinking and high waves caused by a powerful tropical storm of Hong Kong. Three workers from the boat were hoisted to safety sadly 27 are still missing.\par \par \uc2 Did you know that more than half of all Americans take vitamins or supplements? Maybe you`ve wondered if they actually make a difference? Well now, new analysis of dozens of studies is giving us a sharper view of just what they might mean for better health. Meagan Fitzgerald explains.\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: For many of us getting in staying healthy is usually top of mind, a typical go to, vitamin. After all Americans spent nearly $50 billion alone on supplements last year. But a new analysis published in the Journal of American Medical Association says for the average healthy adult they may not be necessary, they reviewed 84 studies to assess the impact of vitamins and supplements in preventing heart disease and cancer. Dr. Michael Berry is Vice Chair of the \uc2 U.S. Preventive Services Task Force\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BARRY, U.S. PREVENTATIVE SERVICES TASK FORCE VICE-CHAIR: We don`t have enough evidence to recommend for/or against them, with those two exceptions, vitamin E and beta-carotene which we recommend people not take.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: According to the JAMA`s analysis, beta-carotene was associated with an increased risk of lung cancer and death from heart disease. While Vitamin E had no proven effects. Dr. Jeffery Linder wrote an op-ed about the study.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Is it pointless to take vitamins?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEFFERY LINDER, NORTHWESTERN FEINBERG SCHOOL OF MEDICINE GENERAL INTERNAL MEDICINE CHIEF: If there was a big benefit from multivitamins and supplements, we`d sort of know by now.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: But the vitamin industry which spent nearly a billion dollars on marketing in 2021 refutes those statements.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL FABRICANT, NATURAL PRODUCTS ASSOCIATION CEO & PRESIDENT: Always talk to your physician, but they`re there to supplement the diet. They`re not there to be the principal element of the diet.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Doctors do say children and those with chronic illnesses can stand to benefit from vitamins as well as those who are pregnant, but ultimately the key to a healthy long life. Good old fashioned diet and exercise. Meagan Fitzgerald, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Still ahead, good news from Yellowstone National Park after those historic floods. And lighting up the sky, the new high tech way some cities are celebrating the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:51]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Just in time for the holiday weekend, a sign of progress at Yellowstone National Park. Its North Loop reopen today after historic flooding caused the whole park to close just last month. Storms washing out roads and causing rockslides from trails and campsites do remain off limits. The nation`s oldest national park is celebrating its 150th Anniversary.\par \par \uc2 There`s something different lighting up the skies over some cities this weekend, a big shift in how they`re celebrating the Fourth of July holiday. Instead of fireworks, they`re turning to high tech drone shows. We sent our Erin McLaughlin to Gypsum, Colorado and find out what`s behind this change.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: There the sights and sounds of the Fourth of July. But this year across America, towns are doing something different to celebrate this stars and stripes, ditching fireworks for drones.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I thought it was really cool.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: It`s growing in popularity out west where the fire danger is higher than ever. Each year the number of wildfires spiked dramatically on the fourth.\par \par \uc2 MONICA RODRIQUEZ, LA CITY 7TH DISTRICT COUNCILWOMAN: We cannot ignore the environmental impacts and the circumstances that we`re in today.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Here in the small town of Gypsum, Colorado. That`s Mayor Steven Carver`s top concern. So this year, he`s hosting the town`s first ever drone show.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN CARVER, MAYOR OF GYPSUM, COLORADO: I do think it will replace the fireworks.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Why?\par \par \uc2 CARVER: Well, our community understands the fire danger here.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: And it`s not just the fire concerns. Recent data shows costs are up 35% across the fireworks industry due to inflation and supply chain disruptions.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PAPPAS, SUPERIOR FIREWORKS COMMUNICATIONS & TECHNOLOGY DIRECTOR: It used to cost about eight to $10,000 to ship a container of fireworks. And today we`re looking at $35 to $40,000.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Driving the demand for drones sky high.\par \par \uc2 PAUL WHITNEY, BLUEMEDIA EXECUTIVE PRODUCER: We`ve seen about 70% to 80% increase in volume as far as calls and requests for our drone shows.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: This year drones taking center stage from sea to shining sea, drone company UAV is completely booked this holiday weekend. For the show and Gypsum, they show us how they first calibrate 100 drones. And they line them up. Each drone is pre-programmed and computer controlled.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): And where is this technology going?\par \par \uc2 PAPPAS: I think we`re at the very beginning. So I can`t imagine what`s going to be like five years.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: The sky`s the limit?\par \par \uc2 PAPPAS: Sky`s the limit.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: And they say each show tells a story.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): So what story are you telling tonight?\par \par \uc2 CARVER: Well, I just want the story of independence to be brought to us through the drones to our citizens at Gypsum.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Tech lighting up America`s skies on its 246th birthday. Erin McLaughlin, NBC News, Gypsum, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And when we come back, signs of respect, the man on a mission to honor those who served one flag at a time.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:56:33]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: There`s good news tonight about celebrating the American dream man, the unique way one man is honoring the stars, stripes and those who have served.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JC CORTEZ, PROFESSIONAL CARPENTER: Make sure you check the power button on him.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Inside this humble workshop near Los Angeles, JC Cortez is doing something extraordinary. The professional carpenter is putting together American flag kits to share it for free with his southern California community.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): You`ve installed more than 130 flags since 2018?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: Yes, sir. Each flag that we install has a solar light attached to it because I think it`s important to have the flag displayed properly, have it properly lit overnight.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: It`s a patriotic mission inspired by JC`s former neighbor, an Army veteran named Robert Searle.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: His flag flew every day, 365 days a year and you always had a properly displayed and that`s where I learned that from.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: It`s a gesture JC`s kept going to let those who serve know just how much they matter.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): I think it`s so important, JC, they see they we recognize the greatness of the United States and of its people.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: It`s very important and it`s all thanks to our veterans. They paid the ultimate sacrifice for our freedom. And now I`m going to make sure that I spread that around.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: JC and his group Flags Over Orange, get requests for new flags on social media or simply through word of mouth.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: That`s beautiful medal.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And show up to honor veterans like William Wells (ph). He was in the Air Force during the Korean War.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM WELLS (ph), AIR FORCE VETERAN: That was so darn happy to see what he did. His wife, Han (ph), is grateful to.\par \par \uc2 HAN (ph), WILLIAM WELLS` WIFE: We`re so appreciative of the light that he put on the pole because I used to come out at nighttime and have to take it down.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: JC has also become friends with former Marine Ed Andrade (ph).\par \par \uc2 ED ANDRADE (ph), MARINE ARMY VETERAN: I had an old flag up which was nice because he -- they came in, took the other one down put it up.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: The flags and friendly conversation were also a sign of respect for retired Lieutenant Colonel John Shawra (ph).\par \par \uc2 JOHN SHAWRA (ph), RETIRED LIEUTENANT COLONEL: It was really nice. It is meaningful, it means a lot.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: For this busy dad, that`s a lesson he hopes to pass on to the next generation.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What do you want your six kids to learn about giving back to this country?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: I want them to learn that at times you have to give more than what you receive. And that`s the only reason we do, not for recognition, not for payment. We do it because it makes people feel good. That`s how I raised my kids.\par \par \uc2 Looks good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And JC never charges for flags, solar lights or installation. He gets donations from his local Elk`s Lodge and \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , among others.\par \par \uc2 That`s NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for this Saturday. Kate Snow will be here tomorrow night. I`m Jose Diaz-Balart thank you for the privilege of your time, and good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220703ei7200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Two Police Officers Shot To Death Along With Their K9, Several Other Officers Injured In Kentucky\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Back here at home now to that deadly shootout in Kentucky, what one sheriff is describing as pure hell. A suspect arm with a rifle opening fire on officers who`d come to serve a warrant. Two officers were killed along with a police dog. Gabe Gutierrez now on what`s been an especially dangerous year for cops.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Late today in Kentucky, a somber procession honoring those killed in the line of duty. Police Captain Ralph Frazier and sheriff`s deputy Will Petry were gunned down Thursday along with K9 Drago. Four more officers wounded during a standoff with a domestic violence suspect.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HUNT, SHERIFF, FLOYD COUNTY: They encountered just what I told you, pure hell. When they arrived, they had no chance.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Investigators say the suspect opened fire on deputies who were serving a warrant in Floyd County Kentucky. He allegedly began shooting a rifle, barricaded himself inside his home for hours before he was taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: This guy had a seem to be a plan. And he pretty much executed that plan almost to precision\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Shootings at police officers are up 19 percent from this time last year. At least 178 officers shot in 2022 so far, 33 of them killed. There have been at least 35 ambush-style attacks on officers this year.\par \par \uc2 KEITH BARLEY, FLOYD COUNTY ATTORNEY: These are human beings. These are people with children, spouses, and mom and dad. In their world, we`ll never be (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The suspect in Kentucky was arraigned today and pleaded not guilty. He`s being held on $10 million bail. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Gabe Gutierrez with that tragedy out of Kentucky tonight. Gabe, we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, we want to turn now to the ongoing fallout from the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade and the confusion for people in so many states as laws have been rapidly changing. In South Dakota today, a telemedicine ban involving abortion medication became law. In Oklahoma, Planned Parenthood and other providers filed a lawsuit to challenge the state`s abortion bans. In Kentucky, the Attorney General there filed with an appeals court to allow the state`s trigger law ban on abortions to go into effect after being blocked by a judge.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220706ei710008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Governor Of New Jersey Signed Two Bills To Protect Providers And People Coming Out Of State Seeking Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: And in New Jersey, the governor today signed two bills to protect providers and people coming from out of state seeking abortions. All of this as President Biden met with nine Democratic governors to discuss how their states are moving forward to keep abortion accessible. Stephanie Gosk now on the battle on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The battle over abortion laws is heating up in no place more intensely than Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 TANYA ATKINSON, PRESIDENT AND CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD OF WISCONSIN: Our incredible staff had to go out and say to people who are in the waiting room that they could not have the procedures.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: What was that seem like that day?\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: It was -- I mean, it was traumatizing.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Abortion stopped immediately at this Madison clinic and across the state after the Supreme Court`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: It`s a felony and it comes with potential jail time and potential significant fines.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Like many states, Wisconsin had an abortion ban on the books, an old one that went into effect when Roe v. Wade was overturned. Wisconsin`s law written in 1849 before the Civil War before women could vote is a near total ban. The only exception, to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KAUL\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL, WISCONSIN: We don`t have any precedent for how this 19th century ban applies to 21st-century medicine. You know --\par \par \uc2 GOSK: \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 , the Democratic Attorney General is suing the Republican- controlled state legislature to get the ban overturned. He and the governor had vowed to protect doctors from prosecution in the meantime. But there is only so much they can do.\par \par \uc2 KAUL: We don`t have the authority to tell DAs they can`t use their resources to enforce a law that`s on the books.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But right now, there`s some legal ambiguity and confusion, isn`t there?\par \par \uc2 KAUL: Absolutely. I mean, we have started receiving questions already.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Kaul says that is why he`s taking it to the courts.\par \par \uc2 MATT SANDE, DIRECTOR OF LEGISLATION, PRO-LIFE WISCONSIN: He`s our state`s top law enforcement officer His duty is to enforce laws that protect human life, not sue to overturn them.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Matt Sande who lobbies legislators for the organization Pro-Life Wisconsin wants an even more restrictive abortion law that more clearly defines when a mother`s life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 SANDE: We`re going to need a pro-life legislature. We`re going to need a pro-life governor to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Both the Governor and the Attorney General are up for reelection, so are more than 100 state legislators. The law here will come down to the vote in November. And in a state where voters are largely split down the middle, it`s a toss-up.\par \par \uc2 So, the push is on.\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: People have to get out and vote. They absolutely have to get out and vote. And we can -- we can create change.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Here and in so many states, the future of abortion in voters hands now more than ever. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, in 60 seconds, just in time for the holiday. The new warnings about sharks after several attacks on both coasts. Are they coming closer to shore? Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220706ei710008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court`s Shift\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Pete Williams \par \uc2 509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Welcome back. The Supreme Court term is over, now leaving the country even more divided, especially on the issue of abortion. Pete Williams tonight on what to expect when the court with a solid conservative majority takes up new hot-button issues this fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The abortion ruling is the most dramatic example of how far and how fast the conservative supermajority moved the court to the right. It didn`t just uphold Mississippi`s law banning abortion after 15 weeks, it also overturned the constitutional right to abortion. It didn`t only strike down New York`s law requiring a special need for a concealed carry permit, it said all gun regulations must have historic roots.\par \par \uc2 It didn`t just restrict the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , it said Congress has to explicitly let federal agencies take major actions. And it reduced the separation of church and state ruling the taxpayer money can be used at schools that offer an explicitly religious education and ruling for a football coach who led prayers on the 50-yard line immediately after games.\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, FOUNDER, SCOTUS BLOG: There are six relatively young Supreme Court justices who feel incredibly emboldened, incredibly aggressive to move American law to the right. And they are not stopping with this. The courts shifted to the right when \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  retired, succeeded by \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and even more so with the death of \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  succeeded by Amy Coney Barrett, with \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , three new justices appointed by Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  calls the new conservative majority restless. All three liberals said the court overturned Roe at practically the first moment possible. But conservatives welcomed the change.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK: It`s a court that is very much committed not to conservative political outcomes, but certainly to a conservative approach to reading the Constitution and the law.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And as \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  takes her place, the court faces more contentious questions next term. Should it end affirmative action in college admissions? Do businesses have religious freedom to refuse to serve same-sex weddings? And should legislatures not courts get the last word and election disputes?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The court may be moving faster than Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  would prefer, but he and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  are at center in the majority in 95 percent of the term`s decisions. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, Pete Williams for us tonight. Pete, we thank you. And when we come back, a broadcasting legend signs off. The music of his life next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220706ei71000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for July 1, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Tom Costello, Bill Karins, Andrea Mitchell, Matt  Bradley, Stephanie Gosk, Kerry Sanders, Pete Williams \par \uc2 4,041 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Tonight, breaking news, flights at some of the nation`s biggest airports grounded.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Thousands of flights delayed or canceled, severe weather, plus that pilot shortage creating holiday travel chaos. Airports packed, one airline offering passengers $10,000 to get off an oversold flight. Plus, record numbers expected on the highways despite still sky high gas prices and now storm clouds on the horizon. We`ll show you were.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittany Griner`s trial in Russia now underway facing drug trafficking charges and up to 10 years in prison. Some calling it a show trial. Could she go free in a prisoner swap?\par \par \uc2 The deadly new Russian attack on civilians in Ukraine. At least 21 people killed after missiles hit an apartment building and a recreation center.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, in the line of fire, two police officers shot to death along with their K9, several other officers injured. The chilling image of the suspect in court as police killings are on the rise in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And shark attacks from Florida to California, a 17-year-old girl attacked at a popular beach. New warnings tonight to swimmers as sharks move closer to shore. What you can do to avoid what lies beneath.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (on camera): Good evening, I`m Tom Llamas in for Lester tonight. We begin with that breaking news. Massive ground stops and delays across some of the country`s biggest airports, making the holiday travel chaos even more complicated. A difficult summer travel season is now going from challenging to worse.\par \par \uc2 Before the severe weather even moved in, Americans facing a rare combination of high gas prices, high airfare and mass cancellations because of staff shortages across the country. The July 4 getaway is underway but tonight, it`s unclear how far people will actually get. Nearly 50 million people navigating through clog airports and roads. Gas prices down slightly but still painfully high for so many Americans.\par \par \uc2 Tracking it all for us tonight, NBC`s Tom Costello who starts us off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Friday night and America`s July 4 getaway is looking dicey tonight. Critical airports up and down the East Coast in ground stops with severe thunderstorms from Orlando in the south through Atlanta up to D.C., Baltimore, Philly, and New York, adding to the day`s growing tally of flight cancellations and delays on one of the busiest travel days in the nation`s airports.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`ve never come to an airport before I`m wondering whether my flight is going to take off or not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our flight got canceled yesterday, so now we`re hopscotching around to get home.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Roughly 4,000 cancellations Justin Sunday morning amid the nationwide pilot shortage. Meanwhile, record numbers of Americans on the roads. Despite the highest July 4 gas prices on record, $4.84 a gallon, the national average though down 18 cents in two weeks, pricey enough to force some Americans to rethink their getaways.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I wanted to kind of go far away and it`s just too expensive to do a round trip of gas, you know, or even just fell out multiple times.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: But a record 42 million Americans are paying the price.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: During the week, it`s all work from home. So, I guess it sort of evens out. A new poll finds while 45 percent of Americans are cutting back on driving, 61 percent of people taking a summer trip are still choosing to drive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Delta fight number 1393 went to Seattle, Washington.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: No surprise given unprecedented numbers of airline delays and cancellations this summer. Starting today, Delta, American, and United are all trimming their schedules further to align with their staffing levels just as passenger levels hit post-pandemic highs. Today, Delta issued a fare difference waiver for anyone wanting to change their flights this weekend. As the \uc2 TSA\uc2  chief cautions, it`ll take weeks before things return to normal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEKOSKE, ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 TSA\uc2 : Once you get into the middle part of August and then a past Labor Day, things get a little bit more calm over the course of the fall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Underscoring the pressure that the airlines are under Delta reportedly offered passengers this week on a flight from Michigan to Minnesota $10,000 if they would get up -- give up their seats in an oversold flight. Delta apparently was even willing to pay the passengers right then via Apple Pay. Tom, back to you.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: $10,000 for a single seat. All right, Tom Costello for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned, we`re already seeing serious flight delays from the threat of severe weather. Tens of millions more could see strong storms before the weekend is done. Bill Karins is watching it off for us. Bill, walk us through tonight and the weekend.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:02]\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST (on camera): And Tom, these storms tonight have been hitting the worst areas like LaGuardia Airport, Dulles, Orlando International Airport. We`re going to see scattered storms continuing to severe threat is ending around the Washington D.C. area. And then we`re going to go from there to the south. It has been horrendous day to travel on I-10 through areas of Texas from Houston all the way over through Louisiana, especially around Beaumont and Port Arthur. That`s going to improve a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And a heads up to all of our friends trying to travel tomorrow afternoon evening on the I-95 corridor. 50 million people at risk of severe storms. We`re talking New York, Boston, all the way down to D.C. So, travel problems at the airports when these storms come through.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s your weekend forecast. We are going to be watching these storms on Sunday down in the south. Gorgeous day at Great Lakes Ohio Valley, and then for your Fourth, we keep that nice weather in the Northeast with just scattered storms in the south. The worst is now and tomorrow. LLAMAS: All right, it could be frustrating for so many. Bill, we appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 We want to head overseas now. \uc2 WNBA\uc2  superstar Britney Griner`s trial started today in Russia. She`s facing up to 10 years in prison on drug charges and now her wife says the Biden administration is not doing enough to bring her home. Andrea Mitchell has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  superstar and two-time Olympic gold medalist Brittney Griner handcuffed wearing a Jimi Hendrix T-shirt after more than four months in detention on the first day of what legal experts call a show trial.\par \par \uc2 Accused of drug smuggling, the Russian say, they found vape cartridges containing cannabis oil in her luggage. The possible penalty, 10 years in prison. Griner`s wife Cherelle is on edge.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, WIFE OF BRITTNEY GRINER: She`s walking into a situation where their judicial system has a 99 percent conviction rate. So, in their system, there is no innocent in their system, it`s just guilty.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The U.S. considers Griner wrongfully detained. Embassy staffer with her in court.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH ROOD, DEPUTY MISSION CHIEF, U.S. EMBASSY MOSCOW: She asked me to convey that she is in good spirits and is keeping up the pace.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Today, we spoke to Dawn Staley, Griner`s Olympic coach and friend.\par \par \uc2 It certainly seems that this has been very, very difficult. For one thing, she`s six foot nine and they`ve got her traveling back and forth and in court in a cage.\par \par \uc2 DAWN STALEY, GRINER`S OLYMPIC COACH: You see Brittany appear in court or outside of court or walking in and being handcuffed. You just feel for her. She is a free-spirited woman whose got a great heart.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The State Department says freeing Britain is a top priority. But Cherelle Greiner says their rhetoric and actions don`t match. She wants a meeting with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 GRINER: The most beneficial thing that I`ve been told is that, you know, you meet with President Biden. You know, he has that power. He is the person, you know, that ultimately will make that decision for B.G. to come home.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Russian media reports say the Kremlin wants a trade for arms dealer Victor Boot, nicknamed the merchant of death currently serving a 25 year sentence in the U.S. Meanwhile, Griner`s trial is just beginning and could go on for months.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): U.S. officials tell me Russia has a practice of jailing Americans on trumped up charges to use as bargaining chips to trade for hardened Russian criminals jailed in the U.S. Kremlin spokesman, Dmitry Peskov, today denied grinders arrest was politically motivated. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Andrea Mitchell for us tonight. Andrea, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 We now go next door to Ukraine where there`s been another deadly Russian attack on civilians, including on an apartment building. The missiles hitting at 1:00 in the morning just as families were sleeping inside. Matt Bradley is in Ukraine with those images tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, along Ukraine`s southern coast, rescue workers desperately searching for survivors after Russian missiles struck another civilian target overnight, this time destroying a 12-storey apartment building and a nearby Recreation Center.\par \par \uc2 We heard strikes. We live close by, said this witness. We help those who survived and those who unfortunately died.\par \par \uc2 The strikes killed at least 21 civilians including one child according to cranes emergency services. It was one of the deadliest attacks for Ukrainian civilians since the war began over four months ago. It also injured 38 others including six children. Tonight, Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  condemning the attack.\par \par \uc2 We don`t want Ukrainian warnings to start as tragically as today with Russian missile strikes like the one on Odessa region, he said.\par \par \uc2 Once again, Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov denied that Russia`s military had targeted civilians. He said the strikes were against military facilities. But it`s just the latest in a string of Russian airstrikes and civilian areas far from the front lines.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, 18 people were killed when a Russian missile destroyed a busy shopping mall in Kremenchuk. President Zelenskyy has urged the world to ostracize Russia by designating it a terrorist state, punishment he says for a shocking civilian death toll that continues to rise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY (on camera): Today, the Pentagon announced $820 million in new security assistance for Ukraine bringing the total U.S. military aid to $6.9 billion since Russia`s invasion began more than four months ago. Tom?\par \par \uc2 [19:10:08]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Matt Bradley from Ukraine tonight. Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home now to that deadly shootout in Kentucky, what one sheriff is describing as pure hell. A suspect arm with a rifle opening fire on officers who`d come to serve a warrant. Two officers were killed along with a police dog. Gabe Gutierrez now on what`s been an especially dangerous year for cops.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Late today in Kentucky, a somber procession honoring those killed in the line of duty. Police Captain Ralph Frazier and sheriff`s deputy Will Petry were gunned down Thursday along with K9 Drago. Four more officers wounded during a standoff with a domestic violence suspect.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HUNT, SHERIFF, FLOYD COUNTY: They encountered just what I told you, pure hell. When they arrived, they had no chance.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Investigators say the suspect opened fire on deputies who were serving a warrant in Floyd County Kentucky. He allegedly began shooting a rifle, barricaded himself inside his home for hours before he was taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: This guy had a seem to be a plan. And he pretty much executed that plan almost to precision\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Shootings at police officers are up 19 percent from this time last year. At least 178 officers shot in 2022 so far, 33 of them killed. There have been at least 35 ambush-style attacks on officers this year.\par \par \uc2 KEITH BARLEY, FLOYD COUNTY ATTORNEY: These are human beings. These are people with children, spouses, and mom and dad. In their world, we`ll never be (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The suspect in Kentucky was arraigned today and pleaded not guilty. He`s being held on $10 million bail. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Gabe Gutierrez with that tragedy out of Kentucky tonight. Gabe, we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, we want to turn now to the ongoing fallout from the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade and the confusion for people in so many states as laws have been rapidly changing. In South Dakota today, a telemedicine ban involving abortion medication became law. In Oklahoma, Planned Parenthood and other providers filed a lawsuit to challenge the state`s abortion bans. In Kentucky, the Attorney General there filed with an appeals court to allow the state`s trigger law ban on abortions to go into effect after being blocked by a judge.\par \par \uc2 And in New Jersey, the governor today signed two bills to protect providers and people coming from out of state seeking abortions. All of this as President Biden met with nine Democratic governors to discuss how their states are moving forward to keep abortion accessible. Stephanie Gosk now on the battle on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The battle over abortion laws is heating up in no place more intensely than Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 TANYA ATKINSON, PRESIDENT AND CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD OF WISCONSIN: Our incredible staff had to go out and say to people who are in the waiting room that they could not have the procedures.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: What was that seem like that day?\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: It was -- I mean, it was traumatizing.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Abortion stopped immediately at this Madison clinic and across the state after the Supreme Court`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: It`s a felony and it comes with potential jail time and potential significant fines.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Like many states, Wisconsin had an abortion ban on the books, an old one that went into effect when Roe v. Wade was overturned. Wisconsin`s law written in 1849 before the Civil War before women could vote is a near total ban. The only exception, to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KAUL\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL, WISCONSIN: We don`t have any precedent for how this 19th century ban applies to 21st-century medicine. You know --\par \par \uc2 GOSK: \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 , the Democratic Attorney General is suing the Republican- controlled state legislature to get the ban overturned. He and the governor had vowed to protect doctors from prosecution in the meantime. But there is only so much they can do.\par \par \uc2 KAUL: We don`t have the authority to tell DAs they can`t use their resources to enforce a law that`s on the books.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But right now, there`s some legal ambiguity and confusion, isn`t there?\par \par \uc2 KAUL: Absolutely. I mean, we have started receiving questions already.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Kaul says that is why he`s taking it to the courts.\par \par \uc2 MATT SANDE, DIRECTOR OF LEGISLATION, PRO-LIFE WISCONSIN: He`s our state`s top law enforcement officer His duty is to enforce laws that protect human life, not sue to overturn them.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Matt Sande who lobbies legislators for the organization Pro-Life Wisconsin wants an even more restrictive abortion law that more clearly defines when a mother`s life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 SANDE: We`re going to need a pro-life legislature. We`re going to need a pro-life governor to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Both the Governor and the Attorney General are up for reelection, so are more than 100 state legislators. The law here will come down to the vote in November. And in a state where voters are largely split down the middle, it`s a toss-up.\par \par \uc2 So, the push is on.\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: People have to get out and vote. They absolutely have to get out and vote. And we can -- we can create change.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Here and in so many states, the future of abortion in voters hands now more than ever. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, in 60 seconds, just in time for the holiday. The new warnings about sharks after several attacks on both coasts. Are they coming closer to shore? Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, Americans are heading to the beach this holiday weekend and increasingly so are sharks leading to some very dangerous encounters. Kerry Sanders now with a new shark attack on a Florida beach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The shark attack happened along Keaton beach in the Florida Panhandle where just four days ago a shark was spotted close to shore. This attack Thursday when 17-year- old Addison Bethea swam the shallows with others hunting for scallops. That`s one area residents say a shark-like this one, at least nine feet long, grabbed a hold of her leg.\par \par \uc2 LYNN AIBEJERIS, RESIDENT, KEATON BEACH: That shark wouldn`t tie loose, and it kept thrashing around and thrashing her.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Witnesses believed it was a bull shark. Addison`s survival credited by her family to her quick-thinking brother who`s a firefighter. He tied a tourniquet to her ravaged bloody leg.\par \par \uc2 GINA DEESON, CAPTAIN, TAYLOR COUNTY SHERIFF`S OFFICE: It was actually someone who had a boat there nearby, that they don`t even know who that person is, that we`re able to help them get in really quickly.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: This shark attack comes on the heels of a shark attack in the Florida Keys on Wednesday, a suspected Great White attack off the coast of Monterey, California eight days ago. Off Long Island, New York beginning today, stepped up shark patrols in park because the Fourth of July is such a prime beach weekend. The concern, climate change means warmer waters, which brings sharks closer to shore.\par \par \uc2 DAVID OCHOA, FORT LAUDERDALE OCEAN RESCUE: This is the sharks home. They`re here. But we`re not part of their diet pretty much. We`re -- they`re not out to get us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: One way to protect yourself is swim in a beach that has a lifeguard. If they spot a shark, they can call you in. Statistically, you are more likely to be killed by fireworks than a shark. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A great reminder. All right, Kerry, we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And we`re back in a moment with the Supreme Court`s shift, and now ready to take on even more hot button issues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Welcome back. The Supreme Court term is over, now leaving the country even more divided, especially on the issue of abortion. Pete Williams tonight on what to expect when the court with a solid conservative majority takes up new hot-button issues this fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The abortion ruling is the most dramatic example of how far and how fast the conservative supermajority moved the court to the right. It didn`t just uphold Mississippi`s law banning abortion after 15 weeks, it also overturned the constitutional right to abortion. It didn`t only strike down New York`s law requiring a special need for a concealed carry permit, it said all gun regulations must have historic roots.\par \par \uc2 It didn`t just restrict the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , it said Congress has to explicitly let federal agencies take major actions. And it reduced the separation of church and state ruling the taxpayer money can be used at schools that offer an explicitly religious education and ruling for a football coach who led prayers on the 50-yard line immediately after games.\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, FOUNDER, SCOTUS BLOG: There are six relatively young Supreme Court justices who feel incredibly emboldened, incredibly aggressive to move American law to the right. And they are not stopping with this. The courts shifted to the right when \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  retired, succeeded by \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and even more so with the death of \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  succeeded by Amy Coney Barrett, with \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , three new justices appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  calls the new conservative majority restless. All three liberals said the court overturned Roe at practically the first moment possible. But conservatives welcomed the change.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK: It`s a court that is very much committed not to conservative political outcomes, but certainly to a conservative approach to reading the Constitution and the law.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And as \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  takes her place, the court faces more contentious questions next term. Should it end affirmative action in college admissions? Do businesses have religious freedom to refuse to serve same-sex weddings? And should legislatures not courts get the last word and election disputes?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The court may be moving faster than Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  would prefer, but he and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  are at center in the majority in 95 percent of the term`s decisions. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, Pete Williams for us tonight. Pete, we thank you. And when we come back, a broadcasting legend signs off. The music of his life next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Finally tonight, he`s a living catalog of jazz and inspiring voice of Jazz radio who has signed off from his famous show for the last time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BOB PERKINS, RADIO HOST: Good to be here at 88 years of age.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voiceover): For nearly six decades, when it came to jazz, Philadelphia turned to that one unique voice. WRTI`s Bob Perkins known as B.P. who kept them swinging, spinning everything from Duke Ellington to Miles Davis. And over the years, meeting the likes of President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  and touching the lives of everyday listeners and renowned musicians. He described the complicated, beautiful, and uniquely American art form this way.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: When you can take a guitar, saxophone, and a piano, and make them talk, you`re playing a part of your life. You must have experienced something that made you come up with a note, you know.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: But now, at age 88, B.P. is ready to sign off.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Thank you so much for sticking with me all these years.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Perkins says it`s time to pass the ball to the next era of jazz lovers. His message to his weekday listeners, stay the course.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Keep listening. Keep singing. Keep listening no matter how many times, you know, get as bad as it can be. You`ve always got that song. And keep that song in your heart, in your head, and keep singing, and I guess keep swinging too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: B.P., we will definitely keep swinging. We thank you for all your time and service. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. A programming note, this Sunday night, a primetime special to help the people of Ukraine. That`s at 7:00 Eastern right here on NBC.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for watching. I`m Tom Llamas in for Lester And all of us here at NBC News, good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220702ei71000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Florida Judge Temporarily Blocks And Abortion Restriction Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: In state houses and courthouses across the country, people are grappling with rapidly changing laws governing abortion. And Anne Thompson has the latest now on the next wave of restrictions that could be on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Moves on the state level to restrict abortions hitting legal obstacles. Florida`s ban on abortions after 15 weeks scheduled to take effect tomorrow blocked in court today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN COOPER, JUDGE, LEON COUNTY FLORIDA 2ND JUDICIAL COURT: That it violates the privacy provision of the Florida constitution.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Disappointing the governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Yes, these are unborn babies that have heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Kentucky`s trigger law a near-total ban put on a temporary hold too applauded by that state`s governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): The trigger law in Kentucky is extremists.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Now, a new salvo in the abortion rights war. Conservative lawmakers looking at potential laws to keep women from crossing state lines for legal abortions by targeting corporations paying their travel expenses. Arkansas State Senator Jason Rapert leads the National Association of Christian Lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 JASON RAPERT, ARKANSAS STATE SENATOR: We`re are exploring legislation and drafting language that would stop some of these woke corporations from using shareholder money illegally and without real authority to traffic people for the cause of an abortion.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Some say any effort to stop women from crossing state lines is a step too far.\par \par \uc2 SARA ROSENBAUM, PROFESSOR, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY HEALTH LAW AND POLICY: This would be like Virginia passing a law saying that it`s illegal for me to go to Maryland to get dental care or illegal for me to have my primary care physician be in the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Today, 90 local prosecutors from across the country have signed on to his statement refusing to prosecute those who seek, provide, or support abortions, calling criminalizing abortion care a mockery of justice. St. Louis County District Attorney Wesley Bell.\par \par \uc2 But if your state bans abortion, isn`t it your job to enforce the law?\par \par \uc2 WESLEY BELL, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, ST. LOUIS COUNTY: It`s well-settled law that prosecutors have discretion over the limited criminal justice resources that we have. And we make decisions all the time in that guiding principle is public safety and justice.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: A nation still debating what`s enforceable and what`s law in a post-Roe world. Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220702ei6u0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The President`s Stark Criticism Of The Court Over Abortion With The New Call For Action In Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kelly O`Donnell \par \uc2 435 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: And this evening, President Biden slamming the Supreme Court for its decision overturning Roe v Wade, accusing the justices of outrageous behavior and urging a controversial change to Senate rules in response. Kelly O`Donnell has that story.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): From a world stage moment in Spain, President Biden accused the highest court of upending American freedoms.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  in overruling not only Roe v. Wade, but essentially challenging the right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Consequences he sees as so dire, the president urged Congress to restore abortion rights with new law. But in a 50-50 Senate, Democrats don`t have enough votes, making a surprise announcement today --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And if the filibuster gets in the way --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The President said he would back a limited change to Senate rules to allow a simple majority vote to make abortion legal again nationally.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: -- require an exception to the filibuster for this action to deal with the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s a flip, breaking his decades-long filibuster support.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ending the filibuster is a very dangerous thing to do.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: But tonight, top Democrats say they cannot make the change because a few Democrats won`t agree. While top Republican \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said the President`s attacks on the court are unmerited and dangerous. Today, I asked about the political pressure he faces.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): Are you the best messenger to carry this forward when Democrats, many of them, many progressives, want you to do more?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, I am the President of the United States of America. That makes me the best messenger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): Tomorrow, the President will meet with Democratic governors on abortion rights at the state level and will announce his own next steps. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220702ei6u00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 30, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams, Kelly O`Donnell, Anne Thompson, Gabe  Gutierrez, Tom Costello, Ron Allen, Stephanie Gosk, Maggie Vespa \par \uc2 3,873 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, on a historic day for the Supreme Court. The justices revealing crucial rulings on the environment and immigration.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): In a setback for the Biden administration, the court setting new limits on the EPA`s authority, but a victory for the White House on immigration, allowing the president to end the Trump-era remain in Mexico policy. All this \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  becomes the first black woman on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And the President`s stark criticism of the court over abortion with the new call for action in Congress as a Florida judge temporarily blocks and abortion restriction law.\par \par \uc2 We`re tracking hundreds of new flight cancellations into the holiday weekend. Delta pilots demonstrating. How you can avoid travel chaos. And the President`s answer when asked how long soaring gas prices will last.\par \par \uc2 The intriguing new discovery of a never served a restaurant in the case of Emmett Till, the teenager whose brutal murder almost 70 years ago galvanized the civil rights movement.\par \par \uc2 The fleecing of America. The family-run ministry and the millions and assistance they got for employees who didn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  tonight ending a term that reshaped the court itself and the country in dramatic fashion. The first Black woman to be confirmed as a Supreme Court Justice is sworn in today. Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  replacing retiring Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . She joins a court that just days ago rocked the country with its ruling overturning the long-established constitutional right to abortions.\par \par \uc2 The term ending today with split decisions for the Biden administration on immigration and the environment. In a win for its border policy, the court saying the administration acted properly in trying to end the Trump-era policy that requires asylum seekers wait in Mexico while their cases are decided. But the court also undercut the administration`s climate change efforts, saying the \uc2 EPA\uc2  overreached in its efforts to move the country from coal production to cleaner alternatives. Justice Correspondent Pete Williams has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In one of the most important environmental rulings in decades, the court said the \uc2 EPA\uc2  does not have broad authority to limit greenhouse gas emissions by shifting energy production away from coal-burning plants and toward cleaner sources like wind and solar. By a six to three vote, the court said such a major action would require explicit permission from Congress. Writing for the majority, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said it is not plausible that Congress gave \uc2 EPA\uc2  the authority to adopt on its own such a regulatory scheme.\par \par \uc2 JODY FREEMAN\uc2 , HARVARD LAW SCHOOL: It has really cut off the agency`s ability to do the farthest-reaching most impactful thing, to cut carbon emissions from the power sector. That`s the sector of the economy that produces about a quarter of our greenhouse gas emissions.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: For the dissenters, Justice Elena Kagan said the decision means the court appoints itself the decision-maker on climate policy. I cannot think of many things more frightening. The court`s ruling also could have broader implications because it said only Congress, not federal agencies can set the rules on what it called major questions. Some Legal experts say that`s a recipe for inaction.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE EMERSON, UCLA LAW SCHOOL: Congress doesn`t have the time or the expertise to be able to address all of the major social and economic problems on its agenda. And in addition, it`s often incapable of reaching grand bargains.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: In a separate case, the Biden administration can go ahead with its efforts to end the Trump program that forces migrants seeking asylum to wait in Mexico the vote five to four with Roberts and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  joining the court`s three liberals.\par \par \uc2 Today also mark the end of Justice Stephen Breyer`s 28 years on the court. His last official act was to join Roberts in administering the required two oaths of office to \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  who becomes the court`s first Black woman.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: So help me God.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE, \uc2 U.S. SUPREME COURT\uc2 : And now, on behalf of all the members of the court, I am pleased to welcome Justice Jackson to the court and to our common calling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Pete, looking ahead, the court said it will hear a case that could affect the coming presidential election.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Yes, Lester. The court agreed to decide whether state legislatures and not state courts get the last word on how elections should be conducted for federal candidates. Republicans say that`s how it`s supposed to work. But opponents say that would leave state courts unable to defend voters when their rights are threatened, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Pete Williams outside the Supreme Court for us tonight, thank you. And this evening, President Biden slamming the Supreme Court for its decision overturning Roe v Wade, accusing the justices of outrageous behavior and urging a controversial change to Senate rules in response. Kelly O`Donnell has that story.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): From a world stage moment in Spain, President Biden accused the highest court of upending American freedoms.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  in overruling not only Roe v. Wade, but essentially challenging the right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Consequences he sees as so dire, the president urged Congress to restore abortion rights with new law. But in a 50-50 Senate, Democrats don`t have enough votes, making a surprise announcement today --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And if the filibuster gets in the way --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The President said he would back a limited change to Senate rules to allow a simple majority vote to make abortion legal again nationally.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: -- require an exception to the filibuster for this action to deal with the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s a flip, breaking his decades-long filibuster support.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ending the filibuster is a very dangerous thing to do.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: But tonight, top Democrats say they cannot make the change because a few Democrats won`t agree. While top Republican \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said the President`s attacks on the court are unmerited and dangerous. Today, I asked about the political pressure he faces.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): Are you the best messenger to carry this forward when Democrats, many of them, many progressives, want you to do more?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, I am the President of the United States of America. That makes me the best messenger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): Tomorrow, the President will meet with Democratic governors on abortion rights at the state level and will announce his own next steps. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In state houses and courthouses across the country, people are grappling with rapidly changing laws governing abortion. And Anne Thompson has the latest now on the next wave of restrictions that could be on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Moves on the state level to restrict abortions hitting legal obstacles. Florida`s ban on abortions after 15 weeks scheduled to take effect tomorrow blocked in court today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN COOPER, JUDGE, LEON COUNTY FLORIDA 2ND JUDICIAL COURT: That it violates the privacy provision of the Florida constitution.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Disappointing the governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Yes, these are unborn babies that have heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Kentucky`s trigger law a near-total ban put on a temporary hold too applauded by that state`s governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): The trigger law in Kentucky is extremists.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Now, a new salvo in the abortion rights war. Conservative lawmakers looking at potential laws to keep women from crossing state lines for legal abortions by targeting corporations paying their travel expenses. Arkansas State Senator Jason Rapert leads the National Association of Christian Lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 JASON RAPERT, ARKANSAS STATE SENATOR: We`re are exploring legislation and drafting language that would stop some of these woke corporations from using shareholder money illegally and without real authority to traffic people for the cause of an abortion.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Some say any effort to stop women from crossing state lines is a step too far.\par \par \uc2 SARA ROSENBAUM, PROFESSOR, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY HEALTH LAW AND POLICY: This would be like Virginia passing a law saying that it`s illegal for me to go to Maryland to get dental care or illegal for me to have my primary care physician be in the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Today, 90 local prosecutors from across the country have signed on to his statement refusing to prosecute those who seek, provide, or support abortions, calling criminalizing abortion care a mockery of justice. St. Louis County District Attorney Wesley Bell.\par \par \uc2 But if your state bans abortion, isn`t it your job to enforce the law?\par \par \uc2 WESLEY BELL, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, ST. LOUIS COUNTY: It`s well-settled law that prosecutors have discretion over the limited criminal justice resources that we have. And we make decisions all the time in that guiding principle is public safety and justice.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: A nation still debating what`s enforceable and what`s law in a post-Roe world. Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, let`s circle back now to the court`s ruling allowing President Biden to end the remain in Mexico policy at the border. It has migrant advocates celebrating but critics say it will send the record number of border crossings soaring even higher. Gabe Gutierrez now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, another tragedy at the Southern Border. Four migrants killed in Texas as authorities chased alleged human smugglers. It comes just days after 53 migrants died in San Antonio. As the immigration debate ramps up, now, the Supreme Court has ruled the Biden administration can end the Trump era remain in Mexico policy.\par \par \uc2 FELICIA RANGEL-SAMPONARO, IMMIGRANT ADVOCATE: I was very happy to hear it.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: We first met immigrant advocate Felicia Rangel-Samponaro a year ago at a migrant tent camp in Reynosa, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 There`s an overwhelming sense of desperation here.\par \par \uc2 Since then, she says that side of the border has grown increasingly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:09]\par \par \uc2 RANGEL-SAMPONARO: Asylum seekers get murdered all the time in Mexico. There are kidnappings every day.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Remain in Mexico and required asylum seekers to wait south of the border while the U.S. asylum cases were processed. Its supporters argue that today`s Supreme Court decision will encourage even more migrants to come to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 DAN PATRICK\uc2  (R-TX): This is devastating for the state of Texas and the United States of America. We`re going to see more people here illegally than live in the two largest cities in America, New York and Los Angeles combined.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: There were more than 239,000 illegal border crossings in May, the highest monthly total ever recorded.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): If you don`t have any repercussions, and they feel that all they got to do is see a speed bump at the border, of course they`re going to continue coming.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The Biden administration is also trying to lift Title 42, another Trump-era border restriction. But a federal judge has kept that in place for now, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, one of the biggest July 4 getaways ever is underway. A stress test for airlines already struggling to meet summer schedules, canceling hundreds more flights today as Delta pilots picketed. Here`s NBC`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Walking information picket lines at seven airports nationwide today off duty Delta pilots in contract talks, who say they`re working excessive overtime as the airline tries to fly at schedule without enough pilots.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE EICKHOFF, FIRST OFFICER, \uc2 DELTA AIRLINES\uc2 : Those are fatiguing schedules. We`re working as hard as we can.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Delta, United, and American have struggled since Memorial Day. More than 3000 total cancellations just since Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We don`t have a lot of money. We don`t have a lot of time. So, when you do that, there goes your vacation.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: In an email to customers, Delta CEO today said, this level of disruption and uncertainty is unacceptable. Meanwhile, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  identifying volatile weather again today leading to more delays and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NOLEN, ACTING ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 FAA\uc2 : Today, Airlines are carrying the highest numbers of passengers since early 2020.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: But the vast majority of Americans, 42 million, are driving this weekend, a new record, and paying record July 4th pump prices $4.86 a gallon, though down 16 cents in two weeks. President Biden says the embargo on Russian oil means Americans will continue paying a premium at the pump.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As long as it takes. Russia cannot in fact, defeat Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Higher oil prices also lead to higher jet fuel and airline ticket prices, cutting into vacation budgets on the road and in the air.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): If you`re traveling, especially with kids this summer, add extra time, pad time to your flights in the event they`re canceled or rescheduled so you can make adjustments without throwing your entire vacation into chaos. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, seven decades later, the discovery of an arrest warrant never served in the case of Emmett Till. What his relatives are calling for now.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with a surprising development of the case of Emmett Till, the 14-year-old Black child murdered for allegedly whistling at a white woman. 70 years later, an unserved warrant uncovered for a suspect in the case, a suspect who`s still alive. Here`s Ron Allen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Some 67 years after the gruesome kidnapping, torture, and murder of 14-year-old Emmett Till, a document just found in the basement of a Mississippi courthouse reigniting his family`s decades-long quest for justice.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH WATTS, COUSIN OF EMMETT TILL: We dropped to our knees, we prayed, we shouted, we embraced each other.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Till`s cousin Deborah Watts says the family found an unserved warrant for the arrest of Carolyn Bryant Dunham for kidnapping, the woman who Till supposedly made a rude gesture toward in a store in 1955. Her husband Roy Bryant and his half-brother then abducted and murdered Till. His mutilated body in an open casket one of the most iconic images of the Civil Rights era. Till`s killers both now dead were acquitted by an all- white jury but later confessed in interviews. But Dunham now in her 80s was never arrested or charged.\par \par \uc2 Do you really think this is a turning point?\par \par \uc2 [19:15:19]\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I do. I have to hold out hope. And there is a note on the reverse side of the warrant that indicates that she was not found in the county, but no one ever really searched for her.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: No comment today from state and federal investigators who through the decades have reopened and close the case as recently as last year. Some Legal experts say prosecutors would likely have to obtain a new warrant if they choose to pursue the case. And no comment today from Dunham herself. We were unable to reach Dunham.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I believe that Mississippi has blood on their hands and they have an opportunity now to clean that up and to make sure that justice prevails.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: A family`s fight endures in the brutal killing of a young boy that changed an entire nation. Ron Allen, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next for us tonight, the fleecing of America. A family that duped the American government out of millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Millions of businesses applied for government help during the heart of the pandemic including one Florida-based family ministry that received millions of taxpayer dollars to pay employees. Stephanie Gosk got what authority say really happened in our series The fleecing of America.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA EDWARDS, CANADIAN: His word is his will.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): When the pandemic hit, Canadian Joshua Edwards asked the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to help his family`s ministry stay afloat. He said they needed $6 million to keep paying more than 450 employees according to court records with the operation run out of this Florida office building. The government approved the PPP loan, even adding another $2 million, making it eight in total.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the problem. The only employees were the Edwards family, four of them. And Canadian records show the ministry`s monthly income was just $5,500. The application was a total fabrication signed off by an 88-year- old accountant in Canada suffering from dementia, as per court documents. Warren Smith is the president of Ministry Watch, an evangelical watchdog.\par \par \uc2 So, how didn`t it flip a switch somewhere in the government when they saw this application?\par \par \uc2 WARREN SMITH, PRESIDENT, MINISTRY WATCH: Well, it should have. This would have been easy. If you just did a couple of \uc2 Google\uc2  searches on this, the bank would have known that there was nothing there.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: This money was supposed to go to businesses that were keeping people employed. And here you have this ministry taking advantage of that allegedly.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. Well, this kind of fraud is just absolutely reprehensible because there was a limit to those funds.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Instead of funding a ministry set up to help the poor, the family put a downpayment on this $3 million house in a gated community blocks from Disney World. Within months, federal agents started asking questions. It was September 2020 when Secret Service agents came to this home where the Edwards family was living. They wanted to know what they were doing with millions of dollars in taxpayer money. But when they got here, no one was home, the cars were gone, and the house was cleared out.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service quickly asked Florida Highway Patrol to pull them over.\par \par \uc2 Police were waiting on this highway on ramp for the Edwards as they headed north. When they stopped them, the father said that they were on their way to a conference in Texas, but he couldn`t provide any details. And according to the authorities, in the backseat, there was a paper shredder the family bought the week before and garbage bags filled with shredded documents.\par \par \uc2 Federal agents seized the $8 million and briefly took them into custody for an immigration charge that was later dismissed. The next day, they were let go. And almost 20 months later, no more criminal charges. The Edwards still own their house, so we knocked on the door. No one answered but neighbors told NBC they still live there.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to the Secret Service and they said the investigation is ongoing and they can`t provide any more information. The U.S. Attorney in Florida would not comment. Alex Little is a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 If I go to a bank and I steal $3 million and the police show up at my door and I say oh, I`m so sorry. I`m so sorry I robbed that bank. Here`s your $3 million back. Don`t I get arrested?\par \par \uc2 ALEX LITTLE, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: You`d absolutely get arrested. And I think we can expect and should expect the federal government to take the same steps.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Why hasn`t there been an arrest?\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: That`s a great question. And it should be the sort of case that federal prosecutors are interested in and ultimately ended up prosecuting.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Just last year, the family released new videos asking for donations, but quickly took them down. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Orlando.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next for us tonight, how a teenager makes the cars he loves picture perfect.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, tonight, lots of kids are fascinated by cars but one young man has transformed that sense of wonder into mind bending photography. Here`s Maggie Vespa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Anthony Schmidt`s love of cars is all about perspective. At 14, he is waiting to see the world from the driver`s seat. In the meantime, focusing his love through a lens of vintage surf scene. Church parking of the past, a classic, fueling up. On his iPhone, this teenager captures auras of eras and tricks the eye manipulating the miniature transforming it into life-size. His parents` home --\par \par \uc2 Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 Now a warehouse for his muses.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SCHMIDT, YOUNG PHOTOGRAPHER: 1908 \uc2 Ford\uc2  Model T right there.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: 3000 of them.\par \par \uc2 So, you know every single one.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: What`s that one?\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: 56 Ford Sunliner.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: What about the one next to it?\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Same, 56 Ford Sunliner.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Up here?\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: 57 Chevrolet Bel Air.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: That colossal capacity for detail stems from Anthony`s superpower.\par \par \uc2 And what do you like about having autism?\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Not much like about having a talent and having the -- probably if -- I wouldn`t probably be all over the internet if I didn`t have it.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: This Seattle area boy born with autism is a smash hit on social media.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  142,000, Tick Tock about 600,000.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: He boasts books and calendars too, a dream for mom.\par \par \uc2 RAMONA SCHMIDT, ANTHONY`S MOTHER: All the attention from different people for his photography makes me feel so good.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Much of Anthony`s collection, rooms of cars, a wall of plates, came from fans of his drive.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: All autism people out there should always chase their dreams. It`s the same with anyone. You should always chase your dreams.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Shifting perspectives on cars and life. Maggie Vespa, NBC News, Woodinville, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: I think I spotted our old family car in that collection. What a great hobby. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220701ei6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Growing Confusion Over Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 566 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HOLT: Five days after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the country is now caught in a confusing patchwork of -- patchwork of abortion laws and challenges with many patients and providers alike wondering what they should do. Anne Thompson now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The battle over Kentucky`s abortion trigger law moving to a courtroom today.\par \par \uc2 BRIGITTE AMIRI, DEPUTY DIRECTOR, \uc2 ACLU\uc2  REPRODUCTIVE FREEDOM PROJECT: Your Honor, there is a real vagueness problem here with the trigger ban.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Kentucky, one of 12 states where the future of abortion services is now in court causing confusion for patients, doctors, and lawmakers alike.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE GOODRICK, FOUNDER, CAMELBACK FAMILY PLANNING: I don`t think it could get more confusing.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Gabrielle Goodrick leads Arizona`s Camelback Family Planning Clinic performing abortions until Friday. Now, its website says it`s temporarily suspended elective abortions, unsure what`s legal.\par \par \uc2 GOODRICK: So, none of us want to get involved in a lawsuit or get arrested.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: If you went ahead and provided abortion services, what`s at risks?\par \par \uc2 GOODRICK: I think being arrested, charged with a felony, losing your medical license.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: There were two abortion laws on Arizona`s books. One sign this March which bans abortion after 15 weeks, but does not repeal the second more restrictive law. That one enacted when Arizona was just a territory, bans all abortions unless the mother`s life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 CATHI HERROD, PRESIDENT, CENTER FOR ARIZONA POLICY: With the overturning of Roe versus Wade, Abortion should now be illegal in the state of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Abortion rights opponents argue for the older law.\par \par \uc2 In effect, does Arizona have two laws on abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERROD: Arizona does not have two laws on abortion. It has one law that prohibits abortion except to save the life of the mother. It`s not confusing.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But a county judge block that ban in 1973, leaving some to wonder where that law stands. And now, the confusion expanding to emergency contraception. A Missouri Health System stopped providing emergency contraception after that state banned abortion. St. Luke`s Health System concerned its clinicians could be in legal Jeopardy. Tonight it reversed course providing the pills again, but saying the ambiguity of the law continues to cause grave concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Anne, when do we see some clarity here?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (on camera): That`s the big question, Lester. We asked that question to the \uc2 Arizona Attorney General`s Office\uc2 . And it told me that it is preparing a legal brief to be filed in federal court about the state`s laws next week. Perhaps that`ll give us some answers.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Also, in Kentucky, we could get a ruling as soon as tomorrow on whether or not there will be a temporary hold on that state`s trigger law. It is very confusing.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It sure is. Anne, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220701ei6t0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 29, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander, Anne Thompson, Ron Allen, Kristen  Welker, Tom Costello, Maggie Vespa, Courtney Kube \par \uc2 3,897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the growing fallout and new details after that bombshell January 6 testimony.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The growing calls for former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to testify about the former president`s plan to go to the Capitol. Plus, former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson firing back. What she just said about the now disputed portion of her testimony. Did the former President tried to grab the steering wheel and lunge at a Secret Service agent? What the Secret Service is saying tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also, the growing confusion over abortion. In Kentucky, the trigger law battle taken to the courts. And in Arizona, the argument over differing laws. R. Kelly sentenced to 30 years in prison after years of allegations. The reaction tonight.\par \par \uc2 Devastating new images of that Russian attack on a shopping mall in Ukraine as the U.S. announces a new show of force in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of flights canceled, thousands since the start of the week. The new warning tonight from airlines ahead of the busy holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the massive fire engulfing at camp dining hall. And a terrifying moment inside Yellowstone. A bison attacking a visitor. His condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 And finally, a salute to a world war two hero who showed us the meaning of courage.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. She captured the nation`s attention with her recounting of what she said she saw and heard from within President Trump`s inner circle on January 6. Tonight, some of the accounts of former White House staffer Cassidy Hutchinson are being disputed, including what happened inside the Presidential SUV when then- President Trump angrily demanded to be taken to the Capitol where crowds were gathering.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, her attorneys writing on her behalf. Ms. Hutchinson stands by all of the testimony she provided yesterday. And this evening, Hutchinson`s testimony about senior Trump staffers expressing fears that day might end in violence has the January 6 Committee looking to speak to a new potential key witness, the then-White House counsel who according to Hutchinson warned we`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen. Peter Alexander now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): First, Cassidy Hutchinson`s explosive testimony, now the fallout. The Committee`s top Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  tonight calling for former Trump White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to testify on the record after Hutchinson detailed Cipollone his efforts to reject Mr. Trump`s original plan to join his supporters as they converged on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER ASSISTANT TO CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : Mr. Cipollone said something to the effect of please make sure we don`t go up to the Capitol, Cassidy. Keep in touch with me. We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Hutchinson, a 25-year-old former aide to Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , also testified she heard Mr. Trump demand arm supporters be allowed through security ahead of his January 6 speech.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I have heard the President say something to the effect of I don`t -- I think that they have weapons. They`re not here to hurt me.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Mr. Trump denies that claim. And tonight other portions of Hutchinson sworn testimony are being disputed, like when she said Deputy Chief of Staff Tony Ornato told her Mr. Trump got physical with his lead Secret Service agent Bobby Engle inside the Presidential SUV.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Mr. Engle, grabbed his arm. He said, sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We`re going back to the West Wing. We`re not going to the Capitol. Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engle.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But now, a source close to the Secret Service and Ornato tells NBC News Mr. Trump never grabbed the steering wheel and never assaulted Engle, and that Ornato never told Hutchinson those events occurred but they don`t deny Mr. Trump was furious and wanted to go to the Capitol. Secret Service insider say Engel and Ornato remain close to Mr. Trump. The Secret Services its agents are available to testify under oath.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  is disputing Hutchinson`s account that he asked for a pardon. Meadows is declining to cooperate with the committee citing executive privilege. And former White House lawyer Eric Hirschmann is challenging Hutchinson`s testimony that she wrote this note with wording for Mr. Trump to condemn the violence.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): That`s your handwriting, Miss Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: That`s my handwriting.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But Hirschmann spokesman says Hirschmann wrote the note. Top Republicans tonight are slamming Hutchinson`s testimony.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): It looks like you know one more day of salacious headlines. This is why Pelosi set this up from day one to be a partisan committee, a witch hunt, just to keep going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , not to get facts.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But committee Democrats are dismissing any criticism.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:02]\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Nobody is challenging the central material facts of her testimony that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  were perfectly aware that there were armed people in the crowd which he then aimed like a missile at the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Late tonight, Hutchinson`s lawyers saying she stands by all of the testimony she provided yesterday under oath.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, now there`s word about whether the wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  will be asked to testify.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, Lester. Ginni Thomas is a conservative activist who sent text messages to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  pushing the White House to overturn the 2020 elections results. Tonight, in a letter to the committee that was obtained by NBC News, Thomas` lawyer says he has not seen any reason for her to testify, and that the committee he says needs a better justification why it`s relevant. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander starting us off, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Five days after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the country is now caught in a confusing patchwork of -- patchwork of abortion laws and challenges with many patients and providers alike wondering what they should do. Anne Thompson now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The battle over Kentucky`s abortion trigger law moving to a courtroom today.\par \par \uc2 BRIGITTE AMIRI, DEPUTY DIRECTOR, \uc2 ACLU\uc2  REPRODUCTIVE FREEDOM PROJECT: Your Honor, there is a real vagueness problem here with the trigger ban.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Kentucky, one of 12 states where the future of abortion services is now in court causing confusion for patients, doctors, and lawmakers alike.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE GOODRICK, FOUNDER, CAMELBACK FAMILY PLANNING: I don`t think it could get more confusing.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Gabrielle Goodrick leads Arizona`s Camelback Family Planning Clinic performing abortions until Friday. Now, its website says it`s temporarily suspended elective abortions, unsure what`s legal.\par \par \uc2 GOODRICK: So, none of us want to get involved in a lawsuit or get arrested.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: If you went ahead and provided abortion services, what`s at risks?\par \par \uc2 GOODRICK: I think being arrested, charged with a felony, losing your medical license.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: There were two abortion laws on Arizona`s books. One sign this March which bans abortion after 15 weeks, but does not repeal the second more restrictive law. That one enacted when Arizona was just a territory, bans all abortions unless the mother`s life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 CATHI HERROD, PRESIDENT, CENTER FOR ARIZONA POLICY: With the overturning of Roe versus Wade, Abortion should now be illegal in the state of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Abortion rights opponents argue for the older law.\par \par \uc2 In effect, does Arizona have two laws on abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERROD: Arizona does not have two laws on abortion. It has one law that prohibits abortion except to save the life of the mother. It`s not confusing.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But a county judge block that ban in 1973, leaving some to wonder where that law stands. And now, the confusion expanding to emergency contraception. A Missouri Health System stopped providing emergency contraception after that state banned abortion. St. Luke`s Health System concerned its clinicians could be in legal Jeopardy. Tonight it reversed course providing the pills again, but saying the ambiguity of the law continues to cause grave concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Anne, when do we see some clarity here?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (on camera): That`s the big question, Lester. We asked that question to the \uc2 Arizona Attorney General`s Office\uc2 . And it told me that it is preparing a legal brief to be filed in federal court about the state`s laws next week. Perhaps that`ll give us some answers.\par \par \uc2 Also, in Kentucky, we could get a ruling as soon as tomorrow on whether or not there will be a temporary hold on that state`s trigger law. It is very confusing.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It sure is. Anne, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 He was one of the biggest names in pop music, but today in New York, R. Kelly was sentenced to decades in prison for racketeering and sex trafficking going back decades. Ron Allen with late details now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Disgraced music superstar R. Kelly now likely to spend the rest of his life in prison, sentenced to 30 years, convicted of luring young women and girls into his orbit and sexually abusing and exploiting them. Judge Ann Donnelly saying Kelly left a trail of broken lives in his wake. You raped them, beat them, separated them from their families, saddled them with an incurable sexually transmitted disease.\par \par \uc2 JOVANTE CUNNINGHAM, ACCUSER OF R. KELLY: 30 years did he do this and 30 years is what he got. I thank all of you all that have stood and supported us for so long.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Earlier, several of Kelly`s victims confronting him in court with emotional impact statements calling him shameless, disgusting, a predator. I wished I would die because of how low you made me feel said the woman identified only as Jane Doe.\par \par \uc2 LIZZETTE MARTINEZ, ACCUSER OF R. KELLY: It has happened to me a long time ago. I was 17. I`m 45 today. I never thought that I would be here to see him be held accountable for the atrocious things that he did to children.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: The multi-platinum artists once known as the Pied Piper of R&B was convicted of nine counts of sex-related crimes last September after decades of allegations of sexual misconduct. Crimes dating back to the 1990s. Among his first victims, the singer Aaliyah who prosecutors accused Kelly of marrying illegally when she was 15 to avoid being charged with sexually abusing her.\par \par \uc2 Today in court, Kelly stared down at the defense table, never looking at his accusers, and choosing not to address the court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:15]\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Kelly`s attorneys say he`ll appeal the conviction. And he still faces more sex-related charges in Illinois and Minnesota. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Ron Allen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn out of that horrific situation in San Antonio, Texas. Dozens of migrants trapped in a semi-truck and abandoned in the heat. The death toll has now risen to 53. Morgan Chesky tonight with new details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, stunning new evidence in what officials are calling the nation`s deadliest case of human smuggling. This empty trailer now tied to the deaths of 53 men, women, and potentially children left packed inside. Among the dead, four Hondurans, including two brothers, Alejandro and Fernando Caballero.\par \par \uc2 Today, their mother mourning their loss, saying her sons were anxious but excited for a future where they could work to build their mom a new home.\par \par \uc2 Mexican authorities cooperating with us investigators are painting a grim picture of the deadly journey after crossing the border in Laredo. Mexican officials say at 2:45 p.m. Monday, the truck passed through a border patrol checkpoint in Encino, Texas. A camera capturing their driver identified by Mexican authorities as a now-detained U.S. citizen who then drove through Cotulla before stopping 146 miles later in San Antonio on 100-degree day.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG LARRABEE, DEPUTY SPECIAL AGENT, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: It`s a lot of coordination. So, it`s suggestive of a higher-level organization.\par \par \uc2 Special Agent Craig Larrabee runs Homeland Security Investigations covering the Southern Texas border.\par \par \uc2 These organizations potentially cartels. They`re really looking at these people as a product.\par \par \uc2 LARRABEE: Just a product, a commodity. I mean that`s -- it`s just simple. More people, more money, more profit.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: On the border, Governor Greg Abbott promising additional checkpoints. Today, alongside the remote road where so many lives are lost, a somber Memorial marched by flowers and bottles of water for those no longer here. Morgan Chesky, NBC News, San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, the U.S. announcing a major new military commitment in Europe in response to Russia`s war in Ukraine. Kristen Welker was at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit in Spain and has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, new video shows the horror when a Russian missile hit that crowded shopping mall in Ukraine. Many still missing from Monday`s deadly attack. Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy says Russia is now a terrorist state. All of it intensifying the pressure on world leaders as the critical NATO Summit gets underway in Spain. \uc2 NATO\uc2  today declaring Russia its most significant and direct threat.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re stepping up. We`re proving that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is more needed now than it ever has been.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: In an attempt to counter Russia, today, President Biden announcing plans to bolster U.S. forces in eastern Europe, including a permanent U.S. base in Poland and increased rotations in the Baltics. That in addition to billions and military aid already delivered to Ukraine. But President Zelenskyy addressing \uc2 NATO\uc2  today says it`s not enough. And Ukraine needs $5 billion a month in assistance.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, STATE DEPARTMENT: We`re going to help Ukraine for as long as we can as fast as we can. And that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We pressed a key Biden National Security Council official, John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 Would you say that Russia is winning this war right now?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I would not say that Russia is winning this war. The Ukrainians have an unbelievable ability to defend themselves. They are acting bravely and skillfully in the field. We`re going to help them continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Another major development, \uc2 NATO\uc2  officially invited Sweden and Finland to join after Turkey dropped its opposition. A show of force against Russia as its war in Ukraine enters its fifth month. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Kristen, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the massive fire at a summer camp. And air chaos, thousands of flights already canceled this week. The new warning from airlines ahead of the holiday.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now to the ongoing travel mess. Today, again, hundreds of flights cancelled ahead of the busy July 4 weekend. If you have upcoming travel plans, experts recommend padding your schedule as much as possible. Here`s Tom Costello with airlines warnings tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The email warning from Delta, prepare for operational challenges this weekend. Passengers can rebook their flights with no fare difference or change fees. So far, roughly 3000 flights canceled this week alone even on blue sky days.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For now, we are a little bit stranded. We don`t have a way to get to Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  warning today summer storms are creating problems.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Obviously there`ll be delays throughout the southeast no matter what airport you`re at from Charlotte all the way down southbound.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: American and delta have canceled the most flights this week. The biggest problem, still not enough pilots to fly their schedules after thousands took early retirement during the pandemic. Tomorrow, off-duty Delta pilots plan to pick at outside airports nationwide demanding changes to pay benefits and schedules.\par \par \uc2 JASON AMBROSI, CHAIRMAN, DELTA MASTER EXECUTIVE COUNCIL: The cumulative effect of continuously flying overtime month after month after month just adds up. And you know, pilots are making that tough safety call and it`s sad to say I`m not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Delta says its goal is to provide pilots with the best compensation based on pay, retirement, work rules, and profit-sharing. United, American, and several regional carriers have already raised pilot pay even tripling it. But Transportation Secretary Buttigieg tells Lester raising the mandatory pilot retirement age beyond 65 is not likely.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Right now, we have these kinds of requirements based on safety judgments. And I haven`t seen any change in the safety rationale for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): The hope is that the airlines will be able to right size their schedules for the staffing levels they have in time for Thanksgiving and Christmas. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It was a frightening scene today at a Maryland summer camp where a fire engulfed a dining hall. About 100 firefighters responded. Thankfully, all the campers and the counselors at the historic Camp Erie are safe. Parents had been assured that camp will continue uninterrupted.\par \par \uc2 Up next, are there signs of trouble ahead? Months ago, companies couldn`t hire fast enough, but now, some are turning to layoffs.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A man is recovering tonight after he was gored by a bison in Yellowstone National Park. Park officials say the 34-year-old man from Colorado was with a child and close to the bull when it charged near Old Faithful. And the man was hospitalized with an injured arm. In May, a 25- year-old woman was also gored and tossed 10 feet into the air.\par \par \uc2 After months of what`s been termed a great resignation and worker shortages, some companies are now laying off employees particularly in the high-tech sector. Here`s NBC News Maggie Vespa on the shifting job market.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Amid low unemployment and ongoing labor shortages, pink slips are raising potential red flags.\par \par \uc2 NICK BUNKER\uc2 , NORTH AMERICA ECONOMIC RESEARCH DIRECTOR, INDEED HIRING LAB: These recent layoff announcements, they`ve been very concentrated in one or two sectors.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: With major companies across finance and tech laying off hundreds or in some cases more than 1000 employees. The latest, CNBC confirming automaker \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is eliminating about 200 jobs in one California Office. This after reports that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  vowed to cut 10 percent of the company`s salaried headcount. The CEO blaming rising costs on supply chain delays.\par \par \uc2 Also on the layoffs list, streaming giant \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , the online lecture circuit MasterClass, real estate company \uc2 Redfin\uc2 , as well as big-name banks like \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 . And following big losses in crypto markets, cryptocurrency exchange \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  letting go of more than 1000 employees, including Ulysses Garza who was working in customer experience.\par \par \uc2 How did that hit you?\par \par \uc2 ULYSSES GARZA, EMPLOYEE, COINBASE: It was weird. I felt numb for a little while. I didn`t -- I couldn`t really process it.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Garza and his wife just bought a house and are expecting a baby boy, a difficult time to be part of a growing trend.\par \par \uc2 GARZA: Every day, I go on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  and I see more posts about it. It`s shocking.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE CHOI\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND COO, COINBASE: I think that anyone would acknowledge that we`re in a very rapidly changing economic environment.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Some executives calling layoffs preparation for a potential recession ahead. But experts point to the 11.4 million job openings and a 3.6 percent unemployment rate nationwide.\par \par \uc2 BUNKER: I think what these layoffs are a few clouds on the horizon. And there`s some concern that there might be more clouds coming and that they might build up to a significant storm and that`d be a recession.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: American workers hoping the clouds pass quickly. Maggie Vespa, NBC News Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next here for us tonight, our tribute to an American hero whose legacy will live on in legend.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally tonight, he was the last Medal of Honor recipient of World War II. Woody Williams died today but Courtney Kube takes us inside his remarkable story 98 years in the making.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today, a chapter closed in American history. The last living Medal of Honor recipient from World War II, Hershel Williams, affectionately known as Woody, passed away. Born on a West Virginia dairy farm in 1923, he enlisted in the Marine Corps and was sent to Iwo Jima with a Third Marine Division in February 1945. Marines had been fighting to take the island for four days, raising the iconic flag over Mount Suribachi.\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL WILLIAMS, VETERAN, WORLD WAR II: It not only lifted our spirits here on this rock, it lifted the spirits of America.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: But Woody`s Marines were pinned down by Japanese troops firing from fortified bunkers. Using a flame thrower, Woody fought for four hours, stopping the attack and saving U.S. lives.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It wasn`t anything outstanding that particular day, it was just another day of battle.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: In October 1945, President Truman presented him with a medal of honor, but Woody said it didn`t belong to him.\par \par \uc2 COL. JACK JACOBS (RET), NBC NEWS MILITARY ANALYST: He said this metal is not mine. It belongs to all those Marines who didn`t come back. I only hold it in trust.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Woody returned home and continued to serve for more than 65 more years at the Department of Veterans Affairs, and then until his final days, working with Goldstar families.\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: Woody lived a life that reminded everybody how easy it was to do the right thing. He was a mentor to a lot of us.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: The Marine Corps is fortunate to have many heroes, but there is only one Woody Williams. Semper Fidelis, Marine. Woody Williams was 98 years old. Courtney Kube, NBC News, the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We honor him and all those he represents. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thanks for watching. I`m Lester Holt, please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220630ei6t000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The New Surge In Demand For Abortion Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: New Fallout tonight after the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade. In Texas, a judge temporarily blocked a full abortion ban allowing procedures to resume for up to six weeks. But in South Carolina and Tennessee, new laws are now in effect banning abortions after the sixth- week mark of pregnancy. While in Wisconsin, the Democratic governor backed a legal challenge to stop his state`s ban.\par \par \uc2 Now, many women are turning to medication abortions leading to increased demand for abortion pills here Stephanie Gosk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover) Today, the \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary pledging to make the abortion pill more available.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: \uc2 HHS\uc2  will take steps to increase access to medication abortion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The demand is there. Within hours of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. The phones at the Repro Legal helpline are ringing off the hook.\par \par \uc2 JILL E. ADAMS, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, IF/WHEN/HOW: We have seen an unprecedented number of inquiries coming through.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The organization answers women`s legal questions about accessing abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Our job is to tell people what the legal landscape looks like, what their rights and legal risks are.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: More than 50 percent of all abortions are medication abortions. Using a two-pill regimen called Mifeprex or its generic version. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has approved the drug for up to 10 weeks into a pregnancy with six states currently enforcing total abortion bans with limited exceptions, and more likely to do so soon.\par \par \uc2 Women in those places who want the pills are pursuing a wide range of options, each with legal, logistical, and potentially health risks. Some are going out of state for the required telehealth appointments and to get shipments of the drug. The bills can only be prescribed by certain providers and sold by a limited number of suppliers according to \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations.\par \par \uc2 ELISA WELLS, CO-FOUNDER AND CO-DIRECTOR, PLAN C: The traffic to our site just exploded on Friday. We went from about 3500 visits to 209,000.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Plan C is an online portal that provides state-by-state information on how to obtain abortion pills, including how to get them from overseas from suppliers the organization has vetted but that don`t have \uc2 FDA\uc2  authorization.\par \par \uc2 Doesn`t the \uc2 FDA\uc2  specifically warn people not to do that?\par \par \uc2 WELLS: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  does warn people not to go to overseas pharmacies, but they don`t actually prohibit it. We know that self-managing abortion with medication abortion pills is safe.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But there can sometimes be complications. And the \uc2 FDA\uc2  recommends follow-up care which women may be reluctant to get in states where abortion is illegal. Law experts also wonder what legal steps those states may take to block the use of the pills.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER, LAW PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA DAVIS: How do states stop people from getting In using these bills unless they`re willing to punish the people who live in their own borders.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Right now, no state has gone that far. But the legal landscape is changing quickly and the abortion pill is right in the middle of it. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:10]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, our exclusive with Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg. His response to concerns we will see even more delays this July 4th and his challenge to airlines.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220630ei6s000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fighting On, The New Battleground In State Courts, Judges In Louisiana And Utah Blocking Those States Abortion Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: We move now to those ongoing battles raging in the wake of the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe versus Wade the focus now shifting to new legal fights and legislation in states from coast to coast. NBC`s Stephanie Gosk have the story for us this morning. Hi Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:01]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Savannah, good morning. The Supreme Court`s ruling has left many rattled in the country. But the most immediate and dramatic impact has been in those states where abortion was banned in just a matter of hours.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Just days after that landmark Supreme Court decision, many states across the country still coping with the aftermath, and some seeking abortion facing uncertainty. Abortion is now illegal in seven states, and at least a dozen more are expected to ban or restrictive procedure in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 In both Louisiana and Utah judges have issued temporary restraining orders preventing the state`s abortion bans from going into effect. Abortion rights groups are suing the state of Louisiana arguing the ban violates the state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): All of this is a bit of whiplash, though, right, for you and also for patients, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN PITTMAN, DIRECTOR OF HOPE MEDICAL CENTER FOR WOMEN: We`ve gone from the lowest of the lows to a temporary high and I would only say to our patients it`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): But in most of the states where those bans are in effect, women who want an abortion will have to cross state lines to get one to the 20 states where abortion remains legal. Something that group, Texas Right to Life is hoping they can stop by putting social programs in place to help.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE PRESIDENT: We really want to pay attention to making sure we`re protecting pregnant students and pregnant workers.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Abortion rights supporters say that is not good enough. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , vowing the administration will fight to make sure abortions remain available.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We will do everything within our power to ensure that women have access to the medication they need, and which has been by the way \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved and that they will have freedom of travel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In Louisiana, they`ll be back in court on July 8th when that temporary restraining order could be lifted. A ruling is also expected similarly in Utah. Pro-abortion rights activists don`t hold out much hope for these efforts. But they say their legal teams are committed to keep trying guys.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Stephanie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6s000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Today for June 28, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Craig Melvin, Morgan Chesky, Maggie Vespa,  Stephanie Gosk, Peter Alexander, Richard Engel, Al Roker, Tom Llamas,  Emilie Ikeda, Janis Mackey Frayer, Carson Daly \par \uc2 6,461 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:18]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Tragedy in Texas, the bodies of at least 46 migrants found inside a tractor trailer on a sweltering hot day in San Antonio, some survivors including children rushed to nearby hospitals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF CHARLES HOOD, SAN ANTONIO FIRE DEPARTMENT: They were suffering from heat stroke, heat exhaustion, no signs of water.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning, the search for answers in one of the worst human smuggling cases in years.\par \par \uc2 Derailed, an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train in Missouri colliding with a dump truck and coming off the tracks, at least three people killed, dozens more injured, dazed passengers moments after impact.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you all right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And the Boy Scout troop that sprang into action.\par \par \uc2 Fighting on, the new battleground in state courts, judges in Louisiana and Utah blocking those states abortion bans overnight, as chaos and confusion continues at clinics across the country.\par \par \uc2 Capitol Hill surprise, the January 6th Committee at an unexpected last minute hearing today, the witness in the inner corridors of the White House who just came forward, we`re live in Washington.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, remarkable discovery, the wreckage of a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  ship lost in World War II nearly 80 years ago, the deepest shipwreck ever found, how it was located and the wartime account of a sailor who was there.\par \par \uc2 And Today exclusive, the fast food worker who did not miss one shift in nearly three decades of service.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just really want to tell everybody out there how thankful I am.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Getting the due he deserves and internet gold rush and finally a well-deserved day off as he visits us live right here in our studio, today, Tuesday, June 28th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hi everybody. Good morning. Welcome to Today on a Tuesday morning. Good to have you along with us. We got Hoda off this week and Craig we want to get right to this heartbreaking news out of San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Terrible story overnight, all unfolded when someone heard a cry for help from inside a semi-truck trailer. When it was opened, the unthinkable discovered, 46 migrants dead, amid 100 degree temperatures.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: There were some survivors and now more than a dozen others are hospitalized this morning. San Antonio`s mayor was emotional overnight talking about the scene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RON NIRENBERG (D), SAN ANTONIO, TX: Tonight we are dealing with a horrific human tragedy. So I would urge you all to think compassionately and pray for the deceased, the ailing, the families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: An astonishing human story there. NBC`s Morgan Chesky joins us from that scene. Morgan what`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, officials are calling this one of the worst mass casualty incidents in this area in recent history. And this remote road is where this massive investigation is now playing out that semi-truck where those individuals were found about 100 yards behind me and that is where this grim discovery was made late yesterday, 46 bodies inside that trailer some, Savannah, hot to the touch. And this morning we know there are 16 others fighting to survive in area hospitals, including four children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): A sweltering summer day in Texas, with temperatures topping 100 degrees, contributing to one of the country`s deadliest human smuggling cases in recent history.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Quite a few of them are already deceased.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): The tragedy unfolding Monday in a rural part of San Antonio, 46 people found dead in the back of this stifling truck. The first calls coming in just before 6:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The truck driver is running southbound on foot along the railroad tracks.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Police say a local worker made the terrible discovery after hearing cries for help. Authorities found the abandoned semi-truck with its doors partially opened.\par \par \uc2 HOOD: We`re not supposed to open up a truck and see stacks of bodies in there.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Officials say the likely cause of death, heatstroke. Incredibly 16 others survived, including several children, but they were in an extremely weakened state when they were taken to area hospitals.\par \par \uc2 HOOD: The patients that we saw were hot to the touch. They were suffering from heat stroke, heat exhaustion, no signs of water in the vehicle that was a refrigerated tractor trailer. But there was no visible working AC unit on that rig.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): This horrific scene echoing a similar tragedy in San Antonio where 10 people died crammed inside a tractor trailer back in 2017. This year has already seen record numbers of border encounters in the southwest, according to \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2 . Texas Governor Greg Abbott tweeting, quote, these deaths are on Biden. They are a result of his deadly open border policies. San Antonio`s Mayor with a different perspective.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:25]\par \par \uc2 NIRENBERG: There are, that we know of, 46 individuals who are no longer with us, who had families who were likely trying to find a better life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: And this morning, while the White House has yet to make an official statement, they have referred to the statement made by DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas who placed the blame for this squarely on top of the smugglers and said a federal investigation is ongoing. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And Morgan has there been any word on potential suspects?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: We have heard from authorities who say they have three individuals in custody. But at this time, they are not saying whether or not they are directly tied to this smuggling plot. In the meantime, this crime scene remains absolutely expansive across this remote area. We`re told that they`re going to have canine units out here today, making sure that absolutely everyone who was on board or near that truck, Savannah, has been accounted for.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Morgan Chesky at that scene for us, thank you, Morgan.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Also this morning, a team of \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigators is heading to the scene of a deadly \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment in Missouri. At least three people were killed, dozens more hurt when a train heading from Los Angeles to Chicago collided with a dump truck. NBC`s Maggie Vespa joins us with the very latest on this. Maggie, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Craig, good morning. In addition to those three fatalities that you mentioned, two people on board the train and the driver of that dump truck as you said dozens more were injured. And with that I want to step out of the way and show you this crash site. You can see the wreckage were being kept aways back from it because it is so large. And as you said, a 16-member team from the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  on its way here now two among other things, look for that trains event recorder and any video it may have captured.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Damn it, it happened.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): This is video of passengers inside the train in the immediate terrifying aftermath of Monday`s \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment. Outside, dazed survivors sitting on top of the overturned train.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you all right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It felt to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, we hit a truck. Someone was crossing tracks.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): Eight cars and two locomotives from Amtrak Southwest Chief Train 4 derailed after colliding with a dump truck. Authorities described the location where it happened as an uncontrolled crossing, meaning there were no traffic signals or crossing gates. There was a stop sign however.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On highway 11, in west Chief 4 train, we have an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment with multiple injuries, multiple fatalities, requesting all available units to respond.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): More than 200 passengers and 12 crew were aboard, multiple law enforcement agencies, first responders, and medevac units were on scene within minutes.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN DUNN, MISSOURI STATE HIGHWAY PATROL: There are multiple injuries and we can confirm there are three fatalities.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): The train could have been traveling at up to 79 miles per hour. At that speed, experts say it would need more than a mile to stop.\par \par \uc2 JASON DRINKARD, HIGH SCHOOL TEACHER: It looked like a bomb had gone off inside of the train car.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): High School teacher Jason Drinkard was heading to a conference in Chicago with his wife and six students.\par \par \uc2 DRINKARD: I can`t even begin to describe the scene of people that were injured bleeding, trying to get out of the train. My brain said this might be when I die.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): This derailment is the third fatal \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  accident this week fatal collisions in Brentwood, California and Birmingham, Alabama left four others dead. Local resident Mike Spencer says the crossing in Missouri was an accident waiting to happen.\par \par \uc2 MIKE SPENCER, LOCAL RESIDENT: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  comes through here at anywhere from 75 to 100 miles per hour. So it doesn`t give you much time to cross. If you make a mistake, this is, you got the potential of happening just like it happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VESPA: So many stories on board that train as you can imagine, here`s one more. We`re told to Boy Scout troops were also onboard, 16 scouts, eight troop leaders coming home from New Mexico, and guys, thankfully we`re told they`re all seemingly OK because they actually stayed on site after the crash to help injured passengers. Craig?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Of course they did, they`re Boy Scouts. Maggie Vespa for us there in Missouri, Maggie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We move now to those ongoing battles raging in the wake of the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe versus Wade the focus now shifting to new legal fights and legislation in states from coast to coast. NBC`s Stephanie Gosk have the story for us this morning. Hi Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:01]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Savannah, good morning. The Supreme Court`s ruling has left many rattled in the country. But the most immediate and dramatic impact has been in those states where abortion was banned in just a matter of hours.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Just days after that landmark Supreme Court decision, many states across the country still coping with the aftermath, and some seeking abortion facing uncertainty. Abortion is now illegal in seven states, and at least a dozen more are expected to ban or restrictive procedure in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 In both Louisiana and Utah judges have issued temporary restraining orders preventing the state`s abortion bans from going into effect. Abortion rights groups are suing the state of Louisiana arguing the ban violates the state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): All of this is a bit of whiplash, though, right, for you and also for patients, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN PITTMAN, DIRECTOR OF HOPE MEDICAL CENTER FOR WOMEN: We`ve gone from the lowest of the lows to a temporary high and I would only say to our patients it`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): But in most of the states where those bans are in effect, women who want an abortion will have to cross state lines to get one to the 20 states where abortion remains legal. Something that group, Texas Right to Life is hoping they can stop by putting social programs in place to help.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE PRESIDENT: We really want to pay attention to making sure we`re protecting pregnant students and pregnant workers.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Abortion rights supporters say that is not good enough. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , vowing the administration will fight to make sure abortions remain available.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We will do everything within our power to ensure that women have access to the medication they need, and which has been by the way \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved and that they will have freedom of travel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In Louisiana, they`ll be back in court on July 8th when that temporary restraining order could be lifted. A ruling is also expected similarly in Utah. Pro-abortion rights activists don`t hold out much hope for these efforts. But they say their legal teams are committed to keep trying guys.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Stephanie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Now to a surprise twist in the January 6th investigation, the House Committee looking into the insurrection suddenly adding an unexpected public hearing for leader today. And NBC`s Peter Alexander is at Washington for us. So, Peter, what can we expect from this surprise hearing? Do we know what may have prompted the change of plans?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Craig, this is an extraordinary moment to be clear, because the committee had said that it wouldn`t resume hearings for another couple of weeks. But three sources familiar with the matter tell NBC News this morning that the surprise witnesses a woman named Cassidy Hutchinson. She`s a former top aide to President Trump`s then Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . And Hutchinson will be the first White House employee to testify publicly in front of the Committee.\par \par \uc2 She was often in the room where critical conversations happened around January 6th. In last week`s hearing it was Hutchinson`s recorded closed door testimony already providing some of the biggest revelations so far, where she named several Republican lawmakers who led efforts to overturn the 2020 election and requested presidential pardons after the riot. She`s believed to have been there when Meadows described hearing Mr. Trump make supportive comments about the chance by the rioters to hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Craig?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`ll take it, Peter, a question, because it seems obviously that this was unplanned. There`s certainly suspense when the Committee suddenly calls the hearing and has this witness out of the blue. But on the flip side of it, is there a risk in case it doesn`t meet expectations?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You know, Savannah, you make a good point. It`s obviously pulling off a surprise in Washington that carries risks always but speaking to a source familiar with the investigation overnight, they emphasize to me that there`s urgency in hearing from Hutchinson among the reasons for secrecy, they say are security concerns related to her. And overnight John Dean though the President Nixon`s White House Counsel, whose dramatic testimony was crucial during Watergate, he warned the Committee that they face a high bar for a surprise witness.\par \par \uc2 He says if it`s not really important information, it will hurt the Committee`s credibility. So we will watch and see. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Peter, thank you. And we should mention NBC News will have live coverage of today`s hearing starts at 1 o`clock Eastern here on NBC, 12:30 on NBC News Now online.\par \par \uc2 Much more to get to this morning, including the ongoing search for survivors after a Russian missile strike on a crowded shopping mall in Ukraine, it killed at least 20 people. NBC`s chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel has made his way to the scene this morning. Richard, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Savannah. This attack shocked many in Ukraine not because Russia attacked Russia attacks all the time. But the location we are nowhere near the frontlines. This has been a quiet area and what was hit is just a shopping mall full of civilians. And Ukrainian say it is made them feel that nowhere in this country is safe. And perhaps that was what Russia was trying to achieve.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voice-over): After working through the night, the rescue crews this morning look exhausted mostly they are searching for remains. It`s quiet now. The only sounds are shovels. Last night right before the Russian attack, the mall was noisy, full. Ukrainian say, 1,000 people were inside. Some of the children`s toys, backpacks, and a soccer ball remain. A strong smell of fire is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:28]\par \par \uc2 Investigators are on site too, collecting evidence of possible war crimes. It`s illegal to deliberately bomb civilians. Ukrainian say that`s exactly what Russia did and is doing across the country. The mall was so crowded because the area had never been hit before, people felt safe. Earlier we spoke with President Zelenskyy, who accused Russia of trying to break Ukrainian spirit.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translation): They cannot break us even if they decide to bomb us all and all our cities. They cannot break our nation.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): Why do you think this is happening that there are now thousands of allegations of Russian war crimes here, thousands of them?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Thousands.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Why?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Very difficult question. I think that is that is not even a question, that is the something was, you know, something was broken, you know, with mentality with them. Something --\par \par \uc2 (through translation): The entire beast lurking inside of them has erupted out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Russian officials have claimed without providing any evidence that it was the Ukrainians who bombed this shopping mall themselves in order to gain international sympathy, particularly in the West. Ukrainian officials say that is exactly why the investigators here it`s to prove that these Russian claims are a lie. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Richard Engel at the scene for us, thank you. And you can see Richard`s full conversation with Ukraine`s president today on NBCNews.com.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: 7:16 right. We`re going to switch gears. And Mr. Roker hurricane season is upon us. It looks like you`ve got your eye on something.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: It is. We are watching this one area of tropical disturbance south of Louisiana. It`s got a 30 percent chance of developing in the next five days. It`s slow development. It`s possible going to make its way in next week doing something. But right now, you can see the development zone from anywhere from coastal Texas right on down to the Texas Mexico border. We`re watching this very closely.\par \par \uc2 And the other problem is the very warm water temperature, seven to eight degrees above average in the Gulf so that we could have to worry about rapid intensification as the system develops. Either way, it is going to be bringing heavy rain from the Panhandle all the way to the Texas border where we could be looking at four inches of rain or more. If there`s a silver lining it`s there`s a bit of drought there so this could help. We`re also watching potential tropical cyclone number two, its winds right now, 40 miles per hour, 420 miles east of Trinidad where they`ve got Tropical Storm watches up.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to watch this thing develop. If it does become a hurricane, usually we see, don`t see hurricanes in this area until about August 1st. This one would be just about a month early. So again, we`re going to be tracking this closely as well. We`re going to get to your local forecast for the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, Mr. Roker. Thank you. Good, sir.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead here on a Tuesday morning with millions on the move heading into this holiday weekend, we are going to share the proactive steps that you can take right now to hopefully avoid some costly delays at the airport.\par \par \uc2 [07:19:40]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And then inside a historic discovery, nearly 80 years in the making. A Navy ship that played a key role in World War II, now the deepest shipwreck ever discovered, we`ve got a first look at that remarkable find in the stories of the ships heroes. But first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Coming up, a story that we and a lot of others absolutely love.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, yes, the fast food worker who has not missed one ship, not one in 27 years, he`s missing his ship to celebrate with us after local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:20]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:19]\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: There we go.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: If that little face doesn`t make you happy, nothing will. 7:30 Tuesday morning, 28th of June. We`re just days away because "The Minions" their sweet laugh, their unintelligible language, it`s all coming this Friday, their new movie out. It`s not just about "The Minions" you guys. It`s about Gru. It`s his origin story. How did Gru become Gru? Well, Steve Carell himself will be right here to answer some of those penetrating questions.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: I didn`t know that minions were so big in real life.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: They looks so small on the screen.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Did you bring your little guy?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: He`s here. He`s psyched. He`s be here later. But yes, he`s really pumped.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, we`re looking forward to that. Let`s get a check of your 7:30 headlines. First though. Ghislaine Maxwell set to be sentenced today for helping wealthy financier Jeffrey Epstein sexually abused underage girls. Back in December jury convicted Maxwell on sex trafficking and conspiracy charges. Prosecutors say she deserves between 30 and 55 years in prison. Her lawyers have asked the judge to impose a sentence of less than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The future of \uc2 Cleveland Browns\uc2  quarterback Deshaun Watson will be hashed out today during a disciplinary hearing. Watson has agreed to settle 20 of 24 civil lawsuits for sexual misconduct, but is still facing punishment from the league. According to multiple reports, the \uc2 NFL\uc2  has recommended that the quarterback serve an indefinite suspension of at least one year. But a former federal judge acting as a disciplinary opposite for the league will make the final decision.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right to Wimbledon now, British tennis star Jodie Burrage is being praised this morning not for her play on the court but rather something she did during a break in the match. Watch this. Burrage notice a ball boy who was feeling faint on the sidelines so she rushed over to make sure he was OK. She realized he needs some sugar so she gave him a sports drink, some nutritional gel, then she gave him some water, and some candy even from a spectator, which they had passed down. The match was stopped for about 10 minutes until the boy was helped off the court. Now I know what you`re thinking here. Did she win the match? No, she did not win the match. And experts are saying because she gave away her water.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: No, I`m joking. No, she`s a winner. She didn`t win the match.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She won hearts.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: She won hearts. That was such a classic move.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, it was.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It is incredible. I watched a lot of Wimbledon yesterday. I missed that. But Serena and Coco played today. It`s for your tennis update.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now to the major issue in the minds of millions of Americans getting ready to fly to their July 4th destination.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes. For weeks we`ve been talking about the problems at our nation`s airports. And take a look already just this morning, nearly 900 flights delayed or canceled, a number that`s only expected to grow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: NBC`s Emilie Ikeda is here with more. But she`s also got some good advice for travelers this morning. Hey, Emilie.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning to you all. I wish I could say there was a way to guarantee a trip without any hiccups, but there are at least some steps you can take to help reduce the risk of travel chaos. Experts say the key to avoiding airport Armageddon is being proactive in preparing for last minute changes and responding to them as new analysis predicts some 13 million Americans will take to the skies this holiday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): It`s the final countdown to Independence Day but flying to your destination may require infuriating dependence on airlines riddled with problems. According to travel app Hopper, more than one quarter of U.S. flights are now being delayed half of them for an hour or more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our flight is canceled and as you can see, lines are pretty long to figure out how to rebook and there`s no one really kind of assisting and trying to help us figure out how to get home.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Chicago, Midway, Baltimore, and Newark among the country`s most disruption plagued airports were a perfect storm of staffing shortages, bad weather and soaring demand has some passengers rethinking their plans.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Is there anything you would do differently for your next trip?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not go. I am not making any more plans for travel. This is it.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): U.S. airports just saw their busiest travel day so far this year, nearly 2.5 million passengers on Sunday. A record anticipated to be broken this holiday weekend. So how can travelers best avoid airport Armageddon? Experts say first try to travel on off peak days, AAA says Friday July 1st will be the busiest day at airports, the least congested days Saturday the 2nd and Monday the 4th. Next sign up for your airlines free text alerts and download their app for the fastest flight notifications.\par \par \uc2 When packing remember, less is more. Try to avoid checking luggage to miss the long lines that check in and baggage claim. Still be prepared for last minute cancellations. If you need to rebook, try the airlines international call center. Even if you`re on a domestic flight they can still help and wait times can be shorter.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:01]\par \par \uc2 HAYLEY BERG, LEAD ECONOMIST AT HOPPER: For travelers who haven`t booked yet, consider adding a flight disruption protection product that allows you to rebook yourself immediately on the next available flight regardless of the airline.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): If you get stuck and can`t fly out until the next day, ask the airline to put you up in a hotel, though they`re not required by law to do so, and the best advice, remember to be nice.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Can your attitude towards the customer service reps impact the outcome?\par \par \uc2 BERG: I would say that a positive attitude is probably only going to get you further.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Keeping the skies friendly, even when the process is turbulent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Some good advice for life there. And so Emilie here`s the thing, this happened to me last week. Your flight gets canceled. Why is it so hard to book another flight these days? What`s happening on that front?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Yes, that`s a great question, that`s because the flights are already packed. Airlines have trimmed their schedules as they contend with staffing shortages, making flights more competitive. American announcing last week it`s stopping service to four cities. But if you do find yourself having trouble rebooking a flight, one other tip to take into account reaching out to the airline on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or other social media platforms especially if other customer service lines are busy. And we know a lot of people are --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: But if you do that, you should also be nice when you reach out --\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Absolutely. That`s the key here, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Be kind.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you Emilie.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Still ahead this morning, imagine never missing a single day of work, no vacations, no sick days, a fast food worker is being celebrated for doing just that for 27 years. We`re happy to say, Kevin Ford is getting a well-deserved break this morning. He`s here live.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: So great. But first we also want to take you four miles below the Pacific surface to the scene of an incredible discovery you see it right here. A Navy ship from World War II found some eight decades later. How it was finally located right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:55]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re back 7:40 on this Tuesday morning with In-Depth Today. And this morning, an amazing discovery, four miles beneath the ocean surface.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  ship, its whereabouts unknown since it sank during World War II back, and get this, 1944 has finally been found.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: NBC`s Janis Mackey Frayer has this remarkable story. Janis, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANIS MACKEY FRAYER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning. After nearly 80 years of sitting on the ocean floor in the darkness. The Samuel B. Roberts has been found. The famous ship that went down fighting is making history again has the greatest wreckage ever discovered.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): Deep in the sea off the Philippines, a remarkable discovery the wreckage of the USS Samuel B. Roberts, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  destroyer that sank during World War II nearly 80 years ago has been on the floor of the Western Pacific Ocean over four miles deep. It`s where a team of American and British explorers found the battered ship split into. The gun mount and torpedo tubes still intact. They could even make out the whole number 413.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR VESCOVO, FOUNDER AND CHIEF SUBMERSIBLE PILOT AT CALADAN OCEANIC: When the ship goes down in battle, it vanishes into the black of the ocean. But finding the wrecks I think does bring some level of peace and respect to those that fell in the battle.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): At 22,600 feet down. It`s the deepest shipwreck yet, further into the sea than Mount Kilimanjaro is high and nearly twice the depth of where explorers found the Titanic. The incredible imagery of the ship captured by a high tech sought after deep sea detective work and a lot of luck.\par \par \uc2 KELVIN MURRAY, DIRECTOR OF EXPEDITION OPERATIONS & UNDERSEA PROJECTS AT EYOS EXPEDITIONS: Finding a small wreck like this, the Sami B. in such a significant way has just been, well, we couldn`t have wished for more really.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): The Sami B. as the ship is known is famous for a heroic stand against the Japanese, during the battle off Samar in the Philippine Sea in October 1944.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Guarding the invasion of the Philippines, a vast American naval was battleships, cruisers, destroyers, submarines.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): Part of the Taffy 3 Task Force, the Navy escort was never meant for heavy battle, but made a torpedo attack at point blank range into the teeth of Japanese cruisers. Jack Yusen, a gunner on the ship, recalled before he died in 2016, how they were outnumbered and outgunned.\par \par \uc2 JACK YUSEN, SHIP GUNNER: The shells are coming in right over. We could see them right over our heads. And then I knew, boy, this is for real. It`s going to be a tight to the finish.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): The order came to abandon ship. Survivors watched her slip into the sea, along with 89 men.\par \par \uc2 YUSEN: I was just crying, my heart.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): The ship a last witness to battle now an artifact for historians.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SCHICK, THE NATIONAL WWII MUSEUM CURATOR AND RESTORATION MANAGER: They are considered a war grave, you know, look but don`t touch. It is sacred territory. It is essentially an American graveyard.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voice-over): The Expedition crew kept its distance for other reasons too. The cannons are still loaded, frozen in time on the sea floor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: There were four U.S. ships that went down in that area including the USS Johnson, which was found last year by the same explorer and was considered the deepest wreck at over 21,000 feet until this discovery of the Sami B. So their plan now is to find those two other ships to help complete this incredible connection to history. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What a treasure. Janis, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That is cool. Kind of makes you wonder what else is, you know, miles beneath the surface.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Lurking below.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So much we don`t know about the ocean.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Mr. Roker --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, sir?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Time for another to check the weather?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, we will. And in fact we`re looking at some temperatures that are finally cooling down along the west coast. But as you move inland, we`re looking at temperatures that are above normal. Billings you`re going to be at 93 today, 10 degrees above average, Salt Lake City flirting with 100, North Platte, Nebraska, 96 degrees, 10 degrees above average.\par \par \uc2 And then tomorrow, those temperatures get near record highs for Minneapolis, Pierre, Omaha, Chicago is going to be 85 degrees, Memphis 92, not too bad but still a little on the warm side. And as we head up toward the latter part of the week, temperatures warming up, Cleveland, hello, Cleveland, you`re going to be in the mid-90s by Friday, D.C. into the 90s, Boston staying pretty warm, by Friday 94 degrees, Nashville into the 90s as well.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:23]\par \par \uc2 And as we look into the July, third week or July fourth week, we`re looking at warmer than average temperatures for much of the midsection of the country, colder than average out west and also up into New England. And for July, warm in the northeast and warmer than average through the Central Plains, a little on the cool side of the Pacific Northwest. Precipitation wise, wetter down through the Southwest, Southeast but drier in the midsection of the country. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, Mr. Roker, thank you. Good sir.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new images of the Queen, stepping up this morning at a very special parade in Scotland. We`ll have more on that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And then a new honor for the most decorated Olympian of all time, Michael Phelps, now a Hall of Famer. We have a powerful message he delivered with family right there by his side, right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:21]\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL PHELPS, AMERICAN SWIMMER: We need to give these athletes the support that they deserve and they need. So I can`t wait for the future because there are kids out there that aren`t -- they aren`t afraid to dream. And they aren`t afraid to dream as big as they possibly can. And I am so excited to see what their dreams and their passions lead them to. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Mr. Phelps. Carson joining the party here that of course our good friend Michael Phelps adding another milestone to his already legendary career.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: That`s right. He was inducted into the U.S. Olympic and Paralympic Hall of Fame on Friday. And overnight, he did also post some pictures of the events with his beautiful family by his side. Phelps saying that he was humbled and honored and he use his speech to talk a lot about mental health and the impact that it has on athletes a cause of course, that he`s championed for years now. He`s even shared quite a bit with us here on today. He said it`s now his biggest journey and called for more support for athletes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: He also thanked and dedicated the honor to his family for support along the way. And of course, we`ve been following in their footsteps these little adorable Phelps kids, you might remember this moment from the Rio Olympics. Remember when his wife, Nicole, introduced us to Boomer. And of course we were obsessed with Boomer.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Roll that beautiful Boomer footage.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: There you go. There`s that beautiful baby.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean just look at this kid.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And another inductee skiing legend of course, Lindsey Vonn, she thanked her family, dedicating the award to her mother, who is currently fighting ALS. Vonn said her mom taught her so much about strength and character. Both those athletes, such a privilege to watch them compete with so much fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: By the way, one of Hall of Fame induction, they also. They had Lindsey Vonn, Billie Jean King, Mia Hamm, Michelle Kwan, the figure skater.\par \par \uc2 DALY: By the way, can I just say Boomer Phelps already more athletic than me.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 DALY: By a longshot.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Even as an infant.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on PopStart, what would you do with a huge music star walked into your local pub, sat down to piano and started performing, not bad? Well, there he is. You know that guy. That`s Chris Barnes Coldplay fame, he surprised a group of fans. We`ll tell you how that went.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Plus, Steve Carell stopping by to talk about going back in time for "Minions: The Rise of Gru" and look who`s outside as well. But first your local news,\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220628ei6s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why Critics Say They`re Providing Misinformation To Women Seeking Care\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Cynthia McFadden \par \uc2 809 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: There are more crisis pregnancy centers nationwide than abortion clinics. At least 13 states have spent nearly half a billion taxpayer dollars to fund them. But tonight, NBC News investigates the care they provide as critics warn they spread false information. Cynthia McFadden has our report from Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MCFADDEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL AND INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): We`re heading to a crisis pregnancy center in Texas, one of more than 2500 in the country. They say they provide free services and abortion information for pregnant women. What they say is that they`re often affiliated with groups supporting abortion alternatives.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is it in here?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: To learn more about what happens inside we sent into NBC News producers to ask for pregnancy counseling. At a center in the Dallas area, a volunteer told our producer they don`t offer abortions, adding abortions will cause infertility. When asked about the abortion pill, the volunteer said my job is not to scare you. You never get over seeing that baby.\par \par \uc2 She then pointed to a small plastic model like this saying, can you imagine one of these in your panties?\par \par \uc2 [19:15:07]\par \par \uc2 DONNA HOWARD, TEXAS STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 SARAH DAVIS, FORMER TEXAS REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE: So, as a taxpayer, this is infuriating, a horrible thing for a woman to be told, and medically totally an inaccurate. That`s a waste --\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: As a nurse, as a mom, you totally shocked me with that. It`s a lie. And it is shaming and it is -- IT is grotesque.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Texas State Representative Donna Howard, a Democrat, found an unlikely ally in former Republican State Representative Sarah Davis. They`re calling for these nonprofits to be regulated and demanding accountability for a program called alternatives to abortion which helps fund crisis pregnancy centers. In Texas, they outnumber abortion clinics nine to one.\par \par \uc2 When I say alternatives to abortion, what is the first thing that comes to mind?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: Wasted money, wasted taxpayer dollars, lack of accountability, and actually no metrics that indicate that has anything to do with averting abortions.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: It`s just a program to make Republicans feel good, like they`re doing something to help women.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Does it help women?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: No.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: The program will cost Texas taxpayers $100 million over the next two years.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: This is probably the only program at least that I`m aware of where the legislature basically writes a blank check without knowing what they`re getting.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: While some of the centers provide free services like diapers, ultrasounds, and STD testing, none of the Texas facilities are licensed medical providers, though some women report believing that they are. At another center, our producers visited near the border, a female staffer implied abortions can cause cancer and infertility. She then played a video saying they also cause mental illness. All of that categorically false. Alternative to abortion and Texas has also received federal tax dollars, $45 million over the last 15 years, money intended as temporary help for needy families.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: These are desperate people who need these funds. And instead, it`s going to a program that provides things like pamphlets, that provides classes.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Would you so far as to say that in some instances they are actually committing fraud?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: They point to the misinformation. And a case currently being investigated in which state funds from the program were allegedly used by the president of the center to buy land for hemp productions.\par \par \uc2 Who`s to blame?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Well, the legislature.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: Yes, but the leadership that we have in this state has pushed an agenda that has made it impossible to have any real dialogue about what is really going to make a difference in the lives of these women and their families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN (on camera): Neither the Texas governor nor the lieutenant governor responded to our request for comment. But the Charlotte Lozier Institute, an anti-abortion rights group, told NBC News that the centers provide vital services and have a satisfaction rating of over 95 percent.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Cynthia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, amid historic heat waves, we`ll show you the new tech being used to keep workers from overheating.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220630ei6r0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Clashes Moving From The Streets To The Courts With At Least Eight States Seeing Legal Challenges To Trigger Laws Banning Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the battle lines over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Clashes moving from the streets to the courts with at least eight states seeing legal challenges to trigger laws banning abortion. Several went into effect when the Supreme Court overturn Roe v. Wade just days ago with abortion now illegal or about to be in more than a dozen states. How activists are scrambling to provide services. And the state already expecting tens of thousands more patients.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the future of abortion pill who one governor said she will hold legally responsible for shipping it. President Biden under pressure to act as Republicans a regroup ahead of the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our investigation inside crisis pregnancy centers. What really goes on inside them and what we discovered about how taxpayer money is funding them?\par \par \uc2 Just breaking, an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train with more than 200 passengers derails Missouri turning deadly. What we`re learning about how passengers escaped the overturned cars. Plus, the latest on survivors.\par \par \uc2 The deadly Russian missile attack in a crowded shopping mall in Ukraine. President Zelenskyy calling the number of victims unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 Preventing related deaths as temperatures reach new records. The new wearable technology aimed at saving lives.\par \par \uc2 And Serena Williams back on the court. Inside her return to Wimbledon.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. A rapidly shifting patchwork of abortion laws tonight as the battle in the wake of Roe v. Wade`s demise spills from the chambers of the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  to state courtrooms across America. Tonight, abortion-rights supporters in more than a half dozen states putting up legal challenges trying to block abortion bans, many of the new laws designed to automatically go into effect following the court`s decision, including Louisiana where a judge temporarily halted enforcement of that state`s trigger law.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend in cities across the country, thousands marched in protest to the Supreme Court`s decision to end a constitutional right to abortion. Today`s Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  savoring the court`s decision after years of work, but new polling suggesting the decision is not in sync with public opinion. But NPR/PBS/Marist poll found 56 percent of Americans oppose the Supreme Court`s ruling versus 40 percent who support it. Blayne Alexander has much more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No justice --\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No peace.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With federal protection of abortion rights gone, both sides are turning out in the streets, while others are fighting the changes in court.\par \par \uc2 Trigger laws in at least eight states are now facing legal challenges. Today, a judge temporarily blocked enforcement of Louisiana`s law, meaning the state`s three clinics, which had stopped providing services after Friday`s decision, can once again perform abortions for now.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there was a growing patchwork of laws from state to state. Right now, abortion is illegal with few exceptions in at least seven states. And in the coming weeks, at least six more are also expected to put bans in place effectively wiping out abortion access across a wide portion of the south.\par \par \uc2 For tens of thousands of women, Illinois would be the closest option. The state`s 17 Planned Parenthood clinics are preparing to serve them.\par \par \uc2 AMY WHITAKER, CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER, PLANNED PARENTHOOD OF ILLINOIS: We are expecting to see 20 to 30,000 more out-of-state patients every year in the state of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: How will your clinics be able to handle that increase?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: We are going to be able to handle that increase by increasing the number of appointments we have, by hiring more staff.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Georgia, the Attorney General has taken steps to allow the state`s heartbeat law to take effect which would ban abortions after six weeks. But some anti-abortion groups are demanding a total ban.\par \par \uc2 RICARDO DAVIS, PRESIDENT, GEORGIA RIGHT TO LIFE: That will declare the paramount light to life of every human being from the earliest biological beginning to natural death.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You`re not calling for any exception, rape, incest.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: That`s correct. That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Other Republican-led states are also eyeing more restrictions. South Dakota`s governor says she is concerned about abortion pills that come by mail.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): I don`t believe women should ever be prosecuted. I don`t believe that mothers in this situation ever be prosecuted. Now, doctors who knowingly violate the law, they should be prosecuted definitely.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Meanwhile, Democratic leaders are looking at ways to protect abortion rights. Washington Governor \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  has declared his state a safe haven for women seeking abortion care.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 JAY INSLEE\uc2  (D-WA): We want them to know there`s a road in the West Coast protecting women on the West Coast and the citizens that come from these other states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:05:02]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Blayne, this evening, another court, another state weighed in on one of these trigger laws.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): That`s right, Lester. A judge in Utah has issued a temporary restraining order effective immediately, meaning that abortions in the state. Now, the judge says that the ban caused irreparable harm saying it deprived women of safe and local medical treatment. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Blayne Alexander, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220630ei6r0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Crowd-Pleasing Summer Wines At Different Price Points For Your Celebrations\b0\par\par\uc2 Carson Daly, Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 4,008 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:51:08]\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY: And we`re back at 8:50 with "Today`s Can`t Miss Summer."\par \par \uc2 Warm weather usually brings a few more social get-togethers. And whether you`re hosting or a guest, wine is usually at the center of attention at the party.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes. So, what is good to drink in the heat? Our friend and wine expert Vanessa Price is here with the ultimate line-up. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA PRICE, FOUNDER OF THE VINUM COLLECTIVE: Good morning. How are you?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Good morning, Vanessa.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`ve got all different kinds and none of these is really a budget-breaker here, so that`s great.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: No. Yes, so we`ve got four buckets. We`ve got $10 and under, $20, $30 and $40 and under.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Great.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, so $40s a little expensive. But, all right, let`s start with the $10 and under.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: That`s true. Working our way, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This is the wine spritz.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now, I was thought that was just like putting a little seltzer in your wine.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It is a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: But it`s just we`re going googe (ph) it up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, show us how you do it.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So a lot of people think about Aperol Spritz for the summer.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Wine Spritz is another nice alternative. The easiest rule of thumb is three parts wine to one part club soda.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: And you can go two different routes. You could do bitters, which is what we have here. Or if you so desired, you could do some fresh cut fruit.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DALY: What`s the difference?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So, one`s going to give you a little bit more of a like a bitter aromatic profile and one`s going to give you a little bit more of a fruity profile.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Can I try this?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: And one`s going to give you a little bit more of a fruity profile.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh boy, here we go.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So, it kind of depends on what your preference is.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We have to pace ourselves. We`ve got a whole line -- OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So, this one`s a bit more like a cocktail, if you know.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I like that it gives you that little --\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s that little --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Not very sweet. It`s not the --\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And what is this, a white wine? It looks a little pink (ph).\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes, so this is an Amalaya white blend.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s coming from a region in Argentina called Salta.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Which is actually one of the highest vineyards in the world.\par \par \uc2 DALY: It`s probably expensive (ph).\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And this one`s less than $10 you say.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s $10 right on the money.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DALY: The fruit.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So the idea with this is that you want to do something that`s light, fresh, crisp. So, this is going to be totally different, right?\par \par \uc2 DALY: That is different.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s really interesting. Yes. How you go bitters to fruit it`s a totally different profile. So, it just depends.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, it really is. OK.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Do you want it more fruity, more sweet or do you want more sort of bitter and zinger and savory?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Very refreshing. Now --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Which one do you like better?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I think I like the bitter one.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I do too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Isn`t it surprising?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It surprises me.\par \par \uc2 DALY: To eyeball it though, I would have gone here because it reminds me - -\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Fruity.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: -- of sangria or something.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Exactly. It`s pretty.\par \par \uc2 DALY: But -- and I don`t really like bitters. I`m not sure what it is, but this one tastes better I think.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It tastes good.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: I`m having --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now, I`m very excited about our next rosa, which I love.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Me too. Me too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You`re making --\par \par \uc2 PRICE: I`m super excited about it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- rosa ice cubes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: How do we do this?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Rosa all day, Savannah. It`s here.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Well, so it`s called --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, seriously.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- in the French Riviera it`s called von piscine or a la piscine (ph) meaning by the pool. And the idea is that on a hot summer day your wine gets hot really quick, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: And so, we`re all tempted to put ice cubes in our wine, which is totally fine, but what it does over time is it dilutes the wine, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Right, waters it down.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So -- exactly. So, if instead, ahead of time, you freeze your wine --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- and then as you`re drinking, right? So, we`ll go ahead and pour some of this to taste.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s OK too do?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: And it will freeze? Because some alcohols don`t freeze.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes. So, that`s -- so that`s actually a great point, Carson, thank you. So, actually what you want to do is make sure that you freeze your cubes at least a day and a half.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Did you guys all hear that? Vanessa just said that`s a great point, Carson.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s an excellent point actually.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s not a phrase we hear around here often.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No. But, you know what, in the wine segment --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: I`m here all week. I`m here all week.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- we do. I know, exactly. I`m like, this is kind of your wheelhouse though. Cheers. OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So -- yes, cheers. So --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Why do these ice cubes look so cool? They`re --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Because they`re rosa ice cubes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Oh, so that`s the wine?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes, it`s the actual wine.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I missed something.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh wow, the highs and the lows.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: You made the point, again.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Dang it (ph). And I just blew it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Almost.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: And yet --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Almost. Almost had it.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- but so the important point is here you have to freeze --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That is --\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- it at least a day in advance.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Because it takes longer to freeze than water.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Those melt so it`s like a never-ending glass of wine?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s like a never-ending really cold glass of rosa.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK. I`m on my way back.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes. Yes. Yes. He`s with us now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now, does it have to be rosa? Like, could any white wine work if you wanted?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: You could do it with any white wine.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: This is sort of the classic presentation.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: You also see it sometimes with champagne.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Nice. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Cool, right?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s super cool.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And any old ice cube tray would work?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Any old ice cube tray, but --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- you just need a longer period of time than you would for water.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Interesting, definitely (ph).\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, now what`s our next one?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: OK. So, this is something that I thought, I just wanted to point out, because we see screw cap a lot. Like if you see our first wine, that was a screw cap and our second one --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I love a screw cap.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Me too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But is like day class-a (ph) or is it OK?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Well, that`s the thing. The perception has long been that it means inexpensive --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s cheap wine.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- cheap, not good. But, all the way up to really high-level producers, they use screw caps. And the reason for that is they want to keep their wines fresh. They want to keep them crisp. They want to keep them fruity. Which is exactly the profile that you want for summer, right?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Why thank you, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. I like it too if you don`t --\par \par \uc2 DALY: So, corking doesn`t have any effect on the taste of the wine? Or it`s not integral to the taste?\par \par \uc2 [08:55:00]\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It does. It`s integral to the taste if you`re going to age your wine.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: But if you`re not going to age your wine, which you`ve got a really fresh pinot grigio here from Livio Felluga, this is a --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s a tasty one.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- northern Italian producer. You know, then we`ve also got, next to it we`ve got the -- they call it Sauvignon Blanc, they just feel the blanc is redundant. We can see it`s white.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: But these are both really fresh, really crisp. But, you can see there`s a bit more texture, a bit more flavor than you would think of for maybe a less expensive style.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Very different.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes. Super good, right?\par \par \uc2 DALY: No, very good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I like the screw top if you only want to have one glass and then you just screw it on --\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Screw it right back on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It just keeps better.\par \par \uc2 DALY: When have you only ever had one glass?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, fair, fair, fair. Only two glasses. Ok.\par \par \uc2 DALY: My parents had -- in the `80s, you`d open up my frig and there was boxes of wine --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: -- with spigots. And it would be one red and one -- do you think box wine will ever come back?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: I am a huge advocate of boxed wine.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Really?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: If we do this again I`m bringing boxed wine.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. Great.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK. Let`s do that. That would be fun.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That would be fun.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, then finally --\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s my speed right there.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: OK, cool.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- these are reds that you`re chilling?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Yes. So, this is --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I thought that was against the law.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Oh no, no, no, no, no, no. This is something that I am such a huge advocate of and I`m so excited. These are both a bit more off the beaten path style.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So you have the Anne-Sophie Debois Beaujolais, which is your first glass. And then the second one is the Quinta do Javali, which is a Portuguese red.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It`s so good, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Chilled too. I`m shocked. It`s really good.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: So, like here`s the thing that`s important about this.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: You can`t do the big reds, right. Like cabernets, shiraz, because the same thing that makes them good --\par \par \uc2 DALY: We just got to have a sip, because we got to go --\par \par \uc2 PRICE: -- could make them sort of metallic.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, oh my, we`ve got to guzzle. Vanessa, you`re amazing.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Too much fun.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: More -- I know, exactly. We`re back after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: This morning on the "3rd Hour of TODAY," facing off. Protests and rallies following the Supreme Court`s landmark ruling striking down Roe v. Wade. We`re live with the immediate impact and what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Then, travel nightmare. Another weekend filled with flight cancellations and delays as millions plan to fly over the 4th of July weekend. What`s being done to make sure they get off the ground.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "Motivational Monday." The skin care company that`s giving animals a second chance. Today, Monday, June 27, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza, this is the "3rd Hour of TODAY."\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Good morning, everybody. It`s a Monday morning. The "3rd Hour of TODAY." I`m Al, along with Craig, Dylan. Sheinelle off today. And obviously, a busy weekend.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And a historic weekend in this country.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes. Indeed. Protests and rallies taking place all over America in response to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 `s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade Friday declaring the constitutional right to abortion no longer exists. NBC`s Blayne Alexander joins us this morning from outside the state house there in Atlanta, Georgia\par \par \uc2 So, Blayne, just let`s start by walking through what this decision means and where have some of these laws already changed?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You know, Craig, it was pretty striking on Friday. We saw some states change their policy almost immediately. There are about 10 states that actually restricted access to abortion almost as soon as that decision came down. Nine of them banned the procedure outright with very few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 I, actually, over the weekend, spoke to a clinic director in Alabama, Huntsville, Alabama. He told me that had into walk into a full waiting room and tell the women who were there that he could no longer help them. He described disbelief and tears, just a range of emotions there. So, those are places where it took place almost immediately and handful of states, we`re expecting it to see go into effect really any day now. For instance, Tennessee and Idaho, both of their trigger laws give about 30 days or so. So, we`ll see that ban take place within the next month.\par \par \uc2 And then, there are others that have already started the process. Here in Georgia, the attorney general has asked the court to allow a law to take effect that would reduce abortion access from 20 weeks down to six, Craig.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, Blayne, the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  estimates about 24 percent of U.S. women will have had an abortion by the age of 45 and about 60 percent of those women already have children. So, what are the reactions we`re seeing on both sides of the issue around the country?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You know, Al, I really think that the demonstrations just kind of tell the story. We`ve seen so many people, and not just those fighting for abortion rights, but those who have been spending years, decades trying to end abortion, trying to overturn Roe v. Wade. I spent a lot of time talking to people those on both sides of this issue.\par \par \uc2 For those who are fighting for abortion rights, they use words like gutted. They felt betrayed by the government, one person told me. They said that even though that draft opinion had come out and they knew what was coming, that didn`t help soften the blow at all. But when I spoke with those who had spent years trying to end abortion, they essentially told me that they broke down in tears, tears of joy, said that there was relief.\par \par \uc2 What`s interesting to me though is when talking to people on both sides, one place where they do agree. They say that their focus now is going to be getting the women the resources that they need for a safe pregnancy and beyond. Guys.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST AND METEOROLOGIST: And, Blayne, there are abortion pills, what is the legal status of those? I mean, will people in states that restrict abortion be able to obtain those, say, by mail?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That is a great question. You know, if you are in a state where abortion is illegal, you won`t be able to get them from a doctor in your state. That`s very clear. But where it gets tricky is where you are talk about telehealth appointments or getting those by mail, and that is something that some states are also going try to and crack down on. But those are federally regulated pills, those are federally approved.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, on Friday, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said that states cannot ban those pills for that very reason. They are \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved. It is something that is very likely going end up in court. Also, the reality of it is -- it`s very difficult to kind of regulate mail like that. It is something, though, that is certainly going to be pertinent for a lot of women.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2020, according to one study, more than half of all abortions were medication abortions, guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Blayne Alexander for us there in Atlanta. Blaye, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And then, we turn to another topic that`s affecting millions of Americans this weekend and beyond. Travel chaos at airports all around the country. Over the weekend, airlines canceling well over a thousand flights, 10,000 more actually delayed.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And it`s leading up to what is expected to be an incredibly busy 4th of July weekend. NBC`s Sam Brock is following the story from \uc2 Miami International Airport\uc2  where travelers had a rough go of it this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Sam.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Guy, good morning. Definitely not a great time to be a traveler, as many people have already found out. The projections for the holiday weekend for July 4th somewhere in the neighborhood of 11 million people are planning on flying. That is right about where we were before the pandemic hit. But the problem is, we don`t have the staffing levels for our airlines that we saw in 2019 before that point in time.\par \par \uc2 So, what is going on right now, an absolute cascade of cancellations and also delays. There are already almost 700 cancellations according to FlightAware in the United States this morning, that is on the heels of more than 1,400 cancellations over the weekend, more than 10,000 delays. So, what`s happening is people nationwide are getting to airports and they`re either their seeing flights canceled or being told rebook, waiting in long lines that can go on for entire terminals only to find out there is no more availability to switch their flights.\par \par \uc2 In terms of things that you can keep in mind that might be helpful as you`re trying to navigate that, well, for one, book directly with the airlines, trying to change flights with the third-party is very difficult. Even if it is a little more expensive, try to get that direct flight because as the day goes on and you see more accumulations of delays, that is going make it harder for you. And then, try all of the tools in your tool belt. Stand near a gate agent. Try to reach someone on the phone as you are trying to rebook. And also, there is a chat feature on most airlines apps that you can access as well.\par \par \uc2 Any and all ways that you can try to get a human being to help you would certainly help as you are trying right now to go through what the country is dealing with, a really difficult situation trying to get from point A to point B. In Miami, Sam Brock, NBC News. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow. All right.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: You know, it`s one of those things you are at the airport and you`re like, oh, it is not going happen to me. My flights is not going to get delayed. But then it does because everyone is getting delayed or canceled. So --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It happened to me last week. I know it happened to your daughter.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We went to -- yes. According to -- Leila was trying to get back to Paris and she was delayed over three hours. So --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I will say and I think Sam alluded to this, when I -- when my flight was canceled last week, on Delta, I walked over to the \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  counter and I stood in line and I waited and I asked --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Asked for the next flight.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean, I had to, you know, pay through the nose for it but --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: You got to get home.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I didn`t have a choice.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. Well, let`s take another turn this Monday morning. We have an exclusive --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: -- the studio. The winner of this year`s World`s Ugliest Dog Contest. The 17-year-old chihuahua mix, Mr. Happy Face.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And his owner Jeneda Benally.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Mr. Happy Face.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, my goodness. Can I pet him?\par \par \uc2 JENEDA BENALLY, MR. HAPPY FACE OWNER: Yes, you may.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I always tell me kids to do that. Don`t just --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes. Always ask.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: -- stick your hand in the dog`s face.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: Yes, always ask.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, how does it feel?\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: I don`t know. How does it feel Mr. Happy Face? You know, this is such a whirlwind. It is really an incredible opportunity that is gifted through Mr. Happy Face to -- you know, to just talk about amazing things that people can do and the possibility. I mean, when I adopted Mr. Happy Face, I did not think about anything more. I didn`t imagine the possibility of who he could be to so many people. And seeing that when people see him, they smile. They make him happy.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes. I can`t stop smiling.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: So, I named him Mr. Happy Face thinking that his face was happy. But when I -- I have the opportunity as the one who is holding him to look at everybody else`s faces and see how happy they are by looking at him.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: How did you choose him?\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: I actually -- he chose me.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I knew that was coming. I saw it coming.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: Yes. Did you?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: So, he chose me. I was at the shelter. And I had hoped to adopt this other really cute sweet dainty little dog, and she had just been adopted. And I already had driven just, you know, an hour and forty-five minutes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Rigth.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: And so, I asked for -- I asked, who here is most likely to not get adopted?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And boom. But who has the last laugh? Mr. Happy Face.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Mr. Happy Face.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Jeneda, thank you so much for both being here. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: He likes you, Dylan. I could tell.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I know.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: But we also want to invite people. I`m on tour this summer and he will be doing meet and greets and we might go different hospitals as well.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. We`ll put the information on our website.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: So sweet.\par \par \uc2 BENALLY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Jeneda, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thanks, Happy.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: All right. Coming up next, we are sticking with the animal theme in "Motivational Monday." The skin care company that is doing something beautiful at a farm that is giving animals a second chance.\par \par \uc2 Then, later, in our series "How to Adult," the important difference between being happy and feeling content. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6r000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests And Celebrations Pouring Onto The Streets\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Jesse Kirsch \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: All right. Let`s get to your news, 8:00.\par \par \uc2 Reaction to Friday`s Supreme Court ruling on abortion played out at protests and parades this is weekend. And critics of the decision are now concerned about what might be targeted next. NBC`s Jesse Kirsch is live in New York this morning with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Jesse, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Ever since the Supreme Court overturned Roe on Friday, people have been taking to the streets both slamming and celebrating the decision. One of the latest protests here in New York, a reminder that other key Supreme Court decision`s fates are also now uncertain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Women`s rights are human rights. Women`s rights are human rights.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): This morning emotion still raging about the Roe V. Wade reversal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Like, they can`t decide on our right to choose what we want to do with our healthcare, with our reproductive healthcare. Like, it`s really messed up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re absolutely overjoyed by today`s decision.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): One of the latest mass protests against the ruling during New York City Pride.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Women, feel like are under attack. And men can`t just standby and, you know, be on the sidelines and just let it happen.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Planned parenthood kicked off this Sunday march after Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  argued rulings that guarantee rights to contraception, same sex intimacy, and same sex marriage should all be revisited and overruled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m just happy that people feel, like, they can come out here and be themselves and show support.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): After Friday`s ruling, crowds both dismayed and thrilled grew outside the fenced off Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (on camera): Today, they`re continuing well beyond the nation`s capital with thousand marching in the streets.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Thousands of proabortion rights activists jammed New York City traffic for hours. Others blocking the Los Angeles freeway. Some demonstrations turning violent at a Cedar Rapids, Iowa protest, Friday. Police say, a truck hit and injured someone.\par \par \uc2 While in Arizona, authorities say groups tried breaking into the State Capitol with the legislators still in session as State troopers deployed teargas. And in L.A. during an abortion rights protest, Former "Full House" star Jodie Sweetin was pushed to the ground by police. And in Rhode Island, Democrat Jennifer Rourke says her GOP opponent for State senate attacked her at an abortion rights rally. John Lugo, who`s a Providence police officer and was off-duty at the time has dropped out of the race and is charged with assault.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ROURKE, (D) RHODE ISLAND STATE SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s extremely disappointing. Political violence like this is not something that is new, especially being a black female candidate running for office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (on camera): There are protests and rallies expected to continue. The question now, can the passion on both sides of this issue carry through to the midterm elections. People on both sides of this issue have said they feel there`s something to vote for in the fall. However, there are others who say immediate action is needed now. Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Jesse Kirsch, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: This morning, Ukraine`s President, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  address a group of seven economic summit in Germany.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And appearing by video link, President Zelenskyy asked President Biden and other allied leaders for antiaircraft defense systems. His appeal coming a day after Russia unleashed a deadly airstrike on Kyiv. Zelenskyy also asked for help to export Ukraine`s grain harvest which has been largely bottled up by a Russian naval blockade. The \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders pledged new sanctions to reduce Russia`s income from oil and gold exports.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Queen Elizabeth is in Scotland where she attended her first public event today since her platinum jubilee weekend. The Queen greeted with a music and military pageantry in the Scottish capital of Edinburgh. She was there to take part in the ancient Ceremony of the Keys. During that ritual, she is symbolically offered the keys to the city then she returns them to Edinburgh`s elected officials for safekeeping.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6r000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Demonstrations Coast To Coast And Getting Heated In The Wake Of That Historic Supreme Court Decision Overturning Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 2,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:19]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Goodwill morning. Battle lines.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My body.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: My choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Demonstrations coast to coast and getting heated in the wake of that historic Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade. Just ahead, where abortion is already banned, some patients finding out in the waiting room, what some companies are doing to support abortion rights. And the legal landscape, what the decision means for issues like contraception and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, Russian forces carrying out the most intense bombing campaign in Ukraine in weeks, new strikes in the capitol of Kyiv. President Biden meeting with world leaders in Europe this morning. Will the West stay united? Where there live.\par \par \uc2 Travel chaos, problems multiplying at the nation`s airports after yet another weekend of big crowds and canceled flights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can`t even see the end of the line now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up, what to expect for this week`s Fourth of July rush.\par \par \uc2 Frightening moments, dramatic video showing the pact stands suddenly collapsing at an arena in Colombia. At least four people killed, hundreds injured. We will have the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, very close call. Lightning strikes a boat filled with passengers off the coast of Florida, this morning, the dramatic video and the rescue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I love them all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re all angels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And it saves lives.\par \par \uc2 And box office king.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m ready to fly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: With "Elvis" ties "Top Gun" for the top spot over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM CRUISE, AMERICAN ACTOR: This is your captain speaking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But "Maverick" flies right by. The billion dollar mark, can Hollywood rejoices, today, Monday, June 27th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb, live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hi everybody. Good morning. Welcome to Today. Good to have you with us Monday morning. Hoda is off. And did you have a good weekend?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I had a great weekend. It was a fast weekend as they all are with the kids, how about you?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Long weekend with the kids, same thing.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: True.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We got our top story this morning. New details on that fast moving fallout from the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. The reverberations just starting to be felt coast to coast this morning. Some states already taking action.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: In fact the moment that decision was released on Friday, demonstrators on both sides of the issue erupted outside the High Court, similar scenes quickly unfolding across the country throughout the weekend.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So here is the landscape this morning, at least 10 states restrict abortion access, some banned the procedure altogether. We`ve got it covered. We`ll start with NBC`s Blayne Alexander in Atlanta for us. Blayne, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Savannah, good morning to you. In some places that change was immediate. Clinics across the country either shutting their doors or stopping services as the court`s decision came down. In other states, they immediately started putting change in motion. Here in Georgia, the Attorney General has asked a court to allow a law to take effect that would reduce abortion access from 20 weeks down to six weeks. And of course all of this comes as we are seeing nationwide protests people either celebrating or mourning the court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My body --\par \par \uc2 CROWD: My choice.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): In every corner of the country, emotions spilling into the streets. Anger and fear facing relief, joy, all responding to the end of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The feeling of just total despair.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have gained protection for the most innocent members of the human family.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Some weekend protests turned to violent. In Arizona, officers use tear gas to break up protesters at the state capitol. And in Rhode Island, a police officer running for State Senate was arrested after allegedly punching his female opponent during an abortion rights rally. He has since pulled out of the race. At an abortion rights protest in Los Angeles, viral video shows "Full House "actress Jodie Sweetin pushed to the ground by police officers. The \uc2 LAPD\uc2  says it is aware of the video and use of force will be evaluated. Following the court`s decision, Friday, at least 10 states immediately restricted abortion access with many banning the procedure altogether. Including Alabama, where at the Women`s Center in Huntsville, Dalton Johnson (ph) had to break the news to women already in the waiting room.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What was it like in that room?\par \par \uc2 DALTON JOHNSON (ph), CLINIC OPERATOR: It was just raw emotions. It was everything from people that were upset, angry. People breaking down, crying.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): In Arkansas, where abortion was legal up to 20 weeks, doctors now could face up to 10 years in prison for performing the procedure, the only exception life of the mother. At least three more states shut down services with the law still unclear, but at least 16 states and Washington, D.C. have laws protecting the right to an abortion. Among those states, Illinois, we`re over the weekend President Trump took a victory lap on his conservative court appointments at a rally with Republican House member, Mary Miller, who made these controversial comments.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:26]\par \par \uc2 REP. MARY MILLER (R-IL): I want to thank you for the historic victory for white life in the Supreme Court yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Miller`s campaign says she miss read her prepared remarks and mixed to say right to life. The pivotal Supreme Court case originated with this Mississippi abortion clinic where this morning women are still attending appointments. State law allows 10 days before it has to shut down. Tyler Harden runs Planned Parenthood in the state.\par \par \uc2 TYLER HARDEN, MISSISSIPPI STATE DIRECTOR, PLANNED PARENTHOOD: You have appointments in these next 10 days, keep those appointments and make those appointments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And this morning, we are getting a new look at just how Americans feel about the court`s decision. A new poll out this morning from NPR and \uc2 PBS\uc2  news hour shows that a majority of Americans, 56 percent say they oppose the court`s decision, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Blayne, thank you very much. That sets the table for our next conversation with NBC`s justice correspondent Pete Williams. And Pete, the Supreme Court has spoken but that is not the end of the legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: No and in fact, they`ve already started in the states. Planned Parenthood is challenging the trigger law now in effect in Utah, saying that even though the Supreme Court has ruled that there`s no right to abortion in the U.S. Constitution, the right is nonetheless protected by Utah State Constitution, because it provides greater family related legal protections and guarantees of personal autonomy. There`s a similar suit in Florida, it says the state`s privacy laws protect the right to abortion. And the courts are going to have to answer some other questions. For example, can estate make it a crime to travel to another state to get abortion services? Or can it ban advertising or counseling about how to get those services where they`re legal? And do the banning states have to allow exceptions for rape, incest, and protecting a woman`s health?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`ll add one more. Another big question is over abortion by medicine, the abortion pill as it`s called. Some states may want to target that medication.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, and already have. In the states that ban abortion, it is illegal to get those bills from a doctor. And some states are trying to go further and also banned receiving the pills by mail from a state where they`re legal. The Biden administration says the states can`t do that because those pills are approved by the federal government and the courts will probably have to decide that too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And it also raises the question when you look at this decision that it dismantles the legal underpinning of Roe versus Wade. But does that have an application to other cases, for example, gay marriage or contraception? \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in his concurring opinion, said yes. Do you expect those issues to come up before the court in the future?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don`t see them coming quickly. But the reason for the question is that Friday`s ruling says there is no constitutional right to abortion because it is not deeply rooted in the nation`s history and tradition. And certainly same sex marriage and access to contraceptives would flunk that test. So Justice Thomas says the court should take another look at them now. But the majority and the ruling said four separate times that it was only about abortion and doesn`t threaten those other rights. So about all you can say is that they appear to be safe, at least for now. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Pete Williams, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, now we turn to the potential political implications here. What impact will the overturning of Roe v. Wade have on the crucial midterm elections now just four months away? Our chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker joins us from the Bavarian Alps where President Biden is in the middle of a quick trip to Europe. So, Kristen, before the President left for Europe, we heard him talk about the Supreme Court`s decision, essentially putting abortion on the ballot. What does this potentially mean for Democrats in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning, Craig. The battle over abortion is creating a remarkable backdrop to this G7 Summit, Democrats who were bracing for major losses in the midterms see the abortion issue as a way to energize their voters and potentially hold on to more seats. Now President Biden has directed his administration to protect access to things like contraception, as you just heard, and the abortion pill. But look, he acknowledges his powers are really quite limited in this space. And before he left for Europe, as you said Craig, the President did call on Congress to make abortion legal at the federal level. That`s something that Democrats right now aren`t even close to having enough votes for.\par \par \uc2 So the President`s also urging voters to elect candidates who support abortion rights. The challenge, though, for Democrats is balancing their message on abortion, with other really pressing issues for voters like soaring inflation, high gas prices, and of course the reality check here, Craig, it could take years for Democrats to make abortion legal at the federal level again, if it happens at all, Craig.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So Kristen, with Democrats hoping to use this decision to energize their base, to galvanize their base, how are Republicans navigating this issue going forward?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: They say it`s a motivating issue for their voters too. In fact, a number of Republican lawmakers cheered the news over the weekend and also insisted there is more work to be done. They are calling on Congress to ban abortion at the federal level. So that`s the other side of the coin. But the challenge for the GOP is convincing those voters who were motivated by overturning Roe. Some of them single issue voters that the fight really isn`t over. And there`s a larger internal debate within the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 You heard Pete talk about this and Blayne, over just how far to push the limits on abortion at the state level with some states already passing laws that ban abortion with no exceptions in cases of rape and incest. And this is really interesting. According to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " over the weekend, former President Trump who of course, appointed those three conservative justices who made this all possible has publicly hailed the ruling. But he`s also privately expressing concerns this could hurt Republicans at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 Now a source close to Mr. Trump would not comment on the report, but he told me overnight, Mr. Trump sees Roe as a complicated political issue putting Republicans in uncharted territory. Craig?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Our chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker traveling with the President at the G7 Summit. Kristen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6r0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Attends \uc2 G7\uc2  Meeting In Germany\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Kelly O`Donnell \par \uc2 1,694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: President Biden is in Germany this morning taking part in the G7 Summit, meeting with world leaders about a variety of global issues and hearing from some of his counterparts who say they are shocked by the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 NBC News Senior White House Correspondent Kelly O`Donnell is traveling with the president near the German border in Austria.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, what more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Willie. The setting here, spectacular, and the meaning, serious. The Ukraine war, food shortages and a new global partnership on infrastructure that President Biden will talk about in a speech tonight as he tries to keep allies united while there is so much division back home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voiceover): Welcomed by the \uc2 G7\uc2  host nation Germany, President Biden focused on unity among allies over Ukraine, meeting with Chancellor Scholz.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: We have to stay together.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voiceover): Beyond Ukraine, global inflation is a priority.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to continue working on the economic challenges we face.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voiceover): The president returns to the world stage here with his closest allies at a moment when some of them are openly stunned by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . Criticism and disbelief blasted out on social media. Canada`s \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  called the decision horrific, adding, no government, politician or man should tell a woman what she can and cannot do with her body.\par \par \uc2 France`s Emmanuel Macron, abortion is a fundamental right for all women.\par \par \uc2 And Great Britain`s \uc2 Boris Johnson\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , U.K. PRIME MINISTER: I got to tell you, I think it`s a big step backwards.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voiceover): Their dismay shared by President Biden who lashed out at the court before leaving the U.S.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the Supreme Court has made some terrible decisions.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voiceover): With limited options, the president said his administration would preserve abortion rights where still legal and keep up the political fight.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And we are going to take actions to protect women`s rights and reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (voiceover): In Rural Illinois Saturday night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters cheered his role, shifting the High Court to a conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 While the former president praised the abortion decision.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: A victory for the rule of law and above all, a victory for life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): The former president was campaigning in Illinois to help support Republican Congresswoman Mary Miller. She is seeking a second term. And Miller has been controversial having quoted Hitler. She added to that fuel on the fire by how she described the abortion decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARY MILLER (R-IL): I want to thank you for the historic victory for white life in the Supreme Court yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, Miller`s office says she misspoke and didn`t mean to say white life, but the right to life. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Well, Kelly, there`s certainly a lot for the president to contend with here back home. But what is on the president`s agenda during that trip abroad?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We will be watching a lot of things. The meetings he`s having one-on-one with other world leaders and how the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders will work to try to keep pressure on Russia. We also expect Ukrainian president, Zelenskyy, to address the leaders here virtually about the latest in the Ukraine war. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And that war is escalating. Kelly O`Donnell traveling with the president. Kelly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Todd is NBC`s Political Director and Moderator of "Meet the Press." Chuck, good morning. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s go back to Roe v. Wade here. The NBC "Wall Street Journal" poll conducted just in May, the most recent results we have show only 30 percent of Americans thought Roe v. Wade should be overturned, nearly two-thirds say it should be upheld. It`s not, of course, the job of the Supreme Court to listen to public polling, but it is a fascinating snapshot that so much of the country wanted this kept in place and yet, the Supreme Court decided to strike it down.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MEET THE PRESS MODERATOR: Willie, this isn`t new. For the almost entirety of Roe`s life span as law of this land, it`s basically had 55 to 60 percent support in the United States. It is never really moved. It has never really budged at all, which makes this another odd decision.\par \par \uc2 This is not -- you know, I have seen a lot of stories about how, boy, this is a real victory in the pro-life movement and how they did it and it was a grassroots campaign. Well, yes, it was except they never changed public opinion. So, by that score, it was an unsuccessful campaign, but they found other ways to do this, right, success in state legislatures and from there, creating new litmus tests for judges.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll tell you, at the end of the day, it is an opinion that doesn`t come across mostly conservative or follow judicial restraint because ask yourself this, Willie, whatever you think of Roe, Roe never made somebody do something they did not want to do. Now, we have a ruling that essentially makes a woman do something she doesn`t want to do, depending on the state she lives in. That in itself seems to be a radical activist court change, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And we will see what the impacts are as we move into the fall. President Biden, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , others saying this is an issue to go and vote on.\par \par \uc2 So, Chuck, the Roe decision on Friday overshadowed what really was an extraordinary week before the January 6th Committee, a couple sets of hearings. You had the former acting attorney general basically laying out a president pressuring him to try to help overturn the election, ultimately, to install a different attorney general when he wouldn`t do it. What did you make of what we heard and how hard are these hearings hitting the American people as we watch?\par \par \uc2 TODD: First of all, I am impressed that it`s a consistent 10 plus million people tuning into each one of these hearings each time. I mean, people are paying attention. And I think you are starting to see it break through to a point, I mean, we`ll see how much on the right side of the aisle digest this over time.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I thought this last hearing, it really sort of brought into focus I think how multifaceted this scheme was by the president and his sort of team that he put together to do this. I mean, they were ready in advance. They were -- they had put a former White House lawyer, put him into the Justice Department to help Jeff Clark write these memos. They had sort of were looking for every soft spot they could find.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was pretty clear that there was this sort of orderly effort. First, go to the courts, then hit the states. Get these alternative slates of electors. OK. That`s now working. Let`s get the Justice Department to say there is election fraud. That should at least create a pause. And when that didn`t work, let`s pressure \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . And then, of course, we`re going to find out when that didn`t work was the next step, the violence. Obviously, that is what happened, but was it something the president sequentially ordered?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And the president telling the leadership at the Justice Department according to their notes and their quotes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, just say the election was corrupt and let me handle the rest. Chuck, thank you very much. Boy, a lot to talk about this morning on "Meet the Press." Chuck will be joined by Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff of the January 6th Committee, as well as Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  of New York and the Republican governor of Arkansas, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After months of fighting in the eastern part of Ukraine, the Russian military has turned its sights again to the capital city of Kyiv. The mayor of Kyiv reports several explosions there overnight. A rocket destroyed that nine-story residential building, more than two dozen people were rescued including a seven-year-old girl. Meanwhile, Ukrainian troops now have almost fully retreated from the City of Severodonetsk in the eastern part of the country, as separatist fighters supporting Russia advance there.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Let`s turn now and get a quick check of your local Sunday weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Straight ahead, the highs and lows of the week, including a dramatic rescue in a pool after a swimmer for Team USA faints underwater and her coach dives in to save her life.\par \par \uc2 And the beautiful moment when a disabled boy takes his first steps right into the waiting arms of his twin brother.\par \par \uc2 But up next, after the Supreme Court ended 50 years of federal abortion rights in a decision celebrated by many Americans, others now worry about the court`s next moves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, SCOTUSBLOG AUTHOR: All of the majority said in its Roe ruling was, look, we are not calling this into question today, but what it implied was, come talk to us tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s coming up on "Sunday TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6q0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 Where The Fight Over Abortion Rights Goes Next And Which Other Landmark Cases The Supreme Court May Soon Review\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: Friday`s decision by the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade was the culmination of a half century fight for anti-abortion activists in America. For many other Americans though it was a devastating defeat, eliminating abortion access altogether in some states, and raising concerns about which other hard earned federal rights the court might be ready to strike down next. NBC News Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali has our Sunday Focus.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Amid the emotional responses it to the Supreme Court overturning Roe --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How do you think I felt, man? This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Women will die.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want to be able to choose. I think every woman should have the right to choose.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Questions now about what comes next.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s open season for what we look at as human rights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s terrifying. And if they are going to do this again, what`s next?\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): With the nation`s patchwork of states solidifying abortion restrictions, advocates are now bracing for more possible roll backs of rights won at the nation`s highest court. In overturning 50 years of precedent Friday, conservative justices arguing the right to an abortion is not deeply rooted in the nation`s history and tradition.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  goes further in a concurring opinion, writing, the court must revisit and overrule past landmark decisions on civil rights, including legalized contraception, same sex intimacy and gay marriage. Effectively saying, the same logic in overturning Roe should be applied to those protections.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The court says that its opinion doesn`t undermine those decisions, but the doctrinal test that it adopts absolutely calls those decisions into question.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Other conservative justices like \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  says, Friday`s ruling on Roe does not reverse precedent on other issues. Kavanaugh also swore during his 2018 confirmation hearing that he believed Roe was settled precedent.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Senator, Roe v. Wade is an important precedent of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Some senators who voted to confirm him, like Republican \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  now saying she feels misled by the judge. Democrat Joe Manchin echoing that in a statement, saying, I trusted the judges when they testified under oath that they also believed Roe was settled precedent.\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, SCOTUSBLOG AUTHOR: Nobody who is relying on an existing right to privacy, like a right to same-sex marriage, can rest easy. All of the majority said in its Roe ruling was, look, we are not calling this into question today, but what it implied was, come talk to us tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): But as scholars seek to make sense of it all, lawmakers looking for larger remedies, like changing Senate rules to legislate around reproductive protections, as well as reforming the way presidents are elected. In an op-ed Saturday, two Democratic senators suggesting even fixing the outdated electoral college that allowed presidential candidates who lost the popular vote to take office and nominate three of the five justice who voted to overturn Roe, a reference to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  losing the popular vote in 2016.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And Ali joins me now live. Ali, good morning. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 As you said, Justice Thomas was explicit about the decisions on issues like contraception and gay marriage he thinks are worth looking at now. So, do experts really expect those kinds of cases to come before this court?\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): Look, Willie, maybe not immediately, but eventually, it`s possible. As one expert told me, people take signals like these from the court very seriously. Roe had nearly 50 years of precedent, gay marriage, for instance, less than 10. And if the court`s logic is that, if it`s not explicitly in the constitution, then it`s up for debate, well, a lot wasn`t in the constitution, including the words she and woman. Whether these cases come before the court and, of course, their ultimate decision also has little to do with majority public opinion.\par \par \uc2 In an NBC News poll, 65 percent of Americans support the right to same-sex marriage. A record high number. But in that same poll, 63 percent of Americans said Roe should not be overturned just days before it was. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: A parallel that has a lot of people bracing to protect their own rights now. Ali, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, a new "Sunday Sitdown" at the National History Museum of Los Angeles with actor, writer and star director Jon Favreau on his latest project that brings dinosaurs to life and a resume that includes marvel, "Star Wars" and "Swingers."\par \par \uc2 And then, a life well lived. The county clerk who made history when she committed the revolutionary act of issuing a marriage license to gay couple.\par \par \uc2 And as we head to break, our "Photo of the Week." A bison joining a crowd gathered in Yellowstone National Park to watch an eruption of the famed Old Faithful Geyser. Much of that iconic park remains closed during this busy summer travel season after historic flooding damaged roads there. But the Southern Loop, home to Old Faithful, reopened to visitors this week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [08:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Cocktail? The guy behind the guy.\par \par \uc2 JON FAVREAU, ACTOR AND FILMMAKER: A scotch on the rocks, please. Any scotch will do as long as it`s not a blend, of course, single malt. (INAUDIBLE) perhaps. Maybe a glen. Any glen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Scotch on the rocks?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6q0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for June 26, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Andrea Mitchell, Garrett Haake \par \uc2 8,752 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST (voice over): This Sunday, the end of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN:: Complete and utter joy that it was finally overturned.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN:: It feels like a betrayal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Supreme Court overturns the right to abortion, nearly 50 years after Roe.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: Let`s be very clear. The health and life of women in this nation are now at risk.\par \par \uc2 TODD: States already moving to stop abortions, with more to come.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN:: Restoring the state of Arkansas the authority to prohibit abortions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This morning, the legal, medical and political fallout from this historic decision.\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to Republican Governor Asa Hutchinson of Arkansas and to Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  of New York.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the January 6th Committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): President Trump ultimately wanted the Department of Justice to say the election was, quote, "corrupt," and, quote, "leave the rest to me and the Republican congressmen."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Former President Trump`s scheme to stay in power, pushing for an ally to run Justice and claim election fraud, relying on Internet rumors and baseless claims.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN:: In my recollection, he said, "We`ve got lots of theories; we just don`t have the evidence."\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guests this morning, committee member Adam Schiff, and President Biden signs the first gun safety legislation in decades, even as the Supreme Court expands gun rights.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News chief Washington correspondent Andrea Mitchell; Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ; NBC News senior Capitol Hill correspondent, Garrett Haake; and Wall Street Journal columnist Peggy Noonan.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Good Sunday morning, after a week that was among the most significant in our recent history. Even before the Roe decision, the January 6th Committee heard evidence of an attempted coup led by a defeated president who sought any way possible to hold on to power. And we saw the Supreme Court significantly expand gun rights, even as President Biden signed the first gun safety bill into law in decades.\par \par \uc2 But it was the court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade and nearly five decades of abortion rights that gripped the nation and prompted celebrations and bitterness from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 What we know is abortion is likely to be banned in roughly half the states. And Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  suggested birth control, gay rights and same- sex marriage could be next. And we now know that Donald Trump`s 2016 election, which gave him three Supreme Court picks, all of whom voted to overturn Roe, is easily now among the most consequential ever in our nation`s history.\par \par \uc2 What we don`t know is the political fallout from all this. Will there be room in the Republican Party for people who actually want some abortion rights? Can Democrats channel their anger and frustration to win elections and pull even with Republicans for this November?\par \par \uc2 And thanks to how this court was put together, its credibility has never been more in question. And this decision came at a time when the country is already dangerously divided.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s a sad day for the court and for the country.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): Today`s historic Supreme Court decision is a victory for the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nearly 50 years after Roe.\par \par \uc2 WALTER CRONKITE, FORMER ANCHOR, "\uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS": In a landmark ruling, the Supreme Court today legalized abortions.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): The decision by the high court to eliminate the constitutional right to an abortion was met with celebrations and anger, with protests in cities across the country.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN:: It`s shocking. This is something that women should have a right to.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN:: It`s a roller coaster of emotion. It`s -- it`s complete and utter joy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The justices decided 6-3 to uphold Mississippi`s law banning abortion after 15 weeks, the case actually before the court. But then they went further, deciding 5-4 to overturn Roe. All three of Trump`s appointees were in the majority. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said he would have taken "a more measured course."\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): We couldn`t be happier with this decision. And I`ve never been prouder to be a Mississippian.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2  (D-IL): We are headed down a dangerous spiral that will erode our democracy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The decision set in motion trigger bans in 13 states. In seven of those states, abortion is now already illegal. At least two dozen states are certain or likely to ban abortion soon.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KEVIN STITT (R-OK): Swift action to certify Section 861 banned abortion in our state immediately.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We`re fighting like hell here in Michigan to protect Michigan women`s access to abortion and reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now there are questions about health implications, from access to care to fertility services to emergency contraception, about the criminalization of abortion care and about what`s to come for other privacy rights.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:11]\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The Supreme Court is a different branch of government. They can look at whatever comes before them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote in a concurrence, "In future cases, we should reconsider all of this court`s substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence and Obergefell," decisions on contraception, sex, and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Alito`s majority opinion differed, saying "abortion is fundamentally different" from those precedents.\par \par \uc2 Now, without the votes in the Senate to codify Roe into law, Democrats are turning to November.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Reproductive freedom is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 AMY KLOBUCHAR\uc2  (D-MN): The answer is simple. It`s November. It`s the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hoping the abortion issue will divide Republicans, drive base turnout and turn swing voters, suburban women in particular, from a focus on the economy to the fight over reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 One of the states with a trigger law was Arkansas. And hours after the Supreme Court released its opinion, the state officially banned all abortions except to save the life of the mother. There is no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is the Republican governor of Arkansas, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor Hutchinson, welcome back to "Meet the Press," sir.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): It`s great to be with you, Chuck. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with the -- with what the Arkansas law does, no exception for rape or incest. Do you believe that`s a mistake?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, first of all, this is a day that those in the pro-life movement has worked for over 40 years. I didn`t think it would come this quickly. And the decision of the Supreme Court was really something that will save lives. In Arkansas, we immediately followed the direction of the law. It triggered the ban of abortion except, as you said, in the case of the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 What that means is that we`re going to work hard to make sure that mothers have the services that they need. We`re going to expand adoption services to meet those needs. That`s what`s critically important right now. And we have to remember, this is not a nationwide ban on abortion. Every state is going to have the ability, under the court decision, to make its own decisions. Arkansas made its choice previous to that based upon the trigger law, and other states are going to make different decisions. This is fundamental, as the court said, in our democracy, that ultimately the people decide their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, if a 13-year-old, though, in Arkansas, is raped by a relative, that 13-year-old cannot get an abortion in Arkansas. Are you comfortable with that?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I`m not. I would have preferred a different outcome than that. But that`s not the debate today in Arkansas. It might be in the future. But for now, the law triggered, with only one exception, while you can debate whether there ought to be additional exceptions, every state`s going to make a different determination on that under our constitution. And this is going to continue to be discussed. But as this particular point, the only exception in Arkansas is to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s a few other questions, I think, that people in Arkansas have about, sort of, now what? Emergency contraception, the so-called Plan B pill -- is it illegal for someone in Arkansas to take, or not, now?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, first of all, you know, the fact that Justice Thomas said that, you know, some of these other cases ought to be looked at, there was no one that joined in that opinion. So this is not about contraceptions; this is not about same-sex marriage; a very limited decision on this particular issue of abortion. And so in Arkansas, the right to contraception is important. It`s recognized. It`s not going to be touched. And that`s -- that`s the outcome here. And, again, every state can debate that, but I don`t see that`s a threat. And it`s very important now to assure women that the access to contraception is going to be able to continue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does -- but does the morning-after pill qualify as contraception?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, every state, again, will make that determination, and...\par \par \uc2 TODD: So it`s not clear?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: ... those regulations...\par \par \uc2 TODD: In Arkansas?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: ... that they will determine will be reviewed by the court. In Arkansas, this should not be an issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What about IUDs? There are some -- that`s -- it`s a fertilized egg, which under Arkansas`s law, might be considered an unborn child. Are IUDs part of this ban?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: No, it`s a contraception, and that is continued to be permitted. It`s not an issue in this.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 This is about abortion, and that`s what has been triggered. And it`s not about contraception. that is clear. And women should be assured of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what is -- what about for miscarriages? Because there`s going to be -- the treatment for miscarriages looks very similar to the treatment of abortion. Is there going to be a department in Arkansas that -- that inspects all miscarriages or investigates miscarriages?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: No. Absolutely not. We`re -- obviously have oversight of abortion clinics to make sure that they follow the law. But, again, Chuck, this is about a limited ruling on abortion that is historic in nature, will save lives. But the other decisions, when you`re talking about miscarriages, the exception is, of course, is the life of the mother, or there`s a medical/health emergency, and that`s between the physician -- they`re going to make those judgments in how to handle those things. This is simply about abortion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is between a doctor and the woman then, when it comes to those decisions?\par \par \uc2 I say that so the doctor can -- can feel as if there`s no criminal liability thereunder when they treat women and decide that -- that an abortion is necessary due to save her life. That doctor is not going to investigated; that doctor is not going to get harassed?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: They certainly should not. But it`s very important that doctors have always made life and health and death decisions. That`s what they`re trained to do. They have to make those medical judgments. And it`s not the state`s judgment to reconfigure those or to rethink those. They make those judgments. And the decision that has to be made is whether there`s an abortion, and then you go after the provider as a criminal penalty, not the woman. That`s a very important thing.\par \par \uc2 What I`ve told my Department of Health is this, one, enforce the law; but, secondly, I want a plan in which we can provide greater resources to women who are going through unwanted pregnancies, to make sure that we have the pregnancy centers. We put a million dollars more into funding of those to provide resources for the women. That`s what I`m telling my Department of Health.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Whatever you thought of Roe, that decision never forced anybody to do anything they didn`t want to do. This decision now will force a woman to carry a pregnancy that they perhaps didn`t want to do. Does that at all make you uncomfortable that we now -- you are forcing somebody to do something they don`t want to do? Roe didn`t do that. This ruling does.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, no. I think it`s a very appropriate ruling. Obviously, when you`re looking at the government and the power of the government forcing someone to carry a child to term, you`ve got to think that through. And legislators are thinking that through. And whenever you`re looking at the opportunity to save the unborn child, that is the rare circumstance in this case that you use the power of the state to say, unless the health of the mother is at risk, let`s carry that child to term.\par \par \uc2 And so when you`re saving life, that`s an appropriate role of the state, and that`s what the courts have said the states can determine. Other states might make a different judgment. That`s why we have elections. That`s why we have elected representatives to make these best judgments pertaining to life. And that`s what Arkansas did under these circumstances. It will continue to be debated, but for right now, that`s the judgment that we`ve made.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re thinking about running for president. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is -- is pushing for a nationwide ban on abortion. If you ran -- run for president; if you got elected president, would you advocate and sign into law a nationwide ban on abortion?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: The answer is, I don`t believe that we ought to go back to saying there ought to be a national law that`s passed. We`ve fought for 50 years to have this return to the states. We`ve won that battle. It`s back to the states. Let`s let it be resolved there. I think there`s also a constitutional issue as to whether the federal government has that authority to begin with, under the commerce clause.\par \par \uc2 But I also would add that it`s important that, in the Republican Party, we`re a broad-based party and we`re going to have people disagree on this issue, and this is healthy for the debate. We`re going to not start excluding people because they might have a different, nuanced view on this. That`s what a robust party does. That`s what the debate will be in the future. By and large, it`s different than the Democrat Party that says abortion without any limits.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:01]\par \par \uc2 We say there ought to be limits. We ought to work to protect life. But there`s a lot of debate within that, and that will continue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Asa Hutchinson, Republican from Arkansas, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective on this issue, sir.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Great to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now, Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  of New York.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 Look, before I get to my first question, I want to get your reaction to the governor of Arkansas, especially specifically to this issue of it is -- that he is comfortable with the government essentially forcing a woman to carry out her pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Well, you know, I think, naturally, I couldn`t disagree more with Governor Hutchinson. But I think what`s really important is honing in on this idea that the governor and the Republican Party and frankly the Republican legislature in Arkansas cares about the life of a woman and the life of a mother.\par \par \uc2 The state of Arkansas and Governor Hutchinson governs -- governs over a state that has the third highest maternal mortality in the United States. Seventy-one percent of the women who die are black women as well. This is a state that has 26 percent child poverty, where one in four children are living in extreme -- are living in poverty in the state of Arkansas. And forcing women to carry pregnancies against their will, will kill them. It will kill them, especially in the state of Arkansas, where there is very little to no support for life after birth, in terms of health care, in terms of child care, and in terms of combating poverty.\par \par \uc2 This decision and this policy will kill people, no matter what -- what their spin and what their talking points are. And that`s what the data shows and that`s what the statistics show.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman, I know most of what may change things is going to have to be done at a ballot box. But what would you like to see the Biden administration do between now and November?\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: I think what we need to do is show the American people that, when they give the Democratic Party power and when they actually do vote for us, that we will be using and we are willing to use the power that they do give us in order to merit increased expansions in our majority.\par \par \uc2 So in terms of what I think the presidential -- the president`s administration should be able to do, we have many ideas. We have some ideas coming from Senator Warren, signed a letter along with 25 other Democratic senators asking President Biden to explore opening health care clinics on federal lands in red states in order to help people access the health care and abortion services that they need.\par \par \uc2 We also must, and President Biden did indicate that he was going to start looking into expanding abortion access via the Pill, as well as educational efforts henceforth. But also, what I believe that the president and the Democratic Party needs to come to terms with is that this is not just a crisis of Roe; this is a crisis of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has dramatically overreached its authority. We had two conservative senators in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , Senator Manchin and Senator Collins...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: ... come out with a very explosive allegation that these -- that several Supreme Court justices misled them in -- during their confirmation hearings and in the lead-up to the confirmation. This is a crisis of legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 We have a Supreme Court justice whose wife participated in January 6th and who used his seat to vote against providing documents that potentially led to evidence of such to -- to investigators in congress. This is a crisis of legitimacy. And President Biden must address that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: it`s interesting you say that. I want to put up the \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  quote in particular, on his reaction. Because he -- he used the word -- he said, "They testified under oath."\par \par \uc2 He made sure that was in his release. It sounds like you believe, OK, that -- he might as well be saying they lied to him, that he feels like they lied to him and they lied...\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: They lied.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... under oath. You think the House Judiciary Committee should begin the process of an investigation there?\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: If we allow Supreme Court nominees to lie under -- under oath and secure lifetime appointments to the highest court of the land and then issue -- issue, without basis -- if you read these opinions, issue without basis, rulings that deeply undermine the human and civil rights of -- of the majority of Americans, we must see that through. There must be consequences for such a deeply destabilizing action and hostile takeover of our democratic institutions.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:06]\par \par \uc2 To allow that to stand is to allow it to happen. And what makes it particularly dangerous is that it sends a blaring signal to all future nominees that they can now lie to duly elected members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in order to secure Supreme Court confirmations and -- and seats on the Supreme Court. This is what we`re saying...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think lying...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think lying in a confirmation hearing is an impeachable offense?\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: I believe so. I believe so. I believe lying under oath is an impeachable offense. I believe that violating federal law in not disclosing income from political organizations, as \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  did years ago, is also potentially an impeachable offense. I believe that -- that not recusing from cases that one clearly has family members involved in, with very deep violations of conflict of interest, are also impeachable offenses. And I believe that this is something that should be very seriously considered, including by -- by senators like \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you a slightly bigger-picture question here. Jason Linkins wrote this in the New Republic, and it seemed to ring true with a lot of folks, under the headline, "The Democrats` Theory of Change: Wait For the Republicans To Screw Up."\par \par \uc2 "For the GOP, change comes after long periods of hard work, steady funding and maintaining enthusiasm and momentum through period of setback. For Democrats, change is reactive, coming only after the GOP`s ambitions have hurt just enough people to make Republican rule untenable." What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I think that that -- it does speak to a generational change within the Democratic Party. You know, I believe that there are -- you know, there is an establishment within the Democratic Party that has a nostalgia for a better time of politics from decades ago. You hear this very often when you hear certain Democrats say that they wished for a strong Republican party.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is that we have a new and very different Republican Party that -- that more people are recognizing but, frankly, the roots of which have already been there, which is a lack of -- of respect for the -- for American institutions, a lack of respect for the law and a deep desire to debase very, very critical underpinnings of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And so I do believe that there has been a weak Democratic strategy in the past, and we cannot continue to use those same play books. Right now, as I say, is that this is overtime for our democracy, and we must be aggressive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , Democrat from New York, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Of course. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, the legal, medical and political fallout from this monumental Supreme Court decision. The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:25:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here, NBC News chief Washington correspondent Andrea Mitchell; NBC News senior Capitol Hill correspondent, Garrett Haake; Wall Street Journal columnist Peggy Noonan; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I want to start with \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and what she put out. "Throwing out a precedent overnight that the country has relied upon for a half a century is not conservative. It is sudden, and a radical jolt to the country that will lead to political chaos, anger and a further loss of confidence in our government."\par \par \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  used judicial restraint, Peggy. I -- I`m curious of what happened to small C conservatism and judicial restraint here?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY NOONAN, WALL STREET JOURNAL COLUMNIST AND FORMER SPEECHWRITER FOR PRESIDENT RONALD REAGAN: Well, another way -- I understand what \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  said, but another way to view this is a long, 50-year struggle that observed all processes and appealed to the public and tried to get support and played the long game and did its very best, has suddenly, it seems, but after 50 years, succeeded in the court. And the end result of that is that a big, huge, dramatic question will not be settled by the court, as it were, as it had been 49 years ago, but will actually go back to the people of the country who, state by state, will decide through their legislators and representatives what their local abortion law will be. I do not see this big decision as a settlement of this question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: But I do think it has put forward a legitimate and to me desirable democratic settlement. And it will be turbulent for the next few years. It just will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kimberly, what was interesting, she talked about the long 50-year campaign on the right to get this done. One thing the campaign never succeeded at doing was changing public opinion.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, SENIOR OPINION WRITER, BOSTON \uc2 GLOBE\uc2 : No, public opinion has (sic) changed here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 STOHR: And it -- and that`s what is remarkable, at how the conservative bent, the fast change of this Supreme Court that was done by design, by pure construction, has gone so far against public opinion. I personally don`t believe that people`s civil rights and constitutional rights should be up to public approval, but the fact that it is going so far against it speaks to that.\par \par \uc2 And my quibble with what Peggy has said here is that it interesting that the court picks and chooses when they take this democratic route. They didn`t do so with the second amendment. They imposed their will on even states that wanted to put better protections there. But on this issue, they`re deciding to. And it`s not -- this goes so far beyond abortion. And abortion is important, and reproductive healthcare is important, because it touches that. But this is getting to the very heart of the 14th amendment, of what protects all the vulnerable people in this country.\par \par \uc2 And what they are saying is, unless you have a long history of having this country respect and protect those rights, then you may be out of luck when we have the cases in the future. And as a black woman in America, that`s terrifying.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Andrea, we`re -- I`ve actually talked to some legal experts, building off of what Kimberly said, that said, eventually, if this gets back to the Supreme Court, it may be under an equal protection standard because of, look at what we`re going to see around the country. You`re not going to -- women are not going to have the same or equal rights depending on the state they live in.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: Well, this is contributing to the balkanization of our country. Our country is now going to be so divided -- just legally divided. Half the states are not going to permit abortion. And that is going to prohibit abortion from those who cannot travel. And immediately, there are people in Arkansas and Mississippi who are going to have to go 1,000 miles. That is absolutely -- to Illinois. I was talking to several people in those states yesterday, as we were anchoring, on you know, on this very subject. And they can`t travel 1,000 miles. They don`t have the money. They don`t have the money for day care. They don`t have the ability to -- you know, to get there for all of the pre-testing that you`re supposed to have and for the follow-up exams.\par \par \uc2 And what is so remarkable is that, when Roe was decided, and I`m, you know, old enough, you know, to have lived as an adult pre-Roe, and I know what the situation was, and, actually, public opinion has not really changed...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: ... over these 49.5 years. What`s happened is that the court has changed. You now have a super-majority, where the chief justice does not even have sway, and has not for the last year and a half or so, because of the super-majority, who is basically ignoring public opinion. And they`re not supposed to be political, but they`re going their own way, a super- majority that can do things, whatever they choose to do.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:04]\par \par \uc2 Roe was decided seven to two in an opinion by Justice Blackmun, a Republican, supported by justice -- Chief Justice Burger, a Republican, during the years of Richard Nixon, and was decided, you know, on a basis of what they interpreted -- now, it has been questioned because they chose privacy and not equal protection.\par \par \uc2 But what you`re now going to see is more of this red/blue state division and on a powerfully emotional issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 Garrett, bring me into Congress. You know, when Roe was decided in `73, the two parties were equally divided on abortion.\par \par \uc2 There were a lot of pro-life Democrats. I mean, and look, a lot of people have noted, one time, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was pro-choice. One time, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was pro-life, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`ve been through this. George H.W. Bush, the Bush family, donors to Planned Parenthood back in the day.\par \par \uc2 Is there a -- if this new Republican majority comes in the House, is there going to be an abortion ban?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Kevin McCarthy certainly left that option on the table, suggesting that he would put forward, you know, or would support a 15-week abortion ban if one came up in the House, as I suspect one probably will, especially if the majority is big enough in the House.\par \par \uc2 I mean, ironically this has been Kyrsten Sinema`s position on not getting rid of the filibuster, that this vote will come up, and that the filibuster may be the only protection that Democrats have left, either, you know, in two years from now or potentially in four years from now under a Republican president, to prevent a national abortion standard. Now, conservatives have made the argument that they want this to be decided at the state level.\par \par \uc2 But I think Democrats, as you just heard from Congresswoman Ocasio-Cortez, you know, read the writing on the wall here. You can say one thing, whether it`s in a confirmation hearing, or what have you. But Republican - everything they have done suggests they want to see this banned at a national level.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, to your point about the parties being Balkanized on this, you`re not winning a Republican primary for a State House seat in Missouri, or Texas, or a state like that unless you believe in, you know, abortion ban with no exceptions. That`s the direction that party`s going, and Democrats have to be prepared for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Peggy, politically, during the era of Roe it was the Democrats that would be infighting over, where do you draw the line? Late- term abortions. Republicans did a great job of wedging. Because they`re -- now, all of a sudden, it`s the Republicans-\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Yeah, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -that are going to have to because some are going to want exceptions. Governor Hutchinson talked about it. But Garrett brought up a point. Is that going to survive primaries?\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Well, this is going to work its way out. I would say everybody here, I think, agrees that when polled, the American people say they`re for Roe. They`re for - for the right of abortion as seen in the Constitution. But the minute you get beyond that, to questions of the cutoff, "Is it 12 weeks or 14?" Then, then support goes down.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: It all becomes more complicated. The fact was, I think, legally Roe and Casey just were sort of incompatible with getting all of those reservations worked out in the law. So I think that is part of what drove it. Look, you know what the Republican Party should do now? It should use this victory, if you see it that way, to change itself and become a party that helps women --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: But that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Change its reputation -\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: But that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: - and become a party that helps women and children, becomes -\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right -\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: -- responsible and supportive.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Why are we here? We are here because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  got together and made a litmus test for overturning Roe for Supreme Court nominees. \uc2 Sandra Day O`Connor\uc2  did not have that litmus test. David Souter didn`t have it. \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  didn`t have it.\par \par \uc2 That is why we are here is because Republicans from the top set the tone.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  did not even get a hearing.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s the good news. I`m going to give you guys more time, but just not right now. It`ll be later in the show.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the story that in any other week would have led our show and dominated the news, the riveting testimony about former President Trump`s schemes to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Adam Schiff joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:57]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Almost lost in all the news about Roe v. Wade was the consequential week in the January 6th hearings. We had two of them. On Tuesday, the congressional panel tied former President Trump to a plot to have states create fake elector slates. And they showed how he pressured state officials to overturn his election loss in any way they could find.\par \par \uc2 Then on Thursday, we heard how Mr. Trump sought to install an ally at the head of the Justice Department, Jeffrey Clark, to falsely claim the election was fraudulent and coerce states to flip their results.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD DONOGHUE\uc2 , FORMER ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: I said, Mr. President, within 24, 48, 72 hours, you could have hundreds and hundreds of resignations of the leadership of your entire Justice Department because of your actions. What`s that going to say about you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The actions of Rosen and Donoghue there basically kept us within two hours of a constitutional crisis.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Adam Schiff, who led Tuesday`s hearing.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schiff, welcome back to "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with what`s coming first. We were going to have two more hearings this coming week. You`ve delayed two weeks, I know. Can you give us a little bit more on what more has come in, and on what specific areas has more information come in, that you feel as if you need more time?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, the next couple hearings will cover the run-up to January 6th, the marshaling of this mob that appeared on the mall that day and attacked the Capitol. And then the final hearing will cover what the president was doing, and more importantly, what he was not doing as we were being attacked.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In those hours on January 6th itself.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Exactly. Basically, the president`s flagrant dereliction of duty while the Capitol was being attacked. And this will give us a little bit more time to get prepared for those two hearings. A lot of work, as you might imagine, goes into each and every one of these hearings, and we keep learning new information all the time that we want to incorporate.\par \par \uc2 So this will give us a little more breathing space to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve spent a couple hours with a documentary filmmaker. You`ve now gotten all of his video information. Does it add, does it add any facts that you did not know?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: You know, it`s hard for me to characterize because we have information from other sources that the public hasn`t seen yet. But it certainly adds to the footage of that day, some new perspectives that we haven`t had, at least captured on film. I don`t want to get too far ahead. Part of what`s made these hearings successful is their combination of what`s already known and things that the public hasn`t known yet. And, most important, we do try to tie it all together and show the public where it all fits its pieces.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, one of the - if there`s a search for a smoking gun here, it is to find evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he lost, but went about this anyway. Have you found specific evidence to know that he knew he lost and that this belief defense is one that is just not going to work?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: I think we continue to see powerful evidence of that. And just this week, and every now and then, you see something that tells you in microcosm what it`s all about. In the last hearing, the president saying to top Justice Department officials, "Just say it was corrupt and leave the rest to me and the Republicans."\par \par \uc2 [09:40:13]\par \par \uc2 That`s an acknowledgment by the president after making all these fruitless claims of fraud and being told, That`s not true. That`s not true. That`s not true.\par \par \uc2 The president didn`t care. What he wanted them to do was just say it was corrupt. And Jeffrey Clark, this unqualified environmental lawyer that he was going to put at the top of the Justice Department, drafted a letter to Georgia falsely claiming that the Justice Department believed there was sufficient fraud to overturn the election and Georgia should call its legislature back into session.\par \par \uc2 The president understood that all the Justice Department leadership thought that was completely bogus. And he was prepared to go along with it, except for the threat of mass resignation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that -- by the way, that meeting, January 3rd, that two hours from the constitutional crisis, is it when that fails, should we then see the pressure campaign on Pence that basically begins 48 hours before January 6th? It intensifies. Does that no-go intensify this? Is that the connective tissue there, the conclusion we should draw?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: I think you see the pressure continue to build, and build, and build. These lines of effort that were failing were never completely walked away from. The pressure on the state legislators continued right up until the sixth. The effort to get these fake electors counted by the vice president, the pressure campaign against the vice president, all of that continued.\par \par \uc2 But on the 6th, the last ditch effort was to get that crowd to stop what was happening in the building. And we see then that January 6th was not a day in isolation. It was the culmination of several efforts, the violent culmination of an effort to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A couple things, this week, boy, the Department of Justice has certainly let the public know it was doing a lot more on January 6th than we`d seen in the past. I think there were subpoenas served on at least nine people in four states. A search warrant was executed on the home of Jeffrey Clark. You`ve been critical in the past of the Justice Department. You felt as if there was maybe, maybe they were being a little passive.\par \par \uc2 Do you still have that criticism or do you look at their actions this week and does that reassure you a little bit?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: It certainly seems that there`s a greater sense of urgency than I`ve seen before. At the same time, I have yet to see any indication that the former president himself is under investigation. And I concur with what Judge Carter out in California said, that there`s sufficient evidence to believe the former president violated multiple federal laws.\par \par \uc2 And you can`t hold anyone immune. You can`t say there`s a different standard, different rule of law for former presidents, particularly when you took the position while they were in office that they couldn`t be indicted.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This country`s, I`d characterize it as dangerously divided right now. And an indictment of a former president, that could throw gasoline on this fire. Choosing not to hold him accountable could also throw gasoline on this fire. What is -- there are no good outcomes here. What`s the worst outcome on that front, and do you understand why the attorney general may be really struggling with this decision?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, I think it`s a very difficult decision, but I don`t think it`s a difficult -- that is, to prosecute. It`s not a difficult decision to investigate when there`s evidence before you. And I think the worst-case scenario is not that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs and wins, but that he runs and loses and they overturn the election, because there`s no deterrent, because there`s no effort to push back and to hold people accountable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What if you prosecute him and he gets off?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, that`s always a risk. But the fact is, if you follow the evidence where it leads, if you believe that you can prove beyond a reasonable doubt, then you have a duty to prosecute. And the decision not to investigate or not to prosecute becomes a political decision that, well, this person is immune from the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Adam Schiff, Democrat from California. So we`ve got a two-week break, and then a couple more hearings. Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, the other, other big story of the week: The Supreme Court`s expansion of gun rights. The ruling was on a New York state law that was over 100 years old. But what happened in New York is not staying there.\par \par \uc2 Back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:20]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, data download time.\par \par \uc2 The abortion decision was not the only news from the Supreme Court this week. On Thursday, the high court struck down a New York law that strictly limited who can carry a gun in public. It was over 100 years old.\par \par \uc2 The 6-3 ruling immediately, though, raised questions about similar laws in other states. The long term, it could have even broader impacts on all sorts of other gun rules nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you. First of all, the law that was impacted was New York state, but it could impact laws on the books that are very similar to New York`s when it comes to who can carry a gun in California, New York, Maryland, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line is it may be only a handful of states, but a lot of people reside in these handful of states, 25 percent of the population impacted by that ruling directly.\par \par \uc2 Now, what was interesting is what was said in the ruling. The justification of this ruling was this: To justify a firearm regulation the government must demonstrate that the regulation is consistent with the Nation`s historical tradition of firearm regulation.\par \par \uc2 It`s a pretty broad new standard that the Supreme Court wants to apply to all sorts of gun laws. So what could that mean? Well, how about high- capacity magazine bans?\par \par \uc2 About 10 states plus the District of Columbia has those. Are those in the tradition of gun regulation that goes back more than 100 years? It`s unlikely the court would find that. Those could be targeted by gun rights organizations to try to get those bans ruled unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Then, there`s red flag laws. In fact, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , the Congress and President Biden just signed into law a new bill that would actually encourage more states to have red flag laws. But that doesn`t mean that gun rights organizations think they are constitutional. And they may go back to the court to see if that standard could be used to rule these 19 states and some others unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, a lot of gun laws under a lot of jurisdictions could now be vulnerable with this new standard that the Supreme Court has come up with.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, why former President Trump thinks the abortion decision may be bad politics for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back.\par \par \uc2 One person whose legacy is going to get defined by the overturn of Roe v. Wade is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, Andrea Mitchell, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t seem to be very excited about this. He`s, you know, he called it a victory for life at his rally in Illinois yesterday, but he is mumbling and grumbling all over the place about this. And he believes this is going to hurt Republicans in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: No, absolutely. Look, he -- when he ran, he`s a fake evangelical and he`s a fake supporter of banning abortion. And all of this was antithetical to him, and it was politically important in Iowa and other places as he was running.\par \par \uc2 So this is not his core beliefs because he is not ideological. He`s just pragmatic. And he can see the handwriting on this is that that while the economy is the biggest issue and will continue to be the biggest issue, and inflation, driving the midterm elections, it is going to be abortion rising as a big issue.\par \par \uc2 And it`s especially going to be important in trigger states, in states with a total ban, in Michigan, Wisconsin, where governors could change hands, the people who could veto Republican legislatures.\par \par \uc2 One thing that has been overlooked is how in 2010, this really emerged, and Republican activists, not Republicans generically, but activists who really wanted to ban abortion, seized on this with the Tea Party movement in 2010 and it was a census year, so they grabbed state legislatures -- about 10 state legislatures changed hands from Democratic to Republican. And it is at the state level, as Asa Hutchinson says.\par \par \uc2 But at the state level, this became baked in with redistricting. And more and more states now have Republican legislatures who will ban or restrict abortions if governors or state officials are not there to veto it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: This has always been a challenge for Democrats to keep their voters focused on state legislatures, state houses, governors` races, AGs` races, you know, having \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  for eight years is one thing. But if all of these state legislatures are flipping, you`re in a world of hurt.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t see this issue playing as much on the federal level, maybe at the margins in some of these Senate and House races. But again, if you`re running for governor, if you look at the governor`s race in Pennsylvania, could be ground zero. Some of these state House races in places like Michigan, that could go either way. I`m curious if it has an effect in Texas at all.\par \par \uc2 This doesn`t play. A total abortion ban doesn`t really play in Plano or The Woodlands. But is that enough to help the Democrat running for governor in Texas?\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: But the problem for Democrats here, is as we`ve seen with Republicans, this is not something you can flip overnight.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: This is a decade -- generation-long movement on the right that has led us here. And the Democrats, it`s not clear to see if they`ve even started what will have to be a generation-long movement to move this backwards. Not just with the Supreme Court, but at the state level. As you are saying, I think it`s going to take horrific details about how women are dying because of complications in a pregnancy and other terrible outcomes for this, before the public really understands how important this is.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I actually wonder if there`s a blueprint in what we just saw with the guns legislation, where it was a decade in that case, but it took Sandy Hook and the absolute horror of that for Democrats to get electorally organized around the issue of guns. They spent a decade doing it. They were able to get a big breakthrough this week, whether this ruling is enough to start a process --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: They don`t have decade. They`ve got a presidential election and a midterm. And they`re going to lose control, potentially, and won`t be able to do anything. There`s no way that they can do anything in the Senate unless they get rid of the filibuster rule.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No, not happening.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Can I note?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: I think something that is important for liberals now, realize this: a big decision came down. For 50 years, the courts had put it one way. Liberals will now have to get in the game in a whole new way democratically. And that is through persuasion, not anger, rage, all of that stuff.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But persuasion didn`t --\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Persuasion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The right didn`t need persuasion.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Oh, the right I think talked about this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But they never got it. But they never -- I don`t mean -- the persuasion argument never worked.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: I think you are wrong. No, no. People said consistently, yes, Roe. Abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But the minute you got past the fourth, fifth, and sixth week, that`s where the persuasion was.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure. But do you think, though, that they --\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: But that`s where they did, I think, change minds.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess, Peggy, what I just don`t understand and what, it feels like why a boomerang is coming, it`s, the life side was winning incrementally. And now it`s like they`ve rushed to crash the car. And now, you know, we don`t like when anything is rushed and overdone.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Well, I don`t think it was rushed, but it sure feels sudden. Do you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Abrupt.\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: Yes, abrupt. I experienced that so much on the day of Dobbs, so many emails and texts came in from people, normal humans who said, "I had no idea this was happening."\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: But Peggy --\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: And they were just shocked. So there was an abruptness.\par \par \uc2 But I think persuasion was part of this whole thing, you know? You had to persuade a lot of people to make a movement triumph.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: But you cannot just--\par \par \uc2 NOONAN: It wasn`t just raw muscle. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: You can`t have persuasion -- no, I`m sorry. That was bad on me. You can`t have persuasion when you have a young Supreme Court. They are going to be here for decades. This is a super majority that is not going anywhere anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 And it was done because of, as I say, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  not getting a hearing, and then three Trump justices who misled the Senate. And so whether you like that or not, it can`t be done --\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: --at the Senate level.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That is all we have for today. This conversation will keep going, but I have to turn the cameras off.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220626ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Growing List Of States Where Abortions Are No Longer Legal\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE Many have already made the procedure illegal. Here`s Blayne Alexander with what could come next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): At the Women`s Center in Huntsville, Alabama, today is a dark day.\par \par \uc2 DALTON JOHNSON, ALABAMA WOMEN`S CENTER DIRECTOR: The women -- the emotions that they were going through, it was just -- it went down to my soul.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Director Dalton Johnson says the clinic usually sees about 30 patients a day, women crossing state lines for services. But the Supreme Court decision marking the end of Roe v. Wade spelled the immediate end of abortion access in Alabama, a 2019 law passed in the state now allowed to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): You have several dozen patients already in the waiting room. When you broke the news, what was it like in that room?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It was just raw emotions. It was everything from people that were upset, angry. People breaking down crying, people in disbelief.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Following the Supreme Court decision Friday, abortion is no longer legal in these nine states. And in at least three others, providers have stopped services for fear of prosecution with the laws still uncertain.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a win for unborn children.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Arkansas, doctors who perform the procedure could now face up to 10 years in prison. In neighboring Louisiana, all three abortion clinics in the state have ceased operations. Angie Thomas (ph) had been working toward that moment for years.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS (ph): We are so thankful for this day in history where we as a state get to protect our unborn children and help moms.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Across the country, more changes are on the way. Georgia`s Attorney General has asked the court to allow the state`s so-called heartbeat law to take effect. It would reduce abortion access from 20 weeks of pregnancy to six.\par \par \uc2 Back in Mississippi, more protests today outside the clinic at the heart of the Supreme Court case now set to close within days.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Getty Israel worries what that will mean for black women.\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL, FOUNDER OF SISTERS IN BIRTH: The highest proportion of black people in the country are here this is ground zero for black health.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: While Black Women comprise 38% of Mississippi`s population, they account for 74% of the state`s abortions.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What did those numbers tell you?\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL: I say abortion as another birth, was priority.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Israel runs Sisters in Birth Clinic where she provides resources to pregnant women and says the state`s high poverty rate and insufficient access to health care mean black women will be hit hardest by the court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL: It`s easy to end abortion. That`s just a law. It takes effort and leadership and resources to really improve health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Blayne joins me live from Jackson, Mississippi. Blayne, what options will it be for women in states like Mississippi as access to abortion goes away?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, Jose just moments ago the Secretary of Health and Human Services \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  told NBC`s Kate Snow at the Aspen Ideas Health conference that his department is looking into providing transportation services for women who are seeking to cross state lines for that procedure. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Blayne Alexander at Jackson, Mississippi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220630ei6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The World`s Ugliest Dog\b0\par\par\uc2 Morgan Radford, Peter Alexander, Joe Fryer, Kerry Sanders \par \uc2 2,564 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:47:07]\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD: We are back on a Saturday morning and "NBC NEWS NOW" anchor Joe fryer is joining us and I do hope that you guys are as excited as I am about this one because we are about to find out who was crowned the World`s Ugliest Dog. Do you know that was even a thing?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER: Yes, lots of suspense. And here, this might be one of the best assignments out there. And there was only one NBC person who was made for this job.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yeah, that`s right. That`s Kerry Sanders.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: There he is.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: -- California where they crowned this year`s World`s Ugliest Dog. Kerry is not only our correspondent on the story, he was also a judge because Kerry, like you`re an expert of this? I don`t know. Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good morning. Look, guys, you might say, wow, a contest were ugly is cute. But in this case, I think it`s more appropriate to say bow wow.\par \par \uc2 And yes, we are here at the fairgrounds in Sonoma County and Marin County where they hold the contest for the World`s Ugliest Dog and we do have a winner. Number one U-G-L-Y in the world.\par \par \uc2 But before we meet the winner, as they say, let`s meet the contestants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice over): America has gone to the dogs and it`s just a distraction we need.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Booster Kong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is Monkey.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And monkey in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A blue factored Brussels Griffon.\par \par \uc2 This has more readout and I`m going to say her breeding one time because I`m not good at it. She`s a Xoloitzcuintli.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Somewhere inside this hairball, there`s a Pekinese named Wild Thing.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know whether you`re a Star Trek fan, but I`m thinking the trouble with Tribbles.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Into the machinery all right, and then, probably, and all the other food processors too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He`s Tribble.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: He`s Tribble.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sometimes he`s just trouble.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Mr. Happy Face is a rescue adopted by his owner in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is her first competition and so he is -- he gets a little overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Is it all the paparazzi around?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is (INAUDIBLE), it is the paparazzi. You totally got it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: In all, eight dogs competing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is Jenny the Korean pug from Korea.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Some from as far away as Asia, vying for the crown of ugliest.\par \par \uc2 Among the judges, me.\par \par \uc2 We need for our contestants some applause. COVID put a two year break on the ugliest dog in the world competition but like a dog with a bone, the county fair just wouldn`t be the fair without this event.\par \par \uc2 The crowd and the judges, like a pack of dogs in agreement.\par \par \uc2 The ugliest dog in the world Mr. Happy Face.\par \par \uc2 What`s Mr. Happy Face is thinking right now?\par \par \uc2 [07:50:07]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He`s wondering if there are any treats that are part of this.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And your answer to that one is?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is of course.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): Mr. Happy Face, the ugliest dog in the world. Not just the face a mother could love. Joe, Morgan, Peter, I think it`s appropriate for me to say woof.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: That`s Mr. Happy Face, I just want to see like a sad face on that dog, right? Like that was a joyful Mr. Happy Face right there. Sort of lacking the celebration.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Yes, that was -- I have no words. That truly is a face that I guess we all love now.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: -- selfie with the dog.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right, Kerry.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That was great. Look, we`ll be right back just after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Still to come right here, the NASCAR drivers feeling the need for speed as another exciting race gets going this weekend.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That`s right. We`re going to talk to Dale Earnhardt Jr. He`ll join us live in just a moment. We`ll tell you what to expect. First, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice-over): Good morning. To the streets. The Roe vs. Wade reversal setting off a flood of emotions nationwide, many furious.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Others ecstatic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was in the middle of praying when we found out the decision was made. And I am -- I`m overjoyed at out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Protesters in Los Angeles shutting down the freeway while others marched outside the homes of the justices who ruled in favor of overturning Roe. The decision deepening the political divide in America with Democratic lawmakers saying the only way to change it back is to vote this November.\par \par \uc2 We`re live with the latest and new questions about what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Celebrity sound off. Music and Hollywood legends taking the social media reacting to that historic abortion ruling, from Taylor Swift to Pearl Jam, Lizzo even pledging half a million dollars for Planned Parenthood and the fight for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 And start your engines. High Flying speeds are coming to Nashville this weekend with a return of NASCAR`s \uc2 Ally\uc2  400. Who will go home with a coveted win? Will speak live and NASCAR legend Dale Earnhardt Jr.\par \par \uc2 Today Saturday, June 25, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): We`re the (INAUDIBLE) from Virginia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we just celebrated our 36th wedding anniversary.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At the "Today Show."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE) and Johnny. Greenville, PA. Surviving 75 years of marriage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I think (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): Happy 100th birthday mom, grandma great grandma.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Happy (INAUDIBLE) hon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): We love you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Happy century mark, happy 75th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: A lot to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Love that.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, love that. Congratulations to all of them.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Welcome back to "Today" on the Saturday morning. My friend Morgan Radford is here with us in our D.C. studios. Kristen is traveling overseas as you can see, she is with Julie Andrews in the Austrian Alps right now just across the border from Germany. What a beautiful spot there ahead of the President`s trip to Europe for a high stakes G7 summit.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, we`re not going to make this thing this morning. But we will check in with you for some news in just a few moments from now.\par \par \uc2 And that is where we start on what is a serious news day. The swift and emotional reaction to the Supreme Court`s ruling to overturn Roe vs. Wade taking away the constitutional right to an abortion. Overnight, crowds of people on both sides taken to the streets from L.A. to New York, Miami and elsewhere. The impact has been immediate six states with trigger laws now banning abortions on Friday. We have team coverage this morning.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin with NBC`s Jesse Kirsch. He`s in New York City this morning. Jesse, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Peter, good morning after that draft opinion leaked in recent weeks, this was no surprise, but that did not stop people from turning out by the thousands to have their opinions heard both in support of and against this Supreme Court decision. In New York, for example, we saw people blocking traffic for hours. Groups also rallied right outside of a fenced in Supreme Court at times. There were moments of tension with people who were supporting this 4-5 decision by the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Celebrations for the overturning of Roe also occurred in other cities across the country. Meanwhile, other protesters took their voices directly to the homes of some of the Supreme Court justices who voted to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line now, the question is, does this momentum carry through to the fall to the midterm elections? And we`ve talked with people on both sides of this issue who tell us that they believe there is something to vote for this fall.\par \par \uc2 Peter and Morgan back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right. Jesse Kirsch on the ground in New York City with more protests expected this weekend. Jesse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Let`s go and bring in NBC`s justice correspondent Pete Williams now with more on this historic ruling. Pete, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Good morning to you Morgan. It is a momentous decision for a couple of reasons it overturns five decades of abortion precedence that millions of American women had depended on. And this is also the first time the Supreme Court ever took away a widely recognized constitutional right. The court voted five to four to overturn Roe and the follow on rulings that said states can`t ban abortion before the age of fetal viability. The Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said going that far wasn`t necessary to uphold Mississippi`s law that banned abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The vote on that was six to three.\par \par \uc2 Writing for the majority Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  said, no right to abortion is protected by any constitutional provision. He called the Roe decision egregiously wrong and deeply damaging.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:06]\par \par \uc2 So now that abortion is no longer a constitutional right, it`s likely to become illegal soon in about half the country. And half a dozen states, it`s already illegal as of this morning. In a joint dissent justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan said the court ruling means that from the very moment of fertilization and woman has no rights to speak of, they said a state can force her to bring a pregnancy to term even at the steepest personal and family cost.\par \par \uc2 Justices Alito and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  had been calling for the Court to overturn Roe for years, now they finally have enough likeminded colleagues with the Supreme Court`s new conservative supermajority to make it happen. Morgan, Peter.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right. NBC`s Pete Williams for us here in D.C. Peter, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And President Biden is condemning the court`s decision while also calling on Americans to make their voices heard at the polls this fall.\par \par \uc2 Kristen is in the alps near the German border this morning. She is there ahead of the President`s high stakes \uc2 G7\uc2  summit with allies. Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hi, Peter. Good morning to you. President Biden is about to leave for Germany for the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit here. But this trip will undoubtedly be overshadowed by the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. In his remarks on Friday, the President making it very clear he`s gearing up for the battle ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Sad day for the court and for the country. You can act, you can have the final word. This is not over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And, Peter, as you pointed out, the President is already making this an issue in the midterms calling on voters to elect candidates who support abortion rights and urging Congress to make abortion legal, something Democrats don`t currently have the votes for.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President also vowed to use his executive authority to protect women`s reproductive rights, but he also acknowledged his powers are quite limited. For Republicans the ruling is a victory in a decade`s long battle. Former President Trump who appointed the three conservative justices who made the ruling possible called it quote, the biggest win for life in a generation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, before President Biden leaves Washington, he will sign the most consequential gun legislation in decades. Fourteen Republican lawmakers join Democrats to pass the bill into law. Now the gun bill passed just hours after the court`s decision on Roe and a day after the court weakened gun laws in some states. This new law will include enhanced background checks, incentivizing states to put red flag laws in place as well as closing the so-called boyfriend loophole for domestic abusers.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of things the President is tracking, and we are as well ahead of this diplomatic Summit. Peter and Morgan.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, just a whirlwind 24 hours, the President was hoping to tout that progress on gun legislation and then the Roe ruling came in. Really just a remarkable day here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, a full plate ahead for you. We`ll see you shortly. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And switching gears now it is time for a "Weekend Morning Boost." Joe is back with that. Joe, what do you have?\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hey there. So I don`t know about you, but military homecoming videos always give me the chills and I`ve got one for you this morning. That is certainly no different.\par \par \uc2 Second grader Nikole Carvahal and her classmates thought they were gathering to meet one of their favorite authors on their last day of school. But her mom Army Sergeant Lilibeth Contreras was actually gearing up to surprise her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE CARVAHAL: It was very surprising, and I spoke up, I miss her so much.\par \par \uc2 SGT. LILIBETH CONTRERAS: It`s been a rough time not being able to spend as much time with her. She`s my reason for everything. She my reason to push forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Oh, the two had been apart for the last 14 months while Contreras was stationed in South Korea using video calls to stay in touch, mom`s going to be home for about a month before deploying again. They`re using their time wisely by heading to \uc2 Disney\uc2  next week. And she clutches that army bear anytime they have those video phone calls when they talk to each other, which means so much her use are clutching that little teddy bear right there.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That is a tearjerker. A beautiful reunion. Joe, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We thank mom for her service and daughter for her service too, right it`s a family affair being a part of the military community.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, right here we are feeling the need for speed as we talk live to racing legend Dale Earnhardt Jr. How about that? We`ll be talking about NASCAR`s next big race in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: We`ll be right back just after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6p0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Roe Versus Wade Reversals Setting Off A Flood Of Emotions Nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Jesse Kirsch \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:20]\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS HOST (voice over): Good morning. Taking to the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The Roe versus Wade reversals setting off a flood of emotions nationwide. Many angry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Others ecstatic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was in the middle of praying when we found out the decision was made. And I am -- I`m overjoyed now.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Protesters in Los Angeles shutting down a freeway while some people marched outside the homes of the justices who ruled in favor of overturning Roe. The decision deepening the political divide in America with Democratic lawmakers say the only way to change it back is to vote this November.\par \par \uc2 This morning new questions about what happens next, what it means for women, what the future of abortion rights look like. And could contraception and gay rights be next? We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Historic gun bill. President Biden signing the most significant gun legislation in decades in the law this morning. The bipartisan bill passed by the House on Friday strengthens background checks, closes the so-called boyfriend loophole and incentivizes states to put red flag laws in place. Can it make a real world difference?\par \par \uc2 Travel nightmare. Chaotic scenes at airports nationwide only expected to get worse ahead of next week`s busy Fourth of July weekend. With airlines blaming the \uc2 FAA\uc2  for massive staffing shortages, will air travelers get any summer relief? All that. Plus, it`s cute to be ugly. Sorry, Westminster. There`s another dog show in town. But this one doesn`t crown the top dog. It awards the least attractive pooch around.\par \par \uc2 We`ll celebrate the ugliest of them all. Today, Saturday, June 25th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to today and thank you for joining us on this Saturday morning. There is a look at the Supreme Court. I`m here with Morgan Radford while Kristen is along Austria`s stunningly picturesque border with Germany ahead of President Biden`s trip to Europe for a high stakes G7 Summit. Kristen will be checking in with you shortly. But we want to get right to our top story right now.\par \par \uc2 That is the reaction to the Supreme Court`s historic ruling overturning Roe versus Wade taking away the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEW YORK-BASED CORRESPONDENT: Thousands of people took to the streets just overnight from New York, Los Angeles, Miami and more. Many expressing sadness while others say they are overjoyed by this ruling. The impact of that ruling was immediate. Six states have already banned abortions with more following soon due to so-called trigger laws. Now eventually half of the states are expected to ban abortions.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Our team has found out this morning covering the ruling, its impact and the implications for the country`s future. We want to begin with Jesse Kirsch. She is in New York with the very latest on those overnight protests. Jesse, Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS WEEKEND CORRESPONDENT: Peter, good morning. This was one of the places where thousands of people rallied across the country here in New York and in cities all over the U.S. both decrying and celebrating this historic decision. And while it was expected after that leaked draft opinion, emotions are still running high.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice over): From just beyond the Supreme Court steps to cities, including New York, Denver in Miami, the Roe v. Wade reversal sending thousands of Americans into the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal. It feels like my country doesn`t love me and appreciate my body as a woman.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: And Washington crowd swelled along a fence in court. Meanwhile in L.A., protesters took over the 101 Hollywood Freeway. Agony for some jubilation for others. With the five-four decision overturning nearly 50 years of precedent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was in the middle of praying when we found out the decision was made. And I am -- I`m overjoyed now.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: New York, protesters blocking traffic for hours holding rallies too.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Supreme Court has (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My daughter`s lost rights to deal with her own body as she ages and that terrified for her future.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Other celebrating like in Detroit and St. Petersburg, Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Means that the floodgates of the killing of innocent human life is over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m frankly stunned that it happened. I wasn`t sure whatever happened in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Overnight, protesters also outside homes have some Supreme Court justices who voted to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Some believe the energy can last through the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s talk of codifying Roe v. Wade in Congress we need to elect people who will not do that, who will respect life.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:08]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think this is a fire that`s going to set the set across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Demonstrations have been largely peaceful. However, yesterday, according to an official familiar with the matter, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  warned that political violence is likely to intensify in this country, specifically against judges and state officials in the wake of this decision, clearly a tense time and the United States. Morgan and Peter, back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden among those calling for the protest to be peaceful. Jesse Kirsch in New York City this morning. Jesse, thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220630ei6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 TODAY SHOW for June 25, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Morgan Radford, Jesse Kirsch, Pete Williams,  Kristen Welker, Ali Vitali, Joe Fryer, Blayne Alexander, Bill Karins, Clay  Anderson, Guad Venegas, Kelly Sanders \par \uc2 6,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:20]\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS HOST (voice over): Good morning. Taking to the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The Roe versus Wade reversals setting off a flood of emotions nationwide. Many angry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Others ecstatic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was in the middle of praying when we found out the decision was made. And I am -- I`m overjoyed now.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Protesters in Los Angeles shutting down a freeway while some people marched outside the homes of the justices who ruled in favor of overturning Roe. The decision deepening the political divide in America with Democratic lawmakers say the only way to change it back is to vote this November.\par \par \uc2 This morning new questions about what happens next, what it means for women, what the future of abortion rights look like. And could contraception and gay rights be next? We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Historic gun bill. President Biden signing the most significant gun legislation in decades in the law this morning. The bipartisan bill passed by the House on Friday strengthens background checks, closes the so-called boyfriend loophole and incentivizes states to put red flag laws in place. Can it make a real world difference?\par \par \uc2 Travel nightmare. Chaotic scenes at airports nationwide only expected to get worse ahead of next week`s busy Fourth of July weekend. With airlines blaming the \uc2 FAA\uc2  for massive staffing shortages, will air travelers get any summer relief? All that. Plus, it`s cute to be ugly. Sorry, Westminster. There`s another dog show in town. But this one doesn`t crown the top dog. It awards the least attractive pooch around.\par \par \uc2 We`ll celebrate the ugliest of them all. Today, Saturday, June 25th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to today and thank you for joining us on this Saturday morning. There is a look at the Supreme Court. I`m here with Morgan Radford while Kristen is along Austria`s stunningly picturesque border with Germany ahead of President Biden`s trip to Europe for a high stakes G7 Summit. Kristen will be checking in with you shortly. But we want to get right to our top story right now.\par \par \uc2 That is the reaction to the Supreme Court`s historic ruling overturning Roe versus Wade taking away the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEW YORK-BASED CORRESPONDENT: Thousands of people took to the streets just overnight from New York, Los Angeles, Miami and more. Many expressing sadness while others say they are overjoyed by this ruling. The impact of that ruling was immediate. Six states have already banned abortions with more following soon due to so-called trigger laws. Now eventually half of the states are expected to ban abortions.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Our team has found out this morning covering the ruling, its impact and the implications for the country`s future. We want to begin with Jesse Kirsch. She is in New York with the very latest on those overnight protests. Jesse, Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS WEEKEND CORRESPONDENT: Peter, good morning. This was one of the places where thousands of people rallied across the country here in New York and in cities all over the U.S. both decrying and celebrating this historic decision. And while it was expected after that leaked draft opinion, emotions are still running high.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice over): From just beyond the Supreme Court steps to cities, including New York, Denver in Miami, the Roe v. Wade reversal sending thousands of Americans into the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal. It feels like my country doesn`t love me and appreciate my body as a woman.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: And Washington crowd swelled along a fence in court. Meanwhile in L.A., protesters took over the 101 Hollywood Freeway. Agony for some jubilation for others. With the five-four decision overturning nearly 50 years of precedent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was in the middle of praying when we found out the decision was made. And I am -- I`m overjoyed now.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: New York, protesters blocking traffic for hours holding rallies too.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Supreme Court has (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My daughter`s lost rights to deal with her own body as she ages and that terrified for her future.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Other celebrating like in Detroit and St. Petersburg, Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Means that the floodgates of the killing of innocent human life is over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m frankly stunned that it happened. I wasn`t sure whatever happened in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Overnight, protesters also outside homes have some Supreme Court justices who voted to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Some believe the energy can last through the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s talk of codifying Roe v. Wade in Congress we need to elect people who will not do that, who will respect life.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:08]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think this is a fire that`s going to set the set across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Demonstrations have been largely peaceful. However, yesterday, according to an official familiar with the matter, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  warned that political violence is likely to intensify in this country, specifically against judges and state officials in the wake of this decision, clearly a tense time and the United States. Morgan and Peter, back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden among those calling for the protest to be peaceful. Jesse Kirsch in New York City this morning. Jesse, thanks.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And as of this morning, some states have already banned abortions while others have plans to do so very soon. NBC`s justice correspondent Pete Williams is here with more on the Supreme Court`s historic ruling. Hey there, Pete, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Historic is the right word, I think, Morgan. And it`s historic in many ways, it not only overturns five decades of abortion precedent. It`s also the first time the Supreme Court ever recognized a constitutional right that was widely accepted and then took it away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (voice over): By a vote of five to four the court overturn Roe and the follow on rulings that said states cannot ban abortion before the age of fetal viability. Three of those in the majority were appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who said he would put "pro-life justices on the court." Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said going that far wasn`t necessary to uphold Mississippi`s law banning abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The vote was six to three to do that. The court`s opinion overturning Roe track closely with the draft that leaked in May touching off nationwide protests, including in front of the homes of some of the court`s conservatives, writing for the majority Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  said no right to abortion is protected by any constitutional provision. The Roe decision he said was egregiously wrong and deeply damaging.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE: When I saw the final decision and the courts -- the justices had stuck with that decision. It was the -- I got to say dream come true, but this is what we`ve been working for for so long.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Unlike other court rulings based on a right to privacy Alito said abortion is different because it involves a potential life. So he said the ruling does not undermine the rights to same sex marriage or contraception. Though Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said the court should take another look at those two. Now that abortion is no longer a constitutional right. It`s likely to become illegal soon and about half the nation in half a dozen states it`s already illegal as of this morning.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in a controlling concurrence said states that ban abortion cannot make it a crime for their residents to travel to a state where abortion is legal. In a joint dissent, Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan said the court ruling means that from the very moment of fertilization, a woman has no rights to speak up. They said a state can always force a woman to give birth and transform what when freely undertaken is a wonder into what when forced may be a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It basically says that women`s ability to have autonomy over their bodies and ability to plan for their futures is disregarded. Has no constitutional weight whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Justices Thomas and Alito had been calling for the Court to overturn Roe for years. Now they finally have enough like-minded conservative colleagues with the Supreme Court`s new conservative supermajority to make it happen. Morgan? Peter?\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right. Pete Williams for us there. Pete, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And President Biden is condemning the court`s decision while also calling on Americans to voice their -- have their voices heard at the polls this November. He says this is not over. Kristen is along Austria`s border with Germany this morning ahead of the president`s trip there for the G7. Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Peter, good morning to you from the beautiful Alps. President Biden will be heading to Germany this morning for critical diplomatic talks about the Supreme Court`s landmark decision to overturn Roe v. Wade will undoubtedly loom large over this trip and of course the political landscape at home with the midterms just months away.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, this is a victory after a decade`s long battle. The president though accusing the court of leading the country down an extreme and dangerous path.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice over): This morning, President Biden gearing up for the fight ahead in the wake of the Supreme Court ruling to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s a sad day for the court and for the country. You can act. You can have the final word. This is not over.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The president calling out his predecessor by name for appointing the three conservative justices who made this day possible.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Three justices named by one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are the core of today`s decision to upend the scales of justice and eliminate a fundamental right for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And the president vowed to use as executive authority to protect women who have to travel to states where abortion remains legal and to ensure access to pills that can end unwanted pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:09]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But he acknowledged his powers are limited, calling on Americans to elect lawmakers who support abortion rights and urging Congress to codify abortion access into law. For his part, former President Trump took a victory lap around the decision, calling it the biggest win for life in a generation. According to a May NBC News poll, 63 percent of Americans do not think Roe should be overturned.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill, many Republicans cheered the news.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (D-NY): Today`s historic Supreme Court decision is a victory for the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Today`s decision is a vindication for the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: While some Democrats warned of dire consequences, including Congresswoman Barbara Lee who had an abortion before Roe. Lee said she crossed the border into Mexico and had the procedure in a back alley.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE (D-CA): First of all, I was terrified that I might die. But secondly, I was terrified because I knew it was illegal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So the big question now, what will the impact be on the midterms? The makeup of Congress now takes on a whole new meaning with both sides trying to cement their position on abortion at the federal level. Democrats are making this a key issue in the midterms already. Fundraising off of it, organizing around it, and Republicans are aiming to do the same arguing their new mission is to make sure abortion remains banned.\par \par \uc2 But talking to my sources on both sides of the aisle, there is broad concern this moment will only hardened battle lines in this already deeply divided country. And Peter, you and I have covered so many of these diplomatic Summits. This decision to overturn Roe has really just created a remarkable backdrop to this one.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. The president expected to arrive there later this afternoon. Kristen, thank you so much. We`ll check in with you again in just a little bit. And now Senator Amy Klobuchar of Minnesota is on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  and she is joining us. Senator Klobuchar, it`s nice to see you this morning. Let me get right to it, right in the court`s majority opinion. You saw that Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  says that the Roe decision was egregiously wrong and deeply damaging, insisting that it should be up to the states to decide. So, on such a divisive issue, why isn`t that a reasonable approach?\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Peter, I don`t think that a woman in Minnesota should be treated differently than a woman in Texas. These are basic women`s rights that have been in place for 50 years. And I think we should trust women to make their own decisions about their health care, instead of thinking that politicians or the government should step in. And that`s why you`re seeing this outpouring, women across the country, not just Democrats, moderate Republicans, Independents stepping in and saying enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 These justices who`ve been appointed by conservatives, who -- this was a long plan for a long time, they are out of step with the American people. And the only way you can peacefully respond to this is at the ballot box. And that`s where this is all about November.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Senator, you say this has been a long plan for a long time. You sit on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  and we know that the abortion question is almost always a litmus test for potential justices. But now based on how the current justices on the bench answered those questions historically, many Americans feel like they simply cannot trust the answers given at those hearings. So, how do they regain that trust, Senator?\par \par \uc2 What do you do in Congress to make these hearings have consequence?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: You know, we have three branches of government for a reason, Morgan. And like everyone else, of course, I oppose these justices. I thought that this was something like this was in the works. And I`m very, very disappointed in how they answered those questions. But to me, the answer is not just stopping, hearing, stopping asking questions. To me, the answer is how do you change this?\par \par \uc2 And you change this with the people. As you just pointed out, in your poll, the majority of the people are with us on this. And it`s not a drill anymore. It`s real. Republican governors are racing to the state capitals to try to pass laws now, to ban abortion. Over half the states appear as though they will ban abortion. And basically, the answer is, it`s not just the federal level, it`s the governor`s races, it`s the legislature races.\par \par \uc2 If people care about their fundamental rights, and they don`t want to go back not just to the 1950s but to the 1850s that they want a world where their kids like my daughter has as many rights as I had or her grandma had, then the answer is action. The answer is voting, because we can change this at both the local and the federal levels. But it is all about if people are willing to stand up and not just let it happen to them.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Senator Amy Klobuchar, we appreciate you making time to speak to us this morning. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: It`s great to be on. Thanks to both of you.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:07]\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Let`s turn now to Jennifer Mascott. A former law clerk f or Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . She also clerked for \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  when he was an appeals court judge. And she`s now an assistant professor of law at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2 . Professor Mascott, thank you so much for joining us this morning. First up, look, the majority opinion decided that abortion was not explicitly protected in the constitution and that it isn`t a "deeply rooted tradition."\par \par \uc2 But there are a lot of not just traditions, but also rights that Americans have become accustomed to. So, after nearly 50 years of precedent, how is abortion and reproductive autonomy not considered a deeply rooted tradition here in America?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MASCOTT, FORMER CLERK FOR \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2  AND \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 : Well, as you`ve noted, yesterday, was a very significant day at the core. But I think it`s important to remember to the context that for the first 185 years after the constitution was put into place, and the Supreme Court was created, the court did not weigh in, really at all on the important question of abortion.\par \par \uc2 And so yesterday, what the court really did was go back to that almost 200- year long approach and tradition. It took itself out of the seat of power, recognizing that there are rarely deeply held views on both sides of this issue, that it`s a very strong issue with lots of deep moral and social questions. And it`s best handled by states, the Congress, elected officials and local people being able to decide these important social and policy questions rather than the court developing a one-size fits all solution that it`s trying to discern from a constitutional text that really doesn`t address it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I think, Professor Mascott, critics would push back and say the word she is also not included in the constitution. Let me ask you, though, about Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , he said that the court was going too far that all it needed to do was uphold Mississippi`s law. Mississippi itself is, you know, said that Roe did not need to be overturned. So why did the court feel the need to do that?\par \par \uc2 MASCOTT: Well, you know, quite frankly, I think yesterday`s decision actually showed a quite amount of consensus really on the court because in contrast to a lot of big decisions, there were not a lot of justices that chose to join only parts of the opinion. Chief Justice Roberts actually made the sixth vote in support of the judgment that the Mississippi law was constitutional. Five justices were joined Justice Alito is opinion in full.\par \par \uc2 And so the chief seems to be taking a slightly different approach. But even he recognized that to uphold Mississippi`s law really was intention with Roe and Casey. I think the majority opinion for Justice Alito noted and explained how the reasoning of Roe itself had been criticized from the beginning, even people who agree with the bottom line of the decision, the court itself is undermining the reasoning in the opinion consistently in different ways over the past 50 years.\par \par \uc2 And so, Justice Alito, and the majority applied the starry decisis factors to Roe and found that here because of the absence of workability, their view that it was egregiously wrong. And this deeply, deeply held views on both sides really made this an issue that was not for the court to impose a one-size fits all solution, but to leave to the states and people in Congress to look at. And I think in the long run, this decision will really do a lot to take politics out of the Supreme Court because issues of this level of importance really should not be decided for all of us by just nine justices in robes.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right, Jennifer Mascott, thank you so much for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We want to turn to another major story this morning. That is President Biden signing historic and bipartisan gun legislation into law this morning. The first in nearly 30 years. NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali is covering that for us this morning, Ali, Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning, Peter. A bright spot this morning for Democrats. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  signing landmark bipartisan legislation on gun violence prevention this morning. It caps off a roller coaster of emotions for lawmakers here in DC. .within hours of the Supreme Court`s decision on Roe and just one day after the court overturned some states gun laws.\par \par \uc2 House lawmakers voting to pass the most significant package of gun violence prevention legislation in nearly three decades. 14 Republican lawmakers breaking ranks with Republican leadership, joining Democrats to pass that bill, including the Republican lawmakers who represent Uvalde Texas. Tony Gonzalez, as well as \uc2 Chris Jacobs\uc2  who represents the area near Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 That final bill including increased funding for mental health programs and school security, as well as closing the so-called boyfriend loophole for domestic abusers, enhancing background checks for buyers under the age of 21. And incentivizing states to put red flag laws in place. Peter?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right. Ali Vitali with that headline today. We`ll keep a close eye as the president prepares to sign it this morning. Ali, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And let`s turn now to the weather with tens of millions of people under heat advisories once again this morning. Bill Karins is in today for Somara. Bill (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Just as folks are preparing for some of those summer breaks. We`ll be talking about it. Bill, we`ll see you in a moment. Still to come right here. We`re going to have a lot more on the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade this morning. We will take you to the abortion clinic at the center of the storm and find out what it means for their patients going forward.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Plus, cancellation chaos at airports all across the country. What you can expect the next time you fly. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Still to come on TODAY. Mirror, mirror on the wall who is the ugliest dog of all? We are going to introduce you to the precious pooch that just took home top honors at this year`s ugly dog contest.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: First look, this is going to be competitive. Plus, get ready to hit the gas. Dale Earnhardt Jr. joins us live to talk about this weekend`s big race at the Nashville superspeedway. But first.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:18]\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We are back this Saturday morning June 25th, 2022. And here is a live look at the Supreme Court where on Friday, the highest court in the land ruled to overturn Roe v. Wade and end the constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: You can see some of the security barriers there put up a matter of weeks ago after the draft ruling came out. We`ll have much more of that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But we want to begin this half an hour with a check of some of the mornings other headlines and we start with that breaking news out of Norway were shooting at a nightclub overnight turn deadly. Two people there were killed, 14 others wounded after a suspect opened fire at a popular gay bar in the center of Oslo.\par \par \uc2 Police have arrested the gunman. They were investigating the shooting as a possible terrorist attack, though it is not clear if there was any connection to the city`s Pride celebrations. A Pride Parade there scheduled for today has now been cancelled.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: \uc2 Juul\uc2  e-cigarettes will remain on store shelves at least for now. A federal appeals court on Friday put a temporary hold on the government`s order for the company to stop selling electronic cigarettes.\par \par \uc2 Now, in their emergency motion, \uc2 Juul\uc2  called it an extraordinarily unlawful action that would cause irreparable harm. The temporary hold has an effect until July 12th, while \uc2 Juul\uc2  appeals that sales ban.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: And the \uc2 Tampa Bay Lightning\uc2  are hoping to keep things alive with a third straight Stanley Cup title in their dreams. The two time defending champions beating the \uc2 Colorado Avalanche\uc2  three to two on Friday in game five, preventing Colorado from clinching the Stanley Cup on home ice.\par \par \uc2 Ondrej Palat scoring the game winner for the Lightning late in the third period. The Avs currently leave that series three games to two, they now head back to Tampa for game six on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Now, back to our top story this morning, the Supreme Court`s historic ruling that overturns Roe v. Wade. Some states have already banned abortions with others set to do so in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 Reaction has been swift with the abortion rights advocates worried for the future of women, all women.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Blayne Alexander is in Jackson, Mississippi for us this morning just outside of the clinic, that`s really at the heart of this Supreme Court ruling. Blayne, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, you`re right, Morgan, good morning to you. In the state of Mississippi, there is one abortion clinic, it`s right here behind me. And because of the state`s trigger law, in about 10 days, this clinic is going to have to shut its doors for good.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re already seeing a number of protesters here outside of the clinic, even before it opens its doors. And it comes as many people across the country continue to gather to either celebrate or mourn the court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER (voice over): Across the country, this is the sound of the end of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is a great victory to be sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have dangerous times ahead for health for women.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: A nation polarized but perhaps especially so in Mississippi, the state that sent the case to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re not going down, we`re not giving up.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: The Jackson Women`s Health Organization still seeing patients even as Friday`s ruling came down.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you that any patient who contacts us, we`ll see them. We`ll make sure we see them (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: In Mississippi, 59 percent of people believe abortion should be banned in most cases, among them, Terri Herring.\par \par \uc2 Do you consider today a victory?\par \par \uc2 TERRI HERRING, PRESIDENT, CHOOSE LIFE MISSISSIPPI: Absolutely. I mean, this is a victory for the babies.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: She runs Choose Life Mississippi, which helps fund pregnancy resource centers around the state.\par \par \uc2 HERRING: What we hope is that that will see more women that women will not choose to go out of state but they`ll seek help right here in Mississippi. And that will have answers for many of the problems that they face.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: Getty Israel runs Sisters in Birth, a clinic that also serves pregnant women, but she believes women should have a right to choose that pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GETTY ISRAEL, FOUNDER, SISTERS IN BIRTH: Mississippi is already the most unsafe place for black babies to be born. I`m going to say for any baby to be born.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: And are you concerned that this ruling will make those numbers --\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL: Those numbers will increase. They will increase.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: The court`s decision means effectively ending abortion not just in Mississippi, but across a wide section of the South.\par \par \uc2 Tyler Harden is the Mississippi State Coordinator for Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean for Mississippi women?\par \par \uc2 TYLER HARDEN, MISSISSIPPI STATE COORDINATOR FOR PLANNED PARENTHOOD: It just adds another barrier to the already existing barriers that people face when accessing abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER (on camera): And guys, that`s something that certainly a lot of people have been speaking about that many women will not have the resources the ability to go out of state to seek those abortion services should they choose.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re seeing a growing number of companies saying that they will pay for their employees to go across state lines and get that service.\par \par \uc2 A number of companies including places like \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Dick`s Sporting Goods, all of them saying that they will help make that service accessible, guys.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right, Blayne Alexander down in Mississippi for us. Blayne, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:03]\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Let`s check in with this weekend`s forecast right now. Bill Karins is watching that for us. It`s going to be a hot one for folks and some other folks who are keeping their eyes on some storms coming this way.\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, possibility. Will Bonnie form? We`ve already had our a named storm. So, will Bonnie form this week? There is a possibility. We have two areas that we`re watching. The first is our tropical disturbance that will be forming in the Gulf of Mexico. There`s nothing there yet. But there is a 20 percent chance of development in this region. And if it does happen to be, somewhere near the Texas or Louisiana coastline. Again, only a 20 percent chance, but it`s close enough here to the Gulf of Mexico and the coastal areas that will watch that.\par \par \uc2 And if that area doesn`t form, look right behind it, far out in the Atlantic, kind of rare for this time of year to have something this far forming out in the Atlantic but a 60 percent chance this does develop.\par \par \uc2 And if it does, it would eventually be heading towards the Caribbean Sea. And in case you`re wondering where it would happen. After that, it looks like it would travel through the southern route through the Caribbean Sea.\par \par \uc2 As of now, it doesn`t look like that would be making a turn towards the United States at all.\par \par \uc2 So, again, we got a couple of days to watch that one. And we`ll keep our eyes there for our friends and the Gulf of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 That`s a look at your national forecast. Now. Let`s look at the weather outside your window.\par \par \uc2 CLAY ANDERSON\uc2 , STORM TEAM4 METEOROLOGIST: Good morning, everyone. Meteorologist \uc2 Clay Anderson\uc2 . Outside of our windows here, 70 degrees winds now switching more to the south east, but they`re less than 10 mile per hour.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures are going to really climb everyone in the early afternoon from 11:00 a.m. well into the mid 80s across our area. So, make sure you are hydrated. They`re going to tap out near close to 90 degrees here in town.\par \par \uc2 And for tomorrow, we`ll do it all over again. We`re close to 90 degrees for your Sunday before storms on Monday. Have a great Saturday.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: And that`s your Saturday forecast.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right, Bill Karins for us. Bill, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And stay with us because just ahead from the highways to the skies, why heading out for a summer vacation may actually be a pretty big headache these days.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: We`ll have that right after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:31]\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: We`re back on this Saturday morning and if you`re like the rest of us, you and your family are probably itching to get away for a summer vacation after being stuck so close to home for the last several years throughout the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That`s right, but as you`ve seen gas prices, they cost an arm and a leg and flying is just a plain nightmare these days thanks to mass cancellations with a July 4th holiday just around the corner.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Guad Venegas is in Los Angeles covering the story for us this morning. Guad, hi there.\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi Morgan, Peter. Well, as the summer travel season hits its stride, more people are heading to the skies and most likely have been delayed or canceled. Now, it isn`t much better traveling by car with record high prices at the pump. Yet, despite these challenges, a large number of Americans are still looking forward to travel this summer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS (voice over): With no COVID restrictions and summer in full swing, travelers are once again taking to the skies as airlines struggle to keep up with demand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On my way to the airport, I see my flight is canceled. So, I`m like, oh, well, this is unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: More than 700 flights were canceled Friday across the U.S. with over 6,000 delayed. Airlines now bracing for a Fourth of July weekend surge as they deal with the ripple effects of a pandemic, including a lack of pilots.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SHUPE, CORPORATE COMMUNICATIONS MANAGER, AMERICAN AUTOMOBILE ASSOCIATION: All of a sudden, people want to hop on those planes and take those vacations again. So, there are some staffing shortages that have been causing some problems within the airlines recently.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Travelers can also expect to pay more.\par \par \uc2 Where can consumers expect the biggest price hikes?\par \par \uc2 SHUPE: Airfare is going to be about 14 percent higher than last year. Hotel accommodations are going to be about 23 percent higher than last year. We`re still going to be paying pretty high prices at the pump when we fill up and head out for our Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Yet, despite the higher prices, many of us still plan on getting away AAA anticipating nearly 48 million Americans will go on vacation over the coming week ahead of the Fourth of July. The vast majority are expected to travel by car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m confident I`m going to Vegas, regardless of the gas price. I mean, it`s going to eat up my wallet but I`m going to go have fun.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: But fun still comes at a cost. This morning, the price of gas still sky high at an average of 4.91 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS (on camera): It`s going to be a challenging summer for travel but especially for airlines. The latest Air Travel Consumer Report from the Department of Transportation indicates that consumer complaints against airlines rose more than 300 percent this year as compared to pre-pandemic levels and is growing every month so far in 2022, Peter, Morgan.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Yes. So much to look forward to and already so many frustrations for all those folks trying to get away. Guad, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Coming up right here next, U-G-L-Y, you don`t have no alibi, you ugly. With apologies to who was a fishbone somebody like that, but also kind of cute in a weird way. We`re going to introduce you to the World`s Ugliest Dog.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That`s right. Kerry Sanders who also served as a judge last night has that story right after this. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:47:07]\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: We are back on a Saturday morning and "NBC NEWS NOW" anchor Joe fryer is joining us and I do hope that you guys are as excited as I am about this one because we are about to find out who was crowned the World`s Ugliest Dog. Do you know that was even a thing?\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Yes, lots of suspense. And here, this might be one of the best assignments out there. And there was only one NBC person who was made for this job.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yeah, that`s right. That`s Carrie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: There he is.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: -- California where they crowned this year`s World`s Ugliest Dog. Kerry is not only our correspondent on the story, he was also a judge because Kerry, like you`re an expert of this? I don`t know. Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good morning. Look, guys, you might say, wow, a contest were ugly is cute. But in this case, I think it`s more appropriate to say bow wow.\par \par \uc2 And yes, we are here at the fairgrounds in Sonoma County and Marin County where they hold the contest for the World`s Ugliest Dog and we do have a winner. Number one U-G-L-Y in the world.\par \par \uc2 But before we meet the winner, as they say, let`s meet the contestants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice over): America has gone to the dogs and it`s just a distraction we need.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Booster Kong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is Monkey.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And monkey in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A blue factored Brussels Griffon.\par \par \uc2 This has more readout and I`m going to say her breeding one time because I`m not good at it. She`s a Xoloitzcuintli.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Somewhere inside this hairball, there`s a Pekinese named Wild Thing.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know whether you`re a Star Trek fan, but I`m thinking the trouble with Tribbles.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Into the machinery all right, and then, probably, and all the other food processors too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He`s Tribble.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: He`s Tribble.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sometimes he`s just trouble.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Mr. Happy Face is a rescue adopted by his owner in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is her first competition and so he is -- he gets a little overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Is it all the paparazzi around?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is (INAUDIBLE), it is the paparazzi. You totally got it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: In all, eight dogs competing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is Jenny the Korean pug from Korea.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Some from as far away as Asia, vying for the crown of ugliest.\par \par \uc2 Among the judges, me.\par \par \uc2 We need for our contestants some applause. COVID put a two year break on the ugliest dog in the world competition but like a dog with a bone, the county fair just wouldn`t be the fair without this event.\par \par \uc2 The crowd and the judges, like a pack of dogs in agreement.\par \par \uc2 The ugliest dog in the world Mr. Happy Face.\par \par \uc2 What`s Mr. Happy Face is thinking right now?\par \par \uc2 [07:50:07]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He`s wondering if there are any treats that are part of this.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And your answer to that one is?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is of course.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): Mr. Happy Face, the ugliest dog in the world. Not just the face a mother could love. Joe, Morgan, Peter, I think it`s appropriate for me to say woof.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: That`s Mr. Happy Face, I just want to see like a sad face on that dog, right? Like that was a joyful Mr. Happy Face right there. Sort of lacking the celebration.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Yes, that was -- I have no words. That truly is a face that I guess we all love now.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: -- selfie with the dog.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: All right, Kerry.\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That was great. Look, we`ll be right back just after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 P. ALEXANDER: Still to come right here, the NASCAR drivers feeling the need for speed as another exciting race gets going this weekend.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That`s right. We`re going to talk to Dale Earnhardt Jr. He`ll join us live in just a moment. We`ll tell you what to expect. First, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220625ei6p000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Some Abortion Providers Are Relocating\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Yamiche, thank you. With abortion restrictions likely in more than half the country, some states where it will remain legal are anticipating an influx of patients from elsewhere. Some abortion providers are even relocating. Here`s Stephanie Gosk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With the stroke of a pen, the Supreme Court has likely triggered a massive abortion migration in this country. More than 300,000 abortions were performed last year in the 26 states where the procedure could soon be illegal or highly restricted. Now, those people may choose to cross state lines. In Tennessee, the Attorney General wants the state`s six-week ban to start right now.\par \par \uc2 HERBERT SLATERY, ATTORNEY GENERAL, TENNESSEE: The people of Tennessee for the first time in 50 years will have a chance to weigh in on this issue.\par \par \uc2 NIKIA GRAYSON, CLINICAL SERVICES DIRECTOR, CHOICES MEMPHIS CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH: This is where we have -- do our abortion procedures back here.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: These are your --\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Two abortion room.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: -- procedure room. You have two rooms.\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: We recently visited the Choices Healthcare Clinic in Memphis. Soon the doors to these rooms will be closed for good.\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Our patients are predominantly Black. The majority of them are uninsured or underinsured.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: They have been bracing for this court decision for months.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer pepper, PRESIDENT AND CEO, CHOICES MEMPHIS CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH: So, we just kind of all this is going to be really bad for us. And so, I said to my executive team, there`s this place called Carbondale.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Carbondale is in southern Illinois, three hours by car from Memphis. It`s where they plan to open a new clinic in August, but were reluctant to show us pictures because of security concerns. Clinician Nikia Grayson will be driving back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Will it be big enough to handle what is really going to be potentially a flood of patients?\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Now, that`s a good question. I don`t know. I think that we will be the only clinic right now in the southern part of Illinois. We will handle as many patients as we can.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:04]\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In some states where abortion will still be legal, access is being expanded. Connecticut passed a law allowing non-physicians to provide abortions. But anti-abortion activists hope increasing support systems during pregnancy will change people`s minds.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, PRESIDENT NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE: I don`t know of anybody that is or any state that is thinking that they can stop people from traveling to another state. We are going to have to just push even harder than we have been to encourage women to let their baby live and to seek out the resources that are available.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But for many of those who choose not to, getting an abortion just got a whole lot harder. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Memphis, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, today`s other major story, final bipartisan approval in Congress of the most significant gun safety bill in decades.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with another major story tonight. Congress passing the biggest changes to our nation`s gun laws in nearly 30 years. Garrett Haake is at the Capitol. And Garrett, this bipartisan bill breaks decades of congressional gridlock on guns.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. Lawmakers in both parties held this as a significant breakthrough on an issue in which Congress has traditionally failed to act despite enormous public pressure to do so. Now, this bill centers around enhanced background checks for any would-be gun buyers under 21, new resources for states to enact red flag laws, and a $7 billion investment in mental health programs.\par \par \uc2 The Senate acting first passing the bill late last night and then a motional vote as gun violence survivors and advocates looked on. 15 Republicans joining all Democrats in support. House GOP leaders urged their members to oppose this bill which they said infringed on Second Amendment rights, but 14th House Republicans voted for it anyway, including the representatives of both Buffalo and Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 So, tonight, Lester, all that`s left is for President Biden to sign this bill into law. We expect to see that in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Garrett Haake, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Richard Engel`s exclusive with Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy. His first public comments about those two captured Americans.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220628ei6o0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Has Overturned Roe V. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams \par \uc2 775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and his opinion writing Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. A draft of the decision leaked to the public weeks ago took away the suspense of the moment, but not the euphoria of victory for abortion opponents, nor the sting of defeat felt by abortion-rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the scene right now of crowds gathered outside the Supreme Court in protest and celebration. Our team ready to cover it all starting with Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Outside the court now ring with the security fence opponents of abortion rights cheered.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Complete and utter joy that it was finally overturned. But the determination, a scaling determination the battle is not over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Legal abortion our demand.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Legal abortion our demand.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: For others, it was what they were dreading ever since the court signaled in December it was likely to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal. It feels like my country doesn`t love me and appreciate my body as a woman.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The court overturned nearly 50 years of abortion precedents in a ruling that was a first never before has the court granted and then take it away a widely recognized constitutional right. The court voted five to four to overturn Roe. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said going that far wasn`t necessary to uphold Mississippi`s law banning abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The court voted six to three to uphold that law.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion overturning Roe tracks closely with the version that leaked in May touching off nationwide protest including at the homes of some of the courts conservatives. Justice Samuel Alito`s majority opinion said no right to abortion is protected by any constitutional provision. The Roe decision he said was egregiously wrong and deeply damaging.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIVE COMMITTEE: Like Winston Churchill said, this is the end of the beginning. It`s been a 50-year battle to protect unborn children. But we now have those battles in every state.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Unlike other court rulings based on a right to privacy, Alito said abortion is different because it involves a potential life. So, he said the ruling does not undermine the rights to same-sex marriage or contraception, though Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said the court should take another look at them too.\par \par \uc2 The ruling does not make abortion illegal but it`s no longer a constitutional right, so that leaves the issue up to each state. It`s likely to become illegal soon in about half the nation. Some states have already banned it as of tonight. The rest of the banned states are likely to follow in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:08]\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in a controlling concurrence said states that ban abortion cannot make it a crime for their residents to travel to a state where abortion is legal to get the procedure. In a joint dissent, Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , and Elena Kagan said the court ruling means that from the very moment of fertilization, a woman has no rights to speak up. They say a state can force her to bring a pregnancy to term even at the steepest personal and family cost.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: People are going to be heading to the streets both in the wake of this decision immediately and in the days and months to come, and making clear that the majority of people of the United States support abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Pete is joining us now from outside the Supreme Court. Pete, what`s it been like out there today?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Well, this is the biggest crowd I`ve ever seen outside the Supreme Court after a decision. It`s been building steadily all day, people celebrating and mourning the decisions. And while it does return the issue to the states, the dissenter say, there`s nothing in this ruling that would prevent Congress from banning abortion nationwide if it ever chose to. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220628ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Calling This A Sad Day For America, Framing The Upcoming Midterms As An All-Out Battle For Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 712 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Blayne Alexander, thank you. President Biden condemned today`s decision declaring that Roe will be on the ballot in this fall`s Midterm elections, while conservative Republicans applauded the ruling. Peter Alexander now with more from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEW CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: A Somber President Biden tonight declaring this a low moment for the nation`s highest court.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s a sad day for the court and for the country. Now, with Roe gone, let`s be very clear, the health and life of women of this nation are now at risk.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The White House mindful of how personal this ruling is inviting more than a dozen senior aides, all women, to witness the President calling the decision the result of an extreme ideology.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It was three justices named by one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Make no mistake, this decision is a combination of a deliberate effort over decades to upset balance of our law.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Top Republicans touting the ruling as a hard-won victory.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The right to life has been vindicated. The voiceless will finally have a voice.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): It will save countless innocent children.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Two prominent senators now suggests they were misled by assurances made during recent confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 BRET KAVANAUGH, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Roe v. Wade is an important precedent in the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: A good judge will consider it as precedent of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Republican \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  slamming today`s decision as inconsistent with those justices` testimony. So, what comes next? With his authority limited tonight, President Biden is calling on Congress to guarantee the right to an abortion. But Senate Democrats currently do not have the votes for that. The President vowing his administration will protect women`s access to \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved medications like abortion pills and contraception and promising to defend a woman`s ability to travel to other states where abortion remains legal.\par \par \uc2 63 percent of Americans do not believe Roe should be overturned. Democrats including the nation`s first woman vice president hoping to redirect that outrage to this fall`s Midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You have the final word. So, this is not over.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This fall, Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: A defining cultural clash in a country now even more deeply divided.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, President Biden is suggesting the court`s ruling could lead to other personal freedoms being rolled back. What`s he saying about that?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. The President sided Justice Thomas`s majority opinion that the right to contraception and same- sex marriage should be reconsidered. President Biden arguing the court is now taken the country down an extreme and dangerous path. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander from the White House.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn now to Yamiche Alcindor. As we just heard, the President and House Speaker saying this fall, Roe is essentially on the ballot in the Midterms, but to what end?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, much like the impact of this decision, the political fallout was immediate ahead of the Midterms. Democrats will be hammering home the point of voters that polls show the majority of Americans want abortion to be legal in some form. But the reality is this is not a one-issue election. Democrats will be trying to focus voters on abortion while Americans are juggling multiple challenges including record-high gas prices and historic inflation.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220628ei6o0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 24, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams, Blayne Alexander, Yamiche Alcindor,  Stephanie Gosk, Garrett Haake, Richard Engel \par \uc2 3,965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the landmark ruling the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade taking away the constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Protests and celebrations erupting outside the court after the historic 5-4 decision overturning nearly 50 years of abortion rights, leaving the matter of two states now to decide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Complete and utter joy that it was finally overturned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The majority opinion written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  saying Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. The three liberal justices, in their dissent, warning millions of American women have lost a fundamental constitutional protection. The impact, roughly half the states expected to ban abortion, 13 states with trigger laws banning abortion immediately or soon. Clinics in several states already turning women away.\par \par \uc2 President Biden calling this a sad day for America, framing the upcoming midterms as an all-out battle for abortion rights. And the far-reaching fallout. Could a national ban on abortion be in the future and how Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  opened the door to overturning the rights to contraception and same sex marriage too.\par \par \uc2 Another major story tonight, Congress passing the most significant gun safety bill in nearly 30 years now on its way to President Biden`s desk.\par \par \uc2 And our NBC News exclusive, one-on-one with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . His message about the Americans captured war fighting in Ukraine. What he`s now vowing to do.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC Nightly News with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. Where do privacy and personal freedom begin and where did they end? Questions that have framed an enduring divide in modern America. One many thought was settled nearly 50 years ago when the Supreme Court affirmed a constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Today the court answer that question again, this time on a five to four vote taking away what was regarded as a fundamental right. Right now, new laws banning abortion are taking effect in several states just hours after the court overturn the landmark Roe vs. Wade decision leaving it to states to decide whether abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and his opinion writing Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. A draft of the decision leaked to the public weeks ago took away the suspense of the moment, but not the euphoria of victory for abortion opponents, nor the sting of defeat felt by abortion-rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the scene right now of crowds gathered outside the Supreme Court in protest and celebration. Our team ready to cover it all starting with Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Outside the court now ring with the security fence opponents of abortion rights cheered.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Complete and utter joy that it was finally overturned. But the determination, a scaling determination the battle is not over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Legal abortion our demand.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Legal abortion our demand.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: For others, it was what they were dreading ever since the court signaled in December it was likely to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal. It feels like my country doesn`t love me and appreciate my body as a woman.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The court overturned nearly 50 years of abortion precedents in a ruling that was a first never before has the court granted and then take it away a widely recognized constitutional right. The court voted five to four to overturn Roe. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said going that far wasn`t necessary to uphold Mississippi`s law banning abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The court voted six to three to uphold that law.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion overturning Roe tracks closely with the version that leaked in May touching off nationwide protest including at the homes of some of the courts conservatives. Justice Samuel Alito`s majority opinion said no right to abortion is protected by any constitutional provision. The Roe decision he said was egregiously wrong and deeply damaging.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIVE COMMITTEE: Like Winston Churchill said, this is the end of the beginning. It`s been a 50-year battle to protect unborn children. But we now have those battles in every state.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Unlike other court rulings based on a right to privacy, Alito said abortion is different because it involves a potential life. So, he said the ruling does not undermine the rights to same-sex marriage or contraception, though Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said the court should take another look at them too.\par \par \uc2 The ruling does not make abortion illegal but it`s no longer a constitutional right, so that leaves the issue up to each state. It`s likely to become illegal soon in about half the nation. Some states have already banned it as of tonight. The rest of the banned states are likely to follow in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:08]\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in a controlling concurrence said states that ban abortion cannot make it a crime for their residents to travel to a state where abortion is legal to get the procedure. In a joint dissent, Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , and Elena Kagan said the court ruling means that from the very moment of fertilization, a woman has no rights to speak up. They say a state can force her to bring a pregnancy to term even at the steepest personal and family cost.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: People are going to be heading to the streets both in the wake of this decision immediately and in the days and months to come, and making clear that the majority of people of the United States support abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Pete is joining us now from outside the Supreme Court. Pete, what`s it been like out there today?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Well, this is the biggest crowd I`ve ever seen outside the Supreme Court after a decision. It`s been building steadily all day, people celebrating and mourning the decisions. And while it does return the issue to the states, the dissenter say, there`s nothing in this ruling that would prevent Congress from banning abortion nationwide if it ever chose to. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The ripple effect was immediate just after the decision some of those trigger laws already in effect. Some women are unable to get abortion services planned for today. Blayne Alexander with more now from Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Outside the Mississippi clinic at the heart of today`s Supreme Court decision --\par \par \uc2 DIANE DERZIS, OWNER, JACKSON WOMEN`S HEALTH ORGANIZATION: We do abortion and we`re proud to do abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Even though the days for that are now numbered, at the Jackson Women`s Health Organization, the requests are only growing. In 10 days, because of the state`s trigger law, Mississippi`s only abortion clinic will shut down for good.\par \par \uc2 DERZIS: I will tell you that any patient who contact us, we`ll save you. We`ll make sure we see them during that days.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But in some states, change is already happening. Of the 13 trigger law states, abortion is now illegal with a few exceptions and at least five of them following today`s ruling, including Missouri, Oklahoma, and Arkansas where through tears, the Attorney General signed the certification late today.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE RUTLEDGE, ATTORNEY GENERAL, ARKANSAS: Restoring the state of Arkansas the authority to prohibit abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Wisconsin, Planned Parenthood immediately stopped abortions following today`s ruling. The Medical Director had to break the news to patients already in the waiting room today.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHY KING, MEDICAL DIRECTOR, WISCONSIN PLANNED PARENTHOOD: Today, I had to look people in the eye and turn them away when they were seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Across the country, emotional reaction on both sides.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My first reaction was rage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is an amazing victory.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And while some lawmakers celebrate the decision --\par \par \uc2 PHILIP GUNN, MISSISSIPPI SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: This is a profound change in the law in our country and it will save millions of lives of unborn children.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Others --\par \par \uc2 GOV. NED LAMONT (D-CT): My wife is in tears. My kids are incredibly distraught. My sister says I thought the courts are there to protect our freedoms, not roll them back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Back in Mississippi, today`s ruling is a victory for Terri Hearing.\par \par \uc2 TERRI HERRIN, PRESIDENT CHOOSE LIFE MISSISSIPPI: My eyes just filled with tears. This is a day to rejoice.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: She has spent nearly 30 years fighting to end abortion and says she`s proud Mississippi played a role.\par \par \uc2 What does today mean for you?\par \par \uc2 HERRING: You know, personally, you know, we didn`t know if this will happen in our lifetime, right? We`ve been advocating for a long time. So, seeing this happen in my lifetime and knowing that we have the opportunity to be a part of that is -- it means us being a part of changing history. ALEXANDER: But for Tyler Harden who runs Planned Parenthood in the state --\par \par \uc2 You`ve gotten dozens of calls since this decision came down. Are women scared?\par \par \uc2 TYLER HARDEN, DIRECTOR, PLANNED PARENTHOOD MISSISSIPPI: Yes. And to be honest, I`m scared as well.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The closest clinic she says will be in Florida, a seven-hour drive.\par \par \uc2 HARDEN: That means that people will have to find funds to travel. People will have to find funds to get hotel rooms, buy food.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Is that a feasible option for them?\par \par \uc2 HARDEN: It`s not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Blayne joins us now from Mississippi. And Blaine, I know one of the issues, particularly there is the ability of people to travel out of state. It`s particularly hard for many women.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): That`s right, Lester, especially here in Mississippi that has the highest poverty rate of any state in the country, especially among Black women. That`s a group that many fear will be most impacted by today`s decision. Now, I`m speaking with activists on both sides. One place they agree, a focus should be getting women the resources they need for a safe pregnancy and beyond. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Blayne Alexander, thank you. President Biden condemned today`s decision declaring that Roe will be on the ballot in this fall`s Midterm elections, while conservative Republicans applauded the ruling. Peter Alexander now with more from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEW CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: A Somber President Biden tonight declaring this a low moment for the nation`s highest court.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s a sad day for the court and for the country. Now, with Roe gone, let`s be very clear, the health and life of women of this nation are now at risk.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The White House mindful of how personal this ruling is inviting more than a dozen senior aides, all women, to witness the President calling the decision the result of an extreme ideology.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It was three justices named by one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Make no mistake, this decision is a combination of a deliberate effort over decades to upset balance of our law.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Top Republicans touting the ruling as a hard-won victory.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The right to life has been vindicated. The voiceless will finally have a voice.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): It will save countless innocent children.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Two prominent senators now suggests they were misled by assurances made during recent confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 BRET KAVANAUGH, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Roe v. Wade is an important precedent in the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: A good judge will consider it as precedent of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Republican Susan Collins slamming today`s decision as inconsistent with those justices` testimony. So, what comes next? With his authority limited tonight, President Biden is calling on Congress to guarantee the right to an abortion. But Senate Democrats currently do not have the votes for that. The President vowing his administration will protect women`s access to \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved medications like abortion pills and contraception and promising to defend a woman`s ability to travel to other states where abortion remains legal.\par \par \uc2 63 percent of Americans do not believe Roe should be overturned. Democrats including the nation`s first woman vice president hoping to redirect that outrage to this fall`s Midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You have the final word. So, this is not over.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This fall, Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: A defining cultural clash in a country now even more deeply divided.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, President Biden is suggesting the court`s ruling could lead to other personal freedoms being rolled back. What`s he saying about that?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. The President sided Justice Thomas`s majority opinion that the right to contraception and same- sex marriage should be reconsidered. President Biden arguing the court is now taken the country down an extreme and dangerous path. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander from the White House.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn now to Yamiche Alcindor. As we just heard, the President and House Speaker saying this fall, Roe is essentially on the ballot in the Midterms, but to what end?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, much like the impact of this decision, the political fallout was immediate ahead of the Midterms. Democrats will be hammering home the point of voters that polls show the majority of Americans want abortion to be legal in some form. But the reality is this is not a one-issue election. Democrats will be trying to focus voters on abortion while Americans are juggling multiple challenges including record-high gas prices and historic inflation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, this decision is the culmination of conservative activism and the strategy in the Senate to really create a conservative majority on the court. Now, the question is will Republican lawmakers face any backlash or will it strengthen their movement and lead to federal action to ban abortion? What`s clear is that abortion will now likely be a top issue in the Midterms as this new political landscape takes shape. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yamiche, thank you. With abortion restrictions likely in more than half the country, some states where it will remain legal are anticipating an influx of patients from elsewhere. Some abortion providers are even relocating. Here`s Stephanie Gosk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With the stroke of a pen, the Supreme Court has likely triggered a massive abortion migration in this country. More than 300,000 abortions were performed last year in the 26 states where the procedure could soon be illegal or highly restricted. Now, those people may choose to cross state lines. In Tennessee, the Attorney General wants the state`s six-week ban to start right now.\par \par \uc2 HERBERT SLATERY, ATTORNEY GENERAL, TENNESSEE: The people of Tennessee for the first time in 50 years will have a chance to weigh in on this issue.\par \par \uc2 NIKIA GRAYSON, CLINICAL SERVICES DIRECTOR, CHOICES MEMPHIS CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH: This is where we have -- do our abortion procedures back here.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: These are your --\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Two abortion room.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: -- procedure room. You have two rooms.\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: We recently visited the Choices Healthcare Clinic in Memphis. Soon the doors to these rooms will be closed for good.\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Our patients are predominantly Black. The majority of them are uninsured or underinsured.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: They have been bracing for this court decision for months.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer pepper, PRESIDENT AND CEO, CHOICES MEMPHIS CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH: So, we just kind of all this is going to be really bad for us. And so, I said to my executive team, there`s this place called Carbondale.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Carbondale is in southern Illinois, three hours by car from Memphis. It`s where they plan to open a new clinic in August, but were reluctant to show us pictures because of security concerns. Clinician Nikia Grayson will be driving back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Will it be big enough to handle what is really going to be potentially a flood of patients?\par \par \uc2 GRAYSON: Now, that`s a good question. I don`t know. I think that we will be the only clinic right now in the southern part of Illinois. We will handle as many patients as we can.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:04]\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In some states where abortion will still be legal, access is being expanded. Connecticut passed a law allowing non-physicians to provide abortions. But anti-abortion activists hope increasing support systems during pregnancy will change people`s minds.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS, PRESIDENT NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE: I don`t know of anybody that is or any state that is thinking that they can stop people from traveling to another state. We are going to have to just push even harder than we have been to encourage women to let their baby live and to seek out the resources that are available.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But for many of those who choose not to, getting an abortion just got a whole lot harder. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Memphis, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, today`s other major story, final bipartisan approval in Congress of the most significant gun safety bill in decades.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with another major story tonight. Congress passing the biggest changes to our nation`s gun laws in nearly 30 years. Garrett Haake is at the Capitol. And Garrett, this bipartisan bill breaks decades of congressional gridlock on guns.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. Lawmakers in both parties held this as a significant breakthrough on an issue in which Congress has traditionally failed to act despite enormous public pressure to do so. Now, this bill centers around enhanced background checks for any would-be gun buyers under 21, new resources for states to enact red flag laws, and a $7 billion investment in mental health programs.\par \par \uc2 The Senate acting first passing the bill late last night and then a motional vote as gun violence survivors and advocates looked on. 15 Republicans joining all Democrats in support. House GOP leaders urged their members to oppose this bill which they said infringed on Second Amendment rights, but 14th House Republicans voted for it anyway, including the representatives of both Buffalo and Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 So, tonight, Lester, all that`s left is for President Biden to sign this bill into law. We expect to see that in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Garrett Haake, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Richard Engel`s exclusive with Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy. His first public comments about those two captured Americans.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now, to our NBC News exclusive as Ukraine`s forces withdrawn from the critical eastern city of Severodonetsk. President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , sitting down one on one with our Richard Engel. The Ukrainian leader talking about the Americans captured while fighting in Ukraine and more. Speaking throughout the interview in both English and Ukrainian.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): We met President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  in his office,\par \par \uc2 Good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 While he makes many appearances, he does few interviews.\par \par \uc2 Two Americans answered your call, as many did, to come here and fight for Ukraine`s democracy. They were captured. The Kremlin won`t rule out that they could face the death penalty. Is there something you would like to say directly to the families of those two Americans?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT, UKRAINE: They`re heroes. For me, they`re the same like Ukrainians because they give -- they gave the main things they had, their lives, their lives. But I`m sure that I will fight for them and we`ll get them back. And of course, they will come back to your families, to their children.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: We spoke just moments after Ukrainian troops under heavy Russian fire for weeks were forced to withdraw from the city of Severodonetsk in the east, giving Russia a new strategic foothold.\par \par \uc2 It seems like there`s small advances from each side every day and huge numbers of casualties. Can you continue in this? How do you change the stalemate instances?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): Their military outnumbers ours by 10 to one. No matter how strong we are, they outnumber us by 10 to one. It is very difficult.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Are you worried that the West will lose interest in Ukraine, become exhausted from this war, focus on other things, and that you`ll be increasingly isolated over time?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): I am worried about this thing indeed. The war has no boundaries. The war is currently happening in Ukraine. But that means that the war is happening everywhere in the world. I`m sure about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: President Zelenskyy thanked the United States and President Biden directly for the weapons he`s received so far, but said he needs a lot more. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Richard Engel, thank you for that. And Richard`s conversation was part of the Aspen Ideas Festival for which NBC is the media partner. You can watch the full interview this Tuesday on nbcnews.com.\par \par \uc2 Up next here, as we continue, some of the iconic moments that will define this consequential day for America.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: After a pivotal day for America, we leave you with what Americans were saying today as the course of the country was rewritten by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The Supreme Court has now overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel betrayed and I feel lost. I feel like I`m 19- years-old. I`ve had my first year of college. I shouldn`t be worried about my rights being taken away from me. And it hurts that I have little hope for the future.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve had people in my family have abortions and they have felt the regret years and years down the line. If Roe v. Wade wouldn`t have been legal inside of the United States, I would have family members that I don`t have today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s shocking. This is something that women should never rights to. I just don`t. I can`t.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every child deserves life. Human rights start at conception and end at natural death. And that is what I`m going to fight for until the day that I die.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: This is the first time the Supreme Court has ever granted a constitutional right which did so when Roe was decided in 1973 and then took it away, a popular right that was widely recognized.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thank you for watching everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Clea Shearer Gives Update On Her Breast Cancer Journey\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 4,325 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: We are back with the story featuring someone we love dearly here at "TODAY".\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yes, as one half of "The Home Edit", Clea Shearer has inspired people to get organized. And now, she`s got a brand-new mission, doesn`t she?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes, an important one. After finding a lump during a self-exam in February, Clea was diagnosed with Stage 2 breast cancer. And this morning, she`s sharing her story for the first time on television. And she`s also announcing an initiative that she hopes will save lives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I`m going to try and do a rainbow lineup of books up here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Clea Shearer has made a career of conquering clutter.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: You want to be either to, like, either need it, use it, or love it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Alongside her "Home Edit" co-founder, Joanna Teplin, she`s built an empire organizing the closets and pantries of celebrities and regular clients, even us here at "TODAY."\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): What happened? How did you do this?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): But in February, just weeks after her 40th birthday, everything changed.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): I know you guys live for the Lazy Susan.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We love.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Clea was in New York to appear with us on "TODAY" when she felt a lump in her breast.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: We had so much to celebrate. Our tv show, the trailer was just released, the show was coming out April 1st. We had a magazine launch. We had --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): You`re on top of the world.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: We had so much goodness. But when I felt that lump, I just was, like, I can`t put this off just because life is too good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Her OB-GYN told her it would be months before she could get an appointment but Clea was determined and pushed to get checked by another doctor. The test confirmed she had cancer.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): You saved your life.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): By knowing your own body.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): Trusting yourself and not letting it go.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Correct. I would be in a really different place right now if I had postponed what I knew was something. I didn`t know that it was going to be cancer, but I felt something. If I hadn`t done that, I have no idea what stage my cancer would be in.\par \par \uc2 Although I`m terrified, absolutely terrified of chemo, I am not terrified of the plan.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Through a double mastectomy and chemotherapy, Clea has documented every step in candid videos and posts for her millions of \uc2 Instagram\uc2  followers.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: And I had to, like, wipe the hair from my head.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): You`ve been really honest, you know. Some days are good, some days are not.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I think that one thing that I`ve realized about breast cancer, is it really -- it attacks your femininity in such ways that we don`t even know that we`re attached to. But, you know, there are other things that are really beautiful about it, too. All my scars feel, like, strange. Like, they feel, like, battle wounds.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): Did you know you had this within you?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Nope, I sure did not. I definitely thought I was more of a delicate flower than I am.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I think that`s -- it`s been a real learning, for me. I surprised myself with the things that I`m able to do.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): So, have you organized any medical offices, doctor`s offices, chemo rooms? I mean, you`re still being Clea, right?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I`m still being myself. My daughter actually, with her therapist, made a list of all the things that have changed and all the things that have stayed the same. And in the stayed the same, my mom still organizes by the rainbow. You know, she can`t help herself.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): Have you been surprised at the outpouring in how much love and support and well wishes you have received?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I have been surprised by that. I didn`t really anticipate kind of the rolling impact that it would continue to have. The piece that I find the most incredible, though, isn`t just the outpouring of love but it`s the outpouring of stories. The huge amount of people who have now, unfortunately, found cancer in their own body and wouldn`t have if I hadn`t, kind of, rang the bell about it.\par \par \uc2 I so appreciate the love.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voiceover): Now, she`s hoping to help even more people launching the Clea Shearer Breast Cancer Research Fund.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Our goals are really raising awareness for self-advocacy and making sure that screenings are available to women everywhere. And ultimately the way to cure cancer is through research. And, you know, hopefully this fund helps propel that research forward. I`m not going to get to the other side of cancer and be like, well, I did it. This is going to be a piece of my life forever. And I think that just making my cancer count is what brings me the most joy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (on camera): She`s really inspiring and the message that she has is so important because, you know, she did -- I don`t even know if it was a breast self-exam.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She just felt something.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Felt something.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And here she is. It`s -- she`s on top of the world with all this stuff. And she`s like, I can`t blow this off because I know it`s something. She trusted herself. She calls the docs, they`re like, we`ll get you in a few months.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC`S TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And she says, no. No, I need to get it checked out. And I think that it`s, you know, I`m so inspired by that because I think if it would have been me, I`d be like, well, I guess -- I`m sure it`s fine.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m sure it`s fine, right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know, you want to just blow it off and think it -- sweep it under the rug. And if she had, she`d be in a lot worse place. When they had the double mastectomy, they found out actually it`s Stage 2 cancer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow. And for her, I mean, we knew she was a giving person just because that`s her nature. But to see her right now, she`s still going through treatment -- she`s got a foundation. Like she`s busy. She`s staying that way.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She is. I mean, she`s totally determined to make something good come of this by telling this story, by being really honest about it. She said, you know, she said, I shaved my head and I thought I was being proactive. She said, but when my hair started falling out -- it broke her heart. It was really, really tough. So, there are high moments, there are low moments but she`s just determined.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And then all the people who found their cancer because she told her story. Like, I think that`s the whole -- that`s everything.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Making her cancer count.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She`s got -- still a long road ahead.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know, she`s just in the first phase of chemo. She`s got two more phases.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She`s going to have radiation. But she`s really positive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Man, if a positive attitude can kick cancer --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No, kidding. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- then it has no chance.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Because Clear is incredible.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: If you want to learn more about this research fund that she`s starting, you can go to today.com.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Mr. Roker, how about a check of the weather?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. Let`s show you what we`ve got and let`s look for today. And as we move from camera to camera -- I`m sorry, it was just moving there. We`re looking at a little wet weather making its way to the Northeast, we`ll see a few showers here. Some wet weather through Northern and Central California. The heat though is the big story, triple digits stretching from Central California to the Southwest on into the Gulf Coast and even up into the Central Plains.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at hot conditions. Record highs through the gulf. Severe storms will be firing up later today in the mid part of the country and we`re looking at wet weather throughout the Northeast. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: What now?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What time is it?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Best time of the morning, "POP Start".\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hit it, girl.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s a smorgasbord for you this morning. All right.\par \par \uc2 First up, the royals. This morning, the \uc2 University of Cambridge\uc2  unveiled the first joint portrait of the Duke and Duchess of Cambridge. Do you want to see it?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. William and Kate pictured side by side.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The Duchess in a gorgeous emerald gown.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I like that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But, of course, this painting was commissioned last year and completed by award-winning British artist Jamie Coreth.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It looks like a photo.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It really does.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I was just going to say, it`s as photo realistic.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Gorgeous. About the work, Coreth said he wanted to show the royals as both relaxed and approachable. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: He nailed it?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I think it`s cool that they`re not looking straight at us.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: They`re looking over there. I think it`s something --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s stunning.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: How long did they have to pose for that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I don`t know, Al. We`ll have to call and ask them. So, it you want to go see it --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- it currently hangs in the UK`s Fitzwilliam Museum where members of the public can visit the artwork for the next three years before it finds a new home in other community spaces and gather -- galleries around Cambridgeshire. So, we want everybody to be up ther