fair city. And lovely to meet you, ma`am. Thank you for being here. 104, how about that? All right.\par \par \uc2 Let`s show you what`s happening. I got to get a Smucker`s Jar for her. Anyway, let`s look at the weekend. Warmer highs in the northeast and New England, record heat down through the Gulf Coast, severe storms in Northern Plains, sunshine out west. On Saturday, summer warm continues in the Northeast, Mid Atlantic States, more records in the Gulf, sunshine out west, downright hot in the Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 Sunday, Sunday. No relief from the heat through the Gulf, the Northwest is still heating up. Look for that perfect day around the Great Lakes and more monsoonal moisture through the southwest. Oh, look who`s here to dry off, very nice.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s wet. My dress is wet. I told you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s what`s going on the country, here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [08:35:15]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. You know what?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: He says go give you a smile, it gave us a smile.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It did.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It was fun, y`all. You guys should the fountain.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s rejuvenating.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Go to shower camp.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: It`s the Fountain of Youth.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It is. It really is the Fountain of Youth. Coming up, with crinkled script here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re going to have the story about the fight over what`s fair when it comes to women`s sports may deeply personable -- personal for a family with a transgender little girl. Hallie Jackson has the story right after this.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Hi, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220625ei6n0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 23, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Garrett Haake, Pete Williams, Gabe Gutierrez, Al  Roker, Morgan Radford, Stephanie Gosk, Emilie Ikeda, Miguel Almaguer \par \uc2 3,926 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC ANCHOR (on camera): Breaking News Tonight, the gripping testimony before the American people on Donald Trump`s efforts to pressure the Justice Department to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Former top justice officials testified before the January 6 Committee about the tense Oval Office meeting three days before the attack where the former president proposed replacing the Acting Attorney General sent with a lawyer who would pursue his false election claims. One witness saying Trump told him to just say the election was corrupt and leave the rest to him and the GOP. And reveal the names of the GOP lawmakers who allegedly sought pardons in the aftermath of January 6.\par \par \uc2 The other major story tonight. The landmark Supreme Court ruling expanding gun rights overturning a more than century-old New York law restricting concealed carry. The immediate impact as the Senate clears the way for a final vote on the first major gun safety bill in decades.\par \par \uc2 The unrelenting heatwave threatening tens of millions. Al Roker is here.\par \par \uc2 And the dramatic rescue of the Team USA swimmer fainting in a pool during competition. The incredible underwater images. Her coach diving in and saving her life. Tonight, that swimmer sharing her story in an NBC News exclusive.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. An unsent letter described as a murder suicide pact by a former Trump White House lawyer stood at the core of testimony in today`s January 6 Committee hearing. Three former top Justice Department officials describing former President Trump`s efforts to enlist help from the department to perpetuate his unsupported claims of election fraud, including pressure on the DOJ to send an official letter to Georgia election officials.\par \par \uc2 That letter falsely stating the Justice Department had identified significant concerns that may have impacted the outcome of the election. When DOJ leaders refused to sign it, the President reportedly telling them just say the election was corrupt and leave the rest to me.\par \par \uc2 Today`s hearing focusing on the actions of former Assistant Attorney General Jeffrey Clark who witnesses say stood ready to do Mr. Trump`s bidding. Let`s get late details from Garrett Haake.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The January 6 committee tonight laying out their case that former President Trump attempted to force the Department of Justice to help him overturn the 2020 election, pressuring senior officials to make claims of widespread fraud that did not exist.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): President Trump ultimately wanted the Department of Justice to say the election was "corrupt" and "leave the rest to me and the Republican congressman."\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: A quote supported by a witness`s handwritten notes. Mr. Trump weighed replacing the Acting Attorney General Jeff Rosen with a lower-level official, an environmental lawyer named Jeff Clark who had drafted a letter to key states falsely claiming the DOJ had found significant fraud there.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HERSCHMANN, FORMER WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISOR: And when he finished discussing what he planned on doing, I said good (BLEEP). Sorry (BLEEP) congratulations, you just admitted the first step or act you take as attorney general would be committing a felony in violating Rule 6-C. You`re clearly the right candidate for this job.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD DONOGHUE\uc2 , FORMER ACTING DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: I made the point that Jeff Clark is not even competent to serve as the attorney general. And I said, that`s right, you`re an environmental lawyer. How about you go back to your office and we`ll call you when there`s an oil spill.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHMANN I thought Jeff`s proposal -- Clark`s proposal was nuts. I mean, this guy, at a certain point -- you know, listen, the best I can tell is the only thing you know about environmental and elections challenges is that they both start with E. And based on your answers tonight, I`m not even certain you know that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: DOJ leadership threatened to resign en mass.\par \par \uc2 DONOGHUE: The leadership will be gone. Jeff Clark will be left leading a graveyard.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Clark repeatedly invoked his Fifth Amendment rights when questioned by the committee. Federal agents searching Clark`s Virginia home on Wednesday, seizing his electronics. Committee evidence showing Clark was recommended to the President by Pennsylvania Republican Scott Perry, who was among several members of Congress who testimony showed later asked for presidential pardons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Perry asked for a pardon too, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Perry? Did he talk to you directly?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Perry had previously called claims he asked for a pardon a "soulless lie." Top DOJ officials debunking conspiracy theories pushed by the President and his aides, including a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  video suggesting Italian satellites were used to switch Trump votes to Biden, flagged by Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONOGHUE: I emailed the Acting Attorney General and I said pure insanity which was my impression of the video which was patently absurd.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: While Trump allies dismiss the committee`s work as a democratic distraction.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): It`s a political committee. And the one thing I do know and the American public knows, gas has gone up $1.86 a gallon since the day \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  announced that committee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Well, Garrett, the committee referencing today what Trump White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  called a murder-suicide pack. What more do we know about his cooperation with the committee?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE (on camera): Well, the committee says they`ve had informal conversations with the former White House Counsel, but today renewed their call for him to testify on the record for their investigation. It`s a missing piece, Lester, that the committee very much would like to fill in before their hearings resumed in mid-July.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Garrett Haake, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to our other top story, today`s landmark Supreme Court ruling on gun rights. The court striking down a New York State law saying it went too far in restricting who can get a permit to carry a gun in public, but the impact goes far beyond New York. Pete Williams now and the ruling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For the first time in American history, the Supreme Court ruled that when the Second Amendment says there`s a right to keep and bear arms, that means the right to carry a handgun outside the home for self-defense. The Court struck down a New York law that required showing a special need beyond the general desire for self-defense to get a permit for carrying a concealed gun in public.\par \par \uc2 By a vote of six to three, the court said no other constitutional right require showing some special need to exercise it, and that`s not how the Second Amendment works. Writing for the majority, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said local governments can still ban guns from sensitive places, but he said that doesn`t simply mean anywhere the public congregates.\par \par \uc2 And Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  said states can still impose licensing requirements, like background and mental health checks and firearms training.\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, CO-FOUNDER, SCOTUSBLOG: The Supreme Court today said you can`t require someone to justify why they want to concealed carry a gun, but it was leaving open where the state could restrict these guns and who could have them.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The decision will have an immediate impact on New York and five other states with similar restrictions. But in the rulings most far- reaching language, it said a concern for public safety isn`t enough to justify new gun controls. Justice Thomas said gun regulations must be "consistent with this nation`s historical tradition of firearms regulation."\par \par \uc2 ADAM WINKLER, LAW PROFESSOR, UCLA: I think this language is going to make it really difficult to justify gun laws going forward and will lead to court striking down a wide variety of gun safety laws.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: In dissent, the court`s three liberals listed nine recent mass shootings including at the school in Uvalde, Texas and the supermarket in Buffalo, New York. Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  said courts must consider the serious dangers and consequences of gun violence that leads states to regulate firearms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): We`ll get more decisions tomorrow. Nine cases remain from this term, including the one on whether to overturn Roe v. Wade, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Pete, what is today`s ruling tell us about the makeup of the court currently?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It says that this court`s supermajority is now flexing its muscles, the conservative supermajority. Remember, the court has ducked this question of guns outside the home for 14 years. This term, the conservatives had the votes to answer it the way they wanted and were likely to see this same 6-3 voting pattern in decisions yet to come, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Pete Williams at the Supreme Court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The response to today`s ruling was swift from New York`s governor and the mayor of New York City vowing new gun laws in response. Gabe Gutierrez has that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With gun violence surging across the country, tonight some city leaders are blasting the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR, NEW YORK CITY: We cannot allow New York to become the Wild, Wild West. That is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  says local officials are weighing their legal options. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  has recovered more than 3000 guns so far this year and gun arrests have hit a 28-year high.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: The decision ignores this shocking crisis of gun violence every day, engulfing not only New York, but engulfing our entire country.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: In a state still reeling from last month`s Buffalo mass shooting that left 10 people dead, a defiant Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is considering calling a special session of the legislature to come up with new regulations.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): We can have restrictions on speech, you can`t yell fire in a crowded theatre, but somehow, there`s no restrictions allowed on the Second Amendment?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But the president of the organization that brought the lawsuit welcome the ruling, which he says protects the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 TOM KING, PRESIDENT, NEW YORK STATE RIFLE AND PISTOL ASSOCIATION: The time has come for this. The judges decided and they`re going to have to live with it.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Other states with similar laws to New York`s are now scrambling to defend them.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PLATKIN, ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL, NEW JERSEY: What works In a rural state like Wyoming doesn`t necessarily work in a densest state in America like New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Do you expect more legal challenges to the laws in New Jersey as a result of this ruling?\par \par \uc2 PLATKIN: Yes, I think the Supreme Court invited open season on challenges to gun laws.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: As the High Court struck down some gun restrictions --\par \par \uc2 SEN. BRIAN SCHATZ (D-HI): The yeas are 65.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Today the Senate cleared a key procedural vote on others advancing a bipartisan bill to combat gun violence. A final vote could come tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe joining me here right now. Let`s circle back to that Supreme Court decision. New York officials very quick to point out this does not mean folks can immediately start carrying guns in public.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Yes, Lester. Private businesses can still prohibited weapons. There will still be background checks. What we expect is that lawmakers will now debate what exactly are sensitive area is where weapons can be banned. Is that hospitals, subways? That`s also an open question right now, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, one year after the collapse of the Surfside condo tower that killed 98 people, a judge today approved a $1.2 billion settlement for the families of those who died and survivors. More than two dozen defendants were sued for negligence, including the building security company and a consulting engineer.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, much of the southern half of the country remains in the grip of a dangerous heatwave with more records broken today. Al Roker has it all for us. Al, is a relief on the way?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR (on camera): Well, not until early next week, Lester. But in the meantime, 28 million of us under heat advisories, watches, heat warnings as well stretching from Texas all the way into Florida. We are looking at record low warm temperatures from Dallas, Shreveport, Atlanta, Tallahassee tonight. So, those temperatures don`t drop down that much, so they bounce back up big time tomorrow afternoon, with records possible in Dallas, Stillwater, Birmingham, Albany, Tampa and New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 And then again, as we go into the weekend, those temperatures hang around. But as you can see by Monday, temperatures start to come down a bit to still above averages but at least cooler than they`ve been. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Al, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 As the country awaits the Supreme Court`s ruling on Roe v Wade, many women in states with restrictive new abortion laws are looking for other options even crossing into Mexico in growing numbers. Here`s Morgan Ranford.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For many women, Dr. Franz Theard is their last chance. His New Mexico practice is one of just a few remaining abortion clinics along the U.S.-Mexico border, just one mile west of the Texas State Line.\par \par \uc2 DR. FRANZ THEARD, OWNER, WOMEN`S REPRODUCTIVE CLINIC OF NEW MEXICO: We`ve increased by 100. So, we do about 250 a month now.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Abortions per month.\par \par \uc2 THEARD: Per month.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: That jumped since last September when a Texas law banned abortions as early as six weeks into pregnancy. Before that, the state allowed them up to 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 THEARD: Not doing an abortion because you have six weeks or five weeks is almost like not doing abortions at all.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: But some of those who can`t find the help they`re looking for across state lines are leaving the country entirely.\par \par \uc2 Many American women are coming here to Mexico to buy abortion pills, the most commonly used is not require prescription and costs about a 10th of the price. We`re about to meet two women who provide abortion services out of their home here in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Just outside of Monterrey, activist Vanessa Jimenez and Sandra Cardona founded La Aborteria where they provide the abortion-inducing drug misoprostol and guide women on how to take it. They say they`ve seen a dramatic surge of American clients since the Texas law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 So, before, you were seeing two to three American women a year. Now, you`re seeing five American women a week. And sometimes more?\par \par \uc2 And where are they coming from specifically?\par \par \uc2 So, the majority of women you`re seeing are coming from Texas.\par \par \uc2 And they`re not alone. Another Mexican organization which opened its doors in January says Americans now make up 25 percent of its customers.\par \par \uc2 Can you give me an example? What are the women coming from Texas telling you?\par \par \uc2 It`s fear not only of going to jail, but the economic penalties. And you`re saying even though there hasn`t been a case into the law change in September in Texas, it`s enough to inspire so much fear of fear.\par \par \uc2 A fear that`s driving many women even further from home. Morgan Radford, NBC News, Monterrey, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, with cases of monkey pox on the rise, shortages of vaccine are being reported as health officials voice growing concern.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with the growing monkeypox outbreak. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  saying today there are more than 3000 cases and one death in 48 countries. New York City today began offering the monkeypox vaccine, as Stephanie Gosk reports, the clinic was quickly overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today in New York City, monkeypox vaccines were given out for the first time, only available for those at high risk, gay or bisexual men who have had multiple partners in the last two weeks. But the one clinic in Manhattan giving out shots was overwhelmed by demand, closing its doors to walk-ins by midday.\par \par \uc2 TIM HICKERNELL, ACTOR: I have monkeypox. I was notified by a sexual partner that I had encountered.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Actor Tim Hickernell is suffering from sores and flu-like symptoms.\par \par \uc2 HICKERNELL: What started as three sores became more. And even to this day, I feel like I`m finding ones on my body. Like, today just found one on my hand.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: According to the CDC, there are 173 cases in the U.S. across 25 states. Most cases are mild.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: At this point, the general population should not be worried that monkeypox is going to cause another pandemic. We`re just not there yet.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But high-risk groups are being asked to remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: It could appear as an intimate partner contact is the key component and that you have to have close physical contact.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Monkeypox is not nearly as contagious as COVID. Still the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  is considering declaring a global health emergency, a decision could come by the end of the week. While here in New York Hickernell hopes to help by telling his story.\par \par \uc2 HICKERNELL: I just feel it`s important to educate one another and look out for each other and just keep talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Awareness, say medical experts, will be the best defense Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There`s more to tell you about including complaints against airlines through the roof and the troubling new signs for what millions of travelers might face as we head toward the July 4 holiday.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The summer travel season is heating up and so is frustration. New data out showing complaints against airlines have surged 300 percent above pre-pandemic levels. As Emilie Ikeda reports, the frustrations are growing ahead of the July 4 rush.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For thousands of Americans this week, their summer getaways landing them in an airport Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`ve had multiple delays and chaos.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: More than 2000 flights axed just since yesterday. Travel app Hopper reports more than a quarter of recent flights have been delayed fueled by industry staffing shortages, soaring demand and severe weather.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was in the airport for over five hours, maybe six.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The chaos comes just as airports brace for another flood of travelers July 4 weekend. An anticipated 11 million to pass through airports over the holiday despite the soaring cost of travel. The price to fly spiking 45 percent since 2019.\par \par \uc2 Are we going to see the chaos in the airports continue?\par \par \uc2 HAYLEY BERG, LEAD ECONOMIST, \uc2 HOPPER\uc2 : We are expecting to continue to see significant delays, demand, people booking travel and wanting to travel has well-outpaced airlines rebuilding their capacity.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: To improve reliability, major airlines continued to trend summer schedules. Today, United announcing its cutting 12 percent of daily flights here at Newark.\par \par \uc2 AAA believes the airport frustrations will push more to the road this Independence holiday. According to one study, many are eyeing Las Vegas consider the top destination for July 4.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So we`re going to drive there actually even with the gas prices being so high.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Million sent to crisscross the country next week over a holiday experts fear will be marred by mishaps. Emily Ikeda, NBC News, Newark, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A history-making moment at the Westminster Dog Show. Trumpet, a 4- year-old bloodhound from Illinois becoming the first of his breed to win Best in Show at the 146th annual K9 competition. This year show drew more than 3000 dogs in more than 200 different breeds.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the American swimmer saved by her coach and a remarkable rescue. Our exclusive interview with both coming up.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, tonight, the harrowing moments in a pool for an American swimmer who passed out but is alive thanks to quick action from her coach. Miguel Almaguer spoke with him both in an exclusive interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: United States of America Anita Alvarez.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It was another stunning performance for American artistic swimmer Anita Alvarez, who drew cheers from the crowd and a smile from her coach until a wave of concern washed across the World Aquatics Championship in Budapest, Hungary as Alvarez suddenly plunged into darkness.\par \par \uc2 What`s the last thing you remember?\par \par \uc2 ANITA ALVAREZ, SWIMMER: I remember feeling like it was a really great performance. I started to feel a little bit of numbness in my -- in my fingers and then honestly just kind of everything went black and that was kind of -- kind of it. It all happened really fast.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Alvarez, a two-time Olympian, was in trouble. Slowly and motionlessly the 25-year-old drifted to the bottom of the pool.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA FUENTES, COACH FOR ALVAREZ: As soon as she was going down, immediately I know that something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Sensing Alvarez had fainted, Coach Andrea Fuentes did what stun lifeguards waited to do. Desperately reaching for Alvarez, Fuentes use all of her strength to wrap the swimmer in her arms and lift her to the surface.\par \par \uc2 FUENTES: It`s like in slow motion to me. Like, for Anita, it was fast, but for me it was a year.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Underwater for roughly two minutes, Alvarez was rolled onto the pool deck where she finally started gasping for air.\par \par \uc2 When did you know you`d be OK?\par \par \uc2 ALVAREZ: I think right away I knew I would be OK. I mean as soon as I started breathing and was awake and everything.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Likely fainting because of exhaustion, this rescue was incredibly the second time Alvarez was saved by Fuentes during competition.\par \par \uc2 ALVAREZ: I mean, I say this all the time to her and to other people. I`m just so grateful to have her as a coach.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tonight, the bond of a lifetime and the photo more powerful than words. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: What a drama. I`m so glad she`s OK. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220624ei6n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 19, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Welker, Jo Ling Kent, Richard Engel, Gabe  Gutierrez, Tom Costello, Miguel Almaguer, Cynthia McFadden, Yamiche  Alcindor \par \uc2 3,863 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  chief on the hot seat over the nationwide baby formula shortage.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The fiery hearing on Capitol Hill. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner is saying the formula crisis should begin to ease within days, but it will take a few weeks for supplies to return to normal. A bipartisan anger. Could the Biden administration have acted sooner to help families now in desperate need.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the Senate approving $40 billion of new aid for Ukraine. The bill now going to President Biden to sign. The President today welcoming the leaders of Sweden and Finland to the White House backing their efforts to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but could objections from Turkey derailed their bid.\par \par \uc2 The dramatic courtroom outburst.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey, you`re a coward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Buffalo shooting suspect facing victims` families for the first time.\par \par \uc2 The CDC panel voting to recommend COVID boosters for children 5 to 11. It comes amid a new surge. Cases rising in at least 47 states.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma passing a bill that`s set to become the strictest abortion law in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Stock sliding again today on Wall Street. Growing fears the U.S. is headed for a recession.\par \par \uc2 And record gas prices and soaring airfare. What to know before the busy summer travel season kicks off.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. With the nationwide shortage of baby formula testing parents who are desperate to safely feed their infants, there may be better news to report tonight. A day after the White House announced major new steps to speed supplies of formula, a clearly impatient congressional panel press the head of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  as to when the formula shortage will ease.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Robert Califf\uc2  facing a bipartisan grilling, telling lawmakers within days it will get better, but adding it will be a few weeks until we`re back to normal. And we`ve just learned the first flights carrying formula here from overseas could start as soon as this weekend. Kristen Welker has more.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, good evening. With millions of American families still desperately scrambling to find food for their babies, there was bipartisan outrage on Capitol Hill today directed at the Biden administration, the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , and manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voiceover): Lawmakers on both sides of the aisle grilling the \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): Why did the \uc2 FDA\uc2  not spring into action?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is an emergency that have been way too long.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Commissioner \uc2 Robert Califf\uc2  pressed on the question millions of parents want to know, when will formula be back on shelves.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ROBERT CALIFF\uc2 , COMMISSIONER, \uc2 FDA\uc2 : We should begin to see improvement within days.\par \par \uc2 REP. JULIA LETLOW (R-LA): So, I could tell my constituents that within a matter of days, they`ll be able to find formula on the shelves?\par \par \uc2 CALIFF: Within days, it will get better, but it will be a few weeks before work back to normal.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The Biden administration facing fierce criticism for what many are calling a slow response to the crisis that`s been brewing for months, after Abbott, the nation`s largest formula manufacturer, shut down in Michigan plant in February due to contamination, snarling nationwide supply. A whistleblower raised concerns to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  as far back as October.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY HARRIS (R-MD): It`s hard to see any evidence that \uc2 FDA\uc2  has made it a priority to get this plant cleaned and cleared and back online.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  giving a target date for when the plant could reopen.\par \par \uc2 CALIFF: We had to really rustle this to the ground with Abbott. I think we are on track to get open within the next week to two weeks.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: President Biden taking action invoking the Defense Production Act to speed up the return of formula to store shelves and ordering the Pentagon to direct planes to fly in formula from overseas. The first flight expected to go from Zurich, Switzerland to Plainfield, Indiana could happen as soon as this weekend. But questions raised about whether it will meet \uc2 FDA\uc2  standards.\par \par \uc2 CALIFF: We will not let -- enter formula into the U.S. That is not safe.\par \par \uc2 DELAURO: You don`t have in place a mechanism that would guarantee the safety right now. You just don`t.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The demands for results mounting with stores across the country running out of stock and families running out of options. Kristen Welker, NBC News, the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How did you choose those names?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT\uc2 , NBC NEWS CHANNEL BUSINESS AND TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): I`m Jolene Kent in Austin, Texas. Sidney Galvin has been on a desperate hunt for formula to feed her triplets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These are babies. You know, like, they shouldn`t have to go through this.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Galvin depends on the Women, Infants, and Children nutrition program or WIC for Formula. She was supposed to be able to get nine cans for each baby per month with the benefit. But because of the shortage, she can`t find it. She has been forced to pay hundreds of dollars out of pocket for other formula not covered by WIC.\par \par \uc2 Switching what your feeding is not easy. How are the baby`s doing?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They weren`t tolerating it very well at first, actually. They had a lot of reflux and they were puking a lot of their -- what they were eating up.\par \par \uc2 KENT: To fill the gaps, she`s driven nearly an hour to Mother`s Milk Bank, the largest breast milk donor bank in the country. What is demand been like for the families who can`t get formula?\par \par \uc2 KIM UPDEGROVE, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, MOTHER`S MILK BANK AT AUSTIN: They`ve been calling on a steady basis. These formula fed infants and their families who can`t find formula. That increased percentage is 300 percent. Those families are in crisis.\par \par \uc2 KENT: The lab is working overtime first to screening their donors. Then their milk is pooled, pasteurized, and tested to make sure there`s zero bacteria before being distributed to babies in need. Texas has been hit particularly hard. Formula out of stock rates are at 50 percent here.\par \par \uc2 Abbott Nutrition\uc2  whose voluntary recall and plant closure exacerbated the shortage was the primary infant formula provider for 85 percent of WIC infants in the southwest region. Even though the \uc2 USDA\uc2  has authorized states to allow recipients to purchase alternative formulas, more than half of the states do not tell parents what the substitute formulas are on their website. Sidney just hopes a solution is on the way.\par \par \uc2 How long does one pack of breast milk last for triplets?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It only lasts about five days, to be honest. It`s something, so I`m just like grateful for it.\par \par \uc2 KENT: \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2 , NBC News, Austin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Senate today approved another $40 billion in military and humanitarian aid to Ukraine. And President Biden gave Finland and Sweden strong backing for \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership as Russia tries to tighten its grip on eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Richard Engel has the latest. And we have to warn you, some of the images in this report are quite disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At the White House today, it looked like smooth sailing that Sweden and Finland will become the newest members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  to deter Russia from invading them like it did Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They meet every \uc2 NATO\uc2  requirement, and then some.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: But there`s a catch. Turkey, which like all \uc2 NATO\uc2  members must sign off, is saying no. President Erdogan calling both Sweden and Finland guest houses for terrorists. He`s talking about the Kurds, specifically the Kurdish Workers Party or \uc2 PKK\uc2  and associated Kurdish groups in Syria.\par \par \uc2 Turkey considers them its biggest national security threat and says Finland and Sweden have ignored Turkish extradition requests. U.S. officials in private suspect Turkey wants concessions but will ultimately go along.\par \par \uc2 SAULI NIINISTO, PRESIDENT, FINLAND: We will commit to Turkey`s security just as Turkey we will commit to our security.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: But \uc2 NATO\uc2  squabbles only help Russia, which is tightening its grip on southern Ukraine after its victory in Mariupol. A Ukrainian medic now in Russian custody smuggled helmet camera video she took in March to the Associated Press of Russia`s brutality in Mariupol. Including a moment when Ukrainian doctors tried but failed to save a wounded Ukrainian child.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: There are suggestions tonight from a top Russian official that Russia plans to annex Mariupol and large parts of southern Ukraine. President Putin hasn`t publicly commented. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Richard Engel inside of Ukraine tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The suspect in the supermarket shooting in Buffalo appeared in court today. A prosecutor announcing a grand jury handed up a first-degree murder indictment covering all 10 victims. Today was the first time family members came face to face with a suspect with someone in court yelling to him "you`re a coward."\par \par \uc2 Children ages 5 to 11 will soon be eligible for COVID booster shots as cases keep climbing in much of the country tonight. Gabe Gutierrez with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Late today, CDC advisors voted 11 to one to recommend \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 `s COVID booster shot for children ages 5 to 11 after the \uc2 FDA\uc2  also greenlit it on Tuesday. The booster doses will soon be available to children five months after they received their first two shots.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They have good immune system. I don`t understand why they needed a booster.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Others are asking what`s taking so long for any vaccine for kids under five. The head of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  science and advisory panel will consider Moderna`s application soon.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ROBERT CALIFF\uc2 , COMMISSIONER, \uc2 FDA\uc2 : Children are not just small adults and so you have to do specific studies.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: This as the U.S. sees 100,000 new daily COVID infections for the first time in months even without recording all at home test results. Over the past two weeks, cases nationwide are up 55 percent, but deaths are down almost 20 percent. New York City is back under high COVID alert now recommending mask indoors for people over two, but no mandate.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:14]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s a minor inconvenience. But I think you know if that`s what this is suggesting, you know, might as well take all precautions.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: As the U.S. prepares for wave after wave.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: People that really need to consider getting boosted are any adults over the age of 60. The evidence is clear. And then if you`re between the ages of 50 and 60 and you have other chronic conditions, I would also encourage you to get a booster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): And now, kids ages 5 to 11 can begin getting those booster shots as soon as the CDC director signs off. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Gabe, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The state of Oklahoma is on the verge of adopting the strictest abortion law in the country. State lawmakers passing a bill today that would ban abortion at fertilization with some exceptions to save the life of the mother or in cases of rape and incest that are reported to police. Oklahoma`s Republican governor is expected to sign it.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, can we avoid a recession? Challenges and warning signs as Americans prepare for an expensive summer season. Our in-depth look at an economy facing growing headwinds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There are growing worries tonight about the possibility of the country slipping into recession. As the economy continues to send mixed signals, inflation remains at nearly a 40-year high, prices at the pump hitting especially hard. The Stock Market was down today adding to big losses in recent days. Lower profits at some big retailers still drawing concern. And of course unemployment remains low at 3.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have two views of the economy as we come closer to the start of summer. First here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In North Texas, the Washington family says skyrocketing rent, food, and gas prices are forcing them out of their townhouse. Now looking for an RV to buy as they juggle competing bills for a family of six.\par \par \uc2 LACIE WASHINGTON, RV SHOPPER: Groceries or clothes for my kids. Groceries or electricity.\par \par \uc2 LORNE WASHINGTON, RV SHOPPER: Pay on the car, another pay on rent, or pay rent, pay the utilities.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: And while gas prices have hit new highs, diesel fuel is up a staggering 75 percent in one year, a record. Biting into Pam Polyak`s Milwaukee trucking company that ships food across the Midwest just as her own drivers need cost of living increases. All of it getting passed on to grocery shoppers.\par \par \uc2 PAM POLYAK, OWNER, POLYAK TRUCKING AND LOGISTICS: I see a great tug and pull between costs and expenses and then what we`re getting paid which just means money is going to get tight for everybody.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Now, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is facing one of its toughest challenges in decades, raising interest rates to cool inflation without killing consumer demand and jobs and pushing the economy into recession.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: We need to see inflation coming down in a convincing way. That`s what we need to see.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: But already, \uc2 Equifax\uc2  reports more consumers with low credit scores are falling behind on their credit card payments, car, and personal loans.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN SULLIVAN, CNBC ANCHOR: It is possible that all the experts, all the economists get it wrong and the U.S. economy does avoid a recession. But for that to happen, we need to see prices start to moderate or come down very soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): The very low unemployment rate has been a bright spot. But we`re now seeing some companies announcing hiring slowdowns including Meta \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  announcing layoffs, and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says it is now overstaffed after being on a hiring binge for years. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And so many families feeling that economic pressure with the unofficial start to summer travel just around the corner. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): When nearly 40 million Americans hit the road for Memorial Day, they`ll be cruising into the start of a summer travel season that could be the most expensive and frustrating many have experienced.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:06]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`d never seen anything like this before.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Driving, flying, and lodging now hitting staggering highs with roughly 90 percent of Americans planning a trip in the next six months. Airlines like United call this the strongest travel environment they`ve ever seen. Despite high ticket prices, inventory is low and many flights have been sold out for weeks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh my goodness, the airport was tremendously packed.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: On Memorial Day, with more than 12 million expected to fly, the average domestic airfare will run $400 up 28 percent from the same weekend in 2019. Once you land, a typical hotel room will run $163, up 30 percent from the same holiday weekend last year.\par \par \uc2 Are people still willing to pay top dollar for trips?\par \par \uc2 HAYLEY BERG, LEAD ECONOMIST, HOPPER: We`ve seen demand grow 50 percent faster than it typically does at this time of year. So, the higher prices have not yet slowing down Americans who are looking to travel.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The pinch at the pump may be just as painful. The national average for a gallon of gas just hit a new record high $4.58.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tonight, the soaring cost of summer travel and the price Americans are willing to pay to get away. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, why dozens of election matchups that usually fly under the radar are crucial to the 2024 Presidential Race.\par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Normally, they get little attention, elections for the state officials who run elections. But with many Republican candidates supporting former President Trump`s false claims about the 2020 election, several races for Secretary of State are in the spotlight. Here`s Cynthia McFadden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MCFADDEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL AND INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Detroit, Michigan, the morning after the 2020 presidential vote.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Stop the count! Stop the count! Stop the count!\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Republican poll challengers and others claiming election officials are inside rigging the election in favor of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . NBC`s most recent polling suggests about 38 percent of voters across the country still believe the election was stolen despite audits in 39 states and more than 60 courts confirming the results.\par \par \uc2 One of the key culprits they say, \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2 , Michigan Secretary of State, a former law school dean and election law expert.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been called a traitor. It`s been said that you should be locked up. Some of this would very high up in our government.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE, MICHIGAN: Even the President himself had called on me to be arrested and tried for treason, potentially executed.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: To hear that the President of the United States, when he loses the election in Michigan, decides that the way to deal with it that is to accuse you of treason, to ask isn`t there some way to arrest her?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: We should stop expecting that there is a bottom so the lengths that people will go to overturn legitimate election results and seize power in our country.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: From right inside the White House. A spokesman for the former president denied President Trump ever said such a thing. Despite it all, she`s running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA KARAMO (R-MI), SECRETARY OF STATE CANDIDATE: Who does this woman think she is?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Her opponent, Kristina Karamo, endorsed in this Michigan rally by none other than former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello, Michigan. The presidential election was rigged and stolen. Kristina, come up. Kristina is a true American patriot.\par \par \uc2 KARAMU: President Trump, you inspired so many people to run for office to stand up and fight back.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Karamo declined NBCs requests for an interview, but her podcast lays out some of her positions.\par \par \uc2 KARAMO: Our election cycle of 2020 has been a robbery. The Secretary State is the one that should be under investigation. She should be going to jail.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: This year, voters in 24 states will decide who should run their elections. NBC news reached out to all of them. In 14, an election denier is on the ballot, including in key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: The Big Lie is not just spreading, but it`s deepening its hold on the American people.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Next week`s primary and Georgia is of particular interest as Republican Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  famously stood up to President Trump`s demand he find 11,800 votes is running against election denier Jody Hice\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WALDMAN, PRESIDENT, BRENNAN CENTER: We need these people to call balls and strikes. But if you have an umpire who doesn`t believe baseball exists, that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Michael Waldman runs the nonpartisan Brennan Center.\par \par \uc2 WALDMAN: We need to have some confidence that someone will count the votes. If we can`t have that confidence, it`s very hard to have a real democracy.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Tonight, many like Waldman wondering if those chosen to run the nation`s elections might undermine the very democracy they will swear to defend. Cynthia McFadden, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In a moment, reframing fine art. Inside a groundbreaking new exhibit.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, tonight, the unprecedented exhibit Connecting the past and the present in eye-opening ways. Yamiche Alcindor with the art inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It`s an art exhibit with a lesson.\par \par \uc2 KANITRA FLETCHER, CURATOR: It goes to show how Black people and Black cultures are integral to the Making of the Modern World.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Curator Kanitra Fletcher is describing Afro Atlantic histories in new collection at the prestigious \uc2 National Gallery of Art in Washington\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What`s the importance of having this art in this building, given how different it is to everything else?\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: We want people to understand that work by Black diasporic people is just as treasured and important as the European art that you see in the other galleries in this building.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: More than 130 works meant to take viewers through a global journey from slavery, to emancipation, to the modern fight for civil rights and equality.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: This is the key image of the exhibition. And in this work, they`ve taken on the guise of the Statue of Liberty. They`re thinking about citizenship and sovereignty and who gets to represent nations. Often it`s not a Black person.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: A message that resonates with young artists from the \uc2 Duke Ellington School of the Arts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How did you feel walking through the exhibit?\par \par \uc2 NAA ANYELE SOWAH-DE JESUS, STUDENT, DUKE ELLINGTON SCHOOL OF THE ARTS: I felt empowered. I felt like I was seen, I was heard.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERKO, STUDENT, DUKE ELLINGTON SCHOOL OF THE ARTS: When you go to like regular museum, I don`t see other artists that are like me.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And why is that important to see Black artists that represents you?\par \par \uc2 BERKO: Because it shows that like, I can do the same thing. It`s something I can achieve.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: The art also made them think about the national battle raging over teaching race and history.\par \par \uc2 JAN MOLLAZADE, STUDENT, DUKE ELLINGTON SCHOOL OF THE ARTS: A lot of people want to erase history. And this kind of doesn`t let that happen.\par \par \uc2 SOWAH-DE JESUS: If you know anything about it, is that art survives. And this art will survive and it will prevail.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: A powerful message amid powerful art. Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thanks for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220520ei5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 With Voting Set To Begin, Serious Twist Shaking Up The Country`s Most Closely Watched Primary Election\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Andrea Mitchell \par \uc2 461 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: And we are just hours away from polls opening in Pennsylvania for one of the most anticipated primaries of the Midterms. And there have been some stunning turns in the final stretch including a health scare for the Democratic front runner. Here`s Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With one day to go, some shocking last-minute twists in the high stakes Pennsylvania Senate race. Democratic front runner John Fetterman revealing he suffered a stroke.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: It was on Friday. I just wasn`t feeling very well. So, I decided you know what, I need to get checked out.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Still in the hospital, Fetterman says he`s feeling better and he`s on his way to a full recovery, but will not attend his election night rally tomorrow. On the Republican side, after surging into a three-way tie, conservative commentator Kathy Barnette dealing with images from January 6 of her marching alongside Proud Boys who were later arrested and indicted. Barnette says she did not enter the Capitol and has no connection to the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 Helping fuel her rise, the leaked Supreme Court draft that could overthrow Roe v. Wade with a new NBC News poll showing voters focus on abortion now on the rise.\par \par \uc2 How important do you think abortion is to voters here in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it should be the number one issue for Pennsylvania voters at the moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It really pains me to see so many abortions that are taking place.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Republican politicians aren`t talking very much about abortion as an issue. But Republican women voters sure are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If a country loses their value for life, then I think we`re lost for good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It scares me that it`s a bunch of old men that get to dictate what the majority of the people don`t want.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do I think abortion should be outlawed? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Other mothers in both parties still want a choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Women should have the right to their own body.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t think that folks that, you know, don`t agree with it should be making decisions about, you know, my body.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: A lightning rod issue that could have a major impact at the polls tomorrow. Andrea Mitchell, NBC News Harleysville, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, our exclusive with a passenger who landed that plane when the pilot couldn`t. How he stayed focused through it all.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220518ei5g0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 16, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kate Snow, Miguel Almaguer, Jo Ling Kent, Andrea  Mitchell, Sam Brock \par \uc2 3,760 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, our special coverage from Buffalo, the deadly hate-fueled mass shooting at a supermarket.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): New in the investigation, authorities saying the 18-year-old accused of killing 10 at the store in a predominantly Black neighborhood was previously spotted in Buffalo back in March. And witnesses telling me they saw him at the store the day before the shooting. It`s being investigated as a racist hate crime.\par \par \uc2 Police confirming he posted a 180-page racist rant. Were wanting signs missed? And the 10 victims, we are learning much more about them tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  opening another hate crime investigation into the shooting at a California church. One killed, five injured, all members of the Taiwanese community. Policing the shooter chain the doors and open fire. How brave churchgoers subdue him.\par \par \uc2 The major baby formula maker reaching a deal with the FDA to reopen a plant that was closed down over safety concerns. How soon could it impact the shortage?\par \par \uc2 Tracking severe storms along the east coast. Tens of millions on alert.\par \par \uc2 And our exclusive with the passenger who landed a plane with no experience. What went through his mind when the pilot suddenly fell ill?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt reporting tonight from Buffalo, New York.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening from Buffalo. A still proud but deeply wounded community asking tonight why us. Why a teenager traveled hundreds of miles to this city, to this block, to this grocery store, to allegedly seek out black people to kill.\par \par \uc2 Today, we learned the gun attack that killed 10 and injured three may not have gone entirely as planned. Police believe the shooter pumped up on false and racist conspiracy theories intended to head down the street and shoot more people. Thankfully he was stopped and arrested.\par \par \uc2 It came on the same weekend as a mass shooting at a Southern California church, an attack on Taiwanese church members being investigated tonight as a hate crime that was politically motivated. We`ll have more on that in a few moments, but let`s begin here with a pain remembrances and fear in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Tonight, Buffalo still struggling to come to terms with how someone could turn a supermarket into a terror target and if warning signs were missed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Three down inside so far, two outside.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Police say the 18-year-old gunman had a disturbing plan when he stormed into this Tops grocery store Saturday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Send as many cars as you possibly can.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A plan fueled by racist hatred.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA, COMMISSIONER, BUFFALO POLICE DEPARTMENT: This is an absolute racist hate crime. This is someone who has hate in their heart, soul and mind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This might be an individual in body armor.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Police say the suspect was wearing full body armor and tactical gear had driven 200 miles to get here and was live-streaming the horror online. This video showing the alleged shooter`s arrest at the scene. Tonight authorities saying the suspect may have been casing the location.\par \par \uc2 GRAMAGLIA: The individual was here a few months ago back in early March.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Grady Lewis was across the street Saturday and saw the gunman opened fire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m seeing a guy in camouflage just shooting, shooting, shooting. And I`m seeing a security guy running to the store.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Lewis told me he saw the suspect at the store the day before the attack. And so did Veronica Love and her son Daniel who worked across the street.\par \par \uc2 Where was he?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Next door in front of my barbershop, he was sitting right in front of -- right in front just using the WiFi.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Like so many here, they are still stunned by the nightmare the gunman unleashed.\par \par \uc2 You could have been over there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I could have been over there. I frequently go over there. My daughter was -- had to list for her Sunday meal.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In court this weekend, this suspect pleaded not guilty, while tonight there are new details on the investigation. Authorities have interviewed the suspect`s parents and confirmed the suspect posted a 180-page racist rant, including saying he targeted the neighborhood because it had the most Black people.\par \par \uc2 And NBC News learning this is not the first time the suspect came in contact with law enforcement. Last June, police were called after he allegedly threatened to carry out a shooting at his high school. He was given a mental health evaluation and released.\par \par \uc2 GRAMAGLIA: There was nothing picked up on the State Police intelligence, nothing that was picked up on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  intelligence.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And tonight, this community is remembering 10 victims from ages 32 to 86 killed while they shopped on a Saturday afternoon. Authorities call retired Buffalo Police Officer Aaron Salter a hero. He was the security guard on duty firing multiple times at the suspect.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:03]\par \par \uc2 JOHN GARCIA, SHERIFF, ERIE COUNTY, NEW YORK: I can`t say enough about our retired fellow colleague Aaron Salter who confronted this individual to save the lives of others.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Family members remembering 65-year-old Celestine Cheney.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA JONES, GRANDDAUGHTER OF CELESTINE CHANEY: She loves everyone, anyone. Even my friends I`ve been around, they make me feel like -- she makes like at home. And that`s -- she`s home. Like, that`s what my grandma is.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And today, I talked to the family of Andre MacNeil who was killed while he was shopping for a birthday cake for his 3-year-old son.\par \par \uc2 MALIK ELLIOTT, BROTHER OF ANDRE MACNEIL: We are -- was getting ready for a birthday party. And that`s why Andre went to Tops to get a birthday cake, a birthday cake.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: How did you find out the news?\par \par \uc2 ELLIOTT: I couldn`t -- I can`t imagine how I found out the news. I was laying on my couch praying for the city. The last thing my brother told me on the phone was I`m one hour away. I`m one hour away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Pain goes deep here. Let`s turn now to more on the hate crime investigation unfolding right now. In his rant online, the suspect claimed he was radicalized on social media sites where experts say extremists often flocked to freely share their racist views. Kate Snow has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He came into my community with hate. He drove hours to come here and do what he did.\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Zenaida Everhart`s (PH) 20-year-old son Zaire (PH) was working at Tops helping an older woman with her groceries in the parking lot when he was shot in the neck and the woman was shot and killed in front of him.\par \par \uc2 Incredibly Zaire survived. His mom also happens to be Director of Diversity and Inclusion for a New York State Senator.\par \par \uc2 This young man apparently had a document that he put up online. He broadcast part of it live.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I think the first thing that we have to do is talk about it and stop acting like there is not racism in this country.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Asian American women were targeted in Spa shootings in Atlanta in 2021, Mexican shoppers at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in El Paso and 2019, Jewish worshippers were targeted at a synagogue in Pittsburgh in 2018, Black parishioners at the Mother Emanuel AME Church in Charleston in 2015. The shooter in Buffalo etched references to previous shootings on his weapons.\par \par \uc2 In a racist rant, he claimed he was radicalized on 4chan and repeatedly cites the false unfounded great replacement theory, which suggests Jewish elites are bringing people of color into the U.S. to replace whites. Scott Richman with the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2  says it`s easy to find on social media sites that don`t have moderation policies.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT RICHMAN, WESTCHESTER, NEW YORK REGIONAL DIRECTOR, \uc2 ANTI-DEFAMATION LEAGUE\uc2 : We`re talking about Discord. We`re talking about Gab and Parler and telegram. Sites like that that are frequented by extremists.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And they don`t have rules about what can be said.\par \par \uc2 RICHMAN: People can put whatever they want on there.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Senior law enforcement officials say the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is seeing a growing number of racially motivated extremism incidents fed by social media and also gaming platforms. The Buffalo shooter live-streamed his attack on Twitch before it was taken down. But that video is still circulating, an outrage for Garner Whitfield who lost his mother Ruth on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our matriarch, our hero, our angel was taken from us inappropriately. It`s hurtful but it makes us angry also.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: He and attorney Benjamin Crump say the shooter isn`t the only one who should be held accountable. They`re considering action against social media companies.\par \par \uc2 BEN CRUMP, ATTORNEY: But we also help the whole accountable the individuals who curate this hate, who are indoctrinating these young people with this hate.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Hate that hurts so deeply.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kate, what are the social media companies saying about all this?\par \par \uc2 SNOW (on camera): So, tonight, we heard back from a couple of them. Telegram tells us that they -- that content that promotes violence is forbidden and that they are working to remove anything related to the shooting here. We also heard from Discord. They say, hate and violence have no place on their site. They say they`re assisting police, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Kate Snow, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And we know much more tonight about the suspect in that deadly hate crime attack at a church in Southern California and how its members became heroes by acting on their own to prevent more bloodshed. Miguel Almaguer now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, investigators say the mass shooting on a Taiwanese congregation in Laguna Woods was a hate-filled attack. Deputies say the gunman had chained the exit shut here, sealing some locks with superglue before unleashing a spray of gunfire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The shooter has been mitigated and is no longer a threat.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The 68-year-old suspect is a U.S. citizen born in China who authority say sought out the Taiwanese group specifically calling it a politically motivated hate incident. During the attack, police a 52-year- old Dr. John Chang rushed the gunman, jamming his weapon, allowing other parishioners to hog tie the suspects legs with an extension cord. Dr. Chang was shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:26]\par \par \uc2 DON BARNES, SHERIFF, ORANGE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA: Without the actions of Dr. Chang, it is no doubt that there have been numerous additional victims in this crime.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Armed with two handguns and a bag of Molotov cocktails, the sheriff says the shooter wounded five others before he was taken down.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A man go through their church, and then shoot, shoot them.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The victims ranged in age from 52 to 92.\par \par \uc2 The suspect, a Las Vegas security guard, drove 275 miles to get here. In his car, investigators say they found notes showing hatred of Taiwanese people.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Majority of the people in attendance were elderly, and they acted spontaneously, heroically.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Members of the church had greeted the suspect warmly when he arrived unaware he would target them moments later. Tonight, bloodshed staining a house of worship where fellow church members helped each other survive. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News, Laguna Woods, California.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, Ukrainian authorities say that more than 260 soldiers have been evacuated in buses from that steel plant in Mariupol that was surrounded by Russian forces they were taken to towns controlled by Russia, including 53 seriously wounded soldiers brought to a medical facility, hundreds more remain in the plant.\par \par \uc2 And there is an important development tonight of the nationwide shortage of baby formula. Abbott one of the major producers reaching an agreement with the FDA to reopen a key manufacturing plant. \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  now that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT\uc2 , NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, possible signs of hope for millions of parents nationwide.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Look at this.\par \par \uc2 KENT: As the FDA struck an agreement with \uc2 Abbott Nutrition\uc2  on next steps to reopen the formula factory in Sturgis, Michigan. Abbott CEO saying we know millions of parents and caregivers depend on us. And we`re deeply sorry that our voluntary recall worsened the nationwide formula shortage.\par \par \uc2 If the FDA and a federal court give Abbott the green light, it would take two weeks to reopen and another six to eight weeks to get that formula on shelves. The plant has been shuttered since February over bacteria possibly linked to infant illnesses. Abbott says there`s no conclusive evidence linking that formula to those sicknesses.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT CALIFF, COMMISSIONER, FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION: We`re taking a number of measures, including getting all the manufacturers to step up getting the Sturgis plan up and going.\par \par \uc2 KENT: The monthslong shortage is hitting low-income families disproportionately hard.\par \par \uc2 ELOIZA OBREGON, SIT SUPERVISOR, WIC PROGRAM: We definitely go home with little hole in our heart.\par \par \uc2 KENT: After the White House proposed loosening restrictions last week, many parents who rely on government assistance from the Women Infants and Children program known as WIC are still unable to track down formula.\par \par \uc2 How frustrating is it when you issue a benefit to go get formula and there`s no formula on the shelf?\par \par \uc2 OBREGON: There was really ever I think -- we always issued a formula thinking I really hope this participant can find some formula. That`s the biggest strain on our staff.\par \par \uc2 KENT: By one estimate, up to 68 percent of all infant formula sold in the U.S. is purchased through WIC.\par \par \uc2 How much do you worry about the babies?\par \par \uc2 OBREGON: That is our biggest worry. Our biggest worry is that babies get sick, that babies are not able to tolerate different formulas, that babies are being offered one formula one week and another formula another week.\par \par \uc2 KENT: This shortage has charitable organizations like baby to baby stepping into help distributing the formula they have to hospitals, foster care organizations, and shelters.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SAWYER PATRICOF, CO-FOUNDER, BABY2BABY: In 2022, we have requests for over eight million cancer formula. So, we`re not going to meet that.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Baby2Baby has direct relationships with smaller formula makers but say even that is not enough.\par \par \uc2 NORAH WEINSTEIN, CO-FOUNDER, BABY2BABY: Know that is such a long road ahead. And so, I think we`re rightfully nervous.\par \par \uc2 KENT: An immediate crisis requiring a new long-term solution. Joe Ling Kent, NBC News Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, with voting set to begin tomorrow, serious twist shaking up the country`s most closely watched primary election.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It has been burning for five weeks, a huge wildfire, though authorities now say it`s the largest in New Mexico`s history as well as the biggest current fire in the country. The Calf Canyon/Hermits Peak Fire has burned almost 300,000 acres, more than 460 square miles with about 260 homes destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are watching dangerous storms on the move tonight along the east coast. 52 million people in the risk zone from North Carolina to New Hampshire where wind gusts is up to 70 miles an hour, large hail and isolated tornadoes. And numerous reports are coming in of downed trees and wires across Pennsylvania and upstate New York.\par \par \uc2 And we are just hours away from polls opening in Pennsylvania for one of the most anticipated primaries of the Midterms. And there have been some stunning turns in the final stretch including a health scare for the Democratic front runner. Here`s Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With one day to go, some shocking last-minute twists in the high stakes Pennsylvania Senate race. Democratic front runner John Fetterman revealing he suffered a stroke.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: It was on Friday. I just wasn`t feeling very well. So, I decided you know what, I need to get checked out.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Still in the hospital, Fetterman says he`s feeling better and he`s on his way to a full recovery, but will not attend his election night rally tomorrow. On the Republican side, after surging into a three-way tie, conservative commentator Kathy Barnette dealing with images from January 6 of her marching alongside Proud Boys who were later arrested and indicted. Barnette says she did not enter the Capitol and has no connection to the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 Helping fuel her rise, the leaked Supreme Court draft that could overthrow Roe v. Wade with a new NBC News poll showing voters focus on abortion now on the rise.\par \par \uc2 How important do you think abortion is to voters here in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it should be the number one issue for Pennsylvania voters at the moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It really pains me to see so many abortions that are taking place.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Republican politicians aren`t talking very much about abortion as an issue. But Republican women voters sure are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If a country loses their value for life, then I think we`re lost for good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It scares me that it`s a bunch of old men that get to dictate what the majority of the people don`t want.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do I think abortion should be outlawed? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Other mothers in both parties still want a choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Women should have the right to their own body.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t think that folks that, you know, don`t agree with it should be making decisions about, you know, my body.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: A lightning rod issue that could have a major impact at the polls tomorrow. Andrea Mitchell, NBC News Harleysville, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, our exclusive with a passenger who landed that plane when the pilot couldn`t. How he stayed focused through it all.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: He has been hailed as a hero for landing a plane when the pilot suffered a medical emergency. Well, now, for the first time, that brave passenger is sharing his heart-pounding story in a "TODAY`S SHOW" exclusive. Here`s Sam Brock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DARREN HARRISON, PASSENGER: Either you do what you have to do to control the situation or you`re going to die. And that`s what I did.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: There`s no time to panic.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: No, none.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For Darren Harrison, the circumstances were clearly stacked against him.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: All my GPS have gone out. I have no idea where I am. And then it was, well, what do you see? I see the state of Florida and I see a small airport.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: In this exclusive interview with "TODAY`S" Savannah Guthrie, the man with a career in flooring sales explains how we became impromptu pilot when the real pilot passed out of the controls.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: I reached over his body because he`s at this point unresponsive. He`s kind of put my arm over toward my elbow sitting here and I grabbed the controls of the airplane and slowly started to pull back on the stick and turn.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: How did you know how to do that?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Just common sense, I guess being on airplanes.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Any sudden yank, Harrison says, would cause the plane to stall. The pilot is suffering what we later learned was a heart-related aneurysm. And the 39-year-old Harrison knew their collective fate laid his hands as he reflected on his wife and the daughter they`re expecting.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Did you think about your little girl?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: I did. Oh yes. It`s one of the first things that went through my mind as I was always climbing to the front and that plane was going to dive. I was just looking going I can`t die today. I`ve got -- Brittany is pregnant, I got baby on the way, today is not my day.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: In a story layered with obstacle after obstacle, Harrison`s heart and faith found the flight path to survival. Sam Brock, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: I`m glad we were hearing his story.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, some final thoughts from here in Buffalo after this latest mass shooting in America.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, from yet another American cities scarred by racially motivated violence we have come to know all too well, overwhelming feelings of sadness and frustration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Almost immediately, they began rolling off our tongues, the others, Charleston, El Paso, Pittsburgh, a well-worn path of racially fueled anger and violence. We knew there would be more. and so it`s hard to describe what we`re feeling today as shock. What we are is sad, we are angry. We are tired, so tired.\par \par \uc2 And hardest to say, we feel helpless because we are somehow unable to defuse the ticking time bombs among us. The individuals who adopt a twisted logic in a language of madness, brooding, rationalizing, and seeing those unlike themselves as the enemy and they are capable of violence.\par \par \uc2 Their danger and intent always seems crystal clear after the fact when the shooting stops. But we`re burdened by the lingering question, how do we find them before it starts?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself at each other. Good night from Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220517ei5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Officials In Buffalo, New York Are Calling The Deadly Attack On Shoppers And Employees At A Supermarket There A Racially Motivated Hate Crime\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Jesse Kirsch \par \uc2 2,795 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I`m taking inflation very seriously.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s been very frustrating.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve got a serious situation here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s scary not knowing if you are going to be able to feed your kid.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, SUNDAY TODAY, HOST: Good morning and welcome to "Sunday TODAY" on this May 15th. I`m Willie Geist.\par \par \uc2 This morning, officials in Buffalo, New York are calling the deadly attack on shoppers and employees at a supermarket there a racially motivated hate crime. An 18-year-old alleged gunman is being held without bail charged with murder in the killing of 10 people with three more injured. A security guard inside the supermarket this morning being called a hero after he was fatally shot while trying to stop the attack. We will have the latest in a live report and get new details when we speak with the local district attorney just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Then, Savannah Guthrie`s exclusive interview with Darren Harrison, the passenger you probably heard about this week, who had no flight experience but successfully took over the controls and landed a plane in South Florida after the pilot fell unconscious. We will hear the full story of what happened that day from the hero himself coming up.\par \par \uc2 But let`s begin this morning in Buffalo where an 18-year-old is being held without bond charged with killing 10 people inside a grocery store filled with shoppers. Police say the attack was motivated by race. NBC Jesse Kirsch is outside of the supermarket in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 Jesse, Good morning. What is the latest there?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Willie, this morning, the grocery store behind me, which became a killing field, is still a crime scene. There is a small memorial off to the side here in an area that was allegedly targeted by someone specifically looking for a lot of black people. Police say that this suspect showed up here in tactical gear and shattered what was a peaceful day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): This morning, Buffalo, New York, reeling after police say a gunman went on a racist grocery store rampage, shooting 13 people, killing 10.\par \par \uc2 BYRON BROWN, BUFFALO MAYOR: We are hurting, and we are siege seething right now as a community.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Authorities say all but two of the victims were African-American.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GARCIA, ERIE COUNTY SHERIFF: This was pure evil. It was straight up racially motivated hate crime.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): The aftermath unvarnished tragedy. First responders seen here next to an apparently lifeless body.\par \par \uc2 CARIOL HORNE, WITNESS: I saw a lot of people who were in distress. They didn`t know if their loved ones were OK.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Police say, the attack began around 2:30 p.m. Saturday. But the suspect opening fire in the parking lot of a \uc2 Tops Friendly Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DISPATCH: Shots fired. Send as many cars as you possibly can.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Then moving inside the store where a security guard fought back. That former Buffalo police officer being called a hero.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA, BUFFALO POLICE COMMISSIONER: He fired multiple shots. Struck the suspect. But because he had heavily armored plating on, that bullet had no round. The suspect engaged our retired officer, and he was ultimately shot and deceased at the scene.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Police say, the attacker finally surrendered after putting a gun to his neck and then, being persuaded by officers to drop it.\par \par \uc2 PAYTON GENDRON, SUSPECT: Yes, sir. I understand my charges.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Prosecutors say 18-year-old Payton Gendron of Conklin, New York is the man responsible, charged with one count of murder in the first degree. Authorities say he traveled hours to Buffalo armed with an assault weapon. Three senior law enforcement sources tell NBC News it appears the N word was etched into one of multiple weapons recovered.\par \par \uc2 Gendron allegedly wrote a manifesto inspired by white supremacist ideology, to which said, the rampage was briefly live streamed on its platform before the feed was removed. The tragedy marks the fourth mass shooting at a U.S. grocery store since March 2021. And it`s the second rampage to shake New York in just over a month.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Yes, I`m angry. I`ve seen violence from guns on the Brooklyn subway and now, in the streets of Buffalo. It has to stop.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voiceover): Tops Friendly Market sharing a statement saying in part, we are shocked and deeply saddened by this senseless act of violence and our thoughts and prayers are with the victims and their families.\par \par \uc2 Yet another American community now grappling with the unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We can`t let an evil person divide this community and an evil person divide our country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (on camera): A senior law enforcement official tells NBC News authorities are looking at the alleged manifesto filled with racist and anti-Semitic tropes. That potential disturbing manifesto also claims Gendron was radicalized on an extremist from because he was bored during the start of the pandemic, just part of the investigation into an allegedly heinous crime. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: At once tragic and infuriating. Jesse Kirsch in Buffalo for us this morning. Jesse, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 John Flynn is the district attorney for Buffalo`s Erie County. He charged him with first degree murder overnight.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Flynn, thank you so much for taking some time with us this morning. The hearts of the country are with you this morning. As I said, there is rage and we saw it from the law enforcement officials yesterday about what happened there.\par \par \uc2 Let me start by asking you about motive. We are hearing about this manifesto online filled with racist and anti-Semitic language. We are hearing about the gun. We have seen the images of it with racist language there. The sheriff was quick to say this is a straight up racially motivated hate crime, his words. What is the motive so far as you can us tell this morning?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FLYNN, ERIE COUNTY (NY) DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Well, Willie, there`s evidence that appears that we have ascertained right now that show that the motive potentially is race related. You know, right now, he has been charged with murder in the first degree under New York State law where he has allegedly killed multiple people.\par \par \uc2 There`s also a provision in the murder in the first-degree statute that talks about an act of terrorism. That the terrorism mechanism and the hate crime mechanism in New York State law are not actually, per se, crimes of themselves. They are enhancers, for lack of a better word.\par \par \uc2 So, if you are charged with a hate crime in New York, you have to have an underlying crime and then, the hate crime aspect would be an enhancer. It`s the same with the terrorism charge. So, you know, right now, he has been charged already with the highest count that we have in New York State, which is murder in the first degree\par \par \uc2 So, what we are looking at now are basically just potentially additional charges related to racial and terrorism that we could add on.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: So, was he known to police, Mr. Flynn? There`s some reporting out there that he had threatened to shoot up his high school not long ago. Was he on the radar of law enforcement as far as you can know?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Well, he was not on the radar of law enforcement here in Buffalo. He was not on the radar that I know of to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . You know, it`s my understanding that there was an alleged incident where he is from, which is in Broome County, which is some three and a half hours from Buffalo where there was some incident last year where he made some threats. You know, I`m aware of that. We are looking into that. We are going to, obviously, contact Broome County and find out what exactly happened, which could kind of help our case in the terms of, you know, his intent and what his thinking was when he came here to Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: So, before I let you go, I must ask you about the security guard, the retired Buffalo police officer who stood in the doorway, literally, and exchanged gunfire with this man who killed 10 people yesterday. Heroically. Probably saved a bunch of lives. What more can you tell us about him?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Well, I can tell you, he is a hero. We knew he was a hero before yesterday. He is a retired Buffalo police officer, you know, who spent the better part of his life defending the citizens of Buffalo. When he retired from the Police Department, he took a job as a security guard at this Tops store. A Tops store that I know well. You know, down the street from the Tops store is a cigar shop that I`m frequent. So, I`m very familiar with this area and this Tops store.\par \par \uc2 And he is a hero, like you said, Willie. He used his weapon yesterday and exchanged gunfire, again, allegedly. The defendant was wearing tactical gear. So, you know, he was not injured. But, again, as you said, he is a hero, has been a hero for a number of years in this community.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Without question. Erie County District Attorney John Flynn who has charged the allege gunman with first degree murdered and may, as he says, add hate crimes as we go along in this investigation. Thank you very much for your time this morning, sir. And please send our sympathies and love to your community this morning. We are all feeling for you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: President Biden meanwhile spoke out overnight about the shooting saying, "hate must have no safe harbor." NBC`s Josh Lederman is traveling with the president near his home in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Josh, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Willie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 President Biden issuing a statement just before midnight, praising law enforcement for their bravery in responding to this incident. The president calling it an incident of domestic terrorism and a hate and a hate crime and says, hate must have no safe harbor.\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And now, the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  says, federal prosecutors will investigate this as a hate crime. Now, the shooting took place just a day after Mr. Biden warned about a potential summer surge in gun violence as he implored the use of leftover COVID funds, about $10 billion, to plus up police departments.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the president heading back to Washington where he is going to be speaking at a memorial service for fallen police officers. He may have a chance at that event to address this crisis, which comes as President Biden has been trying to show he is focused on addressing gun violence, even as gun violence advocacy groups criticize the president for not making this a priority. They say that just since President Biden was inaugurated, there have been more than 850 mass shootings in America. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We will see if that focus sharpens now after the incident in Buffalo. Josh Lederman with the president in Wilmington, Delaware. Josh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of people yesterday took to the streets across the country to march in support of abortion rights. The demonstrations come in response to the leak earlier this month of that draft opinion from the Supreme Court that suggests the court is prepared to overturn Roe v. Wade. A new NBC News poll out this morning shows stronger support than ever for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Todd is NBC`s political director and moderator of "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 Chuck, good morning.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re going to talk through that NBC News polling this morning on abortion as we look to the midterms, perhaps, as Josh said, the president will turn his focus as well to gun rights, inflation also on the table. But what did you find in the poll?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MEET THE PRESS MODERATOR: Well, look, I think what you saw on some aspects, not a shock, with the economy and it continues to hurt President Biden. His numbers now, his personal numbers have now fallen to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  level. And I say fallen because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has never improved his standing, the public. There is no buyer`s remorse, but I think it just shows you the fact that Biden`s numbers have fallen there. I think it shows you how polarizing he has become at this point.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at the abortion issues in here, we measured the abortion issue in here, we measured it a number of ways. What you see is, you know, without this issue over Roe v. Wade being overturned, it would look like the Republicans were basically on a straight-line path to winning both the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 But all of a sudden, what you see here with abortion rights, you see one in five voters saying, this is a single-issue vote for them and they mostly fall, 70 percent, on the choice side of this. You see suddenly Democrats a lot more interested in the midterms now than they were two months ago by a plus 13 points.\par \par \uc2 So, what it is, is you see a leveling -- it`s not a level playing field, a leveling of the playing field between the two parties right now due to this abortion rights issue.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And then, we may see that ruling coming up in a matter of weeks. Chuck, thanks so much. We will look for much more this morning on "Meet the Press" when Chuck will have special coverage of the tragedy in Buffalo. Among Chuck`s guests, New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  led a congressional delegation on a surprise trip to Ukraine on Saturday. NBC`s Erin McLaughlin is in Kyiv with more.\par \par \uc2 Erin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Willie.\par \par \uc2 That`s right. On Saturday, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  arriving here in Kyiv with three other senior Republican lawmakers, meeting with President Zelenskyy as well as other Ukrainian officials as the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is trying to push through that massive $40 billion aid package that Ukraine says it badly needs to win this war. During the visit, President Zelenskyy the lawmakers for their support.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, what`s being seen as a massive morale boost here in Ukraine, last night`s Kalush Orchestra, the Ukrainian rap band won the Eurovision 2022 song contest with their song called "Stefania," which was originally written for the lead singer`s mother, but has since become a war anthem of sorts.\par \par \uc2 After their performance, the band thanking the audience for their support, calling for more support for Ukraine as well as the devastated Port City of Mariupol. President Zelenskyy congratulating the band, saying that Ukraine will host next year`s Eurovision song contest. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: All right. Erin McLaughlin in Kyiv, Ukraine again for us this morning. Erin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now and get a quick check of your local Sunday weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 GEIST: -- honor bestowed by Ukrainian President Zelenskyy upon a two-year- old Jack Russell terrier.\par \par \uc2 And the budding grade school track phenom who became a viral star this week after she lost her shoe, went back for it, and still came back to dust the field.\par \par \uc2 But up next, our "Sunday Focus" on the story that captured the country this week, a heroic passenger with no flight experience taking over and landing a small plane after his pilot had a medical emergency. We have Savannah Guthrie`s exclusive new interview with the man himself, Darren Harrison.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Everyone has been amazed at how calm you were. Did you feel calm?\par \par \uc2 DARREN HARRISON, HERO PASSENGER: I did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s all coming up on "Sunday TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220517ei5f0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for May 15, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Al Sharpton, Al Cardenas, Ashley Parker, Emilie Ikeda,  Al Sharpton \par \uc2 8,938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:17]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC ANCHOR (voice over): This Sunday, mass shooting in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED BUFFALO POLICE DISPATCHER: Shots heard. Send as many cars as you possibly can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ten people dead, nine of them African-American, in what appears to be a racially motivated shooting.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: People in a supermarket shopping, and bullets raining down on them.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This was pure evil. It was a straight-up racially motivated hate crime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ll get the latest from the scene. And I`ll talk to Buffalo Mayor Byron Brown and to New York`s governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, the economy and the midterms.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: Right now, America is fighting on two fronts. At home, it`s inflation and rising prices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: With costs going up, outpacing wage growth.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Now I could go in a grocery store and spend $60, and I`m looking like, "What did I purchase?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: Our brand-new NBC News poll shows flashing warning signs for Democrats, but Republican leaders worry some of their candidates are too out of the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Two men sleeping together; two men holding hands; two men caressing, that is not normal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guest this morning, the independent Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  of Vermont.\par \par \uc2 Also, abortion politics.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Safe, legal abortion now! Safe, legal abortion now!\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thousands march nationwide in favor of abortion rights...\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I can`t believe we`re going back to this. And it`s a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... as many states move to restrict those rights in anticipation of a Supreme Court decision that overturns Roe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are Washington Post White House bureau chief Ashley Parker; the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , host of "Politics Nation" on MSNBC; Republican strategist Al Cardenas; Washington bureau chief for USA Today Susan Page; and \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  contributing columnist Matt Bai.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history. This is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, it`s happened again, a horror all too familiar in the United States. This time, the scene of what appears to have been an act of domestic terrorism was a supermarket in Buffalo, New York. A white teenager, a boy, really, only 18, wearing body armor and carrying an assault rifle, opened fire at a supermarket in an African-American neighborhood. Ten people were killed, nine of them black. And three others were wounded, two of them black.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the gunman was captured, a racist manifesto he apparently posted emerged online, filled with racist and Antisemitic views, including the theory that whites are being replaced by people of color.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say the gunman, who drove some 200 miles to carry out the slaughter, also live-streamed the massacre with a camera apparently on his helmet.\par \par \uc2 It was the latest in recent mass shootings aimed at ethnic groups: Charleston, South Carolina, African-Americans in church; Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, Jewish victims in a synagogue; El Paso, Texas, Latino victims at a \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2 . And now we add Buffalo, New York, to the list. We`ve also, of course, seen a rise of hate crimes against Asian-Americans as well.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, I`m going to talk to Buffalo`s mayor, Byron Brown, and to New York`s governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . But we`re going to begin our coverage with our NBC News correspondent Emilie Ikeda, who is in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 And, Emily, where are we on the investigation? I know the gunman was arraigned. What`s next?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Chuck. Investigators are looking into this as a hate crime and racially motivated violent extremism. And there`s a number of signs pointing and explaining why, one, 13 people were shot between the parking lot behind me and the supermarket, 11 of those were black.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are also looking into an apparent manifesto that claims that the suspect purposefully targeted this area of Buffalo because of the number of black people here.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I`ll point out, take a look at these images of what sources tell us are the suspect`s weapons. They are blurred, and that is because there are messages of hate on them, racial slurs. And the other thing I`ll mention we`re learning more about this morning are the victims. These are people who were partaking in one of the most typical, normal activities on a Saturday afternoon, shopping in the supermarket, the governor telling me one of the people was simply trying to buy cupcakes for a birthday party. Another person shot and killed was the security guard. He tried to stop the suspect.\par \par \uc2 Here`s more from officials on that.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA, BUFFALO POLICE COMMISSIONER: One of the individuals inside the store is a security guard, a beloved security guard, who was a retired buffalo police officer, a hero in our eyes, engaged the suspect, fired multiple shots, struck the suspect. But because he had heavily armed -- armored plating on, that bullet had no round. The suspect engaged our retired officer and he was ultimately shot and deceased at the scene.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:13]\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The suspect -- suspect is being identified as 18-year-old Payton Gendron. He did appear in court last night. He`s charged with first-degree murder. If convicted, he could face up to life in prison. We`ll see him appear in court later this week on Thursday. Still very much a developing situation, Chuck, but we expect later this morning to learn more information on both the victims and what led up and what happened in this just absolutely horrific tragedy. This community is shaken to its core, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we obviously want to learn more about his family`s situation as well. Emily, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is the mayor of Buffalo, Byron Brown.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mayor, welcome to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BYRON BROWN, (D), BUFFALO, NEW YORK: Thank you very much, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I know this is an excruciating time to be mayor, and that the city`s in a lot of pain here. What can you tell me about the latest in the investigation?\par \par \uc2 You know, all of it is still under -- apparently racially motivated; it certainly seems like we`re getting close to dropping the "apparently."\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We are getting closer to dropping that. Law enforcement has been working together around the clock since this occurred, at every level, federal, state, county, city, and pieced together very quickly that this was a racially motivated attack. The individual that committed this crime drove from several hours away. They were not from this community. And they drove here with the express purpose of taking black lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Mr. Mayor, to, sort of, see this, we have this toxic stew of this growing right-wing ideology, easy access to guns and our permissive Internet culture that, sort of, rewards sharing some of this violent ideas.\par \par \uc2 Where do we go first? How do we -- how do we -- how do you make African- Americans in Buffalo feel safe today? Let me start there.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, the thing that this tragedy shows us is that this can occur anywhere. It`s not just Buffalo. It`s how to make people all across this country feel safe. It`s not just African-Americans; it`s how to make people in urban America, suburban America and rural America feel safe. The only way to do it is to really get to the point of sensible gun control in this country, to end hate speech on the Internet and social media, to stop the proliferation of hateful ideology. Those are the ways that we make black people feel safe in Buffalo, that we make people feel safe all across this country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Have you had -- been able to spend any time with victim families? What have they been telling you? What -- what would they like you to do?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Tremendous pain. Victims` families just thinking about their loved ones, the precious lives that were lost senselessly, the hurt that they feel, the pain that they feel, and wanting to go on because their loved ones would want them to go on.\par \par \uc2 Buffalo is a loving community. We are known nationally and internationally as the city of good neighbors. So we will, as a community, wrap our arms around the families of those we lost. We will continue to lift them up. We will continue to lift this community up. And we won`t let an act of a hateful madman bring this community down and keep our community from moving forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But there`s only so much you can do as mayor. You`re going to need some help from the federal level here. What would you like to see from the federal government to make your job easier to keep Buffalo safe?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, we`ve seen many of these mass shootings around the country, year in and year out, month in and month out. And it`s always the same refrain. People send their thoughts and -- and their prayers. Lawmakers in Washington say that there`s something that must be done.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:03]\par \par \uc2 And then there are some on one side of the aisle that block anything from being done. And it seems like there are those that believe owning a gun is more precious than the sanctity of human life. So I think people all across this country have to rise up. They have to speak more loudly and more clearly that there must be gun control in this country.\par \par \uc2 This is a uniquely American phenomenon. These mass shootings don`t happen in other countries across the world. We have to ask ourselves, and more than ask ourselves, we have to take action to stop it, to stop it after this Buffalo, New York, incident, to make sure that other communities, that other families don`t go through this again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sadly, Mr. Mayor, what you just said to me has been said to me by a lot of mayors in moments like this, and you just pointed it out. And that`s the -- the "wash, rinse, repeat" cycle of Washington not listening to the mayors on this is -- is very frustrating. You know, I know there`s not a lot more that can be done, I guess, other than -- than rhetoric here. But is there something else you`d like to see done?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, I would like to see sensible gun control. I would like to see ending hate speech on the Internet, on social media. It is not free speech. It is not the American way. We are not a nation of haters. We are not a nation of -- of hate. We need to send a message that there is no place on the Internet for hate speech, for hate indoctrination, for spreading hate manifesto.\par \par \uc2 So I`d like to see real, deliberate action taken on gun control and ending hate speech on the Internet. I will be a...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: ... stronger voice for that. I`ve heard from mayors all over the country in the aftermath of this incident. I`ve heard from mayors, actually, all across the world. And I believe that what happened in Buffalo, New York, yesterday, is going to be a turning point. I think it`s going to be different after this in terms of the energy and the activity that we see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mr. Mayor, I hope you`re right. I hope you`re right. We keep wondering, when is the incident going to be the one that finally shakes us out of this?\par \par \uc2 Mayor Byron Brown, Buffalo, thank you so much, sir.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is New York`s governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who is also a Buffalo native.\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome to "Meet the Press," on what I know has been a pretty difficult 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D), NEW YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me just start. Look, this is your hometown. I imagine the pain is -- is immense, no matter what. But this is your hometown. Tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: It`s gut-wrenching, Chuck. I -- this is my hometown. I`ve lived here my entire life. I lived in the city, just, you know, a few minutes from here. I worked in local politics here with a guy named Tim Russert as a teenager. So these streets are my streets. And it is just shattering to the collective psyche of this wonderful, tight-knit community to know that there`s such hate out there that someone is willing to travel a distance to come and shatter the lives of so many just well-meaning people just out there grocery shopping.\par \par \uc2 So, no, it -- it hurts. It hurts like hell.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is a toxic stew of a rising right-wing extremism ideology that`s out there, white supremacy, easy access to guns, and a permissive culture on the Internet. And it all met together in this horrendous attack. Where do you begin, when it comes to figuring out how we can tackle all of this?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Well, you`re right. There`s three components to this, the white supremacist terrorism; the radicalization that`s occurring of our young people, particularly, who are vulnerable to these evil ideas. It`s -- it`s all induced by the Internet. And the fact that platforms are willing to share this information, allow it to be posted, a manifesto that`s been out there that describes in great detail how someone wants to have an execution of individuals in a community that`s targeted because it`s the highest black population within a geographic area?\par \par \uc2 That`s all out there -- and also the fact that this could be live-streamed? How long was it live-streamed before someone paid attention?\par \par \uc2 These companies make a lot of money. They`re very profitable. And in my judgment, they have the opportunity to be doing far more monitoring and shut things down before it gets to this situation.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:09]\par \par \uc2 But also, it`s about access to guns. And in New York, we have the toughest gun laws in the nation. But right now, we have a case before the Supreme Court that could be decided in a matter of weeks, that could allow people to have a -- a gun that`s concealed, to walk in the Tops behind me with a concealed weapon this time.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got to deal with that access to guns that`s coming in from places like Pennsylvania, only a few minutes from where the -- the accused lived. I mean, he could have just gone right over to Pennsylvania and was able to enhance the gun he bought legally in New York but also to have an increased capacity magazine, which is exactly what we think he did, as well as just the -- the pervasive attitude that this replacement theory...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: ... that you`re going to target blacks and immigrants and Jews because they`re going to replace the whites? So you`re absolutely right. It is a trifecta. It is a storm here. And all of us have to be vigilant and work closely together to stop this now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me -- let`s start -- let me ask on guns. It`s -- a lot of people are going to scratch their heads and go, "Wow, an 18-year-old could legally buy most of these weapons?" And the answer is yes, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Well, in many states, legally, yes. In the state of New York, what he was able to buy was an AR-15. But he was able to enhance the magazine capacity. I mean, you can`t have that many rounds purchased legally here in the state of New York. But you just go over to Pennsylvania, go to a gun show.\par \par \uc2 This is why, when I became the new governor, just a few months ago, I started a nine-state gun interdiction task force, because we know most of the illegal guns being used on the streets of New York City and now on the streets of Buffalo are coming from out of state.\par \par \uc2 We need a national response. Since my time in Congress, we have been trying to get a national response. And so individual states will do the best they can. And I`m going to be proposing more gun laws on Tuesday. I had a prescheduled press conference even before this event occurred.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: But we need other states to step up. We need the federal government on our side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let`s talk about holding these Internet companies responsible. Obviously, there`s this law on the books that -- that allows the Internet to, sort of, escape liability on so many things that, frankly, we as television broadcasters cannot escape the same liability.\par \par \uc2 Do you think they should be held responsible for the spread of -- for the easy spread of this propaganda?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: I hold them responsible for not monitoring and alerting law enforcement. That`s exactly -- because the issue here is that it is fomenting -- people are sharing these ideas. They`re sharing videos of other attacks. And they`re all copycat. They all want to be the next "great white hope" that`s going to inspire the next -- next attack.\par \par \uc2 We can`t let that continue. And we know where it`s occurring. It`s not happening in the basement of a -- of a KKK meeting anymore, where you have a limited number of people who are succumbing to these evil influences. This is happening globally. They`re looking at what happened in New Zealand and what happened in Pittsburgh and what happened in South -- they read this; they absorb this. This becomes part of their mentality. And they share it with others through the Internet. That`s the responsibility of the Internet. And the -- the individuals who are responsible are the ones who own these companies. And I`m going to be talking to them directly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We also have TV commentators and some political figures that, sort of, appease this right-wing extremism, sort of, you know, anybody that pushes back, maybe they come after it on speech grounds, freedom of speech or things like this. But it certainly seems as if there is a growing virus on the right, on the far-right here, that is spreading dangerously?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: And they need to be held accountable as well. And any government leader that does not condemn this and condemn it today is a coward, and they`re also partially responsible. So let`s just be real honest about the role of elected leaders and what they need to be doing, just calling this out and not coddling this behavior and saying that it`s, "Well, that`s just young people and they`re sharing their ideas."\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I`ll stand -- I`ll protect the first amendment any day of the week, but you don`t protect hate speech. You don`t protect incendiary speech. You`re not allowed to scream "Fire!" in a crowded theater. There are limitations on speech, and right now, we have seen this run rampant. And as a result, I have 10 dead neighbors in this community. And it hurts. And we`re going to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It really does hurt. Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , really appreciate you spending a few minutes with us. And just know, we`re all thinking of everybody there...\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... in Buffalo. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, not only was the horror in Buffalo all too familiar, it is also the most recent example of a mass shooting inspired by right-wing hate. The panel is next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:21:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here, the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , host of "Politics Nation" on MSNBC; Washington bureau chief for USA Today, Susan Page; \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  contributing columnist, Matt Bai; Republican strategist Al Cardenas; and Washington Post White House bureau chief Ashley Parker.\par \par \uc2 Reverend Sharpton, we have a toxic stew here, white supremacy ideology that`s spreading, easy access to guns, permissive Internet culture that -- that almost encourages sharing of this far-right ideology. Where do we start?\par \par \uc2 REVEREND \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , HOST, MSNBC`S "POLITICS NATION": We start by changing the tone, nationally. We cannot just keep going through, as you said to Mayor Brown -- you`ve heard the mayor say this before, and the government, federal government, doesn`t do anything.\par \par \uc2 Last night, when I started getting calls from our National Action Network chapter in Buffalo of what happened, and then I started getting calls from government officials, the first thing I said is, President Biden needs to call a summit meeting of black, Jewish, Asian leaders, and sit down and talk about the growing problem of hate crimes and that this government will not stand by and allow this to happen.\par \par \uc2 We need to have a tone where young guys like this understand the federal government will come down on them; they`re monitoring what`s going on and they`re not going to tolerate it. He should do this right away. We -- I mean, we`ve gone from Tree of Life to -- from Charleston to now we`re in Buffalo. And we`re just putting out regular press releases, rather than dealing with this with the urgency that it requires.\par \par \uc2 And I reached out to \uc2 ADL\uc2  head \uc2 Jonathan Greenblatt\uc2  and others and said, "We ought to jointly go to the White House and deal with this because it is not just blacks; it is Jews; it is Asians; it is LGBTQ. It`s hate everywhere.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Latinos, in El Paso.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Latinos -- really, Latinos. So there`s hate everywhere. The president needs to preside and say this can`t be tolerated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Al, on the far right, there is this growing virus. And not enough -- it feels like not enough leaders on the right call it out. They, sort of -- there`s an appeasement of it, to be generous.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Listen, there used to be political parties. There used to be serious politicians who would step up and speak out against these kind of things. When \uc2 David Duke\uc2  ran for office, hey, I spoke out, as party chairman, and others did. It was well known that he was an individual on his own, not part of a family.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Now, as to the contrary, people keep silent. And that silence is interpreted in ways that are not healthy. I`ll say this. I`m frustrated, in America, that we`re so incapable of doing things. When George Floyd was killed, there were massive demonstrations. I thought there was going to be a move for social justice. And very little has happened.\par \par \uc2 You know, when, in Florida, we had Parkland, and then we had the mass shooting in a -- in a gay dance hall where a nephew of my wife was killed, there was some limited action.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:02]\par \par \uc2 But when you look at this, the frustration in America has to do with the fact that horrific things are happening; government`s not doing anything and political leaders are not doing anything. And that`s what the calamity is.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF, THE WASHINGTON POST: And the other component of this, of course, is guns. The reverend is right that tone matters incredibly, but then you look at how all of these hate crimes are committed and they are all committed with guns. And this is an area where Congress has been able to do absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 And there will be -- you saw it, people you were interviewing talking about sensible gun reform. But you look at Sandy Hook; you look at kindergartners massacred. You look at Mother Emmanuel, a shooting in a church. You look at what happened in Las Vegas, at the country music concert. Guns have now touched every aspect of society, and Congress has been able to do absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : So why do we feel so helpless on mornings like this, that we`re just having the same conversation we`ve had before?\par \par \uc2 Because there are in fact things that we could do. Law enforcement could do more to surveil these toxic sites. Social media companies could do more to bring them down. The news media could do more to cover them. Lawmakers could do more to find some kind of common ground on -- on guns. And Americans could stand up and say, "This -- these shootings, these hate shootings do not reflect America; this is a radical fringe." Americans need to stand up and say, "We won`t stand for this anymore."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MATT BAI: Yeah, I mean, we`ve talked about this. I covered Columbine, you know, back in the -- when this was new. This is now part of the culture. It`s a recurring thing. And Ashley`s right. Congress has done absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, to speak to what Reverend Al was saying earlier, you know, I don`t think -- and we may disagree about this; I`m certain, you know, I can have a disagreement with a lot of people -- I don`t think this is a more hateful, more racist country than it was 25 years ago, certainly not 50 years ago. I do think we have a segment of our political leadership that has emboldened and legitimized a very extreme and -- and dangerous segment of our political dialogue. And they have to take responsibility for that. There`s a culpability for that. And it is tied to the violence.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: But, Matt, racism has gone from being fringe to mainstream.\par \par \uc2 BAI: Yeah, for sure.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: That`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 BAI: That is the problem.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s an organizing tool.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It feels like a political organizing tool at times.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But the problem is, it has been normalized. You have to remember, this is an 18-year-old that is accused in Buffalo. When he was 15, Charlottesville happened. The president of the United States at that time said there are "good people," or "fine people, on both sides." So this gives them comfort.\par \par \uc2 That`s why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who I believe is a decent and good man, needs to set a different tone. And we need to deal with guns. Because a tone was set while this guy was a kid, being impressionable, that this is all right, to be marching, saying "Jews will not replace me." That`s what he saw at 15 years old.\par \par \uc2 BAI: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And he saw it from the White House.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And there`s something particularly devastating about the fact that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he was impelled to run for president because of Charlottesville.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And he seemed to mean that sincerely, and -- and now you look at what`s happening, and it feels like nothing has changed. The culture is the exact same.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It does seem, Matt and Susan, that every time there is an attempt to, sort of, deal with -- particularly deal with domestic terrorism, this white supremacy, you know, issue, all of a sudden, there`s a whole bunch of Republicans in Congress that start screaming "Speech."\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, free speech is important. Free speech is not a license to endorse things like replacement theory, this terrible, terrible theory that there`s -- and leads, many of them, Jews trying to replace America with a black and brown nation to dilute the power of white people. That is un- American. What elected official...\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s inhumane.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s more than un-American. It`s inhumane.\par \par \uc2 BAI: Chuck, I`m as close to a free speech absolutist as you`re probably going to find. But -- but, you know -- and I really believe that free speech is threatened in a lot of corners of this society. But free speech demands leadership. When you have a society that is free, you also have to have a society with leaders who stand up for morality and -- and for the right instincts in the culture, so that -- so that you don`t take things that are on the margins of the society, as you say, and bring them into the mainstream and legitimize them. We have failed on that count, and we continue to fail. And these are the consequences.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Every day. It`s every day. You know, immigration`s a big deal to me. For immigrant children, babies, to be blamed for formula shortage. I mean, come on. I mean, we`re trying to find hate everywhere we can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re absolutely right. And there are people on the right that are intentionally trying to create that divide on that issue, too.\par \par \uc2 All right. When we come back, I`m going to talk to Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . We`re going to talk, a little bit, about the midterm elections. We have a new NBC News poll that shows Democrats are headed for some big trouble in November. So I talk about that with the senator in an interview we taped before the events of yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:32:51]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to politics. It`s a truism that voters` views about the economy begin to harden in an election year around May. If that theory holds, the Democrats are in some serious trouble right now, because in our new NBC News poll, just 16 percent say the country is headed in the right direction, while a whopping 75 percent say we`re on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 In fact, that wrong track number has been 70-plus for seven months, which in the past has signaled big losses for the governing party.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s numbers have hit a new low, only 39 percent approve of his job performance, against 56 percent who disapprove. In fact, the president has now become as unpopular as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in this poll.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another warning sign for Democrats -- voters are split over who they want controlling Congress, 46-46. That sounds close, but for what it`s worth in 2010, Republicans had just a two-point lead on this question nationally, and they wound up gaining six Senate seats and 63 House seats.\par \par \uc2 If there is anything approaching a silver lining for Democrats, it`s the issue of abortion, given the leaked Supreme Court draft decision. Sixty percent say abortion should be legal all or most of the time -- the highest we`ve ever recorded in our poll -- versus just 37 percent who say abortion should illegal all or most of the time. And abortion has now become the top issue for people who will decide how to vote based on a single issue.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, I spoke to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , the independent senator from Vermont about what he thinks Democrats can do to avoid a shellacking in November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with what you know probably instinctively these days, but our polling has found. 75 percent of the country thinks we`re headed in the wrong direction. In fact, just 28 percent of Democrats right now think the country is headed in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 Where do you stand? Do you think we`re headed in the wrong direction?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): I sure do. Look, what we`re looking at is a nation today where the billionaire class, the people on top, are doing phenomenally well, Chuck. The middle class continues to decline. We`re seeing increased concentration of ownership in America, which has huge impacts for ordinary people.\par \par \uc2 You got three Wall Street firms -- \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 , \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  and State Street -- that have assets of over $20 trillion, controlling hundreds of corporations.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:09]\par \par \uc2 You`re seeing millions of people unable to afford the cost of prescription drugs, unable to afford health care, unable to put gas in the tank. So, how could anyone think that what`s going on right now is good?\par \par \uc2 And obviously, what I and other progressives are trying to do is put together a movement which tells the billionaire class in this country -- whose profits are soaring, who are getting richer and richer -- you know what, you can`t have it all. We need an economy that works for the middle class, the working class, the elderly, the children, not just the few on top.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting when you talk about the movement. Because there`s definitely -- we see it in our own polling -- there are more people that agree with sort of your mindset, your framing: we need big structural change.\par \par \uc2 But what do we do now before the elections? You know, at the end of the day, you have the Senate that you have, not the one that you want. What can get done now that could actually mitigate what looks like it`s going to be a tough year for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, we passed early on in the Biden administration one of the most significant pieces of legislation in the modern history of this country and that was the American Rescue Plan, which, Chuck, in my view, did a whole lot to help people deal with the economic turmoil that we saw as a result of COVID and massive unemployment.\par \par \uc2 What we have got to do right now -- it`s not hard. You know what you do? You listen to the American people and not wealthy campaign contributors. What does that mean? It means that, right now, you lower the outrageous cost of prescription drugs in this country. You have the guts to take on the pharmaceutical industry that, right now, has 1,500 paid lobbyists in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 You expand Medicare. I happen to believe, as candidates that I support believe, that healthcare is a human right, not a function of making huge profits for the insurance companies and the people who own them. You`ve got to move toward expanding healthcare. You`ve got to raise the minimum wage to a living wage. You`ve got to create millions of jobs by dealing with the existential threat of climate.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, you`ve got to do what the American people want and understand that it is women who have the right to control their own bodies, not the government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay, everything you said there was being addressed in Build Back Better, okay? It wasn`t as big as you wanted it. It was too big for some.\par \par \uc2 But why do we have nothing, right? That is the head-scratcher here. Understand you don`t have it all. Why is it zero? Why is there nothing?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: It`s -- well, it should not be a head-scratcher. You`ve got two members of the senate, Senator Manchin and Senator Sinema, who have sabotaged what the president has been fighting for --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a strong word, "sabotage".\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: - who have sabotaged -- well, you help me out with a better word here.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got 48 members of the Senate who wanted to go forward with an agenda that helped working families, that was prepared to take on the wealthy and the powerful. You had a president who wanted to do that. You had two people who prevented us from doing it.\par \par \uc2 You have a better word than sabotage? That`s fine. But I think that is the right word.\par \par \uc2 And I think pressure has got to be put on the part of people in West Virginia, in Arizona, to say, you know what, why don`t you stand up for ordinary Americans and not just your wealthy campaign contributors? Why don`t you have the guts to take on the drug companies and the insurance companies and the fossil fuel industry?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me ask you, though. I`ve -- look, I`ve talked to Pennsylvania Democrats and they also blame Senators Manchin and Sinema. They don`t blame the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Is that healthy for the party, that all of this concentration on -- should you have found something to do that isolates the Republicans rather than you guys ended up dividing the party against itself?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No, I don`t see it that way, Chuck. I mean, I think on all of these issues we had zero Republican support for the proposals in Medicare, in Build Back Better that were enormously popular. So it`s certainly not just Manchin and Sinema. They are two out of the 50 Democrats who went in the wrong direction. But every Republican did.\par \par \uc2 So if your point is it`s not just Manchin and Sinema, you`re right. It is every Republican. Now, the problem, I think, is we have not done a good job in making that clear to the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the vote to codify Roe this week. This is one of those votes that had 49 Democrats, not 50.\par \par \uc2 Should it have been a -- should you -- should the leadership have put a bill on the floor that could have gotten 52 votes, including Collins and Murkowski, to isolate the 48 Republican noes?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No. I mean, I think -- we`ll see what happens. But to answer your question, I think nobody should think that this process is dead. We should bring those bills up again, and again, and again. I can`t -- I was just at a rally earlier, you know, on Saturday in Montpelier, Vermont, my state capitol.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people cannot believe that you have a Supreme Court and Republicans who are prepared to overturn 50 years of precedent.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:07]\par \par \uc2 So I think what we should do is, on this bill, end the filibuster, do everything that we can to get 50 votes on the strongest possible bill to protect a woman`s right to control her own body.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before we had this leak opinion about Roe being overturned, you didn`t, you didn`t use the abortion issue as a reason not to support people. You would have -- you`ve supported pro-life progressives, if they were progressive on economic issues.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I recall a mayor of Omaha. I recall a race like that. Has your mind changed?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: There you go. A mayor of Omaha --\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I understand that. But has your philosophy changed? Is that a litmus test for you now for progressives?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No. Well, I do find it interesting that in this particular moment, when literally a woman`s right to choose is, is right at stake, that we have too many Democrats who are prepared to support a candidate in Texas who`s one of the few anti-pro-choice members of Congress. I`m supporting --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re speaking of Henry Cuellar. You think that`s a mistake?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yeah, I do. And I think - I`m supporting Jessica Cisneros down there. I hope she wins.\par \par \uc2 I`m supporting Summer Lee in Pennsylvania. I hope she wins as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think at this point, being pro-life, you can`t be in the Democratic Party or in the Democratic coalition? Are you -- are you ready to go that far?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, I mean that`s a -- that`s a -- you know, we will see. All that I can say is I think you have the overwhelming majority of people who consider themselves to be Democrats to be pro-choice. I have been pro- choice my entire life, and the candidates that I am supporting now are all pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 Look, a woman`s right to control her own body is really on the line, and we`ve got to do everything we can to defend that right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Last question. Are you, are you definitely going to rule out ever being a presidential candidate again, or if there`s an open race in 2024, could you run?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Why did I think -- why did I think you might ask that question?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2  wrote it in his memo.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You don`t -- look, no politician I know rules out, you know, all options. You know, everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, right now my focus is to do everything that I can to elect candidates to Congress who are going to stand up and fight for working families in this coming midterm election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  from Vermont, always a pleasure, sir. Thanks for coming on, sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, something truly extraordinary for television. We did a war game that the "Meet the Press" team organized on what might happen if China move to seize Taiwan and the U.S. tried to stop the invasion. You want to stay with us for this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:4:32]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 In our final episode of this season`s "Meet the Press Reports", we put together a remarkable war game simulation of how the U.S. might react if China invaded Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 The national security think tank, Center for a New American Security, or CANS, convened two teams, the blue team representing the United States and the red team, representing China.\par \par \uc2 Which side would prevail? Would China attack the U.S. mainland? Could nuclear war break out?\par \par \uc2 The conflict takes place in 2027. There are three rounds of strategy. And I spoke to the game master after each round to see where the conflict stood.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As you can see here on the map is a very large concentration of Chinese People`s Liberation Army forces at potential ports of debarkation for an invasion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We want to focus on a last-ditch effort to deter. This is a time to be sending the strongest possible message to Beijing, both privately and publicly, that there will be very severe costs if they actually go through with this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: China has sort of seen our reaction to Ukraine, and we want to make sure that we are surprising them with how we react here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hit the Americans as hard as we possibly can in the Western Pacific. Keep them out of the fight while we move on Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 JOEL WUTHNOW: I would support an early, knock-out punch against Guam.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The priority for the first 24 hours is to try to go as fast as possible against Taipei, right? I think it matters to deter United States, Japan, Australia and other countries from intervening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Stacie, high level here. What just happened with move one?\par \par \uc2 DR. STACIE PETTYJOHN: China`s invaded Taiwan. It began by attacking Taiwan`s outlying islands near the mainland. Then, it followed it with a large air and missile strike on Taiwan and on U.S. bases in Japan, and on U.S. bases in Guam and the Northern Marianas.\par \par \uc2 In response to that, the United States followed up with bomber attacks on U.S. -- Chinese ships in port. And there was an air battle over Taiwan, where American aircraft flying from the Philippines came in and engaged in combat with Chinese aircrafts that were trying to bomb Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So after move one, can you assess which team is winning?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you want to find out what the winning move was, and I`m sure you do, check out Meet the Press Reports wherever you get NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Republicans seem poised for huge gains in November. But could the abortion issue help generate some Democratic enthusiasm?\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:51:51]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Before the events of 24 hours ago, we were going to do a pretty big lead-in to the midterms. There`s still a big primary day on Tuesday. You know, Reverend Sharpton, our poll, it`s interesting, and you heard the interview with Senator Sanders. How do you fire up the left in an environment like this for the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`ve got to turn people on to turn them out. And I think the issues of the left that the African American or Black community, Latinos and all, are concerned about are not really the issues that they can say that we`ve had deliverables on -- George Floyd Justice in Policing Act, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Bill.\par \par \uc2 I think the president needs to do some executive orders. I think that we need to start seeing some progressive fighting in the trenches on a lot of these issues. All of this trying to be moderate in tone and style as you are in politics is not going to work. People came out --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Should Buffalo galvanize this even more?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Buffalo and women`s right to choose put in the right context, because what people need to understand is that if the Supreme Court in fact sends this back to Mississippi, this decision, they are saying Roe versus Wade no longer protects you. We`re going back to states` rights.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you start talking about states` rights, that means something else to me as a Black. Wait a minute. We`re going back to states` rights? Wait a minute. Or LGBTQ. We`re going back to states` rights?\par \par \uc2 This decision by the Supreme Court could galvanize everyone if it`s, if it`s put in the right context that this is a states` rights decision. If you say that states can decide women`s right to choose, they can decide LGBTQ rights, they can decide voting rights, Blacks -- they can galvanize, if the Democrats come forward and galvanize.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Al, in our poll, look, it is still advantage Republicans. You can see it in the polling. But wow, has the abortion decision suddenly given life to the Democratic coalition. You can see that, if it indeed happens, it might level or get close to leveling the playing field.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Yeah. I think, you know, a question I ask myself, is that gender equal? You know, the abortion issue. Is it age driven? Is it a top issue for some people, especially those over 50?\par \par \uc2 Having said all of that, there`s so much passion out there, particularly on the left, that it should count for something, especially turnout.\par \par \uc2 You know, may I add, you know, maybe out of the box, but how shocked I am about the primaries in Ohio and Pennsylvania. As a national observer, I mean, these are two Midwestern states seeking to replace Rob Portman and \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , two center-right --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very conventional Republicans --\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Conventional, yeah.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, here I am watching these two primaries that are about to develop. I`m saying, how did Ohio and Pennsylvania kind of change so radically in, like, four years?\par \par \uc2 BAI: Not only that, but we remember when \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  was not a conventional Republican. He was --\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Right, right, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAI: - dubbed a far right renegade.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: And the Democrats on the other hand, you know, really showed a steady hand. Tim Ryan and, and what`s coming up in the primary. But the Republicans have made a total turnaround.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So here`s what`s fascinating, Ashley and Susan, it`s that Donald -- you know, we`re all trying to figure out, does Donald Trump`s endorsement matter? Well, we`ll see. Trumpism is dominating these primaries, and it`s actually hurting Dr. Oz. I think, I think the former president picked the wrong Trump candidate.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, NBC NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, there is also a world where, you`re right, MAGA has become, in certain ways, bigger than former President Trump. And, you know, I think that`s what you`re seeing with the late surge from Kathy Barnette in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And it`s fascinating. Even if you watched him as a president, there were moments when Trump as president felt pulled between being the promise of the dealmaker that people hoped, and his far right base, what folks were saying on Fox News. And in all of those moments, he always retreated to the far right base, because there`s a part of him that, that lives a little bit in fear of them. He absolutely leads them, but he also worries about them.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Trump wins anyway in this Pennsylvania primary, whether his endorsed candidate wins or one of the other two candidates. They`re all bunched together. We don`t know who`s going to win.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They all want to be Trump. They have not run away from him, not like a -- \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 , who`s actually running against Trumpism in North Carolina. You`re not seeing that here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: He`s still the face of the party. Fifty-five percent of Republicans told you in your poll that they want him to be the face of the Republican Party, this after all that`s happened, his loss in the last election.\par \par \uc2 But in fact, his statement against the candidate who`s on the surge in Pennsylvania said, she can`t win in the general, but by the way, if she does, I`m all for her.\par \par \uc2 BAI: I mean, to go back to something we were talking about earlier in the last segment, the ideology pre-existed Trump. Trump didn`t invent MAGA or MAGA-ism, whatever you want to call it that`s dominating the party. He unleashed it and legitimized it and its worth.\par \par \uc2 But to an extent, I think on some of these primaries, we focus too much on ideology. To the extent that we even know what`s going on in the electorate, a lot of it`s this era of personality, right? It`s persona.\par \par \uc2 When you look at a Kathy Barnette, when you look at John Fetterman on the left, I mean --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you brought up John Fetterman. I was just going to say, Kathy Barnette and John Fetterman, they`re not your cookie cutter politicians.\par \par \uc2 BAI: No. And story and persona and the celebrity you build matters a lot. Dr. Oz may not win that primary, but he`s only there, as legitimate as he is, because he`s a TV star.\par \par \uc2 This is the era, as much as the ideology that Trump unleashed, it is this cult of personality in our politics over party, over ideology, over any other affiliation that he represents, and that will long outlive him.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Here`s the problem. Big personalities win elections. Big personalities have trouble governing once they win office.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, governing. I mean, you wouldn`t put "governing" and "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 " in the same sentence.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is that he not only is a big personality, he became a big personality for feelings that were already there. He tapped into what was there. And he became the crass representation of that.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of people that felt that way, and act as uncouth as he acts, now felt like, hey, I`ve got a shot because not only does he speak to what I want, he is who I am -- a very crass, uncouth kind of person.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ashley, ultra-MAGA. This was a -- this ultra-MAGA messaging by the team Biden, this was focus grouped?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Yes. It was the result of, believe it or not, about six months of research by liberal groups. Biden has decided to try to re-appropriate MAGA. He added on "ultra" with his own flair. And what you`re seeing, of course, is that Republicans and Trump are re-appropriating it. They`re proud to be MAGA. They are proud to be ultra-MAGA.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s what they did with fake news.\par \par \uc2 BAI: That`s going to change --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Like what they did with fake news, anyway.\par \par \uc2 MATT BAI: That`s going to change the outcome of all the elections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you all for doing this. This was a tough show. These are tough issues once again, we`re all dealing with, but we need to deal with them.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Keep our friends in Buffalo in your thoughts today.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I`ll see the GW Class of 2022 in a little bit. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220515ei5f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Nationwide Protests For Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Emilie Ikeda \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART: Now back to those nationwide abortion rights protest, Emilie Ikeda has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Coast to coast, 10s of 1000s of abortion rights supporters making their message loud and clear. Hundreds of bands off our bodies marches kicking off with some are calling the summer of rage.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What brought you out here today?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m angry. I think this is something that I thought was a done deal, and for us to go back in history and change it.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: They`re outcry in direct opposition to the league Supreme Court draft opinion poise to overturn Roe v. Wade clearing the way for states to restrict abortion access.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK MAJORITY LEADER: The public is overwhelmingly on our side, there are marches like this occurring all across the country.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: In New York protesters shutting down the Brooklyn Bridge, crowds marching to the Supreme Court in Washington D.C. and the northwest Illinois clashes with counter protesters.\par \par \uc2 Today`s crowds reminiscent of the women`s march in 2017, following the inauguration of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president. All three of former President Trump`s appointees appear in favor of overturning Roe v. Wade, in addition to justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who spoke out about the leak overnight.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS, U.S. SUPREME COURT: When you lose that trust, especially in the institution that I`m in, it changes the institution fundamentally.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:01]\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: A resounding message today from the masses to the highest court in the land.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Protesters here are already eyeing other ways to protect abortion access like packing the Supreme Court and getting out the vote during midterms so Congress can turn Roe v. Wade into federal law. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Emilie Ikeda in New York, thank you. Overseas in Ukraine, another high profile U.S. delegation met with that nation`s president today. Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  led the group of Republican senators in face to face talks with President Zelenskyy in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220517ei5e000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 TODAY for May 14, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Kristen Welker, Somara Theodore, Steve Patterson,  Jesse Kirsch, Josh Lederman, Erin McLaughlin, Emilie Ikeda, Joe Fryer;  Kristen Dahlgren, Michelle Grossman \par \uc2 7,350 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice-over): Good morning. Heating up. Unseasonable record warm forecast in nearly half the country from Arizona all the way to Maine. Temperatures in Texas expected to hit triple digits, raising new concerns about whether the power grid there can handle it. We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Growing desperation.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE JONES, MOM: How will I feed my baby?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Parents scrambling to find baby formula amid a nationwide shortage many store shelves bear. President Biden under fire saying his administration is racing to get it fixed immediately.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a process we`re working on it very, very hard. There`s nothing more urgent.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This morning, that frantic effort to resolve the crisis and a look at the breast milk bank some parents are now turning to in order to feed their babies.\par \par \uc2 Losing trust? Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is speaking out overnight about the future of the High Court after the publication of that leaked draft opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF U.S. SUPREME COURT: And when you lose that trust, especially in the institution that I`m in, it changes the institution fundamentally.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: His comments come as abortion rights protests are planned from coast to coast today.\par \par \uc2 On the run. An urgent search in Texas after an inmate serving a life sentence for a murder allegedly stabbed a prison guard and escaped from a transport bus.\par \par \uc2 Those stories plus out of bounds overnight. Golf legend Phil Mickelson dropping out of next week`s PGA Championship. Mickelson facing scrutiny for controversial comments he made about Saudi Arabia, the defending champion now leaving the field wide open for someone else to win the coveted major.\par \par \uc2 TODAY, Saturday, May 14, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to TODAY. Thanks for joining us on this Saturday morning. And it`s only May, but you can feel the humidity already creeping in as you`re headed into the building today.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: You sure can and it`s so hot. In some parts of the country, it`s creating some real challenges for folks. So we`re going to talk about that. We`re in fact covering several developing stories this morning, including an update on how Queen Elizabeth is doing a day after she was seen smiling at a public appearance on Friday. This, days after she canceled a separate appearance, raising concerns about her health.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Also, overnight, a chaotic scene in Milwaukee. Hundreds of people running for cover after gunshots rang out outside the Bucs-Celtics game.\par \par \uc2 But this morning, we want to begin with our top story, and it is that record heat expected for nearly half the country on seasonably warm temperatures for the month of May. Dallas could even see triple digits today and that`s already causing serious concerns across Texas, the power grid there with officials asking residents to conserve power this weekend. Somara Theodore is tracking how hot the forecast will be. Somara, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. You got to think after multiple weekends being cool throughout the spring months it is going to feel like we`re just stepping right on into the middle of July. I want to point out Burlington, 89 degrees. That is 22 degrees above the norm for this time of year, 80s throughout much of the Great Lakes region, 90s down into Texas. And we can`t talk big heat without talking Texas.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. Dallas, you`re expected to surpass your record tomorrow with a high of 98 degrees, possibly reaching the triple digits. As we head out towards areas like El Paso, 100 degrees out there, Phoenix 105, the record 107, so coming awfully close. And as Peter mentioned there, they`re asking residents to not go up below or above 78 degrees for -- or I`m sorry, go below 70 degrees on their thermostats because they don`t want the grid system to fail.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll tell you something this is not just a weekend uptick. We are actually tracking this heat sticking around with us through much of next week, Atlanta, Georgia in the 90s on Wednesday. Even in parts of the Mid Atlantic, mid to upper 80s, and near 79 degrees on Tuesday in Washington DC. So this heat is going to stick around with this throughout much of the week.\par \par \uc2 The other thing we are tracking is the radar. We`re keeping a close eye on thunderstorms. Today, we could see some severe weather and more severe weather for your Sunday. We`ll talk more about that and how some of the heat could actually work to really energize the storms that we`re seeing today. For now, Kristen and Peter, back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Alright, Somara, thanks so much. We`re going to get the rest of your forecast in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now to the other big story we are tracking this morning, parents of young children are growing worried this morning as that nationwide baby formula shortage shows no immediate signs of getting better.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But the president says his administration is working on getting formula back on store shelves as quickly as possible. NBC`s Steve Patterson is in LA with more on all of this. Steve, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Kristen, good morning to you. The longer this crisis stretches on, the more desperate parents are getting for anything they can do to take care of their children. The shortage is more than just supply chain issues, but also a factory shutdown after quality control concerns and production hasn`t restarted yet. But with millions of infants relying on that formula, families are scrambling for a solution.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON (voice-over): With store shelves still barren across the country, more parents are forced to face their worst fears.\par \par \uc2 JONES: How I`m going to be my baby?\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: A question new moms Stephanie Jones never thought she would ask.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You definitely are trying everything in your power to see what you can do and how you can get the formula.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: As of this morning, a record 43 percent of popular baby formulas are out of stock with major retailers putting limits on how much parents can buy.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know, I`ve gone to pretty much every store and every -- caught every store to search for it and we can`t find it anywhere.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: With criticism mounting over the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s response, President Biden is defending his administration`s efforts to solve the crisis.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is a process we`re working on it very, very hard. There`s nothing more urgent.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: The White House, now promising increased production, additional formula from overseas and more availability to low-income families.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOBOLAJI FAMUYIDE, UNIVERSITY OF MISSISSIPPI MEDICAL CENTER NEWBORN MEDICAL CHIEF: It is a national emergency that`s amplified in our low- income population were mostly minority people and people of color.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Now, Congress is demanding answers from the four companies that control 90 percent of formula manufacturing on how they plan to address the crisis. Meanwhile, parents are growing more desperate, breast milk banks across the country reporting record levels of requests for donor milk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These are the thermal bags that it gets put in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good morning, are you guys getting ready to go home?\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: And pediatricians, handling calls around the clock.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI FOGG, PEDIATRIC MEDICAL DIETICIAN, MEDICAL UNIVERSITY OF SOUTH CAROLINA SHAWN JENKINS: I`ve never thought I would see this in my life. I`ve been practicing for 15 years now.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Medical experts say under no circumstance should parents dilute formula or try to make their own. And most of all, no hoarding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: The Biden administration has launched a website to help parents track down the formula. And if you need help, experts say be ready to think outside the box, tried smaller family-owned pharmacies, and talk to your pediatrician about other nutrition options. Kristen and Peter, back to you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Steve, some good tips there. I really appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  spoke out overnight saying the publication of that leaked draft opinion about abortion has fundamentally changed the court. And it comes as abortion rights rallies are planned nationwide today. NBC`s Jesse Kirsch is in Chicago this morning where thousands are expected to rally there. Jesse, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Peter. Organizers are expecting that and we`re seeing the barricades up here already in Chicago on the city`s west side, the near west side. This is one of several events planned throughout this country today, part of a Bans Off Our Bodies protest in support of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 This is just one of several events that are also rallies planned in New York, Raleigh, Cleveland, El Paso, San Francisco, and many more cities in between. Today`s protests, just the latest since that draft Supreme Court opinion leaked earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 And for days, we`ve seen groups both in supportive and against abortion rights rallying their sides, and some have even gathered outside the homes of certain Supreme Court Justices, prompting calls from some Republicans for those protesters to be arrested, and now, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , the latest justice to comment publicly on this leak draft opinion and what it means for the future of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: We`re now that trust or that belief is gone forever. The -- and when you lose that trust, especially in the institution that I`m in, it changes the institution fundamentally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: And at this rally and those across the country, of course, we`ll be looking to hear from people speaking both in favor of and against abortion rights. This is a divisive issue that is not going away. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, real concerns about the further politicization of the court. Jesse Kirsch in Chicago. Jesse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And now to the crisis at the southern border. This morning, a federal judge is considering whether to allow the Biden administration to lift a pandemic era immigration restriction at the U.S. border with Mexico, it`s called Title 42. And the ruling is expected within days. NBC`s Josh Lederman is traveling with the president. He`s in a foggy Wilmington with the very latest on all of this. Boy, that is some fog there. Good morning, Josh.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Coming to you live from a cloud, Kristen. Good morning. Homeland Security officials are bracing for as many as 18,000 migrants a day showing up at the border if Title 42 is lifted, fueling a political crisis involving the White House, the Congress, and now the courts.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN (voice-over): This morning, the future of Title 42 is in a federal judge`s hands after lawyers for about two dozen states urged the court to override the Biden administration and keep it in place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Today was a great day. I`m very confident.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: The mostly Republican-led states arguing Washington didn`t fully evaluate how a border surge could overwhelm law enforcement and public health resources.\par \par \uc2 REP. EDDIE MORALES, (R-TX): There are a number -- a good number of over 100,000 migrants that are getting ready to cross.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: The policy, a relic of the Trump administration and of COVID-19 letting the government quickly expel migrants more than 1.8 million times since 2021. The White House says now that COVID has improved, it has no choice but to lift it but in the meantime, the judge barring the government from starting to unwind it.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are taking steps to ensure that we are prepared for the lifting of Title 42. Those are proceeding obviously I`m not going to prejudge a court ruling that hasn`t quite happened yet.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Title 42 emerging as a major cudgel in the midterms, Democrats in tough races under growing pressure to keep the policy as recent Gallup polling shows 4 in 10 Americans very worried about illegal immigration and concern growing among independents. Near the Texas-Mexico border, Magali Urbina says dozens of migrants crossed daily through her orchard.\par \par \uc2 MAGALI URBINA, ORCHARD OWNER NEAR TEXAS-MEXICO BORDER: They`re still human beings. They all know they`re breaking the law, but yet, they get across and they`re thirsty. It`s hot.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: The pandemic on the border both hot button issues now colliding just five months before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: And in that hearing yesterday, the judge promising to issue his decision by May 23, the day that the White House has set for Title 42 to go away, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Josh Lederman in Wilmington. Thank you, Josh.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And this morning, another apparent setback for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as Ukrainian forces say they are beginning to take back the city of Kharkiv forcing Russian troops out. Still, Ukrainian officials acknowledge the war there is far from over. NBC`s Erin McLaughlin has the very latest from Kyiv this morning. Erin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Peter and Kristen. President Zelenskyy says no one can predict how long this war will last. This fighting rages on in the east with losses on both sides of this conflict. I want to warn you that some of the images in this report are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): As the bodies of hundreds of Russian soldiers are loaded onto refrigeration trucks, casualties of the battle for Kyiv, Russia is now suffering what the UK says have been significant losses in the East after Ukraine blew up its attempt to cross a key river. And for the first time since the war began, the U.S. Secretary of Defense called his counterpart to the Kremlin. During the hour-long call, he urged an immediate ceasefire in Ukraine and emphasized the importance of maintaining lines of communication, according to a Defense Department readout. This, as Ukrainians take a potential step toward justice.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the first Russian soldiers suspected of war crimes appeared in a courtroom in Kyiv. 21-year-old Vadim Shishimarin is accused of gunning down a 62-year-old civilian in a village outside Sumy. The Russian sergeant has yet to enter a plea. One of more than 11,000 alleged war crimes are now being investigated by the Ukrainian Prosecutor General crimes including unlawful killings, torture, and kidnappings.\par \par \uc2 16-year-old Vlad Buriak is believed to have been kidnapped at a Russian checkpoint while trying to escape the occupied city of Melitopol.\par \par \uc2 OLEH BURIAK, HEAD OF THE ZAPORIZHZHIA RAION ADMINISTRATION: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: His father says the boy dreams of one day being a veterinarian. Vlad stayed behind when the rest of our family evacuated, he says, so he could care for his dying grandfather. He says that a Ukrainian soldier who was held in the same campus his son, testified on the conditions thereafter he was released. The soldier, he says, was beaten and raped.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that that`s what`s happened to your son?\par \par \uc2 BURIAK: Yes, unless I`m dealing --\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: I don`t even want to think about it, he says. I`m just focusing on how to get him out. After Vlad is free, they will fall into my emotions.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Kremlin has extended WNBA star Brittney Griner`s pretrial detention for a month after the basketball player had been detained for carrying oil derived from cannabis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: As both Sweden and Finland have signaled their interest in joining the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance potentially shattering years of neutrality, on Friday, the Turkish president voiced his skepticism presenting a potential stumbling block. The White House says it is looking to Turkey to clarify its position on that, Peter and Kristen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Erin McLaughlin on the ground again for us in Kyiv. Erin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And back in this country. This morning, authorities in Central Texas are searching for a prisoner who escaped to transport bus on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Police say Gonzalo Lopez stabbed the bus driver and then the bus went off the road allowing him to escape. Lopez was convicted in 2006 of killing a man along the Texas-Mexico border. A $22,000 reward is being offered for information leading to his capture. The sheriff says the bus driver`s injuries are not life-threatening.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Also this morning, a bit of a shocker in the golf world. Phil Mickelson, one of the most