BURY: I feel as though I`ve been very responsible. Everybody does not have the same clean beginning slate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): So, keep in mind, there`s still questions around whether President Biden has the power to wipe out students debt. Legal experts say executive action on this could open him up to legal challenges, guys.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Emily, what happens if student debt is wiped out? Does that resolve -- does that solve this problem?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Well, I`ll say this, it solves the problem for people who currently have student loans but come in 10 years, the next generation of students will likely find themselves in the same position unless the root issues are addressed like college tuition, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s part of what is a complex conversation right now. Emilie Ikeda there outside Columbia. Congratulations to all those graduates today. Emilie, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, wishing them well. All right, Joe is back with a look at what`s ahead on POPStart. Hi there, Joe.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: It was the slap heard around the world, you remember that little thing that happened at the Oscars? Well, Chris Rock is finally talking about it, sort of, we`ll tell you what he`s saying after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:47:57]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And we are back now with POPStart. Joe, what do you have?\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Hi, we got a lot this morning. First up is Fred Ward. The actor`s representative share the news on Friday that Ward had died.\par \par \uc2 That was Ward in the 1983 hit "The Right Stuff". He was known for many roles including parts in the movies, "Tremors" and more recently, "Sweet Home Alabama" and on the small screen, HBO`s "True Detective".\par \par \uc2 Ward passed away on Sunday. His cause of death was not revealed. His "Tremors" co-star Kevin Bacon shared on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  when it came to battling underground worms, I couldn`t have asked for a better partner. Rest in peace. Fred Ward was 79 years old.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Chris Rock the actor and comedian is once again sharing some of his thoughts about that incident at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 As you almost certainly recall back in March, Will Smith slapped Rock after the comedian made a joke about Jada Pinkett Smith.\par \par \uc2 Well, now, during the show at London`s Royal Albert Hall, Rock joked I`m OK if anybody was wondering. I got most of my hearing back.\par \par \uc2 Rock is across the pond for a series of stand-up shows. The comedian has been mostly mum on the slap heard around the world and you should not expect him to tell on stage anytime soon. He told the audience I`m not going to talk about it right now. I`ll get to it eventually on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last month, Rock said I`m not talking about it until I get paid suggesting that he won`t address the slap further until he sees a big paycheck. He sort of joke to the audience. He said your tickets were expensive but not that expensive.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, good at least that at this point he`s laughing about it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: (INAUDIBLE) little Cuba Gooding Jr. punch line with the Show Me the Money.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: (INAUDIBLE) paycheck. All right.\par \par \uc2 And finally, "SATURDAY NIGHT LIVE". Only two episodes of the sketch show remain this season starting tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SELENA GOMEZ, ACTRESS AND SINGER: Hi, I`m Selena Gomez, and I`m hosting SNL this week with Post Malone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Two pop stars and two comedy actors.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:02]\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I`m actually also an actor.\par \par \uc2 POST MALONE, SINGER: And I`m not really a pop star. I prefer drama.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK, well, I didn`t know you`re also a bunch of divas, really.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: This is the first time hosting for Selena Gomez, who previously appeared as a musical guest. It`s also a Post Malone`s first appearance.\par \par \uc2 Next week, season finale lineup has also been announced. Natasha Lyonne will host and Japanese Breakfast will be the musical guest. SNL airs tonight on NBC.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the last episode of the season always super fun guests.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, well since there are only two left, I`m going to have to stay up. You`re going to make it Peter?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: No chance.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: You have a DVR.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I`ll watch tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Joe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still to come right here, what might be the best Mother`s Day present ever. We are back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Still to come here on TODAY, the happy reunion between a woman who nearly got into a terrible accident and the heroes who jumped into action to save her.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, the song contest that is about a lot more than just singing, but first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220514ei5e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why Some Doctors Believe Antidepressants Are Being Over Prescribed Especially To Middle Aged Women\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Sheinelle Jones, Al Roker, Maria  Shriver \par \uc2 1,076 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: 8:35, back with Your Health.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Well, this is Women`s Health Month and this morning we`re looking at something that impacts a lot of women in their 40s, 50s and 60s, anxiety and depression.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: Maria Shriver joins us with new information about the causes and the treatments. Maria, good morning.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER: Good morning, Maria.\par \par \uc2 MARIA SHRIVER, NBC CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, all of you. Well, the number of middle aged women taking antidepressants is truly eye opening. Some even say it`s staggering. It`s more than double the number of men of the same age. So we wondered what`s contributing to this high rate of depression among women at midlife, and could there be better treatments to ease their symptoms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): The numbers are simply staggering. About one in five women between the ages of 40 and 59 took an antidepressant in the last 30 days. Among women 60 and up, it`s nearly one in four. That`s twice the rate for men the same age. And while depression is more common among middle aged women, some experts believe antidepressants are being over prescribed to treat their symptoms.\par \par \uc2 DONNA SHOUPE, OBGYN: I`m very concerned. I think it`s wrong. The better treatment is hormone replacement.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): OBGYN Dr. Donna Shoop says during menopause, most women go through dramatic fluctuations in hormones, that cause symptoms like hot flashes and difficulty sleeping, but can also wreak havoc on their mood. Studies find a woman`s risk of depression is two to four times higher around menopause than at any other time in her life. And many experts say hormone therapy is a more effective treatment than traditional antidepressants.\par \par \uc2 Women kind of in their 40s, 50s, when they go in complaining of like I`m anxious, I feel like this impending doom, I`m depressed, and they`re given, you know, Prozac or another SSRI. You`re saying you don`t need that and you actually need HRT instead.\par \par \uc2 SHOUPE: That is absolutely correct. You add back hormone replacement and many of the women go back to normal pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): It worked for Nina Lorez Collins. In her late 40s, she was in perimenopause, changing careers and her kids were off at college. That`s when she says anxiety and depression hit her like a truck.\par \par \uc2 NINA LOREZ COLLINS, AUTHOR: It was really a feeling of like, am I about to go off a cliff. Like I felt like I`m gaining a little weight, I`m not as pretty as I used to be. I was in a bad second marriage. So I thought OK, I`m going to be twice divorced, unattractive, no kids at home, you know, who will want me.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): Her doctor gave her Prozac but the depression kept getting worse. Then her gynecologist added in hormone therapy. Nina says the difference was night and day.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: For me, I honestly have never felt more kind of balanced and happy.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): While many women are afraid of estrogen thinking it will increase their chance of breast cancer, newer studies show the risk is low for women in their 50s. In fact, taking estrogen in midlife is linked to stronger bones and possibly better heart and brain health.\par \par \uc2 SHOUPE: I think the benefits are quite high and the risks are very low.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): Now, scientists at the \uc2 National Institute of Mental Health\uc2  are hoping to develop even better treatments for depression in middle aged women, medications that actually target estrogen receptors in their brains. The goal, to alleviate depressive symptoms without the side effects of traditional therapies.\par \par \uc2 PETER SCHMIDT, CHIEF OF BEHAVIORAL ENDOCRINOLOGY, \uc2 NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF MENTAL HEALTH\uc2 : I think it would be hugely important. Antidepressants don`t work in every woman who is going to be taken.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): To help others like her Nina Lorez Collins runs a platform called Revel, which offers forums and expert advice on menopause and midlife.\par \par \uc2 You went to your doctor for things you did not associate with menopause. Being depressed, being anxious, being irritable and people didn`t connect the dots for you.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: I mean, that`s why I started the community I did. Knowing that you`re not alone and being able to talk to other women about how to get through it is huge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [08:40:02]\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: The bottom line is, experts say any woman in her 40s and 50s experiencing depression or anxiety should talk to her OBGYN about hormone replacement therapy as a possible treatment. And this is really important because so many women don`t know when they`re in perimenopause. They don`t associate insomnia, mood changes with menopause.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And I think you`re right that a lot of people did think that HRT was so connected to cancer that you would just like hands off of that.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Yes, exactly. And that was certainly the message out there. And now people are rethinking that and say, you know, obviously, everybody, if you have a history of breast cancer, that`s really important. But other people who never thought about hormones are finding incredible relief, bioidentical hormones, all different kinds, but have that conversation.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, the great thing is this opens the door to continue having that conversation. So many women are suffering.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And the new research is exciting too, Maria.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you, Maria.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thanks, Maria.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, coming up next, say goodbye to the traditional office attire and hello business comfort.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That sounds good, the latest stretchable, breathable and comfortable fashions to make that return to office more enjoyable. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220515ei5d0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Growing Demonstrations At The Home Of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  Tied To The Potential Overturning Of Roe Versus Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Sheinelle Jones, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 1,503 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: We do have a lot more to cover, we`re welcoming Ms. Sheinelle Jones in. Hi, Sheinelle, how are you?\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning Hoda and Tom. Good morning. The ongoing battle over abortion is taking an alarming turn with scenes like this. Overnight, activists demonstrating at the home of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , to protest the possible reversal of Roe v. Wade. NBC`s Blayne Alexander has the very latest. Good morning to you Blayne.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Sheinelle, good morning to you. You`re right. These protests have become increasingly personal growing from the steps of the Supreme Court to the homes of several Supreme Court justices themselves. Now a recent poll shows that nearly two-thirds of the country support keeping Roe in place as the Senate prepares to vote on abortion rights tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): It`s the latest sign of a deeply divided nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m just very upset that the minority is making the decision for the majority. It`s just wrong. It`s wrong. And this whole thing has become political when it`s health.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Protesters by the dozens taking their anger directly to the home of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , author of the leaked Supreme Court draft decision that could strike down Roe v. Wade and overturn the right to an abortion. It comes after protesters descended on the homes of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  over the weekend, drawing reaction from both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: It is an attempt to replace the rule of law with a rule of moms.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Violence, threats, and intimidation have no place in political discourse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I asked unanimous consent that --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The protests triggered a bipartisan bill to immediately increase security for Supreme Court justices and their families passing the Senate unanimously. A much different fate than the one likely awaiting Democrats as they tried to pass a bill sealing abortion rights into federal law. That vote expected Wednesday is almost certain to fail. But will force senators to address abortion on the record.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: Every American will see how every senator stands. They can`t duck it anymore.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Now as the focus increasingly shifts from the nation`s Capitol to state capitals, groups like Georgia`s Life Alliance are laying the groundwork to strengthen restrictive abortion laws already on the books.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): And do you expect that there is more legislation to come when it comes to strengthening anti-abortion laws here in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA ZOLLER, GEORGIA LIFE ALLIANCE EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: I think that that certainly may happen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Their next school restrictions on abortion pills by mail.\par \par \uc2 ZOLLER: With that then law being mailed, but it would mean that you would have to have a doctor`s care not just a telemedicine visit and then going through the mail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And now while there are more than a dozen states that have laws ready to go to limit abortion access if Roe v. Wade is overturned, it also comes down to a question of enforcement. For instance, at least two attorneys general in two states have said that they do not plan to enforce those laws if Roe v. Wade is overturned. Sheinelle?\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It is 7:17. How about a first check of the weather with Mr. Roker, where are you Al? You`re in Miami Beach.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: That`s right. We`re down here in Miami Beach. We`re taking part in the Aspen Institute Ideas Climate Conference. Our today climate unit is here. We`re talking about all different things. And we`re going to be interviewing a former CEO, \uc2 Google\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Eric Schmidt\uc2 , about how technology can help with climate change.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, let`s show you what we`ve got going on today. It`s a tale of the coast where it`s cooler along the coast with record lows out west, and cool and breezy conditions along the eastern seaboard with record highs in between. We`re looking for cool breezy conditions with showers along the Mid-Atlantic and under the Northeast. And we`ve got rip currents and all kinds of minor beach erosion problems along the southeastern Atlantic coast, all the way down into Miami.\par \par \uc2 Now as we move to the west, you can look at these daily record highs that are being challenged, heat indexes that are going to be into the mid to upper 90s. And look at the records today, stretching all the way from Houston, Texas, up into parts of the Midwest, St. Louis, looking at temperatures that could hit records as well. And that`s going to last right on into the weekend. And the good news guys is we finally are going to be looking at warmer conditions along the eastern seaboard with temperatures getting into the 70s and 80s into the weekend from Boston all the way down to Richmond.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get to your local forecast coming up in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:20:27]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Al, we`ll check back with you in just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Coming up guys, we have new details on the Americans who died under mysterious circumstances at that popular resort in the Bahamas and the many unanswered questions that remain in the investigation. We`ll have the very latest.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Plus, the Queen, opting out of a treasured royal tradition this morning, a decision that`s raising new concerns about her health. We`ll take you live to London. But first is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Coming up, we have something really special this morning, right, an incredible story on the bond between a young patient and a nurse with the heart of gold.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Little Rocco had a need and his nurse, Cassie, went above and beyond to fill it. I was lucky to meet them. We`ll have touching reunion, after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:20]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:19]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back at 7:30. Look at our sunny little corner the world, well it`s about to get the sunny, Rockefeller Plaza. It`s a Tuesday morning, May 10th, 2022. Guy, I look forward to the days where jackets are off.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: But it`s pretty amazing --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: It`s really, really nice.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Sparkling. All right, let`s get right to our 7:30 headlines guys. We got some alarming news this morning from government climate officials. They say over half of the contagious U.S. -- the contiguous U.S. is now in a drought. Conditions are especially bad in the Southwest where dry conditions have led to an early wildfire season, multiple fires still burning across parts of Mexico. And in California, two of the state`s largest reservoirs are at critically low levels. Officials there asking residents in some counties to cut out their watering down to one day a week beginning next month. Al is going to have a lot more on those drought conditions coming up a little later.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Police Massachusetts are still searching for a suspect this morning after an attempted kidnapping that was thwarted by a Good Samaritan. Take a look at this video. The incident was captured right here on the surveillance camera. It happened Sunday night in Burlington about 15 miles northwest of Boston. You can see the victim there struggling just being pulled away there. This is by suspected kidnapper. moments later a driver that was passing by sees what`s going on, stops her car. The suspect that runs away, police credit that driver, the Good Samaritan, for possibly saving that woman`s life.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow. Three people somehow walked away with only minor injuries after attempting an emergency landing in a Pennsylvania field. Look at this. It happened in West Penn Township. The small plane flipped over after a touchdown. Thankfully no one on the ground was hurt and official say engine failure is likely to blame for the crash.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220511ei5a000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 10, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander, Sam Brock, Kelly Cobiella, Stephanie  Gosk, Miguel Almaguer, Kate Snow, Erin McLaughlin \par \uc2 3,910 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, President Biden defending his handling of soaring inflation as gas prices hit a new record.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The average price at the pump hitting an all-time high of 437 a gallon. The president of the White House today saying taming inflation is his top domestic priority. And taking aim at what he calls the GOP`s ultra- MAGA economic policies. How Republicans are firing back.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the dramatic capture. The sheriff saying a fugitive inmate and an Alabama corrections officer planned for a shootout with police before a car crash ended the 11-day manhunt. The officer apparently taking her own life. The arsenal police say that fugitives had and the tip that led officers to them.\par \par \uc2 The deadly missile strike in the critical port city of Odessa, a shopping mall destroyed. Our team inside Ukraine tonight.\par \par \uc2 Celebrity Chef Mario Batali, the verdict in his sexual misconduct trial.\par \par \uc2 The Senate on the eve of a vote on abortion rights. And now the growing battle. Can states block women from going out of state for abortions?\par \par \uc2 The extreme fire danger in the southwest and tracking severe storms in the central U.S.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  weighing in on Donald Trump`s Twitter ban. Would he lifted if he takes over the company?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening filler up is becoming a cringe worthy term for a lot of American drivers these days, with it now costing more than $50 to fill the average size car. The price of gas reaching a new record average today $4.37 a gallon. Michigan, Ohio and New Jersey, all places where the price at the pump has gone up between 17 and 24 cents a gallon in just a week.\par \par \uc2 The price of gasoline the most dramatic symbol of inflation that`s eating at the bottom lines for many Americans. With new inflation numbers expected to be released tomorrow, today President Biden declared soaring prices our top economic challenge, saying he understands the frustration while reminding of the global impact of Russia`s war in Ukraine and the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Peter Alexander reports now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For Americans on the road tonight, another speed bump. Gas prices today reaching a new all-time high.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re horrible\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re ridiculous, man. You can`t -- you know, it seems like you`re working for gas.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: regular unleaded now averaging $4.37 a gallon. And President Biden is again trying to highlight his efforts to slow the spike.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m taking inflation very seriously, and it`s my top domestic priority. I know you got to be frustrated. I know. I can taste it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Inflation now at a 40-year high. Prices surging on gas, groceries, and rent. Auto Repair Shop owner Wilson Hawley says prices for parts are so high it`s costing him business.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The customers aren`t coming in because the prices are too high and they have to hold off on the repairs.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: They`d rather drive without the repairs than come in and pay the price?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Correct.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And single mother Yolanda Alexandria tells us she now has to work multiple jobs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I could go in a grocery store and spend $60.00, and I`m looking like what did I purchase?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: For groceries and higher rent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One paycheck now just covers the rent. I shouldn`t have to work three jobs just do the extra. One job should suffice.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But tonight, President Biden insists he`s not to blame for prices that have been skyrocketing for nearly a year.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think our policies help not hurt.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And taking aim at former President Trump`s MAGA movement and its allies.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The MAGA Republicans are counting on you to be as frustrated by the pace of progress, which they have everything they`ve done, everything they can to slow down, that you`re going to hand power over to them.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But tonight, a top Republican is blasting the president.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Let`s look at the Biden agenda right now. We have 8.5 percent inflation. We`ve got the highest gas prices ever. So, I mean this -- he doesn`t have any ideas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Peter, everyone now bracing for those new inflation numbers due out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. And today, the President told me that he`s weighing whether to lift former President Trump`s tariffs on Chinese products that could help lower costs here. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander in the White House starting us off, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, dramatic new details of how the manhunt for those Alabama fugitives came to an end. The sightings, the tip, and the final encounter with police that prevented a shootout. Sam Brock is in Evansville, Indiana and has the latest.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, two of America`s most publicized fugitives Casey and Vicky White dramatically caught after 11 days on the run. Their Cadillac crumpled after a short-lived pursuit from police in Evansville, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 DAVE WEDDING, SHERIFF, VANDERBURGH COUNTY, INDIANA: Members of the U.S. Task Force basically ram the vehicle and pushed it into a ditch. And we later found out had they not done that, the fugitive was going to engage in a shootout with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: An arsenal of weaponry found in the car.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We could hear her on the line saying she had her finger on the trigger.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: But the only person to fire a shot, the highly regarded corrections officer Vicky White who`s set to receive the Employee of the Year Award next week, who took her own life before deputies could reach her.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  telling NBC News Casey got out of the car, surrendered, and said, please help my wife. She just shot herself in the head and I didn`t do it. Authority say there is no indication the two were married, though they were clearly disguising themselves quite masterfully with wigs and glasses.\par \par \uc2 Tristan Burger was standing at the same Evansville motel right down the hall following the case and had no clue.\par \par \uc2 And you definitely saw her?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I definitely saw her. She`s well --\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Hair dyed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hair dyed, everything.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Officials ultimately getting a crucial tip from James Stinson, an Indiana carwash owner.\par \par \uc2 JAMES STINSON, TIPPED THE POLICE: That truck was sitting right there.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Who spotted an abandoned \uc2 Ford\uc2  F150 with Tennessee plates early in the week.\par \par \uc2 STINSON: I said, this was probably that guy from Alabama. I mean, the windows are down. He`s in ignition, perfectly good truck.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: There`s also the question of why park the car here.\par \par \uc2 Of all the places Casey White could have left his truck, he picked this bay at a carwash next to a sign that says security cameras in use.\par \par \uc2 That security camera capturing the image of Casey that broke the case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Casey White waived his extradition and is headed back to Alabama tonight where he could face a judge again as early as this evening. Now, some relief after a nearly two-week-long saga has ended. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Sam, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to the war in Ukraine now and a new warning this evening about the world`s food supply as Russian forces intensify the result of the critical port city of Odessa. Kelly Cobiella has more from inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the crucial southern port city of Odessa in the crosshairs again. Ukraine says Russia has fired seven missiles at the city hitting a shopping center and warehouse, killing a security guard.\par \par \uc2 This facility has nothing to do with the military, Odessa`s mayor said. There are no ammunitions depot`s here and never have been.\par \par \uc2 Several buildings have been reduced to rubble. Odessa, almost untouched in the first two months of war is now being targeted nearly every day. Tonight, Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy warning the Russian blockade of ports like Odessa is threatening the world`s food supply. Ukraine produces about 20% of the world`s high-grade wheat used to make bread. The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . says nearly 25 million tons of grain is now stuck in silos while the war still rages across the east.\par \par \uc2 Anna and her husband told me they fled their home city of Severodonetsk now under constant Russian shelling.\par \par \uc2 There was no gas, no water, and my best friend was killed when she went out to find water.\par \par \uc2 I`m so sorry.\par \par \uc2 It`s so hard to bear, she says. She told me her husband lost his eye when he was 10 during the seizure of St. Petersburg in World War II. Now both living through another war.\par \par \uc2 Who do you blame?\par \par \uc2 Putin, of course. It`s Russia. We didn`t invade anyone, she says in Russian. Even though we`re old, she told me, we still want to live.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA (on camera): Anna and her husband are now among the more than 12 million Ukrainians displaced because of the war. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly Cobiella, thank you.\par \par \uc2 One of America`s most famous chefs Mario Batali was found not guilty today of indecent assault and battery. A Boston judge delivered the verdict of the non-jury trial. A woman accused fatality of forcibly kissing and groping her at a bar. Batali`s lawyer said the woman lied for fun and she lied for money, referring to a lawsuit she filed.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to the growing state battles over abortion rights after the leak of that draft Supreme Court decision that could overturn Roe v. Wade. New efforts tonight aimed at both abortion protection and possible prosecution. Here`s Stephanie Gosk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): There`s a new abortion law in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 GOV. NED LAMONT (D-CT): We`re doing everything we can to stand up on behalf of the reproductive rights of all of our citizens.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Setting up a battle between states.\par \par \uc2 MATT BLUMENTHAL, CONNECTICUT STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Texas, don`t mess with Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The law expands abortion access and protects providers like those in this clinic from being sued or even criminally charged by another state.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BARNES, PRESIDENT, THE WOMEN`S CENTERS: I`m very grateful for it and I think it was necessary in our political climate.\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: We`re not going to sit here with our residents, with our doctors, nurses, our facilities undefended. We`re going to stand up for these rights here in the state of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:11]\par \par \uc2 GOSK: State Representative Matt Blumenthal who co-authored the bill says it was in direct response to the abortion law in Texas, and in anticipation of Roe v. Wade possibly being overturned.\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: People are already coming to Connecticut from places like Texas to seek safe and legal care.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Missouri State Representative Mary Elizabeth Coleman drafted a bill similar to the one in Texas that would allow private citizens to sue abortion providers.\par \par \uc2 Should a doctor in Connecticut be concerned that they may be sued from a private citizen in Missouri if they`ve performed an abortion on a Missouri resident?\par \par \uc2 MARY ELIZABETH COLEMAN, MISSOURI STATE REPRESENTATIVE: No, I don`t think that that`s an accurate reading of the law.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Her bill has not yet been passed.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Because the abortion issue has been decided by the courts for so long, that taking this back into the state legislatures, there`s rhetoric that is ramping things up in ways that isn`t particularly helpful.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Scholars say the criminal and civil law has not been tested. And anti-abortion states might push boundaries of rows overturned, which is why states like Connecticut are acting quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER, PROFESSOR, U.C. DAVIS SCHOOL OF LAW: You see Connecticut looking at that kind of mess and saying we want clarity. We don`t want to wait for things to shake out in the courts.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: What kind of world are we potentially going into if Roe is overturned?\par \par \uc2 ZIEGLER: I`m afraid that it`s going to be a world where this conflict is even uglier and messier than the one we`re in now.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Hartford, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said today he will restore Donald Trump`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account once his deal to acquire the company is done. The former president was permanently suspended from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after the capital riot. Musk calls himself a free speech absolutist and says only \uc2 Twitter\uc2  accounts that operate bots or spread spam should be banned.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the new fire and severe weather alerts. We`ll tell you where and when. And Americans feeling the housing squeezes rent and mortgage rates soar. What you should know.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re monitoring the extreme fire danger tonight in the southwest. Red flag warnings up in eight states about a dozen massive fires burning the largest in New Mexico, burning more than 200,000 acres. Thousands have been forced to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 And a tornado watch has been issued for parts of Wisconsin after a tornado damage homes and farms in the western part of the state on Monday. The cities of Madison in Green Bay in Wisconsin are in the potential danger zone. To the south, severe thunderstorm watches are up across much of West Texas.\par \par \uc2 Back to the fight now against inflation specifically the severe housing crunch faced by growing numbers of people as mortgage rates and rents keep rising. Miguel Almaguer tonight on the new struggle for Americans trying to find a place to call home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, this is my favorite room in the house.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): As rents rise and now mortgage rates climb, the fissures like so many other American families are trying desperately to buy a home while facing a tougher housing market every day.\par \par \uc2 With low inventory and record high prices, if they wait any longer, their 30 year fixed mortgage could tick up even higher.\par \par \uc2 ALI FISHER, HOMEBUYER: We`re definitely willing to make more sacrifices than we were at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The typical mortgage payment in March was $1,700. Up nearly $400 from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN FISHER, HOMEBUYER: Yes. You kind of don`t know when it`s going to end with the mortgage rates rising, with inflation going up too. I mean, everything is getting more expensive.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Though the housing market remains hot, there are some signs of cooling. The average 30 year fixed mortgage rate has now climbed to 5.36 percent, up two points from a year ago. That`s led to an 11 percent drop in new mortgage applications. Still, many prospective homeowners are lowering expectations and raising budgets.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW DINSKY, CALIFORNIA REALTOR, SAN FERNANDO VALLEY: It`s definitely squeezing some people out, but again, demand is still there and it`s great -- far exceeding supply.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:05]\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: To get their foot into the door, families aren`t just willing to pay more for less. Many are turning to adjustable-rate loans where the interest rate can change after five, seven, or 10 years.\par \par \uc2 MIKE FRATANTONI\uc2 , CHIEF ECONOMIST, \uc2 MORTGAGE BANKERS ASSOCIATION\uc2 : We think of 2022 with higher mortgage rates that will cool down somewhat, we`re back to more typical market conditions.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: But after losing on multiple offers, the Fisher family wants to buy now in order to save later, or else they worry their American dream could become a nightmare. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next for us tonight, a potentially life-saving new test that can detect deadly fentanyl. We`ll show you how it works and why there are critics who oppose it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Today is the first-ever national Fentanyl Awareness Day. It`s an effort by the DEA to spread the word about the synthetic opioid that now accounts for nearly two-thirds of all overdose deaths in the U.S. Kate Snow tonight on the simple tool advocate say can prevent those deaths and they`re giving it away for free.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): On a recent Friday in L.A., students from USC were handing out free kits used to test drugs for the presence of deadly fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One line means there`s Fentanyl present, which means don`t take the drug.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Their harm reduction group is called TACO Teen Awareness Combating Overdose. Madeline Hilliard founded ago in 2020 following a year when a dozen USC students died of an overdose. She and co-founder Jack Elliott were neuroscience majors and saw a need to educate.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:09]\par \par \uc2 MADELINE HILLIARD, CO-FOUNDER TACO: Our goal is to remove any and all barriers so that you have absolutely no reason not to be testing your drugs.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: They`ve handed out 10,000 free test strips at campuses across California and now shipped to schools around the country. Students can also use an app to have strips delivered within 10 minutes for one cent-plus delivery.\par \par \uc2 JACK ELLIOTT, CO-FOUNDER TACO: Fentanyl is a big deal because you can take it accidentally. There could be a couple of grains of sand size amount of fentanyl in your drug and that can kill you.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Of the nearly 100,000 overdose deaths between June 2020 and June 2021, 64 percent were fentanyl-related. For people 15 to 24 years old, 78 percent of deaths involved fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You want to grab the tip of the strip.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: We went to NJ Labs to better understand how the test strips work. In the real world, someone testing a pill or powder would dissolve a tiny portion in water. Similar to a home pregnancy or COVID test, we watch for lines.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA LEE, CEO, NJ LABS: There`s only.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: One line one line means there`s fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 LEE\uc2 : There`s fentanyl on that product, in that solution.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: That means throw the drug away. Do you think these tests are fairly accurate?\par \par \uc2 LEE: They`re fairly accurate and sensitive enough to test very trace low levels of fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Some states consider the test strips drug paraphernalia and make distributing them illegal. Critics worry they encourage people to use drugs.\par \par \uc2 HILLIARD: They were going to use drugs whether we gave them the test strips or not. The differences now with the test strips, if they find fentanyl in it, that means that they are going to wake up tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If I`m going to use drugs, I`m going to test them.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: This 21-year-old college student requested anonymity to speak openly. At a party, she and her best friend`s went to the bathroom to test a bag of cocaine some were planning to use. When the strip showed positive, they sat in stunned silence.\par \par \uc2 If you hadn`t known, some of your friends were about to do it. What do you think might have happened?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One of them could have at least died if not all. It was super jarring to think that like they could have died. And these are people that I like spend every day with. It would have changed like everything about how I live right now and I imagine going forward.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: TACO`s goal is to create positive peer pressure. If you`re going to use a drug, test it first.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Give them to friends, family, whoever.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Hoping that small step saves lives. Kate Snow, NBC News New Brunswick, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next here tonight, one man`s intimate wartime portrait. Tragedy and hope through his own lens.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, he survived unthinkable conditions and wants the world to see what he saw. They are scenes both harrowing and hopeful, as witnessed by a man from Mariupol with a camera. Here`s Erin McLaughlin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: this our Mariupol before war.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Oh, it`s so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 Amateur photographer Efien Sosnowski`s (PH) photos tell the story of a glistening port city full of promise.\par \par \uc2 Oh, beautiful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This our grand theatre which now is fully damaged.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Until the Russian troops arrived in Mariupol. Nothing was left unscathed by the Russian assault.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The best school of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: The school where his children studied, the supermarkets where he shopped, the basement where his neighbors sheltered, all gone.\par \par \uc2 People were in the basement and when the bomb exploded, they were crushed, he says. Nobody could get them out. They`re still there.\par \par \uc2 His images tell a haunting story of tragedy but also of hope, of people pulling together. Despite having little food, no running water or electricity, and yet still finding a reason to smile. As the week`s pressed on, the horror hit closer and closer until a shell exploded when he was desperately searching for his wife inside his mother-in-law`s home.\par \par \uc2 I was covered by the ruins of the building, he says. I could not hear at all. I had a concussion. But I was alive.\par \par \uc2 Following a separate attack, his nephew`s son, an 8-year-old boy and his family all shelter together in a basement where the little boy kept a diary. A page from that diary reads, my grandfather died. I have a wound on my back, torn skin. My sister has a head injury. My mother has a hole in her leg. Yevheni reads the final words of the boy`s entry out loud.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My favorite city, Mariupol, has died. A small boy wrote that his city has died. That`s all.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: A chilling portrait of innocence lost to war, a city destroyed, and a man who says he`ll never be the same. Erin McLaughlin, NBC News Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And one more note. We`re not naming that little boy`s family or showing their faces because they are still trapped in Mariupol and they are desperate to get out.\par \par \uc2 That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Tuesday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220511ei5a0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Today for May 10, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Tom Llamas, Sam Brock, Morgan Chesky, Erin McLaughlin,  Sheinelle Jones, Blayne Alexander, Al Roker, Kerry Sanders, Kristen  Dahlgren, Carson Daly, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 6,464 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Breaking overnight, dramatic end, new video revealing the final moments of that 11-day manhunt for a fugitive inmate and an Alabama corrections officer, the couple found hundreds of miles away in Indiana, leading to a chase, a crash, and that former guard taking her own life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We could hear her on the line saying she had her finger on the trigger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The dangerous convict now behind bars.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF RICK SINGLETON, LAUDERDALE COUNTY, AL: He will be in handcuffs and shackles while he`s in there. So he`s not getting out at this jail again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So what led to the capture and what is next for that inmate, we`ll have a live interview with the sheriff at the center of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Inflation nation, overnight, the price of gas soaring to an all-time high as Americans shell out more and more for fuel, food, and housing, leading to yet another wild ride on Wall Street. The Dow plummeting more than 600 points. So what`s being done to get the economy back on track and help families in need? We`ll have the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Bitter battle, growing demonstrations at the home of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  tied to the potential overturning of Roe versus Wade. Just a head wide lawmakers on both sides say that protest crosses the line ahead of a key vote on Capitol Hill that could fan the flames even more.\par \par \uc2 More questions than answers, police revealing new details linked to those mysterious deaths of three Americans at the same luxury resort in the Bahamas. What we`re learning about the victims and where the investigation stands this morning.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, royal absence, the Queen missing a London tradition this morning for the first time in nearly 60 years, what the palace is saying about her mobility issues and what it could mean for her big Platinum Jubilee next month.\par \par \uc2 And jaw dropping, Andy Warhol`s iconic portrait of Marilyn Monroe sells at auction overnight for nearly $200 million.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s selling here --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Inside the record smashed along the way proving at least one gentleman really does prefer blondes, today, Tuesday, May 10th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hey everybody, welcome to Today. We are so happy you are joining us. It`s a Tuesday morning. Savannah and Craig are out. Look who`s here.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Is this our first time sitting together?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: First time together, a historic moment, so excited.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s good to see you, Tom. I`m so happy that you are here.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Good to see you too.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s get right to the new details overnight on that stunning manhunt that first began back on April 29th. That`s when the inmate Casey White and prison guard Vicky White walked out of that county jail in Florence Alabama.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: For 11 days, the pair made their way across three states, Northern Alabama, Central Tennessee, and finally to Evansville, Indiana, where they were eventually spotted.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, police located them at a motel there, trailing the suspects in a pursuit. It ended with a forced crash late yesterday. Several hours later, Vicky White was pronounced dead at an Indiana hospital. In a moment, we`re going to speak with the sheriff where the manhunt ended. But let us get started with NBC`s Sam Brock. Hey, Sam, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS 