rst thought this could be that guy from Alabama because he`s got Tennessee license plates on it.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): Surveillance video showed Casey leaving in a Cadillac, the authorities using that car to track the pair to a local hotel parking lot. And once they left, eventually the car chase that led to their capture. Casey was already facing capital murder charges for the 2015 death of Connie Ridgeway. Her son, Austin, relieved the manhunt is finally over.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN WILLIAMS, CONNIE RIDGEWAY`S SON: It`s just night and day difference. It`s like you`re going through the darkest night. And then the next day the sun is shining.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): Officials now vowing Casey will be brought to justice.\par \par \uc2 SINGLETON: He will be in handcuffs and shackles while he`s in there. So if he wants to sue me for violating his civil rights, so be it. He`s not getting out of jail again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: If Casey White chooses to waive extradition, he could be back in Florence, Alabama within the next day or two. And guys, authorities here believe it is possible that pair was holed up inside that motel for up to six days consecutively. And plan on spending weeks there. Tom and Hoda back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, Sam, OK, thank you. Let`s get more details behind that chase and the manhunt`s dramatic ending. Joining us now is Dave Wedding. He`s the Sheriff of Vanderburgh County, Indiana, where it all unfolded. Sheriff Wedding good morning to you, good to see you. Wow, it sounds like that couple had been in Evansville for it could be six days, almost a week. Tell us who spotted them and how you finally made that catch.\par \par \uc2 WEDDING: Well, yesterday morning, members from the U.S. Marshal Service out of the state of Alabama and Mississippi, met with my deputies and our Marshals Task Force. And I want to clarify the U.S. Marshal Task Force is comprised of members of the Denver County Sheriff`s Office as a police department and the Indiana State Police. So we had a lot of local law enforcement involvement in this capture. But we were interviewing people, reviewing surveillance videos, and to see if we come up with certain leads to track these people down based on the vehicle being spotted here on May the 3rd while everyone was at our operation center.\par \par \uc2 And as a police officer, located a vehicle that matched the description of the one that they had exchanged on May the 3rd and we went to investigate which led to the chase.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: So Sheriff take us into that Chase, right, we saw how it ended there. What did your officers tell you and what did they discover once that car flipped over?\par \par \uc2 WEDDING: Well, the pursuit was very short. It went up a major artery in here in Vanderburgh County. And they cut across a parking lot at a large factory. They were in a grassy area, so three of our task force members actually rammed the vehicle and pushed it into a ditch so it ended up on its side. As they approached the vehicle, it was obvious that the female driver was unconscious and still had a weapon in her hand. And they removed the murderer from the vehicle simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. And Sheriff according to the U.S. Marshal`s Office, when Casey came out of the car, he said this, please help my wife. She just shot herself in the head and I didn`t do it. I guess just to clarify, how are you certain it was in fact suicide and were the two married as far as you knew?\par \par \uc2 WEDDING: From my understanding, they are not married. They just shared the same last name. The coroner`s office will do a investigation and they will determine if it was a self-inflicted gunshot wound. But the initial indication that it was. But we won`t rule anything out until we have a thorough investigation by the coroner.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Understand. All right, Sheriff Wedding. Thank you so much. We appreciate your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, we want to turn on to another major headline we`re following this morning, the growing concern surrounding your money and the state of the nation`s economy. Americans are waking up to a new record when it comes to gas prices while facing skyrocketing costs on just about everything else. All that as the roller coaster ride on Wall Street rolls on. NBC`s Morgan Chesky is in Plano, Texas for us this morning. \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , good morning.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Tom, good morning. And unfortunately, one of the consequences of prices going up everywhere is a growing number of people in need of those basic necessities. Here at the North Texas Food Bank, their rise in hungry families has absolutely been matching that record rise in inflation that the Fed is now trying to tackle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): This morning, Wall Street is bracing for another bumpy ride.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: NASDAQ going out with a loss of 4.3 percent. The selling continues.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:01]\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): With the Dow dropping more than 650 points, Monday and the S&P 500 sticking to its lowest level in more than a year. What`s driving the market selloff, rising interest rates set by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  aimed at stamping out rampant inflation, as consumers pay more for food, rent, and fuel. Gas prices hitting a record high today, now 4.37 nationwide. In Chicago, some drivers are waiting in line to fill up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was here Friday and the line was all the way out. So I just actually left. So I just did morning drop off, I decided to give it a try right now.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): The rising cost of groceries is forcing even more Americans to rely on food banks. The North Texas Food Bank says it`s distributed more than 12 million meals in April alone, up nearly 25 percent in three months.\par \