 Police have now released the identities of the three American victims. But this morning, there are still a lot of questions about what caused their deaths.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, NBC`s senior national correspondent Kerry Sanders is at the resort where the investigation is ongoing. Hey, Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning guys. The Royal Bahamian Police say, the forensics teams have now scoured the beachfront condo beachfront villa here looking for a common thread that may explain what is so far inexplicable three deaths.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): While the cause of the three American deaths at this popular resort in the Bahamas is still a mystery, this morning, forensics teams are focusing on this split beachfront villa at Sandals Emerald Bay in search of possible toxic contaminants.\par \par \uc2 PAUL ROLLE, COMMISSIONER OF ROYAL BAHAMAS POLICE FORCE: The forensic examination and should be able to help us to determine whether or not there was a chemical or whatever it was.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): The luxury rooms are where 68-year-old Michael Phillips and his 65-year-old wife Robbie from Tennessee were found dead. Overnight, their families telling NBC News, our hearts are grieving and broken. Right next door, 64-year-old Vince Chiarella from Florida also died. His wife of 40 years, Donnis, was severely ill and is now at a Miami Hospital in fair condition.\par \par \uc2 Royal Bahamian police say all four victims had reported feeling sick the night before, and believe the three deaths occurred between 11:00 p.m. on Thursday and 8:30 a.m. Friday. Detectives examining the hot water heater and air conditioner for potentially harmful leaks.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Have you set equipment inside to monitor whether there is any sort of poison or contamination entering through that system now?\par \par \uc2 ROLLE: Held persons are doing some follow up investigations on there. They`ll be able to speak to what they`re doing later.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Working as a travel agent at this office in Maryville, Tennessee, Robbie and her husband frequently posted about their travels online, often staying at different \uc2 Sandals Resorts\uc2  across the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE PHILLIPS, VICTIM: I`m in St. Lucia this morning.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Hours before the couple was found unresponsive, Robbie, writing if you want the most beautiful long private beach, this is it. Relaxing. Now police are investigating what brought this dream vacation to a tragic end for two American couples.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So it seems like the autopsies, Kerry, may hold the key to what happened here. Do we have a timeline on those yet?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, the Royal Bahamian police chief says that it probably took about seven days to get the pathology and toxicology tests back and even longer for the autopsies. Meantime, they may have a new lead because several of the guests here have told NBC News that they complained about the strong odor of insecticide in this area, and so that information has now been passed along also to the Royal Bahamian police who are looking into that as well. Again, those pathology, toxicology, and the of course, autopsies will likely lead to a cause, and then they can go further from there. Guys?\par \par \uc2 [07:35:30]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Kerry Sanders for us there in the Bahamas. Kerry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Just ahead, new concern over the Queen`s health after the royal reluctantly skipped an event this morning. She hasn`t missed in nearly 60 years.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A live report from London right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:19]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, we`re back at 7:40 with an In-Depth in a headline making absence by the Queen.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, this morning, marked the ceremonial beginning of part -- Britain`s parliament. It`s an event filled with pomp and circumstance and typically presided over by the monarch. But for just the third time in her 70 year reign, the Queen was not there.\par \par \uc2 JONES: NBC`s Kristen Dahlgren is in London this morning with more. Kristen, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, guys. Yes, the opening of parliament is one of the biggest ceremonial duties of the Queen. Basically, she reads a speech that`s written by the government laying out its agenda. It`s something that she takes very seriously and this is the first time she`s missed it since 1963.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): This morning, instead of the Queen, it was Prince Charles and Prince William taking their ceremonial places inside Britain`s parliament.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES, PRINCE OF WALES: My Lord, pray be seated.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): A ritual Her Majesty has only missed twice before due to pregnancy. The palace saying the Queen continues to experience episodic mobility problems, and in consultation with her doctors has reluctantly decided that she will not attend. At Her Majesty`s requests, the Prince of Wales will read the Queen`s speech.\par \par \uc2 While royal sources point out the 96-year-old monarch is making calls from home this week. Mobility issues have forced her to cut back on appearances. A service for Prince Philip in March her only public appearance outside the home this year. The palace recently announcing she would not be attending the annual garden parties.\par \par \uc2 DAISY MCANDREW, NBC NEWS ROYAL COMMENTATOR: Having been to one of these garden parties myself, you literally stand up for hours. There`s nowhere to sit down at all and if you`re the guest of honor or if you`re the host as the Queen would be. I mean that would be too much for somebody, a fraction of her age.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): All this as preparations continue for the Queen`s Platinum Jubilee next month. Officials have said decisions will be made day to day on which events the Queen attends during four days of parades, street parties, a horse race, and a live concert to celebrate 70 years on the throne.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is something 70 years of service. That`s amazing. It`s wonderful, exemplary.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN (voice-over): It will also be the first time the royal family is all together since Harry and Meghan stepped down from royal duties and moved to the U.S. two years ago.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH, SUNDAY TIMES ROYAL EDITOR: For the last time we saw Harry and Meghan with all the royal family, you could see the frostiness in the air, it was very tense. So I think if we see them at the service of Thanksgiving at St Paul`s which I think we will, you know, that will be quite a moment to see them reunited with the Queen with other senior Royals. I think there`ll be a hope that perhaps, you know, there might be some other branches going each way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: So a lot going on for this family. Now we are hearing that the Queen was watching from home this morning. And you may have noticed in that video that her absence was definitely marked where her throne normally would be was left empty, just her crown there on a pedestal. And instead of wearing robes, you may have noted Prince Charles, guys, he was in military uniform. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right, Kristen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, it is 7:43. Let`s head down to Miami Beach and check in with Mr. Roker get a check of the weather. Hey, Al.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hey guys, good morning and you referenced and showed some of those wildfires and drought out west. Well, let`s dive a little bit deeper into that. Right now, here is a critical fire danger today. Strong wind gusts through tomorrow, a single digit humidity levels, red flag warnings for 24 of the last 30 days for parts of New Mexico, precipitation this year, well below average. Rain and snow for much of the West, it is bone dry out there with the -- and the summer outlook, a significant fire potential from the Pacific to the plains.\par \par \uc2 We also have severe weather to talk about today. We are looking at a risk of severe weather up in the upper Midwest for storm hazards and possible isolated tornadoes. Also down to the southwest, we`re expecting some severe weather as well throughout parts of Texas, isolated tornadoes very low. But hail and damaging winds, and then as we move on into tomorrow, again, Upper Midwest, 5 million people at risk, tornado risk isolated, but still there and moving on into Thursday. Again, more of a risk of severe weather, enhanced risk as well for 10 million people from Fargo on into parts of Minnesota and on into Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:45:26]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, it`s iconic, colorful, and yes, that`s the real deal with - -\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And expensive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Inside the rich history and the record setting sale overnight. That`s Andy Warhol`s legendary portrait of Marilyn Monroe, coming up right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back with Carson. And Carson, history was made just across the street from us overnight.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. It certainly was. Good morning everybody. Andy Warhol`s portrait of Marilyn Monroe`s remarkable piece of work and one of the pop culture`s most iconic images that just set several records when it was sold. NBC`s Anne Thompson is at Christie`s Auction House with more. Good morning, Anne.\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Carson. Good morning, everyone. This really was an auction for the ages. It took just under four minutes of bidding to set a new record high price for American art at auction, propelled by our endless curiosity about the subject behind me and the iconic artists who created it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We will require the use of a special pebble.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:02]\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): Marilyn Monroe`s image hung over the auction, creating a frenzy of interest from the curious and the very wealthy bidders alike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So ladies judge when we come to the American dream, Warhol`s sublime depiction of Marilyn Monroe.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): All coveting the 40 by 40 inch silk screen of the Hollywood star by Andy Warhol, what Christie calls a Mona Lisa for the 20th century. This morning, the painting holds the distinction of bringing the highest price for any American piece of art and 20th century art at auction.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is selling here.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): A whopping $195 million, just short of the 200 million mark some thought it could bring.\par \par \uc2 ALEX ROTTER, CHAIRMAN OF CHRISTIE`S 20TH & 21ST CENTURY ART: Close to $200 million. Let it sink in. It`s quite something.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): Aside from its subject and artists, the work has a lure of its own. It is one of the "Shot Marilyn`s," so called because of visitor to Warhol`s factory studio in New York City fired a revolver into a stack of four Marilyn paintings, including this one which was not damaged. Titled "Shot Sage Blue Marilyn," it is a pop and artistic sensation previously exhibited in New York, Zurich, London, and Paris.\par \par \uc2 ROTTER: There`s no other painting that represents this diversity and emotions and dislike really capturing what the audience, what people what we are fascinated by because it`s Marilyn.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): Sixty years after her death, Monroe is having yet another cultural moment. On the Met Gala carpet last week, Kim Kardashian wore Monroe`s dress from 1962 when she sang Happy Birthday to President Kennedy. And \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is running a new documentary, "The Mystery of Marilyn Monroe: The Unheard Tapes," looking at her life and death, a fascination pushing the price of American art to new heights this morning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Now this piece comes from the collection of Thomas and Doris Ammann, brother and sister Swizz art dealers. The money raised at the auction will go to children`s charities around the world. And \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports this morning that the buyer is art dealer Larry Gagosian. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh --\par \par \uc2 DALY: That changes things.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It changes a lot.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I did not know that.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: I was going to tell, Anne, that mean Carson had a getaway car ready.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: It`s amazing what art commands what price but then there`s a charitable part of it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thank you, Anne, appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Guys, coming up, we got a musical about a menu item at Taco Bell. It`s really a thing it`s coming up on PopStart.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220510ei5a000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Fight Over Abortion Escalating In Wisconsin\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 585 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: From state capitols to the homes of Supreme Court justices growing protests tonight aimed at shaping the future of abortion rights in America after that leak Supreme Court draft decision that would overturn Roe v. Wade. Blayne Alexander now with that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With the landmark Roe v. Wade decision hanging in the balance, the protests are becoming increasingly personal. In Madison, Wisconsin, a pair of Molotov cocktails shattered the windows at the Wisconsin family action office, an anti- abortion group.\par \par \uc2 SHON BARNES, CHIEF, MADISON, WISCONSIN POLICE: Unfortunately, we`re investigating the fruits of hate and violence in the form of an arson.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Washington, as protesters fill the streets around the Supreme Court, others took their anger straight to the justices themselves, staging sit-ins outside the homes of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . All signs of a deepening divide one week after a leaked draft opinion through the future of Roe v. Wade into question. On Capitol Hill, Democrats will vote this week to advance the bill protecting abortion rights. That legislation almost sure to fail but forcing senators to go on the record.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Choice should not be up to a handful of right- wing judges.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Today, the Senate`s top Republican slammed those efforts.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): It just gives Americans yet another reminder that the radicals are running the show.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Now, the focus is increasingly shifting from the nation`s capitol to state capitols. Already this year, eight states have enacted measures protecting abortion rights, while changes that would bolster abortion restrictions have passed in at least 10 states.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA ZOLLER, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, GEORGIA LIFE ALLIANCE: We are ready for this change.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Martha Zoller runs the Georgia Life Alliance.\par \par \uc2 Do you expect that there is more legislation to come?\par \par \uc2 ZOLLER: I think that certainly may happen. I know for our group, we are going to be reintroducing the bill that would limit the ability to send the abortion -- the chemical abortion cocktail through the mail.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All of it setting the stage for a potential patchwork of policies across the country. Blayne Alexander, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, a fifth COVID way of maybe starting to grip most of the country. Cases are up 50 percent in the last two weeks, hospitalizations are also up, though COVID-related deaths are down 12 percent. The CDC raising several counties to high alert status in states including Massachusetts, Connecticut, and New York.\par \par \uc2 Now to the mystery in the Bahamas. Authorities today identifying three Americans found dead at a Sandals Resort there. Husband and wife Michael and Robbie Phillips from Tennessee, and Vince Chiarella of Florida. Chiarella wife Donna was airlifted to a Florida Hospital where she`s listed in fair condition. The cause of deaths is under investigation. Authorities are examining the hot water heater and air conditioner for possible leaks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220511ei590005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests Rage Across The Country Over Abortion Rights, Including Sit-Ins At The Homes Of Several Supreme Court Justices\b0\par\par\uc2 Blayne Alexander, Willie Geist \par \uc2 636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: The battle over abortion will be front and center on Capitol Hill this week after a weekend filled with protests on both sides in the wake of that leaked draft opinion suggesting the Supreme Court soon will overturn Roe versus Wade. NBC`s Blayne Alexander is in Atlanta with the latest. Blayne, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Wellie, good morning to you. Over the weekend, we did see protests in dozens of cities around the country including right here in Atlanta. Many of them were in those so called trigger law states where abortion would immediately become outlawed if Roe were to be overturned. But now the focus is increasingly shifting to local abortion laws, with many states poised to take action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): In every corner of the country, the fight over abortion rights is heating up. In Wisconsin, a Molotov cocktail was launched through the window of the headquarters of this anti-abortion rights group. And in Washington --\par \par \uc2 CROWD: The whole world is watching.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Protesters set up outside the homes of Supreme Court Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): While more gathered on the steps of the nation`s highest court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m here on Mother`s Day because everyone should have a choice whether they become a mother or not.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): It`s been a week since a leaked Supreme Court draft opinion revealed the future of Roe v. Wade is under threat. A new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll revealed nearly two-thirds of Americans want to keep Roe in place. In Washington, Senate Democrats are preparing to vote to advance a measure that would guarantee abortion rights nationwide. But it`s expected to fail because Democrats do not have the 60 votes required.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two dozen states are poised to ban abortion outright, should the landmark decision be overturned by the Supreme Court. Some state capitals are seeing a flurry of action with lawmakers advancing legislation in preparation.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH NASH, ASSOCIATE DIRECTOR OF STATE ISSUES AT THE GUTTMACHER INSTITUTE: These states have been building restriction after restriction for decades and now they`ve turned that into banned after ban.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): All of it setting the stage for a potential patchwork of laws from state to state from Mississippi Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , who state is at the center of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): The end of the day there is no right to an abortion in the United States Constitution.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): While in Illinois, Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  makes this promise.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2  (D-IL): We`re going to defend people all across this nation who want to if they need to get an abortion, they know that they can come to Illinois and they`ll be free.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:15:01]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And all of this comes as Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Senate top Republican said over the weekend in an interview with USA Today that a federal abortion ban is quote, possible if Republicans regain control in Washington, but he added that discussion is premature. Willie?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Blayne Alexander in Atlanta, Blayne, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220511ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 9, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez, Keir Simmons, Kelly Cobiella, Blayne  Alexander, Dasha Burns, Stephanie Gosk, Ken Dilanian \par \uc2 3,805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Breaking news tonight, the two Alabama fugitives caught after a nationwide manhunt.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Just a short time ago, authorities announcing an escaped murder suspect and the corrections officer accused of helping him were captured after a chase and car wreck in Indiana. The officer taken to the hospital. What we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  speaking to thousands at Russia`s Victory Day parade, marking the defeat of Nazi Germany. The Defiant message he sent to the West and new images, the bombing of a school in eastern Ukraine, more than 60 feared dead. Our teams inside Moscow and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The fight over abortion escalating in Wisconsin. An anti-abortion rights group`s office hit with a Molotov cocktail. Senate Democrats preparing a vote this week on abortion rights as the battle rages from state to state.\par \par \uc2 Mystery in paradise. Police naming three American tourists found dead at a Bahamas resort. A fourth airlifted to the U.S. What caused it?\par \par \uc2 The nationwide shortage of baby formula, the urgent warnings from pediatricians.\par \par \uc2 NBC News investigates the chilling never before heard \uc2 FBI\uc2  interview with a school shooter a year before the killings. Were warning signs missed?\par \par \uc2 And my visit was Sesame Street`s history-making new kid on the block.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening. We begin tonight with breaking news. The manhunt for that inmate who escaped from an Alabama prison with a corrections officer 10 days ago is apparently over. Authority said the inmate Casey White and the officer Vicky White are in custody in Evansville, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  said they received a tip and security camera images from a carwash in that city of a pickup truck they think that couple used. Gabe Gutierrez is here with late details. Gabe, what are authorities saying?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, just a few moments ago, authorities in Alabama said they captured the pair in Southern Indiana after a pursuit ended in a crash. Earlier in the day, security camera footage from a business in Evansville, Indiana showed a man investigators believe may have been Casey White, the inmate who was on the run.\par \par \uc2 10 days ago, police say Vicky White had said she was transporting that inmate, Casey White, no relation, from jail to the courthouse for a mental health evaluation. Officials later confirming the two had been in a relationship as far back as 2020. Investigators say they were following leads on the vehicle and that led them to Indiana. eed\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICK SINGLETON, SHERIFF, LAUDERDALE COUNTY, ALABAMA: This has ended. It`s a very long and stressful and challenging week and a half. It ended the way that we did would -- they are in custody. What I`m very thankful for tonight is that no one was hurt. No citizens were hurt. No law enforcement officers were hurt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Vicky White has just been taken to the hospital for treatment. The \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  say she has a self-inflicted gunshot wound. Casey White is in custody. No law enforcement officers fired any shots, police say. And authorities will move to extradite the pair to Alabama, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe Gutierrez with that late breaking news, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn out of that display of military might in Moscow. President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  marking Victory Day commemorating the defeat of Nazi Germany. And in his speech, Putin defending Russian actions in Ukraine against what he called Neo-Nazis. Keir Simmons is in Moscow. And a viewer of note, Russia is cracking down on journalists and limiting what reporters can say under threat of imprisonment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): A Victory Day parade without a victory, but an uncompromising message. 11,000 marching through Red Square, followed by tanks and nuclear-capable missiles. Russia`s annual military parade commemorating the Soviet victory over Nazi Germany. And among the salutes soldiers straight from the conflict in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We are fighting for our Motherland, for our future, President Putin told them, while blaming \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Ukraine for provoking it. Many Russians rallying around their president.\par \par \uc2 President Putin just walking by there. People cheering and clapping and shouting Russia.\par \par \uc2 Especially the older generation.\par \par \uc2 What did you think of President Putin`s speech?\par \par \uc2 This is a patriot president, this man says. He sacrifices his life for the sake of his people.\par \par \uc2 I love our president, says this woman. He keeps the peace, so there is no war.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:06]\par \par \uc2 But President Putin today acknowledging a toll of grief and death, presenting the Hero of Russia medal to a father for his son, a commander killed in Ukraine. God grant we no longer lose our loved ones, the dad replied.\par \par \uc2 President Putin later working with families holding pictures of relatives lost in the Second World War. In Russia, the past and the presence connected today by its president and its people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Keir joins us from Moscow tonight. Keir, what struck you in terms of differences from previous Russian Victory Day holidays and this one?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (on camera): Well, you know, Lester, in the past, world leaders have attended these events. Today, none did. And a planned flyover of fighter jets and bombers didn`t take place. The Kremlin blamed bad weather. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Keir Simmons, thank you. And in Ukraine, the war grinds on. No lead up by Russian forces today, constant attacks in the East after the deadly strike on a school building that was being used as a shelter. Kelly Cobiella has the latest from inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, dozens are still buried under a mountain of rubble that used to be a school in eastern Ukraine. Russia`s relentless attacks making it dangerous to search for bodies. Ukrainian officials say as many as 90 people were taking shelter in the school`s basement when a Russian missile hit this weekend.\par \par \uc2 A senior US defense official says Russian progress is still limited, including strikes in the south in Odessa. And the last Ukrainian evacuees from Mariupol`s besieged steel plant are now in the relative safety of Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They crushed our city, my city where I lived.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Elsewhere in Mariupol, ceremonies for Russia`s Victory Day holiday. Pro-Russian officials laying flowers, lighting an eternal flame, with the somber wreath not going according to plan in Poland where protesters pelted the Russian ambassador with red paint, chanting fascist and murderer.\par \par \uc2 Back in Ukraine, over two months of war taking a toll. How were you injured?\par \par \uc2 This Ukrainian fighter whose identity we were asked to protect is a father and worked at a telecom company. He told me he was pulling people from bombed homes on the front line when Russian forces opened fire.\par \par \uc2 What did you see?\par \par \uc2 I saw shelling of civilian houses. They don`t look at what the targets are, military or civilian, he said. They flattened everything to the ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA (on camera): That fighter told us as soon as he`s well, he`s going back to the front line. And like so many we`ve talked to, he said he sees no quick end to this war. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly Cobiella in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 From state capitols to the homes of Supreme Court justices growing protests tonight aimed at shaping the future of abortion rights in America after that leak Supreme Court draft decision that would overturn Roe v. Wade. Blayne Alexander now with that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With the landmark Roe v. Wade decision hanging in the balance, the protests are becoming increasingly personal. In Madison, Wisconsin, a pair of Molotov cocktails shattered the windows at the Wisconsin family action office, an anti- abortion group.\par \par \uc2 SHON BARNES, CHIEF, MADISON, WISCONSIN POLICE: Unfortunately, we`re investigating the fruits of hate and violence in the form of an arson.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Washington, as protesters fill the streets around the Supreme Court, others took their anger straight to the justices themselves, staging sit-ins outside the homes of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . All signs of a deepening divide one week after a leaked draft opinion through the future of Roe v. Wade into question. On Capitol Hill, Democrats will vote this week to advance the bill protecting abortion rights. That legislation almost sure to fail but forcing senators to go on the record.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Choice should not be up to a handful of right- wing judges.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Today, the Senate`s top Republican slammed those efforts.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): It just gives Americans yet another reminder that the radicals are running the show.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Now, the focus is increasingly shifting from the nation`s capitol to state capitols. Already this year, eight states have enacted measures protecting abortion rights, while changes that would bolster abortion restrictions have passed in at least 10 states.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA ZOLLER, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, GEORGIA LIFE ALLIANCE: We are ready for this change.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Martha Zoller runs the Georgia Life Alliance.\par \par \uc2 Do you expect that there is more legislation to come?\par \par \uc2 ZOLLER: I think that certainly may happen. I know for our group, we are going to be reintroducing the bill that would limit the ability to send the abortion -- the chemical abortion cocktail through the mail.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All of it setting the stage for a potential patchwork of policies across the country. Blayne Alexander, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, a fifth COVID way of maybe starting to grip most of the country. Cases are up 50 percent in the last two weeks, hospitalizations are also up, though COVID-related deaths are down 12 percent. The CDC raising several counties to high alert status in states including Massachusetts, Connecticut, and New York.\par \par \uc2 Now to the mystery in the Bahamas. Authorities today identifying three Americans found dead at a Sandals Resort there. Husband and wife Michael and Robbie Phillips from Tennessee, and Vince Chiarella of Florida. Chiarella wife Donna was airlifted to a Florida Hospital where she`s listed in fair condition. The cause of deaths is under investigation. Authorities are examining the hot water heater and air conditioner for possible leaks.\par \par \uc2 We`ll turn to the battle for the Midterms now with all eyes on Pennsylvania, the biggest state on the primary calendar next week and the next major test of Donald Trump`s endorsement power. Dasha Burns is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In final countdown to the critical Pennsylvania primary, the former president stomping in a rainy, muddy fairgrounds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You`re going to send Dr. Oz to fight for you in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The celebrity Dr. Mehmet Oz may test the limits of the magic.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I`m tough as nails and I will never let you down. God bless you, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Oz who went to medical school in Pennsylvania decades ago, but has lived and been registered to vote in New Jersey until last year, has faced criticism of his conservative bona fides.\par \par \uc2 The main thing I hear is I know who Dr. Oz is, but he`s not from here and I don`t know what he stands for. So, what do you say to those voters?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want Republican primary voters to be skeptical. Kick the tires. Ask me hard questions. Check out my campaign.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But loyal Trump fans like Melanie Patterson and Celeste Palmera say they just don`t get Trump support for Oz.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Someone is voting --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I haven`t had one person tell something favourable about oz.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have not had it and find that amazing.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: You haven`t met anybody?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. No.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: They spent three weeks door-knocking on behalf of underdog candidate Carla Sands, across deep red Westmoreland County.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She`s running against Oz. Are you familiar with all that?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What do you think of his endorsement of Oz?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m kind of surprised.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Unlike J.D. Vance in Ohio, the latest polls show Oz up just two points after Trump`s endorsement, still virtually tied with David McCormick, a former hedge fund executive from Western PA.\par \par \uc2 And in another twist, many of Mr. Trump`s former aides are throwing their support behind the McCormick. Nearly 40 percent of Republican voters here say they haven`t made up their mind.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Does his endorsement didn`t necessarily seal the deal for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I just need to learn more.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Pennsylvania now the biggest Trump test yet. Dasha Burns, NBC News, Latrobe, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, what`s behind a severe and growing shortage of baby formula? We`ve got important advice from pediatricians. What parents need to know?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, we`re back now with a growing problem across the country. Parents are desperately scrambling to find baby formula as the nationwide shortage grows even worse. Stephanie Gosk now on what pediatricians want parents to know.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Empty shelves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is really scary.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: And worried parents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A couple stores we`ve been in have not had and we`ve had to go to the next location.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: They are looking for baby formula and not finding it, at least not easily.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I myself and down to one can of formula.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: At the end of April, nearly 40 percent of popular baby formulas were sold out across the U.S. That`s up from 31 percent two weeks before. The shortage is getting worse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a pain in a butt to find.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Baby formula, like many things, has been affected by supply chain issues. Making it worse, the \uc2 Abbott Nutrition\uc2  plant in Sturgis, Michigan, one of the largest in the country, has been shuttered for months. The company issued a voluntary recall of multiple powder baby formulas in February and halted production. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is investigating conditions at the factory.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:19]\par \par \uc2 In his statement to NBC, Abbott in parts says it is implementing corrective actions and enhancements and working on resuming operations.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, retailers target \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVs are limiting the amount of baby formula products that can be bought. CVS is telling its customers they can only buy three baby formula products in one purchase.\par \par \uc2 Pediatricians have some safety tips. Don`t dilute formula.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAUREN BEENE, CLEVELAND PEDIATRICIAN, UH RAINBOW BABIES AND CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: If there`s too much water, babies can`t -- their kidneys are not mature enough to process that water and that can lead to serious health consequences.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Consider trying other brands.\par \par \uc2 BEENE: Most babies will adjust and end up doing just as well on a different brand of formula.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Make sure the formula has been stored properly and is not expired. For people who need special formula because of medical conditions, including adults, check in with your doctor. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next for us tonight, were warning signs missed before a school shooting? We hear for the first time what the gunman told the \uc2 FBI\uc2  before the attack.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now to our NBC News investigation into a 2017 New Mexico School shooting and warning signs that may have been missed. The shooter was interviewed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  long before he opened fire. And tonight, for the first time you will hear part of that interview. Here`s Ken Dilanian.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:13]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS INTELLIGENCE AND NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Looking back at something Jamie Lattin can`t escape.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE LATTIN, DAUGHTER DIED ON A SCHOOL SHOOTING: I know she`s not coming back but I haven`t had to hit that wall yet.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Haunted not just by the violent death of her 17-year-old daughter.\par \par \uc2 LATTIN: How do you describe the coolest person you`ve ever met?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: But her belief that it should have been prevented. Casey Marquez, a cheerleader and gymnastics coach was shot and killed alongside Paco Fernandez, a football player, in a hallway of their Aztec, New Mexico High School. The 21-year-old shooter ending his own life as police closed in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s just a shame that he wasn`t on our radar.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: But there were warning signs. Long before the gunman walked through an unlocked school door in 2017, his hoodie concealing a handgun, he left a trail of hate and despair online using handles like future mass shooter. In 2012, he was suspended after writing a timeline of the Columbine school shooting on a whiteboard. He dropped out. And court records say the school never told police what happened.\par \par \uc2 Then came this online threat the year before the killings, plotting mass shooting, any suggestions for weapons that are good for killing lots of people within a budget, prompting the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to send two agents to question him at home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I mean, it`s apparent you`ve studied all the mass shooting -- school shootings. You`ve studied --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not all of them.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: In this audio obtained exclusively by NBC News, the future shooter convinces the agents it`s all a sick joke.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t want to shoot people because I know what it`s been like to be through hell. My violence is through just defending people online.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: What goes through your mind when you hear that?\par \par \uc2 LATTIN: He was mocking them. He mentioned other shooters, but he`s really talking about himself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I guess my question for you is kind of what assurance can you give us?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, you guys can (BLEEP) put me on a watch list and watch and stuff.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: But there is no school shooter watch list. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  urged his parents to monitor him but close the case saying they found no threat to the community. In wrongful death lawsuits filed against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the school district, and local police, Lattin and her lawyers say the system failed.\par \par \uc2 LUIS ROBLES, ATTORNEY FOR JAMIE LATTIN: Had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just simply gone down the road and asked a few more questions, they would have known there`s something very wrong here.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: The lawsuit say the \uc2 FBI\uc2  never told the school about the online threat and that local police did not take critical action.\par \par \uc2 ROBLES: The Aztec Police Department had a police officer who was assigned to the school. That person was never told, you know, by the way, there`s a guy who was a former student who, you know, is threatening to shoot the school.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Citing the litigation, neither the school district nor the Aztec Police would comment to NBC News. In court papers, they deny wrongdoing. A judge ruled the \uc2 FBI\uc2  can`t be sued in this case, but the bureau still declined to talk.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Choi formerly ran the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as active shooter program. She reviewed the case for NBC News and defended how the bureau handled it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Even though he talked about a lot of different things, they are still just words. I don`t see anything that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should have done and they didn`t do.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Lattin says she will keep pushing for accountability, driven by her daughter`s words in a poem found after her death.\par \par \uc2 LATTIN: The songs you`ve heard, conversations you`ve had will soon become a memory.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: To make sure Casey will not be forgotten. Ken Delaney, NBC News, Aztec, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, we`ll take a break. Coming up next, celebrating a new friend in the neighborhood. I speak with Sesame Street`s groundbreaking new star.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Before we go, this is Asian-American and Pacific Islander Heritage Month. And to mark it, a special guest stop by our "NIGHTLY NEWS KID`S EDITION" program recently. Ji-Young, who is Korean American told me why she enjoys her new friends on Sesame Street.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: What do you like to share with people about your background?\par \par \uc2 JI-YOUNG, CAST, SESAME STREET: Oh, I mean, it`s really cool to share things that are special to your family like food, and holidays, and language and stuff because it makes us a stronger community to share all those things that make us different.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And for kids who are watching, I know it`s been a tough couple of years with this pandemic, how do you buck up your spirits when you`re anxious?\par \par \uc2 JI-YOUNG What does anxious mean again?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Well, anxious means you know, you`re a little afraid of what`s going on or maybe you don`t quite understand it, and it makes it nervous.\par \par \uc2 JI-YOUNG: Yes, I felt anxious before. That`s a good word to remember. You know, when you`re having big feelings, the first thing I do is name 