ing efforts this morning to contain wildfires in New Mexico. Wind gusts topping 50 miles per hour are keeping some firefighting aircraft on the ground. Wildfires now cover 275 square miles in the northern part of the state. That is more than twice the size of Philadelphia, thousands of residents have been forced to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A baby formula shortage in the U.S. is actually getting worse. Major retailers are now limiting how many products customers can buy. CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  placing a cap of three baby formula items per purchase both in stores and online, other stores have similar limits. As for what`s to blame, official site various supplier challenges and the shortage is further complicated by the closure of a major formula production facility in Sturgis, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Also making headlines this morning, a shocking and mysterious story out of the Bahamas where three Americans were discovered dead at a popular resort after being found unresponsive in two separate villas.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: NBC`s senior national correspondent Kerry Sanders has made his way to the Bahamas for us. And Kerry, good morning, we hear you`re able to speak to a couple of friends of those victims.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and it`s really tragic. I mean, Vince Chiarella and his wife Donnis had come here to the Sandals Emerald Bay Resort on Great Exuma to celebrate their 40th wedding anniversary. They and another couple sharing in the same kind of villa at the beach front, they started to feel ill. And within 24 hours Donnis was in a hospital being treated and three other Americans were dead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): This morning the picturesque Sandals Resort on Great Exuma, Bahamas now the scene of tragedy, Royal Bahamian police finding the bodies of three Americans at Sandals, Emerald Bay.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s really hard to wrap your