re going to be involved in this investigation?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, the U.S. Embassy has offered services of course they`re dealing with the notification of the next to kin of those who died. It`s expected that the Bahamian authorities later today may actually hold a news conference and give us some more details identifying all those who died here. And also giving us a schedule of when the autopsies will begin because those autopsies may indeed reveal if this was carbon monoxide poisoning or something else true.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:20]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: True. Kerry Sanders for us here on the Bahamas, Kerry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s just unthinkable isn`t?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I now.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: You go to paradise on vacation, you die. And we don`t have any idea why.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And so many families getting ready to travel for summer break too.\par \par \uc2 All right up next, inside that jaw dropping upset at the Kentucky Derby everyone is still talking about.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Rich Strike the horse not even entered one day before the race pulling off an astounding historic victory. So what is next? And how did that horse do it? We`ll ask his trainer live right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:24]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back with a closer look at that stunning upset at the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: We`ve been talking about this all morning. Rich Strike shocked the world winning the coveted first leg of the Triple Crown despite having the longest odds in the race.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We will talk to his trainer in just a moment but first NBC`s Jesse Kirsch joins us with more. Jesse, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Guys, good morning. Every time I watch that replay, I get chills. Rich Strike win one of the biggest upsets in Kentucky Derby history and he did it with help from a jockey who spent a good amount of time at tracks in Ohio including this one where they`re training right now. But on Saturday all eyes, all the magic pointing to the special place in Kentucky, \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rich Strike is coming up on the inside.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): At 80 to one odds, barely anyone give Rich Strike a shot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, goodness, the longest shot has won the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): But this morning, the three year old colt smelling the roses after racing into history.\par \par \uc2 ERIC REED, KENTUCKY DERBY WINNING TRAINER: I failed out in the paddock when he hit the wire. I passed out. I`m so happy. This is something that, you know, is the reason everybody does this.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): Rich Strike`s improbable win, all the more exhilarating because it wasn`t just an upset but a comeback too. Watch the overhead replay. In the final stretch, he went from 15th place to first just in time to win the most exciting two minutes in sports.\par \par \uc2 REED: We just knew that we had a shot because every time he went longer, he got better.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): And became a major moneymaker for anyone betting on the literal dark horse, a $2 bet paying over $160.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): This is where it happened, right here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right here.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): In Youngstown, Ohio Mark Butch (ph) bet $50 each on Rich Strike to win place and show. He made more than 6,000 bucks thanks to a hunch to bet on Rich Strike`s jockey.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Two words Sonny Leon.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): Just one part of the astonishing story behind the horses remarkable run to victory.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In a stunning, unbelievable upset.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): Rich Strike riding high seeming to nip at a pony after the race. He wasn`t even supposed to be in the race until there was a last minute vacancy on Friday. Rich Strike had only won once before and was coming off a five race losing streak into the derby. It`s the second biggest upset in Kentucky Derby history. The biggest in more than a century. Trainer Eric Reed and his father soaking it all in after the race.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your son is a Kentucky Derby winning trainer. How does that feel for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unbelievable. I can`t explain it. It`s unreal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: All of this from a horse that was purchased last June for all of $30,000, you can imagine he`s probably worth a little bit more now. And he`s got a shot to win the Triple Crown, a real life underdog story about an actual dark horse. Willie and Jota back to you. Still cannot believe it out here.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We love the horse. We love the trainer. We love the jockey. But we really love dad, I think. What a great moment. Jesse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Joining us now is one of the guys behind Rich Strike success trainer Eric Reed. Hey, Eric, we heard you say that you practically passed out when your horse won. We`re wondering how you`re feeling this morning.\par \par \uc2 REED: I`m full of life this morning.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I bet you are, Eric. Congratulations. You guys were very confident after the race. You said we wouldn`t put a horse in this race that we didn`t think could win the Kentucky Derby. But now with a couple of days distance, come clean with us. The horse before the race and hour before was 99 to one, 80 to one at post time. Did you really think Rich Strike could win the Kentucky Derby?\par \par \uc2 REED: We thought he would run really good. Nobody thought we would win. But we had set on in the day. We know the horse and the paper and the numbers and the form, I can see him being able to win. But the public didn`t know much about us. They gave us no recognition. They didn`t watch him train. His workout was the best of anybody that worked for the derby at Churchill that week. And his last two races he had finished so fast and ran by everybody in the race. So we knew the extra distance in the longest stretch and this horse had already won by 18 lengths at that track. So in our minds, we thought if he could find his way through the 17 or 18 horses he had to pass he could win. And I have some guys with me that made 60, $70,000 off their bet. So we had absolutely thought we could win.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow. Well that, Eric, that`s pretty amazing. Watching him weave his way through that field of horses was just magic. Was there a point in the race where you`re like I think we have because he looked so far behind through most of it?\par \par \uc2 [07:45:04]\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, I saw him going into the final turn start to move into contention, you know what, I thought, well, this is not a bad spot, and I knew that`s where all the traffic jam was going to take place. And that`s why I wrote Sonny because Sonny knows this horse, and he knew exactly how the horse wants to run. And I lost him right in the middle of the turn. He got that big group of horses and a friend of mine, Ken Tyson was beside me and my dad was in front of me. And I said, Kenny, I can`t find him. Where is he? And that`s about the time that het got to the top of the stretch. And he goes, he just dropped to the rail your fifth.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Wow.\par \par \uc2 REED: And I saw him and I`m looking at I said, oh my god, dad, so we might hit the board in the middle of a stretch when he passed Messier here, Ken Tyson going, oh, my God. Oh my God. And about the second time he said it, he put his head in front and I grabbed my father. I said oh, dad, we`re going to win the derby. And I don`t remember hitting the wire because I went down and they jumped on me like I scored the winning touchdown, the Super Bowl.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Well, you did basically, you really did. And what a ride by your jockey, Sonny Leon, you could almost see a moment from that view above where he decided, hey, let`s go win the Kentucky Derby. You mentioned your dad. We`ve been talking about your own personal journey. You`ve been a trainer for 40 years, your dad Herbert for 60 years. You can see all that emotion after the race. And you`ve been through a lot even recently, Eric. You had the fire at your farm. I think it was about six years ago where you lost some of those horses you loved and you weren`t sure that you`d continue in horse racing to be a trainer. How much more did that moment on Saturday mean because of all you`ve been through?\par \par \uc2 REED: It meant everything. You know, we`ve had a rough six years. We had the barn fire, which was probably the most horrific tragic thing I`d seen at that point. I had two of my best friends, assistant trainers, passed away of cancer a year and a half ago, my grandson was killed in a tragic accident. The only thing that kept us going was these horses and the love and -- that everybody gave us. And, you know, man, Rich is, America`s horses that`s keep getting all these texts and e-mails. He`s not rich. He`s America`s horse. So he`s brought us out of a really dark time.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow, Richie was something to watch. What a beautiful ending. There was a moment when the race was over, Eric, where they were describing him as feisty. He was kind of nipping at a pony and people didn`t quite know what to make of what was happening. OK, someone was punching him off. What was going on there?\par \par \uc2 REED: I`m glad you asked because I want to clear that up. The outrider`s job at the end of the race is to help get the leading horse and get him slow down and take him around. Let him do the interviews. Well, Richie, he was in killer mode, he was going to out run every horse on the track. And he had not had a horse after the finish line come over to him. So when he saw that pony coming his way he thought he had another horse to beat and he was trying to run by it. And the man did his job. He reached out and grabbed ahold of the horse and it made the horse mad.\par \par \uc2 He didn`t know that he was trying to help him. He thought he was supposed to outrun that horse. And he bit the guy`s leg terribly made his arm a couple, a really bad bites. The horse is not a mean horse. He just was in the race mode. And he didn`t understand why they were grabbing to slow him down. That man has saved my horse from injury because had he got up on the air and lost my rider. He could have got loose. I`m sorry for the injuries he sustained.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow, OK.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That`s important.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m glad, you cleared that up.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes, yes, we didn`t know all that. Well, congratulations, Eric. I assume it`s on to the Preakness for you. We can`t wait to watch. You are right, Rich Strike is America`s horse. Don`t forget, you can catch the Preakness Stakes, the next leg of the Triple Crown, Saturday, May 21st here on NBC and on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Learned a lot from that interview. Wow.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Don`t get in front of that horse.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Mr. Roker what about a check of the weather.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: So exciting. All right, let`s show you what we`ve got going on. Unfortunately, as we heard earlier, we`ve got a lot of fire weather possible, 11 million people under red flag warnings from Nevada all the way into Colorado, parts of Texas, dangerous wildfires spread potential. We`re watching that.\par \par \uc2 Here in the east, we`ve got this strong low pressure system off the coast, that`s keeping some wind, rip currents breezy winds. We`ve got some flood advisories out for 5 million people from New Jersey all the way down to the Delmarva Peninsula. Severe weather into tonight in the upper mid- Mississippi River Valley, damaging winds, tornadoes possible, and down into the Gulf, Texas. We`re talking tomorrow late in the day storm hazards, damaging winds, hail, and we can`t rule out an isolated tornado or two.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:10]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that`s your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, royal change of heart, inside Harry and Meghan`s decision to attend the Queen`s Platinum Jubilee after all with their kids.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes, plus why you won`t see them though during one very public event tied to that celebration. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Carson good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Well, good morning to everybody. Well coming up on PopStart today, we can`t wait to chat with Mandy Moore about life in Mother`s Day, this is her. She also chooses from live music. We look forward to all that coming up after a quick check your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:11]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220509ei59000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests Across The U.S. Over Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW: Back in the U.S. now, the nationwide battle over abortion rights is intensifying following last week`s leaked draft ruling from the Supreme Court. This weekend, protests across the country, including at the homes of the justices themselves.\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, a new flashpoint in the battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 In Madison, Wisconsin, police say a Molotov cocktail thrown into the headquarters of an anti-abortion group, vandalized with a warning, spray painted on the wall. If abortions aren`t safe, then you aren`t either, and conservative Supreme Court justices facing fury, protesters outside the D.C. area homes of the court`s Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , as well as Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [18:35:08]\par \par \uc2 The Trump nominee promised senators Roe v. Wade was settled precedent to get confirmed to the nation`s highest court, but now that leaked draft opinion shows Kavanaugh among five judges potentially poised to overturn that nearly 50-year precedent.\par \par \uc2 It comes as new polls show nearly two-thirds of Americans want Roe kept in place, those tensions playing out in protests across the country, Houston, Chicago, and the steps of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m here on mother`s day because everyone should have a choice whether they become a mother or not.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And renewed attention on the state. 13 have trigger laws that would automatically restrict abortion. Among them, South Dakota, already among the most restrictive state for abortion access.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH TRAXLER, PLANNED PARENTHOOD CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER: Some patients I have travel 300 miles to get here because there were the closest abortion center to them.\par \par \uc2 We are the only one in the state.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For some women, they already are very much living a postural (ph) life.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST: Women have less rights under this law than men do. How do you justify it?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): What I would say Chuck is at the end of the day, there is no right to an abortion in the United States Constitution.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Meanwhile the Senate`s top Republican leaving the door opened to a national abortion ban, saying in an interview it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Ali joins me now from the White House. So, you just mentioned the Republican Senate leader. But Democrats are talking about moving on this issue, as well.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Kate. Democrats are forging ahead, trying to codify abortion protections at the federal level. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  will move tomorrow to set up a Wednesday vote on a bill that would enshrine Roe`s protection, but it`s all but certain to fail because they just don`t have the votes. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Ali Vitali, at the White House for us. Ali, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220510ei58000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senate Democrats Are Working On A Bill That Would Legalize Abortion Nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Allie Rafa \par \uc2 1,141 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: This morning, Senate Democrats are working on a bill that would legalize abortion nationwide. The new legislation which is unlikely to pass comes in the wake of a leaked draft opinion that shows the Supreme Court is prepared to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Allie Rafa is traveling with the president in his hometown of Wilmington, Delaware. Allie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFA, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Willie.\par \par \uc2 That`s right. Conservative Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is among the latest to speak out after that leaked draft opinion to overturn Roe v. Wade. The justice telling a judicial conference on Friday, according to Reuters, that the institution can`t be bullied into making certain decisions as lawmakers continue to weigh in ahead of Wednesday`s expected vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAFA (voiceover): Eight-foot-high fencing marks the frontline of the most consequential battle over abortion rights in decades. As fallout continues, nearly one week after the leak of the draft Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade shook the country.\par \par \uc2 While Americans await Roe`s fate, the Senate`s top Democrat is launching a last stitch effort to protect it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY) MAJORITY LEADER: The \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is going to vote on legislation to codify women`s right to seek an abortion into federal law.\par \par \uc2 RAFA (voiceover): But Democrats lack the 60 votes needed to overcome a filibuster in the 50-50 Senate or even pass the bill without it.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: We`re going to see where everyone stands.\par \par \uc2 RAFA (voiceover): Democrats instead turning the vote into a midterm message against Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: You will hear lot from us through the next months, all the way through November.\par \par \uc2 RAFA (voiceover): The vice president even addressing the upcoming battle during a commencement speech at \uc2 Tennessee State University\uc2 , Saturday.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: We are once again forced to defend fundamental principles that we hoped were long settled. Principles like the freedom to vote, the rights of women to make decisions about their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 RAFA (voiceover): Republicans meanwhile focused on unmasking the leaker.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The person who leaked this draft opinion, you`re the dumbest guy in town or gal in town. It`s going to backfire.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): They need to be punished to the full extent that is allowable because this does damage the integrity of the court.\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 RAFA (voiceover): Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  stressing in a statement Tuesday that the draft does not represent the court`s final decision, directing Supreme Court martial colonel Gail Curley to investigate the source of the leak.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAFA (on camera): Meanwhile, back on Capitol Hill, Senate minority and possibly majority leader come November. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , telling USA Today this weekend that national ban on abortion is possible If Roe is overturned this summer. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Allie Rafa with the president at Wilmington, Delaware. Allie, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Todd is NBC`s political director and moderator of "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 Chuck, good morning. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to talk about the practical implications of the overturning of Roe v. Wade, if that does indeed happen in just a moment. But let`s talk about the politics that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and others were just discussing an Allie`s piece there. It`s been interesting to see Democrats energized by this going into the midterms where they`re worried, obviously, and also to watch Republicans, how they`ve handled this.\par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe v. Wade has long been the holy grail, and yet, they`re more focused on the leak than on the substances, Roe v. Wade. What are the politics here?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MEET THE PRESS MODERATOR: Well look, I think -- watch what they do, not what they say. And it looks pretty clear to me that the Republicans are nervous, that -- the elected Republicans that is -- that this is going to backfire on them, that it hands Democrats something to get their base out.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just look at younger voters, Willie, they were already having -- Democrats were having a hard time sort of making the case that, hey, look at what unified democratic government has gotten you, you know, build back better hadn`t gotten well. So, there was a lot of nervousness. What`s it going to take to get the Democratic base excited?\par \par \uc2 Well, in our politics these days, Willie, you know, this fear works more than success to rally the troops. And I think that the Democrats now have their fear issue. And if this can turn out, younger voters, at presidential levels at a midterm then you`re looking at a more leveled playing field than we expected going into this midterm.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And then, the Democrats think they need that to have any chance, at least, in the House. Chuck, thanks so much. We`ve just scratched the surface.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, buddy.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Chuck has a lot to talk about this morning on a special edition of "Meet the Press" about the future of abortion rights in America. Among Chuck`s guests, Mississippi Republican Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  and Michigan`s Democratic attorney general Dana Nessel.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now get a quick check of your local Mother`s Day forecast.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Straight ahead, the highs and lows of the week, including the amazing story behind one of the world`s biggest stars, \uc2 Ed Sheeran\uc2 , teaming up for a song with a Ukrainian band whose lead singer is on the frontlines of the war.\par \par \uc2 And a beautiful moment from baseball when a home run ball leads to hugs, tears, and a young fan meeting his larger-than-life hero. But up next, our "Sunday Focus" on that leak that shook the country this week as a draft opinion from the Supreme Court chose Roe v. Wade may be struck down after nearly 50 years. If so, what is the future of abortion in America?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN, HOPE MEDICAL GROUP FOR WOMEN: If we cannot no longer provide care here. I have no idea where a lot of these women will go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s all coming up on "Sunday TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220510ei58000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 8, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Matt Bradley, Ali Vitali, Sam Brock, Emilie Ikeda,  Kerry Sanders, Jesse Kirsch, Catie Beck \par \uc2 4,177 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:30:00]\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the surprise visit inside Ukraine as the U.S. announces new sanctions against Russia. First Lady Jill Biden and the first lady of Ukraine meeting face-to-face, the first time President Zelenskyy`s wife has been seen in public since the war began.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: This war has to stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: President Biden today meeting virtually with Zelenskyy and world leaders and diplomats returning to the U.S. embassy in Kyiv. But the war rages on. Ukraine says a Russian attack on a shelter kilted 60.\par \par \uc2 Protests across the U.S. over abortion rights, even outside the homes of Supreme Court justices, and police say a Molotov cocktail caused this damage at the offices of an anti-abortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 The price of gas about to top off at a new national high, some states smashing records.\par \par \uc2 Mystery in the Bahamas, three Americans dead at the same resort. The investigation now into what killed them as these villas that cause thousands per night.\par \par \uc2 New video of the corrections officer believed to be on the run with an escaped inmate.\par \par \uc2 And the greatest underdog victory in more than century, the amazing angle on this historic derby run you have to see.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. The first lady of the United States said she intentionally chose to cross into Ukraine on Mother`s Day, a surprise show of support for the Ukrainian people. For half an hour, Dr. Jill Biden met face-to-face with her counterpart, the first lady of Ukraine, \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2  who has been out of sight with her children since the war started. Dr. Biden asked Zelenska how she was doing personally. She answered she gets to hold her children`s hands every night, even if she can`t be with her husband.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy and Biden meantime joined leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2  to commemorate the end of World War II, vowing to help defeat Russia. As Russian attacks continued today, some American staff returned to the U.S. embassy in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley starts us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In Ukraine today, a surprise visit by the First Lady, Dr. Jill Biden entering an active war zone to meet with Ukraine`s first lady this Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I thought it was important to share with the Ukrainian people that this war has to stop, and this war has been brutal, and that people of the United States stand with the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Sunday, spouses took place in a school that was housing those displaced by war. It also marked the first time Ukraine`s first lady was seen in public since war begun in late February. \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2  and her two children have been labeled targets, hiding at an undisclosed location.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I would like to thank you for a very courageous act, she said.\par \par \uc2 The first lady`s visit coming as Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  met virtually with leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2 , a gathering of the world`s wealthiest democratic countries. The U.S. and its allies announcing a fresh round of sanctions targeting Russian state controlled media and elites. Members also agreed to ban all imports of Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 As attacks continue in the east, a Russian missile struck a school. President Zelenskyy says about 60 people sheltering inside were killed. Only seven managed to escape. It`s unlikely others have survived this man said. The crater is down to the basement. It`s horrible.\par \par \uc2 But in Kyiv, the Ukrainian resistance got a surprise guest. Pop Star Bono giving a concert in a metro station turned bomb shelter, a musical encore for yet another bloody Sunday in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY (on camera): So, tomorrow, the Russians will celebrate Victory Day with a military parade in Moscow. But Ukrainians are watching anxiously, fearful that a new and even harsher assault could be coming. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Matt Bradley, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back in the U.S. now, the nationwide battle over abortion rights is intensifying following last week`s leaked draft ruling from the Supreme Court. This weekend, protests across the country, including at the homes of the justices themselves.\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, a new flashpoint in the battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 In Madison, Wisconsin, police say a Molotov cocktail thrown into the headquarters of an anti-abortion group, vandalized with a warning, spray painted on the wall. If abortions aren`t safe, then you aren`t either, and conservative Supreme Court justices facing fury, protesters outside the D.C. area homes of the court`s Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , as well as Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [18:35:08]\par \par \uc2 The Trump nominee promised senators Roe v. Wade was settled precedent to get confirmed to the nation`s highest court, but now that leaked draft opinion shows Kavanaugh among five judges potentially poised to overturn that nearly 50-year precedent.\par \par \uc2 It comes as new polls show nearly two-thirds of Americans want Roe kept in place, those tensions playing out in protests across the country, Houston, Chicago, and the steps of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m here on mother`s day because everyone should have a choice whether they become a mother or not.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And renewed attention on the state. 13 have trigger laws that would automatically restrict abortion. Among them, South Dakota, already among the most restrictive state for abortion access.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH TRAXLER, PLANNED PARENTHOOD CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER: Some patients I have travel 300 miles to get here because there were the closest abortion center to them.\par \par \uc2 We are the only one in the state.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For some women, they already are very much living a postural (ph) life.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST: Women have less rights under this law than men do. How do you justify it?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): What I would say Chuck is at the end of the day, there is no right to an abortion in the United States Constitution.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Meanwhile the Senate`s top Republican leaving the door opened to a national abortion ban, saying in an interview it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Ali joins me now from the White House. So, you just mentioned the Republican Senate leader. But Democrats are talking about moving on this issue, as well.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Kate. Democrats are forging ahead, trying to codify abortion protections at the federal level. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  will move tomorrow to set up a Wednesday vote on a bill that would enshrine Roe`s protection, but it`s all but certain to fail because they just don`t have the votes. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Ali Vitali, at the White House for us. Ali, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The price of gas is going up again. We`re now poised to reach a new nationwide record as soon as tomorrow. Last year, as we emerged from COVID lockdowns, the average cost per gallon was just $2.96. Today, the national average is just under $4.32 and rising.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Sam Brock with just how high prices could go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS (voice over): States coast to coast are smashing the records for gas prices tonight, from New York and Illinois, to Oregon and Washington, ride as the beaches beckon for travel season, and families are forced to deal with the familiar foe, high fuel costs.\par \par \uc2 Is it sticker shock?\par \par \uc2 DANETTE JONES, FLORIDA RESIDENT: I just close my eye. I pretend that I am not spending any money. I see it once my account get close to zero.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: With the standard of value shifting dramatically.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right now, currently, you know, I come here because you know it`s $4.09 and I kind of support it.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The numbers poised to pop as many people are on the move.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN, GASBUDDY PETROLEUM ANALYSIS HEAD: It`s never a great time to see rising in gas prices, but the worst timing is right now and lead up to Memorial Day. The term, staycation, could again see resurgence.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The current average across the U.S. is just under $4.32 a gallon, up from $4.14 a month ago. For one South Florida father, he had to reverse course and cancel a family trip planned for this summer.\par \par \uc2 Is it making you think twice now about filling up your tank?\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL MESTRE, FLORIDA RESIDENT: Three times, to tell you the truth.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: As for those feeling the pinch at the pump, it`s not spread evenly. This map from AAA with the most expensive states in red shows the west coast region in the northeast hammered particularly hard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hopefully they don`t get much higher, but just try to look for the best price and that`s about it.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Making this Memorial Day holiday for many a financial balancing act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Sam joins me from Miami. Sam, a lot of us don`t want a staycation. How high do they think the prices could go?\par \par \uc2 BROCK (on camera): Look, Kate, analysts right now think that $4.50 a gallon nationally is a virtual guarantee, with many big cities like Miami at $5.50 or higher. And if China eases lockdowns globally, demand goes up, that means prices will as well. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Sam Brock, thank you.\par \par \uc2 COVID cases are rising sharply nationwide with new sub-variants popping up and posing new challenges to current vaccines. But how concerned should we be?\par \par \uc2 Emilie Ikeda, reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tonight, COVID is making a comeback with the number of cases surging more than 50 percent in the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHWIN VASAN, NEW YORK CITY HEALTH AND MENTAL HYGIENE DEPARTMENT COMMISSIONER: It feels like ground hog day for so many of us during this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: All but one state seeing an increase in infections, even as numbers are likely underreported due to at-home-testing. New York again the epicenter of what is looking like a fifth wave, Governor Hochul testing positive just today.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever get the sense that it feels every time it feels the pandemic might be fading, we start to see these cases come up again?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SILVERMAN, PENELOPE NYC OWNER: We are -- I`m on a tight rope. It`s minute to minute at this point. As a restaurateur, you don`t know what to think.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Omicron emerged just six months ago, sending cases and hospitalizations soaring to unprecedented heights over the winter. Since then, new subvariants have continued to pop up and are driving the latest uptick in infections.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:02]\par \par \uc2 One of them now makes up more than one-third of current cases.\par \par \uc2 Research suggests the new versions are getting better at evading protection from vaccines and previous infection. That means if you contracted omicron, you could get sick again.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: So, it`s just a way of the virus getting smarter. That happens every time it reproduces. So, this means it translates to more people getting infected as a result.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: But in a glimmer of hope, health experts say the severity of cases does not appear to be worsening. So far, hospitalizations and deaths aren`t rising as quickly, which the White House credits to high booster rates, treatments and testing. Though it`s what lies ahead that is of greater concern with estimates of 100 million infections by fall or winter.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR: If we don`t get ahead of this thing, we`re going to have a lot of waning immunity, this virus continues to evolve, and we may see a pretty sizable wave of infections, hospitalizations and deaths this fall and winter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Emilie joins me now on set. So, are we getting any closer to vaccines that could target these new variants?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): Kate, so \uc2 Moderna\uc2  and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  are actively working on new shots to better target the super contagious strains, but what`s not clear is how many people would even take advantage of another booster. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right, Emilie, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, mystery in paradise, how did three Americans die at an exclusive five star resort in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: We`re back with the mysterious death of three Americans in the Bahamas. They were found unresponsive in two different villas at an exclusive five-star resort. Kerry Sanders is there and spoke with fellow guests who knew the victims.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): On the island paradise of Exuma Bahamas, a mystery is still unfolding tonight. Why are three American tourists who stayed at the Sandals Emerald Bay Resort dead? Hotel guests we spoke with say the two couples did not know each other, were on either side of a split beachfront villa. At least one couple had complained to friends about nausea and dizziness.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is a tragic situation and it`s really hard to wrap your brain around.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: The resort posts the villas can cost thousands of dollars a night. Kevin and Kara Rainbolt (ph) from Nashville say they had made friends with Vince and Donnis Chiarella, who were celebrating a 40th wedding anniversary.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very sweet couple. Sorry. Very sweet couple, just really good hearted.\par \par \uc2 [18:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Donnis Chiarella survived and was airlifted to a Miami hospital.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We were told, I don`t know firsthand, was that Donnis woke up feeling paralyzed.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Paralyzed?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Paralyzed is what was reported to us. Couldn`t move, said her arms and legs were swollen and that she could see Vince lying on the floor.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: At that point, it was -- it may be that he was dead at that point?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It could be, yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Health experts tell police here, dizziness, nausea, temporary paralysis are symptoms that suggest carbon monoxide poisoning. Bahamian police are examining the villa`s water heater and air conditioner and the propane supply for a leak.\par \par \uc2 The yellow crime scene tape has been taken down. The resort remains open, most guest unaware of what happened.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My biggest concern was three people passed away within several hours of each other all in the same villa, another became very ill. So, am I safe to go to sleep at night?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): Sandals management says it`s sending it`s condolences to the families involved. Meantime, a government autopsy may reveal what killed these Americans. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Kerry Sanders, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the jaw dropping come from behind win that`s got everyone talking. Did you see it? The horse, Rich Strike, took first place in the Kentucky derby yesterday. And get this, he was such a long shot, as of just days ago, he wasn`t even supposed to be in the race.\par \par \uc2 Jessie Kirsch now on how he pulled it off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rich Strike is coming up on the inside.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS (voice over): He was a literal dark horse at 80-1 odds.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my goodness, the longest shot has won the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Rich Strike, the longest long shot of the last century to win the most exciting two minutes in sports.\par \par \uc2 ERIC REED, RICH STRIKE TRAINER: I fell down to patio (ph) when he hit the wire. I`m about passed out. I`m so happy.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: The three-year-old colt wasn`t even supposed to race until the day before --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re off.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: -- when another horse scratched. And the overhead replay is even more incredible. That`s Rich Strike in 15th place, heading into the final stretch, somehow waving and charging to the front, with help from Jockey Sonny Leone.\par \par \uc2 REED: Sonny Leone, they know who he is now.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: But Leone was already a familiar face at race track in Eastern Ohio. Announcer Matt Hook even has a catch phrase for days when Leone wins more than once.\par \par \uc2 Does it surprise you that he pulled it off?\par \par \uc2 MATT HOOK, TRACK ANNOUNCER: Not that Sonny pulled it off. I`m surprised Rich Strike pulled it off. I know Sonny is good enough. He`s got a lead strength and he`s an elite tactician.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: There`s where it happened right here. Mark Butt says knowing Leone from this Youngstown track inspired $50 bets on Rich Strike to win, place, and show, winning Butch more than $6,000.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I still got the chills and I`m still shaking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Forget Rich Strike, Lightning Strike.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: With a thunderclap echoing around the world.\par \par \uc2 Jesse Kirsch, NBC News, Youngstown, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: We are back in a moment with the new way homeowners of fighting back against giant companies that are driving up real estate prices.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Officials have a possible new clue in that Alabama manhunt mystery. This newly released video shows Corrections Officer Vicky White leaving an inn the day she disappears along with Inmate Casey White. That was April 29th. The two are not related, but the Lauderdale County sheriff believes that she helped him escape the jail where she was awaiting trial for murder.\par \par \uc2 Now to our series, Priced Out, tonight, the little known reason why it`s becoming so hard to buy a home. In some areas, big corporations are buying up residential real estate and then renting it out. Now, neighborhoods are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 Catie Beck has more on what`s being called the investor invasion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS (voice over): Keri Miller says her community in Charlotte, North Carolina, looks different than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 KERI MILLER, AVALON AT MALLARD CREEK HOMEOWNERS ASSOCIATION TREASURER: If you put in the effort and people see you putting in the effort, they are going to want to put in the effort.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Lawns are better kept. Neighbors are looking out for each other. She says a frightening shooting outside a rental home last February sparks change.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: It took that for folks to really open their eyes.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Miller blames the problem on what she calls the investor invasion, a growing trend of corporations buying up single family homes and converting them into a rental properties.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: People come in and buy a property and you never see them again.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Across town in the Potters Glen Subdivision, Vincent Evans says a flurry of investor buying was followed by rental properties with poor up keep, a lack of tenant screening and criminal activity.\par \par \uc2 VINCENT EVANS, POTTERS GLEN HOMEOWNERS ASSOCIATION PRESIDENT: These investment companies, they want to make money. That`s what they`re there for.\par \par \uc2 BECK: There`s no question that investors are making big buys in residential real estate. Numbers from last year show they were behind 18 percent of all home sales nationwide. And here in Charlotte, 32 percent of all home sales went to an investor.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You`re pricing people out and not giving people the opportunity to acquire that equity.\par \par \uc2 BECK: With high demand and low inventory, first-time home buyers face poor odds to beat out big money investors, some backed by Wall Street. So, homeowners association leaders like Miller and Evans have gone to work changing the rules.\par \par \uc2 EVANS: We added a clause that new homeowners have to live in their house for two years before it can be rented or leased. So, while we don`t target the investors, per se, it does that in a roundabout way.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Miller`s board put a one-year requirement in place, as well as capping rental capacity at 47 percent of homes in the community. Both have seen a significant decline in investor buying and say conditions and safety have improved. But not all are convinced the process is fair.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOWARD, NATIONAL RENTAL HOME COUNCIL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: This is about home owners associations attempting to dictate who can and cannot live in their community.\par \par \uc2 BECK: The investor industry says HOAs are overreaching their authority.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: These artificial constructs created by home owners associations infringe on the pursuit of housing by making housing more restrictive, less accessible.\par \par \uc2 BECK: What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 EVANS: I disagree. When you purchase your home here, you have the opportunity to read and review the documents. You sign off on it.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Do you think more HOA should be fighting back?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Absolutely. I want to let them know, like if you build a relationship within your community, you can get this done, as well.\par \par \uc2 BECK: An ongoing debate on what is fair when some take profit in a community and others take pride.\par \par \uc2 Katie Beck, NBC News, Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: When we come back, dynamic duo, the remarkable story behind the success of this mother-daughter pair of sheriff`s deputies.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: There`s good news on this Mother`s Day about leading by example, and the sheriff`s deputy daughter who decided to honor her mom by following in her footsteps.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice over): This Mother`s Day weekend, East Baton Rouge Louisiana sheriff`s deputies Kawande Harris-Lane and Katrea Harris are celebrating a milestone. The mother/daughter duo, now united in serving their community.\par \par \uc2 When did you know you might want to follow in your mom`s footsteps?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So I was just like, I want to be just like here when I grew up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That`s the first time I heard it. That again broke my heart.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: For both mom and daughter, the journey wasn`t easy. You were 13 when you got pregnant, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was 13 when I gave birth, yes. Luckily this was the outcome of it and so I didn`t let to stop me.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: The young mother persevered and sacrificed, earning one degree after the next, all the way to PHD inspiring her daughter along the way. What was your mom like growing up?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t think I remember her ever sleeping. She was always on the go. She`s still like that until these days. Anything I had going on, she was there.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: When Katrea was diagnosed with a chronic bowel disease at just 9 years old, Kawande quit her job to take care of her, then joined the sheriff`s department a couple years later.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I remember her saying, if I`m strong, then it`s going to show you that young be strong, too.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Katrea has been hospitalized more than 90 times in her life. In 2020, she lost the ability to walk. Last year, she had major surgery. So it was stunning these January when she started the grueling training to become a law enforcement officer, being sworn in just days ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When they announced her name, it was like, I had to stay professional because I was in uniform, but I wanted to be that parent that ran around the arena to say that`s my child. It`s my daughter.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Colleagues now, they`re celebrating Mother`s Day knowing they`ve always got each other`s back.\par \par \uc2 What would you say about your mom`s role in all of this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She`s the whole reason I applied in the first place.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: How proud are you Kawande?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m very proud. This doesn`t happen every day. I mean do the same profession and they have push on the shove one day, I make and save her life and she making save my. This Mother`s Day is one of the best in my books.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (on camera): And we would like to wish all the mom`s out there, a very, very happy Mother`s Day. That is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for this Sunday night. I`m Kate Snow. For all of us here at NBC News, stay safe and have a great night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220509ei5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for May 8, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ali Vitali, Sara Fagen; Kimberly Atkins Stohr \par \uc2 9,545 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:21]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, the fate of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A divided country reacts to that draft Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve been praying for this as long as I`ve understood what a baby was. And it`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is an attack on women.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In Washington, strong reaction from abortion rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: It`s an assault on women.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , (D-MI): I`m going to fight like hell to protect this right for Michigan women.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LISA MURKOWSKI (R-AK): It rocks my confidence in the court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: While most Republicans shy away from the issue and instead focus on the leak.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): It`s a breach of protocol. I think it will compromise the ability of the court to find consensus.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): The most egregious breach of trust at the Supreme Court that has ever happened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Plus, the fallout. Will this galvanize Democrats heading into the midterm election?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): They want to get off on the things that are so unpopular about this president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Also, could a nationwide ban be the next step? Are other rights in jeopardy? And can either party find a position that meets Americans where they are? I`ll talk to Mississippi`s Republican Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , who supports banning most abortions, and to Michigan`s Attorney General Dana Nessel who opposes an automatic abortion ban that would go into effect in her state.\par \par \uc2 Also, inside the court`s decision-making process with former Supreme Court clerks \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  and Jennifer Mascott.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Plus our panel, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, Sara Fagen, Josh Gerstein, and Ali Vitali. Welcome to Sunday and a special edition of MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is a special edition of MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A good Sunday morning and a happy Mother`s Day to all the moms out there including my mother, grandmother and wife. The draft decision overturning Roe v. Wade has only added to the growing perception that the Supreme Court is just one more partisan institution, now a creature of politics, no longer above it. The leaked decision was a victory for conservatives who have worked decades for this moment, and it was a shock to millions of Americans who have never lived in a world without abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 In this morning`s special edition we`re going to cover this story from all angles, the real-world fallout, the confusing checkerboard of state laws that would soon be in effect along with real questions about what other rights might be in jeopardy. The political fallout, many Democrats believe the issue will help them in the midterms, but the idea that overturning Roe would energize Democratic voters has never been tested. It will be now.\par \par \uc2 Plus the court itself, and its broken confirmation process, manipulated most recently by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  to manufacture this conservative majority that appears ready to overturn Roe in a country that apparently wants it preserved. With 50 states making their own rules, it all adds up to further division in a country already split in two.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Women`s rights in America are under attack.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: We are going to win in 2022. Roe v. Wade is not going to change the outcome.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice-over): After the leak of that early draft ruling reversing Roe --\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, HOST: The Supreme Court appears poised to overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- governors and state legislators are rushing to define their positions in a post-Roe world.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BILL LEE (R-TN): The lives of unborn children are -- it`s very important that we protect the lives of them.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Where is the Democratic Party? Where`s the party? Why aren`t we calling this out? This is a concerted, coordinated effort. And yes, they`re winning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Should Roe be overturned, at least 25 states will likely ban abortion. Thirteen of them have trigger bans, meaning it will immediately be outlawed.\par \par \uc2 TAMMI KROMENAKER, DIRECTOR, RED RIVER WOMEN`S CLINIC: It means lights out in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER, DIRECTOR, JACKSON WOMEN`S HEALTH ORGANIZATION: It`s a shot in the face, it`s just like, bam, to women of color especially.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE GLENN FOSTER, PRESIDENT, AMERICANS UNITED FOR LIFE: We`re going to continue to focus on those women, focusing on the humanity of those women.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Democrats are hoping to turn voter attention away from high inflation ahead of the midterms by drawing a sharp contrast on abortion rights --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and warning that Republicans, via the court`s conservative majority, could take away or erode other rights, from access to contraception to same-sex marriage protections. National Republicans have focused on the leak itself --\par \par \uc2 [09:05:03]\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): The leak is reprehensible.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Egregious breach of trust.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: We`ll find the leaker.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- and largely avoided questions about the future of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, that`s not the story for today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A document circulated by the NRSC, the campaign committee in charge of getting Senate Republicans elected, advises Republican candidates to -- quote -- "be the compassionate consensus builder on abortion policy."\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): There should be an exception for rape and incest. So I think that`s where -- I think that`s where the American public is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Already, Democrats are raising the issue in competitive midterm races, from Michigan to Florida, New Hampshire, Nevada to Wisconsin --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GODLEWSKI, WISCONSIN STATE TREASURER: I`m at the Supreme Court, where it looks like Ron Johnson is going to get exactly what he wants, overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- as both parties try to define each other by their most extreme positions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you ever support a ban that does not make exceptions for incest or rape or life-threatening conditions?\par \par \uc2 GARY BLACK (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I support a total ban.\par \par \uc2 JOSH CLARK (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 KELVIN KING (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: There should not be exceptions.\par \par \uc2 JON MCCOLUMN (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: The answer is no.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In a recent Pew poll, just eight percent of Americans say abortion should be illegal in all cases, without exception. Thirty-seven percent overall say it should be illegal, including those who want exceptions. Sixty-one percent say it should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 Still, though a majority of Americans support Roe, Senate Democrats do not have the votes to codify it in legislation. They expect to take it up and fail to pass it later this week.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: This is not just one vote and then this issue goes away. You will hear a lot from us through the next months, all the way through November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mississippi is one of the 13 states with trigger laws that would ban abortion once Roe is overturned. And, of course, the draft opinion is about a case in Mississippi. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  is the governor of Mississippi, an opponent of abortion rights, and he joins me now. Governor Reeves, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): Well, thank you for having me on this morning, Chuck. And as an aside, happy Mother`s Day to all those moms out there, particularly the first lady of Mississippi and my mom and grandmom.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me -- let me double down on that and wish them a happy Mother`s Day as well. Let me ask about this case itself. One of the odder aspects of your law is that this law appears to have been passed with the intent of hoping it would not be enacted. The reason I say is you passed a 15-week ban after this trigger law was passed in `07, and I know you were a lieutenant governor when the 15-week ban was. But the whole point of this was to get a case to the Supreme Court to overturn Roe. So will the 15-week ban, if Roe is overturned, ever be implemented in the state of Mississippi?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, you`re right that the trigger law was put into effect in 2007. I should point out, and I think this really points towards, sort of, the changing approach with the two political parties on this issue, but the 2007 trigger law was actually enacted with a Democrat speaker of the House. And it was enacted with a Democrat chairman of the Public Health Committee.\par \par \uc2 But when we enacted the 15-week ban, our initial intent and our goal was really just to save babies` lives. We believe that if Roe was not ever overturned, that it was certainly the viewpoint of Mississippians that if we could enact laws that would save babies` lives that it would be an endeavor to do so.\par \par \uc2 If in fact the leaked opinion, Chuck, is accurate, and if in fact this court votes to overturn Roe, you are correct. Our trigger law will go into effect, and we will ban abortions with the exceptions of rape and the life of the mother because of that trigger law that passed in 2007.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I noticed that trigger law doesn`t include an exception for incest. Why?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, I wasn`t in the legislature or the executive branch at that time. That was a decision that was made by the Mississippi legislature. And I think it`s certainly a conversation. There are exceptions for rape. There are exceptions for the life of the mother. And we`ll see what happens based upon the ultimate outcome of this Dobbs case that is before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What about contraception and birth control, particularly IUDs? I mean, is this total ban that gets put in on abortion, is that going to have an impact on women that decide to have certain types of birth control like IUDs?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: I don`t think that it is going to apply to those that choose to use birth control. I believe that clearly a life begins at conception. And I am trying very hard to make sure that everyone in America knows that the overturning of Roe certainly puts the decision-making on abortion policy back in the elected representatives in each of the 50 states.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:05]\par \par \uc2 That, by the way, is where the decision-making was in America for the first 200 years of our country until 1972 when, in my opinion, Roe was wrongly decided.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting you say that that the Supreme Court`s going to take something, take away a right that was in the hands of individual women. And I want to play something for you from Senator Kirsten Gillibrand, who believes this decision essentially takes away some of her citizenship. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-NY): I hope every human being in this country understands that when you take away a woman`s right to make her decisions about her health and well-being, she is no longer a full citizen. She no longer has freedom. She no longer has bodily autonomy. She no longer has basic civil rights or civil liberties.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Respond to her. How does this not take away a woman`s right to control her own body?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, that right that she is talking about, there is no right to an abortion in the United States Constitution. In fact, there`s also nothing in the U.S. Constitution which precludes individual states from regulating abortion policy.\par \par \uc2 And what I would say to you, Chuck -- and look, I`m empathetic to all of these ladies who find themselves in very difficult times and very difficult decisions. But what makes this different, what makes abortion different, is if you believe as I believe, that`s an unborn child in that mother`s womb. That is -- what we are trying to do is stand up for the rights of those unborn children. Stand up for those who absolutely cannot stand up for themselves. That`s why this decision is so important. It`s also why there`s very high emotions on both sides of this particular topic. And I understand that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you`ve just said that you believe life begins at conception. If there is legislation brought to you to ban contraception, would you sign it?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, I don`t think that`s going to happen in Mississippi. I`m sure they`ll have those conversations in other states.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you`re not answering the question.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: As is always the case with things -- well, that`s always the case. There`s so many things that we can talk about. What the next movement in the pro-life movement in my view, Chuck, is simple. And that is we must prove that being pro-life is not just about anti-abortion. What we want to do next is we want to continue to focus on the two things that are very important, and that is ensuring that those expectant mothers have the resources that they need. That`s why in Mississippi this year we invested significant additional resources in pregnancy resource centers and that we are working to build a system. We have 37 of those in Mississippi, and we`re working to build a system throughout our state to ensure that every expectant mother has access to the information and the education that they need.\par \par \uc2 The second piece of the equation, and the second piece of the next phase of the pro-life movement, is we`ve got to make sure that we make it easier on those babies that are born, either through the potential for adoptive services -- we`ve got to make it easier for adoptions. We`ve got to find resources for adoptions. We`ve got to also make sure that we improve our foster care system. In Mississippi, for instance, we invested over $100 million combined of our ARPA funds as well as other state funds to improve technology at our Department of Human Services, at our Child Protection Services so that those babies that are not adopted that end up in our foster care system, that we care for them and we do so in a way that recognizes the importance of the next phase of the movement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor -- Governor, though, you`re a state that really doesn`t do a good job of helping children. One in three Mississippi children live in poverty. If you`re going to order women to be pregnant -- and that`s what your law is going to do, the state is going to order women to stay pregnant. You`re talking about providing resources while they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 What are you going to do for that child after they`re born? What are you going to do for that mother? You know, again, I look back at Mississippi`s numbers here. Child poverty is already at a level that is, to me, should be unsustainable. Why should we believe that you`re going to provide the resources for these women if they have these babies?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, look, Chuck, I mean, it`s a fair question, and one that what I want you to know is that when I was sworn into office in the middle of January of 2020, in my inaugural address I made it very clear that my job as governor is not to try to hide our challenges, not to try to hide our problems, but to find solutions to those problems and that`s what we`re going to continue to do.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:02]\par \par \uc2 It`s the reason I just mentioned that we are investing $100 million in technology for our Department of Child Protection Services. There`s no doubt that our -- we have a long history of health outcomes that are not acceptable. We have a long history of poverty. And the way in which you address poverty -- government has real challenges doing that. But the way in which you address poverty is by improving educational attainment, improving educational outcomes, and by improving the opportunities and job skills for those individuals so that they can go to work, and provide for themselves and provide for their families. And those are the -- it`s a multifaceted approach. And it`s certainly not easy. And I would never argue that it is. But I can tell you with certainty that in Mississippi we`re working hard every day to address each and every one of those issues.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor, the last thing is, at the end of the day, women have less rights under this law than men do. How do you justify it?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: What I would say, Chuck, is at the end of the day there is no right to an abortion in the United States Constitution, that the issue with abortions that makes it very different is that there is a life. There is an American child in that womb. And it`s incumbent upon those of us who are elected to stand up for the rights of those individuals that can`t stand up for themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , Republican governor of Mississippi, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The story in Michigan is a bit different from Mississippi`s. Michigan, typically a blue state, has an abortion ban that was passed in 1931, and it would go into effect if Roe v. Wade was overturned. The state`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that she will keep fighting like hell to preserve abortion rights. Dana Nessel is Michigan`s attorney general. She joins me now. Madame Attorney General, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL (D), MICHIGAN ATTORNEY GENERAL: Thanks for having me, Chuck, and happy Mother`s Day to my mom and to all the moms out there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a happy Mother`s Day to you and also your mom. Let me start with the decision you`ve already made. You said if Roe is overturned, you are not going to enforce this 1931 law. But you can`t prevent others in the state from enforcing the law. Explain.\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Well, there`s 83 duly-elected prosecutors for every county in our state. As attorney general, I have statewide jurisdiction. And I ran on a platform of understanding that likely during the course of my term, Roe v. Wade would be overturned. And this incredibly draconian and strict 1931 law would criminalize abortion in this state with virtually no exceptions. No exception for rape, for incest, no exception for medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 And understanding that the lives of our 2.2 million women who are of childbearing age in this state, their lives would be at risk. I refuse to enforce this draconian law that will endanger their lives and put in jeopardy the health, safety and welfare of the lives of each and every woman in the state of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I want to bring up -- it`s part of the Michigan penal code, and I want to note one other aspect of this law. It actually makes mention of the use of drugs. "Any person who shall in any manner sell or publicly expose for sale drugs or combination of drugs, designed expressly for the use of females for the purpose of procuring an abortion, shall be guilty of a misdemeanor. The druggist or dealer selling the same shall register the name of the purchaser, the date of the sale and then the name and residence of the physician prescribing the same."\par \par \uc2 Madame Attorney General, this is a -- it seems like a draconian law and puts the doctor and the woman in criminal jeopardy. Am I reading that correctly?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Yes, I think you absolutely are. And if you look again at the phrasing of the -- of the law that prohibits abortion in the first place, it talks about administering medication for an abortion. And so, you know, even if the woman were to procure abortion medication, and then take it on her own, she herself would be guilty of that crime.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re talking about not just throwing providers and anybody who works with a provider under aiding and abetting theories, in prison, but also women themselves, who procured abortion through abortion medication. So, it`s a really scary set of circumstances for women here in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I mean, another instance, the tragedy of a miscarriage. Does this mean a doctor cannot perform the procedure that`s necessary, essentially, when you -- when you`ve identified a miscarriage? It seems to me that some OBGYNs are going to think they are committing a crime if they essentially just do what most doctors would do right there, which is the D&C.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:08]\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Yes. I think that what`s going to happen is doctors will be so afraid that there will be investigations into these procedures, even understanding that many times those procedures are performed where there -- you know, there is no viability any longer. But because it`s the same procedure that you might perform for an abortion, they will be so concerned that these cases will be investigated, it will have a chilling effect, and you won`t have basic medical health care that is required for women not to have extreme health problems or even die. Doctors simply are not going to perform those procedures anymore because they don`t want to go to prison for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s talk about the various forms of relief that you and other abortion rights supporters in Michigan are seeking. You`ve got the governor`s, I guess you`d call it a lawsuit to this -- or trying to get clarification from the Supreme Court, state Supreme Court to, I guess, to wipe out the law. Planned Parenthood has filed a suit against you, which you have said is misguided. But obviously they`re trying to attain a similar outcome from the state Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And then there is a petition campaign circulating to put a constitutional amendment on the ballot. Those deadlines are in July. What is the -- what is the safest way to protect abortion rights in Michigan right now, of those three paths?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Well, firstly, I think each and every eligible voter in the state should be signing on to the you know, Reproductive Rights for All petition, and then coming to the polls and voting on it in November. And voting for every pro-choice Democrat up and down the ticket, whether it`s for federal office or for state office.\par \par \uc2 But in addition, you know, I have great hope and I put my full support and my attorneys are representing the governor in her lawsuit and I`m very hopeful that the Michigan Supreme Court will find that under our equal protection clause, under our due process clause of the Michigan Constitution that, you know, the right to an abortion is, you know, fundamental under our Michigan Constitution, even if the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  decides otherwise.\par \par \uc2 But let`s make it very clear the radical and extremist position of the Republicans in our state and all around the country completely contradicts what the public wants, and it really does place the lives of women in jeopardy. Let`s be clear, women in my state, and in states all over America are going to die because of this position. And I heard the Mississippi governor when you interviewed him, he refused to answer the question of whether or not he would sign a bill completely outlawing the use of birth control.\par \par \uc2 That is not in line at all with how Americans see their rights. And politicians do not belong in our doctor`s offices. They don`t belong in our bedrooms. And they should not be making these kinds of decisions on behalf of the American public and on behalf of women across America.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I just want to clarify, is the constitutional amendment the better guarantee here? Because maybe you get a ruling from this version of your state Supreme Court, but I assume you don`t want to be relying on that in the future.\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Yes, that`s correct. I think -- I think we need to see both. I would like to see a ruling from the Michigan Supreme Court so that there`s not even any period of time under which women are denied reproductive health care in Michigan. But then in addition to that, absolutely, this needs to be codified into our state Constitution. And if we have enough people that care about this issue and who come out to vote in November, I see that happening. And I think that is -- that both of these courses of action need to occur in order to better protect women in our state.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dana Nessel is the chief law enforcement officer as the attorney general in the state of Michigan, and suddenly may be confronting this sooner than she ever imagined. I appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, what are the chances that the draft decision will change substantially? And if it doesn`t, what other rights might be in jeopardy? We`ll speak to two former Supreme Court law clerks to take us inside the court`s machination. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:26:25]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. One reason abortion rights supporters are so upset with the draft decision involves something called stare decisis. It`s the idea that courts are bound by legal precedent. Both Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  suggested in their confirmation hearing that precedent would be important in considering Roe. Here`s Kavanaugh from his confirmation hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , U.S. COURT OF APPEALS, D.C. CIRCUIT: It`s settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court entitled to respect under principles of stare decisis. And one of the important things to keep in mind about Roe v. Wade is that it has been reaffirmed many times over the past 45 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: A lot of people heard a lot in that sentence. I think that all we can say now is he stated fact. I`m joined now by two legal experts to discuss the draft decision and where the Court might go from here. \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  is a former acting U.S. solicitor general. He clerked for \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and has argued 45 cases before the Court. And Jennifer Mascott is an assistant law professor at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2 . She has clerked for both Kavanaugh and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . I should make the note for Kavanaugh it was at the circuit level, if I have that right.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to both of you. Hopefully you can take us inside the Court here a little bit. I want to --the leak decision was not the first leak. There was a first leak, and it was a few days before "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board. I want to note a little something here, Neal. "The oral argument suggested that five justices leaned toward doing so, but a ferocious lobbying campaign is trying to change their minds. The particular targets are Justice Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Within five days, this editorial was going to speculate correctly that Alito was writing the majority opinion. It appears there is a debate happening in the court. Take us inside the court. When you read this, you put it together, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, FORMER ACTING U.S. SOLICITOR GENERAL: Yes. So I think that the leak that came this week from "Politico" is the more extreme one, because we actually see the draft opinion, something that, to my knowledge, has never happened. So the process is there was an oral argument in December, the justices all voted in a conference.\par \par \uc2 Nobody else was allowed into that conference. They take a tentative vote. The senior-most justice then assigns the opinion to herself, himself, or whomever. Here it looks like it was assigned to Alito. And then that draft was circulated in February for comment by the other justices.\par \par \uc2 It`s of course possible that votes change. That sometimes happens. But it`s extremely rare. So what this decision says -- and you started with stare decisis -- it`s so important because this draft decision says they`re going to overrule Roe v. Wade, overrule Planned Parenthood v. Casey. It`s as extreme an opinion, Chuck, as you could imagine. It looks like it was written by Robert Bork, the failed Supreme Court nominee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jennifer, you`ve clerked for \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  on the Circuit. Is he -- do you sense that he`s waiting to see what the chief justice, who may be writing another opinion that seems to be the speculation that would preserve Roe and allow maybe a new definition of viability. Do you sense that that is something he`s open to?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER MASCOTT, ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF LAW, \uc2 GEORGE MASON UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Well, first let me just join you both in saying I think the leak this week was unprecedented, it`s an outrage. I mean, the Supreme Court`s a very collegial institution. The justices across the nine chambers work in a lot of trust and confidence with each other and I think are trying to neutrally apply the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s actually quite breathtaking how detailed and well-reasoned and thorough this opinion was as of February. And so I think what Justice Kavanaugh and the rest of the justices will be doing is applying the Constitution as they understand it and are not going to allow themselves to be bullied or intimidated by this outrageous leak this week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But going back to whether Kavanaugh is weighing the precedent issue here, did Susan Collins only hear what she wanted to in that statement from \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MASCOTT: Well, I think what this draft opinion makes very clear is that the Court itself has never been able, after all these decades, to settle on what justification -\par \par \uc2 [09:30:07]\par \par \uc2 In fact, that decision`s been divisive and criticized from the day that it came down. And so I think the justices are looking at the text of the Constitution and recognizing that perhaps the Court has had a hard time settling on the justification because there`s just no right in the Constitution on this complicated moral issue that more properly belongs with the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Neal, when you read Alito`s opinion, did you say to yourself -- would it change how you might have defended Roe or argued it in the first place?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I totally disagree with my friend Jennifer. There was nothing persuasive in that draft whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 Roe was, of course, a contentious decision. It was -- but it was decided by a 7 to 2 majority of the Court. Five of those seven were Republican- appointed justices. And the Supreme Court in 1992 sailed well past Jennifer`s point. They said, "Even if Roe might be - have different theoretical bases, privacy, equal protection, whatever, it`s now the law of the land. The Court`s credibility is staked on it being the law of the land. Social expectations have crystallized around it."\par \par \uc2 So for them to overrule this, Chuck, which is the super precedent of super precedents, calls every other precedent into question, from gay marriage to contraception, as you were talking about before with the Mississippi governor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask, actually, about the - because in the draft opinion Alito tries to say, "Hey, I don`t want this to be used for anything else because privacy has been used for contraception, privacy has been used to justify same-sex marriage." Just because you say it doesn`t mean it`s true.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, we learned this with Bush v. Gore. So why -- why shouldn`t folks who are in same-sex marriages be -- why shouldn`t they be nervous right now?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Unfortunately, I think they have to be nervous right now. It`s the rationale that the Court -- that Justice Alito used in the draft opinion. If that draft opinion rationale stays the law of the land, it says basically the right has to be historically rooted in the deep traditions of the people. That is not, I think, a test that gay marriage would meet if you have that kind of, you know, really harsh reading.\par \par \uc2 And it does reflect that underlying conception that some justices on this Court, you know, pretend to be talking about the original intent of the Constitution, and they are anything but when it comes to striking down the Voting Rights Act, they make up the doctrine when they call corporations people, they make up the doctrine. It`s not in the text of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jennifer, why should folks who are in same-sex marriage be comforted by what Alito wrote in the draft opinion?\par \par \uc2 MASCOTT: Chuck, with respect, Neal I think here is just flat wrong. And I think President Biden`s remarks just this week show why. Even he acknowledged that we`ve got complicated questions here involving a child that makes this case fundamentally different from any other issue. We have another human life at issue. These are complicated questions the American people feel strongly about.\par \par \uc2 And the language that Neal`s quoting, "deeply rooted," comes from a 1997 opinion that itself was quoting opinions back to the 1930s. This is nothing new. This is an unprecedented issue, unique of its kind, and I think it`s quite telling that the instant this draft was leaked, people started talking about other questions, not this one, as if this opinion itself really is not all that objectionable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t believe that this is getting rid of a right to privacy, that basically it`s saying, "There`s no right to privacy in the Constitution?"\par \par \uc2 MASCOTT: I don`t think that this opinion says that more generally because it`s focused on the right of and interest of the child. And it`s essentially, here the Court is actually doing an admirable thing, I think, in the draft opinion, in taking itself out of the seat of power and leaving decisions up to the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Neal, where does this go? Is the Supreme Court going to hear more abortion cases, assuming they do overturn Roe, for now and perpetuity?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I think they will. And the idea that this draft is going to return things to the people I think is just ludicrous. I mean, I`d feel a lot better if the Republican Party wasn`t a party built on basically keeping the voters away from the polls and the like.\par \par \uc2 You can`t preach the language of democracy on Monday and then on the other six days of the week try and take it away from the American people.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, Chuck, I think whatever the Court does here, there`s going to be abortion case after abortion case. Your guest just a moment ago discussed things like contraception. Louisiana`s banning IUDs and the like. So absolutely, they`re in the thick of it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, I literally have to go. Do you expect this opinion to change much from the draft opinion? You don`t.\par \par \uc2 MASCOTT: Not at all. I respect the justices, they will not be intimidated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what do you expect?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I can`t predict that one at all. This is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think -- you think it`s possible they rehear the case, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Oh, I do. I think they could rehear the case. And I also would never count the chief justice out. His vote didn`t matter in December, but it might matter now.\par \par \uc2 MASCOTT: Rehearing would be a disaster. A disaster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Jennifer Mascott, thank you both for being here. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Democrats are hoping that overturning Roe will generate anger in their base. We see it happening. Will it lead to a big turnout in November? That part we don`t know yet. Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:37:33]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, panel is here.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali; legal affairs reporter Josh Gerstein, the man who broke the Supreme Court story of all time, if you will, for "Politico"; former George W. Bush White House Political Director Sara Fagen; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ", also a holder of a law degree, so watch out.\par \par \uc2 Josh, let me start with you. Has it sunk in what this, what your report perhaps has done to the Supreme Court for a generation?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GERSTEIN, SENIOR LEGAL AFFARIS REPORTER, POLITICO: Well, it`s starting to. I mean, when that large black imposing fence went up around the Court earlier in the week, it certainly indicated that the Court itself now realizes that whatever decision it issues on this is going to have pretty dramatic, momentous implications and probably is likely to anger a number of people on one side or the other.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think it`s going to change the way you get to cover the Court?\par \par \uc2 GERSTEIN: Well, it probably makes it even harder to press some of the issues for access to the Court. We`ve had a lot of concerns about the Court and transparency, even through this period of COVID over the last couple of years, but even before that. So, they`re probably going to be even more skittish about that than they were before, but it was not a terribly transparent institution to start with.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Look, we talk -- a lot of times we`ll say, hey, boy, this is going to change everything politically.\par \par \uc2 Ali, this feels -- did you sense that on Capitol -- is this actually a moment where we say, "Everything`s changing," and it`s really changing?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, I mean, I think that Democrats and Republicans both have been talking about this in theory for a really long time. I know it`s not popular to say, we don`t know, but this is totally untested. For the last 49 years, women in America haven`t had to grapple with wondering if there are protections in place for abortion.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think from an electorate perspective, all of this remains untested. And so we`ll have to see going forward. I will say, on the Hill, Democrats and Republicans took, of course, completely divergent views here. Republicans, of course, focusing on the leak, not the substance. You`d almost be forgiven if you wondered, hey, didn`t they push for this for several decades?\par \par \uc2 On the Democratic side, though, and we`ll see this next week, they`re going to push for a vote on the Women`s Health Protection Act on Wednesday. It`s going to fail. There seems to be some divide on if it`s better if it fails in bipartisan fashion or in fully Democratic fashion. But nevertheless, it sort of leaves them where they started in terms of having no way to federally codify this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Politically, if you just look at exit polling, Sara and Kimberly, this appears to favor the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:02]\par \par \uc2 Let me put up some battleground state polling on the abortion issue. Majorities in just about every battleground state in 2020 wanted it legal. Only Texas is sort of the outlier -- a plurality, 48-45. And you of course look at Colorado there, they`re very libertarian, not a surprise there.\par \par \uc2 So I understand, Kimberly, why Democrats think this is a game-changer for them for the midterms. Is it?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We`ll have to see, because this is so unprecedented in the way that it`s coming out. And it is a midterm election year to boot. We will have to see. I think we`ve talked before about a lot of other issues that Democrats thought would be big motivators, and it turned out not to be. They turned out -- whether it`s gun control, whether it`s police reform -- and it turned not to be.\par \par \uc2 So I think we will have to see. I think one thing Democrats really should be thinking about is their long game at this point, because this is the result of a Republican long game that was very dedicated for decades in order to put conservatives in office so that they can put conservatives on courts. And Democrats need to develop something like that.\par \par \uc2 SARA FAGEN, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think it`s a bit of a tale of two stories. I do think it will motivate Democrats. I don`t know how it couldn`t. And they`re incredibly depressed. You look at polling.\par \par \uc2 The question, though, is what is the debate? And the debate may cut against Democrats, where you have a very aggressive progressive movement that believes that abortion on demand at any point in a pregnancy, which is not where the public is. So, so you showed exit polling. It shows people who abortion legal. The question is: What is the definition of legal?\par \par \uc2 And so if we have a debate in this country in a number of these states where the Democratic candidate is calling for abortion at any point in the pregnancy and a Republican candidate is calling for a more moderate stance, that is going to accrue to the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But where is there evidence that the Republicans have a moderate stance here?\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: I mean, certainly -\par \par \uc2 TODD: There isn`t -- I mean, that`s the thing. What you outline on the left is correct. But I don`t hear exceptions anymore for rape all the time in many Republicans anymore.\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: We just, we just heard \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  talk about exceptions for rape and the life of the mother. And --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But not incest.\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: And, I imagine that will go through pretty quickly, but we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that -- but see, that`s where -- that`s I think making the point here.\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: And after that point, though, there was a law passed in Mississippi on viability at 16 weeks. So, I mean, as the debate moves to viability, Republicans are in a much stronger position than where the progressive movement is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But is the debate viability?\par \par \uc2 GERSTEIN: Well, I mean, I think the, the real issue is that, while there may be a debate as Sara`s saying, what`s going to happen right away if Roe is struck down at the end of June is all these trigger laws and laws that are already on the books from 100 years ago are going to kick in in many, many states. We`re talking maybe 26 states that will almost immediately end up with very severe restrictions or bans on abortion. So the debate may continue, but in those states, women are going to find a great deal of difficulty in getting access. In some states they may have to travel 1,000 miles in order to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I was -- I was just in South Dakota. It`s one of the states with a trigger law. And they`ve already had one of the most restrictive set of rules there governing what they can do for years. They are so restricted that they`re finding they have to have doctors come in from out of state because local doctors are either too worried about the local repercussions or they don`t want to do it.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, they`re talking about women traveling over 1,000 miles because of just the vastness in some of these rural states. And so when you talk about the trigger law states, effectively what you could end up with is on the coasts, those two patches of blue where it`s accessible, and most of the middle of the country where it`s not, you sort of just have have and have-nots here.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: I mean, I think there`s a -- there`s a lot of manufactured outrage happening around this issue. You know, Illinois is completely controlled by Democrats. And these legislatures, I think, will move very quickly around this legislation.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there`s going to be a period of significant tumult around this. And people who feel very passionately about the protection of life, you know, are going to be loud. But the people who feel passionate about abortion are going to also be loud. And we`re going to spend probably most of this fall talking about abortion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s interesting, Kimberly, that -- my experience with abortion is the party that overreaches gets punished. And what does overreach look like to the public?\par \par \uc2 STOHR: Yes. I mean, I think we`re going to get a good idea about what that overreach looks like as these laws come into play. We also need to talk about what voters were talking about. And especially in statewide races there`s always this big focus on women voters, suburban women voters.\par \par \uc2 But keep in mind that a lot of those women will still have access to abortion regardless of what state they live in. It`s the others, the people who are in a different position, who won`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to pause it there. We want to make note, sadly, of a grim milestone that we`ve reached this past week as a country. According to our count here at NBC News, one million Americans have now died of COVID.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  announced this past week that 15 million more people than had been counted appeared to have died during the pandemic than would`ve under normal circumstances. We`re starting to see countries underreported some deaths and we`re starting to get the reality check now.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:05]\par \par \uc2 So previously, six million more were estimated to have died than what we first thought.\par \par \uc2 It`s a reminder that even as we learn to live with COVID, millions remained at risk and do so now. Keep that in mind, keep them in mind, and we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back.\par \par \uc2 Data Download time. Democrats are hoping the news about Roe will energize their base. And they have their eyes on younger voters, and here`s why. Among younger voters, three quarters say abortion should be legal in all or most cases. That`s 20 percentage points more than the share of voters over 65 who share that view.\par \par \uc2 That large gap matters because those groups of voters do not usually turn out in the same numbers, especially when it comes to the midterms. Let me show you. Look, here`s younger voters in a presidential year. They are, they become almost one in five voters.\par \par \uc2 But in midterm years, you can see here, young voters down to the 12, 13 percent. Even in that good Democratic year of `18, younger voters didn`t quite get out. Meanwhile, those 65-plus voters are far more likely to go to the polls, no matter the election.\par \par \uc2 The question now is: Could a Supreme Court opinion that overturns a nearly 50-year precedent in Roe be enough to alter that picture and motivate a younger electorate?\par \par \uc2 We may just about to be finding out come November.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Donald Trump`s endorsement pushed J.D. Vance to victory in Ohio`s Republican Senate primary. It was a win for Mr. Trump. The game`s about to get a lot harder as May rolls on.\par \par \uc2 Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Literally the day of the leak, or on the eve of the Ohio primary, we got the Supreme Court leak. Too early to really have an impact on what voters may have done, but we did find out that Donald Trump`s endorsement of J.D. Vance did help him win.\par \par \uc2 So, Sara Fagen, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  literally or figuratively in charge of the Republican Party these days?\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: Well, I think that it`s somewhere in between. In a case like Ohio, where it was a pretty crowded primary where none of the candidates were particularly defined, he`s going to have a huge impact and he`s going to make - his endorsement is going to be a big deal.\par \par \uc2 You know, in a case like Georgia where you have an incumbent governor who`s had a long track record, it`s not having as much of an impact.\par \par \uc2 So I think on a case-by-case basis probably net accrues to the president`s favor in terms of these endorsements, but he`s not going to get them all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Sarah Longwell I think put it pretty well, Kimberly: Mr. Trump has already won. Whether Mr. Trump`s handpicked candidates win or not, the Republican field that will emerge from these primary battles will be overwhelmingly Trumpy.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s a fair take.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: I think that`s right. I think one thing we see that is powerful is Trumpism. Regardless of whether Trump himself pronounces the candidate`s name correctly, you have in someone like J.D. Vance, just a mini-Trump, someone who is trying to espouse this populist message even though he himself as the messenger doesn`t represent that. That`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to a tee.\par \par \uc2 And the candidates that are able to capture that and replicate it the best in this particular Republican Party are going to do well, whether Trump endorses them or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We know some Senate Republicans, Ali, were hoping that Trump`s endorsements would not do so well. You know, all right, so he`s not going to do well in Georgia. And Dr. Oz looks like a coin flip at best.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But I don`t think his grip`s weakening.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yeah. I mean, so let`s say he goes 50/50 over the course of the next month. Whether or not you consider that a win is kind of through the eye of the beholder.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:02]\par \par \uc2 I think for Senate Republicans and Republicans in Washington the goal has always just been make sure he`s not endorsing the candidate who can`t ultimately win.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that`s going to ultimately come out in the wash. But I do think that the larger question is: In a place like Georgia where his endorsement probably is not going to have the swing that he wants, Trump`s vendettas have never been party loyal. They have always been just whatever his vendetta personally is.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s where the concern stems from for Republicans in Washington, is, you`re not there for the party. And frankly, a lot of these Republicans that he`s endorsing, they don`t have deep party ties either. And so you could end up with Republicans coming to Washington who are much more indebted to Trump than they are to the people who are in control of the Senate here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know what he hasn`t done, Josh, to keep it sort of in your wheelhouse? He hasn`t really commented on the leaked document. You would think he`d say, look what my judges did. I told you they`d overturn Roe. And now, maybe he`s waiting to make sure it actually happens.\par \par \uc2 But the lack of taking credit, his political antenna to me is telling him this is -- he`s a little nervous.\par \par \uc2 GERSTEIN: Yeah, you do wonder if he`s seeing some of this polling that clearly other Republicans are seeing, saying that this issue may be a loser for them in the fall. And I also wonder in some of these races where Trump`s nomination or his endorsement has made the difference, whether when you get to the general election you`re going to see candidates try to do a straddle, sort of like Glenn Youngkin did, right, where he was sort of semi-accepting Trump`s energy but didn`t quite want to appear with him.\par \par \uc2 Can they maintain that straddle all the way to November or --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s why it makes David McCormick to me the most interesting candidate in Pennsylvania. It`s like, boy, if you`re the Democrats now, you don`t want McCormick to get out of here because he`s both been running as a Trumpy candidate but suddenly he gets to say, "Trump didn`t like me."\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: That`s right. Yeah, David McCormick is a compelling candidate for a lot of reasons. He`s an exceptional person.\par \par \uc2 But the -- look, you`re right. Many of these Republicans are going to have to walk that line. And they`re going to have to do what Glenn Youngkin did, which is to say, I like a lot of what Trump did. I like his policies, and I like his energy. But I`m my own person, and I have my own ideas.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why these incumbents fare so well in these endorsement battles, because they do have their own identity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kimberly, another thing we saw this week: Very quietly, President Biden debuted his -- a new message of sorts, trying to not say the word "Trump" but say, "Hey, this MAGA crowd." And he tried to lump abortion rights and some of these other things in there. It tells me their -- the White House is looking for a new message here. What did you make of it?\par \par \uc2 STOHR: Yeah. No, I think that that`s absolutely right. They`re seeing these - we`re six months out of the midterms. They`re seeing how big the consequences are here, and they are trying to edge with this messaging a little bit, trying to broaden it out. We`ll see how effective it is. But the fact that the message is still being developed--\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: -- this late in the game --\par \par \uc2 FAGEN: Of course, they want a new message, 8.5 percent inflation, a disastrous withdrawal of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali, is there anything that`s going to get passed?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: In the Senate?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I mean, it doesn`t really look very likely. They might be able to do this competition bill that could help with supply chain issues. USICA is what it was called. It`s gone through a million namings since then. But at the same time --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Both the House and Senate have passed a version of this bill. Of course this bill`s going to pass.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Of course. That`s the only one that I think has an ultimate path.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of these energizing issues that we`re talking about, specifically on abortion -- no, these are going nowhere fast. And I think that`s actually why you`re making this turn away from Trump to Trumpism and MAGA, because it`s better to do the broad messaging than it is just on him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 I hope you do enjoy your Mother`s Day. Don`t forget to call her if you haven`t yet. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220508ei5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Leak Of That Draft Supreme Court Opinion Is Putting A Spotlight On Challenges Some Women Face Getting An Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 578 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Tonight, the leak of that draft Supreme Court opinion is putting a spotlight on challenges some women face getting an abortion. Challenges that would become more common if the draft opinion holds. Ali Vitali reports from South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It takes several hours and a security escort for Dr. Sarah Traxler to get to work.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a lot of miles on the airline.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: She lives in Minnesota, part of a team of out-of-state doctors on rotation at this clinic in South Dakota, doing it because few other providers here will. Many fear retribution in the ultra-conservative state like Nurse Misty.\par \par \uc2 If you could talk a little bit about why you`re not using your last name?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s not safe from harassment.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: It`s South Dakota`s only abortion clinic, one of five states with a single health center performing abortions, and a glimpse at what could become reality for millions if Roe v. Wade is overturned. Though abortion is legal here, it`s heavily restricted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For some women, they already are very much living a poster of life.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Three-day mandatory waiting periods after the first visit, and stringent rules on telehealth and abortion pills exacerbate the burden of distance.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just distance but time and money. To get to this clinic patients have to take off work and drive to the southeast corner of a state more than two times the size of the country of Ireland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They drive here on day one and then they have to come back. That is almost a 1300 mile journey for them to make in three days.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Republican governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  hopes to go even further should the Supreme Court end Roe`s protection.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): And I look forward to the day when all unborn lives are protected.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And new fault lines forming around the country. In Louisiana, a new bill could make abortion a homicide. And Oklahoma now the latest state to ban abortions after six weeks when many women don`t even know they`re pregnant yet.\par \par \uc2 A new poll shows 61 percent of Americans say abortion should be illegal in all or most cases. And back in South Dakota, Nurse Misty won`t soon forget the feeling of reading that leaked draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It felt like I was being trampled. And everybody above me knew that I was down there. And because I have a uterus I wasn`t worth picking up off the floor.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Bracing for a world without Roe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, I don`t think we knew exactly that it was going to be so egregious. But we`ve been planning for Worst case scenario.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Ali Vitali, NBC News, Sioux Falls, South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220510ei560005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  Sets A Vote On The Bill To Legalize Abortion Nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Morgan Chesky \par \uc2 1,589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Meantime advocates on both sides of the abortion debate are grappling with what comes next if the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade. That`s especially true in Texas already home to one of the nation`s most restrictive abortion laws. NBC`s Morgan Chesky is in Fort Worth with the latest on that story. Hey, Morgan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Hoda, good morning. And right now if a woman shows up to this clinic, abortion is already outlawed in Texas after just six weeks but if the Supreme Court follows through on this draft opinion overturning Roe abortion could be all but outlawed here in the state of Texas not only that but the state would join a host of other conservative led states following suit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:15:12]\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Overnight, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  speaking out about that draft opinion, blasting the unprecedented leak as quote, absolutely appalling, according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," and vowing it will not affect the courts work. But across the country, states and activists on both sides are getting ready. Texas is at the epicenter of the abortion rights debate. It`s one of 13 states with so called trigger laws that would ban abortion with exceptions for the health of the mother if Roe is overturned Democrat \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  looking to unseat Republican Greg Abbott as Texas governor is seizing on the law Abbott signed last year.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE, FORMER UNITED STATES REPRESENTATIVE: This is not about life. This is about control. And this is about power. It`s about controlling the lives of the women of Texas and taking power away from them.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Abbott is focusing on the leaked draft Supreme Court opinion itself, urging the court to issue it without delay.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): I think the majority opinion in this case must be issued this week to show that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  will not be intimidated.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Louisiana also would outlaw abortion if Roe is overturned and its legislator is going further advancing a bill that would criminalize the procedure, meaning abortion providers and mothers could be charged with homicide. Bradley Pierce helped write the bill.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY PIERCE, ANTI-ABORTION ATTORNEY: We believe that abortion is cruel. And it`s the taking of an innocent life. And that`s we should make that illegal. And there shouldn`t be some exception for mothers.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Connecticut`s Governor signing a sweeping bill into law, offering expanded access to abortion and new protections to providers and patients, including those from out of state. First Lady Jill Biden, in an interview airing this weekend on MSNBC, urging abortion rights advocates to step up efforts in their states.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: The election of the President matters because he`s the one who puts the justices on the court. Our state legislators are going to matter too, so people have to get involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Now in the meantime, Senate Democrats are expected to hold a vote next Wednesday on a bill that would guarantee abortion rights nationwide. But as of right now that measure is expected to fail. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Morgan Chesky for us there in Texas. Morgan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Turning to the weather now including more threats of severe storms across the south and Mid-Atlantic today with nearly 40 million Americans affected. This as parts of Texas and Oklahoma are still recovering from a severe outbreak of tornadoes over the last several days. There`s some new drone footage here showing me the aftermath of a confirmed tornado. You can see campers overturn their trees uprooted at an RV park in East Texas. One resident was hit by a fallen tree. The injury we`re told, though, is not life threatening, thankfully. But that`s a terrifying scene there.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Which is a perfect segue into Al. That whether, it seems like it`s not letting up.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I know and it`s moving east too. And we`ve also got a lot of rain to talk about as we show you what`s happening. We`ve got flash flood watches and flood warnings from Missouri also into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic States. We`ve got a risk of severe weather for 38 million people stretching from Washington out to Cincinnati and down to New Orleans, Savannah, Georgia as well.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at wind gusts of over 60 miles per hour possible, damaging winds, hail, and tornadoes possible as the system moves to the east, so heavy rains soaking the Ohio Valley into the Mid-Atlantic today, a severe risk of weather from Louisiana all the way to Virginia. Tomorrow, a rainy cool day from D.C. to New York, showers and storms will continue across the southeast.\par \par \uc2 As we move into Mother`s Day, this low pressure is going to linger right along the eastern seaboard. So cool, cloudy, not a great day for Mother`s Day unseasonably cool for the Mid-Atlantic and the Northeast. Look at these rainfall totals through Sunday, one to three inches in the Northeast. But as you get into the Appalachians, we could be looking at upwards of four inches or more. We`re going to get to your local forecast coming up in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:20:07]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: For those of you in the South, get ready for some summertime temperature.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Really? All right, all right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s a good tease. Coming up, before that, those new images released in the manhunt, new images for the murder suspect and the corrections officer who helped him escape, what authorities are now saying about where they could be.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Plus, the countdowns on to the iconic Kentucky Derby. We`re going to have everything you need to know about the horses, the hype, and one high profile absence at tomorrow`s annual run for the roses live from \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2 . Look at our duo there. All right, but this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: With an internal hat.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re getting a jumpstart on Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Look at our Plaza, packed with moms and daughters who inspire us. We can`t wait to talk to them and surprise them after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:20]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Come on, 7:30, it`s Friday. Oh, come on. I love this song too. May 6th, 2022. We cannot wait to get outside. Seated at those tables, incredible moms and daughters, they`re filling our plaza. We`re having a little early Mother`s Day celebration. We broke up the umbrellas too. It`s going to be touching. It`s going to be inspiring. We`re going to have some fun. And I think you`re going to feel just really good about yourself. When this little bit is over.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We`re going to celebrate moms and we are also going to have just a few surprises as well.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love a surprise.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: First, though, your 7:30 headlines on this Friday, a former defense secretary under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is making a stunning accusation. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting this morning that in his upcoming book \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  says then President Trump floated the idea of launching missiles into Mexico. According to Esper, Trump said he wanted to quote destroy the drug labs and wipe out the cartels. NBC News has reached out to Mr. Trump`s team but have not received a response. "The Times" reports that the memoir was vetted by the Pentagon to check for classified information, and more than two dozen four star generals and some Cabinet members reviewed the book for accuracy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The man accused of attacking comedian Dave Chappelle on stage this week is now charged with four misdemeanor counts. The Los Angeles District Attorney`s office says the incident was criminal but did not rise to the felony level. Police arrested 23-year-old Isaiah Lee after he allegedly jumped on stage and tackled Chappelle at the Hollywood Bowl Tuesday night. Police said Lee had a replica handgun with a knife blade, but it`s not clear if he actually brandished it. If Lee is convicted, he faces up to a year and a half in prison.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And a history making appointment at the White House on Thursday. President Biden naming \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  as the new White House Press Secretary, she will be the first black woman and the first openly gay person to hold that position. Jean-Pierre has served as Deputy Press Secretary to \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  since the beginning of the Biden administration. Psaki`s last day is next Friday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220510ei560002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 6, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kerry Sanders, Kelly Cobiella, Ali Vitali, Kristen  Welker, Gabe Gutierrez, Emilie Ikeda, Kathy Park \par \uc2 3,731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the deadly explosion rocking a historic hotel in Cuba.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The massive blast killing at least nine people at the Hotel Saratoga in downtown Havana. The urgent Search and Rescue Mission fears more victims may be trapped under the rubble. The question tonight, what caused it.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, at least 50 more civilians evacuated from that besieged steel plant in Mariupol. Russian forces trying to crush the last pocket of resistance that Ukrainian port city and why fighting may escalate this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news, the images from North Carolina, reports of multiple tornadoes touching down.\par \par \uc2 The major discovery in the manhunt for an escaped Alabama inmate and the corrections officer accused of helping him.\par \par \uc2 The plane crashing into a backyard at Houston. All four people on board escaping alive.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy adding more than 400,000 new jobs in April despite soaring inflation.\par \par \uc2 The CDC now investigating five deaths in more than 100 mystery cases of hepatitis in children. What it`s now ruled out as the cause.\par \par \uc2 And inspiring America, my report on strangers opening their homes and churches to Ukrainian refugees.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC Nightly News with Lester Holt. Good evening, terrifying images coming out of Cuba tonight. A five-star hotel in downtown Havana ripped by a massive and deadly explosion. The death toll rising as rescuers search for the missing, dozens of injured were rushed to hospitals. Cell phone video capturing the moment of the explosion as the Saratoga hotel was quickly engulfed in smoke and dust. Its facade crumbled by the blast.\par \par \uc2 Cuban authorities tonight blaming the blast and a gas leak. The hotel once frequented by well-heeled tourists and celebrities, including some Americans, was closed for renovation. Kerry Sanders now with late developments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Moments after the explosion, a witness began recording as the cloud of dust from the blast envelop the streets of old Havana. A witness points and yells there`s people alive, there`s people alive. In the rubble, another says there`s one. There`s one.\par \par \uc2 Bystanders, police, firefighters began climbing over concrete and glass in the frantic search for survivors. Cuban authorities say they believe the massive explosion at Havana`s Saratoga hotel was caused by a gas leak. NBC News`s Ed Augustin lives around the corner from the hotel.\par \par \uc2 ED AUGUSTIN, NBC NEWS JOURNALIST: I was working at home. I heard this massive explosion. I rushed out onto the terrace, and the first thing I saw were plumes of smoke above Havana`s Capitolio building. My first thought that it had been some sort of attack.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Havana`s Governor reports the hotel chef fled the kitchen moments before the explosion to warn that there was a smell of gas from a truck delivering canisters. The chef miraculously survived. The $700 a night hotel Saratoga was just four days for reopening. Inside when it blew up, hotel and construction workers. Other victims, outside on the sidewalk.\par \par \uc2 Officials say at last count, 40 people injured, at least nine dead. The luxury Hotel Saratoga had looked like this popular pre COVID with stars like Madonna, Beyonce, and Jay Z. Tonight, an investigation is underway into what went wrong as families are notified of who died and who is still missing. Kerry Sanders, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In Ukraine, more civilians were evacuated today from the besieged steel plant in Mariupol as the battle there becomes increasingly desperate. It comes as First Lady Jill Biden visits the region. Kelly Cobiella is in Ukraine with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Russian forces are still trying to break the last pocket of resistance at Mariupol steel plant.\par \par \uc2 It won`t take long, this Russian-backed fighter said.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations describing the plant as a hellscape with 200 civilians and hundreds more wounded fighters holed up. Today, 50 more civilians were rescued, evacuated by the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and Red Cross and now in Russian controlled territory.\par \par \uc2 Russian flags are now flying in the city of Mariupol amid fears Moscow was planning a victory parade next Monday although the Kremlin denies it. Today, a defiant President Zelenskyy said Mariupol will never fall because there`s nothing left. Russia has already destroyed it.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It comes after NBC News reports that U.S. intelligence shared with Ukraine led to Ukraine sinking of the Moskva last month. The Pentagon today denied targeting the ship.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, THE PENTAGON: The kind of intelligence that we provide them, it`s legitimate, it`s lawful, and it`s limited.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: In another show of support, another high-profile visit. First Lady Jill Biden meeting with U.S. and \uc2 NATO\uc2  military leadership in Romania today, with plans to visit refugees on Slovakia`s border with Ukraine on Sunday. Marina and her two sons have been living in a shelter since fleeing Mariupol. Her husband is fighting in the plant.\par \par \uc2 After worrying for a week, today, his messages came through all at once. In one message he calls \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  a bald monster saying, all in all, screw him. In another, he wrote, in the morning these bastards fired on us from the ship. Everything is more or less fine. How are you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kelly Cobiella joins us now from inside Ukraine. Kelly, there have been warnings from officials there to be ready for increased attacks this weekend. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA (on camera): Yes, Lester, there`s a fear that with what Russia calls Victory Day approaching on May 9, that there could be a bombardment of strikes across the country this weekend. So, people are being warned to listen and pay attention to those air raid sirens. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly, you and your team continue to be safe.\par \par \uc2 Severe weather in the mid-Atlantic tonight and reports of a tornado touching down in Mebane, North Carolina causing heavy damage to a distribution center there. Several dozen workers there huddled inside. One reported seeing a funnel cloud. The storm system moving across the Mid- Atlantic and northeast into tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the leak of that draft Supreme Court opinion is putting a spotlight on challenges some women face getting an abortion. Challenges that would become more common if the draft opinion holds. Ali Vitali reports from South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It takes several hours and a security escort for Dr. Sarah Traxler to get to work.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a lot of miles on the airline.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: She lives in Minnesota, part of a team of out-of-state doctors on rotation at this clinic in South Dakota, doing it because few other providers here will. Many fear retribution in the ultra-conservative state like Nurse Misty.\par \par \uc2 If you could talk a little bit about why you`re not using your last name?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s not safe from harassment.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: It`s South Dakota`s only abortion clinic, one of five states with a single health center performing abortions, and a glimpse at what could become reality for millions if Roe v. Wade is overturned. Though abortion is legal here, it`s heavily restricted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For some women, they already are very much living a poster of life.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Three-day mandatory waiting periods after the first visit, and stringent rules on telehealth and abortion pills exacerbate the burden of distance.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just distance but time and money. To get to this clinic patients have to take off work and drive to the southeast corner of a state more than two times the size of the country of Ireland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They drive here on day one and then they have to come back. That is almost a 1300 mile journey for them to make in three days.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Republican governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  hopes to go even further should the Supreme Court end Roe`s protection.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): And I look forward to the day when all unborn lives are protected.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And new fault lines forming around the country. In Louisiana, a new bill could make abortion a homicide. And Oklahoma now the latest state to ban abortions after six weeks when many women don`t even know they`re pregnant yet.\par \par \uc2 A new poll shows 61 percent of Americans say abortion should be illegal in all or most cases. And back in South Dakota, Nurse Misty won`t soon forget the feeling of reading that leaked draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It felt like I was being trampled. And everybody above me knew that I was down there. And because I have a uterus I wasn`t worth picking up off the floor.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Bracing for a world without Roe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, I don`t think we knew exactly that it was going to be so egregious. But we`ve been planning for Worst case scenario.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Ali Vitali, NBC News, Sioux Falls, South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Let`s turn now of the economy and another strong jobs report today. More than 400,000 jobs added in April. Unemployment holding at 3.6 percent. But a worker shortage persists with experts saying wages are not keeping up with rising inflation. Kristen Welker is at the White House to tell us more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With growing dissatisfaction over his handling of the economy, President Biden in battleground Ohio touting today`s jobs report.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have now created a total of 8.3 million jobs in my first 15 months in office.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: With 428,000 jobs added to the U.S. economy in April, the country has gained back 95 percent of the jobs lost early in the pandemic, creating opportunities for people like new graduate Jordyn Whitted who just started a communications role with an arts non-profit.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:08]\par \par \uc2 JORDYN WHITTED, RECENT HIRE: And it gave me an opportunity to really loop in my hobbies and my interest is more money. Different title different organizational structure.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But the country is also facing dire economic headwinds. While paychecks are up five and a half percent, prices are soaring even higher with inflation at a 40-year record. Home prices have spiked 20 percent. Gas prices are back near record highs. And the Dow is down nine percent this year, putting a dent in people`s retirement funds.\par \par \uc2 Republicans today blasting the President`s policies, blaming him and Democrats for skyrocketing prices. Adding to the economic turmoil, there`s a record number of job openings. There are currently two jobs available for every person who is unemployed. Among the reasons, some people are retiring early due to the pandemic. Others are holding out for higher pay.\par \par \uc2 Businesses like precision landscaping in Portland, Oregon say they`ve had to increase salaries and even turn down new customers.\par \par \uc2 TYRONE FOSTER, PRESIDENT, PRECISION LANDSCAPE SERVICE: It has been hard to find employees because it`s become a much more challenging environment to find employees. We get competition from areas that we once didn`t.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Recent polls show just a third of the country approves of the president`s handling of the economy, intensifying concerns for Democrats as the midterms approach, Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get to the growing risk of COVID infections yet again, as new and more contagious subvariant spread. Are we going to see new restrictions as a result? Gabe Gutierrez now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, new COVID cases are exploding across more of the country as the so-called BA2 Omicron sub-variant takes hold.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR, NEW YORK CITY: Are we concerned about the numbers? Yes, we are. But preparation, not panic.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: New York City`s COVID transmission rate is up 32 percent in the last 10 days. The city now grappling with what looks like the start of a fifth wave of the pandemic. Are you seriously considering the return of master vaccine methods?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHWIN VASAN, HEALTH COMMISSIONER, NEW YORK CITY: No, but we`re watching the data and the trends in the data to decide whether we need to make the shift in our city.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: New Jersey`s seven day average for confirmed cases is up 137 percent from a month ago. Most of Connecticut is now in a COVID red zone as the state`s positivity rate tops 10 percent. In Massachusetts, COVID cases in schools have doubled in the past week. Even as the state`s largest district, Boston Public Schools continues to require masks for students and staff.\par \par \uc2 Still, there are new concerns about tools in the fight against COVID. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  now saying that \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 `s vaccine should only be given to adults who cannot receive a different vaccine or specifically requested due to a rare but serious risk of blood clots. So, what about those who have already gotten the J&J shot?\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, INFECTIOUS DISEASE PROFESSOR, \uc2 VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY\uc2 : If you`ve received the J&J vaccine, and it`s been at least two weeks since you`ve received the vaccine, you`re past the period of time of danger of that blood clotting syndrome, which is very rare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): And doctors are also looking into so-called rebound symptoms among some patients who`ve taken Paxlovid, the pill used to treat COVID. Experts say the benefits outweigh any risks, Lester. cluster.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And another health concerns tonight, the growing mystery outbreak of hepatitis among children. The CDC is saying the number of cases has grown to 109 including five deaths across 25 states and territories. More than half had an adenovirus infection. But the cause remains under investigation. The CDC has ruled out COVID vaccinations as a possible cause.\par \par \uc2 In Houston today, a small plane crashing shortly after takeoff from Hobby Airport. The plane plowing into the backyard of a home near a high school. Incredibly, authorities say, all four people on board escaped without injuries. The crash lit a gazebo and fire in the backyard, but crews were able to quickly put it out.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, will the discovery of a key piece of evidence shift the manhunt for that escaped inmate and a corrections officer in Alabama. And with inflation on the rise, how you can stay ahead of the soaring costs of summer travel.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s been a week now since that Alabama inmate escaped from a prison and disappeared with a corrections officer. Today, official said the getaway car used by the couple was found in Tennessee, about two hours north of the prison. They said it was abandoned shortly after the escape and that some of Vicky White`s belongings were found inside it.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight the busy summer travel season is fast approaching. And this year, the rising costs of gas and airfare are forcing many American families to change their vacation plans. Emilie Ikeda with ways you can still save.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Inflation is spilling over into summer. And whether it`s the beach, big band, or Broadway, your vacation will carry a heftier price tag.\par \par \uc2 HAYLEY BERG, ECONOMIST, \uc2 HOPPER\uc2 : These prices are truly unprecedented for travelers.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Be prepared for steeper costs across the board. airfare is expected to be the highest in hoppers history for summer travel, averaging more than $380 round trip. Hotel rates surging 36 percent from pre-pandemic numbers and $154 per night. And a similar bump with car rentals where drivers will pay roughly $60 a day.\par \par \uc2 BERG: There`s so much demand in the American market for air travel, car rentals, hotels, even rental homes. And that puts a lot of upward pressure on prices.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: While it was, prompting the majority of adults to rethink their summer vacation plans.\par \par \uc2 KERI BAUGH, TRAVELER: You know, road trips used to be the affordable option and right now they`re less affordable.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: A year ago, the Baugh family`s road trip from Boston to Tampa would cost roughly $375 in gas this summer. They`re looking at more than $500. How are you making up the difference for inflation?\par \par \uc2 BAUGH: We actually just food pack road trip breakfast, lunch, dinner and snacks. And that avoids the cost of having to stop as often.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Trip planners also recommending fly on a Tuesday or Wednesday, postpone your vacation until September or consider a destination with free activities built in like the beach or parks. Experts say it`s not a matter of axing your trips altogether, just simply being savvy with your spending. Emilie Ikeda, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, report on people across the country helping Ukrainian refugees feel at home and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Many of us have been shocked and saddened at the scenes of Ukrainian refugees fleeing their country tried to rebuild their lives amid the chaos. But for many Americans watching from afar, it was a cause for action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): In the chaos of the Lviv train station, I met so many refugees overwhelmed and uncertain.\par \par \uc2 Do you know where you will live?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So far, about 15,000 have found a safe haven in the United States. Ola Potetska (PH) is at St Nicholas Ukrainian Cathedral in Philadelphia. Philly residents Sandy Gerber (PH) took her shopping and helped organize 200 neighbors all pitching in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People are texting me, emailing me. They want to get clothes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Over here at 907 --\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Across the country in San Diego,.Pastor Phil Metzker (PH) runs Calvary Chapel just a few miles from the Mexican border.\par \par \uc2 What inspired you to get the church involved in this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, it takes a village. I mean, you just can`t do this alone. And our church and our whole community rallied very fast.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Thousands of Californians embrace total strangers with open arms and open hearts. Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player Nick Roach and his wife, Anna Marie hosted several Ukrainian families, and felt like history paid a visit.\par \par \uc2 NICK ROACH, FORMER NFL PLAYER: To never expect to really to be a part of helping it or changing it and let alone especially having it literally show up on your doorstep.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: They had 20 unexpected Ukrainian guests the night before. And maybe the visitors have given Americans some inspiration.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that what we`re seeing this outpouring toward the Ukrainian refugees is a template for a better us?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hope so. I really do. I mean, we`ve all banded together to say let`s help these people who are in need. That`s such a powerful message for today. I hope it inspires us all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Don`t miss -- don`t miss the 2022 Inspiration List tomorrow on NBC, MSNBC, and CNBC, and Sunday on Telemundo and across streaming platforms. Up next for us here tonight, greener pastures for some of the Kentucky derby`s greatest heroes.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s a rite of spring tomorrow`s Kentucky Derby seen here on NBC. And tonight, what retirement is like for some of horse racing`s greatest. Here`s Kathy Park.\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At 20 years old, roughly 67 in human years, Son King may be done with racing. But he still wants to win. Even if it`s out running caretakers like 75-year-old Michael Blowin (PH).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I`ve gone with a lot of them, like all for 4000. I`ve never beaten one of them, not once.\par \par \uc2 PARK: This longtime film critic with the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2  treated in a life covering Hollywood headlines to chasing a different set of stars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s only got four teeth left. So his carrots have to get cut up real tiny.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Retired racehorses who`ve slowed down a bit at this Kentucky farm, aptly called Old Friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s like meeting celebrities.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, meeting them is like meeting with a celebrity. So, yes, that`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Horse fans still swoon over Silver Charmer, the 1997 Kentucky Derby and Preakness winner was just three quarters of a length shy of the Triple Crown, beat by Touch Gold. The former competitors now neighbors at Old Friends.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel a great responsibility taking care of these horses?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, but it`s not a burden. It`s an honor.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Their racing days may be over, but these thoroughbreds still have a cheering squad of visitors from around the world.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve seen them win their races. And it`s like, oh my god, I`m seeing them in person. It`s just such a thrill.\par \par \uc2 PARK: In the final stretch of their lives, Blowin says these horses are still leading by example.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything`s on the line. There`s no time to waste now. You got to get it done before the finish line.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Going the distance even in their golden years. In Georgetown, Kentucky, Kathy Park, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220507ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Today for May 6, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Craig Melvin, Jo Ling Kent, Erin McLaughlin, Morgan  Chesky, Al Roker, Sam Brock, Stephanie Gosk, Dylan Dreyer, Mike Tirico,  Carson Daly \par \uc2 6,715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:16]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning, wild ride. Nervous investors from coast to coast after the Dow plunges 1,000 points its worst day in two years inside the turmoil being driven by efforts to get record inflation under control, and what you can do to protect your money.\par \par \uc2 On a mission, as the war rages in Ukraine, First Lady, Dr. Jill Biden heads to Europe overnight for a firsthand look at the humanitarian crisis. While new details emerge about the U.S. intelligence used to help Ukraine sink a Russian cruiser. We`ll have a live report from the front lines.\par \par \uc2 New COVID concerns, with cases rising across the United States, a setback in the vaccine efforts. The alarming risk that has the \uc2 FDA\uc2  now limiting access to \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  shots, as the top health official in New York warns the latest surge could lead to a return of mask mandates. This morning, we`ll go one-on-one with the White House COVID Response Coordinator Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Battlegrounds, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  sets a vote on the bill to legalize abortion nationwide, a direct response to that leaked Supreme Court draft opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade, the Chief Justice speaking out overnight as people on both sides of the debate dig in.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, brazen robbery, in broad daylight, a group of armed suspects holdup a landmark jewelry store in Paris before using motorcycles to escape. Just ahead, the race to find them amid a dramatic string of smash and grabs overseas and here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And run for the roses, excitement growing ahead of one of the most iconic events in sports, the Kentucky Derby. So mix up them in juleps, grab your hats, we`re taking you live to historic Churchill Downs today, Friday, May 6th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, hello, good morning, good morning. Good to see we`re so happy that you`re starting our Friday off with us. Savannah is off, Craig is in. I can`t believe the derby says Sunday. Get ready.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tomorrow, Saturday.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, Saturday.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Get your hat ready.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The most exciting to many sports fan. We`re going to go to \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2  in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s going to be fun. All right, we do have a lot to get to this morning, including that news about the J&J vaccine, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  now looking to limit its use. We`ll talk about that the surging cases and more with Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 , who now leads the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s response to COVID.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Also this morning, some new details about the United States sharing information and intelligence with Ukraine leading to some victories in the nation`s war with Russia. It comes as the First Lady is on her way overseas right now. She`s going to meet with U.S. troops and Ukrainian refugees. We`ll have a full report live from the region straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But we do start with all eyes on Wall Street entering today`s trading after the Dow`s worst day in two years down more than 1,000 points. But this morning, stock futures are up ahead of the morning bell.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So what does it all mean for you and your investments? NBC`s \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  is with us to break it all down. Jo, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT\uc2 , NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, guys. As you said it was the worst day for the markets in two years and today is expected to be rocky again. All the major gains from Wednesday wiped out within hours of trading yesterday and an extraordinary reversal. And with the April jobs report, investors are bracing for another roller coaster ride today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KENT (voice-over): A massive U-turn on Wall Street this week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: S&P 500 declining 3.6 percent. Every sector is feeling the pain today.\par \par \uc2 KENT (voice-over): The Dow plunging more than 1,000 points on Thursday, its worst single day since 2020. The tech heavy NASDAQ also taking a beating, hundreds of billions of dollars of market value wiped out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s rare that the NASDAQ jumps and then falls 4 percent at least on consecutive days.\par \par \uc2 KENT (voice-over): It was a stunning reversal of the massive gains on the markets on Wednesday, when investors initially cheered the Fed`s decision to hike interest rates again in an attempt to get inflation under control. A day later on Wall Street a total about face, investors selling off some of the biggest names, \uc2 Etsy\uc2  sinking nearly 17 percent, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is sliding more than 8 percent, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  also down 7.5 percent, and cryptocurrency taking a major hit too. Popular pandemic stocks also continued their slide, Peloton, Zoom, and \uc2 Netflix\uc2  all down more than 60 percent each in the last six months.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:04]\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN\uc2 , CNBC "SQUAWK BOX" CO-ANCHOR: The stock market is betting what the economy is going to look like not today but today but 12 months from now. And what the market is saying effectively is, it`s going to be worse not better than where we are today.\par \par \uc2 KENT (voice-over): Thanks to the interest rate hike, borrowing money is about to get even more expensive. Experts recommend consumers weather the storm by reducing their debt. And when it comes to your portfolio, they say hold tight.\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: Typically doing nothing is the best trade you can make in an environment like this. There is no question that the next several months are going to be very, very bumpy.\par \par \uc2 KENT: So the advice here really is to remain calm with your investments. But later this morning, we`re going to get the April jobs report. It`s expected to show the unemployment rate ticked down slightly, which will be good news and 380,000 jobs were added back to the economy. But there are still nearly two job openings for every Unemployed Worker right now. That labor shortage still one of the main reasons that prices that inflation is still so high. Craig?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  for us this morning, Jo, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Also this morning concerns over COVID are mounting once again, cases on the rise in the United States. The daily average climbing to 66,000, that`s up 50 percent over the last two weeks, deaths up over 30 percent over that same period. And now the vaccine push is taking a hit, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  moving to restrict the use of \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 `s one shot dose due to the rare risk of blood clots, all this in the wake that the U.S. crossed a grim milestone 1 million COVID related deaths. Joining us now to talk about all of it is White House COVID-19 Response Coordinator, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 , Dr. Jha, good morning to you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR: Good morning, Hoda. Thanks for having me here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The numbers are stunning. Actually, in the last three weeks cases have gone up 70 percent. And we were looking back at the calendar. And it was three weeks ago that the airlines lifted their mask mandates because of that judge`s order. Do you guys see a direct correlation between those mask mandates being lifted in the numbers we`re seeing right now?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, Hoda, it`s a really good question. I think what`s driving the increase in infections across the country right now is this very contagious, new sub variant, BA.2. It is spreading in large parts of the country. It`s the dominant variant here in the Northeast and in the Midwest and in other parts of the country as well. So I think that`s the primary driver, still believe that masking on airplanes would have been helpful and for the federal judge not to intervene. But I think the primary driver here is that very contagious sub variant.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, given that contagious of variant and the numbers going higher, can we expect to see mask mandates put in place again?\par \par \uc2 JHA: So you know what, we`re suggesting, I mean we`re suggesting, what we`re expecting is that we`re going to have localities make those decisions, you know, the CDC has laid out a map and approach to when to think about mask mandates really driven by three factors, infections, hospitalizations, hospital capacity, most of the country remains in the green, not recommending to have indoor mandates. But we will see localities make that decision on a case by case basis.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The numbers are staggering. I mean, when we said the word the 1 million people have died of COVID-19. That`s more than the Spanish flu. It`s actually more than the population of Atlanta, times two. I don`t know if you had a chance to break down those numbers, Dr. Jha. But of those million people, were you able to determine who in that group was vaccinated and who was not?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, so we do track that. What we know is that a vast majority of Americans who have perished from this disease, were unvaccinated. In the latest round of infections that we saw in that big Omicron surged in January, most of the deaths occurred either in unvaccinated people or people who are not boosted. So vaccines remain the best way to protect yourself especially getting boosted. But every one of those deaths vaccinated or unvaccinated is a tragedy and we`ve got to do everything we can to prevent that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s talk about another headline, Dr. Jha, it`s the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2  Dinner. There were a handful of people who tested positive for that. The President was in attendance at the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2  Dinner. It was, you know, 2,600 people, I think in this closed room. Dr. Fauci said himself that he made the choice not to go because of his risk assessment. If you had to do over, would you tell the President of the United States, would you have asked him not to attend that event?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Well, I think if we take a step back here, Hoda, and look at that event, look at events like that, everybody was vaccinated and boosted. Everybody had a negative test. I think the question is, are there ways of making gatherings safer or not? And I believe that the White House Correspondents Dinner did the things that we know make the events safer.\par \par \uc2 You know, the President made a choice and based on all of the facts and based on the fact that there are those sorts of protections in place and I think that`s the safe way to gather together and I actually encourage people if they`re going to gather, to use those tools to make it a safer thing to do.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:13]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, and just quickly at the end here, the J&J vaccine, a lot of folks did get that vaccine. And now we`re hearing from the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , it`s sort of like if you have a choice, choose \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or \uc2 Moderna\uc2 , because of the possibility the remote possibility of blood clots. For the folks who`ve already gotten that vaccine. Are they at any risk?\par \par \uc2 JHA: That`s a great question. Look, what we know, is extremely rare risk. And here`s the key point. All of those rare events happen in the first couple of weeks after you`ve gotten vaccinated. So if you`re more than a couple of weeks out, you`re in the clear. We don`t haven`t seen any cases come up beyond a few weeks. So if you`ve gotten one of these vaccines, the J&J vaccine, you`re protected. And you`re out of that window where that rare risk can happen.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Dr. Jha, do you like your new gig there at the White House?\par \par \uc2 JHA: It`s a challenge. But you know, it is an opportunity to serve the American people. And that really is an honor of a lifetime. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, you`re doing it well. Dr. Jha, thank you so much. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We turn now to the latest from Ukraine as the fighting rages on there. We are learning more this morning about the help Ukrainian forces are receiving from U.S. intelligence. This as the First Lady Dr. Jill Biden heads to the region to meet with refugees displaced by the war. NBC`s Erin McLaughlin is in Kyiv once again forced. Erin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Craig. The First Lady is expected here in the region this morning, Dr. Jill Biden telling the press pool to let -- the trip is meant to let the Ukrainian people know that we stand with them, this as we`re getting more information about America`s involvement in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): The sinking of the Russian cruiser Moskva, Russia`s pride of the Black Sea shocked the world after two Ukrainian anti- ship missiles struck the vessel on April 14th. This morning U.S. officials telling NBC News that happened with the help of the United States, Americans identified and located the vessel when asked by Ukrainian officials.\par \par \uc2 This video not verified by the Pentagon appears to show the Moskva after it was hit, sitting low in the water with smoke billowing. The U.S. did not know in advance that Ukraine was going to target the Moskva and was not involved in the decision to strike the official said adding the intelligence shared was meant to help Ukraine defend itself. The news after yesterday`s "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " report citing unnamed senior American officials that the U.S. has provided intelligence that allowed the Ukrainians to target and kill a number of Russian generals. On Thursday, the Pentagon responded to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " report.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We do not provide intelligence on the location of senior military leaders on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): This as the Russians work to clear the devastated port city of Mariupol so that citizens can mark Monday`s Victory Day this man says as Russia prepares to celebrate the end of World War II, while bloody battles rage at the city`s old steel plant where hundreds of Ukrainian fighters and civilians remain trapped inside.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, President Zelenskyy saying the Russian shelling is ongoing. Just imagine the hell, he says. A hell that Mariupol photographer Afgan Senovsky (ph) says is all too familiar, having fled the city just days ago. His images are chilling, including the photos of a diary written by an eight- year-old boy whose family was injured in the shelling, a page from the diary reads. My grandfather died, the boy writes, I have a wound on my back, torn skin. My sister has a head injury. My mother has a hole in her leg. Afgan (ph) reads the final words of the boy`s diary entry out loud.\par \par \uc2 AFGAN SENOVSKY (ph), PHOTOGRAPHER: My favorite city, Mariupol, has died. Small boy wrote that his city has died.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): The haunting portrait of innocence lost to war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: That photographer is not naming the boy or his family because they`re both trapped inside of that devastated city along with thousands of others. Meanwhile, this morning a spokesperson for the ICRC tells me that another evacuation mission is underway out of that besieged steel plant although we could offer no further details so as not to compromise that operation. Craig?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That diary. It is all just very sad. Erin McLaughlin for us there in Kyiv. Erin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Meantime advocates on both sides of the abortion debate are grappling with what comes next if the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade. That`s especially true in Texas already home to one of the nation`s most restrictive abortion laws. NBC`s Morgan Chesky is in Fort Worth with the latest on that story. Hey, \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , good morning.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Hoda, good morning. And right now if a woman shows up to this clinic, abortion is already outlawed in Texas after just six weeks but if the Supreme Court follows through on this draft opinion overturning Roe abortion could be all but outlawed here in the state of Texas not only that but the state would join a host of other conservative led states following suit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:15:12]\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Overnight, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  speaking out about that draft opinion, blasting the unprecedented leak as quote, absolutely appalling, according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," and vowing it will not affect the courts work. But across the country, states and activists on both sides are getting ready. Texas is at the epicenter of the abortion rights debate. It`s one of 13 states with so called trigger laws that would ban abortion with exceptions for the health of the mother if Roe is overturned Democrat \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  looking to unseat Republican Greg Abbott as Texas governor is seizing on the law Abbott signed last year.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE, FORMER UNITED STATES REPRESENTATIVE: This is not about life. This is about control. And this is about power. It`s about controlling the lives of the women of Texas and taking power away from them.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Abbott is focusing on the leaked draft Supreme Court opinion itself, urging the court to issue it without delay.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): I think the majority opinion in this case must be issued this week to show that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  will not be intimidated.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Louisiana also would outlaw abortion if Roe is overturned and its legislator is going further advancing a bill that would criminalize the procedure, meaning abortion providers and mothers could be charged with homicide. Bradley Pierce helped write the bill.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY PIERCE, ANTI-ABORTION ATTORNEY: We believe that abortion is cruel. And it`s the taking of an innocent life. And that`s we should make that illegal. And there shouldn`t be some exception for mothers.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY (voice-over): Connecticut`s Governor signing a sweeping bill into law, offering expanded access to abortion and new protections to providers and patients, including those from out of state. First Lady Jill Biden, in an interview airing this weekend on MSNBC, urging abortion rights advocates to step up efforts in their states.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: The election of the President matters because he`s the one who puts the justices on the court. Our state legislators are going to matter too, so people have to get involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Now in the meantime, Senate Democrats are expected to hold a vote next Wednesday on a bill that would guarantee abortion rights nationwide. But as of right now that measure is expected to fail. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Morgan Chesky for us there in Texas. \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Turning to the weather now including more threats of severe storms across the south and Mid-Atlantic today with nearly 40 million Americans affected. This as parts of Texas and Oklahoma are still recovering from a severe outbreak of tornadoes over the last several days. There`s some new drone footage here showing me the aftermath of a confirmed tornado. You can see campers overturn their trees uprooted at an RV park in East Texas. One resident was hit by a fallen tree. The injury we`re told, though, is not life threatening, thankfully. But that`s a terrifying scene there.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Which is a perfect segue into Al. That whether, it seems like it`s not letting up.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I know and it`s moving east too. And we`ve also got a lot of rain to talk about as we show you what`s happening. We`ve got flash flood watches and flood warnings from Missouri also into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic States. We`ve got a risk of severe weather for 38 million people stretching from Washington out to Cincinnati and down to New Orleans, Savannah, Georgia as well.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at wind gusts of over 60 miles per hour possible, damaging winds, hail, and tornadoes possible as the system moves to the east, so heavy rains soaking the Ohio Valley into the Mid-Atlantic today, a severe risk of weather from Louisiana all the way to Virginia. Tomorrow, a rainy cool day from D.C. to New York, showers and storms will continue across the southeast.\par \par \uc2 As we move into Mother`s Day, this low pressure is going to linger right along the eastern seaboard. So cool, cloudy, not a great day for Mother`s Day unseasonably cool for the Mid-Atlantic and the Northeast. Look at these rainfall totals through Sunday, one to three inches in the Northeast. But as you get into the Appalachians, we could be looking at upwards of four inches or more. We`re going to get to your local forecast coming up in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:20:07]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: For those of you in the South, get ready for some summertime temperature.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Really? All right, all right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s a good tease. Coming up, before that, those new images released in the manhunt, new images for the murder suspect and the corrections officer who helped him escape, what authorities are now saying about where they could be.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Plus, the countdowns on to the iconic Kentucky Derby. We`re going to have everything you need to know about the horses, the hype, and one high profile absence at tomorrow`s annual run for the roses live from \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2 . Look at our duo there. All right, but this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: With an internal hat.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re getting a jumpstart on Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Look at our Plaza, packed with moms and daughters who inspire us. We can`t wait to talk to them and surprise them after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:20]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Come on, 7:30, it`s Friday. Oh, come on. I love this song too. May 6th, 2022. We cannot wait to get outside. Seated at those tables, incredible moms and daughters, they`re filling our plaza. We`re having a little early Mother`s Day celebration. We broke up the umbrellas too. It`s going to be touching. It`s going to be inspiring. We`re going to have some fun. And I think you`re going to feel just really good about yourself. When this little bit is over.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We`re going to celebrate moms and we are also going to have just a few surprises as well.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love a surprise.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: First, though, your 7:30 headlines on this Friday, a former defense secretary under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is making a stunning accusation. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting this morning that in his upcoming book \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  says then President Trump floated the idea of launching missiles into Mexico. According to Esper, Trump said he wanted to quote destroy the drug labs and wipe out the cartels. NBC News has reached out to Mr. Trump`s team but have not received a response. "The Times" reports that the memoir was vetted by the Pentagon to check for classified information, and more than two dozen four star generals and some Cabinet members reviewed the book for accuracy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The man accused of attacking comedian Dave Chappelle on stage this week is now charged with four misdemeanor counts. The Los Angeles District Attorney`s office says the incident was criminal but did not rise to the felony level. Police arrested 23-year-old Isaiah Lee after he allegedly jumped on stage and tackled Chappelle at the Hollywood Bowl Tuesday night. Police said Lee had a replica handgun with a knife blade, but it`s not clear if he actually brandished it. If Lee is convicted, he faces up to a year and a half in prison.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And a history making appointment at the White House on Thursday. President Biden naming \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  as the new White House Press Secretary, she will be the first black woman and the first openly gay person to hold that position. Jean-Pierre has served as Deputy Press Secretary to \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  since the beginning of the Biden administration. Psaki`s last day is next Friday.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right meantime, the search is once again being expanded for that missing inmate from Alabama and the corrections officer who investigators believe helped him with his escape.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The pair managing to evade arrest now for more than a week. NBC`s Sam Brock is following the manhunt for us. He joins us once again from Alabama. Sam, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Craig, Hoda, good morning the tips have been pouring in from every corner of the country, according to the U.S. Marshal Service, so far, none of them bearing any fruit. But there is optimism right now, this duo will be caught after newly released images of the suspects we`re about to show you and help being enlisted at our borders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): A full week into a frantic manhunt and Casey White and Vicky White are still nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): So you don`t even know what region of the country they might be in?\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF RICK SINGLETON, LAUDERDALE COUNTY, AL: We have no idea.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): The U.S. Marshal Service releasing a batch of new photos and artist renderings featuring the towering six foot nine, Casey, with distinct tattoos on his chest and arms and one on his back depicting the logo of a white supremacist prison gang. Vicky`s rendering show with a five foot five blonde deputy would look like with darker hair.\par \par \uc2 Officials say it`s likely both have altered their appearances. The \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  have branches and task forces across the country aiding the search, though they can`t specify the number of resources, the agency does confirm outreach to border countries saying everything is on the table to find the pair`s location.\par \par \uc2 CHAD HUNT, DEPUTY COMMANDER OF U.S. MARSHALS GULF REGION: We have been in contact with CBP, you know, border patrol, Homeland Security. And we