t Dylan standing by live at \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2 . We got Mike to Rico beaming. We`re going to check it with both coming up right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:25]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We are back, 7:40 with In-Depth Today. Folks, get your hats ready. Get the mint juleps ready because tomorrow is the 148th running of the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, Dylan and Mike Tirico. They`re going to join us from iconic \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2  in just a second but first NBC`s Stephanie Gosk will set the stage for us. Hey, Steph, morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning. It`s that time again the quote, most exciting two minutes in sports, back to full capacity this weekend. But one of the most recognizable names in horse racing won`t be there. Trainer Bob Baffert suspended this year and next, after a failed drug test from his winning horse Medina Spirit. Without Baffert, some say could open the door for a new face of horse racing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): The bluegrass, the mint juleps, and the three-year-old thoroughbreds, the hallmarks of the Kentucky Derby will be on display Saturday for the 148th running of one of the oldest traditions in sports. But this venerated race returns with a tarnished image.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Medina Spirit has won the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): A failed post-race drug test that ultimately banned seven-time derby winner and trainer Bob Baffert from \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2  through 2023.\par \par \uc2 BOB BAFFERT, AMERICAN HORSE TRAINER: I got the biggest gut punch in racing for something that I didn`t do.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): But that doesn`t mean his footprint won`t be there. Trainer Tim Yakteen, Baffert`s former assistant 20 years ago, took over two of the notorious trainer`s horses, Messier and Taiba, who will compete Saturday.\par \par \uc2 TIM YAKTEEN, BAFFERT`S FORMER ASSISTANT: It`s an honor, you know, excitement. I`m really in for a journey here.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Messier is among the favorites but Brown coat, Zandon, is at the top with three to one odds. Fresh off a win and April`s bluegrass stakes. Jockey Mike Smith could become the oldest rider to win the derby at 56 with horse Taiba. While the young 26-year-old, Reylu Gutierrez, hopes to steer towards victory.\par \par \uc2 REYLU GUTIERREZ, JOCKEY: To get this opportunity is very humbling and I just hope that people, you know, can see that, hey, this is the youngest guy here and big things are coming for him.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): As the anticipation builds in Louisville, tourism is at a high bouncing back from the pandemic, attracting horse racing fans from all over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, I`m from Montreal, Quebec, Canada.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Maryland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Modesto, California.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Even a pre derby gala is planned tonight with a star studded guest list including Janet Jackson, Sam Hunt, and Orlando Bloom. All bets are off in the run for the roses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Of the Triple Crown races, the derby is the only one that is uninterrupted since it was first run in 1875. Even during COVID, it managed to go ahead just a little later in the year. You guys may remember it was in September 2020. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we`re looking forward to this. Steph, thank you. For more on the drama, more on the excitement of the Kentucky derby. We got Dylan Dreyer down at \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2  with our other pal. Hey, guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What`s up?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TIRICO, NBC NEWS SPORTSCASTER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: You know, Mike, it`s wild to be here because first of all, we haven`t been here in the last couple years, me personally.\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: Yes, good to have very back.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It`s nice to be back. Last year wasn`t at full capacity. We`re back this year. But someone who`s not back, Bob Baffert, his celebrity around here. Do you think it`ll be felt that his presence isn`t here?\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: Certainly, if you`re watching on T.V., I think Stephanie hit it perfectly in the setup story. Bob Baffert is the human face of this sport for the last two decades, and he`s one of the most successful trainers of all time having won six derbies would have been a seventh last year. But as she mentioned, it was disqualified after about nine month wait for that to finally happen. But the presence of Baffert is here with those two horses, Messier and Taiba, both have chances. They`re trained by a guy who was an assistant for Baffert. So his presence will be a part of this event, even though his familiar mane of white hair will not be.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Of course, you know, I like to gamble. I like to bet on the horses. I tend to go with like a 15 to 20 to one odd but who are the favorites going into the race?\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: Yes. It is not a clear cut. There`s a horse to beat, which is always fun for the wagering and gives you a little bit better return if you find the right one. Epicenter is one of those horses to watch. Steve Asmussen is the trainer of that horse. He`s won more races than any trainer in the history of North American thoroughbred racing, but not the derby. So he`s trying to win his first one.\par \par \uc2 So that`s one horse I would watch. And we mentioned Zandon as well. He`s also trained by Chad Brown, one of the best in the business looking for his first derby. That`s my choice. I`ll give that away early. If you want to change the odds go out there, but that`s the horse I think who`s going to win the derby.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: So those are the horses everyone`s talking about. But what`s it like you know, in the corners of the track here, you know, who has the buzz?\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: This is the week that we do Today show hours. We show up early at the track. There`s a lot of buzz about a horse from China -- from Japan, excuse me, that`s getting a lot of attention. Crown Pride has gotten, one, a lot of attention and two, a great track record. And horse racing in Japan has had a great run in the last six or seven months. The people who know around here are thinking about adding Crown Pride to their betting options for the weekend. So keep an eye on that horse.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:19]\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK. I got to meet Cyber Knife yesterday. Did you know that he has a goat that actually keeps him comfortable and keeps them calm before the race?\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: Well, it`s a five hour show. That`s why we have 16 of us here to cover. I didn`t know that but thank.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes. So that`s why I think I might place my bet on Cyber Knife, 20 to one odds. Guys?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK, all right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you. Thanks Dylan, thanks Mike. We`ll be watching again the coverage of the 148th running of the Kentucky Derby. It starts tomorrow at 2:30 Eastern on NBC, streaming on Peacock. A lot of people are wondering like if it rain, then what goes down?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I wanted to ask Mike because it looks like it`s going to be really rainy today. Maybe a shower tomorrow, but the track looks like it could be a little messy. What`s that going to mean?\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: Yes, Al, thing we always look at when we check out who to bet is a horse favorite because they`ve run well in the mud. Most of these horses have not run on a muddy track. So if you guys can take care of the weather today, dry it out by tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It does dry up pretty quickly though.\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: This track dries really well. So there`s a chance that it won`t be as muddy as it might be if it rained during the day like he did a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, all right.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All eyes in the mudders.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: How can a mud be a thought, I don`t know/ But anyway, let`s show you what we`ve got. Temperature wise and as far as the derby Saturday, 2:30, this coverage begins. Mostly cloudy, breezy, cool, 64, could be a sprinkler or two. But for the most part we think pretty dry. Now what we are talking about is some heat from New Orleans where today it`s going to be 89, Dallas 95, Midland, Texas 102, Denver almost 20 degrees above average at 88.\par \par \uc2 Sunday the heat continues, records possible in Dallas, Little Rock, Jackson, New Orleans, Austin, Roswell, Amarillo, all the way down to Laredo with 106 degrees. And on Monday, dozens of more records likely from Quincy, Illinois, Baton Rouge, New Orleans, Midland, Texas, and this last into next week. Mid 90s, Amarillo, Kansas City, Memphis, New Orleans on down to Dallas and out into Houston, Texas.\par \par \uc2 But here in the Northeast, oh my gosh, temperatures, only 66 in Des Moines today, Cleveland 57, New York 6 degrees cooler than average. Tomorrow the cool continues going right on into Mother`s Day, temperatures stay below average, but then slowly start to warm up as we get on into Tuesday, mid 60s by Tuesday in New York, Hagerstown, Maryland, upper 60s.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right coming up, Al, we`ve got a great celebration, a special one for Mother`s Day and it`s also the start of National Nurses Week.\par \par \uc2 [07:48:29]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So Harry Smith is going to introduce us to a mother-daughter duo going above and beyond to help people in their community get the care they need. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What`s up Carson?\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning everybody. Coming up Swifties are in a frenzy. On PopStart we`re going to check out Taylor surprise release of new music overnight after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220506ei560002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Amber Heard On The Stand For The First Time At Her Trial With Ex- Husband Johnny Depp\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Carson Daly, Emilie Ikeda, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 1,986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: We`re back with Carson. We`re also back with the latest from the high profile trial involving Johnny Depp and Amber Heard.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, the other former married couple suing each other for tens of millions of dollars and exchanging disturbing allegations. NBC`s Emilie Ikeda is here with more. Emilie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Carson, good morning. Amber Heard was on the stand for several hours yesterday emotionally recounting her allegations of abuse by ex, Johnny Depp, including one incident where she says he allegedly threatened to take her life. Her calling the experience the most painful and difficult thing she`s ever gone through.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD, AMERICAN ACTRESS: I said Johnny you hit me. You just hit me.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): In graphic detail, an emotional Amber Heard describing in court her whirlwind romance and marriage to A-list actor Johnny Depp, the relationship budding in 2011 after filming a movie together, but she testified things quickly spiraled out of control.\par \par \uc2 HEARD: He`s the love of my life. He was also this other thing. And that other thing was awful.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): The 36-year-old actress telling the court she was physically and sexually abused by Depp.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:01]\par \par \uc2 HEARD: He`s like grabbing my breasts, he`s touching my thighs, and he rips my underwear off. Proceeds to do a cavity search. He was looking -- he said he was looking for his drugs.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Heard said the abuse was often fueled by drugs and alcohol. Her lawyer sharing this 2013 photo of lines of cocaine, they also showed the jury this image where Heard`s arm appears to be bruised.\par \par \uc2 HEARD: He grabbed me by the arm. And he kind of just held me on the floor screaming at me. I don`t know how many times he hit me in the face.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Heard also describing a scene in which he said Depp held a pet dog out of a moving car.\par \par \uc2 HEARD: And he`s howling like an animal while holding the dog out of the window.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): The "Aquaman" actress taking the stand for the first time in the $50 million defamation lawsuit filed by her ex-husband over a 2018 op-ed she wrote for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " describing herself as a public figure representing domestic abuse. The article never mentioned Depp by name, but his lawyer say it clearly referenced him and cost him big ticket acting jobs. Several years earlier Heard, who is countersuing debt for 100 million was granted a protective order after telling the court he repeatedly hit her. Depp denied those allegations when he testified last month.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY DEPP, AMERICAN ACTOR: I never struck Ms. Heard. I`ve never struck a woman in my life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Johnny Depp has argued for weeks that he is the victim of domestic abuse. We`ll hear more from Amber Heard today who is expected to take the stand again before she`s cross examined by Depp`s legal team, certainly a case that has captured so many people`s attention.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It sure has. All right, Emilie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Emilie thanks for that. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up on PopStart, gang, you know the folks of PopStart love any news on "Top Gun."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: It`s been long enough. Three years this movie supposed to come out. Coming up, we`re going to take you inside, they finally have the world premiere. And that is how, well, it`s no surprise. That`s how Tom Cruise showed up. Yes, we`ll give you the details on him flying himself. But first, a quick check of your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:09]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It`s 8:00 on "TODAY." Coming up, Supreme Court clashes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: My body. my choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pro-life is pro-woman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Protests and political fallout over after the leaked Supreme Court draft that could overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: This is about more than abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What politicians are saying on both sides of the aisle and the wide- ranging implications that decision could have beyond abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Weather whiplash. Severe storms striking parts of the South. Tornadoes tearing through Oklahoma and Texas leaving behind a path of destruction and flooded roads. Al is live with where the system is headed next.\par \par \uc2 Plus, stars line up. Chris Pratt is here live. We`ll ask him about the latest Jurassic World film. His family and some of his other big projects jumping onto the big screen.\par \par \uc2 Then, Rebel Wilson weighs in on her senior year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REBEL WILSON, ACTRESS: I think there has been a mistake and I`ve been put in the wrong room because there`s a bunch of birthday cards for, like, really old person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ll ask what it was like returning to high school in her hilarious new movie when she joins us in Studio 1A.\par \par \uc2 And big announcement with summer right around the corner, we are looking forward to giving you some fun in the sun. So, stay tuned because we`ve got some big news you`ll go wild about. Today, Thursday, May 5, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: First trip to the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Denver, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Howdy, Taylor, Texas.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Mother-daughter trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Phoenix, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Dallas, Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Love you, Savannah and Hoda.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Jacksonville, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Today is my 50th birthday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Shout out to my fellow nurses in Ankeny, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good morning to my third graders in Woodbury, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: The class of 1970 turns 70.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wait. What?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The class of `70 turns 70? Cool. Welcome back, you all. Look at this crowd. OK. They have great timing because we are about to have a major announcement. We`ve been teasing it and here it is. All right. We are kicking off our Citi Summer Concert Series and it`s going to start two weeks from today.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: OK. Guys, I just want to say we`re going to do it in style. Harry Styles.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The very definition of a global superstar. When he was on our plaza --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It brought down the house. Blocks and blocks. Phil (ph), he`s releasing his new album, "Harry`s House." Well, his house is going to be right here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. We cannot wait, you all. We remember this concert so well. And we`ve been waiting patiently for a redo. This is it, you all, so you got to get your tickets.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That was the -- the last big concert we had before it all shut down. Mark your calendars, folks, Thursday, May 19th, exclusively on "TODAY." And to request fan passes, all you have to do is go to today.com/harrystyles. Do it right now. Request them now. There`s a good chance we`ll probably run out at the end to show.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Are you requesting fan passes?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, you know what I was looking --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She just brought out her phone --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- I brought my phone out because my niece is already asking for fan passes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, so it`s big.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s going to be big.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I think you know someone.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let`s turn to our news at 8:00. All right. Security is being ramped up at the Supreme Court after the leaked draft opinion in a crucial abortion case. NBC`s Chief White House Correspondent and Weekend "TODAY" Co-Anchor Peter Alexander, he`s got the latest from the fallout from that leak.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT AND WEEKEND "TODAY" CO-ANCHOR: Hi, Hoda. Good morning to you. With heated clashes outside the Supreme Court and frankly around the country, law enforcement overnight put up a new non-scalable fence around the Court, just like the one that they installed after the January 6th Capitol attack. This all comes as the White House and Senate Democrats are looking for ways to protect women`s reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 But their options right now are limited without the votes in the senate to guarantee abortion rights nationwide. President Biden -- he`s warning that conservative court could unravel other privacy rights like same-sex marriage and contraception and taking aim at Former President Trump`s MAGA Movement. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history. In recent American history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The President`s comments on Wednesday, abortion rights is already heating up as a critical issue now heading into this fall`s midterms with Democrats looking to capitalize, but Republicans insisting it will not affect the outcome this November. Hoda.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Peter Alexander for us there at the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: There is a lot of cleaning up to do this morning after tornadoes ripped across parts of Texas and Oklahoma. Take a look, we`ve got some dramatic videos to show you. You see one tornado as it rips through Seminole, Oklahoma. It`s about 50 miles from Oklahoma City. Storm debris flying across the roads, transformers exploding, lightning bolts flashing in the distance. It`s just terrifying.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of buildings were destroyed. Fortunately, though, no reports of any serious injuries. Al has got more on this severe weather and where we will find it today. Hi, Al.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC`S TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: That`s right, guys. Right now, we`re watching heavy rain developing. We`ve got a flood threat into the evening from Oklahoma all the way into Missouri as the system pushes to the East. They`re going to be looking at another three to four inches of rain before this is all over.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we got a severe threat today for 20 million people, especially from Waco, Texas all the way to Little Rock. Nashville is part of it. Springfield all the way down just to the North of Houston. And then for Friday, we got a risk of severe weather into the Southeast. Mid-Atlantic States, 33 million people, wind gusts of 60 miles per hour. Isolated tornadoes. Hail possible. Heavy rain from Dallas all the way into the Northeast with anywhere from two to three inches of rain with isolated amounts up to four inches. Craig.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right. Buddy, thank you so much. We`ll come back to you in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220507ei550002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Security Being Stepped Up At The Supreme Court As Protests Grow Over That Leaked Draft Opinion, Suggesting Roe Versus Wade Could Soon Be Overturned\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 1,280 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. On edge, security being stepped up at the Supreme Court as protests grow over that leaked draft opinion, suggesting Roe versus Wade could soon be overturned. We are live in Washington with where the battle dividing the nation goes from here.\par \par \uc2 Unfathomable, the U.S. reaches 1 million COVID deaths, a grim milestone in the pandemic once unthinkable and the virus surging yet again. This morning, the hotspots from coast to coast giving health officials new concerns.\par \par \uc2 To close for comfort, dramatic moments as severe weather hammers the south. A driver in Oklahoma, swerving to avoid popping power lines while a powerful tornado bears down one of the eight reported across the region. Just ahead, the damage left in the wake in today`s ongoing threat. Al is tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 Cracking down, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  gets tough on inflation with the biggest interest rate hike in 22 years. What you need to know about that and the impact of shrinkflation. Why some companies are now giving you less but charging you the same.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, unfortunate and unsettling, Dave Chappelle weighs in on that alarming onstage attack during his comedy show. New video showing the moment he was tackled.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVE CHAPPELLE, AMERICAN STAND-UP COMEDIAN: Make some noise for hip hop history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This morning, what we`re learning now about the suspect, how did he manage to slip this weapon past security.\par \par \uc2 And what an entrance, Tom Cruise flies a helicopter to the world premiere of his new "Top Gun" movie overnight, touching down on the deck of an aircraft carrier, the arrival that will take your breath away today Thursday, May 5th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, good morning, good morning. Good to see you on this Thursday morning. Thanks for joining us. Did Tom Cruise just do that? Did he seriously do that?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I have to say it was pretty startling. He got a need for speed. Now he`s got a pilot`s license, I mean.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The guy can make an entrance. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: For "Top Gun 2" which is coming out soon.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ve been waiting for that one. We look forward to talking about that. We do have a busy morning this Thursday including those severe storms that tore through parts of Texas and Oklahoma causing extensive damage. And by the way, more on the way today 20 million people at risk across the country. Al is back here in Studio 1A tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Also ahead this morning that stunning toll in this country`s battle with COVID. According to our NBC News count, deaths of people who have had the virus have just crossed the 1 million mark. That news coming amid rise in hospitalizations in some parts of the country, we`re going to have the latest straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But we do begin with the growing fallout from the leaked Supreme Court draft that would overturn Roe versus Wade with a divided nation waiting the final decision. The battle over abortion is already raging, restrictive bills pending in 40 states this year. NBC`s chief White House correspondent Peter Alexander has the very latest. Hey, Peter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hoda, good morning. That explosive leaked draft opinion has ignited emotions across this country, sparking those new security concerns leading law enforcement to fortify the perimeter outside the Supreme Court, putting up a new eight foot fence just like the one they put up following the January 6th attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Overnight, this new non scalable fencing being installed around the Supreme Court after heated clashes in recent days on the steps of the court and nationwide between Americans on both sides of the abortion battle.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Pro-life is pro-woman.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The question this morning, what happens next if the Supreme Court follows through on its leaked draft opinion that overturns Roe v. Wade? President Biden`s slamming the draft.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is about a lot more than abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Warning the conservative court could unravel other privacy rights like same sex marriage and contraception and taking aim at former President Trump`s MAGA movement.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  slamming the draft as an assault on women.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), HOUSE SPEAKER: Did violence not only to women, but to the Constitution of the United States.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): But Republicans like Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida are taking a wait and see approach largely focusing on the leak.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:05]\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): You know you can leak stuff out of a court which is really unprecedented. But let`s see when you actually have something rendered.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Overturning Roe would not outlaw abortion, but we leave it up to state legislatures to decide whether there should be restrictions. North Dakota is among the 13 states with so called trigger laws that would ban abortion with exceptions for the health of the mother if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It means lights out in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Abortion rights opponents say it will be the moment they`ve worked toward for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, SUSAN B. ANTHONY LIST PRESIDENT: It looks like we may have an opportunity to build consensus and pass laws to save lives all over the country.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): It`s already heating up as a crucial issue heading into this fall`s midterms with Democrats looking to capitalize, but Republicans insisting it won`t matter in November.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): We`re going to win in 2022 and Roe v. Wade is not going to change the outcome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And Peter this draft ruling has prompted Democrats to think of other strategies to try and help protect abortion rights. What do they have in mind?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, Hoda, that`s right. Senate Democrats right now we`re on track to hold a vote next week that would guarantee abortion rights nationwide even though they don`t have the votes and it is doomed to fail. But their goal here is to try to force every senator to put their position on this issue on the record. And with Democrats fearing that they`re poised to lose control of the House and Senate this fall. I spoke to a top Biden advisor who says that they are hoping this threat to abortion rights right now serves as a call to action that will reenergize their base. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Peter Alexander for us there at the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220507ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Trigger Laws\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLTL Some of the states that have those abortion trigger laws we just talked about are preparing for what might be inevitable. Blayne Alexander tonight on how the leaked Supreme Court draft decision is playing out in those states and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In North Dakota, women have one place to go for abortion services, Red River Women`s Clinic in Fargo.\par \par \uc2 TAMMI KROMENAKER, DIRECTOR AND OWNER, RED RIVER WOMEN`S CLINIC: That is a very rural state. We serve a lot of Black, Indigenous, and People of Color in our center. And those are the people that were disproportionately being harmed by this ban.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:06]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That ban is not final. But after the bombshell leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion, providers like Tammy Kromenaker are preparing. She`s posted on the clinic`s website letting clients know their appointments are still good. But she says if Roe is overturned --\par \par \uc2 KROMENAKER: It means lights out in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s because the procedure would immediately be outlawed. North Dakota is one of 13 states with a trigger law.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Across the country, it`s what abortion rights advocates fear the most.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pro-life is pro-woman.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And what anti-abortion groups are hoping for.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  LIST: For almost 50 years, we`ve been working for this point.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of the Susan B. Anthony List says in recent months, she`s talked to governors in about 20 states. Her message, make sure your state is ready to go.\par \par \uc2 DANNENFELSER: Those conversations now are you know, far less conditional seeming, because it looks like we may have an opportunity to build consensus and pass laws to save lives all over the country.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Louisiana, abortion is currently legal up to 20 weeks. But if Roe were to end, Louisiana`s three clinics would immediately shut down.\par \par \uc2 What would you say to women who are still desperate for that procedure and would still seek it out?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS, ASSOCIATE DIRECTOR, LOUISIANA RIGHT TO LIFE: I would say that there are resources available to you, that we want to help you here in Louisiana. The pro-life movement is very supportive of women.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This week, Louisiana State Legislature advanced a bill that would make it more difficult to challenge the state`s trigger law. State senator Cleo Fields was one of just three lawmakers to vote against it.\par \par \uc2 Is this an insurmountable hill that you`re trying to climb here?\par \par \uc2 CLEO FIELDS, LOUISIANA STATE SENATOR: You know, maybe so. But I mean -- but at the end of the day, you know, you got to fight for what`s -- what you feel is right. I just don`t feel that a majority male legislature should be telling women what to do with their bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): And some states are working on the other side as well to strengthen laws that would protect abortion rights should Roe be overturned. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220505ei54000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Protests Nationwide Over That Leaked Supreme Court Draft That Would Overturn Roe Versus Wade Abortion Protections\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, several developing stories, a grim new COVID milestone and the growing fallout of that Supreme Court leak.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): First, the protests nationwide over that leaked Supreme Court draft that would overturn Roe versus Wade abortion protections. President Biden claiming the GOP may target other longstanding rights next, and calling Donald Trump`s supporters "the most extreme political organization in recent American history." How Republicans are reacting as Democrats look to make abortion a central issue in the Midterms."\par \par \uc2 Also tonight a dire new number in the pandemic. COVID deaths in the U.S. now surpassing one million with cases rising again.\par \par \uc2 The Fed announcing the biggest rate hike in more than 22 years, as it seeks to tame soaring inflation. The impact on everything from mortgages, to credit cards, to car loans.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, heavy fighting at the Mariupol steel plant where hundreds of civilians remain trapped. And the new evidence that death toll in the single worst attack on civilians so far maybe double. Initial reports, some 600 killed.\par \par \uc2 Tracking severe storms on the move tonight.\par \par \uc2 And Dave Chappelle attacked on stage. The suspect taken down and arrested. The unusual weapon police say he had.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, just breaking as we come on the air tonight. A stunning milestone reached in this country`s battle with COVID. According to an NBC News count, the number of dead who had the virus in the US has just crossed the one million mark. We`ll get to that in a few moments.\par \par \uc2 But we began here tonight with a scramble over the future of abortion rights. After that leaked draft Supreme Court decision that appeared to show the handwriting on the wall, a court inclined to rule there`s no constitutional right to abortion, sending the decision to the states.\par \par \uc2 Democrats left with no way to stop the train, now looking to make Republican opponents of abortion pay a political price as the Midterm election campaigns heat up. President Biden setting the Democrats tone warning abortion could be the first of many rights stripped away by the court.\par \par \uc2 Emotions running high on both sides with the outcome deeply personal and consequential for so many. Peter Alexander has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, with protests growing --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re going to --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden insisting that leaked draft opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade could lead to the unraveling of other privacy rights, from contraception to gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is about a lot more than abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The President using the draft opinion to slam former President Trump`s allies.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Although the draft is not the court`s final decision, Vice President Harris blasted Republican leaders during an event last night.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body? How dare they?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still, Democrats do not have many options without the Senate votes to pass a law that would guarantee abortion rights nationwide. Instead, Democrats are looking to amplify the abortion issue heading into the Midterms to target Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): This November is going to be a referendum on privacy and your rights to freedom as an American. They don`t want to make that the case. They want it to be oh, it`s inflation. It`s something at the border. No, it`s something more basic than that.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Overturning Roe would not outlaw abortion but with leave it up to state legislatures to decide whether there should be restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Currently, 13 states have so-called trigger laws that would ban abortion if Roe is overturned with exceptions for the health of the mother. Republicans tonight insists there`s a reason Democrats are focusing on the leaked opinion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): They want to change the topic from immigration, from the soaring inflation. They want to get off of the things that are so unpopular about this president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, even without the votes, Senate Democrats are moving forward next week. What`s the thinking there?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. Democrats have taken the first steps toward a vote to guarantee abortion rights that`s all but certain to fail. But they say they still want to get every senator on the record. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220505ei54000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trigger Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: And as you just saw the battle lines are quickly being drawn from coast to coast with people on both sides of this emotional issue, give a new fuel for their arguments. So what is next, NBC`s Blayne Alexander is in Baton Rouge, Louisiana with that part of the story. Hey, Blayne, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Hoda, good morning to you. This abortion clinic here in Baton Rouge is one of three in the state of Louisiana, all of which would be immediately impacted if Roe were to be overturned. It`s because of what`s known as a trigger law something that nearly a third of the country already has in place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Abortion is a human right.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): From Chicago to San Diego.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Abortion is oppression.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): New York to the nation`s Capitol, the fight over abortion rights is spilling into the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve always supported life. I believe that every child should be born.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m afraid for my daughters. I`m afraid for my future granddaughters. I`m afraid for all women.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Even as the nation`s highest court says the bombshell leaked draft is not a final ruling, states are already swiftly preparing for action should the court strike down the landmark decision protecting abortion access. If Roe v. Wade is eventually overturned it will fall to each state to control abortion access. At least 20 would likely ban the procedure outright, 13 of those through so called trigger laws, automatically outlawing abortions immediately with few exceptions. It comes as some Republican-led states are already enacting a flurry of restrictive abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Arkansas is a pro-life state. We have already put a trigger law in place.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): In Oklahoma, the governor signed a law Tuesday banning the procedure after six weeks. And while 16 states have already passed laws codifying the right to abortion, Connecticut has gone one step further passing a bill to expand the list of medical professionals who can perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM TONG\uc2  (D-CT), ATTORNEY GENERAL: Half of this country like Connecticut, led by Connecticut will respect women, patients, doctors.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Nationwide clinics say their patients will be caught in the middle.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER, DIRECTOR OF JACKSON WOMEN`S HEALTH ORGANIZATION: It does make my job harder knowing that women won`t be able to access.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Shannon Brewer is director of Mississippi`s only abortion clinic, the one at the center of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: You`re going to see an influx of women doing things that they -- that are unsafe because out of being desperate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And back to that new law in Oklahoma, it`s just hours old with the impact already stretches well beyond Oklahoma`s borders to women who have come to the state from other states, including Texas to get the procedure. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Blayne Alexander for us, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220505ei540008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Fallout After A Glaring Security Breach Inside Windsor Castle\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Carson Daly, Keir Simmons \par \uc2 1,499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: 7:49, Carson joins a table. We`re turning to an alarming security scare for the royal family, Carson.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, good morning guys. We told you about this yesterday a man managing to talk his way into spending the night at Windsor Castle. That breach as you can imagine, especially concerning ahead of the celebrations coming up to mark the Queen`s Platinum Jubilee.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, Today senior international correspondent Keir Simmons is outside Buckingham Palace with more on the investigation and the fallout. Keir, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Savannah, good morning to you. Good morning guys. And they are getting ready for the Queen`s Jubilee here. The scaffolding that you can see is covering the statue of Queen Victoria. They`re making sure that she will look her best. But now more details about that security scandal surrounding those royal guards who will certainly be prominent at those events, folks will recognize them if you`ve seen the changing of the guard of Buckingham Palace here, the guys wearing the bearskin hats and the red coats. And now they`re just red faced.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:23]\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): Fears for the safety of the Queen this morning after a stunning security breach involving royal guardsmen whose role is protecting royal palaces.\par \par \uc2 KAY BURLEY, BRITISH BROADCASTER: How an intruder pretending to be a priest reportedly spent the night in the barracks.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): It happened at Windsor Castle, the site of Prince Philip`s funeral last year, and Harry and Meghan`s wedding. An intruder gaining access to the barracks and the trust of senior officers of the Coldstream Guards, world famous for their red coats and large bear skin hats. The man arrived last Wednesday evening posing as a priest. He allegedly spent the night eating and drinking and was even given a bed for the night. Only in the morning at 9:20 a.m., the alarm was raised with police and the intruder removed from the barracks.\par \par \uc2 The Queen was not in residence, but the breach brings back memories of a Buckingham Palace intruder 40 years ago, portrayed in "The \uc2 Crown\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name is Michael.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Get out. I promise you, you have nothing to fear from me.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): Who was able to reach the Queen`s bedroom. She famously kept him talking until help arrived. What do you want? If it`s money?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t want money. I don`t want anything. I just want to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): This latest scare, well, far less serious is undergoing a thorough investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : And guys, the Coldstream Guards go back to 1650, so this won`t be the most embarrassing defeat in their history. But a pub landlord reportedly today saying that the same man turned up at that pub claiming to be Prince Harry`s friend from America and asking for free food. That pub landlord told him to swing his hook. So the question is, how come the royal guards --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Swing his hook.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Swing his hook. You haven`t heard that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Does it mean beat it?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Swing his hook. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Keir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DALY: They need to tighten up security all over the globe.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Guys coming up, we`ve got a packed PopStart including the absolutely wild first trailer now available for the "Weird Al" biopic starring Daniel Radcliffe. We`ll have it for you. But first, a quick check of your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It is 8:00 on "TODAY." Coming up, the court and the controversy. The Supreme Court confirms the authenticity of that leaked draft overturning Roe V. Wade. Protesters on both sides of the debate making their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Abortion, it`s not fair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The very latest on the intense back and forth just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Then, status update. The U.S. now officially declaring WNBA star Brittney Griner wrongfully detained after being held in Russian custody since February.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY VANDERSLOOT, FORMER TEAMMATE OF GRINER: We want Brittney to be on everybody`s minds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Just ahead, inside the fight to bring her home.\par \par \uc2 Plus, inflation busters. With the cost of just about everything going up. Vicky Nguyen has tips to help you find extra cash and soothe the sting. What you need to know.\par \par \uc2 And ready to rock. The Rock and Roll Hall of Fame about to announce this year`s inductees. We`ll show you who made the cut in Cleveland. Today Wednesday, May 4, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Minneapolis, Minnesota. Saying hi to my husband and my kids.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mother/daughter trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Baltimore, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Ypsilanti, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To the "TODAY" show for Harvey`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Rivera family trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Sacramento, California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating 35 years of friendship.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And watching the "TODAY" show.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: from North Platte, Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning, everybody. Welcome back to "TODAY." Such cute folks on the plaza. I had kind of crazy eyes in the picture though.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: No, you didn`t.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I did. It was a little weird.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Because you were excited because it`s Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s Wednes-yay.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And it`s May 4th. Do you want to take the honor?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: May the fourth be with you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: May the fourth be with you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Nerds everywhere like me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Applauding, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Busy morning. Let`s get right to your news at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 In the fight over abortion rights has intensified after the leak of a Supreme Court draft opinion suggesting Roe versus Wade could be soon overturned. NBC`s Chief White House Correspondent, Weekend TODAY co-anchor Kristen Welker`s got the latest. Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT AND WEEKEND TODAY CO- ANCHOR: Hi, Savannah. Good morning to you. That bombshell from the Supreme Court still rocking Washington and the rest of the country. And firing up protesters on both sides after the high court authenticated that leaked draft opinion by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  which finds the 1973 Roe V. Wade ruling, which protects a woman`s right to an abortion was, "egregiously wrong from the start."\par \par \uc2 Justices can and do change their positions before a final decision. But the draft shows four other conservatives joining Alito. The three Democratic- appointed justices would likely dissent. And Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ` opinion is still unclear. Now, based on the draft, there are enough votes to overthrow the decision that has established a federal right to an abortion since 1973. And if that happens, laws around abortion would be up to each state. Democrats are now aiming to energize voters around their message that Roe V. Wade should be codified into law by Congress. Vice President Harris with a fiery response overnight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Those Republican leaders who are trying to weaponize the use of the law against women. What we say, how dare they. How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, for their part, many Republicans cheered the possibility that Roe could be overturned but blasted the leak itself. The Supreme Court is expected to issue a final ruling within the next two months. Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Kristen Welker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Overseas now. Russian forces bombed vital infrastructure across Ukraine overnight. Here are the three things to watch. Russian missiles hit three electrical substations in the Western City of Lviv. The attack caused power outages and affected crucial train lines. The associated press obtained graphic video of a town near Kharkiv retaken after two months of Russian occupation. It appears to show the bodies of civilians and Russian soldiers along the streets and inside homes. And President Biden gave a pep talk yesterday at a factory in Alabama that makes javelin anti-tank missiles used by Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220505ei54000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Confirms The Authenticity Of That Draft Opinion Overturning Roe Versus Wade And The Battle Lines Hardened From Coast To Coast\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 1,224 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Shock waves, the Supreme Court confirms the authenticity of that draft opinion overturning Roe versus Wade and the battle lines hardened from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The Chief Justice calling the leak, a betrayal and launching an investigation as both sides point fingers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): If it`s a conservative, you`re a traitor to the cause. If it`s a liberal, you`re the dumbest person in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Complete coverage straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, trump card J.D. Vance prevails in Ohio`s closely watched Republican Senate primary that come from behind victor after a late endorsement from the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J. D. VANCE, AMERICAN POLITICIAN: Ladies and gentlemen, it ain`t the death of the America First agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning, what it means for the race to hold Congress in this fall`s crucial midterms.\par \par \uc2 All eyes on the Fed, another key interest rate hike expected today, a new step in the fight to lower record inflation. Will it work and where your costs are going up.\par \par \uc2 Walkout, new video of the moment a convicted felon now on the run fled in Alabama jail alongside the corrections officer police believe helped him escape. What investigators are now revealing about their relationship as the manhunt enters day six, we`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, round two, millions in the Midwest bracing for another day of severe weather, including the potential for more tornadoes, which you could expect and where the threat is headed next.\par \par \uc2 And no laughing matter, overnight, comedian Dave Chappelle attacked by a man who stormed the stage during a performance at L.A.`s iconic Hollywood ball. Inside the alarming breach, what police are saying about the suspect and the other comedian who just happen to be there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is that Will Smith?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Today, Wednesday, May 4th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hi, everybody. Good morning. Welcome to Today. Good to have you with us on a Wednesday morning. We want to get right to our top story this morning. The Supreme Court has now confirmed that leaked draft opinion is genuine. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  labeling it a breach of trust while adding this draft opinion does not represent a final decision of the court.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, this comes as protesters on both sides of the argument are making their voices heard from the steps of the Supreme Court to communities all across the country.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: In the meantime, strict new laws are already being passed on the state level. Oklahoma`s Governor signing one yesterday modeled after a similar one in Texas that bans abortions after 16 weeks. We`ve got two reports for you. We`ll start with NBC`s Kristen Walker at the White House. Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Savannah and Hoda. Good morning to both of you. The fallout is intensifying this morning after that unprecedented leak of a Supreme Court draft suggesting the court is poised to overturn Roe v. Wade. In a blistering statement as you say, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  confirmed the authenticity of the draft while calling the leak a betrayal and a front to the court and saying the court martial will investigate. All of it could upend the midterms with Democrats fighting to hold on to their majorities and Republicans looking to take back control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Overnight, that bombshell from the Supreme Court continuing to rock Washington and the country firing up protesters on both sides after the High Court authenticated that leaked draft opinion by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , which finds the 1973 Roe v. Wade ruling which protects a woman`s right to an abortion was quote, egregiously wrong from the start. Justices can and do change their positions before a final decision.\par \par \uc2 But the draft shows four other conservatives joining Alito, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , the three Democratic appointed justices would likely dissent, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ` opinion still unclear. Based on the draft, there are enough votes to overthrow the decision that has established a federal right to abortion since 1973. If that happens, access to abortion would become a patchwork system across the country in the hands of the states. Something many opponents of abortion rights have been pushing for.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every state should have the right to allow consensus in its state to make its way into the law.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Democrats now aiming to energize voters around their message. Roe v. Wade should be codified into law by Congress. But currently there`s not enough support in Congress for such a move. President Biden warning, Republicans could try to take away other rights that were granted by the High Court from contraception to same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:13]\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It would mean that every other decision we made to the notion of privacy is thrown into question.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Senator Elizabeth Warren joining a protest yesterday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I am angry because of who will pay the price for this.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): For their part, many Republicans cheered the possibility that Roe could be overturned but blasted the leak itself.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: If it`s a conservative, you`re a traitor to the cause. If it`s a liberal, you`re the dumbest person in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Moderate Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , who voted to confirm conservative Justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh says she feels misled by their responses about Roe v. Wade, during their respective confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT: settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT: That`s the law of the land. I accept the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, Senator Collins saying if the draft opinion is the final decision on Roe versus Wade, it would be quote, completely inconsistent with what Justice Kavanaugh And Gorsuch said in their hearings, and in their private meetings with her. As for the case itself, the Supreme Court is expected to issue a final ruling within the next two months. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right Kristen Walker at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220505ei540005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 4, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander, Blayne Alexander, Gabe Gutierrez, Jo  Ling Kent, Kelly Cobiella, Bill Karins, Miguel Almaguer, Vaughn Hillyard \par \uc2 3,941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, several developing stories, a grim new COVID milestone and the growing fallout of that Supreme Court leak.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): First, the protests nationwide over that leaked Supreme Court draft that would overturn Roe versus Wade abortion protections. President Biden claiming the GOP may target other longstanding rights next, and calling Donald Trump`s supporters "the most extreme political organization in recent American history." How Republicans are reacting as Democrats look to make abortion a central issue in the Midterms."\par \par \uc2 Also tonight a dire new number in the pandemic. COVID deaths in the U.S. now surpassing one million with cases rising again.\par \par \uc2 The Fed announcing the biggest rate hike in more than 22 years, as it seeks to tame soaring inflation. The impact on everything from mortgages, to credit cards, to car loans.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, heavy fighting at the Mariupol steel plant where hundreds of civilians remain trapped. And the new evidence that death toll in the single worst attack on civilians so far maybe double. Initial reports, some 600 killed.\par \par \uc2 Tracking severe storms on the move tonight.\par \par \uc2 And Dave Chappelle attacked on stage. The suspect taken down and arrested. The unusual weapon police say he had.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, just breaking as we come on the air tonight. A stunning milestone reached in this country`s battle with COVID. According to an NBC News count, the number of dead who had the virus in the US has just crossed the one million mark. We`ll get to that in a few moments.\par \par \uc2 But we began here tonight with a scramble over the future of abortion rights. After that leaked draft Supreme Court decision that appeared to show the handwriting on the wall, a court inclined to rule there`s no constitutional right to abortion, sending the decision to the states.\par \par \uc2 Democrats left with no way to stop the train, now looking to make Republican opponents of abortion pay a political price as the Midterm election campaigns heat up. President Biden setting the Democrats tone warning abortion could be the first of many rights stripped away by the court.\par \par \uc2 Emotions running high on both sides with the outcome deeply personal and consequential for so many. Peter Alexander has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, with protests growing --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re going to --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: President Biden insisting that leaked draft opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade could lead to the unraveling of other privacy rights, from contraception to gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is about a lot more than abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The President using the draft opinion to slam former President Trump`s allies.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Although the draft is not the court`s final decision, Vice President Harris blasted Republican leaders during an event last night.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body? How dare they?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still, Democrats do not have many options without the Senate votes to pass a law that would guarantee abortion rights nationwide. Instead, Democrats are looking to amplify the abortion issue heading into the Midterms to target Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): This November is going to be a referendum on privacy and your rights to freedom as an American. They don`t want to make that the case. They want it to be oh, it`s inflation. It`s something at the border. No, it`s something more basic than that.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Overturning Roe would not outlaw abortion but with leave it up to state legislatures to decide whether there should be restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Currently, 13 states have so-called trigger laws that would ban abortion if Roe is overturned with exceptions for the health of the mother. Republicans tonight insists there`s a reason Democrats are focusing on the leaked opinion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): They want to change the topic from immigration, from the soaring inflation. They want to get off of the things that are so unpopular about this president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, even without the votes, Senate Democrats are moving forward next week. What`s the thinking there?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. Democrats have taken the first steps toward a vote to guarantee abortion rights that`s all but certain to fail. But they say they still want to get every senator on the record. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Some of the states that have those abortion trigger laws we just talked about are preparing for what might be inevitable. Blayne Alexander tonight on how the leaked Supreme Court draft decision is playing out in those states and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In North Dakota, women have one place to go for abortion services, Red River Women`s Clinic in Fargo.\par \par \uc2 TAMMI KROMENAKER, DIRECTOR AND OWNER, RED RIVER WOMEN`S CLINIC: That is a very rural state. We serve a lot of Black, Indigenous, and People of Color in our center. And those are the people that were disproportionately being harmed by this ban.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:06]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That ban is not final. But after the bombshell leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion, providers like Tammy Kromenaker are preparing. She`s posted on the clinic`s website letting clients know their appointments are still good. But she says if Roe is overturned --\par \par \uc2 KROMENAKER: It means lights out in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s because the procedure would immediately be outlawed. North Dakota is one of 13 states with a trigger law.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Across the country, it`s what abortion rights advocates fear the most.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pro-life is pro-woman.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And what anti-abortion groups are hoping for.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  LIST: For almost 50 years, we`ve been working for this point.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of the Susan B. Anthony List says in recent months, she`s talked to governors in about 20 states. Her message, make sure your state is ready to go.\par \par \uc2 DANNENFELSER: Those conversations now are you know, far less conditional seeming, because it looks like we may have an opportunity to build consensus and pass laws to save lives all over the country.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Louisiana, abortion is currently legal up to 20 weeks. But if Roe were to end, Louisiana`s three clinics would immediately shut down.\par \par \uc2 What would you say to women who are still desperate for that procedure and would still seek it out?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS, ASSOCIATE DIRECTOR, LOUISIANA RIGHT TO LIFE: I would say that there are resources available to you, that we want to help you here in Louisiana. The pro-life movement is very supportive of women.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This week, Louisiana State Legislature advanced a bill that would make it more difficult to challenge the state`s trigger law. State senator Cleo Fields was one of just three lawmakers to vote against it.\par \par \uc2 Is this an insurmountable hill that you`re trying to climb here?\par \par \uc2 CLEO FIELDS, LOUISIANA STATE SENATOR: You know, maybe so. But I mean -- but at the end of the day, you know, you got to fight for what`s -- what you feel is right. I just don`t feel that a majority male legislature should be telling women what to do with their bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): And some states are working on the other side as well to strengthen laws that would protect abortion rights should Roe be overturned. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn it over their top story, COVID`s horrific toll. Our team here at NBC News has been compiling data daily from states across the country. And late today, we learned that more than one million Americans have now died from the disease, a milestone we didn`t want to think about but knew was coming in a pandemic that`s far from over. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It was once unthinkable, tonight, it`s reality. One million people in the U.S. have now died from COVID 19 according to the ongoing NBC News count.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just miss everything about her. Everything, everything.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Lisa Wilson of Palm Beach County, Florida lost her grandmother Lily May to the virus last year.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was so terrifying. My aunt called me and she says something is wrong with her. She was laid across the bed, and she was like gasping for air.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Lisa would say goodbye not just to her grandmother, but to her uncle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He`s so funny.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And four cousins within weeks. And all six relatives claimed by COVID.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was a very, very hard.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Back in March 2020, the streets were deserted and more trucks first appeared outside hospitals. For many Americans, the idea of a million COVID deaths seemed farfetched.\par \par \uc2 DR. DEBORAH BIRX, FORMER WHTIE HOUSE CORONAVIRUS RESPONSE COORDINATOR: We could get in the range of 100,000 to 200,000 fatalities.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Dr. Angela Chin of \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  diagnosed the first COVID case in New York even before the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  declared the outbreak a global pandemic.\par \par \uc2 When you first made that diagnosis, did you have any idea what this pandemic would become?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA CHEN, EMERGENCY MEDICINE DOCTOR, \uc2 MOUNT SINAI HOSPITAL\uc2 : I don`t think any of us did.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: We spoke with her today in Central Park at the former site of a makeshift field hospital.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: It`s really something that I think has fundamentally changed who I am and how I think about medicine, how I think about my patients.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: So, many families tonight, including Lisa Wilson and her husband are reflecting on the lives lost and lessons learned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All of my family members that`s deceased from COVID, they were not vaccinated. So, vaccines save lives.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Even seven figures can`t measure the grief.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We love you.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Gabe Gutierrez, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And I`ll share some thoughts and perspective on the solemn milestone in just a few minutes. But right now, let`s turn to the fight against inflation. The Fed raising interest rates by half a percentage point, the most in more than two decades, meaning just about everything we buy on credit will get more expensive. \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  has that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT\uc2 , NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With inflation skyrocketing, Willy Price tells us she`s praying prices will come down soon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everything is high. Everything is almost unaffordable. Food is high, the gas is high, electric bills went up.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:04]\par \par \uc2 KENT: Like the majority of Americans, she now lives paycheck to paycheck. These realities pushing the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to take action again today raising interest rates by half a percentage point, the biggest hike in 22 years.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: Inflation is much too high. And we understand the hardship that is causing.\par \par \uc2 KENT: The Feds goal, slow down spending to bring inflation down, which is soaring an 8.5 percent, a 40-year high. Prices are spiking across the board from rent, to groceries, to gas. Today, the national average for a gallon of regular now $4.23, 10 cents higher than just a week ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have not been able to do to compete fill up because, you know, it`s -- I`m on a basically a fixed income.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Now, higher interest rates will make loans more expensive, including new auto loans and credit card debt. Interest rates on mortgages today hitting 5.41 percent up from just under three percent a year ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our business could potentially start to dip based on the fact that people are going to have to change their buying habits.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Chicago restaurant owner Terri Evans worry she`ll have to pass along the higher cost of her business loans to her customers as she tries to expand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s not an easy discussion because we still want to do right by our customers as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KENT: As for fears of a possible recession, Fed Chair Powell says there`s nothing to suggest one is close. He expects more half point increases in the coming months. And the Dow rose nearly three percent on that news. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Jo, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New pressure on Russia tonight. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  proposing a ban on Russian oil imports by year`s end. It comes as fighting rages around that steel plant in Mariupol and elsewhere in Ukraine. And news of the deadliest attack on civilians may have been far worse than reported. Kelly Cobiella is there tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Ukrainian officials say Russia is losing ground despite almost constant shelling on the northeastern city of Kharkiv. Ukrainian troops say they`ve now regained control of at least 11 villages in the region. Soldiers finding homes in ruins bodies of villagers and Russian soldiers left behind.\par \par \uc2 We lived in the basement for two months, this woman says. The Russians pointed guns at us and stole everything.\par \par \uc2 In Mariupol, a new investigation from the A.P. using eyewitness accounts and floor plans suggest the mid-March bombing of the theater they`re killed close to 600 people, not the 300 initially believed, though an exact number cannot be determined.\par \par \uc2 And newly released satellite images show smoke rising from the city`s old steel plant where hundreds of civilians and fighters are hiding.\par \par \uc2 You guys don`t understand how scary it is, this woman said.\par \par \uc2 Marina`s husband is fighting in that plant. When we first met her at a shelter in Dnipro, she told us --\par \par \uc2 I just pray he`s alive and he`ll make it out. She hasn`t heard from him in five days.\par \par \uc2 Mariupol`s mayor today --\par \par \uc2 How many people, how many civilians are still there?\par \par \uc2 The Russians are still storming the plants even though they said they wouldn`t, the mayor said, telling me more than 200 civilians are still inside, including at least 20 children.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, firepower from the U.S. is reaching the battlefield. A senior U.S. Defense official says 90 percent of the Howitzers promised to Ukraine are now in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Tonight, that senior U.S. Defense official says most of the Russian forces in and around Mariupol have now moved north likely to join the fight in Donbas. And only about 2000 are still in the city. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly Cobiella, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, we`re tracking yet more severe weather tonight in the middle of the country and parts of the south. Bill Karin is watching it all. Bill, what`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST (on camera): Good evening, Lester. It`s a dangerous evening and in many cases it`s going to be a sleepless night as these storms will explode in the next couple of hours and travel through Oklahoma and Texas. We have tornado watches that are up already for a numerous locations in Oklahoma and Texas.\par \par \uc2 And this area of red, north of Dallas, right along the Red River all the way from Wichita Falls to Oklahoma City, that`s the areas of greatest concern for not just tornadoes, but strong tornadoes this evening and baseball sized hail falling from the sky. That`s three inches in diameter.\par \par \uc2 And then tomorrow, we track all this severe weather into areas of Arkansas, Little Rock, Memphis, all the way down to Shreveport. And if this wasn`t enough, we also we`re going to see these storms dumping a ton of rain. And we have a flash flood threat tonight through Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Bill, thanks for keeping an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the attack on comedian Dave Chappelle as he was onstage. The charges the suspect faces. And \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . testified before the committee investigating the January 6 attack.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with that shocking moment of the famed Hollywood Bowl. Comedian Dave Chappelle attacked by an armed man who rushed the stage during his performance. Miguel Almaguer on the charges the suspect faces and the security questions being raised.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It unfolded in seconds when a man jumped onto the stage and tackled Dave Chappelle. In video obtained by TMZ, Chappelle pops back up. The attacker chased across the stage. That chaotic scene playing out before a stunt crowd.\par \par \uc2 This man identified as 23-year-old Isaiah Lee was booked on suspicion of felony assault with a deadly weapon. Police say he had this replica gun which has a blade that extends from the barrel.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CHAPPELLE, COMEDIAN: He`s back there being stomped.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: In recent months, Chappelle has come under fire by the trans community which he joked about last night after the attack.\par \par \uc2 CHAPPELLE: That was a trans man.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Police have not yet released a motive. With Chappelle spokesperson saying, he refuses to allow last night`s incident to overshadow the magic of this historic moment." There are now questions over how someone could smuggle a weapon into LA`s iconic Hollywood Bowl where there are metal detectors and security.\par \par \uc2 CURTIS SHAW FLAGG, PRESIDENT, LAUGH FACTORY CHICAGO OPERATIONS: The first thought was how was there not security that was there before he even made it, you know, to have that physical contact. After the attack, Chappelle was joined by comedians Jamie Foxx and Chris Rock who was infamously slapped on stage by Will Smith and broke the tension after the incident.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ROCK, ACTOR: Was that Will Smith?\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: A moment of levity after an attack. That was no laughing matter. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, up next for us. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leaves his mark on a key midterm race. Is it a sign of things to come?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with the January 6 investigation. We`re now learning that \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., the former President`s eldest son was interviewed by the committee investigating the Capitol riot for two hours on Tuesday via video conference according to a person close to him. His sister \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  testified before the committee last month.\par \par \uc2 And for former President Trump, a big first test of his power to steer the Midterm elections. The Senate candidate he endorsed in Ohio, author J.D. Vance, coming from behind in the polls to win that state`s primary last night. Vaughn Hillyard is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The American political right moving even closer to Donald Trump`s image on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: They wanted to write a story that this campaign would be the death of Donald Trump`s America first agenda. Ladies and gentlemen, it isn`t the death of the America first agenda.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: J.D. Vance winning a crowded GOP primary for Ohio`s \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat leaping roughly 20 percentage points since Trump endorsed him.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to pick somebody that`s going to win. And this man is going to win. Come on up. J.D.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Vance is an ally in spreading Trump`s false 2020 election conspiracies. And he`s close to other Trump loyalists like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What ultimately made you decide J.D. Vance?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Vance had said, if Trump enters the White House again, he should push the envelope of executive power.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Fire every single mid-level bureaucrat, every civil servant in the administrative state, replace them with our people.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And part of his America first foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I got to be honest with you, I don`t really care what happens to Ukraine one way or another.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  will now face Vance in the red-trending state.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): People in Ohio look at the candidate, and they say who`s most Ohio? Who`s fighting for us?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: On the Republican side, voters said that person for them is Vance. His victory a win for Trump rattling Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (on camera): This was just the first of several critical tests for Trump`s influence in primaries over the next three weeks. Next up, Nebraska and West Virginia. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Vaughn, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as COVID deaths rise again, America crosses an unthinkable milestone. I`ll have some reflections next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Before we go, a few words about the news that broke shortly before we came on the air tonight. An NBC News count showing this country has now reached more than one million COVID deaths.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Today`s soul crushing milestone comes just as we begin to peek out from behind our masks, lower our guard, wheeling the pandemic to be over. But the slowly rising tally of the dead won`t let it be and forces us to confront some tough questions. Like, how many of those one million deaths might have been prevented?\par \par \uc2 We counted on the tools, the vaccines, the masks, the distancing, but we forgot about the unpredictability of free will, miss trust in science, and simple human behavior. It was hard enough to process 750,000 deaths, 500,000, a quarter-million before that. Each milepost leaving us as unsure as the next as to what such a moment called for.\par \par \uc2 Maybe because the pandemic changed our rituals of death and grief, tearful goodbyes between families and dying loved ones not at bedsides, but via iPad, funerals delayed or bypassed altogether. At the moments we most needed the comfort of others, we were forced to be apart. How could it not have changed us?\par \par \uc2 One million dead sounds like an ending to a horrible story, not a chapter. But that`s what it is. A number that shakes our consciousness, demands our attention, forces us to pause, and consider who and what we have lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): We`ve got to believe there are better days ahead. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220505ei54000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Today for May 4, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Kristen Welker, Blayne Alexander,  Jesse Kirsch, Erin McLaughlin, Craig Melvin, Jo Ling Kent, Dylan Dreyer,  Miguel Almaguer, Sam Brock, Carson Daly, Keir Simmons \par \uc2 6,653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Shock waves, the Supreme Court confirms the authenticity of that draft opinion overturning Roe versus Wade and the battle lines hardened from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The Chief Justice calling the leak, a betrayal and launching an investigation as both sides point fingers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): If it`s a conservative, you`re a traitor to the cause. If it`s a liberal, you`re the dumbest person in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Complete coverage straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, trump card J.D. Vance prevails in Ohio`s closely watched Republican Senate primary that come from behind victor after a late endorsement from the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J. D. VANCE, AMERICAN POLITICIAN: Ladies and gentlemen, it ain`t the death of the America First agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning, what it means for the race to hold Congress in this fall`s crucial midterms.\par \par \uc2 All eyes on the Fed, another key interest rate hike expected today, a new step in the fight to lower record inflation. Will it work and where your costs are going up.\par \par \uc2 Walkout, new video of the moment a convicted felon now on the run fled in Alabama jail alongside the corrections officer police believe helped him escape. What investigators are now revealing about their relationship as the manhunt enters day six, we`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, round two, millions in the Midwest bracing for another day of severe weather, including the potential for more tornadoes, which you could expect and where the threat is headed next.\par \par \uc2 And no laughing matter, overnight, comedian Dave Chappelle attacked by a man who stormed the