majorities and Republicans looking to take back control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Overnight, that bombshell from the Supreme Court continuing to rock Washington and the country firing up protesters on both sides after the High Court authenticated that leaked draft opinion by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , which finds the 1973 Roe v. Wade ruling which protects a woman`s right to an abortion was quote, egregiously wrong from the start. Justices can and do change their positions before a final decision.\par \par \uc2 But the draft shows four other conservatives joining Alito, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , the three Democratic appointed justices would likely dissent, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ` opinion still unclear. Based on the draft, there are enough votes to overthrow the decision that has established a federal right to abortion since 1973. If that happens, access to abortion would become a patchwork system across the country in the hands of the states. Something many opponents of abortion rights have been pushing for.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every state should have the right to allow consensus in its state to make its way into the law.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Democrats now aiming to energize voters around their message. Roe v. Wade should be codified into law by Congress. But currently there`s not enough support in Congress for such a move. President Biden warning, Republicans could try to take away other rights that were granted by the High Court from contraception to same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:13]\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It would mean that every other decision we made to the notion of privacy is thrown into question.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Senator Elizabeth Warren joining a protest yesterday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I am angry because of who will pay the price for this.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): For their part, many Republicans cheered the possibility that Roe could be overturned but blasted the leak itself.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: If it`s a conservative, you`re a traitor to the cause. If it`s a liberal, you`re the dumbest person in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Moderate Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , who voted to confirm conservative Justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh says she feels misled by their responses about Roe v. Wade, during their respective confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT: settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT: That`s the law of the land. I accept the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, Senator Collins saying if the draft opinion is the final decision on Roe versus Wade, it would be quote, completely inconsistent with what Justice Kavanaugh And Gorsuch said in their hearings, and in their private meetings with her. As for the case itself, the Supreme Court is expected to issue a final ruling within the next two months. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right Kristen Walker at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And as you just saw the battle lines are quickly being drawn from coast to coast with people on both sides of this emotional issue, give a new fuel for their arguments. So what is next, NBC`s Blayne Alexander is in Baton Rouge, Louisiana with that part of the story. Hey, Blayne, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Hoda, good morning to you. This abortion clinic here in Baton Rouge is one of three in the state of Louisiana, all of which would be immediately impacted if Roe were to be overturned. It`s because of what`s known as a trigger law something that nearly a third of the country already has in place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Abortion is a human right.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): From Chicago to San Diego.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Abortion is oppression.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): New York to the nation`s Capitol, the fight over abortion rights is spilling into the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve always supported life. I believe that every child should be born.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m afraid for my daughters. I`m afraid for my future granddaughters. I`m afraid for all women.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Even as the nation`s highest court says the bombshell leaked draft is not a final ruling, states are already swiftly preparing for action should the court strike down the landmark decision protecting abortion access. If Roe v. Wade is eventually overturned it will fall to each state to control abortion access. At least 20 would likely ban the procedure outright, 13 of those through so called trigger laws, automatically outlawing abortions immediately with few exceptions. It comes as some Republican-led states are already enacting a flurry of restrictive abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Arkansas is a pro-life state. We have already put a trigger law in place.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): In Oklahoma, the governor signed a law Tuesday banning the procedure after six weeks. And while 16 states have already passed laws codifying the right to abortion, Connecticut has gone one step further passing a bill to expand the list of medical professionals who can perform the procedure.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM TONG\uc2  (D-CT), ATTORNEY GENERAL: Half of this country like Connecticut, led by Connecticut will respect women, patients, doctors.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Nationwide clinics say their patients will be caught in the middle.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER, DIRECTOR OF JACKSON WOMEN`S HEALTH ORGANIZATION: It does make my job harder knowing that women won`t be able to access.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Shannon Brewer is director of Mississippi`s only abortion clinic, the one at the center of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: You`re going to see an influx of women doing things that they -- that are unsafe because out of being desperate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And back to that new law in Oklahoma, it`s just hours old with the impact already stretches well beyond Oklahoma`s borders to women who have come to the state from other states, including Texas to get the procedure. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Blayne Alexander for us, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And now to that breaking news overnight from Ohio, the results of a closely watched primary race with national implications, the Republican who received a late endorsement from former President Trump securing his party`s nomination for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . NBC`s Jesse Kirsch in Cleveland for us this morning. Jesse, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. The State Senate primary race a pivotal test of the former president`s influence over the Republican Party. J.D. Vance, the well-known author of "Hillbilly Elegy" got a late bump in the race after an endorsement for Mr. Trump. And last night he cruised to victory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): This morning, former President Donald Trump`s grip on the Republican Party looks even tighter heading into the pivotal midterm elections this fall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I love you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I love you too.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (voice-over): Overnight, J.D. Vance projected the winner of Ohio`s raucous Republican Senate primary, beating out six other candidates. His profile surging in the final weeks of the race after a late endorsement from the 45th president, some voters skeptical of the "Hillbilly Elegy" author who previously took swipes at Trump 