am here because the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  can change all of this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But their options are limited without 60 votes to pass it or enough Democratic votes to change Senate rules.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the codification of Roe makes a lot of sense.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still, the President tonight would not say if he would support scrapping the filibuster. The draft opinion also a surprise to some Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LISA MURKOWSKI (R-AK): It rocks my confidence in court right now.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: With another recusing two conservative justices of betraying their previous pledges about Roe.\par \par \uc2 BRET KAVANAUGH, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Senator, I said that it`s settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Republicans \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  today saying, if finalized, the opinion would be completely inconsistent with what Justice Gorsuch and Justice Kavanaugh said. Most Republicans are blasting the stunning breach of Supreme Court confidentiality, insisting the leaker should be punished.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): This is the consequence of the Democrats` rabidly partisan effort to undermine the court, to attack the court.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And noting if Roe is overturned, abortion would not be outlawed, but that states would decide whether it should be legal.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): This is going to push the conversation back to the people rather than unelected judges.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:09]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, now Democrats are trying to turn this into a major election issue. What`s that look like?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, that`s right, Lester. With Democrats fearing a possible wipeout in the Midterms, a Biden advisor tonight tells me this threat to abortion rights is a new opportunity to reenergize their base. The President tonight saying it`ll fall on voters to elect pro-abortion rights lawmakers. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220505ei530002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Appears Poised To Overturn Roe Versus Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams \par \uc2 972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the unprecedented leak. The Supreme Court appears poised to overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The court confirming the leaked opinion that would end nearly 50 years of abortion protections is authentic but not final. The leak sending shockwaves across the country. Protests erupting outside the court. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  calling the leak and egregious breach of trust and ordering an investigation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden slamming the draft as radical. The question tonight, what would America without Roe look like?\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the first group of about 100 evacuees from that Mariupol steel plant making it to safer ground. But Russia resuming its attacks on the complex where hundreds remain trapped. Our team inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 New images inside the Alabama jail where a corrections officer was accused of helping an inmate escape. The special relationship the sheriff now says the two had.\par \par \uc2 Voters going to the polls in Ohio`s critical primary. How it`s putting Donald Trump`s hold over the GOP to the test.\par \par \uc2 And using the glass that people throw away to protect the environment. The innovative recycling mission inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. The political landscape rumbling loudly tonight after the explosive leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion overturning Roe versus Wade, the landmark decision that established the constitutional right to abortions in this country.\par \par \uc2 The draft obtained and posted online by Politico. While such a decision by the conservative-leaning court has been widely expected, the magnitude and nature of the leak itself is another story. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  today confirming the draft was genuine but calling the leak a breach of trust and ordering an investigation into who did it.\par \par \uc2 The decision a sharply worded takedown of Roe v Wade has abortion opponents celebrating tonight while leaving abortion rights supporters angry and worried. If the decision carries, legalizing abortions would be left to the states. Tonight, roughly half poised to declare their procedure illegal.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot to unpack here tonight. Justice Correspondent Pete Williams has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We say pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Demonstrators flocked to the Supreme Court shortly after Politico broke the news Monday night, an unprecedented leak of an entire draft opinion in one of the court`s most consequential cases saying the landmark Roe v Wade decision should be overruled.\par \par \uc2 Written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , it says there`s no support for Roe in any part of the Constitution and "a right to abortion is not deeply rooted in the nation`s history and traditions." The authority to decide whether abortion is legal, he says, should be left to the people and their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 While the publication of the draft is a shock, the conclusion of the draft shouldn`t be. It was apparent when the case was argued December 1st that at least five justices were prepared to overturn Roe including Justice Alito who was an aggressive questioner.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, \uc2 U.S. SUPREME COURT\uc2 : Can it be said that the right to abortion is deeply rooted in the history and traditions of the American people?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But the draft opinion says because abortion involves taking the life of a fetus, it`s in a class by itself. Striking down Roe, Alito writes, would not undercut other constitutional protections such as for interracial and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 After at first declining comment, the Supreme Court confirmed 15 hours after the draft was published that it was authentic. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  launched an immediate investigation of who leaked it. He called the leak a singular and egregious breach of trust, and said if it was intended to undermine the integrity of the courts operations, it will not succeed.\par \par \uc2 The work of the court, he said, will not be affected in any way. But the court is now in turmoil with suspicion that one of the courts law clerks might have been the leaker.\par \par \uc2 TOM GOLDSTEIN, CO-FOUNDER AND PUBLISHER, SCOTUS BLOG: This has never happened in American history. And the court may never be the same when it comes to the trust between the justices and all of their law clerks because it has only been about trust.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The case at issue involves the fate of a Mississippi law passed in 2018 but so far blocked by a lawsuit that would ban abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Lower courts said it would violate the Supreme Court`s abortion precedents beginning with Roe in 1973 that say states cannot ban abortion before the age of fetal viability. That`s around 23 weeks into the pregnancy. A decision in the case was expected by late June.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Pete, let me ask you. How does the court plan to proceed in investigating this leak?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Chief Justice Roberts has given that assignment to Gail Conway, the courts marshal, the chief security officer. Leak investigations are never easy. This one won`t be but it could lead to new security measures that make it harder to leak draft opinions, Lester, with a lot of passion here tonight.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:06]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yes, we can hear it. Pete Williams at the Supreme Court tonight, thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220505ei5300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Court Confirming The Leaked Opinion That Would End Nearly 50 Years Of Abortion Protections Is Authentic But Not Final\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 629 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Some states are not waiting for the court to strike down Roe v. Wade. Late today, Oklahoma`s governor signed a bill banning abortions there after about six weeks of pregnancy. Stephanie Gosk on how this could play out across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It is not a final decision, but much of today`s reaction to the Supreme Court imagines the day it will be. There are those celebrating.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Life is precious. It`s a miracle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It certainly is a dramatic moment. Yes, Roe has been enormously controversial for nearly 50 years and we applaud the court for its courage.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Others worry what this will mean.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s heartbreaking. And as a woman who has benefited from Roe v. Wade, but also as a health care provider, this is an attack on women.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The decision to make abortions illegal will not stop them from happening. It will only stop them from happening safely.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Should Roe versus Wade be overturned? 25 states will likely ban abortion. 13 of them have trigger laws, meaning it will be immediately outlawed. Some states are looking to strengthen access. A recent bill passed in Connecticut expands the list of professionals who can perform abortions and protects them from out-of-state lawsuits or criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 ANNA RUPANI, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, FUND TEXAS CHOICE: Our clients are traveling 1338 miles roundtrip.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The fund Texas Choice group helps women travel out of state for abortions. Texas passed a law in September banning the procedure after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been inundated with calls since that law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 RUPANI: It`s been just -- it`s unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The vast majority of those seeking help are low-income and either Black or Latino. Some have been crossing state lines to the only clinic in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER, DIRECTOR, JACKSON WOMEN`S HEALTH ORGANIZATION CLINIC: We were seeing patients three days. Now, we`re up to five. In some weeks, six days a week.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: This clinic at the heart of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: We are looking into -- we`re opening a facility right now in New Mexico that will be one of the states where patients will still be able to obtain an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: So, your organization is actually looking to set up new clinics in other states?\par \par \uc2 BREWER: Correct. We are -- we are not going to just walk away from this.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: What will happen to the women, many of them low income, who every year get abortions in states like Mississippi, Texas, places like that?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE GLENN FOSTER, PRESIDENT AND CEO, AMERICANS UNITED FOR LIFE: We`re going to continue to focus on those women, focusing on the humanity of those women and of providing them with holistic life-affirming options to make sure that they get access to the resources and the help that they need.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: While roughly half of states get ready to meet higher demand for abortions. It is an issue that has long divided the country. And if Roe v Wade is overturned, those divisions may grow deeper. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220505ei5300002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  On The Lessons Learned From COVID To Avoid The Next Pandemic And His High Profile Divorce Announced One Year Ago Today\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie \par \uc2 3,300 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: So we turn to a Today live exclusive. \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  co- founded \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  nearly 50 years ago at the forefront of the computer age that changed the world. And since then, he`s been using the fortune that earned him to change the world, the \uc2 Gates Foundation\uc2  giving away tens of billions of dollars over the past decade. He also famously predicted the world was unprepared for a pandemic in a 2015 TED talk that was unfortunately accurate and has been viewed now 43 million times. Well, he`s sounding the alarm again this morning. His new book, "How to Prevent the Next Pandemic" is out today. It`s also been a year of upheaval in his own life after he and Melinda, his wife of 27 years, announced their divorce one year ago today. So Bill, we have so much to catch up on. It`s good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES, AMERICAN BUSINESS MAGNATE: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, I think this is the definition of what they call a hard sell. You`re out here promoting a book "How to Prevent the Next Pandemic" and, you know, people are sick and tired of hearing about the pandemic. They have COVID fatigue. Why is this the moment to have this conversation?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, I don`t want us to wait until we forget about how awful this has been. I mean, we`ve had tens of millions of deaths, you know, trillions of dollars of economic loss, education loss meant until depression. In with a few key steps, we can make sure this won`t happen again.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: There`s something strangely optimistic about this book, you`ve got a whole bunch of ideas on how to actually prevent the next pandemic. One idea, you compare it to firefighters, we need kind of a global firefighting team that`s ready to find the next pandemic and respond to it. How would it work?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, in firefighting, we`re all trained to know that, you know, there`s the exit. The U.S. alone has over 300,000 full time firefighters. So we take it seriously because if one house burns down, it can, you know, affect an entire community. Pandemics are even worse. And we didn`t practice. We weren`t ready to go. A few countries that were more ready, like Australia have 10 percent the deaths that we have. So the debate about exactly how to invest in that preparedness now is the right time, even though we`re still trying to get out of this one.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, you`ve argued that basically pandemics, disease is inevitable, but pandemics are not. And if you spend billions now, you save trillions later. Is that a pretty good summary of the book?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Yes, a big return.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Tell me about, you have this germ team that you propose. We have the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 , why isn`t that enough?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, they`re not funded actually to go to countries and practice. They`re not funded to fly in when there`s an outbreak. So they need about a billion a year, a 3,000 more people that would stay dedicated to pandemics. You know, pandemics don`t come very often. So it`s easy to take your personnel and go work on other things. And here, we`d make sure that this team had those skills and must always practicing.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: By the way, in some ways we were lucky with this pandemic. It certainly could have been a more contagious virus and it could have been more lethal.\par \par \uc2 GATES: Yes, the lethality, you know, ends up being about 0.3 percent. Smallpox is 30 percent. So, this is not the worst case all the more reason to make these investments in preparedness.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: When you look at how the U.S. and the world responded to COVID-19, whether it`s masks or vaccines, or shutdowns, closures, it`s become so political. I wonder if it happened again, if it was March 2020, all over again, with COVID-19, would we even be able to mount as effective a response as we did last time around? It`s been so politicized?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Yes, it`s unfortunate that we didn`t get trusted voices in both parties talking about the benefit of masks and vaccines, so that it wasn`t a political issue. I think everybody does support the health workers who were heroes. I think they support the innovation where we got the vaccine out faster than ever before, and that has saved millions of lives. Even that vaccine, we can make a better one where you never get infected. So, you know, innovation, like in many areas is where I see a potential for consensus and for avoiding most of the damage.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, the book, if you want to geek out on some of the innovations and where the technology is, a vaccine, you can inhale a pan vaccine. It`s in there. But let`s talk about misinformation because that has been a hallmark, unfortunately, of this pandemic. President Biden rather famously said last July that misinformation on social media is killing people. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Absolutely. It`s been weird that, you know, vaccines have been attacked, as you know, being overall that negative, or there`s some conspiracy here, it`s terrible.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, some of it affects you. You`re part of these conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 GATES: That is a very weird thing that just because I support vaccines to save millions of lives, people are saying no, I, you know, I make money from vaccines, or that I`m trying to, you know, cause death or track or a lot of strange stuff. Hard to understand why that is.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, you know, misinformation is obviously a big issue that a lot of folks like you are worried about. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  just recently announced moves to acquire \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I wonder if you are concerned about the proliferation of misinformation, given some of his views about expanding what he refers to as free speech on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and what you think of the acquisition?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, the digital realm has facilitated, you know, kind of interesting, wrong ideas spreading very quickly. And we need to innovate. So that digital realm is more of a positive thing of getting the truth out, and that people are seeing, hey, this is false.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Do you worry about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, Elon, you wouldn`t want to underestimate Elon. What he did at \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is amazing, helping with climate change, what he did at \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , you know Will he, this time, make that improvement? You know, should there be laws that strike a better balance of free speech versus, you know, conspiracy theories, confusing people? You know, Elon thinks he can improve \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Well, I don`t know specifically what he`ll do. But, you know, it is, there`s an opportunity and we need innovation in that space.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, let`s talk about you personally. It`s been a period of transition. It was actually one year ago today, that you and your wife Melinda filed for divorce. How have you been coming to terms with this?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, the divorce is definitely a sad thing. You know, I have responsibility for causing a lot of pain in my family. You know, it was a tough year. I feel good that all of us are moving forward now. You know, my eldest got married. Melinda and I are continuing to work together. So, you know, it was sad and tragic. But, you know, now we`re moving together.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. She did an interview recently. And she talked about times in her marriage. She said she was lying on the floor, crying. What was it like for you to hear that and to hear it publicly?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, this was a very tough thing. We had a lot of amazing things in our marriage, the kids, the foundation, the enjoyment we had. And so it`s a very hard adjustment. You know, I know divorces are different, but, you know, in just a complete change, you know, we were partners we kind of grew up together. And now that`s different. We`re not married.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thankfully, there were allegations of extramarital affairs and when she was asked about that in the interview she said that is a question that Bill needs to answer. So here you are now, did that happen? Were you unfaithful in your marriage? Is that one of the reasons there was a divorce?\par \par \uc2 [07:40:12]\par \par \uc2 GATES: I certainly made mistakes. And I take responsibility, I don`t think delving into the particulars at this point is constructive, but yes, I caused pain. And I feel terrible about that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What did you learn from that? I mean, you were someone who has this voracious appetite for knowledge. And divorce is an experience that can be a journey to learning something about yourself and change, hopefully, what have you learned about yourself?\par \par \uc2 GATES: You know, there`s areas like climate or, you know, health where I have expertise. And on personal matters like this, I`m, you know, I don`t think of myself as an expert, I should be very humble about, you know, success, you know, has a tricky aspect to it. So, you know, I don`t have great advice for other people.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. I have to ask you about Jeffrey Epstein. Melinda mentioned that that was one of the strains your relationship with him. And you know why -- I guess the question is real simple, I mean, why did you continue to meet with him when you met him, he was already a convicted sex offender. You know, and do you regret that?\par \par \uc2 GATES: I certainly made a huge mistake. Not only meet him in the first place, but I met with him a number of times. I had a goal of raising money for global health. I didn`t realize that, you know, meeting with him, almost downplayed the incredibly awful things he did. You know, I learned more about that over time. But, you know, I`d add that to the list of big mistakes, including, you know, where Melinda`s advice was sound, and I should have followed it sooner than I did.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You never saw anything where you thought, this doesn`t feel right. Melinda kind of had a visceral reaction the first time she met him.\par \par \uc2 GATES: No, he was a bad person. And, you know, I had a reason that I thought those meetings would lead to something good, but I shouldn`t have done them.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And finally, on this topic, you`ve recently gave an interview you said you`d marry Melinda all over again. She says, you guys are friendly, not necessarily friends, but friendly. How do you see that relationship moving forward?\par \par \uc2 GATES: Well, one of the things we built together is the \uc2 Gates Foundation\uc2 . And we love that work. You know, we`ve got all the resources, I was lucky enough to get. We`ve got \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  committed massive resources. And so making sure that is spent well, saves lives. Melinda and I love doing that work together. So I feel very lucky that I still have that with her as well as, you know, we`ve got these three incredible kids.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know. And I heard they`re all moving out of the house. So you`re an empty nester for sure now.\par \par \uc2 GATES: I`ve got a big empty nest.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Bill, thank you so much. Thanks for being with us. And again, the book is called "How to Prevent the Next Pandemic" chock full of ideas. It`s out today.\par \par \uc2 [07:43:26]\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a lot more ahead including what we`re now learning about the sad and sudden loss of country icon, Naomi Judd and the growing tributes to her life and her legacy. But first these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back now, 7:47. Guess where we`re going? Back to Hawaii where Mr. Roker is all decked out too. Hey, Al.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Look at his shirt.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, aloha, guys. Thanks so much. We`ll explain why we`re here for Smuckers mission breakfast in just a bit. But first, let`s take a look at what`s going on out there. Temperature wise, first of all, we`ve got fire danger out west from Winslow to Albuquerque, Pueblo, 3 million people. And that translates to a critical fire risk for a huge area stretching from Vegas to Roswell up to La Junta potential for rapid fire spread today.\par \par \uc2 Back to the east, we`ve got this frontal system that`s pushing in to up to Boston below average temperatures, above average from Cincinnati to Atlanta, but behind it Chicago, Des Moines, Springfield, all very chilly. And then tomorrow that moves to the east bringing cooler weather although right along the I-95 Corridor from D.C. down south, it stays warm. But then everybody cools down by the time we hit Mother`s Day. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hey guys here from Joint Base Pearl Harbor, do you guys like waffles?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, wait until you see the waffles we`ve got. This is the tastiest, most pretty waffles you`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: How did you travel, you got a 12-hour flight home?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: He`s talking to waffles, all right. We`ll come back to in just a bit buddy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead this morning, we`re going to celebrate Teacher Appreciation Day.\par \par \uc2 [07:49:11]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And boy, do we have a special group. Guess what? They`re already having some fun. They`re at the roller rink. We`ve got inspiring stories. We got a couple of surprises along the way too. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Boy, we have missed you, Carson.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Boy, it`s good to be here. Thank you so much. Sheinelle is going to take us on a gilded glamor tour of the Met Gala from last night after a quick message.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It is 8:00 on "TODAY." Coming up, Supreme Court stunner. A leaked draft opinion suggests the nation`s highest court is preparing to overturn Roe versus Wade. So, what would that decision mean for abortion in the U.S.? And how did this unprecedented leak take place? We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Then, remembering Naomi Judd. New details in the country music legend`s death as new tributes pour in. One from her friend Dolly Parton.\par \par \uc2 Plus, TikTok teacher. We are celebrating some incredible educators this morning. Including one Florida teacher using social media to liven up her lesson plan. So, you don`t want to miss the special surprises with have in store for all of them.\par \par \uc2 And red carpet ready. Some of the biggest celebrities step out for the annual Met Gala.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARLIE KLOSS, FASHION MODEL: It`s magic. It`s like a fairytale.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ll show you the can`t miss looks from fashion`s biggest night. Today, Tuesday, May 3, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hello Omaha, Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And Richville, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating my retirement.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From St. Petersburg, Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Here for Shiayan`s (ph) graduation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Hagerstown, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: First mother/son trip to New York.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Nashville.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Good morning to our kids.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In Topeka,\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And Axtell, Kansas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Plymouth, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Celebrating mom`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Huntsville, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Today is our 25th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Happy anniversary. Happy everything. Welcome back to "TODAY" It`s a Tuesday morning. We love when you start your day with us. So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Finally getting a little springy out there on the plaza.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Finally.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Getting that --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- little spring on our steps, too, guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Good to have you back, by the way.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. We`re going to get right to our news at 8:00. A bombshell leak from the Supreme Court. Appears to suggest that the court is poised to strike down Roe versus Wade and federal protection for abortion rights. NBC`s Justice Correspondent Pete Williams has the latest on what is an apparent draft, not the opinion itself.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Pete, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hoda, good morning. There is no way to confirm this and the Supreme Court isn`t commenting. But it`s almost certainly authentic because it was clear back in December when this case was argued that the court was preparing to overturn Roe V. Wade. Politico has now published the full of what it says it the draft opinion written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . And it says, "The inescapable conclusion is that the right to abortion is not deeply rooted in the nation`s history and traditions."\par \par \uc2 The court is considering the fate of a Mississippi law passed in 2018 but so far blocked by a lawsuit that would ban abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Lower courts said it would violate the Supreme Court`s abortion precedence beginning with Roe in 1973 that say States cannot ban abortion before the age of fetal viability, that`s about 23 weeks into the pregnancy. If the Supreme Court does end up overruling Roe it would not make abortion illegal but it would allow each individual State to decide whether to ban it and about half the States have already said that they would. And this is remarkable for another reason, no one can remember a Supreme Court draft opinion leaking like this, especially in such a high- profile case. Hoda.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Pete Williams for us there at the Court House. Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We turn now to the three things to watch in Ukraine where Russian missiles struck the strategic Port City of Odessa overnight. Russia says it hit an airfield being used to transport sent by the West. Ukraine says the strikes took out a bridge and a school dormitory.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, some of the 100 civilians evacuated from that steel plant in Mariupol have started reaching Ukrainian-controlled territory. Russia continued to attack the plant where soldiers and other civilians have been trapped for weeks. And a senior U.S. diplomat is warning that Russia plans to annex much of Eastern Ukraine as early as this month.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220504ei530008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Stunning Breach That Raises Many Questions\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 1,267 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: And as you can imagine, news of this stunning leak has led to a lot of reaction overnight, especially in Washington. NBC`s chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker on the politics of this. Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning to you. There are shockwaves all across the country and here at the White House where they are still processing this news. A senior administration official telling me this morning the White House will not comment directly on the leaked report for the reason that Pete just talked about its unprecedented nature, but what you can expect from the White House today, officials say they will reiterate the President`s strong support for Roe v. Wade, this as People on both sides of this issue have already made their voices heard at the High Court overnight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Overnight, hundreds gathering at the steps of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , protesters erupting there after the dramatic report. In a joint statement Democrats Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  accusing some of the conservative justices on the bench of lying during their confirmation hearings about their willingness to overturn Roe, adding, if the report is accurate, the Supreme Court is poised to inflict the greatest restriction of rights in the past 50 years, not just on women, but on all Americans. While some Republicans focusing on the explosive leak.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: I think this is plainly an attempt by the left to try and change the outcome in this case and corrupt the process.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): The president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  speaking out overnight.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD: We`ve never lost a constitutional right. And it is absolutely outrageous.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): While the group Susan B. Anthony List which opposes abortion rights, said we wholeheartedly applaud the decision. If the High Court does overturn Roe versus Wade over 20 states are poised to immediately ban abortion, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which supports abortion rights, making it almost certain the fight over Roe v. Wade will be a major issue in the upcoming midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): And it makes the election for the House and the Senate more important than any we`ve seen in our lifetime when it comes to the protection of women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So what are people actually saying about this? Well, an NBC News poll in August found that a majority of Americans 54 percent said abortion should remain legal. And you can expect as I just said, this will be a new flashpoint in the midterm elections with Democrats bracing for the possibility of big losses. The question is will they find a way to reenergize key voters with this issue? And will Republicans find a way to use the issue to build on their momentum, Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Kristen Welker at the White House. Let`s get some analysis with Pete Williams, our justice correspondent, also \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , a former clerk for Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , the former acting Solicitor General, and a supporter of abortion rights. And, Neal, before we get into the substance of the opinion, this is beyond unheard of that I am sitting here holding a draft opinion of the Supreme Court. What makes you feel that it is legitimate, that this is not some kind of fake, but that this is a real draft opinion? You`ve worked at the court.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, FORMER CLERK FOR SUPREME COURT JUSTICE \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 : Yes. So and I held draft opinion, Savannah, and they look exactly like this. I mean, I could imagine some deep state for an act -- I mean, some foreign government could have invested the kinds of resources to do this, but it`s impossible -- it`s really impossible. This is a legitimate draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 It uses language that Justice Alito is known to use, as Pete says, it tracks what Justice Alito said at the oral argument. It`s just devastating to read. It`s only a draft opinion. It`s possible the court could change its mind. But what this draft opinion says is that five justices after they heard oral argument in the Mississippi case in December, adopted the most full throated opinion, full throated position they could which is to fully overrule Roe versus Wade and uphold the Mississippi law, which has no rape bricks incest exception, so that can now be the wall in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Pete, as mentioned there appeared to be five justices who are ready to overturn Roe. How far does the reasoning in this opinion go, you know, obviously Roe found a right to privacy in the Constitution that`s been used subsequently to for all kinds of constitutional rights. So how narrowly drawn is this opinion?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, it`s not narrowly drawn at all. And it says that the Roe was fatal from the beginning, from the root on down. And I think one of the things that`s surprising about this leak, Savannah, is I`m not sure what the point of it is. You know, people leak in Washington for all sorts of reasons, try to change policy outcomes. I don`t see how this leak changes anything. I think all nine justices came into the courtroom, knowing how they were going to vote on this case. I think they voted that way after the case was argued. And I don`t think this leak is going to change any minds. I can`t understand unless it`s just I know something you don`t know. I can`t understand the point of this.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, Neal, let`s turn to you on that. Again, you worked at the court, let`s do the forensics, it`s got to be a rather small universe of people who have access to an opinion like that, who might be able to leak it, who would have the motive, and what might that motive be?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I can`t 