0:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Pete, as mentioned there appeared to be five justices who are ready to overturn Roe. How far does the reasoning in this opinion go, you know, obviously Roe found a right to privacy in the Constitution that`s been used subsequently to for all kinds of constitutional rights. So how narrowly drawn is this opinion?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, it`s not narrowly drawn at all. And it says that the Roe was fatal from the beginning, from the root on down. And I think one of the things that`s surprising about this leak, Savannah, is I`m not sure what the point of it is. You know, people leak in Washington for all sorts of reasons, try to change policy outcomes. I don`t see how this leak changes anything. I think all nine justices came into the courtroom, knowing how they were going to vote on this case. I think they voted that way after the case was argued. And I don`t think this leak is going to change any minds. I can`t understand unless it`s just I know something you don`t know. I can`t understand the point of this.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, Neal, let`s turn to you on that. Again, you worked at the court, let`s do the forensics, it`s got to be a rather small universe of people who have access to an opinion like that, who might be able to leak it, who would have the motive, and what might that motive be?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I can`t imagine. I mean, honestly, Savannah, this is so foreign. You know, the justices, the law clerks, take the traditions here, so, so seriously. And as Pete says, it`s hard to understand the point of the leak. I mean, there is a world in which we don`t know how Chief Justice Roberts is going to vote in this case. But Chief Justice Roberts is kind of irrelevant at this point. There`s Justice Alito plus four justices to the right of the Chief Justice who`ve signed on to this tenant of opinion.\par \par \uc2 So even if Chief Justice Roberts vote is in play, unfortunately, his vote doesn`t matter. He`s lost the court. Th